ar\bullet\tab \uc2 Republicans Rally To The Defense Of Georgia Senate Candidate Herschel Walker Facing Devastating Abuse Accusations From His Own Son And A Rep...}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc88"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 Herschel Walker, The GOP Candidate Who Has Called For A Ban On Abortion, Denying That He Paid For An Ex-Girlfriend`s Abortion More Than A Decade Ago}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc89"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 Conversation With Clea Shearer, About Her Breast Cancer Journey}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc90"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for October 4, 2022, NBC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc91"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 How To Identify Your Personal Breast Cancer Risks And The Steps You Can Take To Reduce It}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc92"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 An Exclusive Interview With Katie Couric}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc93"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 Meet the Press for October 2, 2022, NBC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc94"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 Katie Couric`s Message On The Importance Of Those Yearly Mammograms As She Reveals Her Own Breast Cancer Diagnosis}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc95"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 Today for September 29, 2022, NBC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc96"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 Meet The Press for September 18, 2022, NBC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc97"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for September 12, 2022, NBC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc98"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 MEET THE PRESS for September 11, 2022, NBC}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK \\l "toc99"}}{\fldrslt{\fs20\cf2\par\bullet\tab \uc2 DATELINE for September 9, 2022, NBC}}}\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc0}{\*\bkmkend toc0}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for June 19, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Tom Costello, Janis Mackey Frayer, Blayne Alexander,  Richard Engel, Anne Thompson, Diana Olick, Ron Allen \par \uc2 3,901 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, the desperate surge in the North Atlantic for a missing submersible near the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): The U.S. and Canadian coast guard scouring for a sub- carrying five people. Contact lost for more than 24 hours as the tourist vessel made its way toward the Titanic 12,000 feet down. Rough seas, rain, and fog hampering the surge in rescue efforts for those on board, including a billionaire businessman and explorer. Late details just in tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the high-stakes summit between China`s President Xi Jinping and Secretary of State \uc2 Anthony Blinken\uc2 . A chance to reset rising tensions that have only gotten worse since the U.S. shot down a spy balloon. Our one-on-one interview with the Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 The nonstop threat of tornadoes, one dead and almost two dozen injured as multiple tornadoes tear through Mississippi. The search for survivors.\par \par \uc2 A weekend of mass shooting, some of them deadly. But when it happened twice last month in another country, they took quick and decisive action. Richard Engel is there.\par \par \uc2 The new boom in building, not to buy, but to rent. What`s behind it in our series "PRICED OUT." On Juneteenth, celebrations across America and a field of dreams reborn as a testament to the past.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome. Tonight, near the site of one of the 20th century`s worst ocean disasters, a new undersea drama is unfolding. Right now, the \uc2 U.S. Coast Guard\uc2  leading the urgent search for a group of modern-day adventurers after the submersible carrying them to the Titanic shipwreck lost contact with the surface yesterday about an hour and 45 minutes into their journey.\par \par \uc2 Five people are on board the vessel which is used to carry paying passengers to see the Titanic up close. The search happening about 350 miles off the coast of Newfoundland in waters more than two miles deep. Tonight, the company that runs the deep sea charters, Ocean Gate Expeditions, saying in a statement, our entire focus is on the crew members and their families.\par \par \uc2 Tom Costello has late-breaking developments for us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): U.S. and \uc2 Canadian Coast Guard\uc2  ships are this hour rushing to the North Atlantic as two U.S. C-130 planes surveyed the vast ocean surface for any signs of a private submersible and the five people on board, last seen Sunday morning as it descended to survey the wreck of the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADM. JOHN MAUGER, COMMANDER, U.S. COAST GUARD FIRST DISTRICT: During the course of the dive, they lost communications and we have not had communications with the submersible since then.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voiceover): The 21-foot submersible named Titan, made of titanium and carbon fiber, is owned by Ocean Gate, an operator of small subs for research, film production, and tourist trips to the Titanic for $250,000. On board a pilot and four passengers, including billionaire adventurer Hamish Harding, who had taken a submersible to the Mariana Trench and flown in space on \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 ` \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On his \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account Sunday, he signed his name on the logo as he announced he`d be diving to the Titanic. His company later tweeted, 4:00 a.m. start this morning on the RMS Titanic Mission 5. The sub had a successful launch and Hamish is currently diving.\par \par \uc2 Today, Ocean Gate said our entire focus is on the crew members in the submersible and their families. We are working toward the safe return of the crew members. The CEO told our Seattle KING 5 last August he caters to Titanic enthusiasts.\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON RUSH\uc2 , CEO, OCEAN GATE: We have a number of people who come with us, who sometimes we referred to as Titaniacs, people who have just been consumed by the Titanic. And it was just great to take them down.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voiceover): The Titanic rests at treacherous depths, 12,500 feet below the surface. 1,500 people died when she sank in 1912. The missing sub was only supposed to be gone for six to eight hours with 96 hours of reserve oxygen. Already, Canadian P-8 search planes have dropped sonar buoys on the rough seas, listening for the sub in a deep, vast expanse of ocean.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Are they listening for voices? What could they pick up?\par \par \uc2 MAUGER: We`re hoping that they can pick up any unusual sounds, maybe tapping on the hull or voices or those types of things, any signs of the vassal mare in the water. And that`s why we`ve really pressed in with the capabilities that we have.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Now, Tom as I understand it, submersible are meant to dive much deeper than say, a typical Navy sub. If the Coast Guard can locate it under the surface, can they get to it?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:03]\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): They can`t. That`s the problem. They don`t have the deep-sea rescue capabilities that the Navy has. So, both the U.S. and the Canadian navies are rushing those assets to the scene as well as contacting private companies who have those capabilities. But if they find this crew, it`s going to be a very, very challenging rescue operation and the clock is ticking, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Tom Castello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn out of that critical meeting in China today between President Xi Jinping and Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  with relations at a low point. Blinken now touting progress at an interview with Janis Mackey Frayer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 JANIS MACKEY FRAYER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, a high-stakes visit to Beijing, capped by a handshake. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  meeting China`s President Xi Jinping for 35 minutes to stop what officials call a downward spiral in relations.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Direct engagement and sustained communication at senior levels is the best way to responsibly manage our differences and ensure that competition does not veer into conflict.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voiceover): Xi`s message to Blinken, I hope that through this visit, Mr. Secretary, you`ll make more positive contributions to stabilizing China-U.S. relations.\par \par \uc2 There`s no shortage of flashpoints here, tension over Taiwan, China`s role in the fentanyl crisis, unanswered questions about the origins of COVID. Chinese officials not holding back on their grievances either, like U.S. export bans on technology.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, in an interview with NBC News, Blinken said the two-day visit has stabilized ties, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Both China and the United States, I think, recognized that we were in an increasingly unstable place in our relationship. I think this is the start of a process to put a little more stability into it.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voiceover): But China refused Blinken`s request to reopen military crisis lines that were cut off by Beijing nearly a year ago. Since then some close calls, including this Chinese warship sailing within 150 yards of a U.S. destroyer in the Taiwan Strait, and this Chinese fighter jet darting in front of an American surveillance plane, something we saw for ourselves when we were on board another \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  plane intercepted by a Chinese jet.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: We`ve seen a couple of very dangerous incidents in the last couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER (voiceover): China says the encounters are justified to protect its sovereignty. Blinken`s first trip here was called off after that Chinese spy balloon which was shot down after it flew over the U.S. Senior U.S. officials told NBC News it collected intelligence from American military sites.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it was more embarrassing than it was intentionally.\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: So, with Beijing, the balloon incident is over. It`s water under the bridge?\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: Well, we did what we needed to do to protect our interests. We said what we needed to say and made clear what we needed and made clear in terms of this not happening again. And so, as long as it doesn`t, that chapter should be closed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Janis, Secretary Blinken insists the relationship is now stabilized, but was anything concrete accomplished?\par \par \uc2 FRAYER: Lester, relations are at such a low point that even having the meeting is seen by both sides as a positive. Other high-level visits can now follow with President Xi potentially meeting President Biden in the U.S. before the end of the year. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Janis Mackey Frayer in Beijing, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Mississippi, at least one person died and almost two dozen were injured by an overnight tornado, the latest in a wave of destructive storms. Blayne Alexander reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Across the South, it`s the unforgiving sound of summertime severe weather striking in the dead of night. Its latest target, Louin, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, that was big.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voiceover): Overnight, a reported tornado tore through this community an hour outside of Jackson, where people were rushed from their mangled homes on stretchers. Daylight reveals the true scope of the devastation, trees torn to shreds, homes now unrecognizable. All of it leaving more than two dozen people injured and claiming at least one life, 67-year-old Georgine Hayes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She`s a good person, good church person, you know.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voiceover): Her cousin, Lester, lives right across the street. He helped pull her from the rubble.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Her house where a black Jeep at --\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Right there.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was right there. It`s gone. There isn`t anything there.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: So it`s completely gone.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voiceover): It`s the latest in a deadly storm system sweeping across the South and central plains, producing at least 17 confirmed tornadoes in just four days. Among the towns hit, Perryton, Texas where the storms destroyed downtown and this mobile home community, killing three people. Oscar Hernandez lost his four businesses in a matter of minutes. He spoke to NBC`s Priscilla Thompson.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:02]\par \par \uc2 OSCAR HERNANDEZ, BUSINESS OWNER: It`s really hard. It`s really hard. All the lost, all the people that lost their lives.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voiceover): Severe weather continues to sweep the South. Today, a confirmed tornado along the Florida Panhandle and flash flooding in Alabama, an ominous sign of what`s to come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): And across the region, hundreds of thousands of people remain without power in some of the very same places that are seeing triple digit highs making cleanup like this all the more difficult with threats of more tornadoes tonight. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we have breaking news tonight about the first named storm in the Atlantic this season. Tropical Storm Bret has formed in the central Atlantic. It`s about 1,300 miles from the lesser Antilles. It is expected to strengthen to become a category one hurricane by tomorrow night and could impact Puerto Rico by the weekend. We`ll stay on top of that one.\par \par \uc2 Across America, the long weekend, a string of mass shootings. Near Chicago, 23 people were shot, one of them fatally during a Juneteenth event yesterday. In Kellogg, Idaho, four people were shot and killed. A suspect was arrested. And in San Francisco, six people were injured in a shootout involving two cars. Two were hit by stray bullets and two young girls were hit by the cars.\par \par \uc2 With little agreement in Washington over to how to stop the violence, tonight, Richard Engel reports on how lawmakers in Serbia responded following two mass shootings there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Serbians didn`t see it coming. On May 3rd, a 13-year-old boy stormed into a school with his father`s two guns and a hitlist and killed eight classmates. The next day, a man with a history of violence opened fire in a village, killing eight more. But then came more than thoughts and prayers. Tens of thousands demanded not just tighter gun controls, but a reorientation of society away from violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think a lot of us have just had enough.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): Zarco Svechik`s (ph) nine-year-old daughter Zora was in the school when the 13-year-old boy went on a killing spree.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: What would you say to Americans who`ve been through this and then see no change?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would say to them, you know, safeguard your democracy if you still have it and mobilize.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): Within weeks of the shootings here, new laws were introduced and old ones enforced. Now all sales of new guns are banned for two years. Gun owners must submit to in-person psychological and background checks. And owning an illegal gun is punishable by up to 15 years in prison. Many Serbians are turning in their guns voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Here`s a lot of AKs and assault rifles.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): There are 70,000 guns in this police warehouse surrendered under a no-questions-asked amnesty. The Deputy Interior Minister says this is only the start.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: What do you think when you see all of this?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voiceover): I see life saved, he said. Serbia is deeply divided politically, but overhauling gun laws was universally popular. Dobrica Veselinovic, is an opposition politician.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Why is it do you think you were able to cross political divides?\par \par \uc2 DOBRICA VESELINOVIC, SERBIAN POLITICIAN: We are not having these big gun lobbies and the big industry which is behind it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (on camera): The father we spoke, to and many others now want to push further, dialing back violence in the media and in political discourse. Lester.?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Richard, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, tracking who gets an abortion. One state tried to make sure personal data isn`t used to follow women who are driving there for the procedure. Plus, the new housing boom in rentals.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s no secret there is surveillance almost everywhere we go these days. Now, one state is taking action to prevent the tracking of women who may be driving to get an abortion. Anne Thompson explains.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): If it feels like you are being tracked, it may be because you are, at red lights going in and out of the office on your smartphone. But what if you could be tracked seeking a legal abortion simply by your license plate?\par \par \uc2 ALEXI GIANNOULIAS, SECRETARY OF STATE, ILLINOIS: I don`t think people realize how much of their personal data is collected, that their whereabouts are tracked.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): That`s why Illinois Secretary of State Alexi Giannoulias backs what he says is a first-in-the-nation bill to outlaw sharing or selling license plate data for women seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Who has access to license plate trackers?\par \par \uc2 GIANNOULIAS: A number of private and public entities have this personal sensitive data.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): Data he fears could be weaponized as some states try to criminalize helping someone get an abortion even in a state where it is legal. Three other states have passed laws aimed at protecting health data from such efforts.\par \par \uc2 GIANNOULIAS: When they travel, whether it`s to an abortion clinic or the grocery store, we need to make sure this data is protected.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): Illinois Right to Life Executive Director Mary Kate Zander opposes abortion.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Do you think this bill is necessary?\par \par \uc2 MARY KATE ZANDER, DIRECTOR, ILLINOIS RIGHT TO LIFE EXECUTIVE: I think it`s unnecessary. They`re trying to create a narrative around what the pro-life movement may or may not be doing.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): She says her organization is not trying to criminalize women seeking the procedure.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Is the right to life movement tracking license plates of women?\par \par \uc2 ZANDER: No, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Has it done that in the past?\par \par \uc2 ZANDER: No, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Do you see it doing that in the future?\par \par \uc2 ZANDER: Absolutely not. Absolutely not. That`s just not a tactic that we would use.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): With a quarter of their patients now from out of state, Illinois Planned Parenthood CEO Jennifer Welch is not convinced.\par \par \uc2 Patients need to know that when they come for health care, that information won`t be shared with anybody, law enforcement, activists, anybody else.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): Data collection and privacy, a new frontier in the nation`s fight over abortion rights. Anne Thompson, NBC News, Chicago.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Still to come here tonight, the rush to rent. Why more people are saying yes to signing lease and it`s shaking the foundation of housing in America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Trying to buy a home these days is a real struggle. Between high mortgage rates and low inventory, many are fighting themselves priced out. The CNBC`s Diana Olick explains, it`s fueling a boom in so-called build-to- rent properties.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIANA OLICK, CNBC SENIOR CLIMATE AND REAL ESTATE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Single mom Robin Ashford had planned to buy a home near Columbia, South Carolina last year but mortgage rates suddenly shot sharply higher.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ASHFORD, SINGLE MOM: I found out that I was going to have as much buying power as I would have about two or three years ago. And my mortgage, my monthly mortgage payments were too much that I could handle.\par \par \uc2 OLICK (voiceover): So, instead, Ashford turned to this brand new community built specifically for renters.\par \par \uc2 ASHFORD: I don`t have to worry about taxes on the home. I don`t have to worry about if something breaks, you know. It`s the landlord`s responsibility.\par \par \uc2 BRUCE MCNEILAGE, CO-FOUNDER, KINLOCH PARTNERS: They have large open floor plans.\par \par \uc2 OLICK (voiceover): And an increasing share of the landlords are now big builders like Bruce McNeilage`s Nashville-based Kinloch Partners as more Americans decide to rent not only by necessity but by choice.\par \par \uc2 MCNEILAGE: We`ve had a societal shift to people wanting to rent because they want a maintenance-free lifestyle or they want the ability to move to a city and just give the keys back to the landlord at the end of the lease.\par \par \uc2 OLICK (voiceover): Builders put up 68,000 new single-family rental homes last year, a 31 percent increase over the year before, according to John Burns` research.\par \par \uc2 JOHN BURNS, JOHN BURNS RESEARCH AND CONSULTING: I think it`s at the early stages of being a very, very big business, just like the apartment market is.\par \par \uc2 OLICK (voiceover): Burns counted 708 build-to-rent communities coming soon across the U.S. Entire communities of single-family homes built not for sale, but specifically for rent.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The people that want a home, but have now been priced out due to rising prices and then rising mortgage rates, are saying, well, I want a home, I`m going to rent for a while. So, it`s created more demand for the rental homes.\par \par \uc2 OLICK: And renters are demanding more of the builders, amenities like pools, fitness centers, dog parks, of course, on-site maintenance, and as in this community, bigger backyards.\par \par \uc2 OLICK (voiceover): But all that comes at a price. The average rent here is about $2,200 a month and single-family rents across the nation are rising.\par \par \uc2 ASHFORD: That`s something that I do need to think about because trying to stay for a home while paying high rent, it`s hard.\par \par \uc2 OLICK (voiceover): For now, she`s enjoying the new home, even if it`s not her new home. For NBC News, Diana Olick, Blythewood, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: When we come back here tonight, the boys of summer back on this historic field of dreams. The remarkable story of how fans brought this baseball stadium back from the brink.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Across the country today, people mark Juneteenth with parades, festivals, and marches marking the end of slavery in the U.S. And at this beloved baseball stadium, players and fans are reclaiming a part of African-American history on their very own field of dreams. Ron Allen explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At the Jackal`s home opener against the miners in Patterson, New Jersey, a huge comeback. The grand reopening of historic Hinchliffe Stadium itself.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE RANDOLPH, FORMER MLB PLAYERS MANAGER: I get goosebumps when I think about it. We need to keep this kind of venue and places like this, you know, flourishing.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN (voiceover): One of the last ballparks still standing from the days of baseball`s Negro leagues and segregated sports, a hallowed venue that for the past few decades had been shut down, abandoned, and about to be demolished.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: As worse, what do you remember about this?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN LOPINTO, CO-FOUNDER, FRIENDS OF HINCHLIFFE STADIUM: At its worst, what I remember are the trees growing in the stands, people living in the press box.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN (voiceover): Brian LoPinto helped lead a local effort to save the stadium, now a national historic landmark that just underwent a $100 million renovation. The Minor League Jackal`s new home once home for the New York Black \uc2 Yankees\uc2  and New York Cubans where legends such as Patterson`s own Larry Doby, who broke baseball`s color barrier after Jackie Robinson once played.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: A lot of legends played here. Do you feel that?\par \par \uc2 NILO RIJO, PLAYER, NEW JERSEY JACKALS: I can feel it. I mean, I`m grateful that I`m playing, you know, in the same place that a lot of, you know, legends played in.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE SAYEGH, MAYOR, PATERSON, NEW JERSEY: This is the real field of dreams. This stadium is sacred and this stadium tells the story of the struggle for social justice, civil rights, and American integration.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN (voiceover): Reopening day, a chance for fans to celebrate and enjoy sites many thought they`d never see.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: What do you think when you`re in this place now? You know, I mean, what I think is wild.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN (voiceover): And the Jackals held on to win the opener 10-6. All those legends who played here, perhaps giving the home team an edge.\par \par \uc2 Ron Allen, NBC News, Patterson, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Quite a piece of history. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thanks for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230620ej6j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc1}{\*\bkmkend toc1}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for June 18, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Hallie Jackson, Peter Baker, Stephen Hayes, Kimberly  Atkins Stohr \par \uc2 9,631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 June 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:19]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST (voice over): This Sunday, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  responds to the federal indictment of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I don`t know why some of my competitors in the Republican primary presume the president will be found guilty. Look, all we know is what -- what the president has been accused of in the indictment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you were President Biden, would you pardon him right now?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I just think this whole matter is incredibly divisive for the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: As he launches his own run for the White House, he explains where he differs with his former running mate.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: In 2016 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised to govern as a conservative. But he makes no such promise today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: His criticism on how Trump would lead on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: My former running mate, seeing war raging in Eastern Europe, is signaling an ambiguous message, not even able to say who he would prefer to see win the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And now he thinks he can build support for his own campaign.\par \par \uc2 Who is a Pence supporter, and how does it differentiate right now from a Trump supporter?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I know the people in our movement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My exclusive interview with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , making history as the first former vice president to ever directly challenge his running mate in a primary.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson; New York Times chief White House correspondent Peter Baker; Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for the \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ; and Stephen Hayes, editor of The Dispatch.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Good Sunday morning, and a happy Father`s day to all the dads out there.\par \par \uc2 As one former prosecutor put it pretty bluntly this week, two years from now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could be in prison or he could be president. With Trump holding a commanding lead over the Republican field this week, his opponents face the question that will dominate this race over the next six months. Will they, can they and should they go after the former president over his federal indictment and Trump`s legal issues that are likely to come.\par \par \uc2 First, let`s tackle the "Will they?" Clearly, it would be the best way for any Republican candidate not named Trump to get some attention. But so far the most direct criticism has come from single-digit candidates \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and Asa Hutchinson. Hutchinson called on Trump to drop out of the race after the indictment, saying "It`s simply wrong to be discussing a pardon." And Christie said "The conduct (in the indictment) is awful."\par \par \uc2 But as Trump appeared in a Florida courtroom, even opponents who have criticized the charges as "serious" and as -- Donald Trump`s behavior as "reckless", have diminished that very criticism of Trump with attacks against the Justice Department for bringing the charges against him in the first place. And before the case was even tried, they`ve been leaping to a promise of a pardon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: This is my commitment, on January 20, 2025, if I`m elected the next U.S. president, to pardon \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, (R) PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE & FORMER FLORIDA GOVERNOR: I think it would be terrible for the country to have a former president in prison for years because of a documents case. So I would be inclined in favor of a pardon. But I think it`s really premature at this point, when he`s not even been convicted.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA & PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We`re going to clean house at \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Justice. We`re going to do a lot. But part of it is using your Article II powers to redress wrongs, including, potentially, pardons. If there was a distant standard, we`re going to be very inclined to grant clemency in those situations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s tackle part two of this question. Can they go after him on this?\par \par \uc2 In a Quinnipiac poll this week, Trump leads the Republican field by 30 percentage points, garnering 53 percent of the vote, so over a majority. And in an NPR/PBS/Marist poll, while just 43 percent of Americans overall believe Trump should continue running for president given this indictment, a whopping 83 percent of Republicans believe he should keep running. Only 13 percent of Republicans believe Trump has even done anything illegal. Changing those voters` minds in a conservative media ecosystem that, at least for now, plans to defend Trump under any circumstance, will be an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 The final part of this question, should they go after him? One candidate who has a pretty explicit take on the seriousness of protecting classified information, regardless of someone`s money or position, was candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: In my administration, I`m going to enforce all laws concerning the protection of classified information. No one will be above the law. We can`t have someone in the Oval Office who doesn`t understand the meaning of the word "confidential" or "classified."\par \par \uc2 [09:05:07]\par \par \uc2 One of the first things we must do is to enforce all classification rules and to enforce all laws relating to the handling of classified information.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, perhaps that applies to thee and not to me in that case. That was 2016, and his opponent was \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, as one Republican strategist put it, if you aren`t willing to criticize Trump, ask yourself why you`re running to defeat him?\par \par \uc2 The bottom line, the question for all of Trump`s opponents is what kind of party are they running to lead?\par \par \uc2 On Friday I sat down in Huntsville, Alabama, with Trump`s one-time running mate, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . He`s now challenging him for the nomination. And of course I began by asking him about Trump`s indictment this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think, if these allegations are true, he deserves to be convicted?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, first, you know, in moments like this, I think it`s important for leaders in this country and those of us who aspire to leadership to return to first principles. The first principle is no one`s above the law. The second principle is that every American is entitled to a presumption of innocence.\par \par \uc2 Now, I -- I`ve read the indictment in detail. And the allegations in the indictment are serious. And I can`t defend what is alleged. But the former president deserves his day in court. He deserves the opportunity to make his defense in a court of law. And so I want to -- I want to reserve judgment about this until he`s had an opportunity to -- to take his case into the courtroom and take his case to the American people.\par \par \uc2 I will never diminish the -- the importance of protecting our nation`s secrets, particularly those that bear upon our national security. I have a son in the Marine Corps. I have a son-in-law in the \uc2 United States Navy\uc2 . We`ve got to ensure that classified materials, especially those that have been alleged in this indictment, that bear upon our national security, are protected and secured.\par \par \uc2 But that being...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... that being said, we`ve gone through years, Chuck -- we`ve gone through years of...\par \par \uc2 TODD: But during the 2016 campaign, you said what \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  did with the server and the mishandling of classified documents -- in October of 2016, you said it disqualified her to be commander in chief. Does this disqualify \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be commander in chief again?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I stand by that view. It`s one more example of a two-tier justice system that we`ve been living through for seven years. I mean, I have to tell you, after -- after seeing Hillary Clinton given a pass...\par \par \uc2 TODD: By your Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: By the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was by a -- no, no, no, wait.\par \par \uc2 No, no, no, Jeff Sessions had another U.S. Attorney look into everything with Clinton...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... and didn`t bring charges. This is a Republican...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: \uc2 James Comey\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 TODD: This was a Republican Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... in the run-up to the election -- you know, Chuck...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that, but you guys looked at it again.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I`m not -- I`m not correcting you on history.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was given a pass, and then we went two and a half years through a Russia investigation that we now know through the Durham report should have never been begun...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Actually, there was an inspector general report that said there was no politics involved with this, that everything was a legitimate reason to investigate.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re -- again, these are all Republican appointees that have done this.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: OK, Chuck, the independent counsel found that the investigation into Russia collusion should never have been initiated. And yet we lived through two and a half years of a constant barrage, on -- on your network, on the cable network associated with you and many of the other mainstream media, suggesting that there was Russian collusion. We had public officials that said there was evidence of collusion -- never happened.\par \par \uc2 And then -- I thought it was disgraceful the way big media and big tech essentially colluded to suppress the story of the Hunter Biden laptop in the run-up to the 2020 campaign, when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  actually had the laptop and knew it was legitimate. So, look, I -- it`s hard for me to believe that politics didn`t play some role in the unprecedented decision to bring an indictment against a former president of the United States...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me flip it on you...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: But I want to be clear...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that you take it seriously.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... that no one`s above the law, and the allegations are serious.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You believe there`s all this politics that influenced this decision. What was the Justice Department supposed to do? Should they not have charged him because he`s an active candidate for president, even in the face of all of these serious allegations? Should they not have brought charges?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, as I said in the run-up to this indictment, I had hoped that the Department of Justice would see its way clear to resolve this issue short of criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that on the Justice Department or on the former president?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, he could have -- he didn`t respond to a subpoena. He didn`t respond to requests. I mean, as Bill Barr said, he brought this on himself.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Chuck, I -- I understand what`s been reported in the news.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 We`ll -- we`ll see what comes out in evidence in the court of law. But, look, I want to say to you that I had hoped, for -- for several reasons, that the Department of Justice would have done otherwise. Because, as we have seen in the last week, this has only served to further divide our divided politics.\par \par \uc2 And I have to tell you, as someone that has represented the United States on the world stage, as someone who was on the International Relations Committee for 10 years, we`re the gold standard of justice in the world. And to -- to see a former president of the United States to be subject to an indictment by the administration of the current president, that he may be running -- he may well be facing again in the future, that sends a terrible message to the wider world, where you know there are many third- world countries around the world where that pattern is very common.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Flip the script. Imagine deciding that you have evidence that a former president commits a crime and you don`t charge it. That could be just as damaging to our gold standard rule of law example, could it not?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I -- I think the proper answer, right now, because we have an indictment, and I said, the allegations are serious, and the proper approach now is for equal treatment under the law. The Department of Justice, I would like to see bring equal vigor to the investigation into allegations around \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And we have investigations...\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you know they`re not?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... swirling around \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , about...\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you know that they`re not doing that right now?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I -- I want to see them come forward...\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s a U.S. Attorney in Delaware...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... with action that would...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... that`s been looking into this for quite some time.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... reflect their commitment to equal treatment under the law.\par \par \uc2 And, frankly, you know, the fact that -- that President Biden was found to have had classified documents dating all the way back not just to his years as vice president but to his years in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , I -- I would like to see the Department of Justice moving forward...\par \par \uc2 TODD: They have a special...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... vigorously into that investigation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He appointed a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: That`s equal treatment under the law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what -- so you approve of what Garland did by having a special counsel look into President Biden?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, we`ll see what happens, Chuck. I mean, the American people...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Give me your definition of equal treatment...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The American people have seen so far that the former president has been subject to an indictment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But there`s a difference between equal treatment and equal outcomes.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: There is a perception, Chuck...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you looking for an equal outcome or equal treatment? Did you get my point?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I get your point, and I -- I think I`m being very clear on this. I think the American people would like to see evidence that we don`t have a two-tiered system of justice. Seven years, it appears as though -- as though Democrats get one level of treatment and -- and Republicans, especially those of us in the Trump-Pence administration, get another...\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, Mr. Vice President, you bring up seven years...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The American people want to see -- want to see action by the Department of Justice that proves to them, or starts to prove to them, that that`s not the case...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did the Sessions-Barr Justice Department politicize things in favor of the Democrats? Is that your allegation, that, in four of the seven years you`re describing, that the Justice Department that was run by Republican - - former Republican senator Jeff Sessions, two-time Republican attorney general Bill Barr, was politicizing the Justice Department in favor of Democrats?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: What I`m saying to you is that we now know that the Russia collusion investigation should have never been begun, two and a half years, put the country through...\par \par \uc2 TODD: The inspector general never said that, for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: In the days following that, under the Biden administration now, we`ve literally seen the Justice Department targeting parents going to school board meetings. We`ve seen the Justice Department targeting pro-life activists.\par \par \uc2 And -- and I have to tell you, Chuck, I`m still waiting for the rash of prosecutions of people involved in the BLM riots from the summer of 2020. I mean, where -- where is the equal treatment under the law as evidenced in the fact that -- that people are being brought into court, being held for having caused billions of dollars in damage in hundreds of riots throughout the summer of 2020?\par \par \uc2 Look, clearly the American people -- or not -- or I will tell you, among Republicans, a vast majority of Republicans have lost confidence in the Department of Justice. And if I`m elected president of the United States, I`ve said, on day one, we`re going to -- we`re going to clean house at the highest levels of the Department of Justice, and we`re going to find...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve talked about firing \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  on day one.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... men and women of integrity...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... who are respected on both sides of the aisle, who will restore confidence in the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  is a Trump appointee. What makes him so -- so -- a person that runs a politicized \uc2 FBI\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is where -- I understand the accusation. But the facts don`t feel like they fit the accusation.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: All I`m telling you is the reality today -- and, look, I -- I -- I appreciate the service of everybody serving on the top floor of the Department of Justice, but they`ve lost the confidence of the majority of the American people.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:11]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have been asked a few times whether you would pardon the former president if he`s convicted and if you were the president. Would you?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I just think the question is -- is premature. I mean, honestly, Chuck, I`ve -- I`ve pardoned people who were found guilty of a crime...\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s your -- what is your bar...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... when I was governor of Indiana.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... for a pardon?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, let me say, first and foremost, I don`t know why some of my competitors in the Republican primary presume the president will be found guilty. Look, all we know is what -- what the president has been accused of in the indictment. We don`t know what his defense is. We don`t know if this will even go to trial. It could be subject to a motion of dismiss. We don`t know what the verdict will be of the jury. We don`t know the result of an appeal.\par \par \uc2 So I think, look, the president of the United States takes an oath to support and defend the Constitution of the United States. And as I said in my announcement, I -- I`ll always choose the Constitution. But the oath the president takes also requires him to see that the laws are faithfully executed. That means respecting the Article III courts in this country. I think we need to let the courts do their job and -- and let this case work its -- work its way through our judicial system. And if I have the great privilege of being president of the United States, as I did when I was a governor, we would evaluate any request for pardon for any American.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you were President Biden, would you pardon him right now?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I just think this whole matter is incredibly divisive for the country. And, look, I -- I just think, at the end of the day, it is -- it is saddening to me that we are now in this moment, for two reasons. Number one is, look, President Trump was my friend.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You said it in past tense.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, we -- we worked together very closely for four years. I`m incredibly proud of the record of the Trump-Pence administration. Some in this primary have taken to criticizing the record of our administration. But I`m proud of everything we accomplished for the American people. We left America stronger and more prosperous than ever before.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, as you and I have talked in great detail, it did not end well. As I said in my announcement speech, the American people deserve to know that, on that tragic day of January 6th, President Trump asked me to choose him over the Constitution. And I -- I chose the Constitution, and I always will. I had no right to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t this your way of saying he`s not qualified to be president, because he did not -- because, to put it in somebody else`s words, I think it was \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  that says, "You have to choose between the Constitution and supporting him." You chose the Constitution. Is that what he`s demanding?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, look, President Trump was wrong then. He`s wrong now. He`s continued to say that -- that I had the right to overturn the election. I had no right to overturn the election. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will have no right to overturn the election when we beat her in 2024. And, look, the role of the Congress and the role of the vice president on that day is to open and count electoral votes, no more and no less. And I -- I`ll always believe, by God`s grace, that I did my duty that day.\par \par \uc2 But let me say, with regard to going forward, I`m running for president of the United States because I believe our party and our country need new leadership.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  made history as the first former president ever to face federal criminal charges. Why are so many of his Republican rivals rushing to defend him from them? The panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:21:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here, NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson, anchor of "Hallie Jackson Now"; Peter Baker, chief White House correspondent for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ; Stephen Hayes, CEO and editor of The Dispatch; and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for the \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Will they, can they, should they, Hallie? The president`s defenders were out. Here is how the defense of the indictment began this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R) CALIFORNIA: Was it a good picture to have boxes in a garage that opens up all the time? A bathroom door locks.\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS, (R) FLORIDA: There are 33 bathrooms at Mar-a-Lago. So don`t act like it`s just in some random bathroom that the guests can go into.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: He is not a spy. He`s overcharged. Did he do things wrong? Yes, he may have. he will be tried about that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So where are we?\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: OK, will they, can they, should they? The people that you just saw, of course, are going to back \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They`re some of his most loyal allies. They have members of Congress, et cetera. That fits of a piece of where we are right now. Will they, can they, should they?\par \par \uc2 Will they? I will tell you not for a long while. And that`s a quote that I heard in the last 18 hours from a member of a rival campaign. They -- there is very little incentive for most of these people going up against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in this election, based on my reporting, to try to take him on, to pull the knives out right now, for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 They say that`s just not where the voters are; that`s not where the Republican primary electorate is. The numbers that you showed at the top of the segment, Chuck, show that, right? That is actually backed up by facts. Voters don`t necessarily want to see that. You`re going to have \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 ; you`re going to have Asa Hutchinson come out and talk about this. You`re going to have Nikki Haley try to walk this line where it`s incredibly reckless on the one hand, but on the one hand, she`d consider pardoning him, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Can they? They can. We`re seeing what they choose to do.\par \par \uc2 And should they? That`s an assessment that each of these campaigns is going to have to come up with for themselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter Baker, the -- the field is, kind of, split on the pardoning of Trump here. Here are those that are inclined to be for pardoning him, Nikki Haley -- she said -- even used the word "inclined" in favor of a pardon; \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . He is making it a lead part of his campaign. He held up the paper as his commitment to pardon him. Governor DeSantis, very inclined to grant clemency, as he talks about it.\par \par \uc2 But when you turn the page, Tim Scott`s not ready to discuss hypotheticals; \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  implying that, if it`s a fair trial, he wouldn`t pardon him. And then, of course, "It`s wrong."\par \par \uc2 Trump`s blotting out the sun.\par \par \uc2 So if you don`t take him on, on the indictments, you`re not going to get any coverage.\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: No, that`s exactly right. He is the all-consuming part of the Republican primary right now. And to Hallie`s point, I get the point. We`re not having votes yet, so maybe if you`re going to eventually take the knives out, you want to wait until you get closer to when the votes begin, rather than make yourself a target for him for the next three or four or five months.\par \par \uc2 I think the real strategy, though, is they`re just waiting for trump to beat himself. Because right now none of them can beat him. So they`re waiting for him to...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: That worked really well in `15 and `16.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I was just going to say...\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... they didn`t have prosecutors in 2015 and 2016. Maybe this will be different. That`s the theory. There`s not -- this is not the end of the criminal justice system`s handling of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . There may be a third and even a fourth indictment. At some point, the calculation is, does that begin to weigh him down?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are we seeing the Fifth Avenue moment come to life?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN HAYES, CEO AND EDITOR, THE DISPATCH: Yeah, this is it. This is the Fifth Avenue indictment.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s what -- that`s what we`re looking at. Look, I take the point that these campaigns are making that they don`t want to go after him right now, that it might be too early. But come on. He`s the front-runner. If you don`t make this argument, you`re never going to convince voters that they should back away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , if you don`t make this argument.\par \par \uc2 I think there`s this misperception among the Republican rank-and-file that nobody in Washington, none of the Republicans in Washington, none of the candidates opposing him, actually think this is serious, are concerned about this, when in reality, I mean, we know, we`re all having conversations with them. They`re saying to us privately, "Wow, this is bad."\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, look there, right...\par \par \uc2 HAYES: "The indictment was worse than we thought."\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: "This is bad." "He`s on tape, basically admitting it." I mean, this is what you hear from Republicans privately.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:01]\par \par \uc2 And they say in public, "Well, I`m really concerned about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and the Justice Department."\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, SENIOR OPINION WRITER, BOSTON \uc2 GLOBE\uc2 : But how -- how deep of a hole can they dig, though? Because, as you say, this is only going to get worse, if there are indictments out of Georgia; already the indictment out of New York.\par \par \uc2 These are things that they can`t even pardon, so they can`t even use that argument when talking about it. And as things get progressively and progressively worse, where can Republican House members go past, you know, "Well, once you put a lock on the bathroom door, it`s essentially a SCIF; it`s the same thing."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 I mean, they`ve reached such a low point, how much further can they dig? I think it will do long-term damage to the party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me bring up, though, a concern that some Democrats are having, that the White House is being too quiet about this. We had some reporting over the weekend, Kimberly, one anonymous staffer. "It`s malpractice to let them dominate," referring to Trump, to fill in the blanks here. "Biden`s allies should be hitting him hard."\par \par \uc2 A donor, I don`t think it`s sustainable to say nothing,"\par \par \uc2 Another strategist, "In a lot of ways, we`re almost allowing him to fight on his own turf."\par \par \uc2 Look, we`ve heard this before. You know, he fills -- he fills the vacuum. This happened with Mueller. Mueller didn`t say a word, never defended his work. Jack Smith doesn`t -- you know, it`s because it would be inappropriate to do so. And Trump uses those norms to his advantage. But should Biden`s folks; should Democrats be leaning in more?\par \par \uc2 STOHR: Well, here Biden`s in a weird position, right? Because he`s in two roles. One, he`s the president of the United States. And in that role, he`s absolutely right that the president does not have any power and should not have any power to influence the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 He is also the presumptive nominee for the Democratic Party in the presidential. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is his opponent. And is this an electability issue? You bet it is. And so he has to find a way to be able to talk about the rule of law and explain why it`s so important, and somebody who absolutely flouts it is unfit for office, while at the same time keeping his hands off the Justice Department as the commander-in-chief.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... do both?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, I -- I think they`d like to run against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , so they don`t want to do this right now. And it`s important to -- there are some real concerns that Republicans raise that are more than just what-aboutism about the way that the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has conducted itself, particularly during the Trump administration. There are legitimate concerns raised by the Department of Justice inspector general that Republicans can point to. I think there are legitimate beefs that Republicans make about \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and the non-prosecution of Hillary Clinton. Those are fair points. If Democrats were to pile on now in a public way, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  especially, and go after Trump on this indictment, they would just be validating all of those concerns from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , sort of -- it sparked -- she goes, "I can`t Republicans are sticking..."\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: "I`m a little bit surprised," she said...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, yeah, yeah, that was their -- that was, like, the tiptoe.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It was like the float, if you will. I just -- I think this conversation, though, is bringing us back to where we started this discussion, which is half of Republicans don`t believe that the Department of Justice is a fair institution. Half of Republicans, roughly, when you look at polling over the last eight to 12 months, just don`t think that the DOJ or the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is -- is worthy of their favorability, if you will. That is a huge, I think, bigger, macro issue playing out over the course of the next multiple years, for a party that, as you know, has long been built on this idea of law and order. And the shift away from that is really interesting.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it does beg the question, what`s worse, bringing this charge and not getting a conviction, or bringing this charge, getting the conviction and living with the fallout of that? It feels like these are just nothing but bad options?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah, not...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... not a lot of good options. But you asked the question, right, what if you had this set of facts and you don`t bring charges? What does that say about our justice system? Is there, in fact, a get out of jail free card for former presidents; you can do anything you want and nobody ever holds you accountable. That`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Especially if you have the -- just, sort of, hang over the allusion that "I could start violence."\par \par \uc2 Anyway, when we come back, more of my interview with the former vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , on why he`s challenging his former running mate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: In 2016 \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised to govern as a conservative. But he makes no such promise today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:31:46]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who loyally served Trump for four years, is now making the case for against him.\par \par \uc2 In our sit-down, I asked Pence, why challenge his former boss now?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you be running against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he did not ask you to violate your oath? Had he accepted the results of the election, and now he`s running again without that, would you still be challenging him?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE (R), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I would leave that to historians to speculate on, Chuck. I would tell you, at the end of the day, he did. You know, and I always hoped he`d come around on this. As I said recently -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sounds like, had he come around, you might not be running.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: No, let me explain to you. I always hoped he`d come around on this issue -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: --- because I believe no one who puts himself over the Constitution should ever be president of the United States. And I had hoped that President Trump would eventually see that he`d been misled by the so-called legal experts that had advised him wrongly about the role that he thought I had, and still thinks I had that day.\par \par \uc2 But also, I would honestly tell you, Chuck, we have -- we have other differences other than that day. I spoke about it in my announcement speech. It`s not just looking at the past, but it`s the direction of the country, the direction of the Republican Party in the future.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I`m -- I`m pro-life. I don`t apologize for it. And -- but after leading the most pro-life administration in American history, and now my former running mate has taken to calling some pro-life bills at the state level "too harsh."\par \par \uc2 TODD: He called the six-week bill in Florida too harsh. You disagree with him?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I do. But he also blamed electoral losses in 2022 on overturning Roe v. Wade. Look, I think that the cause of life has been the animating force of the conservative movement and the Republican Party for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you think 2022 was about? Was it more about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or was it more about abortion?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Chuck, look, candidates that focused on the future, candidates that focused on the challenges facing the American people in 2022 did very well. Candidates that were focused on the past, were focused on re-litigating the past, did not do well even where we should`ve. But that`s not the only difference, on the right to life. Two other issues that I spoke about in my announcement.\par \par \uc2 And I remember you saying a few weeks back that you always pay attention to announcement speeches --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I do.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- because you know that the candidate probably wrote every word.\par \par \uc2 TODD:\par \par \uc2 I know it`s the one case --\par \par \uc2 PENCE:\par \par \uc2 And I did on this one.\par \par \uc2 TODD:\par \par \uc2 That`s right. I believe it.\par \par \uc2 PENCE:\par \par \uc2 Two other issues that I think bear upon the life of the nation of great consequence where the president and I have parted ways. Because, look, in 2016, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  promised to govern as a conservative. And I`m proud to say that we did for four years under the Trump-Pence administration.\par \par \uc2 But he makes no such promise today. Not only has he been walking away from a clear commitment to the right to life, but, look, we have a national debt the size of our nation`s economy. Joe Biden`s policy is insolvency. He won`t even talk about the 70 percent of the federal budget that represents entitlements, that`s driving that debt.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Neither does President Trump want to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Donald Trump`s position on the national debt is identical to Joe Biden`s. And to me, the Republican Party has to be the party of growth, and fiscal responsibility, and reform. I think we owe it to those - those kids of mine and yours, to my granddaughters, to square our shoulders and - and be straight with the American people about the magnitude of this national debt. It is driving -\par \par \uc2 TODD: I admire you on this issue, but you realize it`s an unpopular issue. The reason - I mean, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  takes -\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I understand.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- entitlements off the table because it attracted more voters in Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, and Iowa.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Look, I get it. I get it. But I think the fact that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came into office, spent $2 trillion in completely unnecessary spending, and ignited the worst inflation in 40 years, the American people are onto it. That, as the government racks up debt, the cost of living is going up in this country. It`s affecting their family budgets.\par \par \uc2 And I think they`re ready for a serious conversation that says, look, we can preserve and protect Social Security and Medicare for people that are in retirement and who will retire in the next 25 years. But for younger Americans, to turn us away from that debt crisis that they`re facing, we`ve got to bring forward common sense and compassion reforms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`d raise the retirement age, for people in 25 years --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I think all these issues for younger Americans are on the table, including allowing younger Americans to invest a portion of their payroll tax in a personal savings account. I call -- I say, take a new deal program, make it a better deal. Give them a better rate of return than they have in the government program.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You realize the politics of this has never worked. Everytime someone`s proposed, whether it was \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  -\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, Chuck, again -\par \par \uc2 TODD: So why do you think the public`s ready this time to listen on this?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, again, I think it`s because they know the country`s in a lot of trouble. We have a debt the size of our nation`s economy for the first time since World War II. And inflation`s gone up 16 percent in the last two-and-a-half years. It`s crushing the family budgets of millions of Americans, two thirds of which are living paycheck to paycheck. They`re starting, in my judgment, to understand that, as government wraps up debt, inflation is happening, and it`s crushing the purchasing power of Americans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we`re actually seeing inflation has been going down over the last year.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: So you`ve got to reform it. Last issue, though, where there`s a difference, Chuck, if I may.\par \par \uc2 TODD:\par \par \uc2 Go ahead, yes. Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: And that`s about America`s place in the world. Look, I think America`s the leader of the free world. We`re the arsenal of democracy. Under our administration, we made the largest increase in investment on national defense in 40 years. And then we unleashed the American military to take down the ISIS caliphate, take down the world`s most dangerous terrorists. We even confronted the Russians in Syria, took out 100 Russian soldiers without one American casualty.\par \par \uc2 And Russia never even attempted to redraw international lines by force during our administration. We did peace through strength. Now, my former running mate, seeing war raging in Eastern Europe, is signaling an ambiguous message. I`m not even able to say who he would prefer to see win the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think the United States needs to stand by the courageous fighters in Ukraine, give them the resources more quickly than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has, to take the fight to the Russians, and repel this invasion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you tell your former colleagues in the House Republican Conference who don`t share this view?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, look, I respectfully disagree. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in 1985 coined what came to be known as the Reagan Doctrine. That was, if you`re willing to fight the communists in your country, we`ll give you the tools to fight them there so we don`t have to fight them here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done such a terrible job explaining the importance of repelling the Russian invasion and supporting the military in Ukraine. And he`s -- and he`s been delivering the equipment slowly. He promised 33 Abrams tanks in January. They`re still not there yet. They`re continuing to drag their feet on aircraft. But look, our national interest there -\par \par \uc2 TODD: You realize it`s half your party that doesn`t want - that doesn`t even want what he`s been sending them.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Yeah, well, it`s -- number one, it`s not half. And number two --\par \par \uc2 TODD: The House Republicans, it is.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I just think - I think the skepticism about support for the military in Ukraine is more a reflection of a lack of confidence in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as commander-in-chief than it is any other issue. What this is going to take --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t think it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out there also fueling - fueling skepticism?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: What this is going to take is leadership, having a president that says to the American people, look, it is in our national interest to see the Russian military repelled from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:03]\par \par \uc2 Not for -- \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  talks about glossy goals of democracy. No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I mean, look, if Russia overruns Ukraine, I predict to you it would not be too long before the Russian army crossed the border, where our men and women in uniform would have to go and fight -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Poland.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- by crossing into a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally. Poland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania. Chuck, and I also believe that by giving Ukraine the ability to repel the Russian invasion, we will send a deafening message to China that the wider world will not tolerate them using force to redraw international lines in Taiwan or anywhere in the Asia-Pacific.\par \par \uc2 TODD: For the sake of the campaign, do Americans deserve a verdict in Trump`s case before the polls, before the election?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I, I expect they`ll get it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`d like to see this trial take place before the election, that the American public should know before the election whether these allegations are true in a court of law?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, look, I think the president should have the right to bring his defense in a court of law --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it should be speedy?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- and if he`s found to be guilty, have every right, as every American does, to pursue an appeal and resolve the issue. But I also believe --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think it`s a mistake to delay this trial after the election?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I also believe the American people would be greatly heartened, given the controversy, the evidence that`s come out publicly, the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  laptop, all the allegations, even -- even news that broke in the last few weeks, the American people would be heartened to see the Department of Justice move against \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , move against the accusations against the Biden family, and continue to pursue just as aggressively an investigation in, into President Biden`s mishandling of classified documents. We`ve got to restore confidence in the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to talk a little bit more about your campaign, which I`m sure you`ll be pleased about, and a little less about Donald Trump`s legal issues. Who is a Pence supporter, and how does it differentiate right now from a Trump supporter? If you are the Republican nominee it means you`re going to win over Trump supporters at some point, but right now they`re not for you. How do you convince them? Because they`re -- because he`s angry at you, they`re angry at you.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, look, I, I think that the people around the country, as I`ve traveled the last two years, have -- have, have, in the main, very humbling views about me and my family. I`ve been stopped in airports and grocery stores and gas stations by Americans of every political persuasion who thank me for my service to the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How many of them were wearing MAGA hats when they --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: And particularly thanked me for the stand that I took on that tragic day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But are people that wear MAGA hats doing that to you?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Look, look, I, I know the people in our movement, and I can honestly tell you that they`re some of the best people in this country. They love America, and they love our Constitution. And I have every confidence, as we carry our campaign forward and we articulate not only the way we kept faith with the Constitution on one day, but our determination to lead our party on that broad, conservative agenda that`s delivered not just great victories for the Republican Party, but for the American people over the last 50 years. I have every confidence that this party will, will come together after a good and vigorous primary and will deliver a great victory in 2024.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you confident that if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  does not have the most delegates that he will concede the Republican nomination?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I -- I am confident the Republican primary voters are going to sort all this out in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And he`ll - will he accept defeat?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I, look --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You saw him once not accept defeat. Do you believe he will accept defeat in this primary if he does not have the most delegates -\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I understand -- I understand your preoccupation with my former running mate, and what he will do or won`t do -- may do\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s the front runner. It`s not a preoccupation, I have no choice.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Chuck, Chuck, look, Republican primary voters know the stakes in this election. They know that we`ve not just got to win, but we`ve got to steer our country back to the things that have made America strong and prosperous and free. I`m confident they`re going to choose a standard bearer that will hold up those conservative values. And I believe in all of my heart that I`ll be that nominee and will lead our party, and more importantly our country, to a great victory in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can see my full interview with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  at MeetThePress.com. For transparency purposes, you`ll see it completely untouched. As the country celebrates Juneteenth, this week on Meet the Press Reports, we take a look at the fight over reparations, starting in the state of California: whether America should pay its Black citizens for hundreds of years of slavery, Jim Crow segregation, and other forms of institutional racism.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:08]\par \par \uc2 This isn`t theoretical. This could start happening in California soon. Watch our latest episode of Meet the Press Reports on Peacock or \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , or right after this broadcast on NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, it is not uncommon for former leaders to be jailed or prosecuted after leaving office, at least around the world. We`ll show you why it`s not exactly unprecedented for a democracy. Data Download is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s arraignment this week was a watershed event for this country. But according to our friends at Axios, since 1980 about half of the nations in the world have seen former leaders jailed or prosecuted, including in quite a few democracies. In fact, among leaders who have left office after 2000, there are 76 countries where we have seen leaders jailed or prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 Let`s look at a few of these cases, and we`re going to start with Central and South America. In Guatemala -- we`re going to start there -- former President Molina and his vice president, Roxana Baldetti, both found guilty of corruption last year. Alfonso Portillo pled guilty to attempting to launder $2.5 million through U.S. banks.\par \par \uc2 In Panama, former Presidents Martinelli and Varella have been called to stand trial in a massive money laundering probe. And, of course, that international corruption scandal in Brazil took down President Lula, but he was able to run for office again and win the presidency after his case was annulled.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to Europe. In France, former President \uc2 Nicolas Sarkozy\uc2  must wear an ankle bracelet for a year after he lost his appeal of a corruption conviction. And that follows the convictions of former President Chirac and Prime Minister Fillon.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to Israel, where \uc2 Bibi Netanyahu\uc2  is currently on trial for corruption charges, even as he currently serves as prime minister. His predecessor, \uc2 Ehud Olmert\uc2 , served 16 months in prison for accepting bribes and obstructing justice.\par \par \uc2 In South Korea, former President Park Geun-hye served five years in prison for corruption charges.\par \par \uc2 And just this past week, former Italian Prime Minister \uc2 Silvio Berlusconi\uc2  passed away at the age of 86.\par \par \uc2 [09:50:06]\par \par \uc2 Well, he too had some brushes with the law. He was investigated a lot. He ended up with one conviction for tax fraud in 2013.\par \par \uc2 And guess what? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wasn`t even the only former government leader to be arrested this week. Meet \uc2 Nicola Sturgeon\uc2 , the former first minister of Scotland. She was arrested for financial misconduct, though she was later released without being charged pending further investigation.\par \par \uc2 So what happened to Trump this week may have been unprecedented in the United States, but for many other democracies and countries, it`s not exactly a groundbreaking development.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, why some Republicans are looking to run further to the right from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Will it work?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 I want to pick up one part of the conversation we didn`t finish, and that is this idea, Hallie, this -- on Biden. Should Biden pardon Trump?\par \par \uc2 Mark Thiessen and Danielle Pletka, who`s a familiar face around the panel every now and then, I thought made a pretty compelling argument as to why maybe President Biden ought to consider a pardon of Trump. Now, he`s not going to do that. There`d be a lot of political consequences here. But it does get at the fact that nobody believes Trump should sit in prison, do they?\par \par \uc2 [09:55:00]\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Part of the reason for that, Chuck, is because of what we`ve seen in the days after the indictment with some of these if not overt suggestions of violence, at least allusions to it. You know, comments like "war phase we`re entering," "eye for an eye," et cetera. There is after January 6th a very real threat that violence could occur because of something that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  may push and I think that`s where a lot of the concern comes in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kimberly, where are you on this --\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yeah. I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- issue of a pardon?\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: I used to think that it was a good idea that Nixon was pardoned in order to keep the country together. I now think that it was one of the biggest mistakes that was made in the history of our government.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I may share that view because I wonder: Would we be thinking about this differently today had we had the premise, "Hey, no one`s above the law"?\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Right. I think we very well may be in a totally different place today if we did not have that and if it was clear that there would be consequences, including criminal ones, for flouting the rule of law. I think that`s one of the reasons I think that would be a terrible idea.\par \par \uc2 And, I have trust -- look, I think after what happened on January 6th with the prosecutions of the people who were there, I think that`s one reason why we haven`t seen the violence when he was indicted, why we haven`t seen violence everywhere.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s consequences.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: -- into -- in the Justice Department. When there are consequences for them, there also should be consequences for the president.\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, CHIEF WHITE HOSUE CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: The difference on Nixon and \uc2 Ford\uc2  though is that Nixon had accepted a measure of accountability by resigning, right? He was disgraced. And what \uc2 Ford\uc2  was saying is, we don`t need to disgrace him further to agree that Watergate was bad and that Nixon did bad things.\par \par \uc2 That`s not the case here. There`s no measure of accountability if he gives him a pardon at this point, and that`s a different historical judgment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Steve Hayes, I want to pivot to something. I`m not going to play this clip because we`re low on time, but \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was on the \uc2 Christian Broadcasting Network\uc2  and he`s trying to hit \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for being soft on abortion.\par \par \uc2 In theory, maybe there is room to Trump`s right to beat him there on the issue, at least in the state of Iowa. But is that something that`ll work?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN HAYES, CEO & EDITOR, THE DISPATCH: Yeah, I think Iowa in particular and in the Republican primary more generally you`re going to find Republican voters who are pro-life and probably will respond to that kind of an argument. The question is whether the Republican Party`s really even an ideological place anymore. It was so interesting listening to the \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  discussion that you had. He`s trying to say, we ought to have a big debate about the size and scope of government.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He wants to have a debate about what the Republican Party should be.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, what the Republican Party should be and should --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s very refreshing.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN HAYES: -- is the Republican Party still a limited government party? We haven`t heard that debate really for eight years in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 I think with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on the abortion question, given what he`s done in Florida, it`s sort of surprising to me that he hasn`t run on it to this point. Remember, he was sort of shy about it. Now, he`s running --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But they are nervous. It makes them less electable, in the general.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Well, it`s also part of the strategy in the primary, which they have to win in order to even get to a general, right? And it`s not just going to be the issue of reproductive rights. The strategy from those that I talk to who are in the DeSantis orbit is to run to the right of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on all of these so-called culture war issues, which is where some of the ideology of the party is.\par \par \uc2 On education, right? On LGBTQ+ rights. On issues like abortion. And to show that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  can win on those issues and that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot, to point to what he`s done --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve heard this argued.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right, yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve heard the argument. Ted Cruz tried this.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah. Well, it was a different environment. I mean, look. We can try to go back to 2016 over and over again, and we do see a fragmented field in which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  profits like then.\par \par \uc2 But I think the context is different, and you`re hearing DeSantis say not only on this issues but even immigration, He didn`t build the wall, which was of course his main argument in 2016.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before we go, one year after the landmark Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade, we here at "Meet the Press" will be examining its impact, what it means for the future of this country and your politics. You can watch our Meet the Press special on abortion this Thursday evening at 10:30 Eastern Time on NBC News Now and after that on demand on \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and Peacock.\par \par \uc2 That is all we have for today. Thank you for watching. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press". Enjoy Father`s Day.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230619ej6i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc2}{\*\bkmkend toc2}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for May 21, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Carol Lee, Symone Sanders, Carlos Curbelo \par \uc2 9,544 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 May 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:18]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, taking on Trump. The Republican primary field will be expanding this week as Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and South Carolina Senator Tim Scott both jump into the 2024 race.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RONALD DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: I think the party has developed a culture of losing.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: It`s time for us to be proud to be Americans and proud of our president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can either candidate make the case he`s the better alternative to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and unite the party in the process, in the primary battle ahead?\par \par \uc2 I`ll talk to Republican Congressman Byron Donalds of Florida, who`s currently backing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, flirting with disaster. Debt ceiling negotiations break down, restart and then stop again.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: Default is not an option.\par \par \uc2 HOUSE SPEAKER KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R) CALIFORNIA: This White House will not acknowledge that they`re spending too much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s really going on inside the talks? Is either side negotiating in good faith?\par \par \uc2 And how serious is the June 1st deadline for a catastrophic default? I`ll talk to Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, abortion politics. Republican lawmakers in Nebraska and North Carolina pass new abortion restrictions, as Democrats prepare to fight back.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ROY COOPER\uc2 , (D) NORTH CAROLINA: Politicians shouldn`t push their way into the exam room with women and their doctors.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Biden`s 2024 campaign is already planning to focus on the new ban in the swing state of North Carolina. How will the battle over abortion rights impact the 2024 race? I`ll talk to North Carolina`s Democratic Governor, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News managing Washington editor, Carol Lee; Dan Balz, chief correspondent for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 ; former Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 ; and Simone Sanders-Townsend, former chief spokesperson for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. This is going to be one of the busiest weeks yet in the presidential campaign, as the 2024 Republican field grows. So do concerns among some Republicans that none of Donald Trump`s current rivals are going to be able to beat him in a primary.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Senator Tim Scott is announcing his campaign tomorrow in his hometown of North Charleston before heading to the early states of Iowa and New Hampshire. And Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  expected to formally file paperwork for president this week, a bit of a soft launch of his campaign, with a video and a donor retreat at the Four Seasons in Miami before formally having the big campaign rally announcement later in the month.\par \par \uc2 But when the year began, DeSantis had the look and feel of not just a front-runner but of a potential juggernaut. But the last months have not been kind. Trump has relentlessly attacked him, and under those attacks, DeSantis has floundered a bit. He has struggled with big donors who have complained quite publicly that he, quote, "doesn`t even return phone calls." Others don`t like his position on abortion, the six-week ban, and believe it would be a problem in a general election. And still others worry about his charisma. He launched a book tour. It was immediately overshadowed when he called the Ukraine war "a territorial dispute." He walked it back after some donors complained.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s the fight with \uc2 Disney\uc2 , where he took a political advantage and a victory, and it has now turned into a political problem.\par \par \uc2 The DeSantis slump has made more Republicans who believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot win a general election flirt with jumping into this race themselves, from New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  to North Dakota Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  to former New Jersey Governor \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And if no alternative materializes there, donors are likely to keep shopping for Republicans with general election appeal, like Governor Ryan Kemp or Governor Glenn Youngkin, who himself rolled out a curious video this week that cast himself as the successor to \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, it appears that the first big fight between the two current front-runners for the Republican nomination will actually be over the issue of abortion and whose position makes them more electable. Ironically, that electability fight may not be about January 6th and Trump`s growing legal baggage.\par \par \uc2 This week Trump said that, quote, "Many people within the pro-life movement feel (DeSantis`s six-week abortion ban) was too harsh," while DeSantis responded to Trump earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: As a Florida resident, you know, he didn`t give an answer about would you have signed the heartbeat bill that Florida did, that had all the exceptions that people talk about. The legislature put it in. I signed the bill. I was proud to do it. He won`t answer whether he would sign it or not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Republican Congressman Byron Donalds of Florida. Donalds, by the way, introduced DeSantis at his election night party in November. But last month, he became one of 11 Florida congressional Republicans to endorse \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [09:05:04]\par \par \uc2 Congressman Donalds, welcome to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. BYRON DONALDS, (R) FLORIDA: It`s good to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before we start with the politics, sort of, the political junkie conversation, I want to talk about the debt ceiling. You`re a member of the Freedom Caucus on here. Where -- are you going to be comfortable with a deal that you probably personally won`t like, but it`s a deal that, sort of, maybe progressives don`t like and the Freedom Caucus doesn`t like, but it`s -- it can pass? Are you going to be able to accept a deal like that?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Chuck, to be blunt with you, we`re not really sure what deal is even on the table. The White House is not serious. They came up with a proposal to spend another $30 billion more than they already wanted to spend. They`re not taking a look at the reality of where our country is fiscally.\par \par \uc2 Look, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is saying that he`s cut $1.7 trillion from the deficit, but everybody`s saying that`s not true. That`s -- what he actually has done is he`s a trillion dollars over what CBO was projecting spending was going to be because, after all of the COVID spending in the last year of President Trump, that Congress wasn`t going to extend that, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 So we`re in a situation right now where the only plan in Washington has been passed by the House of Representatives. It`s the only plan. Joe Biden`s not serious at the negotiating table. I`ve talked with my colleagues who were in that room. So at this point, really the question is what`s the Senate going to do? Where`s \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  in all this? Because the Senate has not done any work at all, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has not done any work at all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get you to respond to something former President Trump said about the debt ceiling in 2019. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: I can`t imagine anybody ever even thinking of using the debt ceiling as a negotiating wedge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why don`t you agree with him on this?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, first of all, he also said the other day on a rival network that he said that when he was president. And when asked why he wasn`t saying it now, is that because he`s not president. Listen, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is always...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t that -- do you notice how absurd that sounds?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That is not absurd. He`s always negotiating...\par \par \uc2 TODD: How is it not absurd? It`s absurd.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: ... Chuck. Chuck, he`s always negotiating. That`s what he does. That`s actually one of the reasons why so many deals for our country worked out to our benefit, as compared to his predecessors, both Republican and Democrat, because he`s always negotiating.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But do you realize how partisan that sounds, "What is good...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That`s not a partisan statement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is good for me is not for thee."\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Listen...\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s basically saying, "When I`m president, there`s no negotiating on this, but, hey, when somebody else is president, screw `em."\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, no, here`s the thing. Let`s be -- let`s be realistic now. When \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was negotiating the debt ceiling -- with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , mind you -- they negotiated that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, they didn`t.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: When they were...\par \par \uc2 TODD: They raised it without any restrictions.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: When they were doing that -- when they were doing that, our economy was thriving. Our debt levels were not where they are right now. We were about $24 trillion, not $32 trillion. We weren`t having massive deficits the way we are right now. It was a very different environment.\par \par \uc2 Now you have $32 trillion on the credit card, and all House Republicans are saying is, "Let`s go back to pre-pandemic spending." That`s common-sense stuff.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But there`s one more thing House Republicans are asking for, which is they want fewer \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. They want fewer attempts to try to properly get tax receipts into the federal government`s coffers. I have never understood the resistance of extra \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, unless you knowingly cheat on your taxes.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That -- first of all, that`s -- that`s salacious, and you know that. Most Americans, by far, pay their taxes and they do it honorably. What House Republicans, and frankly the Republican Party is concerned about is having \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents go after middle-class families and small-business owners. When you have that many more agents, it`s not to go after the rich; it`s to go after middle class.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Again -- again...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That`s what it`s for.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So if you`re -- if you`re paying what you`re supposed to pay, then you should have nothing to fear.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: You would make the assumption that \uc2 IRS\uc2  audits are up, that they`re putting out more liens on the American people. That`s not true. That data is not there. All \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is trying to do is find every possible nickel out of every couch from every American to pay for his radical spending. Why would we do that?\par \par \uc2 Listen, let me contrast for you. When we passed the Tax Cut and Jobs Act, CBO said it was going to cost $2 trillion. That`s actually not true. What ended up happening was the federal government took in $1 trillion more than CBO actually projected. The economy grew another 1 percent over...\par \par \uc2 TODD: We can`t litigate the tax cuts because of what happened with COVID. We don`t know how much this ate into everything. But it certainly looks like there are going to be fewer revenues coming in to the government. But we don`t know. In fairness...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Fewer -- fewer revenues now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s no -- there`s no ability to litigate this because of what happened with COVID and the extra spending.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That is not true, Chuck. In 2019 we took in more revenue than we ever have in the history of our nation, as much of a percentage of our economy than we ever have as a nation. That was 2019...\par \par \uc2 TODD: It also added...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Tax receipts are still up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You realize that President Trump has added more to the deficit than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Some of it`s COVID...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: You`re going to...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Some of it`s COVID. I...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Most of it is COVID, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not most of it. It`s about half of it`s COVID and half...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Most of it is...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... of it`s the tax cut.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: That is not true. Because we raised more...\par \par \uc2 TODD: The numbers are the numbers.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: We raised more revenue than CBO projected. If you bring in more money than your projections are, how are you adding to your deficits, Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if you pay -- if somehow you keep cutting taxes but more revenue comes into the government, that math doesn`t work over time. You can have it in the first -- first year due to some various accounting tricks, but it doesn`t work over time.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:05]\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: No, Chuck, that is not true. The purpose of tax policy is to raise revenue for the federal government, not to equalize society. After the Trump tax cuts were passed, more tax revenue has come to the federal government than at any other time in the history of our nation. Those are the facts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Raw dollars can happen because we do have a growing...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: And a percentage of the economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We do have a growing economy.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Both of them have...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this on caps.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There is -- do you want to see caps on defense spending as well as domestic spending?\par \par \uc2 Because right now there seem to be a dispute whether there`s going to be budget caps only on domestic or Republicans are also wanting caps on defense spending?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: So, internally, we`re having this discussion about defense. We`re looking at trying to see a way to keep defense spending in line because, you know, there are threats all over the globe. We see it in Ukraine. We see what`s happening with China`s saber-rattling with Taiwan, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 So we want to make sure that our military preparedness is where it needs to be. But at the same time, House Republicans are not looking at just letting the Department of Defense just have massive radical increases as well. We have to control spending everywhere in our government.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me go back to the reality of politics. \uc2 Tom Cole\uc2  said any deal is going to lose people like you in the Freedom Caucus and people on the progressive side. And he -- and he added this, to The Dispatch.\par \par \uc2 He said, "We`re not going to have a deal if the president of the United States doesn`t sign it, and we`re not going to have a deal if a majority of the House doesn`t support it. So each side`s not going to just simply give the other side what it wants. It`s Politics 101."\par \par \uc2 I go back to my first question. Do you accept that premise?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: I -- I accept the premise that Joe Biden`s got to come up with a deal. He doesn`t have one. I would ask you -- you should ask him, if he actually would take your question, you should ask him if he has a plan, if he has a strategy. The only thing he`s ever said is we should -- it should just be clean. But nobody with an economic mind is saying, "Yeah, sure, just give the federal government a blank check."\par \par \uc2 TODD: So are you comfortable with breaching the debt ceiling?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: No, I`m not comfortable with that. This should have been done 100 days ago. House Republicans have been working on this for three months. I`ve been in the room. I`ve been in those meetings. And while we were working on a strategy to raise our nation`s debt ceiling, which is something the House of Representatives has passed, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was ignoring it. And \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  was ignoring it. And I think that he has been derelict in his duty.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about the presidential race. We got off more on the debt ceiling than I think either one of us expected. I want to play your celebration of Governor DeSantis and ask you about it on the other side of this.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Sure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: The newly re-elected...\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ... Democrat-beating, freedom-standing-for, America`s governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Obviously, you chose to support, right now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , instead of who you described as America`s governor there. What could Governor DeSantis have done to convince you to stick with him and not the former president?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, first of all, that -- that speech was pretty good, Chuck, you`ve got to acknowledge that. That was good stuff. Anyway...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Look, to me it`s not really about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  versus \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . It`s about what America needs. And that`s where I`ve been really since day one. We -- I talked, a little bit, about foreign policy in the last block. We have a situation right now where Russia`s on the move and China is on the move. We need somebody who can step in, day one, look at \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , look at \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , and say, OK, enough. I`m back. We`re going to get everything in order the way it was before."\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t think DeSantis has that ability?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: I think it`s going to take him time. I think there`s only one person who has that ability right now, and it`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And for all the naysayers who would say, "Oh, he`s unpredictable; oh, we don`t know what he`s going to do," let`s be very clear. When he was president of the United States, the world was in a much safer place. Nobody can say that the world is safer now than when it was when he was president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He called January 6th "a beautiful day." That is not something many people associate January 6th with. Do you really think that`s an electable position?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Look, I think what happened on January 6th was a terrible day for the country. I...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t think it was a beautiful day?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: I was on the House floor. It was a terrible day for the country. But that also being said, we have a situation where we`ve got to get the country back on track. I made a comment the other day that, as bad as January 6th was, the American people aren`t consuming January 6th the way the media and Washington is consuming -- consuming January 6th.\par \par \uc2 Our country has to get back on track.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s pretty scarring in Washington. In fairness to Washington, it was a pretty scarring day for Washington.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: It was. It was. But what`s happening on our southern border right now? That`s scarring for the people who live at the southern border. What`s happening for middle-income families when their wages are being eroded by a massive inflation brought to us by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? That is the stuff that the American people are concerned about right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s been some talk that you might be interested in running for governor in 2026. So let me ask you this. Would you have signed that six- week ban, or do you think that was too much?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: You know, honestly, that`s something where you have to deal with your legislature on that. You have to look at your legislature. You have to deal with the people in your state, and you figure out what that process is going to be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sounds like that -- sounds like that you think six weeks is too much?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, they put a...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: They also put a lot of exceptions into it. Look, I wasn`t in the legislature when they did this. I want to be blunt with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: I`m not sure where -- where the governor was and the legislature was on this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Um-hmm.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: What I will tell you is this is a far better place to be than when Roe v. Wade was the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thinks it`s too much and it`s too extreme. Do you agree with him on that?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: I think he`s going to figure out what he thinks is going to be the best thing for the country. He`s said that already. He`s on the record with that. Look, abortion politics is very personal. It`s very, very personal. And it`s obviously -- we all know it`s hyperbolic in our country. People are all over the place.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t that a reason not to have government try to get so involved in it?\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Well, I would argue that the Supreme Court already did that. They did that for 50 years. And that`s why it was important to return this back to the states. Because the country`s never really had to deal with the politics of abortion.\par \par \uc2 Compare us to the European countries. America`s abortion policy is radical compared to the European countries. Most European countries are at 10 to 12 weeks. That`s where they are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They also have exceptions that allow abortion all the way...\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: The Florida law has exceptions as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... all the way there.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to do more on abortion here in a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 Byron Donalds, Republican congressman from southwest Florida, thanks for coming in.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Any time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DONALDS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Speaking of abortion, Republicans in North Carolina took advantage of their new super-majority, pushed through an abortion ban of 12 weeks over Governor Roy Cooper`s veto.\par \par \uc2 The new law bans abortion after 12 weeks, requires patients to meet in person with a physician at least 72 hours before the procedure. It includes 20-week exceptions for rape and incest and a 24-week exception for what they call "life-limiting fetal abnormalities."\par \par \uc2 So joining me now is the Democratic governor of North Carolina, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor Cooper, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Glad to be with you, Chuck, as always.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me -- let me throw this -- we described how this bill came to be. It -- it is a less restrictive bill than some of North Carolina`s surrounding states. So let me ask you about how The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  described it.\par \par \uc2 They called it this. "It is the first new abortion ban to pass since the fall of Roe v. Wade that does not outlaw all or most abortions, effectively allowing roughly 90 percent of abortions to continue."\par \par \uc2 Are they correct in that assessment? Do you agree with The Washington Post`s assessment of that?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: I don`t think so, but I hope so. We`re going to make sure that this bill gets implemented in a way that we provide the most access possible to women. this bill was contradictory, conflicting, confusing. They wrote it in the middle of the night. It`s going to be open to interpretation.\par \par \uc2 This is in no way a reasonable compromise, as Republicans have presented it. It`s a compromise between the right wing and the radical right wing. It is better than some of the surrounding states. But we know that Republicans are unified in their assault on women`s reproductive freedom. Not a single one of them, not a single one of them stood up, even when they had made promises to the people that they weren`t going to change North Carolina`s abortion law. That tells us where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, the bill also includes millions of dollars of funding to expand access to contraception for low-income and uninsured patients. It increases payments for foster families and expands access to childcare. And it includes -- increases paid parental leave for state employees.\par \par \uc2 Did you find those provisions that were included something you could support?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Well, first, I`ve already issued an executive order for paid parental leave for state employees. And they should be doing these kinds of things anyway. What they spent a lot of time doing is dressing up this bill so that they could attract their swing Republicans. Because they knew they needed every single vote in order to be able to get this bill passed. Democrats were unified on a Roe v. Wade standard.\par \par \uc2 So what they did is try to make this bill ugly. It`s the old "lipstick on a pig" kind of thing. Yes, those investments are important, and should be made anyway, but when you look at what this bill does, I don`t think we can even comprehend yet the pressure that these clinics are going to be under by these additional restrictions, women who are working hourly wages and already have children and have to make multiple trips in order to get reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina has been an access point in the Southeast. We already have long waiting lines. And when you compress the time that women have to make these decisions, I don`t think it`s reasonable to call this thing a 12-week ban because, inside of that 12 weeks, there`s a lot going on that would be obstacles to women in being able to get care.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, the 72-hour provision certainly is a -- is a hurdle, at a minimum, and perhaps a full-on obstacle.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask about the party switcher, Tricia Cotham, who was a Democratic state representative, switched parties, allowing the super-majority to be there.\par \par \uc2 A fellow state rep, a Democratic state rep, Cecil Brockman, said the following, and I`m curious of your response.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:01]\par \par \uc2 "I didn`t see it coming" -- referring to the party switch -- "but I do get how she feels resentful from the constant character attacks that she has received and a couple of us have received. It is a lot of eating your own. The biggest issue with Democrats in the state of North Carolina is that we`d rather be right than win elections."\par \par \uc2 The implication that the progressive left so attacked Tricia Cotham that -- that she felt unwelcome in the party. Was that a -- is that a fair critique?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: You can`t make major policy changes because you get your feelings hurt. Here in North Carolina, we are on a razor`s edge. Major policy changes can happen because of one legislator making a decision. Because of our gerrymandered districts, we are so close to a super-majority. The last four years we`ve had enough Democrats to be able to stop all of the bad legislation. All of my vetoes had been upheld. But when you`re talking about investments in education for our children; when you`re talking about expanding Medicaid, which we were able to get done, by the way; when you`re talking about bills that discriminate, bills that attack voting rights, bills that affect our democracy, those one votes matter.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, it`s a lot of pressure to make sure that we don`t have these radical policy changes in a state that has kept our purple nature. One of the reasons we`re the number one state in the country for business is because businesses say we are stable; we have a good business environment, but we also have stayed out of the culture wars. And if you run for elective office, you`ve got to be tough; you`ve got to be ready to jump into these issues and understand that there are going to be differences of agreement -- differences of opinion, and that people can be pretty rough in -- in that kind of situation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, politics sure ain`t beanbag.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 We do know this. I think abortion and the issue of abortion will be the number one issue in North Carolina come 2024. We will see what it does to the state`s politics.\par \par \uc2 Governor Cooper, Democrat from North Carolina, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, when will the federal government run out of money? Can a deal still be reached to avoid a catastrophic default? You`re going to hear from the secretary of Treasury, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As the Treasury Department warns that the U.S. could breach the debt ceiling as soon as June 1st, this week negotiations were on, and then they were off; then they were on again, as the bases of both political parties balked at some of the compromises that were being made public that were supposedly on the table.\par \par \uc2 A Republican aide told NBC News that House conservatives are, quote, "privately seething at how the talks have been going," saying Republicans are giving away their leverage.\par \par \uc2 Well, then you had 11 progressive Democrats in the Senate and 66 in the House are arguing that President Biden should invoke the 14th amendment and pull out of talks completely, saying the Constitution requires the government to pay its debts.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:09]\par \par \uc2 So with the bases of both parties unhappy, in theory we`re in the sweet spot for a deal to get done, or will the bases derail this deal?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the secretary of Treasury, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Secretary Yellen, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Thanks so much, Chuck. It`s a pleasure to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I want to start with -- today is May 20th. Twelve days is June 1st. How hard is 12 days?\par \par \uc2 President Biden seemed to hint earlier this morning that he thought we`re two to three weeks away. And, you know, 12 days, two weeks; we can argue there. But how hard is this June 1st deadline?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, I indicated in my last letter to Congress that we expect to be unable to pay all of our bills in early June, and possibly as soon as June 1st. And I will continue to update Congress, but I certainly haven`t changed my assessment. So I think that that`s a -- a hard deadline.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to point out something that a lot of House conservatives just simply don`t believe what Treasury is doing. I`m going to read you one quote here.\par \par \uc2 "Some conservative Republicans have said for months that they felt Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  had exaggerated the risk of a default in June in a bid to put pressure on the House Republican majority. `She`s going to play it out now, saying it`s a crisis; the day is here,` said Rep. Ralph Norman, a member of the Freedom Caucus."\par \par \uc2 What do you think you could do to convince Congressman Norman that -- that June -- that this early June deadline is real?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, Treasury has a long history of informing Congress about how much cash and headroom we have under the debt ceiling. And we take pride in the credibility of the forecasts that we make.\par \par \uc2 I would point out that the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  has recently indicated that they expected early June is a -- will be a problem. And forecasters on Wall Street who look at information daily on our cash balances and resources agree.\par \par \uc2 So there will be hard choices to make if the debt ceiling isn`t raised. And, you know, I would simply say, since 1789 the United States has a history of paying its bills on time. That`s what the world wants to see...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: ... a continued commitment to do that. It`s what underlies \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  securities as the safest investment on the planet. And it`s not an acceptable situation for us to be unable to pay our bills.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I just want to, sort of, understand the "go or no go" to June 15th in this way. It`s clear that June 1st is not hard, but it`s the beginning of that period. What is the likelihood we could get to the June 15th tax receipts to avoid breaching the debt ceiling?\par \par \uc2 Can you put a percentage on it? Is it 20 percent, 40 percent, something like that?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, there`s always uncertainty about tax receipts and spending. And so it`s hard to be absolutely certain about this. But my assessment is that the odds of reaching June 15th, while being able to pay all of our bills, is quite low.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough. Let me ask you about what are the sticking points here? Is -- it does appear as if -- we heard President Biden bring up revenue, meaning taxes; maybe it`s closing loopholes, or maybe it`s actually raising a percentage here or there. Is it a hard and fast line?\par \par \uc2 Is the White House going to accept a deal on this to raise the debt ceiling that includes spending caps but does not include any increased revenues or taxes?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, look, you know, the -- the White House is negotiating in good faith with the Republicans to try to find a bipartisan solution. I don`t want to negotiate in public and put down any red lines, but certainly the president has pointed out, and it`s important for the American people to understand, we`re all concerned about deficits and fiscal responsibility. But deficits can be addressed both through changes in spending and also through changes in revenue, and the Republicans have taken that off the table.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:05]\par \par \uc2 Something that greatly concerns me is that they have even been in favor of removing funding that`s been provided to the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  to crack down on tax fraud. We have an enormous gap between the taxes we`re collecting and what we should be collecting, if everyone paid the taxes that they really owe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: And that`s really a reflection of tax fraud. It amounts to an estimated $7 trillion over the next decade. So equipping the \uc2 IRS\uc2  with the funding they need to audit high income individuals and corporations, that`s something that doesn`t cost money. It nets money substantially for the federal government.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, of course, there are revenue proposals that we think make the tax code fairer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s end with a phrase that I talked to you about at the beginning, and that is this idea of extraordinary measures. Is the 14th Amendment fall into the category of extraordinary measures?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, extraordinary measures is used in a different way, but there has been --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand.\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: -- much discussion of the 14th Amendment.\par \par \uc2 And, as President Biden said, I believe, this morning, it doesn`t seem like something that could be appropriately used in these circumstances, given the legal uncertainty around it, and given the tight timeframe we`re on.\par \par \uc2 So my devout hope is that Congress will raise the debt ceiling and we`ll pay all of our bills.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But are you just going to -- if it doesn`t happen, okay, and we`re at this point, are you just going to sit there and pick and choose, or why not go and pay, and then, you know, it`s one of those, are you going to pay all of our debts and essentially let the courts tell us you shouldn`t have?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if we breach this debt ceiling -- I understand not wanting to use it now -- but are we really going to sit there and just let some bills go unpaid, not even trying it?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, we take the debt ceiling seriously as a constraint on our ability to pay bills that are coming due. And my assumption is that if the debt ceiling isn`t raised, there will be hard choices to make about what bills go unpaid. And, you know, defaulting on any of our payments --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, there will be bills -- what you`re saying is if the debt ceiling is not raised, that there`s going to be a default on President Biden`s watch, even though he doesn`t want it, that you`re not going to try any other measures? You`ll allow a default on some debt if Congress doesn`t raise this debt ceiling?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Well, there will be some bill. We have to pay interest in principle on outstanding debt. We also have obligations to seniors who count on Social Security, our military that expects pay, contractors who`ve provided services to the federal government, and some bills have to go unpaid.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So there will be some bills unpaid if the debt ceiling is not raised?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Yes. And many people, including in the credit rating agencies --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Have you decided which bills -- have you decided which bills those are going to be yet?\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Look, I would say we`re focused on raising the debt ceiling. And there will be hard choices if that doesn`t occur. There can be no acceptable outcomes if the debt ceiling isn`t raised, regardless of what decisions we make.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Secretary Yellen, Treasury secretary for President Biden -- appreciate you coming on and sharing the administration`s perspective. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 YELLEN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is about to make it official. But there`s a whole wave of other contenders who are suddenly betting he`s already missed his moment. Is there room for more alternatives to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:37:11]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Panel is here. NBC News Washington managing editor, Carol Lee; Dan Balz, the chief correspondent at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 "; former Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  of Florida; and Symone Sanders-Townsend, she`s host of "Symone" and the former chief spokesperson of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Look, I want to mostly stick to the Republican primary but, Carol, let`s you and I quickly, on this debt ceiling. I feel like it`s a lot of theater and that almost the lack of urgency on the specifics of the end game from Secretary Yellen, tell me they still -- the White House wants to land this plane almost in any way possible.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, NBC NEWS MANAGING WASHINGTON EDITOR: They do. And they also want to be able to land it in a way where all sides declare victory. That`s been the goal from --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Or don`t all sides declare defeat?\par \par \uc2 LEE: -- well, all along. Well, you know --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I just think they all have to admit that they lost something here, right? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes. But the president wants to be able to say that he went in, he fought for priorities that matter to the Democratic Party, that he didn`t give away too much and look, they raised the debt limit. The noise that we`re hearing is, as those of us who covered this, these fights in the past, know, is part of the process.\par \par \uc2 And so, the president`s speaking with the speaker. He`s likely to meet with the speaker and others this week. And they expect to have some sort of deal by the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Our advice: Pay no attention to the posturing for social media and cable news consumption.\par \par \uc2 Let me set up the presidential race. We had a little bit of back and forth already between DeSantis and a Trump supporter, sort of. Let me give you this back and forth this week from DeSantis and the new nominee for governor of Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: We were supposed to have this big, red wave, and other than, like, Florida and Iowa, I didn`t see a red wave across this country. And so I think the party has developed a culture of losing.\par \par \uc2 GOV.-ELECT DANIEL CAMERON (R), KENTUCKY GOVERNOR NOMINEE: A big thank you to President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  for his support and his endorsement of this campaign. Let me just say, let me just say the Trump culture of winning is alive and well in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Dan Balz, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  finally going to come in this week. I can`t help but wonder, imagine, had he flipped his switch on the exploratory committee in January of this year, where he would be.\par \par \uc2 Would Tim Scott be even announcing this week?\par \par \uc2 DAN BALZ, CHIEF CORRESPONDENT, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, Chuck, I mean, I think the question is, was he ready in January and is he ready today, to be a national candidate? And I think what we`ve seen is somebody who`s been, you know, far from sure-footed in his first steps in trying to run a presidential campaign. But he also caught himself in a situation which he wasn`t quite in, but he was trying to get in.\par \par \uc2 And so I think what we have seen is, in some ways, what may be the worst of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  during this period. And I think the question going forward is, will we see a different and more effective \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, Chuck, from campaigns past, candidates, even ones who start out with a lot of hype, have trouble.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Barack Obama in 2007 had trouble in the early spring. And a lot of people were saying, what`s the matter with this guy? I thought he was a really good candidate.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen it in a variety of other campaigns. So, I think that the real challenge for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is to now step up and show that he`s a national candidate with a national message, and with a kind of confidence about how to deal with and go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , I had somebody who`s wanting DeSantis to get there, sort of wonder here, jeez, a lot`s been lost over these last four months. And does see that, you know, maybe Tim Scott and Nikki Haley don`t have big donors coming to them. Maybe \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  doesn`t have Scaramucci and Steve Cohen saying, yeah, we`ll fund your campaign.\par \par \uc2 You know, all of this is because, not of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but it`s actually because \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has looked weak here.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2 , NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: So, look, two different kinds of candidates getting into the race this week. Tim Scott, who`s going to try to get around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who doesn`t have a choice but to try to go through \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in order to get that nomination.\par \par \uc2 I think, you know, if the brawl between DeSantis and Trump gets nasty enough, candidates like Scott could become interesting, could become attractive. And I also think it`s a little early to write off \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I mean, if any of us were starting a presidential campaign today and we had all that money, all the resources he has --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And already in double digits --\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: And --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- by the way.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: -- in second place, we would probably be happy.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, DeSantis has a strong record to run on for a Republican primary. In a general, you know, the social issues might be a challenge, but he has a strong economic record to run on.\par \par \uc2 So look, been a tough last few months. But it`s a bit early to say this guy can`t make it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Symone, what have you learned here? You were involved in a massive, what some people thought was going to become, a demolition derby on the Democratic side in 2019. All of a sudden, I think we thought we were going to have a smaller field, the field is growing.\par \par \uc2 By the way, is Tim Scott the \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  of this crowd, meaning that he may pop here if people don`t, if people aren`t careful?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, HOST, "SYMONE": Is he the \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , or is he the \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , the person that everyone liked, but just could not catch fire with the voters? And I say that, and I like \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2  personally, but we saw what happened in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 If there were less candidates then, if maybe if there were other people not in the race, could someone like \uc2 Cory Booker\uc2 , could have caught fire? I think it`s a question that Tim Scott himself has asked him a couple of times, his team. Obviously, this is something they`ve had to weigh. And they`ve weighed that it`s better to get in than stay out.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think -- I`m with you. I think it`s early, but it`s also late. And, you know, we had this conversation --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yogi Berra. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: -- earlier this week, you know? It`s late early because it`s early, first debates, Republican primary debate is not until the end of August. But it`s late because Republican primary voters start voting on January 8th. That`s the Iowa caucuses.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: So how do you build the infrastructure? How do you -- how do you become a national candidate that can connect with voters from state to state?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the other issue, of course, is the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 I want to put up this graphic, Carol, that we put together. Since Dobbs, it is a consistent issue here for Republicans across the board. So in 2022, you had the Kansas abortion referendum. You had a special congressional election in New York.\par \par \uc2 And you, of course, you had the midterms themselves, where it was clear abortion was a huge issue. And then look what we just learned over the last couple of months. You had the Supreme Court race in Wisconsin, that abortion rights clearly helped the left there.\par \par \uc2 And you have new mayors on the Democratic side in Jacksonville and an Independent; Republicans knocked out in Colorado Springs, all of them over being perceived as too out of the mainstream on social issues.\par \par \uc2 Will this Republican primary even matter in the presidential because of those numbers?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, I can tell you that the Biden team is certainly looking at this and thinking that this is an opportunity for them to expand the map in North Carolina, as well as Florida. A Biden official has told me that they think the six-week ban that Governor DeSantis signed is very significant. That they --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s why they want to go to Florida, you think?\par \par \uc2 LEE: And they are already, they`re, quote/unquote, testing the waters there. They`re on the air. They see an opportunity here. And it all comes down to abortion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Dan, you pointed out in your terrific piece today two key groups in 2022 showed up for Democrats that they didn`t expect -- women without a college degree, and voters under the age of 35.\par \par \uc2 BALZ: Yeah. And I think that the women without college degrees are a prime target. But I think it was the abortion issue that drew them. And I don`t know whether that`ll be the case in 2024. They could easily slip back to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. We have more time, I promise.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the COVID public health emergency has been declared over. So after living with three years of this virus, what was the real impact? Data Download`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:41]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the national emergency that surrounded the COVID-19 pandemic was officially declared over. So we wanted to take a look at just how much this three-year pandemic impacted the country as a whole.\par \par \uc2 And let`s start with the greatest statistic of all, the loss of life. Overall, more than a million Americans died of COVID-19 since it first appeared here in early 2020. That is more people than the populations of Austin, Texas or Jacksonville, Florida, and it`s more people than live in the entire state of Montana.\par \par \uc2 But the good news is, the current death number are among the lowest they`ve ever been. Look at this, just 281 reported last week nationwide. At its peak in early January of 2021, nearly 26,000 Americans lost their life in just a week, just a week.\par \par \uc2 Now, speaking of that -- of those vaccines, according to the CDC, over 81 percent of Americans received at least one dose of a COVID vaccine. That number dropped off a little bit on second doses, as you could see here, nearly 70 percent.\par \par \uc2 Well, let me tell you this. The boosters? They`re not getting -- they`re not getting bought -- used by many folks very often. Mostly, this is folks over the age of 65. It`s an indication of just how comfortable we are now living beyond this virus.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, the economic impact was huge with this pandemic. Did you know we threw over $4 trillion in money from the government to try to offset what was just a huge disruption in the American economy?\par \par \uc2 And even though we threw all that money at the economy, it still impacted our overall national economic trends here -- $14 trillion in lost economic activity from 2020 through the end of last year, according to researchers from the \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Before we go to break, this week, special counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  released his long-awaited report on the Russia investigation. Well, back in 2001, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  investigator Robert Ray joined "Meet the Press" days after striking a deal with the president to avoid an indictment, and he talked about the lessons he learned as an independent counsel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT RAY, INDEPENDENT COUUNSEL: The most important thing I learned is that achieving a just result for a prosecutor`s not so easy. I -- one can think exclusively within the box of being a federal prosecutor, but the position of independent counsel brings I think a larger responsibility not only to the, to the interests of justice, but also to the country`s best interests. And achieving a result that`s satisfactory to all of those concerns is not, is not an easy one.\par \par \uc2 [09:50:08]\par \par \uc2 It is important for the country`s confidence to know that law enforcement with regard to the integrity of public officials can work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We look forward to hearing from \uc2 John Durham\uc2 . We`ve invited him to be on this show.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, we`re going to look at the impact of the Durham report on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and what role it may play for an institution that`s increasingly under partisan attack.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 If you only consume media on the right, you might be excused for thinking this week`s 306-page Durham report on the Russian inquiry was a "bombshell" and "damning" and that the investigation was an "abomination" and a "soft coup".\par \par \uc2 But special counsel John Durham`s actual sharpest conclusions after a four- year investigation were that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  suffered from confirmation bias and, quote, discounted or willfully ignored material information that countered the narrative of collusion between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Russia. The report recommended no wholesale changes to \uc2 FBI\uc2  rules for regulations or wiretaps, and Durham did not send a single person to jail, even though former President Trump once predicted that Durham would uncover the crime of the century.\par \par \uc2 That said, Dan Balz, this is not an \uc2 FBI\uc2  that should feel good about what Durham discovered because at a minimum, this issue of confirmation bias, it goes to the heart of how \uc2 James Comey\uc2  seemed to worry so much about what the perception of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was that he -- whether it was overdid or underdid Hillary stuff and then overdid or underdid Trump stuff.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:00]\par \par \uc2 BALZ: Well, since, since Comey, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been under attack. And the Durham report in many ways confirms what the DOJ IG --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s almost identical, really.\par \par \uc2 BALZ: It`s almost identical.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BALZ: And in some ways backed off some of the rhetoric that they had going into it. But the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s been in a compromised position. And part of that has to do with the nature of our politics today. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has become a politicized institution and a political target, and this is going to make it much more difficult for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  as he goes forward with these investigations, and I don`t see any way out of that in the -- anywhere in the near future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No. And let me throw in this headline from your newspaper, Dan.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  misused surveillance tool on January 6th suspects, BLM, \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  arrestees and others.\par \par \uc2 Symone, I -- look, trust in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is eroding left and right. Feels like we`re in a moment that we need a real Church Committee, that this is a moment like J -- when the J. Edgar Hoover FBI clearly was no longer helping the American people, there was a moment. This feels like we might be in one of those moments.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I would say yes and no, okay? Yes, because obviously, we -- I mean, as Dan was talking, I thought about the activists -- the civil rights activists, \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  activists, Black Panthers --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Who have also been --\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: -- who have been targets of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  for eons and years, okay, going back to well before this current political climate that we sit in.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents, law enforcement across the country, people who work for the government, they are in a precarious position because of this belief in the, quote/unquote, deep state that does not -- I would argue it doesn`t exist. But depending on what you read about this report, you could be persuaded to believe that it does.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents` offices have been targeted, right? Their lives have been put in danger. And so, would a Church Committee do anything but play to -- right into the hands of those in the far wing spaces and places of America?\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hear you, but we got to -- it seems like we have to do something to restore trust. We have to -- and it has to be moderate trust.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: And the reason, Chuck, is that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- you know, the opponents of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the conspiracy theorists, the people who want to diminish our institutions, they just need, you know, 10 percent of the truth, 15 percent of the truth --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, if that.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: -- to make a living and to bring down the institution.\par \par \uc2 So the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has to raise its standards because they can`t even give these people an inch. With an inch, they can bring down these institutions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I`ll be honest. \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  hides.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He hides. He does -- he only goes before Congress, and he doesn`t defend the institution publicly.\par \par \uc2 LEE: There is a lot of consternation within the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about that, about the way he postures publicly. There`s a couple places where this is really going to come to a head this year, and that is the two special counsel investigations, the Hunter Biden investigation and the reauthorization of Section 702 of --\par \par \uc2 TODD: With those FISA warrants, yeah.\par \par \uc2 LEE: The FISA warrant. And that is something that`s going to be a huge fight. The White House has been worried about it already for months.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LEE: It`s going to get worse. And all of this is going to be on display at that time, at the -- by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I have to end it there.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, it has been 54 years since we first landed astronauts on the moon, and now the U.S. is in another space race: back to the Moon and then to Mars. Watch our latest episode of "Meet the Press Reports": "The Race to Mars". It`s on Peacock, \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , or after this broadcast, on NBC News NOW.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230521ej5l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc3}{\*\bkmkend toc3}\b \uc2 A Federal Appeals Court Hearing Arguments On Limiting Access Nationwide To A Widely Used Abortion Pill, A Fight Ultimately Heading To The Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Laura Jarrett \par \uc2 678 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Now to the battle over abortion. A federal appeals court hearing arguments on limiting access nationwide to a widely used abortion pill, a fight ultimately heading to the Supreme Court, while some states restrict abortion further. Here`s Laura Jarrett.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the most commonly used abortion pill still on the market, but its legal future uncertain. A federal appeals court appearing deeply skeptical today of arguments from the Biden administration and pill manufacturer as they try to keep Mifepristone available.\par \par \uc2 JAMES HO, JUDGE: I don`t understand this theme. The FDA can do no wrong. We are allowed to look at the FDA, just like we`re allowed to look at any agency.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): The three-judge panel must decide what to do with that controversial decision out of Texas last month, which invalidated the FDA`s longtime approval of the drug. One judge hearing the case appointed by \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , the other two by Donald Trump. All have a history of supporting abortion restrictions. Judge James Ho calling abortion a moral tragedy in a 2018 case.\par \par \uc2 The stakes of what happens to a pill women can currently get in the mail even higher now with new abortion restrictions emerging at the state level.\par \par \uc2 TIM MOORE, HOUSE SPEAKER, NORTH CAROLINA: We needed to take action to protect the unborn.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): Overnight in North Carolina, a GOP supermajority banned most abortions after 12 weeks with few exceptions, overriding the governor`s veto.\par \par \uc2 DR. BEVERLY GRAY, OBSTETRICS AND GYNECOLOGY ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR, \uc2 DUKE UNIVERSITY\uc2 : The law is incredibly arbitrary. And it was crafted by people who are not physicians. It`s really important to note that when there`s a list of exceptions, someone is always left out.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): North Carolina had been an outlier among the sea of states that restricted access after the Supreme Court decided women don`t have a constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every pregnancy threatens the life of mothers.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): In neighboring South Carolina, a six-week ban like Florida`s is on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:07]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Laura, help me understand where we are. The -- it`s for the abortion pill. The justices agreed last month to keep it on the market during the appeal process.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (on camera): That`s exactly right, Lester. And there`s no deadline for the appeals court to rule on Mifepristone. But once it makes a decision, the case is inevitably headed back to the Supreme Court for a final say.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura Jarrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: President Biden is heading to Asia tonight for the G7 summit as the U.S. inches closer to a critical default deadline. Kristen Welker is in Hiroshima. Kristen, the President with a clear message before leaving today.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Lester, that`s right. President Biden projecting optimism with just 15 days to reach a deal to raise the debt limit or risk economic calamity. While still in Washington, the President said he`s confident the country won`t default, calling a meeting with top congressional leaders this week productive.\par \par \uc2 The president has cut short part of this critical diplomatic trip to deal with the crisis. But despite that, Republican House Speaker Kevin McCarthy blasting Mr. Biden this afternoon for going overseas at all before a deal is done. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230518ej5h0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc4}{\*\bkmkend toc4}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 17, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Gabe Gutierrez, Keir Simmons, Erin McLaughlin, Laura  Jarrett, Kristen Welker, Molly Hunter, Maggie Vespa, Stephanie Gosk, Tom  Costello \par \uc2 3,938 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and wife Meghan pursued by paparazzi.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Conflicting accounts of what happened late last night in the busy streets of New York. Was it a near catastrophic car chase for more than two hours as a spokesperson for Harry claims or what the police called a challenging trip home as photographers pursued them with no reported collisions or injuries? We talked to the cab driver who drove them.\par \par \uc2 An Idaho grand jury indicts Bryan Kohberger accused of killing four college students. The new details just revealed.\par \par \uc2 As a federal court set to restrict access to a widely used abortion pill, the battle bound for the Supreme Court as North Carolina enacts a restrictive abortion law.\par \par \uc2 President Biden heads overseas while striking an optimistic tone that the country will never default as negotiators race to reach a debt limit deal.\par \par \uc2 A former Green Beret killed in Ukraine. What he told NBC News about why he decided to join the fight.\par \par \uc2 The teenager missing for six years finally found after a TV show and a tip.\par \par \uc2 How thieves are getting into your iPhone and stealing everything. The threat and how to protect your data.\par \par \uc2 The first state of the country to ban TikTok. But will it hold?\par \par \uc2 And the Titanic as we`ve never seen it. Spectacular new images that may shed light on why it sank.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome. What happened on the streets of New York City last night involving one of the world`s most famous couples was either a near catastrophic car chase as their spokesman says or arrived that law enforcement sources say was simply a bit chaotic. That spokesman for the Duke and Duchess of Sussex, Harry and Meghan, describing a dangerous two-hour long chase pursued by highly aggressive paparazzi, resulting in multiple near collisions.\par \par \uc2 The episode rekindling grim memories of the wild pursuit that led to the death of Harry`s mother, Princess Diana in a Paris highway tunnel nearly 26 years ago. But tonight in New York, officials are describing what happened to Harry and Meghan as something less harrowing than reported. And that`s where we start tonight with Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Meghan! Meghan! Meghan!\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and his wife, \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 , seen here leading a charity event in New York. And tonight, there appeared to be differing accounts about what happened next.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the Duke and Duchess of Sussex admonishing photographers for a relentless pursuit that lasted two hours and resulted in multiple near collisions involving other drivers on the road, pedestrians, and two \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers. Adding, the near-catastrophic car chase came at the hands of a ring of highly aggressive paparazzi.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS, MAYOR, NEW YORK CITY: The briefing I received, you know, two of our officers could have been injured. I thought that was a bit reckless and irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): Though tonight, at least three law enforcement sources tell NBC News, the couple was followed, not chased, and that the incident was a bit chaotic, not near catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: I would find it hard to believe that there was a two-hour high-speed chase.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): In a written statement, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  says numerous photographers made the couple`s transport challenging, but there were no reported collisions, injuries, or arrests.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Harry and Meghan had private security as they left this venue. And two law enforcement sources tell NBC News that they wanted to return to Manhattan`s upper east side, where they were staying with a friend, and did not want the paparazzi to follow.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): So, the law enforcement sources say the couple was driven up and down Manhattan for more than an hour with a police escort. Then the pair was taken to this police precinct where a taxi picked them up.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Would you describe it as chaotic?\par \par \uc2 SONNY SINGH, TAXICAB DRIVER: Yes, chaotic. You could say chaotic. With me - -\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Catastrophic?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: I don`t know what happened previous in the day, right? With me, it was chaotic, but not crazy, crazy, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Do you think the paparazzi went too far?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: You know, I don`t know. When we were -- they kept their distance when they were following us in my cab.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): Sonny Singh told us he`s the taxi driver who picked up the couple. But he says they drove around for only 10 minutes before the street was blocked by a garbage truck.\par \par \uc2 SINGH: People just came out of nowhere with cameras and started snapping pictures.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Did they seem worried?\par \par \uc2 SINGH: They seem worried. They seem worried and nervous as well.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): He then says he dropped them back off at the police precinct where a different car eventually took them home. \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2 , of course, has railed against the paparazzi for much of his life, accusing them of invading his privacy.\par \par \uc2 HARRY, \uc2 DUKE\uc2  OF SUSSEX: I had to do everything I could to protect my family.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): Though critics say Harry and Meghan also court the cameras and publicity pointing to their \uc2 Netflix\uc2  docu-series.\par \par \uc2 HARRY: No one sees what`s happening behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:02]\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voiceover): It was 26 years ago this summer that his mother, Princess Diana, died during a car chase in Paris.\par \par \uc2 HARRY: You get followed, photograph, chase, harassed. The clicking of cameras and the flashes of cameras makes my blood boil. It makes me angry. It takes me back to what happened to my mom I experienced when I was a kid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe joined us from outside that police precinct. Gabe, was there any video of the pursuit?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Well, Lester, source close to Harry and Meghan says that there is a security video that captured the encounter, but it has not yet been publicly released, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let me turn now to Keir Simmons in London. And Keir, a lot of people immediately thought about Princess Diana when they heard about this.\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: And you know, Lester, inevitably, that is one of the first things that \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  will have thought of when this happened, that terrible night when he lost his mom. Listen, there are parallels. Princess Diana was trying to escape the paparazzi just like Harry and Meghan this week. Princess Diana had lost her royal protection. \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  no longer has royal protection.\par \par \uc2 But there`s also Lester, important context here, too. \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  is fighting a legal battle with the British press. This isn`t just about the past for him. This is about the future. He seems determined to keep fighting, even to change the media. But Lester, there are those who question whether he can win that fight.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Keir Simmons in London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 New developments this evening in the brutal killings of those four University of Idaho students. The suspect has been indicted. And as Erin McLaughlin reports, we will see him in court soon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight in Moscow, Idaho, Bryan Kohberger, the man charged with stabbing four college students to death, now indicted by a grand jury. The indictment includes four counts of murder in the first degree and one count of burglary. If found guilty, he could be sentenced to death.\par \par \uc2 Kohberger has yet to enter a plea, but his previous attorney said he believes he`ll be exonerated. The indictment follows his December arrest at his family home in Pennsylvania. More than 45 days after Maddy Mogen, Kaylee Goncalves, Xana Kernodle, and were found brutally murdered in this college house.\par \par \uc2 According to court documents, Kohberger`s DNA was found on a knife sheet at the crime scene. And his white Hyundai Elantra scene driving here during a routine traffic stop. It was also allegedly caught on camera in the area the night of the killings. One of two surviving roommates telling police, she saw a male figure with bushy eyebrows, a mask, and black clothing in the home that night.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: So, this grand jury indictment simplifies the process for the surviving witnesses.\par \par \uc2 JEAN FISHER, FORMER DEPUTY PROSECUTING ATTORNEY, ADA COUNTY, IDAHO: Absolutely, because they don`t have to testify in front of Kohberger during this preliminary proceeding.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voiceover): News of the indictment is the latest twist in a grisly case that`s left a small university community heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 Last weekend, Kaylee and Maddy both received posthumous degrees.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (on camera): Kohberger will be arraigned on Monday. Legal experts say once he enters a plea, the trial must begin within six months unless he waves his right to a speedy trial. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Erin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the battle over abortion. A federal appeals court hearing arguments on limiting access nationwide to a widely used abortion pill, a fight ultimately heading to the Supreme Court, while some states restrict abortion further. Here`s Laura Jarrett.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the most commonly used abortion pill still on the market, but its legal future uncertain. A federal appeals court appearing deeply skeptical today of arguments from the Biden administration and pill manufacturer as they try to keep Mifepristone available.\par \par \uc2 JAMES HO, JUDGE: I don`t understand this theme. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  can do no wrong. We are allowed to look at the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , just like we`re allowed to look at any agency.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): The three-judge panel must decide what to do with that controversial decision out of Texas last month, which invalidated the FDA`s longtime approval of the drug. One judge hearing the case appointed by \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , the other two by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . All have a history of supporting abortion restrictions. Judge James Ho calling abortion a moral tragedy in a 2018 case.\par \par \uc2 The stakes of what happens to a pill women can currently get in the mail even higher now with new abortion restrictions emerging at the state level.\par \par \uc2 TIM MOORE, HOUSE SPEAKER, NORTH CAROLINA: We needed to take action to protect the unborn.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): Overnight in North Carolina, a GOP supermajority banned most abortions after 12 weeks with few exceptions, overriding the governor`s veto.\par \par \uc2 DR. BEVERLY GRAY, OBSTETRICS AND GYNECOLOGY ASSOCIATE PROFESSOR, \uc2 DUKE UNIVERSITY\uc2 : The law is incredibly arbitrary. And it was crafted by people who are not physicians. It`s really important to note that when there`s a list of exceptions, someone is always left out.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): North Carolina had been an outlier among the sea of states that restricted access after the Supreme Court decided women don`t have a constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Every pregnancy threatens the life of mothers.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (voiceover): In neighboring South Carolina, a six-week ban like Florida`s is on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:07]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Laura, help me understand where we are. The -- it`s for the abortion pill. The justices agreed last month to keep it on the market during the appeal process.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (on camera): That`s exactly right, Lester. And there`s no deadline for the appeals court to rule on Mifepristone. But once it makes a decision, the case is inevitably headed back to the Supreme Court for a final say.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura Jarrett, thanks.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: President Biden is heading to Asia tonight for the G7 summit as the U.S. inches closer to a critical default deadline. Kristen Welker is in Hiroshima. Kristen, the President with a clear message before leaving today.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Lester, that`s right. President Biden projecting optimism with just 15 days to reach a deal to raise the debt limit or risk economic calamity. While still in Washington, the President said he`s confident the country won`t default, calling a meeting with top congressional leaders this week productive.\par \par \uc2 The president has cut short part of this critical diplomatic trip to deal with the crisis. But despite that, Republican House Speaker Kevin McCarthy blasting Mr. Biden this afternoon for going overseas at all before a deal is done. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking news from Ukraine tonight. Another American volunteer, former Green Beret, has been killed in battle there. Molly Hunter has more on his decision to fight as new images show the immense toll of war in the east.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, as this grinding war continues to rob young Ukrainian lives on the battlefield, the family of American volunteer soldier Nicholas Maimer telling NBC News he was killed on Ukraine`s front lines.\par \par \uc2 The 45-year-old Idaho native, now one of at least 10 Americans killed since the war started. He arrived on his own to join the fight in May 2022. And months before his death, he spoke to NBC`s Raf Sanchez, protecting his identity, going by the name Sema. The 20-year \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  veteran training volunteer soldiers outside of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS MAIMER, AMERICAN KILLED IN UKRAINE: I had that strong internal debate on whether I should, what I could contribute, you know, should I accept the risk? And ultimately, I decided that it was worth it to help Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voiceover): And worth the risk.\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Have you been to the east?\par \par \uc2 MAIMER: I signed up knowing that but with the statement that I was willing to fight.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: Ukraine says they now have the momentum in Bakhmut, fighting hard for what little remains. Today, new satellite imagery appears to show the devastating toll from May 2022 to the charred earth of this month.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (on camera): Tonight, Russia is claiming they destroyed launchers, part of U.S.-made Patriot Air Defense Systems in that massive attack yesterday. Well, tonight, two U.S. officials telling NBC News a Patriot system incurred damage, but is fully operational. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Molly Hunter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, a mystery solved. A girl missing for six years found. The new details about the tit that led to her discovery all after a TV show about the case. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We have new details in the shocking case of a missing girl finally found alive after six years thanks to a tip from a teen who saw the girl`s story on a true crime show. Maggie Vespa reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KAYLA UNBEHAUN, SIX YEARS MISSING: Hi, this is Taylor here. I have a cat.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, new details in the crucial tip that brought Kayla Unbehaun home. Six years after police say her mother, who did not have legal custody, kidnapped her. Investigators in North Carolina now say a teen girl working at an Asheville clothing store Saturday spotted Kayla and recognized the now 15-year-old.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN BROWN, ASHVILLE, NORTH CAROLINA POLICE DEPARTMENT: The employee of the store had a personal connection through their childhood.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): The tipster also crediting the hit show Unsolved Mysteries which featured Kayla`s case last fall.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: The media attention that this case has received over the years up to and including the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  documentary most likely heightened her awareness.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:08]\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voiceover): Kayla and her mom, police say, initially giving officers fake names and birthdays. Kayla seeming calm.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: She looked relatively healthy, didn`t appear to be in any type of physical trauma.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Police soon uncovered the pair`s true identities. Tonight, they say Kayla is back home in Illinois. Her mother, Heather Unbehaun charged with kidnapping. The remarkable homecoming following years of pleas from Kayla`s dad, Ryan Iskerka, on multiple true crime shows.\par \par \uc2 RYAN ISKERKA, FATHER OF KAYLA UNBEHAUN: My daughter was gone.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Tonight, Iskerka writing in a statement, I`m overjoyed that Kayla is home safe, adding we ask for privacy as we get to know each other again and navigate this new beginning.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Heather Unbehaun is out on $25,000 bond. Her attorney declined to comment. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Maggie Vespa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the first state to ban TikTok, but will it hold? Plus, a new warning, thieves stealing iPhones, passcodes, and even locking you out of all your data. How to protect yourself? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Montana today became the first state to ban TikTok. The governor is signing a bill that will bar the Chinese-owned video app from operating in the state because of privacy and security concerns. It will also prohibit mobile app stores like \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  from offering TikTok in the state. A challenge on First Amendment grounds is expected as Congress debates limiting TikTok.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the important warning. Thieves are stealing iPhones and passwords to hijack all your personal information. Stephanie Gosk explains how they`re doing it and how to protect yourself.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:15]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Just behind the locked screen of an iPhone, a goldmine of money, memories, and connections, and thieves have found a way in.\par \par \uc2 JOANNA STERN, SENIOR PERSONAL TECH COLUMNIST, WALL STREET JOURNAL: They`re stealing your passcode.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: It`s so sinister.\par \par \uc2 STERN: It`s so sinister but also so simple.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: So, they go to places where people are vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 STERN: Drunk. People are vulnerable. They`re drunk. They`re having good time.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): Joanna Stern covers tech for the Wall Street Journal. She`s done a series of reports exposing a new crime wave. Phones being ripped off at bars leading to tens of thousands of dollars stolen and \uc2 Apple\uc2  accounts locked.\par \par \uc2 STERN: I was blown away by how many people I heard from that had been to different cities across the U. S.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): NBC`s digital team also helped uncover a ring of thieves allegedly targeting gay bars in Manhattan. Victims are drugged. Their phones stolen. Two men John Umberger and Julio Ramirez overdosed and died. Three suspects are charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 Taylor Ashie says he too was drugged and a victim of theft, waking up after a night out and realizing his phone was gone.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR ASHIE, VICTIM OF THEFT: I immediately got up, went to check my computer to see what was going on. I had about 20 to 25 fraud alert emails.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): He says the thieves changed his \uc2 Apple\uc2  ID password, locked him out, and then went on a shopping spree.\par \par \uc2 ASHIE: They added my credit cards to \uc2 Apple\uc2  Pay which I had not previously used, and then they went around New York City using my phone as a credit card.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): Ashie would get roughly $15,000 back but what he couldn`t get restored was his \uc2 Apple\uc2  account.\par \par \uc2 ASHIE: Hours of conversations with \uc2 Apple\uc2 , support staff. I never received my information back.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: We`re talking about personal photos, contacts?\par \par \uc2 ASHIE: Both.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): The key is getting the passcode.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: How quickly can you change my \uc2 Apple\uc2  ID?\par \par \uc2 STERN: All right, so I`m going to tap here at the top. This is your iCloud or \uc2 Apple\uc2  ID area. I`m going to password and security. I`m going to change password. I`m going to put in your passcode. It`s in. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 GOSK: And you`re there.\par \par \uc2 STERN: And I`m there.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): It took her 18 seconds. But there`s more.\par \par \uc2 STERN: These thieves on the streets know how to enable something that`s a pretty hidden setting. It`s called this recovery key.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): The recovery key is supposed to add another layer of protection.\par \par \uc2 STERN: The problem is that the thieves have the recovery key. Once you`ve changed someone`s \uc2 Apple\uc2  ID password and turned on this recovery key, it`s basically game over for the person whose account that is.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): In a statement, \uc2 Apple\uc2  says we take all attacks on our users very seriously no matter how rare. We work tirelessly every day to protect our user`s accounts and data and are always investigating additional protections. There are some steps to take. Make the passcode on the iPhone stronger like using six digits instead of four.\par \par \uc2 STERN: You can also go here to passcode options and do a custom alphanumeric code.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): Another option is going to screen time, then content and privacy restrictions where there are several places you can add a four- digit code that will block access. The changes will make the phone less convenient.\par \par \uc2 ASHIE: I feel like it`s Fort Knox on my phone now.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): But with life`s most important information in the palm of your hand, the hassle may be worth it.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Gosk, NBC News New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: When we come back, the astonishing new images of the Titanic like you`ve never seen it before.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, astonishing new 3D images of a Titanic providing the most captivating view yet of the shipwreck. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The images are breathtaking in their clarity and detail. The Titanic broken in two, 1200 feet below the North Atlantic. Too big for a single photo, the new 3D scan combining 700,000 images reveals the full scale of the disaster. The bow covered in rust. It`s anchor still attached. The boiler room where workers shoveled coal to keep the lights on. The propeller, its serial number, still legible. Scattered on the ocean floor unopened champagne bottles, shoes, hats and jewelry.\par \par \uc2 1500 people died that day in 1912. Edith Haisman was just 16 on board with her parents. We talked to her in 1996.\par \par \uc2 EDITH HAISMAN, TITANIC SURVIVOR: I`ll never forget it, see all the dead bodies floating in the sea. It was terrible.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voiceover): A deep sea mapping company used submersibles to create this 360-degree view. Coming soon, a documentary looking at how an iceberg cut open a ship once branded unsinkable.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: This makes the Titanic tragedy much more real.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY GEFFEN, CEO AND CREATIVE DIRECTOR, ATLANTIC PRODUCTIONS: There`s no doubt about that. People around the world will be able to walk around the Titanic for themselves and they`ll be able to see the wreck, they`ll be able to see the debris field. This is a history where we can live in the past.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voiceover): A virtual view preserved forever.\par \par \uc2 Tom Costello, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The fascination continues.\par \par \uc2 That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230518ej5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc5}{\*\bkmkend toc5}\b \uc2 Frustrated Over Getting Quality Care: What Mothers To Be Want In The Delivery Room\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Zinhle Essamuah \par \uc2 585 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: As Mother`s Day approaches, we`re reminded pregnancy and childbirth can come with health risks. And for Black women, those risks are significantly higher. Zinhle Essamuah now on why more mothers are working with doulas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ZINHLE ESSAMUAH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Kanaiah King`s (ph) first pregnancy in 2021 was traumatic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good job.\par \par \uc2 Everything that could go wrong went wrong.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): She went into labor early at just 24 weeks and had a serious infection.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You know, doctors kind of use the big words, thinking you would know, but I didn`t. It was my first time.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): But the St. Louis native had support from a doula, Robin Lloyd.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ms. Robin would literally stop them like, hey, stop. Let me talk to her. And she would tell me exactly what it meant.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): Birthing doulas are trained nonclinical professionals who support clients through pregnancy, labor, delivery, and postpartum.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We all are going through the same thing.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): The doulas offer one-on-one education.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No question is a dumb question.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): And group courses for expectant and new moms. Kanaiah credits Robin for helping her get through it all?\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: What are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m very grateful for her. I`m very grateful for her. Because if she wasn`t there, I don`t know what would have happened.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: Do you think you would be here and your kids would be here if you hadn`t had a doula?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not at all, no.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  finding doulas-improved outcomes for women and labor. They`re especially critical for Black women, who are three times more likely to die from a pregnancy-related cause than White women.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re so cute.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): Kanaiah`s doula, Robin, works with the nonprofit Parents as Teachers, motivated by her own life-threatening delivery. The nonprofit received state funding, making it possible to provide doula services to families cost-free. Of the nearly 320 families they serve, many unhoused, 95 percent are people of color, and all of their doulas are Black women.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH: Why is it particularly important for women of color and Black women to have access to them?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, I think -- I think access is the key. We wanted to make sure that we had Black doulas serving those families that resemble them.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): Now, Kanaiah is expecting a baby boy in just a few weeks with Robin by her side.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m going to be there. You know, you`re going to have that support and not to worry.\par \par \uc2 ESSAMUAH (voiceover): Zinhle Essamuah, NBC News, St. Louis, Missouri.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, the moving reunion for one man who nearly died in a supermarket and the hero nurses who saved his life.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230516ej5c000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc6}{\*\bkmkend toc6}\b \uc2 New Guidelines For Breast Cancer Screening\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Erin McLaughlin \par \uc2 404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: There is major news tonight about women in breast cancer. Government experts now say mammograms should begin much earlier, at age 40. Erin McLaughlin has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, a leading health panel now recommending mammogram screenings for breast cancer begin for women at age 40, a full decade sooner than the panel`s previous guidelines. Experts say it`s about time.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE JOCHELSON, CHIEF OF BREAST IMAGING, \uc2 MEMORIAL SLOAN KETTERING\uc2 : The science has always been there. That`s why many of us have been recommending 40 for years.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (on camera): So, finally this panel is listening to that science?\par \par \uc2 JOCHELSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voiceover): The draft recommendations come from the U.S. preventative Services Task Force. They say the new guidelines could save 20 percent more lives across the board for all women, emphasizing the new guidelines are especially important for black women.\par \par \uc2 DR. CAROL MANGIONE, US. PREVENTIVE SERVICES TASK FORCE: Black women are 40 percent more likely to die from breast cancer than white women if they get it. They also are more likely to get more aggressive forms of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: The panel stopping short of recommending annual mammogram screenings, maintaining that women still screen every two years.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (on camera): Does this recommendation go far enough?\par \par \uc2 JOCHELSON: No.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN (voiceover): She argues women 40 and over should get screenings every year and says women should start talking with their doctors about risk factors at age 25. Still, experts are hopeful these new recommendations will help send a more consistent message.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:13]\par \par \uc2 MANGIONE: The most important thing for women to know is to begin screening at the age of 40 because it just might save your life.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Erin McLaughlin, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In a moment, NBC News investigates work contracts with the nation`s largest hospital chain nurses say they`ve been a threat to their health and yours.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230511ej590005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc7}{\*\bkmkend toc7}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for May 5, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Laura Jarrett, Richard Engel, Julia Ainsley, Keir  Simmons, Catie Beck, Maggie Vespa, Kelly Cobiella \par \uc2 3,898 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the stunning video just released a former president Trump`s deposition in a civil trial.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): His testimony played for the jury, considering writer E. Jean Carroll`s allegations in her civil lawsuit that he raped her and defamed her. His strong denial and what he says about Carroll, a photo with her and his wife, and an infamous comment he once made about women.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court puts the execution of an Oklahoma death row inmate on hold. My interview with Richard Glossip was said to be his last. Instead, relief for supporters who say his trial was unfair. Their reaction, the moment the news came in.\par \par \uc2 The war in Ukraine, why a Russian mercenary group is threatening to quit fighting in a key city. It`s ultimatum to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Is the U.S. ready for more migrants expected to come to the Southern Border? We put the question to the Homeland Security Secretary.\par \par \uc2 More good news about jobs, but the new warning from President Biden about a potential government default.\par \par \uc2 Will birth control pill soon be available over the counter? The \uc2 FDA\uc2  set to consider it.\par \par \uc2 And the coronation of King Charles now just hours away. Thousands filling the streets for a royal ritual not seen in 70 years.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome everyone. Former President Trump has not testified in the civil lawsuit in which a writer accuses him of raping her more than 20 years ago and then defaming her by saying she made up the allegation. But today, a video deposition given by Mr. Trump was made public for the first time. In it, the former president strongly denies the accusation, intense questioning from E. Jean Carroll`s lawyer.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump was also asked about comments he made about women in that infamous Access Hollywood Tape that came to light before the 2016 election. It`s where we begin tonight with Senior Legal Correspondent Laura Jarrett.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seen under oath, blasting the rape accusation against him from writer E. Jean Carroll.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: She`s accusing me of rape, a woman that I have no idea who she is. It came out of the blue.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Released publicly for the first time today, Mr. Trump`s deposition in a civil case. Carroll is suing him for allegedly defaming her. She says he raped her in a department store dressing room more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: It`s the most ridiculous, disgusting story. It was just made up. If it did happen, it would have been reported within minutes.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: This week, a jury in Manhattan watch clips of the deposition. Carroll`s attorney turning the focus to that now infamous Access Hollywood Tape.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You can do anything.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Whatever you want.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Grab them by the (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And when you`re a star, they let you do it. You can do anything. Grab them by the (BLEEP). You can do anything. That`s what you said, correct?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, historically, that`s true with stars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s true with stars that they can grab women by their (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, that`s what, if you look over the last million years, I guess that`s been largely true. Not always, but largely true, unfortunately or fortunately.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You consider yourself to be a star.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think you can say that, yes.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Mr. Trump had repeatedly said Miss Carroll was not his type.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: It`s not politically correct to say it, and I know that, but I`ll say it anyway.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: But then he mistook Carroll for his ex-wife, Marla Maples, when shown this old photo of him shaking Carroll`s hand.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don`t even know who the woman. Let`s say I don`t know who -- it`s Marla.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You say Marla is in this photo?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: That`s Marla. Yes, that`s my wife.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Throughout the deposition, Mr. Trump steadfastly denying Carroll`s accusation and going after Carroll`s attorney.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You know, it`s not true too. You`re a political operative also. You`re a -- you`re a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Later, turning to her physical appearance.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You wouldn`t be a choice of mine either, to be honest with you. I hope you`re not insulted. I would not, under any circumstances, have any interest in you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Laura, this is the only time the jury is going to actually hear from Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (on camera): Right, Lester. Mr. Trump`s attorney has opted not to put on a defense case. The judge is giving the former president until 5:00 p.m. Sunday to change his mind. If not, closing arguments are expected on Monday. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Laura Jarrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has stepped into temporarily halt the planned execution this month of an Oklahoma man whose case has drawn support from both sides of the death penalty debate. Tonight, my exclusive interview with Richard Glossip on the day a dramatic new twist in his case unfolded.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): Less than two weeks before his scheduled execution, Richard Glossip once again waits at death`s door, like he has so many times before.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And incarcerated individual at Oklahoma State Penitentiary.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Richard, how many execution dates have you had?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD GLOSSIP, PRISONER CURRENTLY AT DEATH ROW: Nine.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And three of those, you were within minutes of being executed.\par \par \uc2 GLOSSIP: Yes, three of them. I was fed my last meals.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Each time he was given a last-minute stay. This morning, Glossip called me from Oklahoma`s death row, his last interview before his new date to die, believing he had 13 days to live. Glossip, a former motel manager, convicted of organizing the 1997 murder of his boss, Barry Van Treese, but he maintains his innocence.\par \par \uc2 I think you know the question I have to start off with and that is did you have anything to do with the murder of Barry Van Treese?\par \par \uc2 GLOSSIP: I did not.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And you might be surprised who believes him and is fighting for him.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCDUGLE (R-OK), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: These people should be ashamed. They should be arrested.\par \par \uc2 These two men are not Glossip`s defense attorneys. They are pro-death penalty conservative lawmakers, Oklahoma representatives J.J. Humphrey and Kevin McDugle.\par \par \uc2 MCDUGLE: I don`t think they`ve got enough to convict him of Jay Walking.\par \par \uc2 J.J. HUMPHREY (R-OK), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: There`s no doubt, no doubt that he`s innocent of murder.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: McDugle and Humphrey are desperate to save Glossip`s life. They point to two independent reviews. Both determine the original investigation was corrupt.\par \par \uc2 HUMPHREY: This is unjust. I mean, this is a case where literally the prosecution team has tried to hide everything that they`ve done. One of the prosecutors, literally, we have it in writing, that she tried to change the testimony of the actual murderer.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Even the state`s Republican attorney general doesn`t think Glossip should be put to death. And in a rare move just this week, the A.G. asked the Supreme Court to grant a stay of execution. The answer came today in the middle of our interview. None of us saw it coming.\par \par \uc2 I just handed a note. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  halts execution of Oklahoma death row inmate Richard Glossip, whose case has drawn support from state`s Republican attorney general.\par \par \uc2 MCDUGLE: Yes, that`s awesome, awesome.\par \par \uc2 HUMPHREY: I tell you, we thought they would do it. Oklahoma is finally going to have the opportunity to look under the hood. The decision granting a temporary stay of execution, giving the court more time to review Glossip`s case. Minutes after the decision, we spoke to Glossip`s attorney Don Knight.\par \par \uc2 DON KNIGHT, ATTORNEY FOR RICHARD GLOSSIP: We are thrilled that this -- that the court granted this stay of execution. This is something that we`ve been asking for a long time. We know that Rich Glossip is innocent.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Yet another reprieve for Glossip for now, but maybe not forever.\par \par \uc2 GLOSSIP: I either let this consume me or I make peace with it and try to live the best I can with what life I have left. And that`s what I try to do. I`m scared to death. Don`t get me wrong. I don`t want anybody to think that I`m taking it lightly or anything, but you have to make peace with the situation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): We have reached out to both the district attorney and the victim`s family after today`s news but have not heard back from either. However, after a recent hearing, the family members said they believe Glossip is guilty.\par \par \uc2 As the war in Ukraine grinds on, tonight, a major rift has escalated between the head of a Russian mercenary group fighting in eastern Ukraine and Russia`s military leaders. And he`s given them an ultimatum. Richard Engel has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It`s been called the biggest land battle since World War II, Bakhmut in eastern Ukraine. For months, a group of mercenaries known as the Wagner group has been leading the charge here where Russia has gained ground. Much to the embarrassment of the Russian army, which to undercut its rival has, according to the mercenaries, been blocking the delivery of supplies.\par \par \uc2 Today, the head of Wagner standing over the bodies of his fighters said he`ll quit Bakhmut next week unless his men get more ammunition. Russia is feeling the pressure.\par \par \uc2 It accuses the United States of conspiring with Ukraine to carry out a drone attack against the Kremlin itself, calling it an attempted assassination on President Putin. Allegations the U.S. and Ukraine strongly deny.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: One thing I can tell you for certain is that the United States was not involved in this incident in any way.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Today, Russia`s Foreign Minister promised a response with what he called concrete actions. And negotiations aren`t going well.\par \par \uc2 In Turkey, talks over access to the Black Sea got heated when a Ukrainian delegate photobombed a Russian negotiator, the frustrated Russian turn back and snatched the Ukrainian flag. Turkey condemned the behavior as undiplomatic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: When asked about the Wagner group`s potential pull out from Bakhmut, the Kremlin`s response was no comment. Lester?\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Richard Engel tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have breaking news about the death of a man in a New York City subway. Jordan Neely died this week after he was put on a choke hold by another passenger. Some riders said Neely had been threatening people on board. NBC has learned that the case is expected to go to a grand jury to decide if charges will be filed against the man who put Neely in that choke hold. He was questioned by police and released.\par \par \uc2 Now, to the border crisis with officials bracing for a rush of migrants when that pandemic border restriction known as Title 42 expires next week. The Biden administration facing bipartisan criticism. And tonight, the Homeland Security Secretary is responding in an interview with Julia Ainsley.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JULIA AINSLEY, NBC NEWS HOMELAND SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, just a week from the lifting of that border restriction known as Title 42, growing numbers of migrants crossing into the U.S. All as the Border Patrol expects illegal border crossings will spike to 10,000 per day when Title 42 lifts.\par \par \uc2 Are you worried about Title 42 lifting next week?\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, U.S. SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: I`m not worried about Title 42 lifting. We recognize the challenge at the border. Our responsibility is to plan and to execute on those plans. And that is indeed what we have done and continue to do.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas here to talk to border agents. The Biden administration has faced criticism over its border policies amid a record number of illegal border crossings. Mayorkas insisting the policies are working.\par \par \uc2 Is our border secure?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Our border is secure. The border is secure because we are maximizing our resources to deliver the most effective results to our border with the most extraordinary workforce in the world.\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: But Hidalgo County Sheriff Eddie Guerra disagrees.\par \par \uc2 Is our border secure?\par \par \uc2 EDDIE GUERRA, SHERIFF, HIDALGO COUNTY: Well, I can tell you no. There`s a lot of places on the southwestern border that the -- our federal partners do not have control.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AINSLEY: As for the bipartisan proposal in Congress to extend Title 42, Secretary Mayorkas told us that the Biden administration has already tried to end Title 42 several times, and now is the time to do so. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Julia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just ours are now, something that Britain and the world have not seen in seven decades, the core nation of a new British monarch. Keir Simmons is watching it all for us from London.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The night before the biggest day of his life, King Charles smiling and laughing. Palace officials say he`s shown no sign of nerves. Today, the Princess of Wales pictured with two first ladies, Jill Biden and Ukraine`s \uc2 Olena Zelenska\uc2 . The King, William and Catherine greeting the public earlier.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM, PRINCE OF WALES: How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : The Princess asked how the children are feeling. \uc2 Prince George\uc2  will be a page of honor tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE, PRINCESS OF WALES He`s excited, a bit nervous. Obviously with a big day ahead. But, No, can`t wait actually.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Even taking a cell phone to console someone`s American relative too ill to make it.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE: I hope you get back as soon.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : The crowds have already camped along the world-famous Mall for a glimpse of history tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 At 5:20 Eastern, a small procession will leave Buckingham Palace with the King to Westminster Abbey. Then the solemn service, not seen this century, will commence. The moment of crowning is expected around 7:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Next, a breathtaking spectacle. From the Abbey, a 4,000-strong military parade would escort the King along this route.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And he won`t answer questions about why.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : But polls show enthusiasm for the monarchy waning and protesters will be out too.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM SMITH, CEO, REPUBLIC: I would think the monarchy is in a lot of trouble because they`ve lost their star player.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : The King is looking to answer those critics by combining tradition with change tomorrow. Manju Malhi, a chef, has been invited for her charity work.\par \par \uc2 MANJU MALHI, CHEF: I feel included and very inclusive and part of British history being a daughter of an immigrant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Meanwhile, Prince Harry is here in London tonight. He will not play a formal role tomorrow. But Buckingham Palace is not saying who will appear on the famous balcony behind me. They say we`ll find out when it happens. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Keir Simmons in London, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the landmark moment in the fight against COVID. Plus, why trade schools are becoming the bold new trend in education. And the battle over birth control. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  is set to consider whether a pill can be sold over the counter.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We are back with a remarkable announcement from the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  that the COVID global health emergency is officially over. It`s a milestone three years after the start of the pandemic that killed millions in upended economies across the globe. Officials say vaccines and high levels of immunity significantly cut the risk, but they caution the virus is still a threat.\par \par \uc2 Turning out of the economy less than four weeks away from the country potentially defaulting if there`s no deal to raise the debt ceiling. Economists say that will be catastrophic. Still, markets close sharply higher today as the economy added 253,000 jobs last month and unemployment fell to 3.4 percent. Catie Beck now on the uncertainty that lies ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): President Biden today highlighting the good.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Unemployment rate is at 3.4 percent, which is the lowest in 50 years.\par \par \uc2 BECK: But accusing Republicans who want spending cuts of putting the economy at risk over the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: They`re trying to hold the debt hostage to us to agree to some draconian cuts, magnificently difficult and damaging cuts.\par \par \uc2 BECK: A possible default on the debt along with rising interest rates and ongoing turmoil among banks fueling fears of a recession. But an uncertain economic future is something Abbie Carlson is preparing for. Her decision to go to trade school wasn`t easy one.\par \par \uc2 ABBIE CARLSON, STUDIES AT A TRADE SCHOOL: People are finding out that with a four-year degree, they`re not making a whole lot of money off the bat and they`re ending up with all of debt.\par \par \uc2 BECK: She`ll graduate with none thanks to state funding for the program. Carlson will finish her training in welding in October.\par \par \uc2 Is the common knowledge among students, we`re going to be hired right away when we leave here?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, 100 percent. If you don`t leave with a job, it`s on you.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Tennessee College of Applied Technology President Nathan Garrett says there`s a waitlist to get in.\par \par \uc2 NATHAN GARRETT, PRESIDENT, COLLEGE OF APPLIED TECHNOLOGY: I think at the end of the day, people are really looking at where`s the value at?\par \par \uc2 BECK: Trade program enrollment is surging with big spikes in construction up almost seven percent, and mechanic and repair programs up five percent last fall, while undergraduate college enrollment has declined in recent years.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I have hope for the future like I never did before.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Hopeful for opportunity even in an uncertain economy. Catie Beck, NBC News, Nashville.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Still ahead tonight, the battle over birth control as the \uc2 FDA\uc2  gets sent away in and whether to sell pills without a prescription.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A revolutionary change could be coming to birth control, pills sold without a prescription. A group of \uc2 FDA\uc2  advisors is set to meet next week to consider the measure. Maggie Vespa has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In an era of historic shifts in reproductive health, a push for an American first over the counter, birth control pills.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that`s great. I feel like it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Without a doubt, this is a right that everybody should have access to.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Advisors to the Food and Drug Administration meeting next week to review drug maker Perrigo`s application to sell its oral contraceptive, Opil, over the counter. It`s been available for 50 years with a prescription like all birth control pills in the U.S. Perrigo aiming to offer it to all women and teens, no doctor needed.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTYN BRANDI, FELLOW, \uc2 AMERICAN COLLEGE OF OBSTETRICIANS AND GYNECOLOGISTS\uc2 : I`m surprised that this hasn`t happened already. People can self-screen. People can read instructions. And so, we shouldn`t be limiting people`s access to care.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: It`s unclear how much Opil would cost, whether it would be available on store shelves or behind a pharmacist counter and whether a minor would need parental consent. An initial \uc2 FDA\uc2  review, flagging concerns about whether adolescents can understand the label`s instructions and whether current and former breast cancer patients, who the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says should not be on hormonal birth control, would know to avoid it.\par \par \uc2 Perrigo writing its application demonstrates that Opil is safe and effective. Experts noting any decision on Opil will have no impact on other prescription birth controls or on lawsuits over the abortion pill, Mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): The FDA`s final decision on the push to sell Opil over the counter is expected this summer. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Maggie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, London`s monarchy mania. Just how far some travel to be in the middle of all the royal excitement.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally tonight, the Royal fans who traveled across the pond for a front-row seat to King Charles` coronation. Here`s Kelly Cobiella.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the flags are flying, the horses prancing, the crowds dressed in their best red, white, and blue for the first coronation in 70 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s just an awesome time to witness history. I mean, we haven`t done this in our lifetimes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: The monarch`s smiling face is everywhere, from windows to fridge magnets.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s our king. He`s going to be our super king.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: A super king with super fans. And we found plenty of Americans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just love it. I just absolutely eat it up.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Braving the rain for a front-row seat to history. Cassandra Estrella Rocha from California changed her vacation plans to be here.\par \par \uc2 Have you been camping here?\par \par \uc2 CASSANDRA ESTRELLA ROCHA FROM CALIFORNIA, AMERICAN TOURIST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 ROCHA: And I`m not a camper.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Why?\par \par \uc2 ROCHA: I want to be a part of history. Like, I just think -- you know, in America, we don`t have things like this, right?\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: As the skies cleared, surprise handshakes from the King, telling greatest hits radio London News --\par \par \uc2 CHARLES III, KING OF THE UNITED KINGDOM: Rain`s a blessing, except for your camera.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: A smiling king, a cheering crowd, and tomorrow, the crowning moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think he`s an amazing, amazing guy, and I`m proud to be a Brit. Long live the queen -- the king.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Kelly Cobiella, NBC News, London.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And NBC`s coverage of Charles` coronation starts at 5:00 a.m. Eastern tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230506ej5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc8}{\*\bkmkend toc8}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for April 30, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Yamiche Alcindor, Ryan Nobles, Claire McCaskill \par \uc2 10,181 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:19]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, age-old problem.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: We now have to finish the job, and there`s more to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: After officially launching his 2024 campaign focusing on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , President Biden dismisses concerns about his age.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I can`t even say the number. It doesn`t -- it doesn`t register with me.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOV. NIKKI HALEY, (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: The idea that he would make it until 86 years old is not -- is not something that I think is likely.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  keeps his focus on Biden and the general election.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: He is grossly incompetent, has no idea what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Are Biden and Trump in a co-dependent relationship? Do they need each other in order to succeed in 2024? I`ll talk to \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , a millennial entrepreneur who is running for the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Disney\uc2  versus DeSantis. \uc2 Disney\uc2  sues Florida`s governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , accusing him of retaliating against the company.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: I don`t think the suit has merit. I think it`s political.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But Republicans are now distancing themselves from the issue as the battle with the entertainment giant escalates.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO, (R) FLORIDA: I do worry that, if this happens too many times, businesses that are thinking about coming to Florida are saying, "Maybe we don`t want to go there."\par \par \uc2 TODD: And border surge. The Biden administration is bracing for a potential wave of migrants at the southern border, when a COVID-era restrictions ends in less than two weeks.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, SECRETARY OF THE DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Our border is not open and will not be open after May 11th.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL, (R) TEXAS: This is irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Will Biden`s new strategy make a difference? I`ll speak to Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "Look For Me There." Grieving for his father and trying to honor his legacy, Luke Russert has written a new book on how he dealt with the sudden loss of his dad.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News Washington correspondent Yamiche Alcindor; former Democratic senator from Missouri Claire McCaskill; NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent Ryan Nobles; and Lanhee Chen, a fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a good Sunday morning. You know, it was 10 years ago this very month that the Senate`s so-called gang of eight rolled out an immigration reform plan that they believed could finally pass Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: We all wish we didn`t have this problem, but we do, and we have to fix it, because leaving things the way they are, that`s the real amnesty.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: I think 2013 is the year of immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it was not, and the laws haven`t changed. Two years later \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would launch his presidential campaign, building his entire political identity on the issue of being tough on immigration and tough on the border. Trump even promised to wall off the nation`s 2,000-mile southern border. And though the wall didn`t materialize, his administration, in an attempt at deterrence at the border, instituted policies that included separating migrant children from their parents and instituting the Remain in Mexico program, which sent migrants back to Mexico while seeking U.S. asylum.\par \par \uc2 Biden endorsed a sweeping immigration plan on his very first day in office, but the plan went nowhere, even though Democrats controlled both houses of Congress at the time. Instead the administration has adopted several of the Trump policies he once criticized, including a transit ban which will force some migrants to apply for asylum from their home countries.\par \par \uc2 Now, as Biden formally runs for a second term, the immigration issue, which he never mentioned this week in his announcement video, continues to be one of his toughest challenges. In 2021, more migrants crossed the southern border than in any year since at least 1960. And in 2022, the record was broken again.\par \par \uc2 In two weeks, an emergency public health rule known as Title 42 that has allowed both the Trump and Biden administrations to expel migrants and block most access to asylum at the border since March of 2020, allegedly to prevent the spread of COVID, will officially expire. And the U.S. relationship with Mexico has perhaps -- the government, that is -- has perhaps never been worse, with cooperation on the drug war at its lowest point in decades, thanks to an administration in Mexico that doesn`t seem interested in dealing with the cartels. Fentanyl, which is produced in Mexico, with chemicals sourced from China, is now the leading cause of death for Americans between the ages of 18 and 49, according to our friends at \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This week, ahead of Title 42 expiration, the Biden administration announced new steps to stem unauthorized migration, including opening new processing centers in Colombia and Guatemala, creating a new family reunification program and increasing assistance to counter smuggling.\par \par \uc2 Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas acknowledged there would be a border surge, but he also said it does not mean more migrants should come.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [09:05:03]\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: The smugglers` propaganda is false. Let me be clear. Our border is not open and will not be open after May 11th.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Secretary Mayorkas joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome back to "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Good morning, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, before I start on this, there`s been a horrific execution- style shooting down in Texas. Authorities are now -- there`s a manhunt right now for somebody who`s armed and dangerous. I know you`ve been monitoring this. DHS is monitoring this. What can you tell us about the alleged suspect, Francisco Oropeza?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Chuck, it`s a horrific crime. And I can assure you and the American people that law enforcement will deliver accountability. The case is an active one, under investigation, so I really can`t comment further.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There is some question about his citizenship. He supposedly had a -- I guess they refer to it as a counselate card from Mexico, meaning he was here legally, but perhaps he`d overstayed?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, Chuck, I -- I won`t comment on it because it is an active case. The tragedy that occurred is -- you described it correctly -- just absolutely horrific. Our hearts go to the -- the victims, the victims` families, those children. And we`ll deliver accountability.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we know if federal authorities are involved in this right now, besides \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the local authorities?\par \par \uc2 Is DHS, are you -- is your team involved, Border Patrol, at all?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So we are monitoring the situation very carefully, but the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is the lead federal investigative agency, and of course working with the locals there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move to the announcement and what`s coming in life after 42. There is already reports in Brownsville, Texas, right now, shelters are overwhelmed. The cities of Chicago and New York are already overwhelmed. What`s May 12th going to look like, if we`re already overwhelmed before the expiration of Title 42?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: A few things, Chuck. First of all, this is a really tough challenge and has been, as we all recognize, for years and years. We are seeing a level of migration, not just at our southern border, but throughout the hemisphere, that is unprecedented. It is, I think, the greatest migration in our hemisphere since World War II.\par \par \uc2 The president, on day one, delivered a solution. He delivered immigration reform legislation that we had hoped Congress would act on swiftly. They haven`t. Within the constraints of a broken immigration system, we are doing so much. Our approach is to build lawful pathways, cut out the ruthless smugglers, deliver lawful pathways so people can access humanitarian relief without having to take the dangerous journey from their home countries and, at the same time, if they arrive at our southern border in between ports of entry, we will deliver consequences.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I want to go through some specific scenarios. So, an unaccompanied minor, under 18, that comes to our southern border on May 12th, what happens?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: We follow the law. And the law provides...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: ... that we take custody of that child, and we have 72 hours within which to transfer that child, that unaccompanied child, to the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 . Then it is for the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , \uc2 HHS\uc2 , to identify a relative, a sponsor, in the United States, to whom they can transfer care of that child.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is at all -- do you accept the idea that that is all considered a loophole to get into the country?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Chuck, we have to take a look at the humanitarian issue here. These are parents that are suffering such desperation, whether it`s by reason of an authoritarian regime, tremendous violence, acute poverty, persecution, that they are so desperate that they send their children to the southern border alone. We have, the law provides, for humanitarian relief for these children. And we enforce that law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. If a family -- when a family comes to the border, if they`ve not done any of this pre-filing, if they`ve not filed for an asylum claim beforehand, they come to the border on May 12th, what happens to a family with children under the age of 18?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So we will exercise our enforcement authorities in a traditional immigration context. Remember, Title 42 is a public health authority, not an immigration authority, that was delivered in response to the COVID-19 pandemic. When that ends on May 11th, we will use our immigration authorities under Title 8 of the United States Code. That family will be placed in immigration enforcement proceedings, removal proceedings. They will make a claim -- if they make a claim for relief, we will adjudicate that claim for relief swiftly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Define "swiftly."\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So it could be days or weeks. It is not going to be months or years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because we`ve seen that, in New York, migrants are getting court dates in 2033.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:03]\par \par \uc2 How does that happen?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So we are -- what we have done now is we are going to be able to exercise our immigration enforcement authorities. We`ve been precluded from doing so by a court. We sought to end Title 42 long ago, with...\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Title 42 does not deliver an immigration consequence. Title 8 of the United States Code does. If an individual is removed under Title 8, they will encounter an at least five-year bar on re-admission to the United States.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s a question I have. There are 2 million asylum cases in the backlog right now. What happens to new asylum claims on May 12th? Do they get put in line behind these 2 million cases?\par \par \uc2 Do they get -- and this is why the quick adjudication, I get it in theory. How is it going to work in practice when we`re sitting on 2 million back -- a 2 million case backlog?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So we`re going to focus on recent border crossers. But the -- the point, the fundamental point, is we have more than 2 million cases in an immigration backlog that has been building year over year over year. What a powerful example of a completely broken immigration system. We have got to fix it. We need legislative reform.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is a -- you know, I`ve gone through this before. I know there`s a comprehensive aspect that we need, but just for border enforcement and the migration, you need more money for what? You want more processing centers in country? Do you want, besides Colombia and Guatemala, do you want to open up centers in Honduras? Could you even open up a center in Venezuela, or do you trust that government to allow us to do that?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, Chuck, we need resources for it all, but remember, the resources will enable us to move more quickly, more efficiently within a broken immigration system. So I just want to be clear that we are working within significant constraints. We need people; we need technology; we need facilities; we need transportation resources, all of the elements of addressing the needs of a large population of people arriving irregularly at our southern border.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s the definition of a secure border to you?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: It is, in the context in which we are working, it is maximizing the resources that we have available to us to deliver the most effective results. And something that is overlooked that I speak about frequently is the fact that our apprehension rates at our southern border are consistent with the apprehension rates in the prior administration. And why is that? It is because of the extraordinary, and extraordinarily heroic work of our United States Border Patrol. But -- but the challenge is enormous.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you about Border Patrol. Because the union doesn`t like you. And they make it pretty clear on social media this month, and last month, the month before. These are all from Border Patrol -- the Border Patrol Union, on you" "The chicken are going to come home to roost because of what he`s done." They call you "a national disgrace." "Remove Mayorkas," was their most recent tweet.\par \par \uc2 How can you lead a group of people whose union wants you out of office?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Chuck, I`m incredibly proud to work with the United States Border Patrol. I have supported them vigorously since my first day in office. I will continue to do so. A tremendous source of pride.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you this...\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you think the disconnect is? Do you think this is -- this is media perception, what? Why do you -- why do you think there -- is this a political disconnect? Is this a red/blue divide, just simply that you`re in a Democratic administration? What do you believe it is?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Chuck, I`m focused on mission. That`s what I`m focused on. I look at their needs. I try to fulfill their needs. We go to Congress and seek support. You know, this is the first administration since 2011 that has plussed up the Border Patrol with more agents. Our request of Congress for fiscal year 2024 is another 350 Border Patrol agents. That hasn`t happened for over 12 years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about the Fentanyl issue. The biggest impediment in dealing with the Fentanyl issue, is it Americans` addiction? Is it the Mexican government`s inability or, frankly, passe -- passe relationship with the cartels, or is it the fact that -- that China so easily exports these materials? What`s the -- what`s the toughest aspect of this?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Chuck, it`s a matter of supply and it`s a matter of demand, no different than the scourge of drugs that has afflicted this country for decades. But I will tell you something, because I was a federal prosecutor for 12 years. I prosecuted cocaine trafficking cases, methamphetamine, black tar heroin. We haven`t seen anything like Fentanyl, its toxicity. It is -- these cartels are peddling in death and destruction. But we have to stop the flow of Fentanyl, and we also have to address the demand.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, in the `80s, we covertly worked with the Colombian government to deal with these cartels in Colombia, and -- and we had success. Is it time to deal -- I mean, I know some people are calling for more direct military confrontation with these cartels, or to call them terrorist organizations. But is it time for a strategy that is similar to what we did with the Colombian cartels in the `80s?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, a couple things. Number one, we are taking it to the cartels with unprecedented strength and focus. And there is a misperception that Mexico is not a good partner in our fight against...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that government an ally, or not? They don`t act like an ally these days.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: They are an ally. And we have a very close partnership with them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Then how come they don`t help on this Fentanyl stuff? They seem to just allay this.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: That is a -- that is a misconception. I will tell you that we have transnational criminal investigative units. Our Homeland Security investigations personnel are in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 TODD: AMLO denies it, though. AMLO himself denies it in public, that somehow they`re -- that they`re having any -- anything to do with Fentanyl trafficking.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Chuck, I can`t speak to his public statements. I can speak to what happens on the ground, operationally. And we work very closely with our Mexican partners.\par \par \uc2 You know, that Fentanyl, though, you know, the precursor chemicals, the equipment used to manufacture it, much of it originates in China. And we`ve got to stop that flow.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what is a good way to do that?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: So, of course, our relationship with -- with China is not very positive. We are working with our Mexican authorities. We are working with our partners internationally to put pressure on China, to interdict the flow, to identify the transport companies, to cut off the finances, to hold individuals and companies accountable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you, you`re in Cabinet meetings. There`s a lot of questions about President Biden and his ability to serve in a second term. You see him up close, face to face. What say you? Is he up for -- is he up for a second term?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Oh, Chuck, 100 percent -- incredibly sharp, incredibly probing, incredible command of the details, probing on the details, asking tough questions. Absolutely. I`m incredibly proud to serve in his administration. I am incredibly proud of the work that we have done across the board...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have full confidence he can serve a second term?\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: A hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Secretary Mayorkas, big task ahead of you. I`m -- we will be back in touch to see how it`s going.\par \par \uc2 MAYORKAS: Thank you, Chuck, look forward to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, he`s the youngest candidate running for president in 2024. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , a millennial businessman running for the Republican nomination, joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As America debates how old is too old to be president, the youngest candidate that`s announced in the 2024 field is currently introducing himself to voters. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  is a 37-year-old multimillionaire who graduated from Harvard College and Yale Law School.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:01]\par \par \uc2 He made his fortune at a hedge fund and in biotech, and he ran an asset management company which calls itself Anti-Woke.\par \par \uc2 A first-time candidate for political office, he says he has plans to start an anti-woke cultural movement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIVEK RAMASWAMY\uc2 , 2024 REPUBLICAN PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: We`re in the middle of a national identity crisis. That leaves a vacuum in its wake. And when you have a vacuum that runs that deep, that is when poison fills the void. Pick your favorite one, wokism, transgenderism, climatism, globalism, COVIDism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ramaswamy, welcome back. Actually, welcome to Sunday "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Thank you. Good to see you again, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with this overall look. You`ve got this anti-wokism. You`ve written books about this. You`ve -- this is your political identity, as you`ve introduced yourself to folks. I get it in a primary. Why are you convinced this message could actually work, if you got the nomination, in a general election?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I think I`m speaking as a member of my generation here, Chuck, but I think it`s true of all Americans. We`re all hungry for a cause right now in America. We`re hungry for purpose and meaning, at a point when the things that used to fill our hunger for purpose, faith, patriotism, family, hard work, these things have disappeared.\par \par \uc2 So I see an opportunity to revive our missing national identity. I think that`s something that Americans hunger for across the political spectrum, answering what it means to be an American today. You ask people my age that question, you get a blank stare in response. I think that is the vacuum at the heart of our national soul. I`m running for president to revive the ideals that actually set the nation into motion. I think it`s going to unite the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting. Your rhetoric can sound uniting, and your answer just now, but then you -- you say the following things. "The trans movement has become a cult." "We need to abandon climate religion in America." "I definitely find the idea of systemic racism revolting."\par \par \uc2 I say this, how do you square those statements with unification?\par \par \uc2 These are divisive times. This is a polarizing time. We`re pretty evenly divided on these cultural issues. How do you unite the country when you`re essentially denigrating the views of half the country?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I don`t think I`m denigrating the views of half the country. I mean, let`s take the touchiest of those subjects right now, on the trans issue. I think that, when a kid says that "I`m born into the wrong body," that "my gender doesn`t match my biological sex," more often that not, that is a case of a mental health disorder. That doesn`t mean you disrespect that person. It means they`re crying out for help.\par \par \uc2 I met with two young women who regret the decisions they made, going through double mastectomies, one a hysterectomy, chemical intervention, now trying to teach kids across the country that, when you`re struggling inside, going through adolescence, yes, that involves some struggle. We live in a cultural moment today where adults are affirming that confusion rather than actually treating them compassionately.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You ever -- you ever talk to...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: That`s cruelty...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You ever talk to parents that have a -- a kid who`s going through this?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I have, actually, yeah, and I think it`s...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah? I mean -- I mean, it`s a -- my point is this...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: It`s a difficult place to be. I acknowledge that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, yeah.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: But what we need to do on both sides here is act with compassion, not really what makes us feel good about ourselves. And I think that`s my main issue across our response...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask you this...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: ... climate. It`s solving the actual underlying issues, rather than what allows you to signal your virtue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. What makes it compassionate, though, to pass a law that denies a parent making their own healthcare decision for their kid? That`s the part of this -- that doesn`t sound very conservative and small-government to me.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, look, there isn`t a state in this union that allows you to smoke an addictive cigarette before the age of 18, that allows you to get a tattoo before the age of 18. That`s a body-altering change that a kid may later regret in life. So I think it is perfectly reasonable to say that, if you`re after 18 years old, you`re free to decide whatever you want to do. That`s what it means to live in a free country. But below the age of 18, I think it`s perfectly legitimate to say that we won`t allow genital mutilation or chemical castration through puberty blockers...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re calling it that...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: ... transition.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... but how do you know it`s that? Again, how do you know -- are you confident that you know that gender is as binary as you`re describing it? Are you confident that it isn`t a spectrum?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I am. I am.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you know this as a scientist?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, there`s -- there are two X chromosomes if you`re a woman and an X and a Y. That means you`re...\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s a lot of scientific research out there...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s a lot of scientific research out there that says gender is a spectrum.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Chuck, I respectfully disagree. Gender dysphoria, for most of our history, all the way through the DSM-V, has been characterized as a mental health disorder. And I don`t think it`s compassionate to affirm that. I think that`s cruelty. When a kid is crying out for help, what they`re asking for is -- you`ve got to ask the question of what else is going wrong at home? What else is going wrong at school? Let`s be compassionate and get to the heart of that, rather than playing this game as though we`re actually changing our medical understanding for the last 100 years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I go back to this. If a parent is dealing with a child that has these -- that may have these issues, trust me, the parent -- the last thing they want to do is consider something like this. But if that is what they think could help their child pursue happiness, or they`re -- not to kill themselves, I -- why take away that option?\par \par \uc2 [09:25:04]\par \par \uc2 Again, why shouldn`t it be up to the parent?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So part of why parents now suddenly feel that way -- let`s ask ourselves that, Chuck -- is we`ve created a culture that teaches parents that they`re being bigoted or tjhat they`re bad people if they don`t actually take those steps. So part of what I think is -- listen, gender dysphoria, for the rare few people who have suffered it, is a condition of suffering. My question is why on earth are we going out of our way to create even more of it?\par \par \uc2 And there`s no doubt that the cultural movement in this country, even education, is creating more gender dysphoria. If it`s a condition of suffering, let`s not create more of it. That`s what we`re doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about the \uc2 Disney\uc2  dust-up...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . On one hand, I assume you agree with pushing back at \uc2 Disney\uc2  the way Governor DeSantis has rhetorically. But is there a point where you think it is too much, to use government to punish business?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Here`s where \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  really lost it here, he`s gone on the wrong path, is he claimed -- and this part actually sounded good to me -- \uc2 Disney\uc2  should have never had crony capitalist lobbying-related privileges in the first place. Here`s the part he doesn`t mention.\par \par \uc2 One of those crony capitalist privileges was, and I think the most relevant one, was codified into law by none other than \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , in 2021. So Florida passed this political anti-discrimination statute, which I applauded at the time, said if you operate Internet companies -- this includes streaming companies like \uc2 Disney\uc2  does -- that you can`t engage in viewpoint discrimination.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s the funny, dirty little secret of that. They wrote into a last- minute exception into that law, for anyone who also operates a theme park more than 25 acres in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: That`s crony capitalism. And so the irony is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who`s now railing against crony capitalism and rolling that back, was the one who actually passed that into law for the case of \uc2 Disney\uc2 . So I think that undermines the credibility of his crusade. I prefer to get to root causes rather than doing political stunts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about the idea of cancel culture, because I feel like the criticism that the right was making of the left two years ago, that it looks like some on the right are embracing cancel culture. I think Bud Light -- I think about the -- the transgender representative in Montana who was basically kicked -- not allowed to speak on the floor. Do you think some of this is going too far?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So, look, I`m an opponent of victimhood culture, cancel culture, you name it. I`ve written a book about this. I do think that the way the culture war ends is not with a bang but with a whimper, where both sides get infected by those same norms, so...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think the -- the right`s been infected by this?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: One of the things I say to conservative audiences across the country is we have to be the party of free speech and open debate. We can`t be the party that says, "I won`t talk to" -- I`m here talking to you on NBC. There`s other candidates in this race that say they won`t talk to NBC News. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is one of them. I go to college campuses other candidates refuse to go.\par \par \uc2 So I think we`ve got to practice what we preach. I`m in this race as a millennial, as a young person who`s lived the American dream, to actually walk the walk when it comes to free speech and open debate. And, yes, I would like to see other Republicans rise to that occasion and do better, starting with the debate stage in our own party this fall.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doesn`t do debates, will you not support him if he`s the nominee?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, I`m not going to let him get away with that. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 TODD: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 What do you mean you`re not going to let him get away with it? He`s -- he can do what he wants to do.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I don`t think the other candidates, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , are going to relish being on that debate stage with me. But I think the way that he`s shown, in 2015, what people gave him credit for was that he was an outsider and a disruptor. I`m the outsider in this race. And I think that, if you want to be part of, like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , an existing establishment that doesn`t want to debate, I think people are hungry for new blood.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What should the party use as leverage to force him to show up on the debate stage?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I think it`s the voters. I think it is my job and it is the job of candidates to tell voters that, if you want someone sitting across the table from \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , if you want someone with the spine to take on the administrative state -- that`s the top of my domestic agenda -- you better darn well not be scared to show up on a debate stage with the new challenger. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did a great job of that in 2015. I`m the outsider in this race.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have indicated you would support a six-week abortion ban if you were governor of a state and all of this. What would you do on a federal level? Would you -- would you create a floor on the federal level?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So I -- I believe in being principled on this, Chuck. I`m unapologetically pro-life and, like many in the pro-life movement, I believe that abortion is a form of murder. Murder, though, is regulated by the states, not by the federal government. I believe in the Constitution. I think Roe was wrongly decided. I`ve said so for a long time. This is a matter for the states, not the federal government. And I -- and I stand on principle there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When does a fetus have constitutional rights?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: So, look, six weeks is -- at the end of life, when you lose brain waves, that`s our -- that`s our moment of death.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does -- where in the Constitution does it say someone has constitutional rights as a fetus?\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: It -- it`s not codified in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: That`s why this is a matter for the states. But I do believe that -- I`m unapologetically pro-life, but one of the areas where we can do better in the pro-life movement is to walk the walk. Provide an easier option for women to get to yes. I support a conversation about adoption, child care, even greater responsibility for men. I think that`s how we turn this issue into being a less divisive one than it`s been.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One of your big applause lines that is a bit of a head-scratcher for me is "Defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So...\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I didn`t say defund the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ; I said shut down the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and replace it with something new.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you want to shut down the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . What are you replacing it with?\par \par \uc2 [09:30:03]\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: I think it`s a new apparatus built from scratch that actually respects the law instead of making it up. The funny thing, Chuck, is if you look at --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  constantly is making up the law?\par \par \uc2 That is a huge charge. They`ve just stopped major fentanyl -- you know, been able to get rid of fentanyl. There`s a lot of work the \uc2 FBI\uc2  does other than responding to complaints from elected officials who don`t like investigations.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well actually if you look over the course of the last 60 years, J. Edgar Hoover what he did to \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , that was an affront. It`s still the J. Edgar Hoover FBI that people walk into down the street here in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 And I personally believe someone who`s running to actually run the executive branch of the government, when you have a bureaucracy whose culture becomes so ossified, every once in a while, you need to turn it over. And I think that, yes, we need federal law enforcement, but that institution has, in a bipartisan way, become so, I think ossified in its own norms, in its own corruption, that we need to rebuild it from scratch and have something new take its place --\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, you`re going to replace the \uc2 FBI\uc2  with a new \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, with a new institution built from scratch to carry out federal law enforcement because the existing \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the people who work there, have worked there for so long that actually they`re going to get -- be getting in their own way. I think that that`s actually important. And by the way, I also support --\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. It does sound like, it does sound like you`re just replacing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: Well, the problem is there`s people who have worked there for decades. And so, what I say is if I`m the U.S. president and I can`t work for the federal government for more than eight years, which I think is a good thing, then none of those bureaucrats reporting into me should either. That`s the point I`m making.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 , you bring some energy to your campaign. We`ll be following it. Thanks for coming on and sharing your views with us.\par \par \uc2 RAMASWAMY: It`s good seeing you, Chuck. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Before we go to break, our "Meet the Press Minute". When President Biden officially announced his reelection campaign this week, his age immediately became an issue. Biden would be 82 on Inauguration Day if he was to win. Trump, by the way, would be 78, second-oldest president ever elected.\par \par \uc2 The average age of a president at the start of their first term is actually 55. Back in 1975, then-President Gerald Ford, who was 62 at the time, mind you, talked about the role age played in his own upcoming election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Here we are looking at `76. And most of the leading candidates are old geysers like me in their 60s. Now, we seem to have misplaced a generation here somewhere. Can -- is the system out of phase? What explains this?\par \par \uc2 GERALD FORD, FORMER PRESIDENT: I think age is a state of mind, obviously a state of health. I`m in the early 60s. I feel as vigorous mentally and physically as I ever have at any time in my life. I don`t think you should rule a person out just because they`re -- my particular age. On the other hand, I don`t think you should rule out a younger person who by experience or intelligence or overall capability is a potential candidate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: That was 48 years ago.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, it`s an extremely co-dependent relationship. Why the president and his predecessor need each other to run their 2024 campaigns.\par \par \uc2 Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is here.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Washington correspondent Yamiche Alcindor; NBC News Capitol Hill correspondent, Ryan Nobles; Lanhee Chen, a fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 ; and former Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche, when we saw the announcement video from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it was not about what he did. It felt like it is about who he`s running against again. And it felt very much almost like -- and they even referenced the first video. He really needs \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be the nominee and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  responded in this Thursday by sort of focusing on him.\par \par \uc2 Are they in a co-dependent relationship?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: There is definitely benefits to having both of those men run against each other, in both of their minds.\par \par \uc2 I also think Democrats are really coming to the realization that the culture wars that we talk so much about Republicans for waging also is something that Democrats should be robustly talking about because their voters are mobilized by that. The first few minutes of that video were January 6th, abortion and then you got into book banning.\par \par \uc2 Having talked to voters, Democrats are very worried about their own freedoms and really want to wrestle that word back from Republicans. So I think you see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in that video making that case and making sure that people say, Okay, you might have done something for the inflation or the bills, but your life is at stake, and I think there`s a little bit of, of fear, frankly, that`s being leaned into there, which we also saw on, on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ryan, what`s interesting to me is on your beat in Congress, you`re already seeing members in each party start to focus on the other. We put up a list here.\par \par \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2 : Politically for us, it`s helpful if former President Trump is front and center.\par \par \uc2 Jon Tester: It`s probably better for Biden to have Trump as the nominee. Then you look at the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Dave Carney, a strategist out of New Hampshire: Hey, Biden is an easy target.\par \par \uc2 Wesley Hunt, a congressman from Texas: Two more years of this, I`m more and more confident every day.\par \par \uc2 It is fascinating that both parties think the other guy is what keeps them --\par \par \uc2 RYAN NOBLES, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: I`ve covered a number of political campaigns where you set the stage as saying, the only person that could beat this candidate is this guy, and the only guy that can beat this candidate is the other guy. And it does seem, in many ways, that that -- that`s what this presidential campaign is shaping up to be.\par \par \uc2 You know, the polls -- you know the poll that we just conducted this week shows pretty clearly that most Americans don`t want to see either of these two guys at the top of the ticket right now.\par \par \uc2 But we`re in a situation where there aren`t clear alternatives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: And I think every time you go back to having this conversation of, if it`s not President Biden, then who is it? And the same thing with President Trump right now. His stranglehold on the Republican base, which I see every single day, particularly in the House of Representatives, is so strong, the idea that there would be some sort of Republican alternative in a serious way right now, you know, just doesn`t appear to be that clear.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire, if Trump weren`t there, would there be more Democratic hand- wringing?\par \par \uc2 FORMER SENATOR CLAIRE MCCASKILL, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Oh, I don`t know. I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 You know, I -- would \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run no matter what? Yes, he would. I mean, inflation`s down 40 percent. He`s created more jobs than almost any president in the history of our country. He had -- got the infrastructure bill through that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  kept talking about for weeks and weeks on end.\par \par \uc2 So, he has accomplishments. And I think he enjoys the job. And I think he would run no matter what, no question.\par \par \uc2 But this is the other thing, Chuck, that people forget. This is never a referendum. Presidential elections, by the time they run around, is always, well, who do you like slightly better than the two you don`t like?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: So it is a binary choice. And even if it`s not Trump, extremism is on the ballot for the Republican Party this time, whether they like it or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s a choice, not a referendum. It`s a choice, not a referendum. Every reelect campaign I`ve covered always love to say that.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And most of the time this century, Claire`s right. It`s -- that has been the case.\par \par \uc2 Lanhee, Trump`s focus on Biden right now. I don`t sense that the Republican primary electorate is that interested in having a primary, do you?\par \par \uc2 LANHEE CHEN, FELLOW, \uc2 HOOVER INSTITUTION\uc2 : It doesn`t feel that way right now. It`s funny because we`re pretty late in the primary cycle. But it still feels early.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was --\par \par \uc2 CHEN: People will say -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, it`s early. No, it`s not. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHEN: I mean, so the reality of it is, I think the way this field -- the way that this contest is shaping up, it`s pretty clear people realize there`s going to be one ticket against Trump.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:08]\par \par \uc2 Trump will be there at the end of the day. The question is who the other person`s going to be.\par \par \uc2 And you see that in the interchanges, for example, between Governor Haley and Governor DeSantis. You`re seeing a lot of people now starting to go after \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  because they perceive -- listen, there is an opportunity to be that other voice against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when we get to early next year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess. But, boy, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , you now have Republicans almost universally now criticizing his handling of the \uc2 Disney\uc2  thing.\par \par \uc2 Let me play an array of Republicans critiquing him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I don`t think the idea of building a prison next to a place that you bring your family`s the best idea.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): I do worry that if this happens too many times, businesses that are thinking about coming to Florida are saying, maybe we don`t want to go there.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR NIKKI HALEY (R-SC), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: If \uc2 Disney\uc2  would like to move their hundreds of thousands of jobs to South Carolina and bring the billions of dollars with them, I`ll let them know I`ll be happy to meet them.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR), PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: I don`t agree how \uc2 Disney\uc2  has handled things, but you don`t use the heavy hand of government to punish a business.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ryan, the number two candidate is getting the pile-on right now, not the frontrunner.\par \par \uc2 NOBLES: Yeah, it`s interesting. I`ve talked to some DeSantis advisers about this, and there is a recognition about how he`s being pillared right now by not just Republicans, but Democrats as well. And what they keep saying is, just wait for him to get in the race.\par \par \uc2 You know, he`s obviously amassing a huge war chest right now, both within his super PAC, and he has the ability to raise a lot of money as a candidate himself. And so their -- you know, their feeling is, until he`s actually a candidate, we really shouldn`t be overstating the difficulty that he`s having in this early part of the campaign.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yamiche, there was a former Florida governor who had so much money that was going to be a huge factor in 2015. How`d that work out?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: With a famous last name, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: With a famous name.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: That didn`t work out very well.\par \par \uc2 And I -- and when you look at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , and I`ve talked to some conservatives about this, they say going to war with \uc2 Disney\uc2 , not only does it look petty and look small, but you`re also going after a huge employer of blue collar people who are going to be your base voters.\par \par \uc2 So if \uc2 Disney\uc2  starts telling their employees, hey, this is a problem. And here`s the person who`s making your life hard, it`s going to be hard for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is this over before it begins, Lanhee? I know nobody wants to say that, but boy, it smells rough.\par \par \uc2 CHEN: I think a lot of this does -- I mean, it`s not about him getting in the race. The question is these primary contests are always sort of battles of attrition, infrastructure building. All that stuff that doesn`t get covered, that`s what matters.\par \par \uc2 And so, he`s got the money to be able to compete in a way that I think people -- people need to pay attention still. I mean, look, the last couple weeks have been uneven. There`s no question about it. But that is not what I pay attention to.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire, Barack Obama was in this position against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and they were going, oh, maybe he`s not going to get there. Do you see any similarities, or do you think DeSantis is in a different spot?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Oh, I think -- you cannot compare DeSantis to people who have been tested on a national stage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and Barack Obama. This guy`s not ready. He declared war, and he doesn`t even know what victory is, against Mickey Mouse. Give me a break.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mickey Mouse.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the culture wars can be used to motivate the base of a political party, but our latest NBC News poll reveals why they may be an important test for candidates in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:46:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 As we head into 2024, the culture wars are dominating the conversation right now, as we continue to debate what kind of country we should become, and how quickly that change should happen.\par \par \uc2 Numbers from our most recent NBC News poll suggest the nation is pretty open to social change. And that possibly could give Democrats an advantage with some of these cultural issues among the national electorate.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you what I`m talking about here. Do we need to do more to increase social justice? It`s a pretty popular expression of support overall, 70 percent of Americans agree with that.\par \par \uc2 And, believe it or not, there`re not a ton of differences by party. 91 percent of Democrats agree with it. But 67 percent of independents, looks like the country as a whole. Half of Republicans believe we need to do more to increase social justice.\par \par \uc2 When you start to ask a little bit more, you start to see some diversions by party. Should we be more tolerant and accepting of the LGBTQ community? Overall, that`s 61 percent, slightly less than the social justice number.\par \par \uc2 And here, you see some divergence, right? Democrats and Independents both have majorities who believe we should be more tolerant with this community. Republicans, however, an outlier with just 38 percent, a lot of that driven by the trans issue of late.\par \par \uc2 And in fact, on the issue of trans people, have we gone too far in accepting trans people? A plurality of the country agree with that statement, that we`ve gone too far, 48 percent. Start to look at it by party, you see a big disparity that goes the other way. Just 19 percent of Democrats believe that, but independents, looks like the nation overall, nearly 80 percent of Republicans believe we have gone too far on that.\par \par \uc2 But I want to give you a historical nugget here to keep something in mind, and it`s the historical trend line of the legalization of same-sex marriage. Less than 20 years ago, it was two to one opposition to same-sex marriage. And in less than 20 years, those numbers completely flipped.\par \par \uc2 And something in our poll we noticed, if you knew somebody who was gay 20 years ago, you were more likely to support-same sex marriage. If you know somebody who is trans, you`re more likely to support an openness to accepting on that as well.\par \par \uc2 Up next, grieving for his father and trying to honor his legacy. Luke Russert has written a new book on how he dealt with the sudden loss of his dad.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 At a crowded \uc2 Orioles\uc2  game at Camden Yards, Luke Russert remembers his dad, Tim, pointing to a hot dog stand, saying, buddy, if we`re ever separated, look for me there.\par \par \uc2 When Tim died at 58 of a heart attack in 2008, Luke followed his father to NBC News, becoming a Capitol Hill correspondent here.\par \par \uc2 But after eight years of working in television, Luke realized he didn`t have a good answer to the question of why he was chasing his father`s legacy. He set off on more than three years of travel to six continents, searching for his father and for himself.\par \par \uc2 And Luke Russert, author of "Look for Me There: Grieving My Father, Finding Myself" joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Luke, welcome back to "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 LUKE RUSSERT, AUTHOR, "LOOK FOR ME THERE": It`s such an honor, Chuck. Such an honor. I still get chills every time I see the logo and hear the music. It`s -- it`s pretty special.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I get chills introducing you right now, I`ll be honest.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You were 22 when your dad died.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it seems that you`re looking back now and realize you didn`t process it then.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Talk about it.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Yeah. Well, he died and it was such a whirlwind of emotions. And we came back to Washington. My mother and I were actually in Italy when it happened, which turned out to be a little bit of a blessing because we had one day to ourselves together as a family, as mother and son, to begin to process what had happened.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He was with you two days earlier, right?\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Two days before he passed away, at the Vatican, which is so fitting considering my father`s Catholic upbringing.\par \par \uc2 But when we came back, we thought that people would mourn him. We had no idea there was going to be such a national outpouring of grief. He had thousands of people at his wake.\par \par \uc2 And I ended up giving a eulogy at Holy Trinity Catholic Church here in D.C., and then eventually the Kennedy Center. And a lot of people saw that, and that sort of catapulted me into a very public, forward-facing space. But I was still trying to sort of reconcile, what should I do? And I think in the beginning --\par \par \uc2 TODD: What were you, weeks from graduating college, right?\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Three weeks out.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- just finished Boston College.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: I had graduated college, and about three weeks later I`m looking out over a set of pews at Barack Obama, \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , Ethel Kennedy, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It was -- it was a whirlwind.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s still surreal to think about that day.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: It is still surreal. And I look back on that, and I think about, man, 22-year-old Luke, you had so much courage, you had so much poise. But you never really dealt with, you lost your dad. You lost your best friend.\par \par \uc2 And you went into this immediately public-facing role because you were trying to preserve his legacy the best you knew, and you were trying to bring comfort to people. But it ultimately did not bring comfort to you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You wrote something that I thought was pretty brave about yourself. You write this: It`s apparent that I`ve spent so much time honoring his legacy that I`ve never truly accepted his death. Worse, by honoring that legacy, I`ve failed to forge my own life.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a -- that`s a pretty brave thing to write publicly, buddy.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: One of the things that I`ve found going through this process was I had written out all these journals when I was traveling around the world, and I didn`t know really what was in them. But when I took the time to read them, I realized I was looking for something.\par \par \uc2 And what I was looking for on this journey was, who am I, independent of my parents? Who am I, independent of my hometown? Who am I, independent of the ways of Washington?\par \par \uc2 And I never was able to truly figure that out because I was so attached to dad. He was my guiding light. He`s gone.\par \par \uc2 I try to stay in that lane, but I never really ask those difficult questions of, who are you? What do you want to do? Because it was greatly uncomfortable. And to ask those questions, I would have to deal with the fact that he was really gone, which was something that I just tried to conveniently power through, and white-knuckle, and not really think about too much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, of all people, it`s \uc2 John Boehner\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- that sort of, what did he, flip a switch in your head? What did he say to you that made you say, wait a minute, I got to rethink this?\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: I was turning 30, I had lost a good friend of mine at 27, and my father died at 58. So I saw the light at the end of the tunnel. Friends were getting married, heck, getting mortgages. So I kept thinking about it, is this everything that`s going to happen with me in my life? Is this really what I want?\par \par \uc2 I was having anxiety around the position. There were so many days where I would white-knuckle it through on television, and I wouldn`t even know why. Why is that? Why am I feeling that way? Maybe I should take some time away.\par \par \uc2 And out of nowhere, \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 , the House speaker at the time, who I covered rather aggressively, says, I need to talk to you. And I thought he was going to be angry about coverage.\par \par \uc2 And he takes me into his office and he goes, what are you doing here? I said, well, you invited me into your office. What do you mean, what am I doing here?\par \par \uc2 He goes, no, what are you doing here on Capitol Hill? You`ve been here eight years, you spent all your 20s here. I`m the Speaker of the House. I want to tell you something.\par \par \uc2 There is a world outside of Washington. There is a world outside of this place, the Capitol. Time is a flat circle here. You could end up 50, 60 years old, and not even know that time has gone by.\par \par \uc2 Make sure this is what you want to do, and go out and do something else if you want. It`s okay. Go learn how to do something.\par \par \uc2 And part of it I think was that Washington can be fleeting, and the connections that you make with people can be purely political. Go out there and really understand human beings.\par \par \uc2 And it was a very impactful statement, because Boehner grew up one of 12 children, Catholic upbringing in Cincinnati, very similar to my father. Rust Belt, worked his way through college.\par \par \uc2 So it didn`t fall on deaf ears at all. I was -- I was really shaken up by it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So I think anybody that`s lost somebody close to them, I think this is a book that can help them. You poured yourself out in this. Not easy to write about your mom. Your mom`s still here, doing great. I mean, Maureen Orth is a force of nature. I love her to death. I know you do, too.\par \par \uc2 How`s this changed things for you two?\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: When one parent dies, your relationship with the surviving one, for the children or the child, always changes. And in my case, it was this mix of, you`re now the sort of man of the house, there are all these logistical things to deal with that dad used to always take care of. But then sometimes it was, well, you`re my son. Just do it.\par \par \uc2 So it was trying to navigate this pattern of, where really are you in the structure? And also as a mom, she was more sort of the disciplinarian where my dad was the sort of, you know, do a little good cop/bad cop. He was sort of the easier guy to get along with at some points.\par \par \uc2 But then I realized when I started traveling -- because my mother was a Peace Corps volunteer at a very young age, when women, when they graduated college, really had limited opportunities. It was, be a paralegal or a teacher.\par \par \uc2 And she said, I want to do something else. So she goes to Colombia, goes to the Peace Corps. And it`s through travel and measuring herself against the world that she really knows who she is.\par \par \uc2 And she had wanted me to do that my entire life. I had never really traveled. And it was not until I did that that I understood her. She had to fight so hard for everything she got, and that is why she demanded a lot out of me.\par \par \uc2 She said, you got to be tough, and you`ve got to show yourself.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Feel like you`ve found yourself?\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: I think we`re always a work in progress. But as it pertains to losing my father, I`m at peace with that. I`ve accepted it, and I`m so much better for it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Where are you headed next?\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: I like storytelling, so hopefully this book does well and that opens up some opportunities in that area.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, man. Nice to see you.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Thank you so much for having me, Chuck. It`s such an honor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Honor`s mine, my brother (ph). The honor is mine.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230430ej4u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc9}{\*\bkmkend toc9}\b \uc2 How More Women Are Boldly Changing The Conversation About Menopause And Money\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: There`s been an empowering shift in how women are talking about menopause, no longer taboo, many are having candid conversations about what they want and need, and big business is listening. Here`s Kristen Dahlgren.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Menopause is more than hot flashes and night sweats. A new study shows a big financial impact too, some $1.8 billion in lost work every year, $26 billion when you add in medical expenses.\par \par \uc2 DR. STEPHANIE FAUBION, DIRECTOR, MAYO CLINIC WOMEN`S HEALTH CENTER: That`s not even counting the lost opportunities women might have because they didn`t take a promotion or maybe they took a lesser job.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: 13 percent of women reported an adverse work outcome due to menopause. 11 percent missed at least one day of work, the average missed three days per year.\par \par \uc2 Do we need to rethink how we`re thinking about menopause in the workplace?\par \par \uc2 FAUBION: I think we`re where we were in the 1970s with pregnancy and lactation in the workplace. Now, we need to be having these conversations about how menopause symptoms impact women. We need to educate managers and supervisors.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: It took a while for Lorraine Grace White to realize what she was feeling was menopause.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I had migraines every day so intense that I could -- I could hardly focus and concentrate. And then there was the mood swings.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: She found Dr. Makeba Williams, an OBGYN at \uc2 Washington University in St. Louis\uc2 , one of a growing number of physicians focused on individualized menopause care.\par \par \uc2 The number of certified menopause practitioners has increased 70 percent in the past 10 years with the potential to almost double by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAKEBA WILLIAMS, OBGYN, WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY IN ST. LOUIS: The average age of menopause in this country is 51 which means that a woman will spend a half to a third of her life menopausal. This notion that I hear all too often that women should just grin and bear it does everyone a disservice.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: And symptoms can be even more severe for African-American women.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: An African-American woman can expect to have these symptoms for 10 years. And unfortunately, she is half as likely to be treated as a White woman who will suffer from these symptoms only about six years.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Williams says there`s hope even hormone therapy once considered too risky now seen as a safe option for many. And for those who can`t take estrogen like women who have had breast cancer, new treatments are being developed. For Lorraine, seeking help has made all the difference.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are a different generation of women and we have things to do. I feel amazing and I can`t wait for the next chapter.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Chapters being rewritten for millions as attitudes toward what was once simply called the change are changing.\par \par \uc2 Kristen Dahlgren NBC News, St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next tonight, songs of survival. The beautiful instruments and the stories to save them.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230428ej4q000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc10}{\*\bkmkend toc10}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for April 23, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Eugene Robinson \par \uc2 9,977 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:18]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST (voice over): This Sunday, the Biden-Trump rematch. As President Biden prepares to announce his re-election bid this week, our new NBC News poll finds voters don`t want him to run again, including half of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: We`ve made a lot of progress, and we`ve just got to keep it going.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And our poll also shows voters don`t want to see former President Trump run again.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: There`s no crime. I got indicted for no crime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And yet he has huge support within the GOP even after his arrest, and he`s the clear front-runner. Is America ready for a Biden-Trump sequel?\par \par \uc2 I`ll talk to New Hampshire`s Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who`s considering his own presidential run in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Plus, triggered.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GILLIS, FATHER OF KAYLIN GILLIS: For this man to sit on his porch and fire at a car with no threat is just -- angers me so badly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The wrong driveway, the wrong doorbell and the wrong car, simple mistakes that all ended in Americans being shot.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mr. Yarl is fighting for his life.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In a nation full of guns and growing distrust, what will it take to make knocking on a stranger`s door feel safe again?\par \par \uc2 And, ethics scandal, after reports revealing Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  failed to disclose real estate transactions from a wealthy Republican donor, Senate Democrats call on the chief justice to testify.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D) ILLINOIS: They have to do something.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why does the highest court in the land have the lowest ethical standards? I`ll talk to the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  chairman, \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for insight and analysis are Politico Playbook co-author Rachael Bade; \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  columnist Eugene Robinson; Amy Walter, editor in chief of the Cook Political Report; and Marc Short, former chief of staff to Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history. This is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 Americans are seething at their political leaders and at each other. That`s according to a brand-new NBC News poll we are releasing right now. They`re dissatisfied with what seems to be inevitable, a 2024 rematch between \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As Biden prepares to formally announce his campaign for a second term early this week, just one in four Americans believe he should run for re- election. Seventy percent say he shouldn`t run. By the way, only 35 percent believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should run again. Sixty percent say no. Only 5 percent of Americans actually want both of these gentlemen to run.\par \par \uc2 And let me show you even more of this poll. Fifty-three percent of 2020 Biden voters say he shouldn`t run; 64 percent of Democrats who voted for Sanders or Warren in the 2020 primaries think he shouldn`t run. And 76 percent of voters under 35 think he shouldn`t run.\par \par \uc2 And what`s the top concern for that? We asked voters. We didn`t prompt them. We said, "Why don`t you think he should run?" Well, these were the responses. It almost is all about his age and his ability to do the job. Again, these are among Democratic primary voters.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the president`s job rating is not in a good place when you`re seeking re-election, sitting at just 41 percent, driven by the 58 percent of Americans who disapprove of how he is handling the economy right now. Just 30 percent of independents approve of the job Biden is doing overall.\par \par \uc2 But as Biden is fond of saying, he is not to be judged by the Almighty, but judge him by the alternative. And guess what? The alternative is even more unpopular among the American public. Let me show you here. Here`s the feelings about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Just one in three Americans have positive feelings about him. A majority have negative feelings, sitting at 53 percent.\par \par \uc2 Among voters overall, a majority believe that the charges in New York are - - are serious enough because he should be held to the same standard as anyone else. Forty-three percent believe he`s being unfairly targeted. And that`s approximately the number that seems to stick behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on almost anything. And among Republican voters, it appears that the New York indictment only galvanized his support. We asked them whether this -- these charges were a politically motivated attack and it means they`ve got to stand behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or do these charges indicate that he`s got a lot of distractions in his way and that it`s important to nominate somebody else who could focus on beating \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 Well, as you can see here, it`s no contest. Nearly 70 percent believe they`ve got to rally around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  right now because of these indictments. Only one in four Republican voters believe it is time to move on. And that translates into the Republican primary matchup here, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  with 46 percent.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , that`s a healthy number for somebody that`s never run for president before. It`s about the same spot that Barack Obama started in against \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  back in 2007.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:03]\par \par \uc2 And when you combine first and second choice here, you see that Trump and DeSantis could very well end up in a neck-and-neck race here, but that`s the point. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  seems to be as strong, if not stronger, with the groups that matter the most when it comes to winning a Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 Now, there`s one more major takeaway from this poll. As the Supreme Court keeps the abortion pill Mifepristone available for now, putting a stay on the Texas ruling which revoked \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for the drug, abortion is energizing voters. Nearly 60 percent, as you can see here, of voters want abortion access to remain legal.\par \par \uc2 And among -- ready for this? Among the 43 percent who told us that abortion is the single issue that drives them to the polls, they believe it should be legal by a 65/34 percent margin. This is another sign that the energy is on the Democratic side of this issue after Dobbs. When Roe v. Wade was law of the land, single-issue abortion voters almost universally leaned Republican.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-twenty-four Republican hopefuls met in their first Iowa cattle call last night, courting the caucus`s most important demographic, religious voters. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t appear at the event. He sent a video claiming credit for the Supreme Court justices he appointed, while his former vice president took \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on, on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I faced down vile attacks to confirm our three great Supreme Court justices, \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I do think it`s more likely that this issue is resolved at the state level, but I don`t agree with the former president who says this is a states-only issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is the Republican governor of New Hampshire. It`s \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who himself is considering a run for president.\par \par \uc2 Governor Sununu, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Thanks. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, the last time you were on, and on many a program, you have made one declaration almost universal about 2024. Let me play a montage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2 , (R) NEW HAMPSHIRE: Look, Donald trump is not going to be the nominee, right? We`re just moving on as a party, as a country.\par \par \uc2 He`s not going to be the nominee. That`s just not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 Here`s the good news.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I`m going to give you the good news. Ready?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 I love you. I`m a big fan.\par \par \uc2 BILL MAHER, HOST, "REAL TIME WITH BILL MAHER": Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: But you`re dead wrong. He`s not going to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All those quotes were from February and March, none of them from April. You`ve seen the numbers. You`ve seen this shift. You`ve seen the energy. We`ve watched Governor DeSantis. What say you now?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah. There`s definitely a shift, but I still don`t think he`s necessarily going to be the nominee.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think your poll is spot-on in all these areas, by the way. I think that`s actually a great poll, and I hope folks listen to it.\par \par \uc2 I`ll say this. Republicans are rallying; they`re supporting former President Trump over these indictments, right? And there`s a lot of support there. Now, does it actually translate into a vote? We will see.\par \par \uc2 I mean, most folks don`t decide who they`re voting for until about three weeks before the election. So there`s a lot of politics to play out. There`s -- not even a single debate has been had. Other candidates are going to get in the race. So I just think it`s so far away.\par \par \uc2 And at the end of the day, we want a winner, right? Republicans want someone who can win in November of `24. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is a loser. He has not just lost once. He lost us our House seats in 2018. He lost everything in `20. We should have 54 U.S. senators right now. We don`t because of his message. So \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is positioning himself to be a four-time loser in 2024. We need candidates that can win.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t the problem, though, that a majority of Republicans don`t believe what you just said, that he lost in 2020? Because it -- the loser message should work, unless you don`t believe he lost?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, it`s not just 2020, right? We got crushed in 2022. We should have 54 \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seats. We don`t, because he is part of that message. We lost in 2018.\par \par \uc2 So it`s not just about whether he won or lost in `20, which he did, of course. But it`s really about -- we can complain about things or we can make sure that -- you know, you can`t govern if you don`t win. And again, so he drags that ticket down. I think that reality is going to really come to bear through the primary process.\par \par \uc2 So I understand folks are supporting him. They think that a lot of the stuff with the D.A. is political, which I believe it is, too, by the way. It`s creating a lot of sympathy. He`s playing the victim card, right?\par \par \uc2 Believe it or not, former President Trump is now playing the victim card, and he`s making some headway with it. But at the end of the day, it has to turn into votes.\par \par \uc2 Look, I talk all the time about, you know, we want a fighter, right? Republicans want someone that`s going to fight for them. But we also want a fighter that can win. He -- he said he was going to go to Washington and drain the swamp. He didn`t do it. He said he was going to build a wall and secure things. He didn`t do it. He said he was going to give us health care reform and be fiscally disciplined, not add $8 trillion -- so -- to the debt. He didn`t do any of those things. And -- and so we want fighters that can actually win and take accountability.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I will put -- I will bring up the good news for you and your potential candidacy.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 The Republican voters who don`t want Trump to run look like voters that would be inclined to support you. They look like New Hampshire Republicans, if you will. They`re more educated, wealthier. They`re more likely to identify as moderates. But here`s the problem. They only represent 26 percent of all Republicans. Do you -- how do you appeal to that Trump supporter who`s a bit motivated by grievance?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Sure. Well, look, I`m -- I`m a very principled free-market conservative, right? When you look at the second amendment, we`re the best in the country, with personal freedoms. We need to remind ourselves as Republicans what we`re really good at, limited government, local control, low taxes, individual responsibility. That`s something that everybody can rally around, and frankly, in New Hampshire, as we say, we`re wicked good at. We`re wicked -- we`re wicked good at that.\par \par \uc2 So when you look at the candidates that are on the ground and results- driven, I`ll put my record of success behind -- against almost any other candidate that would potentially step on the stage. And so results-driven winners is really what is going to drive the voter. We`re still a long way away, again, from what the -- the issues of the voter are going to be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But if you pulled this off, if you became the nominee, you would -- you would defy a conventional wisdom that has been true now for, frankly, for 50 years, and that is, if you`re an abortion rights supporter, you don`t have much of a chance to win the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 I want to show you something here. Among Republican voters, 68 percent believe abortion should be illegal most of the time. Only 28 percent believe there should be legal access to it. How do you convince those that would -- that believe abortion, there shouldn`t be much access to abortion, to support your candidacy?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah, so that`s where I`ll really challenge it. The next generation of Republican, right -- if you look at the polls from about 45 and under, when you look at their priorities, you know, banning abortion is not one of their priorities. It`s not. You know, they care about all these other kind of things in a conservative, fiscally conservative way. They want, kind of, that new generation of Republican to step up. It`s not, kind of, the old school way.\par \par \uc2 And so, kind of, talking about those issues that are important to them, not just us, and how we`re traditional, if we stay in our traditional lanes, we`re going to lose. There`s no doubt about it. So whether it`s me or another candidate that really connects with folks, that kind of inspires that next generation of Republican to get into that voting booth, that`s what`s going to be successful.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think at this point, considering the chaos of the states and the confusion on abortion access, that the federal government needs to come up with a minimum access number, a floor for abortion access?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Look, every time Republicans start talking about abortion, we`re losing. We are. Because it is a state`s issue. That`s effectively what Dobbs has allowed to happen...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Should it be, though?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: When it is a state issue, the voters have direct accountability.\par \par \uc2 I`m sorry?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, it doesn`t have to be. Congress can step in here and -- and...\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: They could, and they shouldn`t.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: In your opinion, do you think it should be a state issue, or do you think it should be standardized in the federal level?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No. It should be -- it`s a state issue. And every time a Republican talks about banning this, or this many weeks here or there, we are losing. We are.\par \par \uc2 Let me -- every Republican potential candidate, take a piece of paper and write this down. It`s a state issue. Let the voters in the states figure it out. We shouldn`t be talking about it on a national level. We`re moving on. And that`s it. That`s the new way.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think 50 years of precedent should have been broken...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: ... with Roe v. Wade. I don`t -- I mean, this Mifepristone stuff, 20 years of precedent, and one judge that no one even knows the name of is going to try to ban all that, that sends -- that sends a lot of insecurity through the system in terms of our messaging as Republicans. Let`s get back to what we`re -- we do best, limited government, local control, a little bit of that "Live free or die thing" we have here in New Hampshire. That is a record of -- a record to actually cross the finish line and have winners in November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about a -- a larger societal problem. These stories of -- of gun -- of people pulling guns on everyday mistakes, knocking on -- pulling into a driveway, knocking on somebody`s door. You have said you believe this gun violence issue is a mental health problem. How do we get to a point where we don`t think we have to pull a gun in order to answer the door or have a gun in order to knock on some neighbor`s door?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah, look, those are absolute tragedies. And, unfortunately, they`re happening more often than we`d like to see. The mass shootings are real. They`re happening at an increased rate.\par \par \uc2 But if you -- if the idea is, well, we should just pass more laws -- if that was the answer, then why didn`t Democrats, when they ran the House, they controlled the Senate, they controlled the presidency over the last two years, why didn`t they do anything? Because they know it`s not about passing more laws. They know that places like Chicago that have the most -- some of the most restrictive gun laws in the country still have some of the -- the worst crime in the -- in, frankly, the most irresponsibility with the mass shootings in all of this.\par \par \uc2 I`m a believer that you`ve got to get to the crux of the issue, right? I`m the governor of one of the safest states in the country, but we also have some of the most flexible pro-second-amendment rules and laws in the country. Because we take those -- those things very responsibly.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 We harden schools. We deal with mental health, especially in kids. We go after the core of the issue, instead of saying, "Well, we should just pass more laws." And if that -- if it were that easy, people would do it, but it`s not. If it were that easy, even Democrats would do it. They didn`t.\par \par \uc2 So stop trying to take these tragedies, these human tragedies, and try to make political fodder out of them. It`s a real losing...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: ... on the Democrats` part.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, Governor, do you accept -- do you accept the premise that, frankly, New Hampshire is not like most of America?\par \par \uc2 And I say this -- it`s a little more rural. It`s a little more -- a smaller state, a smaller population. It`s a different situation in urban and suburban America. And so why do you think -- why do you think that -- that what works in New Hampshire is going to work in urban America?\par \par \uc2 And let me ask this. All we`ve done over the last 20 years is loosen gun laws in this country, and where has it gotten us?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, I would say that, in urban -- in urban environments, they`ve tightened their gun laws, right? They`re trying these red flag laws. Look at the red flag laws in Chicago, just as an example. Over the past two or three years you`ve had nearly 2,000 deaths by -- by firearms and murders. I think the red flag laws have been actually implemented less than a dozen times, right? So as they try these new things, as they try these more restrictive laws in those urban areas that you`re talking about, it`s not working, right? So I guess I would...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You just advocated for a red flag law. I mean...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: ... New Hampshire is working. What those areas are doing doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, doesn`t that tell you that maybe a red flag law isn`t the answer?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, that`s right. That`s right. More laws aren`t the answer. More responsibility...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, no one said more laws, just those red flag laws.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: ... mental health, actually having -- I`m sorry?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, no, I`m just saying the way these red...\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Securing your streets.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah...\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: ... holding people accountable, enough of this bail reform nonsense; holding folks accountable so they don`t feel like they can get away with everything. There`s a lot of that going -- going on across the country in a whole variety of ways, specifically in those urban areas you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is your timeline on deciding whether or not to run for president?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Probably by -- by lunch, something like that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you being -- no, I mean...\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, look...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When -- let me ask you this.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think you have to make a decision by the Fourth of July, publicly?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yes. I think everybody will have to make a decision by Fourth of July, get their exploratory committees. I think there`s a lot of new candidates that are -- that are stepping up saying, "Hey, wait, there`s lanes here; there`s a lot of opportunity here; there`s a lot of, whether it`s money to get your exploratory stuff done." A lot of folks want to get on that stage. I think the thresholds for the debate are going to be very low to start, in terms of polling numbers and donors. So I think you`re going to have a very crowded stage early on, potentially.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The last month of -- of issues that have impacted the Republican Party, has that made you more inclined to run or less?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Neither. It really is -- it`s about family. It`s about what`s best -- you know, I still have a state to run. I`ve got a 24/7 job as governor, so I`ve got to make sure we`re maintaining that. What we can bring to the table, not overcrowding the field, and where I can be most effective for my party, not just for \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 . I want Republicans to win as a team in November of `24. And if I can be effective in that as a candidate or not, well, that will help make the decision.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , Republican from New Hampshire. Governor, we await your decision and we expect you to come here and tell us first.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: You bet, buddy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, an overwhelming majority of Americans, including half of Democrats, do not want to see President Biden seek a second term. I`m going to ask the number two Democrat in the Senate, \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 , for his reaction to those poll numbers, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:20:29]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It is not an easy time to be a Senate Democrat right now. They are stuck defending an unpopular president. More than half of Democrats say Biden should not run again, according to our new poll. They have a precarious Senate majority, as Senator Dianne Feinstein`s has left some judicial nominees in limbo. And now, in the wake of reports that Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  may have violated the law, never disclosing a real estate deal and expense-paid luxury trips from billionaire donor Harlan Crow, Judiciary Committee Chairman \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  has asked the chief justice of the Supreme Court, and head of the judiciary branch of government, \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , to appear before his committee, citing a, quote, "steady stream of revelations regarding justices falling short of the ethical standards expected of other federal judges."\par \par \uc2 And Democratic Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Senator Durbin, welcome back to "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Good to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I want to start with this issue involving trying to create formal ethics standards for Supreme Court justices. You invited Chief Justice Roberts. He appears to have responded by saying "Thank you. Go ask the judicial reform folks for somebody."\par \par \uc2 Have you -- do you believe he has turned your invitation down? Some of us are debating whether that response is an official decline of your invite?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I don`t view that as an official response. And we`re still waiting for the chief justice to answer my invitation. I think that`s the first step that ought to be taken, that the Supreme Court itself, with its leader, the chief justice, make it clear that they`re going to bring reform, when it comes to ethics, to the court, and spell out what they`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 I asked the chief justice 10 years ago the very question we`re asking today, "What is the court going to do to bring its standards of ethics up to the level even of other courts in this country?"\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to talk about the specifics of Justice Thomas here and ask you this question. The sale of Thomas`s mother`s house and the adjacent lots without disclosure on that annual financial filing, would it be a violation of law if he were just on the circuit?\par \par \uc2 Because, in Section 5 of the U.S. Code, it reads, "It shall be unlawful for any person to knowingly and willfully falsify any information that such person is required to report under Section 102. Fail to file or report any information that such person is required to report."\par \par \uc2 Point is, if this were a Judge Thomas, this is perhaps a crime. Now, it is not clear this would apply to Justice Thomas. Is that your understanding?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: That`s exactly the point, Chuck. If this were another judge, if this were a member of Congress, and it happened that some Texas billionaire came in and said, "I`m going to buy your mom`s house and the houses nearby and own this so she doesn`t have to pay rent or a mortgage payment," you`d say to yourself, "Well, that`s obvious. That`s the sort of thing that needs to be disclosed. That`s a clear indication of a conflict of interest. Is there a question that should be raised?"\par \par \uc2 So, yes, there`s no question in my mind that the Supreme Court has exempted itself from standards that apply to the executive and legislative branch and even to other judges.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what could -- I mean, look, we have a separation of powers issue. And we could talk about that, right? The Supreme Court would have to decide whether a law Congress passed to apply to the justices pass that test of separation of powers. That`s a tough test to pass.\par \par \uc2 What would legislation look like that would enforce an ethical code of conduct on justices?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, the code of conduct would look an awful lot like the code that applies to the rest of federal government and other judges and basically would have disclosures, timely disclosures of transactions like this purchase of the justice`s mother`s home. It would also give standards for recusal so that, if there`s going to be conflict before the court and recusal, it be explained publicly, and investigations of questions that are raised.\par \par \uc2 It -- it is the same standard that`s being used across the board, code of conduct, ethics laws, applied to the court. Why this Supreme Court, these nine justices, believe they are exempt from the basic standards of disclosure, I cannot explain. And I think the chief justice should appear before our committee and explain something or explain the changes that he`s going to make.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why not invite Justice Thomas?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I think I know what would happen to that invitation. It would be ignored. It is far better, from my point of view, to have the chief justice there.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:01]\par \par \uc2 Listen, this is \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 ` court. We are dealing with a situation where history will remember it as such. I -- I was there when he went through his confirmation hearing. He is an articulate, well-schooled man when it comes to presenting his point of view. I`m sure he`ll do well before the committee.\par \par \uc2 But history is going to judge the Roberts court by his decision as to reform. And I think this is an invitation on May 2nd for him to present it to the American people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move to some politics here. A super-majority in our poll, Senator Durbin, 70 percent -- you can`t get 70 percent of Americans to agree on much, but 70 percent of Americans don`t think President Biden should seek a second term. Does that give you cause for concern, especially when you look at the numbers inside the Democratic coalition? Particularly younger voters are very skeptical of whether he should run again.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: It`s about 19 months before there`s an election. Most people make their decision on a candidate 19 days before the election. So we are far in advance of any decision-making.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what we do know. This president has shown extraordinary leadership. On the foreign policy side, he has mobilized \uc2 NATO\uc2  and expanded \uc2 NATO\uc2  to respond to a threat by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  on Ukraine and beyond. He has -- he is moving forward when it comes to a relationship with China which is much better for the American economy, I think, in the short term, and I think establishes long-term standards.\par \par \uc2 Then when you look at the domestic front, Governor Sununu doesn`t want to talk about it, but let me tell you, there is a re-alignment of American voters over this issue of choice and women`s reproductive rights. The Republican Party is on the wrong side of history by a long shot. And we`re seeing this realignment in elections.\par \par \uc2 In the recent mayoral election in the city of Chicago several weeks ago, one of the major questions, "What`s your position on choice?" That would not have been a question raised in most mayoral contests before it.\par \par \uc2 We also have the kind of leadership when it comes to climate change, which the American people are wanting. And we have a position that actually makes sense when it comes to gun safety. I listened to Governor Sununu, and it`s mindless to think that we have the kind of death rate going on -- it`s the leading cause of death, gun violence, the leading cause of death of young people under the age of 21 in America. For goodness sakes, doesn`t that tell the story? He says "We want to have limited government." Well, I can just tell you. People want to be safe from assault weapons and the kind of profligate gun ownership that is not responsible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get you to respond to this morning`s New York Times, though, on this issue of President Biden. They -- they believe he hasn`t done enough to reassure Americans that he`s up to the job.\par \par \uc2 They note this. "His standard line is, `The only thing I can say is, watch me,` about the ability to be president and serve a second term in his 80s."\par \par \uc2 "But Mr. Biden," the Times writes, "has given voters very few chances to do just that, to, quote, `watch him.` And his refusal to engage with the public regularly raises questions about his age and health."\par \par \uc2 Again, this comes from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , not some right-wing blog about this. Should President Biden be doing more to show Americans that he`s up to the job?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I think his schedule reflects an active person, mentally and physically, who is engaging with the American people on a regular basis. I don`t know what more they`re asking for, but I`ve been in meetings with him; time after time, his performance tells me he`s up to the job and does it well.\par \par \uc2 Let`s concede the obvious, what his age is, but let`s look at the obvious. He is an active president who is meeting the challenges of America every day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A coalition of more than 60 California progressive groups are calling on Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  to resign now. I know you have not made that call. At what point does her absence make you re-think whether you should publicly also add your voice to the calls of asking her to resign her Senate seat?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  is my friend. She`s my colleague. We sit next to one another on the Judiciary Committee. She`s done extraordinary things in her public career.\par \par \uc2 Let`s face it, she`s gone through several weeks of real travail over this shingles issue that she`s obviously dealing with. She wants to come back. She said to \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  on the phone last week, "I want to get on that plane next Monday and be there." I want her to come back, too. But her future is in her own hands and her family`s consultation. I wish her the best and I hope she can return very soon.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When`s the last time -- have you personally spoken with her by phone or any other means since she got out of the hospital?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: No, I haven`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No?\par \par \uc2 Just Senator Schumer?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: It`s been several weeks. Schumer has, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And when you guys organized with a 50/50 Senate, you had a mechanism that allowed for any, sort of -- any tied committee vote to be where you could move that nomination to the floor for a full vote. You didn`t include that when you guys ended up with a one-seat majority. Was that a mistake?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: No. I think we reflected the reality. A 50/50 Senate said that, if you have a tie vote, you can discharge it to the floor.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:01]\par \par \uc2 When you have a 51-49 Senate, we have a nominal majority. And the rules reflect that reality. We have to have a majority vote coming out of the committee. If we don`t have a majority vote, if it`s a tie vote in the committee, then the nomination does not move forward.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the reality of the composition of the Senate today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay. And any regrets on keeping Senator Feinstein on the Judiciary Committee at the beginning? I mean, this was not news to folks that she had some challenges.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Listen, she`s served on this committee for decades, and served with distinction. And she stepped aside from the chairmanship and gave me an opportunity to serve as chairman, and wanted to remain on the committee for other issues that were important to her. It made sense, and I think it was the right decision at the time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Biden has said he`s not going to negotiate on the debt ceiling at all. Is a point where that -- more Democrats are starting to question whether a no-talk, a no-negotiation stance, is -- whether that`s really a good idea? Do you think that`s still a good idea?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Listen, we don`t need to default in this country. If we default on our national debt, it`s going to cost our economy dearly. We`re going to find businesses unable to proceed and people losing their jobs right and left. That is a terrible outcome. So let`s do the responsible thing and not default, move forward on the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Now, we can have a fulsome debate on the budget, and we will. And I understand it on the spending levels, but not at the expense of jeopardizing jobs and economic growth in America. Don`t default. Avoid default on our national debt.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You didn`t say, don`t talk. I did grab that. I mean, you --\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, of course, we should talk.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: No, no. The conversation should be under way, but it should be on the budget resolution and on the appropriations process and entitlement reform, if that`s part of the agenda. That should all be separate from the question of the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Don`t default. Avoid default.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 , Democrat from Illinois -- Senator, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Good to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And before we go to break, our "Meet the Press" Minute this week. Back in 1991, then Republican Senator John Danforth was Clarence Thomas` Sherpa, as we call it around here, the chief supporter in the Senate for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` nomination. And he shepherded \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  through that contentious confirmation process. And he appeared on this program to discuss the new justice`s judicial philosophy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM RUSSERT, FORMER NBC NEWS MODERATOR: Don`t you have some concerns that you may be back here in the future seeking legislation to undo what \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has done on the court? No concern whatsoever?\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN DANFORTH (R-MO): No. I really don`t, because I think that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` view of judicial restraint is that judges should not be imposing their own philosophical views on the country from the bench. And therefore, I think that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is going to be a judge who does not try to use the Supreme Court as a way of fostering his own political philosophy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  was one of two justices to publicly disclose they opposed what the Supreme Court did on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our new NBC poll shows voters don`t want another Trump-Biden rematch. The panel is here, ready to discuss the impact of this poll, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:13]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Panel is here. Amy Walter, editor-in-chief of "The Cook Political Report"; Eugene Robinson, columnist for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 "; Marc Short, former chief of staff to Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 ; and Rachael Bade, co-author of "Politico Playbook".\par \par \uc2 Amy, let me start with the fact that we`ve seen these numbers, 70 percent don`t want Biden to run again, 60 percent don`t want Trump again. My favorite number is when you put it all together -- there is 5 percent of Americans that actually do want to see the sequel.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, THE COOK POLITICAL REPORT: Which means they`re related.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, it is -- America doesn`t want this.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: No, they don`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And both parties are about to hand us a rematch that`s going to lead to unintended consequences. What vacuums are there that this creates?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It creates a vacuum certainly for a third-party candidate. I mean, the great fear among many Democrats is the encouragement for other candidates to try to get on the ballot. We know there`s already the No Label`s effort, which Democrats have come out against vociferously.\par \par \uc2 If you think about how close 2016, 2020 were, it doesn`t take much for a third-party candidate to pull, to push this to one candidate or the other to win an Arizona, a Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the vacuum for other candidates to come in and to say to America, really? You know you don`t want this. Come vote for me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. You know, Rachael, we`ve been talking about how negative partisanship has been a definer of our politics. And now we`re about to basically get negative partisanship nominees if we`re not careful, right? We`re essentially nominating these people because that`s just all there is.\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE, SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, POLITICO: Yeah. I mean, obviously. I mean, Trump`s numbers right now, he seems to be solidifying things with the base right now with DeSantis, he`s the top person to potentially take him out. His numbers are slipping. And this week, we`re going to see Biden announce that he`s running again.\par \par \uc2 And, I mean, just to go back to the No Labels point, if you look at the No Labels map and who they`re going to be targeting in this next election, a lot of these voters are Biden voters.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, with the margins being as close as they are, this is something why -- this is why Democrats are freaking out about this right now because it`s going to be a problem for him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, go ahead, Gene. Let`s go to the Democratic side, like --\par \par \uc2 EUGENE ROBINSON, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is obviously driven by physical concerns with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . What`s he got to do to fix this?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Well, number one, announce. And number two, keep moving forward.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there were a lot of people who didn`t want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to run the last time, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: And really, until \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  endorsed him in South Carolina and he won that primary, he was not doing well. He was, what, fourth or fifth in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, the only part of the Democratic coalition where a majority of Democrats want him to run again are Black Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Exactly. And so I think the party probably, and I think quite likely both parties, kind of go home to their nominees in the end, right? And so, the question is whether the anti-Trump vote is more substantial than the anti-Biden vote. Which I think it probably is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Marc, I`ve spent years being told by many a Republican consultant, don`t try an electability argument with Republican voters. It never works.\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO VP PENCE: I`m not sure if it does, Chuck. I think there are principle arguments that also work. But, you know, I think it`s an important data point for this moment, but polls are a good reflection of today, not necessarily the future. And I don`t think there`s any doubt that there`s been a lot of Republicans who have rallied around President Trump because of the perceived political prosecution in New York.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, if we went by polls today, in 2008, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  would`ve been our nominee. In 2012, Michele Bachmann. 2016, Scott Walker. And it would`ve been Bernie Sanders on the Democrat side.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s a lot of headwinds still to face President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I want to remind people Barack Obama never -- trailed \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in every single poll in the 2007 calendar year.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:01]\par \par \uc2 Every single one.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Yeah, every single one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He did not overtake her in the contest in the national polls until after he won Iowa.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: He won Iowa.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And so \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , in many ways, is starting as strong as any non- experienced presidential candidate. So -- but I can`t help but wonder, are we catching \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on the way up or on the way down?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Well, I think he caught the wave after the midterms because it was a self-own by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That`s when Trump is the weakest, when it`s not that the forces that dislike him are against him, like we`re seeing with the rally around the indictment. It`s when he himself got himself into a precarious position, either with his tweets or in this case endorsing candidates in the 2022 election who were unelectable.\par \par \uc2 It was a reminder that even though Biden may look really vulnerable, and even though the economy was really bad, Republicans can still lose if they put up candidates like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So he rode that wave.\par \par \uc2 Now that we`re not talking about the midterms anymore, we`re talking about the enemies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  going after him, it rallies the base.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: But we`re also talking about DeSantis himself, though. And he came to Washington --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It`s not like Republicans moving.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Huh?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It`s not like Republicans suddenly don`t like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: No.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: His approvals are as high as they were five months ago.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 BADE: But there are really bad signs. I mean, him coming to Washington this week, this was supposed to be a big moment for him to come to D.C., connect with his old colleagues, get a whole bunch of endorsements.\par \par \uc2 And yet, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won more endorsements this week from Mar-a-Lago in terms of voter Republicans than DeSantis did.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me throw up these quotes. These are stunning, especially when you look at the Republicans that are commenting about their lack of basic relationship with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Both Florida senators claim they don`t talk to the governor.\par \par \uc2 Rick Scott: DeSantis doesn`t talk to me. So I don`t know about DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 : I haven`t spoken to him in a number of months, despite South Florida being underwater.\par \par \uc2 Greg Steube\uc2 , Florida Republican: To this day, I`ve not heard from Governor DeSantis. He fell off a roof, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  called him.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Calls him first thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And then, this is extraordinary, Rachael. A former member of Congress from Michigan, David Trott, I -- called up to go on the record to use, I`m not going to repeat the word, but basically said: DeSantis is not a likable guy, and used a more colorful word.\par \par \uc2 What -- the pattern is developing here.\par \par \uc2 BADE: Yeah. I mean, look, self-inflicted wound here. Be nice. Say hi to people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  strategy.\par \par \uc2 BADE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m sorry, there is a, you know --\par \par \uc2 SHORT: I do think, Chuck, I do think these endorsements are grossly overvalued. I mean, in 2016, when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was on his way to a nomination--\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: He had no endorsements.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SHORT: -- the only two endorsements he had were \uc2 Duncan Hunter\uc2  and \uc2 Chris Collins\uc2 , who both ended up in jail, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: The first two. And then he had Jeff Sessions and \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHORT: So, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BADE: But here`s the problem, though -- if you can`t connect with your own delegation, I mean, Steube told me -- \uc2 Greg Steube\uc2  told me that he had numerous times over the past five years in Congress, reached out to DeSantis to try to get a meeting to talk policy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BADE: Ignored every time. Actually, you know, told to show up at campaign events and then told, you can`t take the stage with DeSantis.\par \par \uc2 This is a problem for him. And if he`s going to alienate lawmakers who understand the electability argument and why that is significant, how is he going to connect with, you know, GOP voters who sort of vote with their gut?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Marc, let me ask you to address it this way. You know how reticent people are to talk about people. The fact that there is a comfort now coming at DeSantis, what does that tell you about DeSantis right now?\par \par \uc2 SHORT: I think it tells you everything you need to know, Chuck. I think it`s where a lot of Republicans view that he`s an easier target. I guess my concern --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a dangerous place to be.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: It is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, when we were talking about Clinton/Obama, Clinton was the easier target back then, not Obama. And you would think it should be Trump is the easier target, and in this case it`s not been.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: I think that`s right. But I think at the same time, I mean, I wouldn`t be as worried about the endorsements. I think for people of principle there`s a bigger concern about what`s happening in using a big government Republican going after private-sector industries.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: And I think that`s a bigger concern for principled conservatives.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: The pursuit of Mickey Mouse is really crazy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The two of you --\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: It`s really crazy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- should be a producer because you`ve just segued into a terrific tease for my next segment.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has been picking a fight with the state`s second largest private employer. I`m going to show you how important \uc2 Disney\uc2  is to the overall Florida economy, and why this battle could backfire.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:10]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 The feud between \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and \uc2 Disney\uc2  escalated yet again this week when the Florida governor proposed developing land near the amusement park into a state park or, even more snarkily, he suggested, a state prison.\par \par \uc2 It was the latest volley in a battle that began last year when \uc2 Disney\uc2  executives criticized DeSantis` parental rights in education bill. It was dubbed the "don`t say gay bill" by many opponents.\par \par \uc2 But it has turned into somewhat of an all-out personal feud between a sitting governor and a business that is very crucial to much of the state`s economy.\par \par \uc2 Let me just show you how important \uc2 Disney\uc2  is to the state of Florida. This is \uc2 Disney\uc2 `s annual visitors to the Disney World amusement parks. Over 36 million people in 2021.\par \par \uc2 And just show you know, that`s actually kind of low right now. Pre- pandemic, close to 60 million visitors. Just, you know-- it works out to about one in five of all Americans, though, of course, there`s some global visitors there.\par \par \uc2 How about in tax revenue that Disney World generates? Well, \uc2 Disney\uc2  itself claims they collected over $1 billion in taxes in the calendar year 2022. That amount of money is more than the entire budget for many of Florida`s state agencies. That`s how crucial this is to the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to the number of employees. It`s the second largest private employer, over 75,000. This is central Florida`s economy, if you will. Seventy-five thousand people employed Disney World, the only private company to employ more people in the state of Florida are -- is the \uc2 Publix\uc2  grocery store chain.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s just the overall economic impact that it does for the entire central Florida region. One estimate by Oxford Economics put it at over $75 billion in economic impact to the state of Florida thanks to Disney World.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t just a mouse that roars. Politically, it could become a mouse that bites as well.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, this week, we saw how simple mistakes could end in gun violence. In a nation full of firearms and fear, what would it take to make knocking on a stranger`s door feel safe again?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 More than a quarter of Americans, 28 percent, say a family member, friend, coworker, or they themselves have been a victim of gun violence. It outnumbers self-identified political independents in our poll by two to one, as more Americans become part of a shared experience that they never wanted.\par \par \uc2 I`m not interested in having a debate about gun laws. It`s more of like, we have a societal and cultural problem. And I -- it seems like it`s now bigger than just a simple fight over the laws, Rachael. I mean, this feeling that you can`t go to some neighbor`s house, and you end up at the wrong door, and you might get shot.\par \par \uc2 BADE: Yeah, yeah, I mean, talk about parents being afraid to send their kids to school. I mean, it is a fear that everyone has right now. And -- and I`m going to take it back to laws because here in Washington, this was story number like 25. I mean, we`re talking about everything else and, and not about this issue.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was asked about it and he said, look, I can`t do anything. Democrats, you know, Sununu pointed out, they didn`t pass laws on this when they had --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s very true.\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE: -- the majorities in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And now, Republicans control the House. Nobody`s talking about it up there, and yet everybody`s talking about it everywhere else.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It seems that there needs to be some leadership to sort of change the culture that says, Marc, you don`t need to pull a gun first. Like that`s where we are. Like why -- people are in a, "I`ve got to have my gun first". That seems to be the uneasiness.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: Well, I think that there`s a devaluation of life, honestly, in our society, Chuck. And I do think there needs to be a greater appreciation for the value of life.\par \par \uc2 But I also think that there`s a lot of media focus, and rightly there should be, on these mass shootings. But respecting everyone who, who lost a life and mourn -- we mourn them, the reality is there`s been 12,800 deaths by guns this year, 169 by mass shootings. There`s a crime problem in our inner cities that we`re not addressing.\par \par \uc2 We focus a lot on the mass shootings without focusing on the real problem of crime in our inner cities. And there needs to be a bigger focus there.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: Well, it`s not just crime in inner cities. It`s crime in suburbs. It`s crime in, you know, rural areas.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:03]\par \par \uc2 I mean, guns are being used everywhere. It`s a gun problem everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And so again, getting back to laws. I mean, Florida after Parkland passed a red, red flag law. And that has been used thousands of times, like probably 10,000 times by now, to take guns out of the hands of people who are deemed, through a process, to be a danger to themselves or others.\par \par \uc2 And I -- who knows how many shootings that has stopped, but I`ll bet it`s some. It`s not -- the number is not zero. So how else do we attack this --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: -- cultural issue other than through laws?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Amy, I don`t even know if we have a shared North Star on this, which is the goal -- you would think a minimum goal that everybody could agree on is we should keep guns out of those who mentally aren`t capable of having it, or who could be trigger happy for whatever reason. But we don`t agree on how -- what --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: On how to judge that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s the -- how do you do that either, how to judge it. I don`t know if these red flag laws are constitutional. We have yet to see it tested, for what it`s worth. So that`s an issue.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also -- we don`t seem to agree that, okay, we`ve got to get rid of mentally challenged people from getting guns, but we don`t know how to do it.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Well, and we don`t -- I do think also what does "mentally challenged" mean, when so many of these shootings are literally, as you pointed out, spur of the moment, heat of the moment? If you have a gun nearby, suicides are also one on that list that you put forward. Of the number of suicides that are aided by guns, how many of those would`ve been prevented if it wasn`t as easy to pick up in your area?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s what I`m uncomfortable with -- is that I would`ve had to say yes on that. I`ve had a family member and a work colleague touched by gun violence. I`m guessing everybody here knows somebody or has somebody.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that`s the part of this that I feel like our politicians aren`t addressing this shared reality. Rachael?\par \par \uc2 BADE: On mental health? I mean --\par \par \uc2 TODD: On all of it.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: On all of it.\par \par \uc2 BADE: Yes. Yeah, I mean, you talk about trying to get some sort of united joint star -- united North Star that people can go to. I mean, you would think that, you know, Republicans always want to talk about mental health, Democrats always want to talk about gun control. I mean, there has to be some sort of overlapping area here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BADE: And like, you know, red flag laws have been successful in parts of the country. Could they do something more at a national level here, in terms of making sure that guns are not finding -- falling into the hands of the wrong people?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: But what`s the one thing that every gun crime, every gun issue, incident, has in common? It`s the gun. I mean, we have more guns than people in this country. I mean, that`s the bottom line. And, now how we address that is a difficult problem.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: We also live in a --\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: But it`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yeah. And we live in a society where it`s heads I win, tails you lose. There`s no such thing as compromise on any issues, because somehow if there`s compromise, I`m going to lose out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But I want to do a political reality here, Marc. We`ve yet to see somebody who is seen as a Second Amendment supporter lose an election because of that issue.\par \par \uc2 SHORT: Well, sure, because I think that as many as we`ve talked about families that have been touched by gun violence, we also have family members or people we know who have also been protected because they had a gun, too, Chuck. And so it`s a complicated issue.\par \par \uc2 And I think you`re right, it is a bigger societal issue than saying, let`s grab everybody`s gun.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BADE: Politically, I mean, you talk about, you know, there hasn`t been a Republican voter --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BADE: -- who has been sort of ousted because of this. And until you start to see -- or not a Republican voter, a Republican candidate, a Republican lawmaker. Until you see something like that, you`re not going to see changes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Abortion is definitely salient --\par \par \uc2 BADE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- with swing voters. It`s not clear yet that gun policy is.\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230423ej4n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc11}{\*\bkmkend toc11}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for April 22, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Courtney Kube, Monica Alba, Ken Dilanian, Erin  McLaughlin, Jesse Kirsch \par \uc2 2,585 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [18:34:30]\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, NBC NEWS HOST: Ahead for us tonight, a giant massive seaweed wider than the U.S. is bearing down on Florida. And a $20 million mystery who swiped a container of gold from a North American airport, tonight on NIGHTLY NEWS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:42:05]\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Jose Diaz Balart.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Good evening. The race to evacuate Americans from Sudan is intensifying as the situation there it gets worse by the moment. Officials in the African nation say that the evacuation of foreign nationals has begun. But the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  and Pentagon have not confirmed any American operation.\par \par \uc2 This is part of Saudi Arabia`s evacuation today. Transporting hundreds by caravan and then by boat to safety. Take a look at this. Airports are not an option. They`re totally blown out from the intense fighting between two warring military factions there. There are an estimated 16,000 American citizens currently in the eastern African country, many with dual citizenship who may not want to leave but there are plenty who do. And they`re getting more desperate by the hour. We begin tonight with Pentagon Correspondent Courtney Kube.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY & MILITARY AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, a glimmer of hope for some Americans trapped in Sudan following eight days of violence and bloodshed. The Sudanese military announcing the United States, Britain, France, and China will evacuate their diplomats and citizens by air with military transport aircraft belonging to their armed forces from Khartoum.\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia completing the first civilian evacuations this morning rescuing more than 150 of its own citizens and those of other countries first by caravan and then by ship.\par \par \uc2 While officials familiar with the planning say the Biden administration is leaning towards evacuating the roughly 70 \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  personnel in Khartoum. Today, the Pentagon saying they`re preparing for various contingencies, but declining to talk about potential operations.\par \par \uc2 There are more than 16,000 other Americans currently sheltering in place, including Rebecca Winter`s (ph) sister-in-law, a teacher in the capital city of Khartoum. She`s now hiding with her infant daughter.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WINTER (ph): The city is in complete disarray. She needs to come home now. We need evacuation for U.S. citizens. And we need humanitarian aid for everyone who`s suffering in this conflict in Sudan.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: This week, the U.S. military moves specially trained troops to nearby Djibouti to be ready to rescue Americans caught in the crossfire between two warring generals fighting for control of Sudan. But with the airport`s still shut down, some Americans are prepared to take things into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m keeping my hopes up but I`m also preparing for having to find my own route.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Like 1000s of Americans still caught in the middle of this deadly power struggle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Courtney, the situation is going more dire every day?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Yeah, that`s right. Trapped Americans are running out of supplies. And now some are looking for their own escape route. But tonight State Department officials are warning U.S. citizens not to take private convoys to potential evacuation points, saying, they cannot guarantee anyone safety. Jose.\par \par \uc2 [18:45:04]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Courtney Kube in Washington, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Last night Supreme Court decision to keep the abortion pill mifepristone available for now means the legal and political battles over access to it are just getting started. Monica Alba reports from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, political leaders reacting to the latest development in the hot button issue of abortion access.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our confidence in our judiciary (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK MAJORITY LEADER: The hard right anti- choice MAGA wing in this country will keep working and working and working to take away all women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: But for now, the most commonly used abortion pill remains widely available for millions of women after the Supreme Court preserved full access to mifepristone, Friday, while the appeals process plays out over a Texas judge`s ruling that would have jeopardized access to the drug. A majority of the justices granting the Biden administration`s request for temporary relief with \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  publicly dissenting. The case now goes back to the Fifth Circuit, where oral arguments are set for May 17. Nothing about the medications availability will change before then, and may not until the matter goes back to the High Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it`s very highly unlikely given that we`re in April already that this case would be heard and resolved. This term that would be extraordinary, much more likely they`re going to set the matter for argument in the fall of 2023. And a ruling would come out in the spring of 2024.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: The White House relieved for now, with President Biden arguing the lower court decision would have quote undermined FDA`s medical judgment and put women`s health at risk. A new NBC News poll shows 58% of adults say abortion should be legal, either always or most of the time, compared to a combined 38% who believe abortion should be illegal either with exceptions or without exceptions. The abortion debate proving to be a key issue for voters as the race for 2024 heats up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Monica Alba joins me now from the White House. Monica, is there any reason to think the circuit court could rule differently than it did last time around?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Well, it`s certainly possible, Jose, because the next three judge panel from the conservative leaning Fifth Circuit that will hear the case next month will be chosen at random. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Monica Alba at the White House, thank you. And now to the new report that the Air National Guardsmen charged with leaking classified documents may have been sharing Top Secret Intelligence online for much longer than previously thought, possibly as long as a year before he was caught. Ken Dilanian has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: New reporting tonight that the accused leaker of classified information began posting secrets more than a year ago, shortly after Russia`s invasion of Ukraine. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  says it discovered that airman Jack Teixeira allegedly began sharing military secrets as far back as February 2022 to a chat group containing 600 members, that`s 10 months before the December 2022 leaks that Teixeira is charged with.\par \par \uc2 Two sources familiar with the matter tell NBC News investigators gave no indication when briefing Congress last week that they were aware of this new set of disclosures. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Pentagon had been scrambling to assess the damage from a leak they first discovered April 5.\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, NBC NEWS, NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: And the longer something is out there floating in the ether, in the cyber world without the government knowing, without them being able to shut it down, the more potential damage can be done.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Teixeira`s lawyer, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and the Pentagon, all declined to comment tonight. The Times match the new posts to Teixeira through a series of digital clues including a handle he had previously used. The newspaper quoted Teixeira boasting of his special access to secrets. I have a little more than open-source info perks of being in a USAF intel unit, he said, seemingly referring to his job as an I.T. specialist and an Air Force Intelligence wing and hinting that he used his I.T. access to get information.\par \par \uc2 "The job I have lets me get privileged above most intel guys," he wrote. Teixeira claim he was hunting through classified computer networks for intelligence on the Ukraine war, including material from the \uc2 National Security Agency\uc2  and GCHQ, the U.K.`s digital spying department.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: This goes way beyond a kid trying to impress his buddies.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: When one chat room member urged him not to abuse his access to secrets, he is said to have replied, too late.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Ken, could these new revelations lead to more charges for Teixeira?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Yeah, Teixeira`s alleged comments in the second chat room really do complicate his legal situation, Jose. He allegedly even offered Teixeira secrets with people outside the United States. The textbook definition of espionage. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Ken Dilanian in Washington, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It has happened again another instance of someone accidentally pulling up to the wrong address and the homeowner opening fire. This is -- this in Florida and the police say the shooter did nothing wrong. Erin McLaughlin has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: As the nation comes to terms with a week of random shootings involving seemingly innocuous mistakes. Tonight, in South Florida questions surrounding a grocery delivery that ended in gunfire.\par \par \uc2 [18:50:12]\par \par \uc2 DIAMOND D`ARVILLE: I had seen him pull out a gun and I said that`s where I was like, we got to go. We got to go.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: 18-year-old Diamond D`arville and her 19-year-old boyfriend, Waldes Thomas say last Saturday night they mistakenly drove to the wrong house to drop off a grocery order. Thomas tells NBC News they were trying to leave the property, alleging the homeowner blocked them with his truck and later opening fire.\par \par \uc2 WALDES THOMAS: They shuffled behind. It wasn`t even front, they shot from behind. So it just -- it just made me feel like it was like a racist or racist thing.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: The car was hit multiple times.\par \par \uc2 D`ARVILLE: Here, right here.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Though neither were injured, according to the police report, the homeowner claims he told his 12-year-old son to order them to leave his property. Moments later he heard the boy calling for help. He alleges he saw Thomas driving erratically, at one point reversing and running over his right foot. He claims he opened fire fearing he or his son were going to be further injured by the vehicle and that his intention was to disable the vehicle. The homeowner has not responded to NBCs request for comment. Investigators later concluded each party appear justified in their actions based on the circumstances they perceived and no charges were filed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Even an innocent person who enters a home or another person`s property could potentially risk deadly force being used against them if the homeowners perception of the threat is reasonable.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Tonight, Thomas says his grocery delivery days are over at a time when many Americans are thinking twice before knocking on a stranger`s door. Erin McLaughlin, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And not a growing mystery, a shipment of gold and other valuables worth $15 million is vanished missing from the Toronto Airport. It could be the biggest heist in Canada`s history. Jesse Kirsch has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: To the north tonight Canadian authorities are trying to solve a multimillion-dollar mystery. Police after a plane landed in Toronto Monday, cargo was moved to a holding facility before someone took off with a high value container.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN DUIVESTEYN, PEEL REGIONAL POLICE INSPECTOR: It did contain gold but was not exclusive to gold can contain other items of monetary value.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: In all, police say the cargoes worth roughly 15 million in U.S. dollars, but they aren`t sharing much else.\par \par \uc2 JOHN PISTOLE, FORMER FBI DEPUTY DIRECTOR: There may be some tactical or strategic advantage for them for not disclosing, so they`re not tipping their hand to the bad guys and where the shipment might be today.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: John Pistole is a former Deputy \uc2 FBI\uc2  director and a former TSA Administrator. He has many unanswered questions.\par \par \uc2 PISTOLE: Was an inside job and what evidence is there of whoever committed the heist if you will, what type of access control system that they go through? Do they have a badge?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: In a statement, the \uc2 Greater Toronto Airports Authority\uc2  emphasizing last week thieves access the public side of a warehouse outside of our primary security line. This did not involve access to Toronto Pearson itself and did not pose a threat to passengers. The alleged airport theft evokes memories of New York`s 1978 \uc2 Lufthansa\uc2  heist becoming the stuff of Hollywood legend in Goodfellas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nobody knows for sure just how much was taken in the daring pre-dawn raid at the Lufthansa Cargo Terminal at Kennedy Airport.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Canada has its own history of high-profile heists. In 2012, thieves snatch millions of dollars` worth of maple syrup according to the \uc2 Canadian Broadcasting Corporation\uc2 . Police believe this latest theft was an isolated incident. But tonight its explanation is up in the air. Jesse Kirsch, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And now the things that go boom in the night. That, the National Weather Service believes is the sound of a meteor and here it is, streaking through the skies over Central Indiana just before 9:00 Friday night. There were sightings in Illinois, Michigan and Kentucky as well.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, there`s good news tonight, the little boy taking some giant steps.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:57:25]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: There`s good news tonight about defying expectations and the little boy who never gives up on his goals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Look, their seven-year-old Lenny Turley born with cerebral palsy. He was determined to take some of his first steps in front of his kindergarten class at High Point Elementary near Columbus Ohio. His trial celebrated by every single student. That reaction deeply touching for Lenny`s parents, Justin and Alicia.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): What was it like when you saw the video of Lenny, not once, twice, walking in front of the class?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN TURLEY, LENNY TURLEY`S FATHER: It was unreal. That kind of support that he got from them. It was overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA TURLEY, LENNY TURLEY`S MOTHER: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: What is the bigger lesson that all of us could learn?\par \par \uc2 A. TURLEY: I think the first thing that comes to mind is hope. Sorry, I told myself I`m not going to be emotional. All kids struggle with something. So if there`s people, whoever`s out there, that struggling with something, there`s always someone out there that`s going to give them support even if they feel stuck.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And get this Lenny is excited to start baseball next month. That`s NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for this Saturday. I`m Jose Diaz-Balart reporting from New York. Thank you for the privilege of your time. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230423ej4n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc12}{\*\bkmkend toc12}\b \uc2 New Legal And Political Battles Over Abortion Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Monica Alba \par \uc2 508 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART: Last night Supreme Court decision to keep the abortion pill Mifepristone available for now means the legal and political battles over access to it are just getting started. Monica Alba reports from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, political leaders reacting to the latest development in the hot button issue of abortion access.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Our confidence in our judiciary (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK MAJORITY LEADER: The hard right anti- choice MAGA wing in this country will keep working and working and working to take away all women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: But for now, the most commonly used abortion pill remains widely available for millions of women after the Supreme Court preserved full access to mifepristone, Friday, while the appeals process plays out over a Texas judge`s ruling that would have jeopardized access to the drug. A majority of the justices granting the Biden administration`s request for temporary relief with \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  publicly dissenting. The case now goes back to the Fifth Circuit, where oral arguments are set for May 17. Nothing about the medications availability will change before then, and may not until the matter goes back to the High Court.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it`s very highly unlikely given that we`re in April already that this case would be heard and resolved. This term that would be extraordinary, much more likely they`re going to set the matter for argument in the fall of 2023. And a ruling would come out in the spring of 2024.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: The White House relieved for now, with President Biden arguing the lower court decision would have quote undermined FDA`s medical judgment and put women`s health at risk. A new NBC News poll shows 58% of adults say abortion should be legal, either always or most of the time, compared to a combined 38% who believe abortion should be illegal either with exceptions or without exceptions. The abortion debate proving to be a key issue for voters as the race for 2024 heats up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Monica Alba joins me now from the White House. Monica, is there any reason to think the circuit court could rule differently than it did last time around?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Well, it`s certainly possible, Jose, because the next three judge panel from the conservative leaning Fifth Circuit that will hear the case next month will be chosen at random. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Monica Alba at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230425ej4m000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc13}{\*\bkmkend toc13}\b \uc2 The Supreme Court Preserving Access To The Abortion Pill For Now In The Most Significant Case Since Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Laura Jarrett, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the breaking news from the Supreme Court of the future of that abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The Supreme Court preserving access to the abortion pill for now in the most significant case since Roe v. Wade. So, what are the next steps? We`ll break it down.\par \par \uc2 The severe storm threat moving east after tornadoes ripped through Texas and Oklahoma. At least three dead, flash flooding and water rescues in Austin, golf ball-sized hail in Illinois, even a water spout in Florida. Millions at risk heading into the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Charges against Alec Baldwin officially dropped. And new details about the gun that killed the Rust cinematographer as production resumes. The new images from inside the film`s Montana set.\par \par \uc2 An American killed in Sudan as fighting rages on. Plus, the latest from U.S. officials on the other Americans trapped.\par \par \uc2 Alarming new findings about climate change. A new report says the melting of glaciers is off the charts. Can anything be done?\par \par \uc2 Is the future of food grown in a lab? It`s better for the climate but what does it taste like?\par \par \uc2 And back in the groove with record sales rising. Why so many are turning to turntables this weekend.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. Facing an 11:59 p.m. Eastern Time deadline of its own choosing and a day of high drama, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has just weighed in on another pivotal and consequential question in the nation`s divide over abortion. The justices tonight deciding what`s next for a Texas Court ruling that said the FDA`s 23-year-old approval of a commonly used abortion drug is no longer valid, a ruling temporarily paused from taking effect up until now. That has had the potential to impact medical abortions even in states where abortion remains legal.\par \par \uc2 Senior Legal Correspondent Laura Jarrett is standing by with late details. Laura, what is the court saying here?\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hey, Lester, good evening. Just minutes ago, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  issued an order to keep a widely used abortion pill on the market. The justices have flatly blocked that lower court decision from Texas that had invalidated the FDA`s longtime approval of Mifepristone and would have made the drugs substantially harder to obtain.\par \par \uc2 Now, Justice Thomas, Justice Alito dissenting to that decision from the rest of their colleagues. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the drug maker had strongly urged those justices to step in, warning essentially if they hadn`t stepped in, all manufacturing and distribution of this drug would have come to a screeching halt. But for now, women can continue to receive the pill by mail and use it until 10 weeks into their pregnancies while this case continues to play out in the court below.\par \par \uc2 Lester, the Biden Administration just issuing obviously a statement praising it. You know, the groups that had filed this suit originally had claimed that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  ignored safety risk, something that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the Biden Administration put -- pushed back on very strongly, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, so Laura, legally, what happens next? The next steps here.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: OK, so, now the case will head back to a federal appeals court for further evaluation of the merits of the lawsuit. This Supreme Court decision was just a temporary issue just because it came up so quickly. Now the case will head back to a three-judge panel on the fifth circuit. It`s made up of mostly conservatives but we don`t know who exactly on that court will hear this case now that it`s back there, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura Jarrett on that breaking news. I`m joined now by Peter Alexander at the White House. Peter, the president as we heard just reacted to this move. What are you learning?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Lester, that`s right. The White House has been bracing for this decision for days. Abortion medications have become an increasingly critical flash point of the states` limit or ban abortions after the Supreme Court`s ruling last year overturning Roe v Wade largely because abortion medication can be sent by mail and taken at home.\par \par \uc2 As for the president, he just moments ago weighed in, releasing a statement that reads in part, Mifepristone remains available and approved for safe and effective use. While we continue this fight in the courts, the president adding, that his administration will continue to defend the FDA`s independence to review, approve, and regulate a wide range of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 The President says the stakes for women could not be high higher and denounces what he called politically driven attacks on women`s health, Lester. The White House had warned if the court upheld the lower court`s decision, it would have put women`s health at risk and have undermined the FDA`s ability to keep safe and effective medications available for those people who need them. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Peter Alexander -- Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, d