 FDA`s independence to review, approve, and regulate a wide range of prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 The President says the stakes for women could not be high higher and denounces what he called politically driven attacks on women`s health, Lester. The White House had warned if the court upheld the lower court`s decision, it would have put women`s health at risk and have undermined the FDA`s ability to keep safe and effective medications available for those people who need them. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Peter Alexander -- Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s other top story, the Deadly severe weather that has ripped through the plains in the Midwest this week with millions still at risk as the storms move East. Our Priscilla Thompson has the latest.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:03]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): A twister tearing through Tyler, Texas. Unrelenting rain in Austin causing dangerous flash flooding and harrowing water rescues.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That water came up to here and you were just like trying to crawl out the window.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People was getting out of their car swimming, so it was pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: As golf ball-sized hail pummeled parts of Oklahoma and Illinois.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Thousands throughout Texas, Oklahoma, and Arkansas remain without power. The governor in Oklahoma declaring a state of emergency after an EF- 3 tornado ripped through several counties killing three. Many there now grappling with the path of death and destruction left behind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think I`m still in shock.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Ralina Vestal is still shaken after riding out the storm in a bunker in her backyard.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This door is probably a good 50-60 pound and it ripped and ended up.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: When she emerged, there was nothing left. She`s now unsure of what comes next.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling about the future?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m scared. We`re trying to find temporary housing or even a hotel that we can afford month to month. So, I don`t -- I`m not sure.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: In the midst of that uncertainty, her fiance`s boss coming over with his family to help lend a hand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We don`t have a whole lot to give. All we have is hands and so that`s what we`re doing. We`re taking care of our family.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: How much do those little things mean in this moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, it`s keeping a smile on my face and I feel like I have hope.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Hope amid so much despair in a community still reeling from disaster. Priscilla Thompson, NBC News Cole, Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And there was some rough weather in Florida today. Take a look at this water spout near Hollywood Beach. Meteorologist Bill Karins is watching other storms in the horizon. What do you got, Bill?\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST (on camera): Yes, amazing video. And as soon as it made its way on shore, we actually call that a tornado. That was the only tornadoes that we`ve had so far today. We`ve had strong storms in Ohio, South Texas, and also into areas of Mississippi. But we`re not watching anything widespread, not like the last couple days.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, that changes. We were thinking that we have a chance for some strong storms to roll through the Mid-Atlantic late afternoon, early evening, from Washington D.C., to Richmond, to Raleigh, Wilmington. Our friends in Myrtle Beach and Charleston, keep an eye to the sky late in the day. Gusty winds, we`re also going to see some small hail, and isolated chances of tornado.\par \par \uc2 So, here`s the timing. This is 8:00 p.m. Notice, New York City, you`re clear for all your day on Saturday. Not until about 9:00 p.m. the rain comes in. But for the Mid-Atlantic down to the southeast beaches, watch out tomorrow afternoon.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors have officially closed their case against Alec Baldwin of the deadly Rust shooting with New Revelations leading to the stunning reversal. But a crew member is still charged. Here`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight seen here for the first time, Alec Baldwin is back on the set of Rusts as he was officially cleared of criminal charges today.\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN, ACTOR: I was holding the gun, yes.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The stunning legal about-face comes 18 months to the day after the death of cinematographer Halyna Hutchins. A source with knowledge of the investigation tells NBC news new prosecutors recently learned the Colt 45 handled by Baldwin had been modified with a new trigger, making it possible to misfire as the actor who`s maintained his innocence said before.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Then, I let go of the hammer of the gun and the gun goes off.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Before stepping away from the case --\par \par \uc2 Do you believe Alec Baldwin pulled the trigger?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA REEB, FORMER SPECIAL PROSECUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The former special prosecutor and D.A. told us they had evidence to convict. But new case attorneys writing new facts were revealed that demand further investigation. Consequently, we cannot proceed under the current time constraints.\par \par \uc2 DANNY CEVALLOS, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: It defies belief that the state is only now learning about how this firearm operates when they are the only people who have had access to it exclusively for over a year and a half.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: While prosecutors maintain the move does not absolve Mr. Baldwin of criminal culpability, experts don`t believe charges may be refiled as the DA`s office warns. Hannah Gutierrez-Reed, the film`s armorer who plans to plead not guilty, still faces involuntary manslaughter charges.\par \par \uc2 CEVALLOS: The case against Gutierrez-Reed is still viable because of her title, her role.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With production resuming on the new set of Rust, NBC News has learned director Joel Souza who was also shot gave a moving speech to the cast and crew of 200. Tonight, Baldwin is also back on the set hoping to finish shooting the Western that started his legal drama.\par \par \uc2 Miguel Almaguer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, the \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  says an American is among hundreds killed in Sudan where fighting between two rival armies rages on. Courtney Kube now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, thousands of Americans among those caught in the crossfire in Sudan. A fragile 72-hour ceasefire not ending the wave of violence. The State Department announcing an American was among the at least 400 people killed as two warring generals fight for control of the country. Sudan`s International Airport shut down, many residents without running water or enough food.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon now sending troops to the U.S. military base Camp Lemonnier in Djibouti to be ready to evacuate Americans.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:22]\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN, U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: We`ve deployed some forces to -- into theater and we haven`t been called on to do anything yet.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: But the Biden Administration is leaning towards an evacuation of the roughly 70 \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  personnel there according to two sources familiar with the planning, while insisting a mission to rescue the rest of the 16,000 Americans in Sudan is still too dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, American Lakshmi Partha Sarathi (ph) among those stranded.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There`s no plane that can safely land. So, at the moment, I`m just kind of taking it day by day, hour by hour.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KUBE (on camera): Tonight, one side in the fighting says they`re ready to partially reopen Sudan`s airports though it`s unclear if they actually control them. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Courtney Kube at the Pentagon, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to harrowing accounts of torture from refugees who fled China and say they still don`t feel safe. Keir Simmons reports. And we have to warn you, some may find what you hear disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Inside this building in the heart of New York, U.S. authorities say China was operating a secret police station, harassing Chinese dissidents. This week, two men arrested. It`s now shut down.\par \par \uc2 BREON PEACE, U.S. ATTORNEY, EASTERN DISTRICT OF NEW YORK: We cannot and will not tolerate the Chinese government`s persecution of pro-democracy activists.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : The U.S. says it`s a global pattern China`s Communist governments going after those who oppose it.\par \par \uc2 We`ve traveled to the edge of Europe, Norway, to meet refugees who`ve fled China. Abduelli (ph) says he was tortured and sexually abused by Chinese authorities.\par \par \uc2 What is the torture?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The torture mostly electric stick. Your skin will be burnt.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : He`s part of a mostly Muslim ethnic minority called Uyghurs from Northwest China. The U.S. and others say they`re victims of genocide which China denies. The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  says more than a million Uyghurs were sent to what China calls re-education camps. China says they harbored separatists and terrorist thoughts, Abdueli says he and his family did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My sister sentenced 11 years and my brother sentenced 14 years.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Abdueli`s niece died in prison.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wea are living because of them. I am living because of them.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : A recent \uc2 U.N\uc2 . report documented intimidation, threats, and reprisals against Uyghurs overseas, and Uyghurs forcibly returned to China.\par \par \uc2 Student Naraq (ph) was in Turkey when he got a threatening call, so he moved again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s a kind of like a warning shot for me that phone call.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : The anonymous phone call.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Abdueli and his wife Miracle fear speaking to us may come at a cost to their families back home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I love them so much and I worry if anything bad happen to them because of me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You are waiting for them to die there.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Tonight, the Chinese Embassy in Washington tells NBC News their accusations are aimed at undermining China`s unity.\par \par \uc2 President Xi says every country is different. America just needs to understand that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We shouldn`t accept dictatorship. We shouldn`t accept sexual abuse. We shouldn`t accept torture.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Keir, let me circle back to this Chinese secret police stations. You`re hearing there might be more in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS (on camera): That`s right, Lester. One watchdog group tells us there are at least four in the U.S. including one in Los Angeles. China says they are just volunteer groups helping Chinese Nationals abroad. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Keir, thanks. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the alarming knew report on how quickly the world`s glaciers are disappearing at home and abroad. What can be done in our series climate challenge.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We are back with our "CLIMATE CHALLENGE" series and a dire new report about Earth`s glaciers. They`re melting at an astonishing rate with catastrophic consequences. Anne Thompson reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CHIEF ENVIRONMENTAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today`s \uc2 U.N\uc2 . report details the relentless advance of climate change on every continent. The world`s glaciers shrinking at a dramatic Pace right before our eyes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY, EXPERT CLIMATE MONITORING AND DATA RESEARCH SCIENTIST, WORLD METEOROLOGICAL ORG.: It`s not just that we`re losing ice, but we`re losing ice faster and faster. And that has consequences.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The 40 glaciers tracked worldwide lost an average of more than 4.2 feet of ice in a year. In Europe, the melting of the glaciers in the Alps is off the charts. Little winter snow and a summer heat wave exacerbated by dark dust from the Saharan Desert that trapped heat accelerating the record melt.\par \par \uc2 Scientist Mathias Hunt showing NBC News the devastating impact in January.\par \par \uc2 MATHIAS HUNT, SCIENTIST: You can see that it`s much more obvious than seeing just a graph with rising temperatures.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: In Switzerland, for the first time, no snow survived the summer melt season even at the very highest sites. In this country, Montana`s Glacier National Park is in danger of losing its namesake ice.\par \par \uc2 The park has lost 85 percent of its ice area.\par \par \uc2 That was five years ago. Today, the government says the park is warming at twice the global average.\par \par \uc2 All this glacier melt contributing to record sea level rise. Here in lower Manhattan, scientists predict the waters behind me could rise another foot by 2050.\par \par \uc2 But we can slow it down.\par \par \uc2 KENNEDY: Every tenth of a degree of warming is worth fighting for.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: To survive more floods, heat waves, and droughts that inflict the lethal human toll of climate change. Anne Thompson, NBC News New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Still ahead tonight, would you eat lab-grown meat? We`ll taste it as more companies try it out and how it could help the environment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The meat on your plate tonight likely comes from a farm, but more companies are designing ways to grow your dinner in a lab. But how does it taste? Our Maya Eaglin went to find out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAYA EAGLIN, NBC NEWS REPORTER (voiceover): It`s Big Tech`s newest take on Big Ag. No farmland, no coops, but these labs in Silicon Valley could be the future of meat.\par \par \uc2 JOSH TETRICK\uc2 , CEO, EAT JUST: There are a lot of benefits in making meat in this way from 70 percent less emissions, 70 percent less water on land. But one of the biggest is you don`t need to harm an animal.\par \par \uc2 Josh Tetrick\uc2  is the CEO of Eat Just. It`s one of only two companies in the U.S. that`s received clearance from the \uc2 FDA\uc2  for human consumption of lab- grown meat. The \uc2 USDA\uc2  still needs to approve it for sale.\par \par \uc2 UMA VALETI, CEO, \uc2 UPSIDE FOODS\uc2 : This is not vegan or vegetarian.\par \par \uc2 EAGLIN: The other company is \uc2 UPSIDE Foods\uc2  headed by cardiologist Uma Valetti.\par \par \uc2 Talk to me about the science here. How does it work?\par \par \uc2 VALETI: The science is fascinating but it`s fairly simple. We take cells from eggs or young animals or mature animals and we identify those cells that are capable of going into fats, proteins, connective tissue.\par \par \uc2 EAGLIN: Those cells are then prepped in a lab and pumped into stainless steel vessels.\par \par \uc2 Inside this bioreactor tank, there are billions of chicken cells growing. It`ll take about a month before they`re ready to eat.\par \par \uc2 Right now, in the U.S., you can only eat cultivated chicken on company premises.\par \par \uc2 It definitely has the chew factor of chicken. It shreds like chicken too.\par \par \uc2 They`re soft in texture. I have to say, it`s very, very, very, very close.\par \par \uc2 On average, Americans eat 100 pounds of chicken a year but raising livestock comes at a cost, contributing 14.5 percent of all human-caused greenhouse gas emissions according to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . The company is making lab- cultivated meats say they can fix that if they can bring down their costs.\par \par \uc2 Currently the only place in the world you can buy cultivated meat is in Singapore where Eat Just sells its chicken at a financial loss.\par \par \uc2 So, if the motivation isn`t profit here, why sell in Singapore?\par \par \uc2 TETRICK: Selling in Singapore is a way of demonstrating that this new approach to making meat is not science fiction but it`s here. It`s reality.\par \par \uc2 EAGLIN: Experts say lab-grown chicken showing up on store shelves in the U.S. is likely still years away.\par \par \uc2 TETRICK: There`s a ton of risk in doing this. There`s a lot of uncertainty. But the other option is not to do anything, and that seems worse.\par \par \uc2 EAGLIN: Pushing for change one bite at a time. Maya Eaglin, NBC News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: When we return, how vinyl got its groove back. The return of the record in the record store thanks to everyone from emerging artists to superstars like Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally tonight, the viral revival ahead of tomorrow`s Record Store Day. Sales of albums now skyrocketing thanks in part to superstars like Taylor Swift. Our Stephanie Gosk has that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At a time when music from any era and every genre lives invisibly in the cloud, Record Store Day is dedicated to music that can actually be held in your hand and to the independent stores that never stop selling it.\par \par \uc2 SHARON BECHOR, ROCK AND SOUL RECORD STORE: It`s the one day that we are guaranteed to have a line outside and people super excited to buy vinyl releases.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Record sales are on the rise for the last 16 years, up 17 percent in 2022.\par \par \uc2 BECHOR: We`ve seen vinyl totally almost decimated. We thought we would close.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Rock and Soul Records Store has been in Midtown Manhattan for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe the future today for a record store?\par \par \uc2 BECHOR: Super exciting. Like, we`re really excited.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Record store day gets bigger every year. Musicians fuel the hype with special releases, limited editions that sell out fast and can only be bought in the store. Last year, the big record was Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 BECHOR: They started lining up 24 hours before.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BECHOR: So, they really wanted that Taylor Swift.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: And this year, it`s also Taylor Swift. But there is a lot more. Billy Joel, Grateful Dead, Stevie Nicks.\par \par \uc2 The best part about Record Store Day is that there is something for everyone, every taste, every generation, including me. It`s the 40th anniversary of Violent Femmes` debut album, Blister In The Sun, like you`ve never heard it before.\par \par \uc2 And without skipping a beat --\par \par \uc2 Oh, come on. Oh, right back to high school.\par \par \uc2 So, get offline, get off the couch and go to the record store. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Well, that sound of a needle hitting the record. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thanks for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night, everyone.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par 