lly sign the most conservative pro-life legislation that they can get through Congress.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Even if it was six weeks?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I`m not going to talk about six or five or seven or 10.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the gun debate, after more mass shootings in Tennessee and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Oh, my God, there`s an active shooter there!\par \par \uc2 TODD: The \uc2 NRA\uc2  holds its annual meeting and Republicans pledge support.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: With me at 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue, no one will lay a finger on your firearms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can the two parties find common ground on issues that have divided the nation for decades?\par \par \uc2 My guests this morning, Democratic Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  of Wisconsin and Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Also, rising tensions.\par \par \uc2 Israel is facing crises at home and criticism abroad.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: They cannot continue down this road.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My exclusive interview with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER BENJAMIN NETANYAHU: People will see at the end that Israel was a democracy, is a democracy and will be even a stronger democracy after this democratic reform.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Finally, how did a 21-year-old gamer, an Air National Guardsman, gain access to the nation`s top secrets?\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson; former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson; Leigh Ann Caldwell of The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ; and Stephen Hayes, editor of The Dispatch.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. Debates over guns and abortion have roiled this nation for decades. This week those debates feel a bit more intense than they have in a while, and there are some deepening tensions between activists who wield power inside Republican primary circles and the overall electorate.\par \par \uc2 Today mark the 16th anniversary of the \uc2 Virginia Tech\uc2  shooting. At the time it was the deadliest mass shooting in U.S. history. Thirty-two people were killed. Now, sadly, it`s been surpassed by mass shootings in Las Vegas and Orlando. After five people were killed this week in a mass shooting in Louisville, Kentucky, six more killed two weeks ago at a school in Nashville, 2024 Republican hopefuls gathered at the NRA Convention and rejected the idea that gun restrictions could somehow limit violence -- gun violence. And the \uc2 NRA\uc2  continued to demand unlimited political allegiance from the GOP.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WAYNE LAPIERRE\uc2 , CEO, NATIONAL RIFLE ASSOCIATION: Gun-hating politicians should never go to bed unafraid of what this association and all of our millions of members can do to their political careers.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : The issue is not too many guns; the issue is too many thugs, hoodlums and savage criminals on our street.\par \par \uc2 GOV. KRISTI NOEM, (R) SOUTH DAKOTA: Little Miss Addie, who is almost two, I want to reassure you, she already has a shotgun and she already has a rifle.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And she`s got a little pony named Sparkles, too, so the girl is set up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: A Gallup poll conducted before the Louisville and Nashville shootings showed 63 percent of Americans are dissatisfied with the nation`s gun laws. But a majority of Republicans, 54 percent, are satisfied. And so far that overall dissatisfaction number hasn`t translated to the ballot box. Not a single pro-gun elected politician has been thrown out over the issue. But that`s not true on abortion. Nowhere is the gap between the Republican base and the overall general electorate greater than on this issue, particularly after the overturning of Roe V. Wade.\par \par \uc2 On Friday the Supreme Court put a temporary hold on a lower court ruling on the abortion pill, while the court considers the Biden administration`s request to preserve \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the drug. The state keeps the abortion pill widely available for now, with perhaps another decision expected by Wednesday to try to clear some of this up.\par \par \uc2 Of course, in theory, with the Dobbs decision, the court thought it was sending abortion back to the states. Clearly, that is not the case. The Dobbs decision is not popular with the public. While 61 percent of voters disapproved of the court`s decision to overturn Roe in a fall NBC News poll, 69 percent of Republicans approved of it.\par \par \uc2 Republican elected leaders are well aware of the political challenges abortion presents right now. In 2024 presidential hopefuls have struggled to answer simple questions about where they stand on abortion rights and access.\par \par \uc2 This week Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  quickly signed a six-week abortion ban before a speech at evangelical \uc2 Liberty University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [09:05:02]\par \par \uc2 But he did it behind closed doors, and then he never mentioned what he did in his speech. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has repeatedly refused to say whether he would even support a federal law that would restrict abortion. And you`ll hear Tim Scott, Nikki Haley, even \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , all of them are struggling to define their views on this issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Is it encouraging a culture of life, though to propose the death penalty for women who get abortions?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I -- I must tell you that I don`t -- I`m not familiar with those proposals, and I don`t -- I don`t -- I don`t have a way of responding to that or confirming it.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I would literally sign the most conservative pro-life legislation that they can get through Congress.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Even if it was six weeks?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I`m not going to talk about six or five or seven or 10.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOV. NIKKI HALEY, (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: I don`t want unelected justices deciding something this personal. We`re not going to let this be a political football. Let`s let the states work this out, if Congress decides to do it, but don`t get into that game of them saying "How many weeks? How many weeks?" No, let`s first figure out what we agree on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  of Wisconsin, who announced this week that she plans to run for a third term.\par \par \uc2 Senator Baldwin, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 TAMMY BALDWIN\uc2 , (D) WISCONSIN: It`s a delight to join you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with the Supreme Court`s decision to put a temporary hold on the ruling having to do with Mifepristone and the future of medicated abortion in this country. Do you think five days is enough time to -- for the Supreme Court to get all the information they need in order to make this decision?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Well, who knows how long that will take, but I`m certainly glad for the five days. But, look, what we have in Texas is a -- a judge who was not guided by science but is part of an extreme Republican concerted effort to ban abortion nationwide. And we do not need judges, politicians or government telling women about what sort of healthcare they can have.\par \par \uc2 It is an issue that is not only playing out in the court in Texas but in -- in the State of Florida, with the governor signing a near six-week ban, Idaho forbidding travel out of state for minors, Wisconsin, where we`ve gone back to literally 1849. That is the date our criminal abortion ban was passed. And that`s 174 years ago. We are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If the Supreme Court decides to allow the circuit`s ruling to hold, which would roll back \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of Mifepristone essentially to pre-2016 regulations -- if that happens, what do you want the Biden administration to do?\par \par \uc2 For instance, do you want them -- do you think they should resubmit Mifepristone to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and get a new approval?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Yeah, so we don`t know what the ruling is going to be. We know that this is a drug that was proven safe and effective back in 2000. We know there have been further \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulations and -- and the drug has been made available now in a recent ruling over the counter. And I don`t think that there should be second-guessing of the scientific-based process that \uc2 FDA\uc2  goes. It has such repercussions beyond the drug Mifepristone to -- you know, we want to be able to rely on a science-driven process that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about, sort of, federal versus state here. In Wisconsin, assuming that the newly configured Supreme Court decides that the 1800 law is essentially null and void, that it`s obsolete, it`s likely that Wisconsin`s abortion law would -- would revert to a law that was passed in the `80s that would essentially make 20 weeks, I believe, the limit on when you can get an abortion. Your bill in the Senate would be Roe v. Wade, which is essentially 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there`s a meaningful difference between 20 and 24 weeks, as this debate moves forward?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Yeah, so the Women`s Health Protection Act codifies Roe v. Wade, but it takes the additional step to tell states that they can`t pass laws that unduly limit access to that right. In Wisconsin you -- you talk about the 1980s law, but there have been laws passed in the `90s and, frankly, quite recently that further restrict a woman`s access to full reproductive healthcare, whether those are waiting periods, mandatory counseling...\par \par \uc2 [09:10:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: ... invasive ultrasounds. These are the types of state interference that the Women`s Health Protection Act would eliminate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just curious, though, how rigid are you on this?\par \par \uc2 In order to get two-thirds of what you`re hoping to get, and you could get 60 votes with 20 weeks, would you do it?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: I -- I certainly would look at that, but here is what the bill does. The bill talks about pre-viability and viability and relies on medical expertise. Because that -- that time may change as medicine advances. So we recognize that from the get-go, and I think that`s the appropriate way to do it. But I do want to get this measure passed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But you are willing to have some compromise if that was the price of getting more Republicans on board? If you could get them on board at 20 weeks rather than 24? We even heard Senator Tim Scott saying that, if 20 weeks is all he could get, looking at it through the other way, that perhaps he would support that. Is that at all...\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: I`m not...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... something worth...\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: I`m not going to negotiate...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that. Is that all something worth at least looking at?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Certainly it`s worth looking at, but I think the way to go is to have medical science establish what`s pre-viable and what`s post-viability, and that`s what the bill does.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you a couple of political questions.\par \par \uc2 Over the last two presidential elections...\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... just one U.S. senator was able to win re-election despite the presidential candidate of the same party winning their -- not winning their state. It was \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  in Maine in 2020.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think you can win re-election if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can`t carry Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Well, I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will carry Wisconsin. Obviously, we don`t know who the Republican nominee will be, but despite Republican national efforts to put the spotlight on Wisconsin by hosting their convention there and having the first Republican primary debate in Wisconsin, I think we will prevail. And I think you can look at last week`s momentum in our state supreme court race.\par \par \uc2 What people said resoundingly is we want our rights and freedoms back, and we`re willing to work hard, go to the polls and win elections in order to do that. That will not diminish in 2024. And if any Republican thinks that voters have simmered down on the abortion issue, they are wrong. It`s going to -- that is going to continue well into the next presidential race.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  is recovering from a tough bout with shingles. There has been some calls for her to resign now. Where do you stand on this?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Well, I certainly wish Senator Feinstein well and am pleased that she has made the decision to have a fill-in on her seat on the Judiciary Committee. I think that is really an important, a responsible thing to do during her absence, because we have President Biden`s nominees waiting for hearings and votes, and we want to keep that moving. But I wish her well and hope she returns to the Senate very soon.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think these calls for her to resign are appropriate?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Look, it`s up to \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  and her family to decide whether she wants to keep on serving, and she`s had a remarkable record, and I respect that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , Democrat from Wisconsin, running for a third term. I appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  of Louisiana, the top Republican on the Senate Health and Education Committee, also, by the way, a medical doctor.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cassidy, welcome back to "Meet the Press," sir.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY, (R) LOUISIANA: Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to start with the dispute over Mifepristone. You`re somebody who -- you`ve not wanted for a while this drug to be available by mail. So I`m curious, why were you not one of the 11 Republican senators to sign on to a brief supporting the Texas ruling?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I`m not quite sure what you`re asking there. I mean, I sign on briefs all day. I`m not sure that has any great importance. I think what is important is that we have a political process by which we resolve these issues. That particular ruling seems to be going through -- it was on a process-oriented thing. Among the reasons that they ruled that way, I`m told, is that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  did not follow correctly the Administrative Procedures Act.\par \par \uc2 Now, that`s a question of fact. It can be resolved by the courts. It`s actively being resolved. I think the broader question is how do you resolve the issues in general?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I think that returning it to the states to allow a state`s values to guide is very important.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:02]\par \par \uc2 In -- in Massachusetts, in New York, California, nothing changes. In my state, a law written by a Democratic female state senator and signed into law by a Democratic governor established a more pro-life statute. That`s the way I think it`s going to resolve politically, I think it`s going to resolve socially and culturally.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And on the decision on Mifepristone, do you believe that the scientific community should, sort of, take precedent here? The American College of Obstetrics -- Obstetrics and Gynecology says that Mifepristone is safe and effective. You have said their word matters when it comes to over-the-counter birth control. Should their word matter when it comes to the use of Mifepristone?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: So I think you`re going back to the kind of assertion that the principal basis of the court case in Texas was about the safety. And I think specifically it said that it was supposed to -- the accelerated pathway was supposed to approve a drug to treat an illness. It is a stretch to call a pregnancy an illness. And so -- and of course, obviously, we know what happens to the unborn baby. And so I`m not quite really sure that you`re framing this question accurately.\par \par \uc2 Now, beyond that is also a cultural and social issue. In my state we are a pro-life state. We have a legislation -- we have legislation which is far more pro-life than it is in, say, California. But the Californians keep their law and we keep our law, and that`s the way it`s going to work out nationally.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. I guess, though, on this Texas decision, it does seem like it`s going to call into question other \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals on other drugs. I mean, do you worry about the up-ending a status quo and, sort of, up-ending the FDA`s authority in a way that will be -- create, sort of, chaos in the pharmaceutical industry?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I think that`s totally alarmist. It`s totally alarmist. And, by the way, when did the \uc2 FDA\uc2  think it can go above the law? It can ignore -- it can ignore the Administrative Procedure Act, which every other agency has to follow, theoretically, but they don`t have to?\par \par \uc2 So I mean, I think that`s alarmist, and I also think that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should not be above the law.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get you to respond to something Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  said about the larger debate about Roe v. Wade and this. Take a listen. I`m curious what -- your reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2 , (R) NEW HAMPSHIRE: Roe v. Wade, whether you agreed with it or not, there was a 50-year precedent there. There`s a 20-year precedent with this thing. So now, to the American public, it looks like Republicans are coming in and trying to massively change and blow up the system and all of this sort of thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think he has a point?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: If he`s speaking about the public`s perception, I would say that be the case -- that would be the case. There was a doctor from New Jersey who was testifying before Congress and just, you know, grieving about how all of her patients were fearful. And I`m thinking, nothing`s going to change in New Jersey. She doesn`t understand the law, or if she understands it, she`s deliberately mischaracterizing it. So there is a perception issue out there.\par \par \uc2 But what Dobbs does, it returns to the states the decisions to allow the decision to be reflective of the state. In New Jersey, it doesn`t change. In Louisiana, it reflects our values -- again, a law written by a Democratic female state senator signed into law by a Democratic governor.\par \par \uc2 So I`m not quite -- so I agree with, kind of, the premise, but -- of what Governor Sununu said, but I`m, kind of, disagreeing with how you`re presenting it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you still -- are you still glad that Roe v. Wade was overturned, or do you think, in hindsight, this was -- you know, you`ve always said this is an uncomfortable -- you know, it`s hard to find a middle ground. Did it turn out Roe v. Wade was the uncomfortable middle ground for America?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I think Dobbs is the uncomfortable middle ground, where people will confront that there is a diversity of opinion, and no one group has the ability to impose their will upon the other. And -- and so, you know, Dobbs, I think, was the correct decision.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you -- are you going to vote against any attempt at creating a federal standard, considering that you believe this goes back to the states, whether it`s -- any attempt, whether it`s a 20 -- at one time, you had supported a 20-week bill. Would that ever -- would you ever be open to that, or do you think, at this point, always leave it to the states?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: First, I think that the Dobbs decision was the correct decision. You can give me theoreticals all day long. You truly can. I`m pro-life, by the way. I am pro-life. But the fact is you`re going to need 60 votes to get something through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 What \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  was just talking about, with there can be no restrictions on the states upon abortion, meaning that a child could be coming through the birth canal and still be aborted? Is that right? No, it`s wrong. And most Americans think that is wrong.\par \par \uc2 The fact is, Tammy will not get that through the -- through the Senate. There`s not 60 votes for -- for allowing that. On a bipartisan basis, it`s already been rejected. So I, kind of, reject giving me theoretical after theoretical, just because I just -- I mean, that`s what you do if you want to fill air time and get people buzzing. But it`s not how we actually govern.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- I hear you, but there seems to be a real debate here on this front. But I`d also want to talk to you about something that you have decided to touch, which is the third rail of American politics, and Social Security.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:04]\par \par \uc2 And before I ask you about it, I want to play this ad that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is running against \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on this issue. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: DeSantis has his dirty fingers all over senior entitlements, like cutting Medicare, slashing Social Security, even raising our retirement age.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: How much harder is the former president and, frankly, most now Republican elected officials who are suddenly running away from touching anything with Social Security, Medicare or Medicaid -- how much harder is this making your job of trying to convince Republicans to -- to have this conversation?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: You know, both \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and former President Trump have the same plan, which is to do nothing on Social Security and to allow a 24 percent cut to benefits from when the fund goes insolvent in about eight or nine years. They both have the same plan. It does make it harder. When your leading presidential candidates have made the decision to deceive the American people and to say there isn`t a problem, when every actuary who looks at this says that there is a problem, and someone who`s currently receiving -- 80 years old, otherwise would be in poverty, will get a 24 percent cut in their benefits by current law, it makes it very hard when they are so irresponsible. It is true for President Biden. It is true for former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you. In this effort to get reform, are you open to tax hikes? I mean, it`s going to have to be some combination of tax hikes...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... and raising -- probably the retirement age.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: What we have is a big idea. And in our big idea, we create a fund which is separate from Social Security. We put about $1.5 trillion in it, and we allow it to be invested in the nation`s economy. There`s no risk borne by the beneficiary, and there are no Social Security dollars put into this separate fund. And we allow it to sit there, and we allow it to grow. And at the end, it bridges -- helps bridge Social Security to sustainability. All the risk is borne by the fund.\par \par \uc2 Now, what you do in the additional 25 percent, that is 75 percent of what we need to do. The additional 25 percent are dials that politically we have to come together and resolve. If one side proposes something, the other side will demagogue it. We see Trump and we see Biden demagogueing it now. We need honesty with the American people. But we have a big idea to solve 75 percent of the problem, and that`s a pretty good idea.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Senator Cassidy, it is -- it is a tough issue for you to try to take a leadership position on. Good luck. We`ll be watching. And I look forward to continuing this discussion down the road. Thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Thank you, Chuck. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, is Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  rebooting a campaign that has yet to begin? The panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here, NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson. She`s also host an anchor of "Hallie Jackson Now" at NBC News; former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson; Stephen Hayes, the editor of The Dispatch; and Leigh Ann Caldwell, co-author of \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  "Early 202" and anchor of "Washington Post Live."\par \par \uc2 [09:25:10]\par \par \uc2 Hallie Jackson, I want to focus on \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  because it, I think, does get us into all of the current hand-wringing inside the GOP, whether it`s on abortion, whether it`s about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and everything. But look at these headlines. It`s astonishing to me, for a candidate that is not in the race yet, we`re talking about trying "to stop Florida Republicans from endorsing Trump," "top Republican donor sours on him over social issues," "down in the polls, The Wall Street Journal says he looks to rebound."\par \par \uc2 He`s not announced yet. This has just been an incredibly shrinking candidacy.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS ANCHOR & HOST OF "HALLIE JACKSON NOW": It will not surprise you that there are people close to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who are downplaying the headlines that you just showed here, Chuck, because they feel like it is still early days yet, although I do think that I wouldn`t be surprised -- he has alluded to, I think, to people that he`s talked to, he doesn`t want to announce anything until after the state legislative session ends. I think it will be -- my sense is it`s going to be fairly soon after that and not a long time after that, because they want to get in this race and start to push back.\par \par \uc2 I think you`re already seeing some of that coming out with these ads now, going against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the SuperPAC. You`re starting to see some of it in the way that they`re drawing some of these policy distinctions on issues like abortion because, on this, they -- I think they have a sense that what`s he going to do? He`s not going to not sign; he`s not going to veto a six-week abortion ban in Florida. And they think that, while this is maybe an issue, I think, in a primary, there is no other good option here. So of course he`s going to do it, and they`re going to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Steve Hayes, there is a Republican donor community that is desperate for a Goldilocks. They`re looking for that person that can somehow bring the Trump wing and the establishment wing behind. And DeSantis was supposed to be Goldilocks. Is the porridge too hot?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN HAYES, EDITOR, "THE DISPATCH", NBC CONTRIBUTOR: It`s a difficult balance to strike, I think. Look, there is certainly a thirst, I think, among Republican elected officials, some Republican rank-and-file voters, to move beyond \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We saw this in remarks over the weekend from Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia. You`ve seen this from top Republican donors. There`s polling that suggests this is the case with Republicans more broadly.\par \par \uc2 What`s curious about what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is doing, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has picked a fight with evangelicals, white evangelical voters, who were the core of his base, blaming them for abortion-related problems after the 2022 midterms. And there`s an obvious place for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to drive a wedge between Trump and his base...\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you think this...\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And he`s choosing not to do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh -- oh, I see. You think he was setting it up on policy but then not doing it?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As you pointed out, he`s not talking about it. He went to \uc2 Liberty University\uc2 ; he didn`t tout it; he didn`t mention it. This seems like an obvious place for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to split Trump from some of the evangelical voters who are frustrated with his comments after the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Leigh Ann, watching DeSantis strikes me -- in baseball, there`s an old term called "rabbit ears." Somebody`s` at bat and they hear all the critiques behind them. And it seems like he hears every criticism and he respond to it. "Oh, you`re not doing this enough. You`re not doing this enough. Oh, I don`t like your Ukraine response." And he`s almost responding to it all. "Oh, wait, too many abortions are happening in Florida. Oh, let me deal with that."\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, CO-AUTHOR OF THE WASHINGTON POST "EARLY 202" & ANCHOR, "WASHINGTON POST LIVE": Yeah. Well, there`s some concern among some Republicans that he`s not yet ready for prime time. That`s one of the messages that Nikki Haley is pushing, and that`s what you hear from Republican sources as well.\par \par \uc2 He`s not tested. He`s been in a bubble in Florida, not talking to the press, not engaging. And he is very popular there, but what happens when he meets a national media? What happens when he meets \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who is masterful at taking down his candidates, or his opponents?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. You know, it`s interesting, Jeh Johnson, one note of caution I throw at some people, as we`re writing off, I think, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  too quick, is about this time Barack Obama, in 2007, supporters were going, "Hey, he hasn`t caught Hillary Clinton yet; what`s wrong? He doesn`t have what it takes." You know, donors were starting to wring their hands. And then -- then the debates happened; the campaign happened, and things took place.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there`s a little bit of over-reaction?\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER SECRETARY, DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Chuck, as you know, in politics, a month is a year; a week is a month. I agree with Steve. I think DeSantis is making a very concerted effort to be the most aggressively conservative person in this field and is trying to go after Trump`s base. I am puzzled that he did not mention the six-week bill at \uc2 Liberty University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Especially at \uc2 Liberty\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I have spoken at \uc2 Liberty University\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Especially the way he did it -- like, he flew -- he quickly flew from Ohio to Florida, signed the bill, so he could get there the next day. You`re like, "Oh, that must be what you`re up to."\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I`ve spoken there, and I -- it`s -- the pro-life stance is the one big, unifying thing among the student body and the faculty at \uc2 Liberty University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, Hallie, let me show you something. Tim Scott did his, sort of, semi-rollout.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me show you two versions of Tim Scott and the abortion issue. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: If that 20-week ban reached your desk, would you sign it?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: Twenty-week ban, definitely.\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to talk about six or five or seven or 10. I`m just saying that, whatever the most conservative legislation is that can come through Congress...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Is what you would sign?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:02]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It underscores this. They don`t know how to -- what to say.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: What may be interesting to you is that there are people close to Tim Scott who are happy with the headline coming out of our colleague Ali Vitali`s interview, because they feel like the line is, he`s going to sign whatever the most conservative policy is when it comes to Congress.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They didn`t like 20 weeks the day before?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Well, and they think that whatever is going to come through Congress will have some conservative consensus. So that is going to be, I think, the line that you`ll see from him moving forward.\par \par \uc2 What`s interesting is I heard from -- on the other side of the aisle, because obviously the big issue and why we`re talking about this, is what does this mean for a general election. When you look at where the American population is, where American voters are at large.\par \par \uc2 And one Democrat phrased it to me this way: Tim Scott`s present, essentially, could be Ron DeSantis`s future, right? The idea that there is some difficulty in coming and answering some of that abortion question, I will say, doesn`t some of this, though, on DeSantis, give you 2015 vibes? All of these other candidates sniping at each other.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Scott Walker?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Not Scott Walker, but the idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is still the leader of the pack. And there has been a hesitancy so far to go after him in an aggressive and fulsome way, I think, because people are seeing where his numbers are post-indictment with the Republican primary base.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Steve, last week, though, Brendan Buck something I thought, he`s like, look, no one`s in charge of the party. And that`s the problem. There is no consensus abortion position and without it, I think the Democrats can define the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, you can make an argument that there are many places on many policies where Republicans don`t have a consensus position because it`s just whatever \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has wanted for the last six years. Look, I think part of this is also how early it is in the process. You have these candidates who are sort of quasi-candidates, right? They`ve come out. They said they want to be president. They`re not really willing to take \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on because it`s early and they`re not articulating their own policy positions. So they`re being asked to react and respond to everything else.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. When we come back, you think it`s tumultuous here in the American domestic political scene? The lone democracy in the Middle East may be even more divided than we are.\par \par \uc2 Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 As tumultuous as the American domestic political debate is these days, the lone democracy in the Middle East may be even more divided. Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu`s plan to weaken the Supreme Court in Israel has triggered nationwide protests. Meanwhile, in just a week, Israel has exchanged fire with Palestinians and Palestinian-linked groups in the occupied West Bank and in the Gaza Strip, as well as with both Lebanon and Syria.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:05]\par \par \uc2 The crises have only deepened the tensions between the United States and Israel that have been simmering a bit for months.\par \par \uc2 And I`m joined now by the Prime Minister of Israel, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Prime Minister, welcome back to "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 , ISRAELI PRIME MINISTER: Thank you, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with your judicial reform package. To say that it has gotten a negative reception might be an understatement. Your own defense minister called for a pause in it. He was concerned about it hurting Israel`s security due to so many reservists being upset about it.\par \par \uc2 The Israeli public, public approval for you has plummeted. The most recent Channel 13 poll has 71 percent disapproving of the job you are doing. Even among your own Likud Party members, more people have a negative view of your job than a positive.\par \par \uc2 And late last week, the credit agency Moody`s downgraded Israel`s economic rating due to what it said was what -- reflects a deterioration of Israel`s governance, as illustrated by the recent events around the government proposal for overhauling the country`s judiciary.\par \par \uc2 Given all of this, have you decided to rethink and pull back on some of your reform ideas?\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: Well, you loaded so many things, many of them incorrect, in your questions. And I`ll try to, how do you say that, unpack that one by one.\par \par \uc2 Okay. The first thing is that I look for a broad consensus on the judiciary. Every democracy, including your own, has continuous tension between the judiciary and the executive or the legislative.\par \par \uc2 That`s happening in America. It`s happening in France. It`s happening in Israel. It`s the natural tension of trying to keep the balance between the three branches of government, and Israel is no different.\par \par \uc2 And Israel, the last 30 years has been a growing arrogation by the Supreme Court of the powers of the executive and the legislative. We`re trying to bring it back into a proper balance, but that obviously has concerns because democracy is based on the will of the majority, the will of the people, as reflected in the government that is elected and the legislature that is elected. And protecting individual rights, the will of the majority, and the rights of the minority.\par \par \uc2 And how to balance that is something that we`re working at now. It`s not easy. I want to make sure the pendulum doesn`t swing from an all-powerful judiciary, always independent, but not always all-powerful, that overrules the government or the parliament.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, I don`t want the Parliament overruling, automatically, every decision of the Supreme Court. So I`ve decided to take a time out, try to get a balance between the opposing views, and there are strongly opposing views on both sides of our society. And I hope to reach this by consensus.\par \par \uc2 I think that`s actually the right thing to do, and I`m doing it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But do you understand why perhaps your opponents in the country just don`t take you at face value about the judicial reforms because you yourself would benefit from these reforms due to your corruption trial and the ability, perhaps, to make this either corruption trial totally go away or be delayed?\par \par \uc2 How -- how can you lead this effort if you yourself may benefit from this effort? How do you make it credible to the public?\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: Here`s another fib, another lie. It`s just not true. My -- my own legal proceedings, which by the way are crumbling, all these charges against me have been crumbling. It`s interesting that the hostile press stopped covering it, stopped covering the trial the minute it began because things are just crumbling one after the other.\par \par \uc2 But be that as it may, it`s important to understand the judges in my case have already been chosen. I`ve made it absolutely, abundantly clear that the reform will not affect any judge who today or tomorrow, in the future has to deal with my case. So my case is completely independent from this. And the thing that people have to understand is --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the Knesset did change -- they did change the incapacity law - -\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: -- a broad swathe of the public wants judicial reform -\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- right? They changed the incapacity law on the prime minister to make sure that if you`re indicted, that that wouldn`t be cause for removal.\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: No, that actually is something that --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Or, excuse me, if you`re convicted, then it wouldn`t be cause for removal.\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: No, actually, that`s not true.\par \par \uc2 The important thing is that it actually would be a cause for removal. It`s the charges that are not subject. Otherwise, you can charge anyone. You can have some politically appointed prosecutor, as Justice Jackson said, a famous American jurist, he said: The power of a politically-motivated prosecution, they can just remove anybody on anything.\par \par \uc2 And therefore, in Israel, under the unanimous decision of the Israeli Supreme Court, you cannot remove a prime minister based on charges -- on charges that can be politicized. So that hasn`t changed, and that is actually enshrined in law and in Supreme Court decisions. Look, the whole thing is -- there is an issue. It`s a real issue.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:01]\par \par \uc2 It affects many democracies sooner or later. It`s affected Israel because of the imbalance of the three branches of government. We`re trying to bring it back in balance.\par \par \uc2 But I think trying to bring it back into balance requires, at least for me as prime minister of a democratic -- a fiercely, a robustly-democratic country called Israel, to try to do it in as broad an agreement as possible. And I`m definitely giving it a shot. By the way, willing to take blows - absorb blows from my side in so doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you accept that this has taken a toll on you politically, that the public doesn`t like this? Do you accept the premise that the public is upset about this?\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: Well, some of it is upset. You wouldn`t know about the other side that seeks -- that wants to have the judicial reform because their demonstrations are not covered.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You once said two terms should be enough for an Israeli prime minister. Why did you backtrack?\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: Yeah, I said -- I didn`t backtrack. I said if you had a presidential system, you could put term limits on the prime minister. There was no elected parliamentary democracy in which you have limitations on terms of service of the prime minister.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I know there`s not there, but you yourself said --\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: You only have that in a presidential system.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You said -- you yourself said you didn`t think staying in power -- that if you can`t accomplish your goals in your first term, you might accomplish them in your second, but you don`t need any more time than that.\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: I was referring to a question on a presidential system.\par \par \uc2 But if you want to know, I think I still have, and apparently the people of Israel think I still have, many things to do. Block Iran`s quest for the bomb, expand the circle of peace with other Arab countries, forge Israel`s economy to bring it to much higher levels. I`ve made it a free market economy.\par \par \uc2 But I think we have incredible potential in all these things and perhaps arrive at a consensual judicial reform. These are four big tickets. By the way, I have another eight, but I`m not going to take your time or your audience`s time to itemize them. I have big goals.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hear you.\par \par \uc2 Very quickly, President Biden -- President Biden has weighed in, concerned about the judicial reform. Do you believe this is impacting U.S.-Israeli relations?\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: No, I don`t. I think -- I think President Biden has been a great friend of Israel. I`ve known him for 40 years, from the time he was a senator, through vice president, through president. I know his commitment to Israel`s security. Friends can have disagreements on occasion.\par \par \uc2 But I share with him the view that we should act - and not only share the view, I`m actually acting on it - to try to reach as broad consensus as possible. I want to tell you one thing, Chuck. When I compare the degree of coordination between Israel and America on security matters, on intel, on cyber, economical operations, I mean, there`s just no comparison to where this was 27 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Today it`s, I think Israel -- I always say America is Israel`s indispensable, and by far the best, ally. But I want to tell you, I don`t think you have a better ally in the world than Israel, because Israel has become a great technological power and a great asset to the United States. And our cooperation, mutual cooperation, saves a lot of American and Israeli lives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Speaking of our relationship, the damaging leaks that came out of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  and the U.S. military, one of those leaked documents indicated that \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , the Israeli version of the CIA, advocated for its members to protest the current government.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe \uc2 Mossad\uc2  opposes your government?\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: No. God, no. I think that was -- first of all, I value American intelligence a little more than that. And I think they probably know the truth.\par \par \uc2 The truth is that the \uc2 Mossad\uc2  legal advisor said that under Israeli law, junior members of \uc2 Mossad\uc2  can participate in their demonstrations, not senior members. That`s, I think, what led to this misunderstanding.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Gotcha.\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: No, I think the \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , the military, our internal security services, are working hand in hand with me as prime minister to assure the security of the country. And they`re doing a damn good job of it, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as you probably can tell, there`s a lot more to that interview. You can see my full interview with Prime Minister Netanyahu at MeetThePress.com. When we come back, gun violence has become the leading cause of death for children and teens. We`re going to break down which communities are getting hit the hardest by this epidemic.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:25]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Data Download time. In 2020, for the first time since the start of the century, gun violence surpassed car accidents to become the leading cause of death for Americans under the age of 18. So let`s take a look at how this happened and how it did not affect all kids and teens equally.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to show you this, in 2019 before the pandemic, over 1,700 kids and teens were killed by guns. By 2021, that number grew by 50 percent. It`s now over 2,500. And this impacts everyone.\par \par \uc2 But the data shows that it does not impact folks equally. In 2021, Black kids were about five times more likely to die from gunfire than White or Hispanic kids. And with a stark 11.8 gun deaths per 100,000 children and teens compared to just 2.3 deaths in White and Hispanic kids, and less than one death per 100,000 of Asian children and teens, that is a massive racial divide.\par \par \uc2 And there are also racial disparities in how young people experience gun violence. In 2021, the overwhelming majority of gun deaths among Black kids were homicides, 84 percent, while just 9 percent were suicides. For White kids, that was reversed, with just a quarter being homicides and 66 percent being suicides.\par \par \uc2 And remember, in 2021, many schools were still doing remote learning. So this data gives us a much clearer picture of gun violence in America and how youth experiences it outside of the mass school shootings that often dominate the headlines.\par \par \uc2 Before we go to break, there perhaps has been no senator more closely associated with the guns issue than \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 . And this week, the California Democrat asked to be temporarily replaced on the Judiciary Committee during her health-related absence. She first appeared on this program as the mayor of San Francisco back in 1984, when presidential candidate \uc2 Walter Mondale\uc2  was considering her as his running mate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 DIANNE FEINSTEIN\uc2  (D-CA): I`m still not seeking the position of vice president. I`ve had an opportunity now to be the first woman and the first mayor to be asked to go through an interview process. And I view that as a major opening of a door, and something that`s very important to do. It is very important that that door be open to a broad selection process, and that people, women, minorities, mayors, senators, be considered in a careful way so that the strongest possible ticket can be put together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, how did a 21-year-old national guardsman, who`s been in uniform just three and a half years, gain access to our nation`s top secrets? Panel is back after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:43]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Jeh Johnson, before you were at DHS, you were at the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How does a 21-year-old Air National Guardsman end up with -- end up with all this access to classified documents? Last time I checked, Hertz Rental Car seems to have more scrutiny of our youth when it comes to deciding who gets that car than apparently the U.S. military.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: That`s my question. How does a 21-year-old enlisted member of the Massachusetts Guard get access to TS-level information?\par \par \uc2 So it`s one of two things. Either it was part of his job to review this stuff and then he took screenshots or hit print and walked out with it, which he shouldn`t have done, obviously. Or I read somewhere he was a specialist, a technician. So he may well have had access to that level of classified information and then reached to get something he should not have read in the first place. And then walked out with it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is this a failure of imagination? I mean if -- look, you put all these on computer networks. You need some tech support. Did they not think that the IT folks might be a vulnerable -- vulnerability? Right?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Hey, don`t look for the answer here. I`m stunned by this.\par \par \uc2 And there are all these questions about, shouldn`t we clamp down? Shouldn`t we clamp down?\par \par \uc2 [09:55:01]\par \par \uc2 Shouldn`t we make it tighter?\par \par \uc2 I tend to be a little philosophical about this. You know, we have an incident like this, one in 10,000, where somebody steals something. And then you have an incident like another Boston Marathon bombing ten years ago and everyone says, why didn`t you connect the dots? Why didn`t you connect the dots?\par \par \uc2 And we want to make it broader, wider, more access. And so it`s like a pendulum. It goes back and forth. And there`s a happy medium somewhere.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Hallie, nothing that was leaked was stuff that we didn`t largely know. Meaning, like we knew Egypt had a history with Russia. Look, there were some specifics that were embarrassing to these other countries. So in some ways, there`s part of me going, oh, it`s nice to know that the intelligence, this is what it is. It`s what it looks like, and apparently this is what it is.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: And I think the sense, and in talking with a couple of folks close to the White House on this, they are sort of pointing to what, of course, President Biden said, that there was nothing contemporaneous. Not when you`re talking about the fact of the leak itself, but the underlying information that was released here. The sense is like, hey, they think people in the country largely support what the Biden administration is doing, let`s say, on Russia and Ukraine. And that this is not going to be a game changer there.\par \par \uc2 I think politically the question is how much hay is there. Although I`ve talked with some Democrats who want to frame it like, Republicans are out there defending this guy, this leaker, it`s really not all Republicans. It`s really just one elected Republican, and that`s \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that seemed to be a bizarre thing she did there.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yeah, more of a detour than a main event.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. The intel committees are going to want some pound of flesh here, aren`t they?\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, EARLY 202 CO-AUTHOR, THE WASHINGTON POST: Yeah, my sources on the intel committees in Congress are saying that this is absolutely going to be an issue. There`s going to be an all-senator classified briefing when they return this week in the middle of the week.\par \par \uc2 And the intelligence committees have a lot of questions. Not only, why do so many people have access to classified information, and what sort of fallout is there from this. They want to know, not from what President Biden says, but what the Intelligence Committee is saying, what the fallout is going to be. So this is going to be an issue. But this has also been an issue when there have been leaks before, after the Snowden issue.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: And, Jeh, you know better. I`m not sure how much change there has been in the classified complex since, after each one. But Congress is going to focus on this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, Steve, this is the challenge. Because of our ability in technology, we have more classified intelligence than ever before because we have so much signals intelligence. So we have this issue with so much incoming that does need to be sorted through and filtered.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: No, I mean, I think this is downstream from the over-classification problem, there`s no question about it. More than a million people have access to \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  top secret clearances. I mean, that`s a problem. I think it needs to be narrowed.\par \par \uc2 I don`t buy the argument from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that this is of no great consequences. And the people I talked to in the intelligence community don`t buy it either. You think about the ways that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has downplayed other issues related to national security and intelligence -- the Afghanistan withdrawal was just fine, went according to plan; there was no real intelligence problems with the China balloon collection; you talk about, on domestic policy, inflation is transitory. I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a credibility problem, generally, but I think he certainly has a credibility problem with respect to intelligence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Really quick.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I think there are those that are trying to draw a distinction, though. Nobody thinks the leak was good. Everybody thinks it was bad, right, as far as the fact of the matter of the leak itself, and then the information that it revealed.\par \par \uc2 That I agree with you. I think Congress is going to hammer this. I think when you look at it through a political, let`s say 2024 lens, I think that`s it too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I will get you on the post-game.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Could`ve been worse. It could`ve been much worse. He could`ve put out stuff that was much more damaging.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, well, we don`t know what else hasn`t been seen, that`s it.\par \par \uc2 That`s all I have for today. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230416ej4g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc16}{\*\bkmkend toc16}\b \uc2 The Battle Over Abortion Rights Is Now Headed Back To The Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Monica Alba \par \uc2 830 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [18:30:21]\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, NBC NEWS HOST: Today tonight, the battle over abortion rights is now headed back to the Supreme Court. Protesters out in force today from Los Angeles to Washington, D.C., as the fate of access to abortion pills could be decided within days.\par \par \uc2 New revelations from those leaked intel documents, reports of up to four more Chinese spy balloons and new questions about why it took authorities so long to notice the leaked documents.\par \par \uc2 Terrifying moments as a bomb is thrown at Japan`s Prime minister. He is rushed to safety before it explodes. What we know about the suspect.\par \par \uc2 A violent coup d`etat playing out right now in Africa. The American ambassador and his staff forced to take shelter.\par \par \uc2 The deadly police mistake caught on video. Officers at the wrong address. When the homeowner opens the door with a gun, they opened fire. How could this have happened?\par \par \uc2 NBC News with rare access as law enforcement busts a giant retail crime ring. Nearly $200,000 in merchandise recovered.\par \par \uc2 And there`s good news tonight. The daughter who reveals to her father after his life saving kidney transplant, she was his donor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m really proud of her. I can`t believe she did it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Jose Diaz-Balart.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Good evening. The nation`s highest court is once again wading into the country`s most divisive issue, abortion rights. Today, there were protesters outside the Supreme Court as well as rallies across the country. The past week has been a dizzying string of competing and contradictory court decisions, some restricting access to the abortion pill, others protecting it.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court now says it will take up the issue, setting a deadline just five days away. At stake, access to mifepristone, the abortion pill the most common way women and pregnancies in the U.S. The Supreme Court could rule to suspend its use nationwide. We begin tonight with Monica Alba.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we didn`t backed down.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Protests from coast to coast tonight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Standup don`t back.\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): As the fate of the nation`s most commonly used abortion pill hangs in the balance. After a week of uncertainty, the Supreme Court weighing in Friday, keeping mifepristone available for at least the next four days. The temporary action allows the full court to review emergency appeals from the Biden administration and one of the drug makers and gives the plaintiffs a group of doctors and advocacy groups until Tuesday to submit their arguments.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HAWLEY, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM SENIOR COUNSEL: It`s really time that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  was held to account for putting politics above women`s health and safety, as well as unborn lives.\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): The high court expected to rule on Wednesday as abortion rights activists expressed concern about what the conservative majority that overturned Roe v. Wade could do now.\par \par \uc2 WENDY FROSH, WASHINGTON, D.C. ABORTION RIGHTS PROTESTER: The same could happen with any other medication if we allow judges to exercise this outrageous authority or misuse of authority.\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): If the Fifth Circuit ruling is upheld, women would have to get the drug in person at the doctor`s office. It would no longer be available by mail and the window for prescription would be narrowed from 10 weeks to seven. The White House standing by the FDA`s, quote, evidence- based approval.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it`s outrageous what the court has done relative to concluding that they`re going to overrule the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): With Vice President Harris making a surprise stop at the Los Angeles women`s march Saturday, vowing to stand up and fight for abortion access.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a moment that history will show required each of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Monica joins us now from the White House. Monica, what could the Supreme Court do Wednesday?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Well, it`s unclear how the justices will rule, but it`s possible the court could extend the temporary pause, keeping things as they were before the Texas ruling, until this can play out in the lower courts. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Monica Alba, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230418ej4f000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc17}{\*\bkmkend toc17}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for April 15, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Monica Alba, Ken Dilanian, Josh Lederman, Aaron  Gilchrist, Shaquille Brewster, Kathy Park, George Solis \par \uc2 3,786 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:30:21]\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, NBC NEWS HOST: Today tonight, the battle over abortion rights is now headed back to the Supreme Court. Protesters out in force today from Los Angeles to Washington, D.C., as the fate of access to abortion pills could be decided within days.\par \par \uc2 New revelations from those leaked intel documents, reports of up to four more Chinese spy balloons and new questions about why it took authorities so long to notice the leaked documents.\par \par \uc2 Terrifying moments as a bomb is thrown at Japan`s Prime minister. He is rushed to safety before it explodes. What we know about the suspect.\par \par \uc2 A violent coup d`etat playing out right now in Africa. The American ambassador and his staff forced to take shelter.\par \par \uc2 The deadly police mistake caught on video. Officers at the wrong address. When the homeowner opens the door with a gun, they opened fire. How could this have happened?\par \par \uc2 NBC News with rare access as law enforcement busts a giant retail crime ring. Nearly $200,000 in merchandise recovered.\par \par \uc2 And there`s good news tonight. The daughter who reveals to her father after his life saving kidney transplant, she was his donor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m really proud of her. I can`t believe she did it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Jose Diaz-Balart.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Good evening. The nation`s highest court is once again wading into the country`s most divisive issue, abortion rights. Today, there were protesters outside the Supreme Court as well as rallies across the country. The past week has been a dizzying string of competing and contradictory court decisions, some restricting access to the abortion pill, others protecting it.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court now says it will take up the issue, setting a deadline just five days away. At stake, access to mifepristone, the abortion pill the most common way women and pregnancies in the U.S. The Supreme Court could rule to suspend its use nationwide. We begin tonight with Monica Alba.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we didn`t backed down.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Protests from coast to coast tonight.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Standup don`t back.\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): As the fate of the nation`s most commonly used abortion pill hangs in the balance. After a week of uncertainty, the Supreme Court weighing in Friday, keeping mifepristone available for at least the next four days. The temporary action allows the full court to review emergency appeals from the Biden administration and one of the drug makers and gives the plaintiffs a group of doctors and advocacy groups until Tuesday to submit their arguments.\par \par \uc2 ERIN HAWLEY, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM SENIOR COUNSEL: It`s really time that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  was held to account for putting politics above women`s health and safety, as well as unborn lives.\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): The high court expected to rule on Wednesday as abortion rights activists expressed concern about what the conservative majority that overturned Roe v. Wade could do now.\par \par \uc2 WENDY FROSH, WASHINGTON, D.C. ABORTION RIGHTS PROTESTER: The same could happen with any other medication if we allow judges to exercise this outrageous authority or misuse of authority.\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): If the Fifth Circuit ruling is upheld, women would have to get the drug in person at the doctor`s office. It would no longer be available by mail and the window for prescription would be narrowed from 10 weeks to seven. The White House standing by the FDA`s, quote, evidence- based approval.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think it`s outrageous what the court has done relative to concluding that they`re going to overrule the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voice-over): With Vice President Harris making a surprise stop at the Los Angeles women`s march Saturday, vowing to stand up and fight for abortion access.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a moment that history will show required each of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Monica joins us now from the White House. Monica, what could the Supreme Court do Wednesday?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Well, it`s unclear how the justices will rule, but it`s possible the court could extend the temporary pause, keeping things as they were before the Texas ruling, until this can play out in the lower courts. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Monica Alba, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The revelations keep coming from that stunning leak of highly classified documents. And there are new questions tonight about just how closely the government is watching the people tasked with protecting the nation`s top secrets. Our Justice Department correspondent Ken Dilanian has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): New information tonight about the Chinese spy balloon shot down earlier this year. The details coming from the classified documents allegedly leaked by 21-year-old national guard airman Jack Teixeira, arrested by federal authorities this week. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " citing documents not seen by NBC News, reports that Chinese spy balloon carried sensors and antennas. The U.S. still had not identified more than a week after shooting it down, and that the U.S. knew of up to four additional Chinese spy balloons.\par \par \uc2 [18:35:16]\par \par \uc2 The paper also reporting on Taiwan`s vulnerability to a Chinese attack, citing a Pentagon assessment that China would quickly gain air superiority if it invaded Taiwan. U.S. Secretary of State Blinken today addressed fallout from the leaked documents.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We have engaged with our allies and partners since these leaks came out, and we have done so at high levels, and we have made clear our commitment to safeguarding intelligence and our commitment to our security partnerships.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN (voice-over): There are also new insights tonight into why the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  didn`t know for months that state secrets had been posted online. At issue, the limits on what kind of checks are done on the 1.3 million Americans with top secret security clearance.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM EVANINA, FMR. NATIONAL COUNTERINTELLIGENCE & SECURITY CENTER DIRECTOR: Right now, there is not a social media evaluation done. There`s not an appetite for that. But what they do online is not evaluated by any agency as part of their security clearance.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN (voice-over): Former counter intel chief Bill Evanina says he pushed for more social media monitoring, but privacy concerns won out. Critics say the government now can`t keep track of all the people with access to sensitive information.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Does the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  have a good handle on who these people are, what they`re doing online, whether they hold extremist views, that kind of thing?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, clearly the answer is no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Ken Dilanian joins me now. Ken, what`s next for the suspected leaker?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Airman Jack Teixeira faces a detention hearing Wednesday, Jose, where the government is likely to ask to keep him in jail. Then, he`ll have to decide whether to fight the charges or cut a plea deal that likely could carry a lengthy prison sentence. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Ken Dilanian in Washington, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Some frightening moments in Japan with the nation`s Prime Minister rushed to safety after someone threw an explosive device in his direction during a campaign stop. The attack coming less than a year after former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  was assassinated. Josh Letterman has the very latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER (voice-over): The crowd reacts the moment the explosive device is thrown, taking the attacker to the ground. Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  immediately evacuated just before the explosion and the smoky aftermath, Kishida safe and unharmed. Authorities telling Japanese media, the suspect threw an eight to 12-inch pipe, likely a smoke bomb, that landed near the Prime Minister. This witness says the object was silver colored and heavy, made of metal.\par \par \uc2 The suspect, a young man seen with a black and gray backpack, struggling with police as he`s carried away. Prime Minister Kishida continued with campaign events later in the day, even apologizing for causing concern. He says we must carry this on together until the end. Tonight, Japan once again traumatized by political violence. The attack bringing back memories of the fatal shooting of former Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  in July, also at a campaign event.\par \par \uc2 While authorities investigate the motive, the suspect is reportedly not speaking with police as tonight security measures for dignitaries are being stepped up.\par \par \uc2 Josh Lederman, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: A dangerous situation unfolding in Africa at this hour with the U.S. Ambassador to Sudan, the embassy team and others in the capital sheltering in place after rebel forces unleashed a violent assault across the country. Aaron Gilchrist is tracking it all.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 AARON GILCHRIST, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, the East African nation of Sudan smoldering in chaos, a deadly coup killing at least 25 and injuring hundreds, the Sudanese Doctors Union telling Reuters. The country`s paramilitary rapid support services taking to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , claiming control of the Army`s headquarters, public radio and television and air navigation throughout the country. It also announced a manhunt for the Army`s commander.\par \par \uc2 The battles leaving smoke billowing from commercial planes at the capital city`s airport. Inside, people fell to the floor trying to take cover as shots erupted on the streets. The fighting even disrupting a Sudanese T.V. broadcast. All this as the U.S. Ambassador to Sudan tweeted he and his staff had to take shelter inside the embassy in Khartoum, the clashes prompting the U.S. to join other world and Sudanese civilian leaders in calls for an end to the violence.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: It`s a fragile situation. This is a real opportunity to finally carry forward the civilian led transition.\par \par \uc2 GILCHRIST (voice-over): For months, tensions have been rising as Sudan transitions to democracy. Disagreement lingering about how the military factions would integrate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Here at home, an evacuation order has been issued for parts of Georgia after a massive fire reignited at a plastic resin plant in \uc2 Glenn County\uc2 . The fire first broke out early this morning and was contained before reigniting later in the day. There are no reports of injuries.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:11]\par \par \uc2 Growing questions in New Mexico tonight after a call for help went horribly wrong. Body cam captured police showing up at the wrong house and then fatally shooting the homeowner. Shaquille Brewster has details. A caution in some of the video is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHAQUILLE BREWSTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, new body camera video capturing the fatal police shooting of a homeowner in New Mexico. The videos from April 5th show three Farmington officers responding to a domestic violence report around 11:30 p.m. identifying themselves as police three times.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Farmington police.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): But the department says officers went to the wrong address.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It might have been 4308.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Was it 43 or 53?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, it might have been 5308.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): Fifty-two-year-old Robert Dotson opens his front door wearing a robe, investigators say pointing a gun at police.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hands up.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): All three officers fired their weapons.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m good. I`m good. I`m good.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): Dotson died at the scene.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: One down at the doorway.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): His wife is heard screaming inside.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Please don`t.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): Appearing to open the door when police say she begins firing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get her in cuffs.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): Minutes later, she surrendered.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Where`s the gun at?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He dropped it and she picked it up and pointed it at us.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): Upstairs are two children heard on a 911 call released by the Farmington Police Department.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There were gunshots in the house and my dad is shot.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): The police chief offered his condolences the next day.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HEBBE, FARMINGTON, NEW MEXICO POLICE CHIEF: Mr. Dotson was not the subject of the call that our officers were responding to. And this ending is just unbelievably tragic. I`m extremely sorry that we are in this position.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): The department is not naming the three officers who are now on paid administrative leave.\par \par \uc2 CARMEN BEST, NBC NEWS LAW ENFORCEMENT ANALYST: BREWSTER (voice-over): It is a tragedy, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): Former Police Chief Carmen Best, who watched the video, says the shooting appears to be consistent with police training.\par \par \uc2 BEST: I would not expect my officer to have a gun pointed in their direction and not react.\par \par \uc2 BREWSTER (voice-over): A reaction stemming from a mistaken address now being investigated by state police.\par \par \uc2 Shaquille Brewster, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Still ahead, ten years after the shattering events of the Boston Marathon bombing, the survivors who never backed down.\par \par \uc2 Also, a rare inside look as law enforcement takes down giant retail theft rings. Look at all the things they recovered.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:45:15]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: A day of hope and healing in Boston, with the city marking 10 years since a horrific marathon bomb attack that claimed the lives of three people and injured hundreds more are. Kathy Park takes us inside the survivor`s decade long road to recovery.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): A powerful tribute today as thousands gathered at the Boston Marathon finish line, a place so many have raced through. But today, everyone was still. Here 10 years ago, an unthinkable attack.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we heard the two explosions. And my family and I knew something was wrong right away.\par \par \uc2 PARK (voice-over): Back to back explosions that killed three people and injured hundreds more. This was Michelle Blackburn 10 years ago, cheering on a friend. The blast nearly took her leg.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BLACKBURN, BOSTON MARATHON BOMBING SURVIVOR: I think that anytime you go through a trauma, it`s very fluid. They spent many years just avoiding it altogether.\par \par \uc2 PARK (voice-over): And this is Michelle today, training to run this year`s marathon until a recent injury took her out for now.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: But I always say forward is the only way forward. So that`s what we`re doing. It`s nothing that`s going to preclude me from running again.\par \par \uc2 JEFFERSON DRISCOLL, BOSTON MARATHON BOMBING WITNESS: I actually knew them.\par \par \uc2 PARK (voice-over): Jefferson Driscoll was 10 when the bombing happened. He grew up with the family of Martin Richard, the youngest victim killed at age eight. Now 20, Jefferson is running his first marathon.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What inspired you?\par \par \uc2 DRISCOLL: The Richard family, their resilience, their kindness, their peace. It`s been amazing. You learn endurance and it teaches you Boston is one family, one big, strong family.\par \par \uc2 PARK (voice-over): Boston strong still on full display a decade later.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Park, NBC News, Boston.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: We are back in a moment with rare access as police track and catch the suspects behind a massive retail theft ring.\par \par \uc2 And life-saving surprise, a father battling for his life learns of the incredible sacrifice made by his daughter.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:03]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: A retail theft is on the rise across America. You`ve surely seen the videos of brazen thieves smashing windows and stealing merchandise. Well, police now and the stores themselves are working together to catch them. George Solis, now with a rare inside look at one operation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you want me to do with it?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE SOLIS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): As the radio calls come in.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK. All right. Copy.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): \uc2 California Highway Patrol\uc2  squads close in. The drama unfolding just before dusk in this Southern California neighborhood. Months of surveillance into an organized retail crime ring.\par \par \uc2 LT. ADAM SMITH, \uc2 CALIFORNIA HIGHWAY PATROL\uc2 : One was going into the stores on multiple occasions over the past three months, gathering as much items as they could.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): All leading up to this. Three suspects arrested on suspicion of organized retail theft, according to law enforcement. This is \uc2 California Highway Patrol\uc2 `s specialized organized retail crime task force in action recovered a handyman`s paradise worth of high end tools.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What was the dollar figure that was recovered in tools yesterday?\par \par \uc2 CAPT. JEFF LOFTIN, \uc2 CALIFORNIA HIGHWAY PATROL\uc2 : It was about $150,000.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): According to the \uc2 CHP\uc2 , this case involved two brothers who repeatedly stole power tools from Home Depots across Southern California and their fences, or the third party they were selling to.\par \par \uc2 LOFTIN: They were concentrating on Milwaukee, DeWitt. You see over here, Makita.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): This high stakes operation, first flagged to the task force by \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , a partnership between law enforcement and major stores to combat the rampant rise of organized retail crime.\par \par \uc2 LOFTIN: As long as we let these people get away with what they`re doing, you and I are going to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): Home Depot`s own investigators were on site to ID the stolen items.\par \par \uc2 RORY STALLARD, HOME DEPOT WESTERN DIVISION AP INVESTIGATION SENIOR MANAGER: That makes a huge difference when we prosecute the case, because now everything`s under one umbrella. We`re dealing with one prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): Organized retail crime is different than shoplifting. The goal is to resell stolen items for a profit, resulting in tens of billions of dollars lost by retailers each year, many instances caught on camera nationwide, from smash and grabs at luxury stores to dragging entire displays out the door.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): This is not just one battery charger. This is a whole kit of them.\par \par \uc2 LOFTIN: Right, entire boxes.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): Since its creation in 2019, the Highway Patrols` unit has recovered more than $28 million worth of stolen goods. Because of that, California Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2  has made fighting organized retail crime a priority even across state lines.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA, CALIFORNIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: We will hold you accountable. We will bring felony charges against you.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): The crime surge forcing retailers to drastically increase security.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  deploys a number of security measures to ensure their products stay safe from security cameras that watch your every move to products that are secured and locked in place.\par \par \uc2 STALLARD: We call it hardening the target.\par \par \uc2 SOLIS (voice-over): If the thieves manage to thwart these growing security measures, the task force says they won`t get far.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We`re going to be following you, and we`re going to catch you.\par \par \uc2 George Solis, NBC News, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: When we come back, the amazing moment, this young girl sees the world like never before.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:55:53]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: There`s good news tonight about the sacrifices we make for the ones we love.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): This is a magical moment the Scarborough family in Tucson, Arizona, will never forget.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Casey.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): That`s Henry and Maraena`s baby girl Ireland putting on glasses for the very first time. Now clearly seen, she`s surrounded by so much love. There were other unforgettable moments too. Here`s army major Eric Cusick, a few weeks back, surprising his kids at their schools in New York after a 10-month mission overseas, a homecoming that means so much to this military family.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): And in St. Louis --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): That`s John Ivanowski, shocked to find out his daughter Delaney was his kidney donor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s fine.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): It`s a secret she kept for months preparing for the procedure when his kidney disease got worse. The gift of life, helping him heal.\par \par \uc2 JOHN IVANOWSKI: I think about it every day. She`s my hero. I`ll take care of her the rest of my days here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I need you to read something for me. OK.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): Across the country, students were celebrating good news like Stephen Russell.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN RUSSELL, STUDENT: That you are to be admitted.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): The multi sports star with Special Olympics, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Here it goes.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): Overwhelmed when he found out he got into \uc2 Kennesaw State University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Way to go Stephen.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): But this was the moment that moved everyone to tears. That`s South K High School Senior Joshua Alvarado in Los Angeles, surprised with a $50,000 scholarship. He plans to study engineering at Stanford. Joshua embraced by his mom, Erine (ph), and his two sisters. The moment bittersweet. That`s because his dad, Mario, died five years ago. Joshua prayed to him after the celebration.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What did you tell him?\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA ALVARADO, SENIOR HIGH SCHOOL STUDENT: I told him that I hope he`s watching.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: He`s proud. No doubt. You should know that.\par \par \uc2 ALVARADO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART (voice-over): The family has overcome struggles, even homelessness. As a first generation son of Mexican immigrants, Joshua hopes his story inspires others.\\\par \par \uc2 (on camera): How would you define the American dream?\par \par \uc2 ALVARADO: The American dream to me, has always been reaching for something more. That belief that there`s better out there and that it`s within your reach if you just work hard enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: That`s NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for this Saturday. I`m Jose Diaz- Balart. Thank you for the privilege of your time. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230416ej4f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc18}{\*\bkmkend toc18}\b \uc2 The Supreme Court Putting A Temporary Hold On A Lower Court Ruling That Would Limit Access To An Abortion Pill Keeping It Widely Available For Now\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Laura Jarrett \par \uc2 509 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: A late and dramatic development in the battle over that abortion pill. Late today, the Supreme Court stepping in and putting a temporary hold on a lower court ruling that would limit access. Laura Jarrett has details for us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  moving quick to block a federal judge from rolling back access to the abortion pill mifepristone at least for now. Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  handing down an order within hours of the Justice Department`s emergency appeal that had warned of chaos and profound disruption if the high court didn`t step in.\par \par \uc2 At issue in the case, a legal morass of complex \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulations over the drug that had recently been made easier to obtain.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They attacked medication that for 20 years the \uc2 FDA\uc2  ruled as being safe.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: But the advocacy groups and doctors that sued to ban the drug claim the \uc2 FDA\uc2  ignored safety risks. Those plaintiffs now have until Tuesday to submit their legal arguments to the justices. At the same time, access to abortion now largely cut off throughout much of the south.\par \par \uc2 After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v Wade, many women traveled to Florida for abortions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Show the bill passes.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Now, the state has banned anyone from seeking an abortion after six weeks of pregnancy though the law won`t go into effect right away.\par \par \uc2 FENTRICE DRISKELL (D-FL), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: You just don`t want women to have choice.\par \par \uc2 CHASE TRAMONT (R-FL), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Here we value life from conception to the casket.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Governor Ron DeSantis signing the bill with a private gathering overnight, a far cry from the jubilant celebration he put on for a 15-week ban passed last spring highlighting a rising tension within the GOP on the issue. As some are mindful of the public polling largely favoring abortion, other presidential hopefuls are leaning into their conservative base with eyes on 2024, including Senator Tim Scott who spoke exclusively to NBC`s Ali Vitali today.\par \par \uc2 TIM SCOTT (R-SC): If I were President of the United States, I would literally sign the most conservative pro-life legislation that they can get through Congress.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: But for now, the issue of abortion access rest with the Supreme Court, with the justices poised to rule next week. Laura Jarrett, NBC News\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230418ej4e0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc19}{\*\bkmkend toc19}\b \uc2 The Battle Over Access To Abortion Pills Headed To The Supreme Court\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Laura Jarrett \par \uc2 571 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Now to the escalating fight over access to an abortion pill. The Justice Department says it will now ask the Supreme Court for emergency action to stop new restrictions from taking effect. Laura Jarrett has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): A fierce battle over abortion now headed to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . The Attorney General vowing to challenge a ruling from a federal appeals court that could jeopardize access to mifepristone, a commonly used abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We believe that the law is on our side.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Overnight, that appeals court blocked a portion of a Texas ruling that invalidated the approval of mifepristone, allowing the drug to remain on the market for now. But the judges also rolled back several \uc2 FDA\uc2  moves in recent years that made the drug easier to obtain. If the Supreme Court doesn`t step in, women may soon be required to visit a doctor`s office in person to receive the pill instead of taking it at home. It will no longer be available through the mail.\par \par \uc2 The generic version also off the table. And the pill will only be approved through seven weeks of pregnancy instead of 10.\par \par \uc2 DR. LAURA MACISAAC, OBSTETRICIAN-GYNECOLOGIST, MOUNT SINAI: Having more in- person visits means women will have to take time off of work, get childcare often during the work week. If the medication can`t get into the hands of the people that need it quickly, then it isn`t available.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Doctors like Laura McIsaac who practice for over three decades alarmed by the ruling.\par \par \uc2 MACISAAC: That will absolutely increase pregnancy complications and increase maternal mortality.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Restricting access to mifepristone at the heart of the original Texas lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MORROW HAWLEY\uc2 , SENIOR COUNSEL, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has basically sanctioned mail-order abortions.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Erin Hawley represents the advocacy groups and doctors that sued the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAWLEY: The Fifth Circuit`s decision is a huge victory for women and for the rule of law. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  did not have a valid basis for approving that drug.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: While the fight continues in the courts, tonight, a clash at the State House. In Florida, anger erupting on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Show the bill passes.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: As the Republican-controlled legislature passed a six-week abortion ban headed to the governor`s desk for signature soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Laura, when do all these restrictions take effect?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (on camera): So, those restrictions should begin early Saturday. I say should because tonight a federal judge in a different case in Washington State reiterated that the Biden administration must not make any changes to the availability of this drug. The government will likely tell the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  it simply cannot comply with all these conflicting rulings.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230414ej4d0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc20}{\*\bkmkend toc20}\b \uc2 The Battle Over An Abortion Pill: Big Pharma Pushing Back Against The Ruling As Some States Start Stockpiling Drugs\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Laura Jarrett \par \uc2 600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Major backlash tonight from the drug industry to that abortion pill ruling by a federal judge. And now, some states are stockpiling abortion drugs as the legal battle plays out. Laura Jarrett has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): A show of force from big pharma now waiting into the battle over a widely used abortion pill. More than 600 drug makers and biotech leaders signing on to a scathing condemnation of that ruling by a federal judge in Texas invalidating the FDA`s longtime approval of mifepristone, accusing the judge of activism.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMANDA BANKS, FORMER CEO, BLACKFYNN THERAPEUTICS: Now -- it`s now been shown that courts can step in and overturn a scientific decision.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Amanda Banks, a former CEO of Blackfynn Therapeutics, who co-authored the letter says the judge`s ruling sets a dangerous precedent that extends beyond the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 BANKS: If one medicine is subject to this kind of outcome, really any medicine is.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: President Biden also slamming the decision today.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My thoughts are completely out of bounds what the judge did.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: While the anti-abortion group that filed the Texas lawsuit is doubling down. Writing in a statement, "The \uc2 FDA\uc2  failed to abide by its legal obligations to protect the health, safety, and welfare of girls and women."\par \par \uc2 With the Texas order is set to go into effect early Saturday unless blocked in the courts, Democratic governors are not wasting any time taking matters into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): We`re taking a close look at all of our options.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Massachusetts, Washington State, New York, and California all now stockpiling years` worth of mifepristone or an alternative abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): So, I`m proud to announce that New York State will create a stockpile of misoprostol, another form of medication abortion.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: It`s all in preparation for a decision that would apply nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Laura, where does all these stand right now as far as the courts are concerned?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (on camera): So, late tonight and tomorrow, we should see more court filings from both the plaintiffs that filed the Texas lawsuit and the Justice Department, which is asking the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals to freeze that judge`s order. Once all those are in midday tomorrow, we could see a decision from that appeals court anytime.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura Jarrett, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the tough new auto emission standard is about to be announced. Will they push more Americans toward electric vehicles. Plus, spring is here and already setting some record temperatures in a lot of cities, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Spring is here but much of the country is seeing record highs temperatures in places like Phoenix, and Las Vegas, even South Dakota hit the 90s. Denver broke its record and temperatures are expected to continue to climb this week bringing record highs to the northeast.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230412ej4b0002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc21}{\*\bkmkend toc21}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for April 11, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Gabe Gutierrez, Maggie Vespa, Andrea Mitchell,  Garrett Haake, Laura Jarrett, Tom Costello, John Torres, Steve Patterson \par \uc2 3,839 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the terrifying images just released of a Louisville mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Police body camera video showing how officers confronted the gunman who police say ambush them after shooting and killing five of his fellow bank workers using an AR-15 he bought legally. One officer shot at and getting back off to reengage. Officers being hailed as heroes for stopping him from killing more people. Were there warning signs and what his neighbors are saying?\par \par \uc2 Damage control. New revelations from those leaked classified intelligence documents putting U.S. alliances in question.\par \par \uc2 The Manhattan district attorney sues a top House Republican claiming interference in the investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The battle over an abortion pill. Big pharma pushing back against the ruling as some states start stockpiling drugs.\par \par \uc2 Tough new auto emission standards about to be announced, but will they push more Americans to go electric.\par \par \uc2 A big upside of artificial intelligence detecting cancer much earlier and predicting when it might strike.\par \par \uc2 And the young bold athletes sharing their culture and moves with skateboarding superstar \uc2 Tony Hawk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome. Once again in the tragic aftermath of a mass shooting, we`re getting a heart-stopping first person look at police risking their own lives to stop and active shooter. Late this afternoon, Louisville police releasing body cam video of officers rushing to a downtown bank yesterday. Immediately coming under fire from a bank employee. He was armed with an AR-15 assault-style rifle carrying out a murderous rampage, fatally shooting five people. The gunman killed by police.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, one of those officers who rushed to stop the gunman, a rookie just days on the streets remains in critical condition. And tonight, there is a deeper understanding of just how close to home these shootings are for many Americans. A new survey out tonight finding one in five U.S. adults say they have been threatened with a gun.\par \par \uc2 Maggie Vespa leads us off tonight with new details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Echoes of gunfire as officers speed up to the scene.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, a firsthand look at police say seven minutes of horror playing out inside a Kentucky bank. Police body camera and cell phone video showing Louisville Metro officers view Monday morning as they race to confront a lone gunman. One taking gunfire as windows shatter, falling, then jumping right back up. Minutes later, killing the shooter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think he`s down.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: What was it like to watch that body camera video?\par \par \uc2 JACQUELYN GWINN-VILLAROEL, INTERIM CHIEF, LOUISVILLE METRO POLICE: It is really -- it shakes the core. It shakes the core to know that life was being lost.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Investigators say Connor Sturgeon seen here on surveillance cameras was armed with an AR-15 rifle which he bought legally from a local dealership six days before the massacre, adding the 25-year-old was a current employee at \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  and knew the colleagues he targeted. Overnight, officials confirming a fifth victim died, 57-year-old Deanna Eckert.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, dispatch audio indicating the shooter may have tried to warn a friend before the attack.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The shooter, 25-year-old white male Connor Sturgeon, 6`4. He texted a friend -- called a friend, left voicemail saying he`s going to kill everyone at the bank, feeling suicidal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We know he left a note. We know he texted or called at least one person to let them know he was suicidal and contemplating harm.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Neighbors of surgeons home which investigators searched yesterday shocked by the news.\par \par \uc2 KERA ALLGEIER, NEIGHBOR OF THE GUNMAN: I can`t say nothing really bad about the guy. He`s very quiet, soft spoken. I just don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Doctors also struggling.\par \par \uc2 DR. JASON SMITH, CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER, UNIVERSITY OF LOUISVILLE HEALTH: There`s only so many times you can walk into a room and tell someone they`re not coming home tomorrow. And it just breaks your heart. When you hear someone screaming mommy or daddy, it just becomes too hard.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Tonight, three remain hospitalized, including officer Nickolas Wilk still in critical condition. The chief adding Wilk was on his fourth shift less than two weeks out of the academy. His twin brother just started. Also wounded with minor injuries, officer Corey Galloway, who investigators say killed the shooter.\par \par \uc2 MONESSA BARD, MOTHER OF JULIANA FARMER: I can`t hear her voice anymore. I can`t touch her. I can`t tell her how much I love her.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: The details do nothing to numb Monessa Bard`s pain. Her 45-year-old daughter Juliana Farmer among the dead. Amid a successful career in banking, she was just recruited back to Louisville and \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  three weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:11]\par \par \uc2 How do you make sense of that? She just got back there.\par \par \uc2 BARD: There is no sense to be had. I just feel like that man stole her from us. I don`t understand his reasoning. I don`t understand why she`s gone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Maggie, officials I know are applauding the heroism displayed by police even after the shooter was down.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Yes, Lester, that`s right. They tell us here yesterday, EMS agencies were actually short-staffed, so a Louisville police lieutenant improvised, jumping in the front seat of an ambulance and driving it to the hospital allowing EMS workers in the back to keep caring for a wounded patient. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Maggie Vespa in Louisville, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And there was yet another deadly mass shooting today, this one in Washington D.C., as a new survey shows just how much Americans are affected by gun violence. Here`s Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): As a funeral for a shooting victim wrapped up in D.C. today, gunfire erupted again. One man killed, three others hurt. From workplace violence to school shootings, according to a new survey from the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 , a majority of U.S. adults have either personally or had a family member who has been impacted by a gun-related incident, including suicide.\par \par \uc2 Nearly one in five Americans have lost a family member to gun violence with people of color disproportionately impacted.\par \par \uc2 So, is this about mental health or is it about easy access to guns?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE ROWE-ADAMS, FOUNDER, HARLEM MOTHER S.A.V.E.: It`s about both of them.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Jackie Rowe-Adams lost two sons, Anthony and Tyrone, to gun violence. She now runs a support group in Harlem.\par \par \uc2 ROWE-ADAMS: When I hear about this constant shooting, it pains me. It hurt so bad because I know another family, I know the mother, father, family is hurting for the loss of their loved one.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: As the nationwide debate over access to guns heats up a day after the mass shooting in Louisville, the city`s mayor fumed over a state law that requires confiscated firearms like the rifle the killer used on Monday to be resold at auction.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GREENBERG, MAYOR OF LOUISVILLE, KENTUCKY: It`s time to change this law and let us destroy illegal guns and destroy the guns that have been used to kill our friends and kill our neighbors.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: While in Tennessee, Republican Governor Bill Lee today called for stronger background checks for gun purchases.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BILL LEE (R-TN): The executive order will for one thing set a 72-hour clock for reporting new criminal activity.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Other states like Maryland, Washington, and Colorado are moving forward with new restrictions as gun rights advocates push back on what they consider unconstitutional overreach.\par \par \uc2 DUDLEY BROWN, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL ASSOCIATION FOR GUN RIGHTS: No amount of gun control is going to stop a criminal hell bent on murder from gaining some tools to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The Kaiser survey also showed that about a third of adults have avoided large crowds due to the possibility of gun violence. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, U.S. officials are scrambling to control the damage from the disclosure of intelligence secrets after dozens of classified documents were leaked online. Andrea Mitchell now with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, those leaked documents causing tough conversations between Washington and U.S. allies. On Ukraine, a U.S. intelligence assessment that Ukraine`s military will fall well short of retaking most of the territory it`s lost to Russia according to a document obtained by the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And a document seen by NBC News revealing Ukrainian agents launched drone attacks against targets in Russia and its ally Belarus, including just outside Moscow despite promising the U.S. it would not strike Russia. Secretary of State Blinken spoke with Ukraine`s Foreign Minister today.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned about cross-border attacks?\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Ukraine has to make decisions about how it can most effectively defend itself against Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Another leaked documents says Egypt`s President El-Sisi recently ordered 40,000 rockets to be produced for Russia according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . Egypt denies it. Since Camp David in 1978, Egypt has received more than $50 billion in military aid and $30 billion in economic aid from the U.S. more than any other country in the Middle East except Israel. How could intelligence officials not know sooner about the leaks?\par \par \uc2 Sir, why didn`t you know about it before April 6? That means that they were up for at least many weeks if not months before U.S. intelligence knew that they were in the public domain.\par \par \uc2 GEN. LLOYD AUSTIN, U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: They were somewhere in the web. And in where exactly and who had access at that point, we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The Pentagon is now in damage control, limiting the number of people who can see such highly classified documents. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea Mitchell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In New York, the Manhattan D.A. prosecuting former President Trump is suing the House Judiciary Chairman Jim Jordan. Garrett Haake joins me now from Capitol Hill. Garrett, explain what this means.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:10]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Lester, Manhattan D.A. \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2  has asked a federal judge to block a House Judiciary Committee subpoena for a former top prosecutor in his office who`s being ordered to testify next week. Now, Bragg also asked the judge to void any subpoenas that might be filed against him or his current staff.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic D.A. argues that Congress has no power to supervise the state criminal prosecutions and the efforts by Republican Committee Chairman Jim Jordan to force testimony or acquired documents related to his prosecution of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are purely political with no legislative purpose.\par \par \uc2 Jordan responding tonight in part in a tweet that reads, first, they indict a president for no crime, then they sued to block congressional oversight. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Garrett Haake, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Major backlash tonight from the drug industry to that abortion pill ruling by a federal judge. And now, some states are stockpiling abortion drugs as the legal battle plays out. Laura Jarrett has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): A show of force from big pharma now waiting into the battle over a widely used abortion pill. More than 600 drug makers and biotech leaders signing on to a scathing condemnation of that ruling by a federal judge in Texas invalidating the FDA`s longtime approval of mifepristone, accusing the judge of activism.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMANDA BANKS, FORMER CEO, BLACKFYNN THERAPEUTICS: Now -- it`s now been shown that courts can step in and overturn a scientific decision.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Amanda Banks, a former CEO of Blackfynn Therapeutics, who co-authored the letter says the judge`s ruling sets a dangerous precedent that extends beyond the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 BANKS: If one medicine is subject to this kind of outcome, really any medicine is.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: President Biden also slamming the decision today.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My thoughts are completely out of bounds what the judge did.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: While the anti-abortion group that filed the Texas lawsuit is doubling down. Writing in a statement, "The \uc2 FDA\uc2  failed to abide by its legal obligations to protect the health, safety, and welfare of girls and women."\par \par \uc2 With the Texas order is set to go into effect early Saturday unless blocked in the courts, Democratic governors are not wasting any time taking matters into their own hands.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): We`re taking a close look at all of our options.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Massachusetts, Washington State, New York, and California all now stockpiling years` worth of mifepristone or an alternative abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): So, I`m proud to announce that New York State will create a stockpile of misoprostol, another form of medication abortion.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: It`s all in preparation for a decision that would apply nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Laura, where does all these stand right now as far as the courts are concerned?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (on camera): So, late tonight and tomorrow, we should see more court filings from both the plaintiffs that filed the Texas lawsuit and the Justice Department, which is asking the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals to freeze that judge`s order. Once all those are in midday tomorrow, we could see a decision from that appeals court anytime.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura Jarrett, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the tough new auto emission standard is about to be announced. Will they push more Americans toward electric vehicles. Plus, spring is here and already setting some record temperatures in a lot of cities, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Spring is here but much of the country is seeing record highs temperatures in places like Phoenix, and Las Vegas, even South Dakota hit the 90s. Denver broke its record and temperatures are expected to continue to climb this week bringing record highs to the northeast.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is expected to roll out strict new emission standards that will require automakers to accelerate the transition from gas to electric, but are car makers and drivers ready? Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): They`re among America`s fastest-selling cars. Average price, an eye-popping $58,000. Electric vehicles, EVs make up seven percent of new car sales. Now, the Biden administration wants to accelerate the green revolution with tougher emission standards and industry sources say requiring that EVs make up at least 54 percent of automakers new car sales by 2030, two-thirds of sales by 2032.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:18]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Running on a cleaner grid, making and inventing the technologies that are going to power the future.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Automakers are already investing billions to transition from gas to electric. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARY BARRA\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 GENERAL MOTORS\uc2 : The quicker we can bring everyone along to EVs, the better it`s going to be for climate.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: We`ve seen the Green Revolution firsthand from the assembly line --\par \par \uc2 3000 pounds of lithium-ion cells helping the Hummer to accelerate from zero to 60 in three seconds.\par \par \uc2 -- to the frontline.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, they want to take their whole fleet electric.\par \par \uc2 Just two years ago, automakers agreed that EVs would make up half their sales by 2030. Now, the industry says the new White House goals aren`t realistic, writing in a statement, this requires a massive 100-year change to the U.S. industrial base and the way Americans drive.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE KREBS, \uc2 COX AUTOMOTIVE\uc2 : And there are a lot of things that have to happen to get us to a full EV.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Including raw materials, charging stations, and the electric grid to power it. For now, sticker shock may convince many Americans to stick with their gas guzzlers. Tom Costello, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, we`ll take a look at how artificial intelligence is already making a difference in detecting cancer.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with our series A.I. revolution and the amazing advances being made in artificial intelligence technology. Tonight, Dr. John Torres shows us how A.I. is a growing weapon in the battle against cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOHN TORRES, NBC NEWS SENIOR MEDICAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): John Roberts is lucky to be alive.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, HAD CANCER: No one ever thinks they`re at risk for developing lung cancer.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: During the treatment for a kidney stone, doctors found a mass in his lung and were able to operate before the cancer spread.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: They would never have found it, and in all likelihood, I would not be here talking with you today.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: The CDC recommends people at high risk for the disease get screened with a low dose CT scan. But even with regular screening, the most skilled radiologists can`t spot everything.\par \par \uc2 DR. LECIA SEQUIST, LUNG CANCER MEDICAL ONCOLOGIST, MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL: This looks normal to you. You don`t see a cancer here.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: How often are we missing lung cancer diagnoses right now?\par \par \uc2 SEQUIST: Well, most people who get diagnosed with lung cancer unfortunately present with stage four metastatic disease because it has spread someplace that is causing them a pain or a symptom, and then they go to their doctor. So, we`re missing most cases of early-stage lung cancer when people don`t have symptoms.\par \par \uc2 The possibilities are limitless.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Dr. Leisha Sequist is part of a team of doctors and engineers from Massachusetts General Hospital and MIT trying to revolutionize cancer detection. Meet Sybil, an artificial intelligence program. The name inspired by the oracles of ancient Greece.\par \par \uc2 SEQUIST: We`ve developed Sybil, an algorithm that is able to tell who might be at risk for lung cancer one, three, five years down the road.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death in the U.S. killing more than 127,000 a year. The Mass Gen-MIT study using three sets of independent scans found that Sybil could accurately predict whether a person will develop lung cancer in the next year, up to 94 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 SEQUIST: This is an immense amount of data in a CAT scan, and we`re only scratching the surface when we just look at it with our eyes.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Usually, a radiologist is looking for nodules or a mass, but Sybil has the power to look so closely at a CT scan that can identify patterns of data associated with cancer even before a tumor appears.\par \par \uc2 So, this is AI in action. What do we see here?\par \par \uc2 SEQUIST: A.I. circled this area in red and said this area has a dangerous pattern. It looks like cancer might come up. Two years later, there`s a cancer right in that same spot where the AI predicted it would be.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: That`s not to say A.I. will replace radiologists, but the research team in Massachusetts believes Sybil`s assistance is the future of the field.\par \par \uc2 Is A.I. going to save lives?\par \par \uc2 DR. FLORIAN FINTELMANN, RADIOLOGIST, MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL: Yes, it will save lives because it will enable us to do more, see more, and manage better than we did without A.I.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Clinical trials for Sybil are ongoing. \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval likely years away. And while doctors are still studying the technology, Don Roberts, who is now cancer free, believes technology like Sybil could help him live longer.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: You need every weapon you can possibly get to combat cancer.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: And A.I. could be a powerful one?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TORRES: Dr. John Torres, NBC News, Boston.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: When we come back, we`ll hit the ramps and the rails with \uc2 Tony Hawk\uc2 , the Navajo Nation, and the skate park inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, tonight, for so many skateboarders, finding somewhere to skate can be as hard as landing your first Ollie. Our Steve Patterson reports Navajo Nation was no exception until now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEVE PATTERSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The Navajo Nation is breathtaking, 27,000 square miles of quiet beauty. But for kids growing up here, maybe too quiet. That`s why so many are attracted to this sound.\par \par \uc2 Navajo Nation loves to skate. Just check out their president doing an ollie.\par \par \uc2 BUU NYGREN, PRESIDENT, NAVAJO NATION: Friends to come out to skate. You challenge one another and you keep each other accountable.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: But the reservation itself has been a skateboarding desert. Kids had to find a ride dozens of miles away just to get their wheels running. 12-year-old Cheyenne has been doing it for years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just liked doing it because it`s fun. It teaches me to be more active.\par \par \uc2 AMY DENET DEAL, COMMUNITY ORGANIZER: These remote communities need more opportunities to have outdoor recreations.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Something skaters could call their own became a community`s cause. And with assistance from an organization called the Skate Park Project and its famous founder \uc2 Tony Hawk\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 NYGREN: Thank you for bringing change in this community.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: The Dene Skate Garden was set in concrete.\par \par \uc2 How important is to have an outlet, especially for kids in a community that`s been less served traditionally.\par \par \uc2 TONY HAWK, SKATEBOARDER: I think it`s hugely important, especially in a community like this. When kids find skateboarding, I think they find something that speaks to them in a deeper way and something that can teach them a lot of life lessons about overcoming their goals, about overcoming challenges.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Now, somewhere like that exist right here.\par \par \uc2 NYGREN: I`m really excited for them so that they can be here, they can be a part of the community.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: A place of pride, a culture of tradition now embracing the great sanctuary of skate.\par \par \uc2 Steve Patterson, NBC News, Navajo Nation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: They make it look so easy. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Tuesday. Thanks for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230412ej4b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc22}{\*\bkmkend toc22}\b \uc2 The Future Of The Pill Used For More Than Two Decades Uncertain After Competing Rulings By Two Federal Judges\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 649 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: This evening, the divide over abortion is centered on a pill used for more than 20 years. But now the focus of competing rulings by two federal courts. Dasha Burns reports and the growing fallout.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the fallout from dueling court rulings. The future of the abortion pill is hanging in the balance after a Texas judge invalidated the FDA`s approval of mifepristone. The Department of Justice is now appealing that decision.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER KLEIN, WHITE HOUSE GENDER POLICY COUNCIL: What this ruling effectively is a backdoor ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What options are available to the administration here?\par \par \uc2 KLEIN: We`ve been waiting for the judge to rule. And what we did is what we believe is the best strategy which is to appeal this decision immediately.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Minutes after the Texas judge`s decision on Friday night, a federal judge in Washington State with equal authority dropping a contradictory ruling ordering the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to maintain access to the drug. It all sowed confusion nationwide and created a legal standoff. Use with another drug, mifepristone is the most common method for terminating pregnancies in the U.S. It`s been approved since 2000 and used safely more than five million times.\par \par \uc2 But the plaintiffs in the Texas case, a group of anti-abortion doctors and medical organizations, allege the drug is dangerous and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t follow proper protocol when approving it. I spoke with the group behind the lawsuit before the ruling.\par \par \uc2 The result if you win is that abortion access will be significantly limited. Is that the goal of this loss?\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST, SENIOR COUNSEL, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: No, the goal of this lawsuit is to protect American women and girls from dangerous chemical abortion drugs. It`s not -- we are not seeking a nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Meanwhile, today, lawmakers in blue states vowed to do whatever necessary to protect reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MAURA HEALEY (D-MA): It harms patients, undermines medical expertise, and takes away freedom.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Tonight, Mifepristone remains legal as the battles rage on in the courts. Dasha Burns, NBC News .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Let me bring in now Senior Legal Correspondent Laura Jarrett. Laura, there was movement on both of these cases in court today.\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): A flurry of court filings today, Lester. The Justice Department and the pill manufacturer both hoping to convince a conservative-leaning court of appeals to hit pause on that Texas decision, to give everyone time to make their arguments without this deadline looming of the ruling set to go into effect on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The court just minutes ago asking to hear what the plaintiffs have to say about all that, but the \uc2 FDA\uc2  also wants Clarity from a federal judge in Washington State who ordered the drug to stay on the market about how to reconcile that order with the Texas ruling.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: This feels like this could be heading to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yes, it really could, and fast. If the Fifth Circuit doesn`t block the Texas decision, the DOJ will take it to 