 the victims. His tribute tonight.\par \par \uc2 The abortion pill battle. The future of the pill used for more than two decades uncertain after competing rulings by two federal judges. The latest action by the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 A Virginia grand jury indicts the mother of that six-year-old boy who shot his first grade teacher in a classroom. What his mother is charged with?\par \par \uc2 The fallout over dozens of leaked classified documents. What they reveal about Russia, the war in Ukraine, Israeli intelligence, and more, and what American intelligence officials fear tonight.\par \par \uc2 President Biden making his most definitive statement yet about running for re-election. What he told our \uc2 Al Roker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She fainted after getting the COVID vaccine and became the focus of a massive internet conspiracy. Now, Tiffany Dover is breaking her silence exclusively to NBC News.\par \par \uc2 And his performance at Augusta as an amateur was nothing short of masterful and is inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome everyone. It seems we can barely catch our breaths or dry the tears before another mass shooting rocks in American city. Just two weeks ago, I stood right here reporting the heartbreaking details of a school shooting in Nashville. Today, that happened at a bank in Louisville, Kentucky in what the city`s mayor calls an evil act of targeted violence after a man in his 20s armed with a rifle went on a rampage in a workplace attack streaming the horror online as he carried out the crime. Four people were killed, nine others rushed to a hospital for treatment. Officers ran to the threat confronting the gunman in an exchange of gunfire killing the shooter. Two police officers were among the wounded.\par \par \uc2 Maggie Vespa is in Louisville tonight and has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, chaos and carnage gripping another American city.\par \par \uc2 CRAG GREENBERG, MAYOR OF LOUISVILLE, KENTUCKY: Let`s be clear about what this was. This was an evil act of targeted violence.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: The familiar terror unfolding in downtown Louisville, Kentucky. Around 8:30 this morning, police say a lone gunman opened fire inside this \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  killing four and wounding nine.\par \par \uc2 SADIQA REYNOLDS, WITNESS: This was terrifying. It was terrifying officers on the scene within three minutes. Investigators say this was not a random act.\par \par \uc2 JACQUELYN GWINN-VILLAROEL, CHIEF, LOUISVILLE METRO POLICE: White male who was employed at \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2 . His weapon of choice was a rifle.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Authorities identifying the shooter as Connor Sturgeon who died at the scene in a shootout with police. He livestreamed the rampage according to investigators who late today executed a search warrant on his home.\par \par \uc2 Victims range in age from 40 to 64 including 63-year-old Thomas Elliott, the bank`s senior vice president according to his LinkedIn account and a friend of Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): Tommy Elliott helped me build my law career, help me become governor, gave me advice on being a good dad.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Among the wounded, two Louisville Metro Police Officers. One officer, Nickolas Wilt, shot in the head. J.D. Worley watched it happen in horror from his office across the street.\par \par \uc2 J.D. WORLEY, WITNESS: Other officers grabbed him and carried him off as he was unfortunately motionless. Never could have fathomed I would ever see something like this happen.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: At 26, officer Wilt is less than two weeks out of the academy.\par \par \uc2 GWINN-VILLAROEL: Nick has come out of brain surgery and is in critical but stable condition as we speak.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Kentucky`s governor praising police who raced to confront the gunman and asking for time to grieve.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: It seems like we argue so much in this country, so much anger. But I still believe that love and compassion and humanity can lead us to a better place. And this is hard. It`s really hard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Maggie, any more details and a possible motive here?\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): So, Lester, according to two senior officials, authorities are investigating this as an instance of workplace violence, noting the suspect who was an employee at that bank may have suffered from mental health issues and adding they expect much more to come out in the coming days. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Maggie Vespa tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 This evening, the divide over abortion is centered on a pill used for more than 20 years. But now the focus of competing rulings by two federal courts. Dasha Burns reports and the growing fallout.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the fallout from dueling court rulings. The future of the abortion pill is hanging in the balance after a Texas judge invalidated the FDA`s approval of mifepristone. The Department of Justice is now appealing that decision.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER KLEIN, WHITE HOUSE GENDER POLICY COUNCIL: What this ruling effectively is a backdoor ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What options are available to the administration here?\par \par \uc2 KLEIN: We`ve been waiting for the judge to rule. And what we did is what we believe is the best strategy which is to appeal this decision immediately.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Minutes after the Texas judge`s decision on Friday night, a federal judge in Washington State with equal authority dropping a contradictory ruling ordering the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to maintain access to the drug. It all sowed confusion nationwide and created a legal standoff. Use with another drug, mifepristone is the most common method for terminating pregnancies in the U.S. It`s been approved since 2000 and used safely more than five million times.\par \par \uc2 But the plaintiffs in the Texas case, a group of anti-abortion doctors and medical organizations, allege the drug is dangerous and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t follow proper protocol when approving it. I spoke with the group behind the lawsuit before the ruling.\par \par \uc2 The result if you win is that abortion access will be significantly limited. Is that the goal of this loss?\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST, SENIOR COUNSEL, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: No, the goal of this lawsuit is to protect American women and girls from dangerous chemical abortion drugs. It`s not -- we are not seeking a nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Meanwhile, today, lawmakers in blue states vowed to do whatever necessary to protect reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MAURA HEALEY (D-MA): It harms patients, undermines medical expertise, and takes away freedom.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Tonight, mifepristone remains legal as the battles rage on in the courts. Dasha Burns, NBC News .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Let me bring in now Senior Legal Correspondent Laura Jarrett. Laura, there was movement on both of these cases in court today.\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): A flurry of court filings today, Lester. The Justice Department and the pill manufacturer both hoping to convince a conservative-leaning court of appeals to hit pause on that Texas decision, to give everyone time to make their arguments without this deadline looming of the ruling set to go into effect on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The court just minutes ago asking to hear what the plaintiffs have to say about all that, but the \uc2 FDA\uc2  also wants Clarity from a federal judge in Washington State who ordered the drug to stay on the market about how to reconcile that order with the Texas ruling.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: This feels like this could be heading to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yes, it really could, and fast. If the Fifth Circuit doesn`t block the Texas decision, the DOJ will take it to the Supreme Court as soon as this week. And if DOJ wins, the plaintiffs could appeal there as well.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn now to that political drama in Tennessee. Late today, the Nashville Council voting to reinstate one of the Black state lawmakers expelled last week over a gun protest. Kathy Park reports he has just been sworn in again.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, vice mayor. I would like to nominate Representative Justin Jones.\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, a political showdown in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ayes 36.\par \par \uc2 PARK: With former Tennessee lawmaker Justin Jones back to the state house just days after his expulsion. The Metro Nashville Council voted unanimously in favor of his reappointment.\par \par \uc2 FREDDIE O`CONNELL, MEMBER, METROPOLITAN NASHVILLE COUNCIL DISTRICT 19: This is a message that partisanship has gone so far as to be a force in violating basic principles of democracy.\par \par \uc2 PARK: This special meeting comes after a rare move by House Republicans to remove three Democratic lawmakers after they joined a gun control rally on the House floor which Republicans say violated the rules.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SEXTON (R-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: You can call it peaceful, you can call it whatever, but they had a protest against house policy on the floor.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: No action --\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No peace!\par \par \uc2 PARK: Among the Tennessee three, only one survived the vote, Gloria Johnson.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA JOHNSON (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: It might have to do with the color of our skin. GOP leaders say their decision was not based on race but that Johnson didn`t go as far as her two freshman colleagues.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No justice, no peace!\par \par \uc2 PARK: Representative Justin Pearson will learn his fate Wednesday in his own district with a special meeting in Memphis. Meanwhile, the political battle doesn`t end for Jones as he awaits a special election and he`s vowing to return.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PARK (on camera): And moments after the critical vote to reappoint Justin Jones, hundreds of his supporters began rushing out of the metro council chambers and began marching straight toward the state capitol. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Kathy Park, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There is growing concern tonight about whether U.S. intelligence sources were compromised by the leak of dozens of classified documents. Andrea Mitchell has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The leaked documents include sensitive and top secret intelligence about U.S. adversaries and allies potentially jeopardizing U.S. spies in hostile countries and electronic surveillance.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: We don`t know uh who`s responsible for this and we don`t know if they have more that they intend to post.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:07]\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The documents show Ukraine running low on ammunition and using U.S. intelligence to strike a Russian target. Russia`s military is struggling in Ukraine. South Korea reluctant to help the U.S. supply artillery to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Is the worst damage the Russian sources and methods behind the inside information from Russia on the defense ministry?\par \par \uc2 JOHN BRENNAN, FORMER DIRECTOR, CIA: I think the greatest concern is that some of our sources both human as well as technical collection systems could be compromised as a result of this. Another document has the leaders of Israel`s intelligence service, the \uc2 Mossad\uc2 , backing anti-government protests though Israel denies it.\par \par \uc2 RON DERMER, MINISTER FOR STRATEGIC AFFAIRS, ISRAEL: The senior director of the \uc2 Mossad\uc2  and our senior officials in the \uc2 Mossad\uc2  were not involved in any way.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The stolen intelligence first showing up on a chat server for the Minecraft video game according to open-source intelligence agency \uc2 Bellingcat\uc2  before appearing on other sites. The slides showing conflicting Russian casualties in Ukraine indicate some of the documents could have been doctored but officials believe most are real.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): The Justice Department is investigating to find the source as officials fear the leak has exposed critical vulnerabilities in U.S. intelligence. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Andrea, separately, there`s news tonight about that Wall Street Journal reporter detained in Russia. What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That`s right, Lester. Secretary of State Blinken has determined the reporter \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  is wrongfully detained falsely charged with spying. Now, the U.S. hostage envoy can begin working with his family to try to get him out. But in violation of Russia`s legal obligations, U.S. diplomats had still not been able to visit him almost two weeks since he was jailed. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Andrea Mitchell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to block Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  from testifying before a federal grand jury investigating Mr. Trump`s rule in efforts to overturn the 2020 election. Lawyers for Trump are asking a federal appeals court to reverse a lower court`s decision ordering Pence to appear.\par \par \uc2 With nearly 600 days until the next election, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  turned heads today with an admission on live T.V. that he plans to run again. Kristen Welker joins us from the White House. Kristen, the timing came as a bit of a surprise.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): That`s absolutely right, Lester. President Biden making his most definitive comments yet that he`s running for reelection in 2024, talking to our \uc2 Al Roker\uc2  during the annual White House Easter Egg Roll.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR: Help a -- help a brother out. Make some news for me.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: .No, no, no. I plan on running now but we`re not prepared to announce it yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Top White House advisors are preparing to make final decisions on launching his re-election campaign according to several sources familiar with the discussions. Several considerations include no major Democratic Challenger has emerged despite Mr. Biden`s sagging poll numbers. Former President Trump who`s running for the GOP nomination has been indicted and is consuming the political spotlight. Still, source familiar with President Biden`s thinking tell NBC News, don`t expect to hear a final announcement until possibly next month, if not later. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House tonight, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, a grand jury indicts the mother of a six-year-old boy who shot his teacher in a Virginia classroom. The charges she faces tonight plus what her legal team is saying.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A Virginia grand jury has indicted the mother of that 6-year-old boy who brought a gun to his school and shot his teacher. Miguel Almaguer reports on the charges she now faces.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have a report of a teacher shot.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, three months after the shocking school shooting, the mother of the 6-year- old Virginia boy who shot and seriously wounded his first-grade teacher is facing criminal charges. Today, a grand jury in Newport News indicting 25- year-old Deja Taylor on charges of felony child neglect and a misdemeanor count of recklessly leaving a loaded firearm so as to endanger a child.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE PARKER, FORMER SUPERINTENDENT, NEWPORT NEWS PUBLIC SCHOOLS: Who would be prepared for a 6-year-old to bring a loaded weapon onto school or a weapon on the school.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:06]\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: After authorities announced the 6-year-old would not be charged with a crime, the Commonwealth`s Attorney saying today every criminal case is unique in its facts and these facts support these charges, but our investigation into the shooting continues.\par \par \uc2 ABIGAIL ZWERNER, TEACHER: I just will never forget the look on his face that he gave me while he pointed the gun directly at me. That`s something that I will never forget. It`s changed me. It`s changed my life.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Abigail Zwerner who told Savannah Guthrie the bullet still remains lodged in her body is suing School administrators for $40 million alleging gross negligence. Zwerner said staff failed to act on several warnings that the 6-year-old had a gun or made threats. There were failures in accountability at multiple levels that led to Abby being shot and almost killed, writes her attorney. We will not allow school leaders to escape accountability for their role in this tragedy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): A lawyer for Deja Taylor, the mother of the 6-year- old, tells NBC News she plans to turn herself in later this week. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Miguel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, she was the target of an Internet conspiracy theory claiming she had died after getting vaccinated. Now, for the first time, Tiffany Dover speaks out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Few Americans know what it`s like to be the target of a conspiracy theory. But since the day nurse Tiffany Dover fainted while getting her COVID vaccine, life has never been the same. And tonight, she`s speaking out for the first time in an exclusive interview with NBC`s Brandy Zadrozny.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRANDY ZADROZNY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tiffany Dover wants the world to know one thing.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY DOVER, NURSE: My message is simple it is that I am alive. I`m well.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: That`s it?\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I hope they believe it.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: They are the massive online community of conspiracy theorists who`ve been convinced she`s dead for more than two years. I made a whole podcast about it. Tiffany wasn`t ready to share her story then, but now --\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I`m ready to just put my story out there, all my story.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: That story begins in 2020. Tiffany was working as a nurse manager at CHI Memorial Hospital in Chattanooga, putting in grueling hours on a COVID unit. When vaccines arrived that December, Tiffany became one of the first people at her hospital to get the shot.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I felt OK during that.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Then she stood up to answer questions.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You want to sit down?\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I ended up passing out. So, that created the opposite effect of what I would have wanted.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: But you got right back up.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I did. And this is something that`s happened to me my whole life. I do have episodes where I pass out.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: But in that moment, a conspiracy theory was born.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: People thought that I was dead. People thought that I was an actress paid to do this, that I was paid off by a big firm. It was completely overwhelming to be honest.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: It snowballed fast.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: I had people showing up at my house. I had people reaching out to my friends, to my family. I`ve been -- received death threats.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Were you afraid for your family?\par \par \uc2 DOVER: Absolutely, yes.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Through it all, Tiffany kept silent. And that only made the online frenzy worse.\par \par \uc2 Why not come out and dispel these rumors?\par \par \uc2 DOVER: Initially that was exactly what I wanted to do. And unfortunately, I was told that that absolutely would not happen.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: The hospital told you not to speak out.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: Correct. Yes, it would be irrecoverable damage is what I was told if I was to speak out and have another episode.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Instead, the hospital put out this video intending to show she was alive. But the so-called Tiffany Truthers picked it apart and used it as fuel.\par \par \uc2 Online, anti-vaxxers and the movement are sort of using you as their poster girl.\par \par \uc2 DOVER: It`s hard to think that you are being used to detour somebody from getting the vaccine. And that was really hard for me to cope with.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Are you saying you blamed yourself?\par \par \uc2 DOVER: Yes, absolutely. I wish that we would have used it as a platform to speak out on the fact that people do pass out after getting shots, vaccines, but that is not a reason to not proceed with getting them.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: CHI Memorial has repeatedly denied knowledge of any directive asking Tiffany not to speak or post, including in an interview for our podcast.\par \par \uc2 KAREN LONG, MEDICA COMMUNICATIONS SPECIALIST, CHI MEMORIAL: I`m not aware of anything that asked Tiffany not to respond.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: The hospital recently telling NBC News we have no new information. A year ago, Tiffany left CHI Memorial. and while she hopes to work as a nurse again one day, for now, she`s focused on reclaiming her life.\par \par \uc2 Even after all of this, there will be people who say, we don`t believe you. What then?\par \par \uc2 DOVER: At this point, you know, I`ve done what I needed to do. I`ve put it out there that I`m alive. They have to choose whether they believe that or not. And that`s all I can do is put out the truth.\par \par \uc2 ZADROZNY: Brandy Zadrozny, NBC News Higdon, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And for the bonus podcast, scan the Q.R. code on your screen or search for Tiffany Dover wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a masterful performance by one of golf`s rising stars.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s not often you see a 23-year-old compete at the Masters, let alone outplay some of the best golfers on the planet. Stephanie Gosk with why Sam Bennett is inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Sometimes, the significance of the moment can be lost on the young, but not this moment.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nothing can top this at 18.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The magic of a standing ovation at the masters on a Sunday is not lost on amateur, Sam Bennett.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s overwhelmed with emotion.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The Texas A&M senior was greeted by his mother to celebrate a remarkable 16th-place finish for someone his age at Augusta National, one of the most difficult golf courses in the world.\par \par \uc2 SAM BENNETT, GOLFER: I haven`t had a kid yet, so that walk up to 18 was definitely the coolest experience of my life.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Bennett stunned golf fans with his first two rounds of the tournament, shooting back-to-back 68s.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What a start for the amateur.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Not just making the cut, the second-best start for a masters in amateur history. At the end of the day on Friday, he was alone in third place paired up with pro golfer Jon Rahm who went on to win the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 BENNETT: If you would have told me I was going to be here when I was a kid, I would have -- I would have thought you were crazy.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: For inspiration, Bennett has a tattoo, words from his father who died from early-onset Alzheimer`s in 2021. Don`t wait to do something, it says, right there on his left arm every time he swings. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230411ej4a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc24}{\*\bkmkend toc24}\b \uc2 The Beginning Of What Is Set To Be The Biggest Battle Over Abortion Rights Since The Overturning Of Roe V. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Allie Raffa \par \uc2 841 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [18:30:00]\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC NIGHTLY NEWS ANCHOR: Tonight, the beginning of what is set to be the biggest battle over abortion rights since the overturning of Roe v. Wade. Protests over a judge`s ruling that would halt the use of the abortion pill. Some healthcare providers are now stockpiling the drug. The Biden administration vowing today to fight the decision as some Democrats call on the president to simply not enforce any ban.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Political firestorm in Tennessee. Why at least one of the Democratic lawmakers expelled by Republicans there may be back in his seat tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Stand-off at sea. Navy ships from Taiwan and China face off as China launches massive war games, including this simulation showing how they could strike the island.\par \par \uc2 Growing violence in the Middle East. Israel attacked by rockets from inside Syria. The retaliation tonight.\par \par \uc2 Should this convicted killer walk free? The governor of Texas vows to pardon a U.S. army sergeant who shot a \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protester.\par \par \uc2 Tiger Woods drops out of the Masters with another injury. New questions tonight about his future in the sport.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That is not easy to see.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: On this Easter Sunday, the pope`s message to the world just a week after leaving the hospital.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is "NBC Nightly News with Kate Snow."\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Good evening and Happy Easter to you. The decision from a federal judge in Texas Friday is still reverberating as we head into a new week. That judge suspended the FDA`s approval of the abortion pill. The president has vowed his administration will fight that decision. Any appeal must be filed by Friday.\par \par \uc2 The pills are already banned in 13 states that have made abortion illegal. If this ruling were to hold up, the pills would then be banned nationwide. The anti-abortion groups that brought the lawsuit argue the drug is not safe. The White House says if a judge can overrule the \uc2 FDA\uc2  on this medication, any judge could ban any drug. We begin tonight with Allie Raffa.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALLIE RAFFA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, battle lines drawn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am very worried about women who need this medication.\par \par \uc2 AMY O`DONNELL, TEXAS ALLIANCE FOR LIFE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: We applaud the judge`s action to issue the stay on the chemical abortion drug, mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA (voiceover): After the most consequential abortion ruling since the reversal of Roe v. Wade, a Texas federal judge suspending \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the most commonly used abortion pill, giving the federal government seven days to appeal before the ruling goes into effect.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES: We intend to do everything to make sure it`s available to them, not just in a week but moving forward, period.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA (voiceover): Reporter: the health and human services secretary, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , vowing the Biden administration is considering all legal options.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Well, we`ve already filed an appeal of this court`s ruling.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA (voiceover): The issue will likely be decided by the Supreme Court after a federal judge in Washington State ruled to protect the drug in more than a dozen states. One Republican lawmaker threatening cuts to \uc2 FDA\uc2  funding if the White House fights the Texas ruling.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): It may become a point where House Republicans on the appropriations side have to defund \uc2 FDA\uc2  programs that don`t make sense.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA (voiceover): Some healthcare providers are now stockpiling the pill, fearing an all-out ban. While some Democrats urge the Biden administration to go even further.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): I believe that the Biden administration should ignore this ruling. It is up to the Biden administration to enforce, to choose whether to enforce such a ruling.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA (voiceover): HSS secretary, Becerra, not committing to that specific option.\par \par \uc2 BECERRA: Every option is on the table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And Allie joins us now from the White House. Allie, will the drug still be available as this case makes its way through the courts?\par \par \uc2 RAFFA (on camera): Well, legal experts say the drug will likely remain available as these cases make their way through the courts, a process that could take months, possibly even years, Kate.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: All right. Allie Raffa at the White House for us, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230411ej49000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc25}{\*\bkmkend toc25}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for April 9, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Laura Jarrett, Amna Nawaz, Brendan Buck, Symone  Sanders \par \uc2 9,991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST (voice over): This Sunday, the abortion legal wars. A Texas federal judge suspends \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for the abortion pill Mifepristone. Another in Washington State directly contradicts him, setting up a likely Supreme Court standoff on the abortion bill.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (R) FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  went beyond their legislative authority.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): The contents of this judge`s ruling is frankly shocking.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): As the abortion issue gives liberals their first majority in the Wisconsin Supreme Court in 15 years.\par \par \uc2 REP. GLORIA JOHNSON (D-TN): Everything we care about is on the line.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): Wisconsin, just the latest in a string of statewide defeats on the abortion issue for the GOP. Is anyone in the party going to listen to voters? Plus, lawmakers expelled. Two Black lawmakers are ousted by the Tennessee Legislature after protesting on the House floor for stricter gun laws.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D), FORMER TENNESSEE STATE REPRESENTATIVE: We called for you all to ban assault weapons, and you respond with an assault on democracy.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SEXTON, (R) SPEAKER OF THE TENNESSEE HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES: They had a protest against House policy.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): The effort to expel a third Democrat failed.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I was a (inaudible) white lady. I don`t know what else it was.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): I`ll talk to the two ousted lawmakers, Justin Jones and Justin Pearson. And Trump charge --\par \par \uc2 ALVIN BRAGG\uc2 , MANHATTAN DISTRICT ATTORNEY: These are felony crimes in New York State, no matter who you are.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voice over): As the New York case progresses, it`s the probe into his mishandling of classified documents that may put Trump in greater legal jeopardy. I`ll talk to one of Trump`s attorneys in that case, James Trusty.\par \par \uc2 And finally, defying China. My exclusive interview with House Foreign Affairs Chairman Mike McCaul as he wraps up his own trip to the Island of Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News Senior Legal Correspondent Laura Jarrett; Amna Nawaz, co-anchor of PBS NewsHour; Republican Strategist Brendan Buck; and Symone Sanders Townsend, Former Chief Spokesperson for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Welcome to Sunday. It`s `Meet The Press`.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is `Meet The Press` with Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a good Sunday morning, Happy Easter, Passover and Ramadan. The spring holidays are here. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  presided over a crowded mass this morning in Vatican City, St. Peter`s Square. He is, of course, in a wheelchair right now after his release from the hospital a week ago. But he did ride through the crowd on this Easter Sunday morning to the cheers of many.\par \par \uc2 But despite the celebrations this morning, let`s be honest, this was no ordinary holiday week. In fact, come November of 2024, let me look back at this week as the most consequential to the future of the Republican Party and to our overall electoral politics, from the dueling abortion rulings Friday night to overt partisan warfare in Tennessee. Ironically, the least consequential event may very well be the former president`s legal troubles, at least the Manhattan case, though it`s still important obviously.\par \par \uc2 But what`s happened this week in Texas, in that courtroom in Wisconsin at that ballot box and in Tennessee in that State House may reverberate the most in our political culture as a widening divide corrodes our institutions from our State Houses to the courts. The national divide was crystallized on Friday night in those dueling abortion rules. Less than an hour apart on specifically the abortion pill over (ph) federal judges, these are legal efforts that were pushed by the political activists, seeking a decision by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 There was no actual medical event, no misuse of this pill to trigger this look at the drug. It was simply political activism. This was a drug that was approved at the end of Bill Clinton`s presidency, four presidencies ago. In Texas, a federal judge invalidated the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for the abortion pill Mifepristone, which in combination with another drug, does account for more than 50 percent of all abortions in the country. An hour later, it was a judge in Washington State that contradicted the decision and he called the drug safe and effective, and ordered the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to make no changes. Obviously, the Supreme Court has to weigh in on this one.\par \par \uc2 But the political divide of the judiciary, which looks very undemocratic these days, is also getting worse on the state level. On Thursday night in a scene that had echoes of the ugly politics of the `60s, southern lawmakers in Tennessee did something unprecedented. They expelled two Black Democratic legislators for taking a gun violence protest over the recent national school shooting to the House floor itself. It was a violation of House rules. But a third legislator, Gloria Johnson who was White survived the expulsion vote.\par \par \uc2 Let`s be honest, 30 years of aggressive gerrymandering has likely brought us to this moment. In Tennessee, Trump won over 60 percent of the vote, but Republicans make up more than 75 percent of the State House. Half of those Republicans didn`t face any opposition in 2022, not even from their own party.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The Tennessee Legislature has also changed the rules on the House floor that essentially stifles and limits dissent. Until this week, only two lawmakers in Tennessee have been expelled from the House of Representatives since the 19th century, one for allegations of sexual misconduct and a second was because somebody was convicted of soliciting a bribe.\par \par \uc2 I am joined by the two lawmakers who were ousted by the Tennessee House this week, Justin Jones and Justin Pearson. Gentlemen, welcome to `Meet The Press.` I`m sure this was not the way you thought you would be appearing on this program or other national programs.\par \par \uc2 Justin Jones, let me start with you. I know you met with Vice President Harris, you met with President Biden. What was their message to you and what did you hear from them?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D), FORMER TENNESSEE STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Well thank you so much for having us on today. I think the most resounding message we`re hearing from the White House and across the world, and people across this nation is that this attack on democracy will not go on unchallenged, that the Tennessee House Republicans` attempt to crucify democracy has instead resurrected a movement led by young people to restore democracy, to build a multi-racial coalition. We are in the midst of a third reconstruction here, beginning here in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 And I think what -- you know, the message is that we will continue to resist, that this is not the end of -- that their decision to expel us is not the ultimate authority, but that the people will hold them accountable both at the county level and in the special election, and going forward there are -- through our legal processes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Justin Pearson, have you talked to any of your colleagues or now former colleagues since this vote? Have any of them explained why you were ousted and Gloria Johnson was not?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON (D), FORMER TENNESSEE STATE REPRESENTATIVE: It`s Justin J. Pearson. And no, I haven`t talked to our former colleagues, but I have to tell you, the reality is an institution filled with people who are more concerned about supporting the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and supporting the Second Amendment than it is protecting the First Amendment rights of children and teenagers to come to the Capitol and advocate for gun violence prevention laws is not the type of legislature that we deserve and need to have in the state of Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, Cameron Sexton, the speaker of the House, called those children and teenagers insurrectionists and it is that type of language, it is that type of political ideology that is destructive to our democracy, and what ends up happening is the perpetuation of systems of injustice, white patriarchy like white supremacy that lead to the expulsion of the two of the youngest Black lawmakers in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Representative Pearson, do you feel like -- you`ve been just elected to the statehouse.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did you ever feel welcome or did you feel unwelcome when you got there?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: No. It has always been a toxic work environment to work in the Tennessee State Capitol. When you have people who make comments about hanging you on a tree and hanging Black people on a tree as a form of capital punishment, when you wear a dashiki on the House floor and a member gets up and they talk about your dashiki, saying it is unprofessional, they`re really sending signals that you don`t belong here, and that is what the underlying and undergrading (ph) comment and responses that we heard on the House floor and the comments to myself and my brother, Representative Jones, was really about.\par \par \uc2 It`s about us not belonging in the institution because they are afraid of the changes that are happening in the society and the voices that are elevated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In fact, Representative Jones, I can`t help but wonder. Look, you`ve been -- you were a well-known political activist to many of these state lawmakers, you had been there for a sit-in having to do with confederate monuments. There had been some run-ins that some lawmakers brought charges against you. They were -- eventually ended up being dropped. Do you think that history of activism made you a target on the House floor the minute you got elected?\par \par \uc2 JONES: I think our presence as young Black voices for our constituencies, people who will not bow down, those who will not be conformed, that`s what made -- put a target on us the day we walked into the Tennessee General Assembly. I mean, you -- this is the first time in Tennessee history we had a completely partisan expulsion by predominantly white caucus, all but one member of their caucus is white out of 75 members, and we are the two youngest Black lawmakers in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 And so what we saw was a system of political hubris. This was not just an attack on us, but it was an attempt to silence our districts, predominantly Black and brown districts, who no longer have representation. 78,000 people in each of the districts do not have a voice and have someone to deal with their constituent relations are silenced, and that`s what this is about, it`s about the people whose voices have been taken on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Representative Jones, Gloria Johnson was telling us -- I think you were on with me on -- earlier in the week -- how they`ve changed the rules even since you got elected and even since Representative Pearson got elected, of like, just on the House floor. Explain some of the rule changes that were made just in the last few years on expressing dissent on the House floor.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, they`ve limited debate to what they say is five minutes, but in reality, you ask one questions, they`ll spend five minutes answering it, and your time is up. You can`t reclaim your time.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 Or the speaker, Cameron Sexton, an autocrat, will not even call on you, will call the question and will cut off all debate. I mean, when we went outside to support the protest, both myself and my brother Representative Pearson, our voting machines were turned off, so we couldn`t vote on the House floor. The Speaker -- I mean, he runs the Capitol like it`s his private palace, and so there is no democracy in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee is the most undemocratic state in the nation. Even in committees, we are silenced from talking about the issues. When we try to talk about the issue of mass shootings that plagued our community, we were silenced, and all they offered our communities were moments of silence, in fact, and empty thoughts and prayers.\par \par \uc2 And our community deserves more than that. But instead of responding to the grief and trauma of our community, the House Speaker Cameron Sexton once again silenced us even the day of our protest and that`s what led us to the well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Representative Pearson, when you look at the maps, right, you see this. There -- definitely, you can see there`s an attempt to limit the political power of Nashville and Memphis, particularly in African-American communities. Is there any recourse that you have here? I mean, this feels like a very aggressive gerrymander that was beyond just protecting incumbents.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON\uc2 : Yes. What we are seeing is the intentional political dilution of votes of people who are more progressive, of communities that are filled with African-Americans and people of color. And that is an intentional and systematic effort of people in the majority party to retain as much power as they can in order to be able to dictate and control what it is that their majority Black populations and progressive populations do.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the Speaker Cameron Sexton called himself at one point an overseer. This is the type of legislature and people in positions of power, in addition to Governor \uc2 Bill Lee\uc2  who we are dealing with. And so, we realize what is going on in the south, which is you have politicians who to vote -- who will vote for them rather than the people who are supposed to be choosing their elected officials, these are the situations that we get in.\par \par \uc2 And in the south in particular, we need to pay attention what`s happening with state legislatures. This is a battleground because if it`s gun violence, if it`s abortion, if it`s education, if it`s health care, the south is continuing to lag behind and the people in positions of power are hurting us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, there`s a dramatic political lines here that have been drawn that we`re going to show graphically in a few minutes. But I want to get to the last few questions for the both of you.\par \par \uc2 Representative Jones, do you plan to accept a re-appointment if it is indeed the case, and if not, do you plan to run any special election to come back?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes and yes. You know, we will continue to fight for our constituents. And one thing I just want to say, Chuck, is that this is -- attack against us is hurting all people in our state. So it is not - even though it`s - this is of course (ph) is impacting Black and brown communities, this is hurting poor white people. Their attack on democracy hurts all of us. And I just want to emphasize that, because I don`t want it just to be about race. But this is about attack on Black, brown and poor white communities and silencing them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: These lawmakers who are more beholden to the \uc2 NRA\uc2  than their own people in their own districts, people like Cameron Sexton.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Representative Pearson, same question for you. I know you may get re-appointed, will you accept the appointment and do you plan on running in the special election?\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Yes. I would be honored to accept the appointment of the Shelby County Commission and to run in a special election, and I`ve already heard that people in the state legislature and in Nashville are actually threatening our Shelby County commissioners to not re-appoint me or they`re going to take away funding that`s in the government`s budget for projects that the mayor and others have asked for.\par \par \uc2 And this is what folks really have to realize, the power structure in the state of Tennessee is always wielding against the minority party and people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And there may very well be some constitutional rights that have been violated here and that could be something that --\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- the federal courts could be looking at. Justin Jones, Justin J. Pearson, thank you both for spending a few minutes and sharing your views with us.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON\uc2 : Thank you so much, we`ll keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Turning now to the unprecedented scene that played out in New York City on Tuesday as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became the first former president charged with a crime, 34 felony counts of falsifying business records, stemming from alleged hush money payoffs he made to adult film star Stormy Daniels at the time that it`s alleged to avoid a scandal ahead of the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s route to the courthouse to be fingerprinted and arraigned was tracking in an O.J.-like style overhead, that you might expect from the New York City media and on the ground. He pleaded not guilty, but it is just the first chapter of what`s likely to be a very busy spring of legal battlements (ph) if the civil trial for battery and defamation, that begins in just over two weeks in one of two cases New York author E. Jean Carroll has brought against the former president.\par \par \uc2 There is the criminal case in Georgia that`s focused on the former President`s efforts to overturn his 2020 election defeat there, and then of course is the special counsel probe into the former president`s potential mishandling of classified documents where the Special Counsel Jack Smith is looking into possible obstruction. The former president addressed that probe at Mar-a-Lago on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You have a radical left lunatic known as a bomb thrower, who is harassing hundreds of my people day after day over the boxes hoax.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 You know the boxes hoax, as we call it. They like to say that I am obstructing, which I`m not, because I was working very nicely until the raid on my home.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now is James Trusty. He is one of the former president`s attorneys in the classified documents case. Mr. Trusty, welcome to `Meet The Press.`\par \par \uc2 JAMES TRUSTY, ATTORNEY FOR \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you for giving me a few minutes. I want to start with that "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " report this week. Let me read an excerpt from it, that there is a -- they believe the special counsel is looking at obstruction of justice as a crime in this situation. Investigators now suspect, based on witness statements, security camera footage and other documentary evidence, that boxes including classified material removed from a Mar-a-Lago storage area after the subpoena was served and that Trump personally examined at least some of those boxes. What`s your client`s response to this?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yeah. Well, that it`s nonsense. I mean, look, there`s been a campaign of leaks from DOJ unlike anything I`ve ever seen. I was a prosecutor for 27 years. I`ve spent 17 at this Department of Justice. I don`t recognize it anymore. They are consistently leaking. The angle they`re pushing on the obstruction is to try to create some sort of daylight between Joe Biden`s possession of documents and President Trump`s, and it`s not going to work.\par \par \uc2 I mean they have literally put in everybody in the grand jury you can imagine. They don`t respect any privilege that President Trump holds, and it`s desperately trying to find an obstruction angle that just isn`t there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The former president himself though basically admitted to the crime. Listen to what he said to \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: I can`t imagine you ever saying, bring me some of the boxes that we brought back from the White House, I`d like to look at them. Did you ever do that?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I would have the right to do that. There`s nothing wrong with it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: But I know you, I don`t think you would do it.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I don`t have a lot of time, but I would have the right to do that. I would do that --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, let me move on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Where in the law does he have a right to these classified documents? Presidential Records Act is clear. There is no -- there is no vagary here.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: No. Well, you are right, but I think you are misinterpreting the Presidential Records Act. You notice, he didn`t say I did this. I possessed it. He said I would have the right. He is correct. Under the Presidential Records Act, which is a non-criminal statute, that`s the key. DOJ and political bureaucrats at NARA criminalized something using criminal tools, grand jury subpoenas, search warrants for a statute that says, look, ex- presidents work with NARA, work with them for years, figure out what stuff they get. It took 18 years, I think, for Nixon`s tapes to finally get to NARA.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a delay built into the process as they negotiate in good faith. In this case, NARA was hyper-sensitive, immediately trying to pounce on President Trump to say he`s holding on to things he`s not entitled to, but the remedy for all of that, if you have that fight between the archivists and the former president is civil litigation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: And they jumped right past that with a very happy and willing DOJ.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you say that, but they actually spent over 18 months before asking for a subpoena. He signed -- he had a lawyer sign that he had returned all classified documents. So, the problem is he actively misled NARA, so it forced a situation where they didn`t know where else to turn other than the law. I mean, he himself lied via a lawyer, which has gotten that lawyer in trouble of course.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Well, you`re putting together a whole bunch of hoops that don`t actually connect. Look, NARA had 15 boxes given to them in January of 2022. Fifteen boxes, just, here it is, take it, use these for archives. I`ve looked through those records, those documents.\par \par \uc2 And you have a process. This is what every other president in history has gone through, is a process of communicating and resolving issues as to what stuff he wants to keep and what stuff he wants to give over.\par \par \uc2 TODD: None of it belongs to him. He has this mistaken - a feeling (ph) that anything -- none of it belongs to him. It belongs to the presidency.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Read the personal records act - the Presidential Records Act. There is the ability of any president to deem things as personal, to say, I`m going to keep these as personal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: If NARA disagrees, they can sue in D.C.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: That`s not what happened here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that, but he seems to -- and you seem to be, I think, maybe unintentionally misrepresenting the law when you talk about Nixon. The law was passed after Nixon. Nixon had a case because the law wasn`t in place, the law was effective with the Reagan presidency in 1981.\par \par \uc2 Does the - does \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  think he should get paid? Is that what`s going on here, because Nixon got paid $18 million, he wants -- is he just holding these documents for some sort of financial settlement?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: That`s a cheap shot. Look --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He keeps bringing up this Nixon thing.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Chuck (ph), OK.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: What other reason is he bringing it up?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: So let`s go more modern day, because you are right about the timing of the Presidential Records Act --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  had multiple recordings he kept in his sock drawer of his presidency, while in the Oval Office, talking to foreign leaders, talking to advisers. He basically said, hey, that stays in my sock drawer, it is personal. NARA didn`t blink. \uc2 Obama Foundation\uc2  had millions of documents in a former, I think, furniture store out in Illinois, arguable whether it was ever really truly secure.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 They acknowledged that they had classified documents, probably to the tune of thousands, and there was never any blink by NARA. They in fact said, we are going to give NARA $3.3 million to help move these documents back eventually, when we have a digitized library. That`s never happened.\par \par \uc2 Now, the payments may have happened, but that`s never happened. NARA was perfectly comfortable accepting money for a move that won`t happen for thousands of classified documents in some place near a McDonald`s in Illinois, and never blinked, never any criminal tools, never any criminal referrals.\par \par \uc2 So look, this is -- this has rotten underpinning in terms of bureaucrats being politicized, followed up by an all-too-eager DOJ to criminalize something that is not a crime. And that was the point of the president`s comment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He has done with classified documents what no former president has ever done. I mean, you keep trying to say -- all of those situations you represent, there was actual cooperation by those former presidents to deal with the dispute.\par \par \uc2 In this case, not only is he not cooperating, he is actively not cooperating. And again, he did not comply with a subpoena. That`s -- at the end of the day, that`s the obstruction charge. Why didn`t he comply with the subpoena? There was a subpoena for all classified documents. He did not comply with the subpoena. He was caught not complying with the subpoena because the search warrant ended up ended up turning up more classified documents. How do you explain him defying a subpoena?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Chuck, the Democratic narrative, which you are touting right now, to try to draw a distinction --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s just a set of facts.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Well, let me finish. Let`s --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, why call it a Democratic narrative? It`s a set of facts. He defied a subpoena.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Because you`re ignoring a set of facts. Let`s talk about Delaware. You have got a vice president that has documents for decades in this Chinese-funded Penn-Biden Center, right? You have got absolute obstruction there because we don`t even have any sort of --\par \par \uc2 TODD: How is that obstruction?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Because he had no right to have those documents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. And did he --\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: He didn`t have any ability (ph) --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And did he refuse to turn them over when he found them?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Well, I don`t know. It was hidden for so many months.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Again, did he turn them over? What has the former president --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Did his lawyers turn that over without any sense of chain of custody or any sorts of clearance?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Has the former president turned this over after a legal subpoena?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: We don`t have the leaks coming from Rob Hur`s investigation to know the details of exactly how stuff was turned over.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, it is your defense that to, hey, we think other people broke the law, so let us break the law?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: No, of course not. Of course not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, I just think that doesn`t seem to be a good defense.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Chuck, the point of it is not to say that somebody else broke the law, and we did too, it`s OK. It`s to point out that the common denominator, whether you are talking about New York, Georgia or DOJ is differential treatment for President Trump than anybody in history.\par \par \uc2 And to sit there and whitewash Delaware and to wonder aloud about 1,800 boxes that are in the Delaware University that Biden put there, I mean, we are shut out from information about a much more egregious and intentional violation of the Presidential Records Act by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , and perhaps even having documents that relate to Ukraine of all places.\par \par \uc2 That`s pretty scary stuff. Now I`m not saying anybody should be criminally prosecuted if you`re a president or a vice president for having some of these documents in your possession, or having people transport them to your home.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think the -- let me ask you this, there are some reports that this -- some classified documents were found in a laptop, they turned over a thumb drive. Can you guaranty that no copies of these classified documents are - still remain at Mar-a-Lago? Do you know this for a fact that, right now, there are no more classified documents at Mar-a-Lago?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yeah. Sure. And I can tell you, the leak about what happened with this additional documents or several documents that were found and the thumb drive is absurd. We actually have a federal prosecutor that was in court that completely mischaracterized that. It`s been the same mischaracterization that the media has run with to suggest that President Trump is just sitting on a mountain of documents. It is not true at all.\par \par \uc2 It was a completely innocuous situation. 4,500 pages of documents with several mixed in, that didn`t stand out to a low-level staffer, period. When we found out that she had it, we said, did you ever make copies of this? Did it ever go anywhere? And we chased down the chain of custody in a professional manner and immediately turned that over to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and DOJ. They tried to run with that as obstruction; it is the exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you know for sure whether the former president got involved with the unpacking and moving of the boxes or not?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Yeah. I mean, can you image -- I mean, does anybody in their right mind really think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came down to Mar-a-Lago, while still president I guess in January, and said, hey, these are the boxes I packed. Let`s be careful with those. That goes in the dining room. That goes in the closet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He told Hannity he could.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: No, he --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He is not denying any of this.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: He said I would have the right to. He didn`t pack the boxes. Come on, I mean, that`s like absurd to think that President Trump --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He didn`t -- he didn`t --\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: -- in the middle of January of 2021 --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He didn`t say I want to keep certain documents? You don`t think he did that?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: No. If you look at the boxes as I have --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You make it seem like it`s hard to believe. Do anybody that has covered and spent time with the former president, I know you are new to him - it`s perfectly -- this is who he is.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: So, you are going to vouch for him packing the boxes? Now come on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just asking like, the idea that he wouldn`t do this -- he himself just said it.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Chuck, you listen to what he said.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That he had a right to it.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: This is the third time I`ve had to correct you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: It`s I would have the right. That`s what he said to Hannity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If he didn`t do it, he`d say he didn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Chuck. No, Chuck. He`s making the point that it`s not illegal for a president to possess documents like this.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, I`ll make it clear to you, since you seem to be struggling with it. President Trump didn`t sit there with masking tape and sharpies and say, hey, let`s sit down Indian style and start packing these boxes and send them over.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t think anyone has alleged he was sitting Indian style. I`ll give you that.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Well, great point (ph) - great distinction. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this. The former president has called Jack Smith a lunatic, a fully weaponized monster, a political hit man. You`ve worked with Jack Smith at the Justice Department for quite a few years. Is that the Jack Smith you know?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: Well, here is the point. I would say two things. One is, the president has every right to be frustrated by being politically targeted. I mean, we are crossing a Rubicon where prosecutors can announce, and this is the New York case specifically and Georgia, hey, I`ll run for office on the idea of taking down \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is flipping the whole idea -- I mean, I was a prosecutor for 27 years. They are supposed to be --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jack Smith -- I am not talking about --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: I`m getting to Jack.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: It is -- you are supposed to be judicious. You are supposed to pursue the evidence where it takes you. You use your discretion as a prosecutor to do what`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You stand by the comments he has made about Jack Smith? Calling him a monster? A lunatic?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: That was -- I`m telling you, there is reason to be frustrated, and there is an unethical nature of this prosecution, when the Attorney General holds a press conference, not even a local D.A. in (inaudible) would do because they know it`s against the ethics of being a prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, you stand by the president politicizing these attacks on Jack Smith?\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: No. Look, the president is - he`s a very resilient guy. He`s a very opinionated guy. I`m not going to spend my time worrying about the politics, worrying about --\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: -- how his poll numbers are through the roof. I mean, I don`t care about that stuff. I`m here in the legal lane, and he`s got a right to be frustrated, but he`s not just frustrated for himself. He`s frustrated for the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, James Trusty, the attorney for the former president. I appreciate you coming on and expressing (ph) --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TRUSTY: All right, man. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- the president`s views (ph).\par \par \uc2 When we come back, it has been a divisive week in American politics with battles over abortion, guns, and race and this doesn`t even count the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  legal situation. The panel will break it all down next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is here. Senior Legal Correspondent Laura Jarrett; Amna Nawaz, co-anchor of PBS NewsHour; Brendan Buck, Former Advisor to Speakers Ryan and Boehner on the Republican side of the aisle; and Symone Sanders Townsend, Host of `Symone` on MSNBC. Laura, this is your first time at the table and my word, we have a lot --\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: There`s a lot.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- to discuss.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: There is a lot of legal news this week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thankfully, all right, let`s -- I want to start with these dueling briefs here.\par \par \uc2 [19:30:05]\par \par \uc2 Look, we can get into the politics of how we got to this moment, but let`s go with mifepristone. Is it going to be on the market this year or not?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: So, as of this moment, it is. Nothing has changed. But we could be entering into a period of significant legal uncertainty if that order in Texas is actually upheld within the next week, if another judge doesn`t block it, another Appellate judge doesn`t block it. Then it is as if the drug was never approved in the first place because he has put the effective date of it on hold, which is not exactly what the plaintiffs in this case had actually asked for. But that`s what he`s done.\par \par \uc2 And yet, at the same time, you have another federal judge, with the exact same level of authority and jurisdiction, who has said the exact opposite. And so, that`s why I think folks are likely predicting it could be headed to the Supreme Court`s doorstep.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Will it go through the appeals -- each -- the two circuits first, or does this get fast-tracked?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, it depends on what the Justice Department does. I think you`re going to see that this week. It`s a strategic call.\par \par \uc2 They have already filed a notice of appeal in the Fifth Circuit. But we know the Fifth Circuit tends to lean conservative. At the same time, now that they have filed that notice, they could also decide to go straight to the Supreme Court and not wait for the Fifth Circuit to act. It`s just a discretionary judgment call whether they want to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Could the Supreme Court just step in and say, no, no, no. We`ll take this now. We see this dispute? Or is it --\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: They`ve got to wait for the Justice Department to tee it up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what`s the -- this is a conservative majority. Is it in line with the federal judge from Amarillo, Texas?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, it`s interesting because the whole rationale for when they overturned Roe was to say, let`s leave this to the states. Let`s let the states decide what to do.\par \par \uc2 This judge in Amarillo, Judge Kacsmaryk, has effectively set a national policy for the entire country and has decided the \uc2 FDA\uc2  simply didn`t have the authority to do this for the entire country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So we throw Wisconsin in with this, Amna, and now we have a full- fledged -- it is hard for me to imagine, and I say this with some trepidation, but it certainly looks like 2024`s going to be another abortion rights election.\par \par \uc2 AMNA NAWAZ, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think that`s right. I mean, I think if anything we saw from Wisconsin from the last year of special elections, and certainly from the November midterms, abortion remains a politically potent issue. And we saw that all the way back to Kansas last August, Michigan and Minnesota earning trifectas in the midterms as well. And then last week, as you mentioned, in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 But I think the context here that Laura is hitting on is particularly important because the unprecedented nature of what we`re seeing is what`s mobilizing people to turn up on this issue, right? You`ve never had a court, correct me if I`m wrong here, legal expert, weigh in and overturn an \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval in this way. And now, we`re seeing that uncertainty Laura was mentioning as well, that`s what`s guiding people to come out because they don`t know what their future is, where you live increasingly determines how you live and your rights as a woman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brendan, "The Wall Street Journal" Editorial Board, who would like to see Republicans get some power again politically, warned them, Republicans had better get their abortion position straight and more in line with where the voters are, or they will face another disappointment in 2024.\par \par \uc2 They say total ban is a loser in swing state. I think total ban is a loser in every state. I don`t think this is just about the swing states anymore. Kansas, Michigan, Wisconsin. There`s a pattern here.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yeah, I don`t know how many more warning signs we need that this is a political problem for us. And yet, we keep going deeper and deeper in this direction.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you think it is? I have a theory that it`s the infrastructure the party has relied upon, the pro-life community --\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- for so long for money and organizing, that they don`t know how to quit it.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yeah. We`ve made a deal with this part of the party for a very long time and made a lot of promises. And I think a lot of people -- look at it in a political sense, there are a lot of people who firmly believe that this is the right moral position to have and so you can`t just walk away from that. But if you take a bigger picture look at it, the politics of it are devastating for us.\par \par \uc2 We can`t win without them. But obviously, winning with them is coming at a significant cost.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Symone, feels like a long way away, remember when Joe Biden`s abortion position was getting questioned --\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS-TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST, "SYMONE": Oh my, goodness.\par \par \uc2 TODD: --by Democratic activists in the primary?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I remember vividly. I think I was there --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess we`re a long way away from that.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Very long way away, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think what Brendan said should be underscored here because for a very long time, this is what the Republican Party apparatus organized around. They did not organize around jailing women for getting an abortion, fining and jailing, potentially jailing doctors. They did not organize around forcing girls to have babies by their rapists. But that is exactly what is happening because of the positions they have advocated for.\par \par \uc2 So when "The Wall Street Journal" says, they`ve got to get their position straight with the voters, I`m confused and don`t understand because I think they`ve been quite clear on what their position is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I think that`s the problem though, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yeah. We said that we were going to send this back to the states because we were going to have some reasonable limitations on abortion. And now state by state doesn`t look very reasonable and we are at the mercy of a bunch of state legislatures. And there`s not a very good way to run a party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Speaking of being at the mercy of the local state legislatures, this situation in Tennessee, Symone, look, there are loud echoes from our recent past in the south and in the `60s.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:02]\par \par \uc2 But this also feels like the inevitable moment that you have when you have these gerrymandered super-majorities that have become calcified.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because this is what it felt like: a whole bunch of people who just don`t deal with dissent.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Absolutely. I mean, look, I was down in Nashville. I spoke with the Tennessee Three and state legislators there. What is happening in Tennessee, and frankly across the South, is in fact Jim Crow. What Jim Crow was blocking Black people from the ability to have participation in their government, disenfranchising lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 And it`s again -- it`s not just Tennessee. Look at what`s happening in Mississippi coming into Jackson and the state legislature stepping in. Look at what`s -- Governor Abbott in Texas. Look at what`s happening in Florida. This is an all out assault.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Government Abbott -- I know we wanted to be quick, and I appreciate you doing that with time -- but he looks like he`s okay if there`s a pardon of a Travis County, a blue county, prosecution, a murder conviction of a \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protester.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: He put out a statement and said, I welcome the pardon on my desk so I can sign it. And so it is an assault. And I think these young people, young state legislators, who stood up, they have galvanized the movement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brendan, not a single national Republican leader has supported what Tennessee has done. It is no doubt in my mind the silence is at least sending a message of, oh my God, that looks terrible, but I don`t think it`s helping the party`s image.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: No. I mean, what do they do? I mean, this happened in Congress in 2016. We had a sit-in where Democrats took over the House floor and we had members who wanted us to arrest them, wanted us to arrest John Lewis on the House floor. But you realize that`s a bad look. And here`s another situation where you have a statue legislature who is defining the party. No one is in charge. And there`s no consequences for any of these legislatures.\par \par \uc2 As you said, their districts are such that they`re not going to be voted out because they did this --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Half of them didn`t face any opponent, Brendan. Half of them didn`t face any opponent at all. It`s stunning.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yeah. Well, this is again a situation where there`s no infrastructure. There`s nobody calling the shots. We are being defined as extreme and it`s why Republicans are on the run in just about everywhere across the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. When we come back, despite the widening partisan divide, there is one area this week where U.S. lawmakers were unified, and intentionally so -- defying China`s display of military force in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 My exclusive interview with House Foreign Affairs Chairman Mike McCaul from Taiwan is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:40:14]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Despite the domestic political divisions on full display this week, the effort that the United States made this week to show a united front on Taiwan was extraordinary, when you think about it, as lawmakers at home and abroad met with Taiwan`s president and promised a U.S. commitment to the island`s security. It was bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 In response, China launched three days of military exercises in the Taiwan Strait, deploying at least 71 fighter jets into Taiwan`s airspace and surrounding the island with war ships, all in an attempt to simulate a naval blockade.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, I spoke exclusively to House Foreign Affairs chairman Mike McCaul, at the end of his congressional delegation`s visit to the island of Taiwan, and I began by asking him about whether the drills represented an escalation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): We have a large number of sorties. Those are Chinese aircraft, fighter jets in the air right now as I speak from the island. And this is in response to President Tsai`s trip to the United States, but also our delegation`s visit to Taiwan and with President Tsai.\par \par \uc2 This is an intimidation tactic that they`re known for. The size of this one is quite large, one of the largest ones we`ve seen. And -- but it`s not going -- it`s not going to intimidate us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: We have every right to be here, to meet with President Tsai. And it actually strengthens our resolve.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Taiwanese themselves seem to be downplaying this. Look, you`ve got a first-hand look. How would you assess their defensive capabilities right now?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: They`re not where they need to be. If we`re going to have deterrence for peace, we need to get these weapons into Taiwan. I`ve -- I sign off on all foreign military weapon sales. Twenty-two weapon systems over three years ago, Chuck, that have yet to get into Taiwan, onto the island.\par \par \uc2 Now, that will provide deterrence to Chairman Xi to think twice, you know, about an invasion. And, and secondly is the combat training that is occurring on the island. We need to ramp that up to a larger scale so they can provide that projection of strength and deterrence. They`re not where they need to be right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you have a sense of what the Taiwanese people want? And I say that in that we know pre-sort of what China did before it took over Hong Kong, it was always this sense that you know, a lot of people in Taiwan wanted some sort of relationship with mainland China that was negotiated, that was sort of respectful. Is that what they still want? Or do they think some sort of military confrontation is now going to be inevitable?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: Well, they don`t want a military confrontation. We certainly don`t want that. I think after Hong Kong, it was a wake-up call for the people of Taiwan. President Tsai, we spoke with her today. That obviously helped her in her re-election.\par \par \uc2 But I also think Putin`s invasion in the Ukraine was an eye-opener, right? It -- it woke up the Taiwanese people that now you`re seeing what we haven`t seen since World War II, and that is dictators invading sovereign territory and getting away with it. So, Putin in Ukraine, wake-up call here. Chairman Xi in his addresses to his Congress about wanting reunification of Taiwan to China, I think they`re very nervous.\par \par \uc2 Now there`s a political debate here, the two different parties. One party wants to talk to China. President Tsai`s party does not want to be a part of China. And I think the next elections in next January are going to be extremely important because I do believe with the former President Ma in China right now, China`s going to try to influence this next election and take over the island without a shot fired.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 I want to play something that Speaker McCarthy said, because he -- it seemed to at least shift a perception of where he is on the issue of Ukraine. Let me play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I think what`s happening in Ukraine is an atrocity, and I think Ukraine -- not just Ukraine, the world has to win there. What Russia has done is wrong. And a phase that I used, the blank check, I use that for anything. Well, I look at every dollar of taxpayers that we would use. But the one thing I know that, in Ukraine, we have to win, because it also would save Taiwan at the same time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you reassured now, and should the Ukrainians, should President Zelenskyy be reassured that House Republicans are not going to stand in the way of more aid to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MCCAUL: You know, I traveled with Kevin, Speaker McCarthy, to Poland, Romania. He`s always believed this, felt this way. When you`re over here, Chuck -- when you talk to -- and I`ve talked to the prime ministers and the presidents of Japan, you know, South Korea, and Taiwan, what`s happening in Ukraine will determine what happens in Taiwan and the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:05]\par \par \uc2 I think the prime minister of Japan going down to Ukraine to signal their support -- and he said himself, what happens in Ukraine today will happen in the Far East tomorrow. I believe the best deterrence to Chairman Xi is a failure for Putin in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: As you might suspect, I have a fuller interview with the Chairman, with Mike McCaul, and you can see the entire thing on MeetthePress.com.\par \par \uc2 When we come back Wisconsin is not the only state where abortion was a losing issue for Republicans in a post-Roe era. We`ll break down that lessons that a string of setbacks in some keys states have for the GOP ahead of 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time. By electing Janet Protasiewicz to the state Supreme Court this week, Wisconsin voters handed liberals their first majority on the court in 15 years.\par \par \uc2 And it gives them the power to decide the fate of many pivotal issues in the state. Most central, of course, to the campaign was abortion rights. So how did this issue deliver the rare double-digit victory for the left in a perpetual battleground state and the most polarized state in the union of Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 Let me show you because it`s not a story that just stays in Wisconsin. Look, here`s the 2020 presidential map there. Biden carried 14 counties. Here`s the Supreme Court map. Protasiewicz carried 23 counties.\par \par \uc2 And look where it is. Any urban area essentially became an abortion rights supporter here. Kenosha, she carried. This is where La Crosse is. This is where Green Bay is.\par \par \uc2 These are areas \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  couldn`t carry. The old days, Democrats used to carry those areas, but not anymore. But they did for this issue.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you Kansas. We had five counties that Biden carried in Kansas. The abortion referendum in August of `22 showed you other urban and suburban areas also supported abortion rights. Wichita, Topeka, Kansas City suburbs, you can see.\par \par \uc2 Michigan had an abortion referendum along with the governor`s race. Again, here`s the map of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And look at the map of the abortion referendum. Basically, in each case, wherever there`s a college town, wherever there`s even a small size city, the abortion rights side seem to do well.\par \par \uc2 [09:50:04]\par \par \uc2 We see it in Michigan. We see it in Wisconsin, and we saw it in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 Then, there`s the issue of turnout. This is the Wisconsin Supreme Court election. In 2019, they had one of these in the spring: 1.2 million votes, 2023, 1.8 million voters. The biggest difference, Dane County and the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2  in Madison. Young voters turned out in droves. That made the difference.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, it was overshadowed by the spectacle of Donald Trump`s surrender at a New York City courthouse, but President Biden may have shifted his reelection timeline again. How much longer does he plan to wait before formally getting in?\par \par \uc2 Panel is back after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 There`s been a lot of political news this week. I do want to get a little more politics out of the way here.\par \par \uc2 Symone, President Biden`s reelection numbers are just not great. There are more Democrats who would like to see somebody else than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I know we`ve just spent a lot of the show, at least two different polls out this week again.\par \par \uc2 And every week that goes by that he doesn`t announce his reelection and, you know, the whispers begin. What`s going on? What are you hearing? Is he going to announce sooner, or is he just enjoying the amount of time he seems to have to do this?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I am hearing that there -- that there are contours of a reelection campaign being put together. But it will be President Biden who will make his announcement.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:01]\par \par \uc2 Obviously, I think he has a trip to Ireland coming up. So anyone looking to see an announcement in the next two weeks, honey, you don`t announce a presidential campaign and then hop an international trip. It doesn`t work like that. So my guess is something early -- early May perhaps.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are the Trump legal -- because that`s what I was being told, was early May. Are the Trump legal problems at all making them think, oh, stay -- let`s wait on being a political candidate?"\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: I don`t think that the -- I don`t think that the Biden folks are concerned about -- are making decisions based on what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is experiencing. But I think the reality is that this week demonstrates that the president and vice president actually are in a very strong position. It was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who were down speaking with the state legislatures in Tennessee. And they told me that that support spoke volumes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to move to another story that broke this week. \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  had an extensive story about a long time relationship he`s had with a billionaire donor, conservative donor to a lot of causes.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  took yachts and private planes, all sorts of trips. I think one of the more egregious elements of this story was funding an organization that Ginni Thomas ended up getting a salary -- a six figure salary for.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Justice Thomas` response: Early in my tenure at the Court, I was advised that this sort of personal hospitality from close personal friends who did not have business before the Court was not reportable.\par \par \uc2 Amna, how serious is this? And is this going to put a spotlight on the Supreme Court that justices are going to feel uncomfortable about?\par \par \uc2 NAWAZ: I think it`s definitely going to increase the heat of the spotlight on the Supreme Court that`s already been there. And to be clear, you know, there had been some reporting around this for years. "L.A. Times" was reporting on this back in the early 2000s, but certainly not to this extent, and not to the degree that "\uc2 ProPublica\uc2 " dug into it.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, if you`re the average American reading about this, this is going to feed into the perception that there are biases and there are problems within the Supreme Court, an institution that has been slowly losing trust in the American public, as have all institutions of power in America more recently.\par \par \uc2 I think the bigger question is, what are the rules? What is the law? What is the policy around these rules for Supreme Court justices? The answer right now seems to be they aren`t really clear.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Laura, look, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  is the head of the judiciary branch, not just the chief justice of the Supreme Court. He`s been very -- he pushes back whenever Congress attempts to put some constraints on the judiciary here. But he also is well aware that he doesn`t want the court`s reputation to be sullied.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Well, the fact of the matter is that, unlike other federal judges, the Supreme Court justices are not subject to the same rules. There is no ethics code, despite protestations from Democratic members of Congress regularly trying to make sure that they get one. They don`t have the votes in Congress to get that done right now.\par \par \uc2 And so, essentially, Roberts has said, look, we look to the ethics code as guidance, but in reality, they`re policing themselves. They`re supposed to disclose gifts. But this whole issue of personal hospitality, which is what \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is alluding to, it was a major loophole until last month.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brendan, the fact of the matter is Congress has much stricter requirements than any of the judges, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: When I worked on the Hill, you could accept a coffee mug, maybe a t- shirt. That`s about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You couldn`t get a yacht?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: You couldn`t fly around the world -- yeah, with a billionaire. Yeah. I think that`s the scandal here is that there really are no rules. And I think there will certainly be -- there`s a lot of interest recently in legislating congressional ethics. I could very easily see that focus shifting over into the Supreme Court. There has to be some level of accountability here. And clearly, it doesn`t exist.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. What a week. And it was supposed to be a holiday.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Enjoy the rest of your holiday weekend. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230409ej4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc26}{\*\bkmkend toc26}\b \uc2 Abortion Rights Activists Vowing To Fight, Others Celebrate The Rule, As The Biden Administration Says It Will Appeal\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Monica Alba \par \uc2 526 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART: President Biden is vowing to fight the Texas ruling. It`s the beginning of what`s expected to be yet another contentious round of court battles over abortion. Monica Alba reports from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: The battle over abortion rights could again be headed for the Supreme Court, with the Department of Justice planning to appeal the Texas ruling that would jeopardize access to the most commonly used abortion pill, setting up a legal showdown, less than a year after the court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [18:35:07]\par \par \uc2 President Biden vowed to fight the decision, writing Friday, if it were to stand, there will be virtually no prescription approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  that would be safe from these kinds of political ideological attacks. The Texas case now goes to the conservative Fifth Circuit, but in the short term, the drug is still available. It`s unclear whether the Washington State ruling which asks for the medication to be kept on the market will also be appealed. If both are heard at the circuit level, it`s unlikely this will be resolved in the courts anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LAM\uc2 , FORMER SOUTHERN DISTRICT OF CALIFORNIA U.S. ATTORNEY: I would not expect there to be much change in the actual law for at least several months, perhaps upwards of a year.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: With the Supreme Court, which currently has a conservative majority watching to see how that plays out.\par \par \uc2 LAM: What is most likely to happen is that there will be a split in the circuits. So the Fifth Circuit will rule one way, the Ninth Circuit will rule a different way. And the Supreme Court will say now there is a conflict between two circuits. And that means we should probably hear this case and decide the issue on the merits.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: As Vice President Harris, who has been the administration`s leading voice on abortion access, slammed the Texas decision as a dangerous precedent.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: There is no question that the President and I are going to stand with the women of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Monica joins us now from the White House. What else Monica, could the Biden administration do here?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Well, the Justice Department could file an emergency appeal with the High Court, while it also tries to get the Texas order blocked by the Fifth Circuit, a strategic call that likely will become apparent in the next week or so. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Monica Alba at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230411ej48000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc27}{\*\bkmkend toc27}\b \uc2 Major Fallout After A Federal Judge Suspends Approval Of The Commonly Used Abortion Pill Set To Begin Within Day\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [18:30:17]\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, NBC NEWS HOST: Tonight, major fallout after a federal judge suspends approval of the commonly used abortion pill set to begin within day. Abortion rights activists vowing to fight, others celebrate the rule, as the Biden administration says it will appeal. New confusion today after another judge rules in favor of the pill. Will the Supreme Court decide the issue? Is their mole in the Pentagon? The race to find out who may be leaking top secret intelligence, the Department of Justice and Pentagon now investigate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They put ourselves at risk, they put our allies at risk.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: China`s major escalation launching massive military drills off Taiwan`s coast just as U.S. lawmakers visit the island. Our correspondent is there.\par \par \uc2 Gunfire at the beach, six people shot most of them teenagers enjoying a senior skip day. Video so the chaotic moments shots were fired.\par \par \uc2 Terrifying new footage of trees falling at the Masters are these the luckiest people on earth. Watch his three trees just barely missed them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Trees are falling off of the ground.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Why play was halted again today. And the need for speed. The new push across the country to raise the speed limit. How fast is too fast.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Jose Diaz-Balart.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Good evening, a new front has opened to the nation`s ongoing battle over abortion rights. Not even a year after Roe v. Wade was overturned leaving it up to the states to determine whether abortions are legal. A federal judge issued a ruling that would halt the use of the commonly used abortion pill nationwide. The decision came down late on a Friday of a holiday weekend. But the reaction today was swift with some small protests across the country. The suspension doesn`t go into effect for seven days. And the Biden administration is vowing to stop it. The case seems destined to rocket up the legal system possibly all the way to the Supreme Court. We have two reports tonight beginning with Dasha Burns, who has been with activists on both sides.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight activist outraged over a Texas judge`s ruling late Friday, suspending the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the abortion pill Mifeprex generically known as mifepristone. Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk writing in his opinion, the court does not second guess the FDA`s decision making lightly. But here the \uc2 FDA\uc2  acquiesced on its legitimate safety concerns. But he stopped short of an immediate nationwide ban, instead allowing the federal government seven days to appeal the decision.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is an assault on the humanity of women. This is an assault on basic reproductive rights. It`s also an assault on democracy.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The outcomes celebrated by the plaintiffs.\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM LEAD ATTORNEY: This is a significant victory for the doctors and medical associations we represent. And more importantly, the health and safety of women and girls.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: We sat down with \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  before the ruling the conservative group behind the lawsuit that was also instrumental in overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 BAPTIST: The goal of this lawsuit is to protect American women and girls from dangerous chemical abortion drugs. It`s not we are not seeking a nationwide abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The drug has been approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  since 2000. Since then, more than 5 million women have taken mifepristone with 28 deaths associated with the drug reported. It`s the most common method for terminating pregnancies in the U.S. But just minutes after the Texas ruling came down a federal judge in Washington State dropped a contradictory decision protecting access to the abortion pill in 17 states and Washington D.C., the dueling rulings now so in chaos and confusion across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Dasha joins us now from New York City`s Washington Square Park where, Dasha, a small group of protesters have gathered?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: That`s right, Jose. Activists here and nationwide criticizing the judges timing, the ruling coming ahead of a holiday weekend. Still protesters here telling me the stakes are too high to stay home as this decision could impact abortion access here in New York and nationwide. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Dasha Burns in New York. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230411ej48000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc28}{\*\bkmkend toc28}\b \uc2 The Issue Of Abortion, In Idaho, Doctors Are Facing Difficult Decisions\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 574 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: On the issue of abortion, in Idaho, doctors are facing difficult decisions. Some choosing to leave the state due to its restrictive abortion laws. Kristen Dahlgren explains.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Not far from Idaho`s Capitol building, Dr. Kylie Cooper and her family are packing up and moving out of the state. The high-risk pregnancy specialist says she can no longer safely practice under one of the nation`s most restrictive abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 DR. KYLIE COOPER, OBSTETRICIAN-GYNECOLOGIST: My husband and I are finding ourselves having conversations about what we would do if I were charged with a felony.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Idaho only has exceptions for rape, incest, or averting the mother`s death.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Do I make the medically appropriate decision and commit a felony or do I follow the law and then watch as this person in front of me gets sicker and sicker?\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: A recent survey shows about half of OBGYN`s in Idaho are considering leaving the state in the next year. All but two of those polled say the state`s abortion ban is the reason.\par \par \uc2 So, you don`t think doctors are leaving the state.\par \par \uc2 RAUL LABRADOR\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL, IDAHO: I don`t think there`s a huge outflow of doctors.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: GOP State Attorney General \uc2 Raul Labrador\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LABRADOR: I think maybe they just need more education on what the law allows.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: So, you`re saying the doctors are misinformed?\par \par \uc2 LABRADOR: I think I`ve heard a lot of things that that borders on misinformation. But I think doctors are safe as long as they`re following the law.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Many doctors say the law can be confusing. In fact, just hours after talking to us, Labrador withdrew a widely circulated letter to a state senator, in which he stated the law prohibits doctors from providing abortion pills or referring women out of state for abortions. Now saying it was not intended as a guidance document, and an updated law made it moot.\par \par \uc2 For Dr. Cooper, it`s a hard day leaving patients like the one who recently asked --\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Is it safe for me to be pregnant in Idaho? And my unequivocal answer was no, it`s not.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But she doesn`t think it`s safe for doctors either. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News, Boise, Idaho.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`ll be back in 60 seconds with a chilling new video showing a tech executive moments after he was stabbed. Plus, the shocking moment that shut down the Masters.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: At the Masters Golf Tournament in Georgia today, this is a terrifying moment high winds knocked down three trees near the 17th tee. Take a look. You can see people scrambling to get out of the way. There are no reports of injuries, but tournament play when suspended and the grounds evacuated because of dangerously high winds, lightning, and approaching thunderstorms.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230411ej470008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc29}{\*\bkmkend toc29}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for April 7, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Priscilla Thompson, Raf Sanchez, Laura Jarrett,  Kristen Dahlgren, Miguel Almaguer, Tom Costello \par \uc2 3,643 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the growing political firestorm over lawmaker expulsions in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Outraged after two Black legislators who were expelled for interrupting debate over gun violence, while a White member kept her seat. Maybe she says because of the color of her skin. Republicans pushing back. Vice President Harris making a quickly arranged visit to Nashville. Plus, the new questions about if the expelled lawmakers could be reinstated.\par \par \uc2 Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  speaking out saying he did nothing wrong by accepting and not disclosing luxury trips over two decades paid for by a Republican megadonor.\par \par \uc2 Violence escalating in the Middle East. Two Israeli sisters shot to death in their car in the West Bank. A new attack in Tel Aviv after new Israeli airstrikes on southern Lebanon and Gaza.\par \par \uc2 The shocking moment at the Masters. High winds toppling three trees falling next to spectators.\par \par \uc2 The newly released surveillance video showing the tech executive pleading for help moments after he was stabbed.\par \par \uc2 An urgent new warning to parents about the dangers of sleeping with their infants.\par \par \uc2 And the stunning act of forgiveness. My story about the man who called for the release of his father`s killer.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome, everyone. The expulsion from office of two Tennessee Democratic state reps over their boisterous call for gun safety reform has sparked national outrage on the left and left Tennessee House Republicans defending against accusations of unequal treatment and racial motivations.\par \par \uc2 The GOP supermajority voting out to Black state representatives while sparing a White female lawmaker who faced the same accusations, accused of leading a disorderly protest for stronger gun laws in the House chamber last week. The deaths of six people in a Nashville school shooting less than two weeks ago have sparked a wave of activism in Nashville and elsewhere and demands for stricter gun measures. But the extraordinary step of selectively removing outspoken lawmakers has cast a chill being felt all the way to the White House tonight.\par \par \uc2 Priscilla Thompson now has late details from Nashville.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, growing fallout in Tennessee after last night`s historic vote. Two Black lawmakers were expelled by the state legislature while Representative Gloria Johnson was not.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA JOHNSON (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Maybe I`m a White lady. I don`t know what else it was.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The trio was accused of breaking house rules and disorderly behavior.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: No action?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No peace.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: After protesting for stricter gun laws in the well of the chamber days after a Nashville school shooting left six people dead.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN PEARSON (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Surely justice will come.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Republican Lowell Russell who voted to expel Representatives Justin Jones and Justin Pearson but not Johnson says Johnson`s actions were not as severe and did not warrant an expulsion.\par \par \uc2 LOWELL RUSSELL She didn`t shout and she didn`t bring the megaphone in. She didn`t use the megaphone. She didn`t engage on the people in the gallery.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: But Jones says this type of action has never been taken against White colleagues who he says have committed far more severe infractions.\par \par \uc2 JONES: One was admitted child molester, one was convicted of domestic violence, one peed in another member`s chair, and none of them were ever expelled.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: A concern echoed by Pearson.\par \par \uc2 PEARSON: The rules will be the in all and be all of this house, then that needs to apply equitably to everyone.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Jones calling the expulsions a political lynching.\par \par \uc2 JONES: A state in which the \uc2 Klu Klux Klan\uc2  was founded is now attempting another power grab by silencing the two youngest Black Representatives.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Republicans say unequivocally, this was not about race.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LAMBERTH, (R-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: Our members literally didn`t look at the ethnicity of the members that were up for expulsion.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: The people united will never be divided.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Still, their actions are drawing widespread condemnation from Democrats across the country, including President Biden, calling the expulsion shocking, undemocratic, and without precedent. Those expelled lawmakers` futures could soon be back in the hands of voters determined by a special election should they decide to run again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Priscilla joining me now. As we await that special election, Priscilla, what happens now?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (on camera): Lester, counties will appoint interim representatives in the meantime. Already, Nashville`s Metro Council has scheduled a meeting for Monday where Justin Jones is expected to be reinstated immediately. And we`re getting late word tonight that President Biden has spoken with all three of those Democrats, inviting them to the White House. Lester?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:09]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Priscilla Thompson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Escalating violence tonight in the Middle East with deadly attacks in Tel Aviv and the occupied West Bank. And Israel striking back at Palestinian militants in southern Lebanon and Gaza. Raf Sanchez is in Israel with late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, a new wave of attacks shaking Israel. In Tel Aviv, Israeli police say they shot dead an attacker who rammed his car into tourists on a city promenade, killing at least one person. Hours earlier, two Israeli sisters killed in a suspected Palestinian ambush in the occupied West Bank. Their mother flown to the hospital in critical condition and fighting for her life.\par \par \uc2 Israel`s Prime Minister at the scene of the attack warning our enemies are once again testing us. The wave of violence sweeping the Holy Lands darkening which should be a sacred week for believers celebrating Ramadan, Passover, and Easter. It began on Wednesday with Israeli forces storming into the Al-Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem, Islam`s third holiest site.\par \par \uc2 And this morning Israeli jets pounding targets in Gaza and unusually southern Lebanon. Airstrikes across two regions aimed at the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militant group, which Israel says was responsible for a barrage of rockets from Lebanon, the largest in nearly 20 years. \uc2 U.N\uc2 . peacekeepers in Lebanon assessing the damage and trying to prevent a rush to all-out war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANCHEZ (on camera): And Israeli police say all the victims in Tel Aviv tonight we`re tourists visiting during this holy period. Meanwhile, a major manhunt is underway for the gunman who killed those two sisters. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Raf Sanchez tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Also, this evening, Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  says he violated no disclosure laws by not reporting luxury vacations he reportedly took four years paid for by a top Republican donor who he says is a longtime family friend. Laura Jarrett has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  breaking his silence after a report detailed his secret lavish vacations paid for by a top Republican donor. Thomas writes, real estate mogul \uc2 Harlan Crow\uc2  is among his dearest friends and that colleagues advised him this sort of personal hospitality from close personal friends who did not have business before the court did not need to be reported.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , Thomas joined \uc2 Crow\uc2  on swanky trips for over two decades, including to this Lakeside resort, taking private jet rides, and island hopping in Indonesia on this super yacht, virtually none of which was disclosed. The luxury travel, a stark contrast to the more modest image the conservative justice has projected.\par \par \uc2 CLARENCE THOMAS\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: I prefer going across the rural areas. I prefer the R.V. parks. I prefer the \uc2 Walmart\uc2  parking lots to the beaches and things like that. There`s something normal to me about it.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: NBC News has not independently verified the \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  report, which claims Thomas`s fellow travelers include GOP donors and corporate executives.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN ELLIOTT, REPORTER, \uc2 PROPUBLICA\uc2 : There`s also just an enormous amount we don`t know about what happens on these trips and who gets invited along.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Crow says Thomas never asked for any of this hospitality. And he has never sought to influence Justice Thomas on any legal or political issue. The Supreme Court does not have an ethics code. And under federal law, Justices don`t have to disclose meals, stays at private homes, or other entertainment that is personal hospitality.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Thomas says he always sought to comply with disclosure guidelines and intends to follow new rules that trips on private jets must be reported.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Laura, there are calls for more rule changes now.\par \par \uc2 JARRETT (on camera): That`s right, Lester. Democratic lawmakers want to pass a bill that would require the court to establish a formal code of conduct. But the Chief Justice says they don`t need one, effectively leaving his colleagues to police themselves.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Laura, we`re also watching some breaking news about a high-stakes lawsuit in Texas over the abortion pill. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 JARRETT: Yes, that`s right, Lester. This just coming down minutes ago. This decision a partial victory for a group of anti-abortion advocates and doctors that had asked a federal judge to block access to the drug most widely used to induce abortions. Now, the plaintiffs had asked that judge to remove it from the market entirely nationwide immediately, but the judge didn`t go that far.\par \par \uc2 Instead, he has suspended the original approval of the drug, acknowledging the plaintiffs do have a right to sue in this case, acknowledging their arguments about safety concerns, but there`s no immediate effect of this ruling. The judge has instead giving the Justice Department seven days to appeal, something Biden administration has almost certainly going to do very quickly. They have signaled that previously.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura Jarrett, thank you for all that.\par \par \uc2 On the issue of abortion, in Idaho, doctors are facing difficult decisions. Some choosing to leave the state due to its restrictive abortion laws. Kristen Dahlgren explains.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Not far from Idaho`s Capitol building, Dr. Kylie Cooper and her family are packing up and moving out of the state. The high-risk pregnancy specialist says she can no longer safely practice under one of the nation`s most restrictive abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 DR. KYLIE COOPER, OBSTETRICIAN-GYNECOLOGIST: My husband and I are finding ourselves having conversations about what we would do if I were charged with a felony.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Idaho only has exceptions for rape, incest, or averting the mother`s death.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Do I make the medically appropriate decision and commit a felony or do I follow the law and then watch as this person in front of me gets sicker and sicker?\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: A recent survey shows about half of OBGYN`s in Idaho are considering leaving the state in the next year. All but two of those polled say the state`s abortion ban is the reason.\par \par \uc2 So, you don`t think doctors are leaving the state.\par \par \uc2 RAUL LABRADOR\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL, IDAHO: I don`t think there`s a huge outflow of doctors.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: GOP State Attorney General \uc2 Raul Labrador\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 LABRADOR: I think maybe they just need more education on what the law allows.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: So, you`re saying the doctors are misinformed?\par \par \uc2 LABRADOR: I think I`ve heard a lot of things that that borders on misinformation. But I think doctors are safe as long as they`re following the law.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Many doctors say the law can be confusing. In fact, just hours after talking to us, Labrador withdrew a widely circulated letter to a state senator, in which he stated the law prohibits doctors from providing abortion pills or referring women out of state for abortions. Now saying it was not intended as a guidance document, and an updated law made it moot.\par \par \uc2 For Dr. Cooper, it`s a hard day leaving patients like the one who recently asked --\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Is it safe for me to be pregnant in Idaho? And my unequivocal answer was no, it`s not.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But she doesn`t think it`s safe for doctors either. Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News, Boise, Idaho.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`ll be back in 60 seconds with a chilling new video showing a tech executive moments after he was stabbed. Plus, the shocking moment that shut down the Masters.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: At the Masters Golf Tournament in Georgia today, this is a terrifying moment high winds knocked down three trees near the 17th tee. Take a look. You can see people scrambling to get out of the way. There are no reports of injuries, but tournament play when suspended and the grounds evacuated because of dangerously high winds, lightning, and approaching thunderstorms.\par \par \uc2 Police in San Francisco are combing through disturbing newly released surveillance video hoping to find new clues in the stabbing death this week of a Silicon Valley tech titan. Miguel Almaguer reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This Chilling surveillance video could be a key piece of evidence. The dailymail.com which obtained CCTV says after tech executive Bob Lee was stabbed multiple times, he appears to try and wave down a passing car for help before the motorists drives away, wounded and stumbling into clear view around 2:35 a.m. Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The location is 365 Main, across from Folsom and Harrison. There`s been stabbing.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The San Francisco Standard says Lee called 911 and screamed help, someone stabbed me.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE JENKINS, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, SAN FRANCISCO: That 911 call of course gives us a sense of time, but unfortunately unless someone says who did it or what happened specifically, its usefulness can be limited.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: San Francisco`s D.A. says she cannot confirm reports a knife was found near the crime scene, but says cellphone data is key.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: We want to know who he has been communicating with just before he was stabbed, who he was with, what cell phone towers that cell phone was hitting. Those are all critical pieces of evidence for us tracing backward to the moment that this stabbing occurred.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:05]\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Perhaps best known for creating the mobile payment service, Cash App --\par \par \uc2 BOB LEE, TECH EXECUTIVE: We still need solutions for the unbanked.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: -- Lee was a tech icon who used to live near the area where he was killed. The police chief facing pressure as the city safety remains under blaring scrutiny amid a rash of negative headlines.\par \par \uc2 BILL SCOTT, CHIEF, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE: We do have some good things to follow up on his case. And they are working tirelessly.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Tonight, the high-profile murder mystery of a tech titan. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We are back in a moment with a warning for parents about the big risks over where your baby sleeps.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We have an important warning for parents. Doctors are reporting a jump in infant deaths linked to unsafe sleeping conditions and co-sleeping with parents. Tom Costello now with what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Cook Children`s in Fort Worth calls it alarming. 30 babies in just 15 months who likely died during unsafe sleeping conditions suffocating next to the adult in the bedding or pillows.\par \par \uc2 DR. GEETHA DEVDAS, EMERGENCY MEDICINE PHYSICIAN, COOK CHILDREN`S MEDICAL CENTER: Co-sleeping is not something that we recommend. We recommend that babies really have their own bed, crib, or bassinet to sleep in.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Last month, the CDC reported a 15 percent increase of sudden infant death cases among African Americans in 2020 during the pandemic. Providers say multi-generational homes where parents sleep with children and have poor access to health care could be contributing factors. Nationwide, each year, 3400 sleep-related infant deaths. The risk is five to 10 times higher while bed-sharing with an adult even higher on a couch or chair.\par \par \uc2 Tragically, it happened to pediatric cardiologist Sam Hankey in 2010. His 3-week-old son Charlie fell asleep on his chest as Sam also slept.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:30]\par \par \uc2 SAM HANKE, PEDIATRIC CARDIOLOGIST: When I woke up, Charlie never did. And this tragedy is something that happens way too often in our country.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Pediatricians say children should sleep and PJs or sleeping sack flat on their backs on fitted sheets, no blankets, pillows or stuffed animals. Sam hanky and his wife have started Charlie`s Kids.\par \par \uc2 HANKE: What we`ve learned is that we`re not alone in this tragedy.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Teaching safe sleep to prevent another baby from dying. Tom Costello, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And I`ll be back with a remarkable story of forgiveness in this season of renewal.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, tonight, in this season of grace and renewal, we have a remarkable story of forgiveness. It`s a story of a man who was willing to open himself up to the humanity and potential of a stranger who took the life of someone dear to him.\par \par \uc2 [19:25:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: I`ve done a lot of interviews with newly released incarcerated people and generally they have been exonerated. That`s not your story.\par \par \uc2 RANDALL BAGLEY, INCARCERATED FOR MURDER: That`s not my story.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: You did it.\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: I did it.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: You killed a man.\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: I did.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): In 1998, Randall Bagley shot and killed Donald Mitchell during a Maryland robbery.\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: I turn to say something to him, and the gun went off.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In an instant, a man robbed of his life and the victim`s young son robbed of a father.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You`re the Boogeyman.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Last September, 24 years later, Idris Mitchell spoke in a Maryland court where that man who killed his dad was asking for a second chance, a reduction of his sentence. Audio recordings captured the hearing.\par \par \uc2 IDRIS MITCHELL, SON OF DONALD MITCHELL: You are the nightmare. I`ve never seen you. I never knew what you look like.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But then, he stunned the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I fundamentally, wholly and wholeheartedly forgive you.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Mitchell asked the judge to release Begley from his life-plus 35 years sentence.\par \par \uc2 When Idris spoke, what was going through your mind?\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: I thought it was unreal. I thought it was unreal.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Mitchell even surprised himself.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I didn`t want to say that I forgave him.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: But more than for forgive him, you also called for his release. You said --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: No, that was unexpected.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It was unexplained.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: That was unexpected. I was going to stop there. And I think what changed, and even advocating for his release was again hearing what he had done.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In Bagley, Mitchell says he found things in common, both high achievers. Mitchell, a Yale grad. Bagley accepted into the first class of \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 `s bachelor degree program for the incarcerated.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Not to be funny, but he had a better GPA than I did at Yale. So, I was like, OK, slightly better. I had a good one. But I think that he simply -- he simply dedicated himself to improvement.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Mark Howard heads Georgetown`s Prisons and Justice Initiative.\par \par \uc2 MARK HOWARD, DIRECTOR, GEORGETOWN`S PRISON AND JUSTICE INITIATIVE: When he started explaining what happened in that courtroom and also the role that this program had played in his journey of getting him to that point, it was very emotional.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A judge recently slashed Bagley sentence, and he was released in January. In some ways setting two men free.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: You wait 23 years to say something. And then when you do, you find yourself actually advocating for the man who killed your father. That`s not -- I don`t think that`s conventional, but it`s what felt right.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Did you deserve that level of forgiveness?\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: I don`t deserve anything. I took a man`s life. I don`t deserve anything. I`m grateful. Happy, but I don`t deserve anything.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Randall, Mr. Bagley, which do you prefer?\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: Whatever.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Whatever?\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: Randall, yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Randall, OK. Are you OK?\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: Are you OK?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: We`re working on it. We`re working on it.\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: Idris.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: So, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: I`m still trying to process it.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And as they recently met for the first time, one question lingered in the air -- why?\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: What made you do it when you could have said so many other things other than I forgive you?\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Because I -- because I think people can change, all right. People can change. You`re 22. It`s been 24 years.\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: It takes a lot to do what you`re doing. It takes a lot. It takes a lot.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 BAGLEY: Takes a lot, bro.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A powerful moment of healing. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Friday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230408ej4700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc30}{\*\bkmkend toc30}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for April 6, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Priscilla Thompson, Kristen Welker, Raf Sanchez,  Courtney Kube, Blayne Alexander, Cynthia McFadden, Miguel Almaguer \par \uc2 3,850 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 April 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the dramatic showdown over guns in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The state`s Republican-led house voting on removing three Democrats from office after they led a protest on the floor demanding stricter gun laws after the Nashville school shooting that killed six people. The rare punishment used only three times before since the Civil War. Outside the building, the massive protest and what happens now with the ousted Democrat seats?\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, in the new report of the U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan, the White House largely blaming the chaos and decisions by Former President Trump and the actions it says the U.S. should have taken even sooner.\par \par \uc2 The rising tensions of the Middle East. Israel striking back tonight after the biggest barrage of missiles from Lebanon in more than 16 years.\par \par \uc2 Tracking severe storms on the move tonight in the Mid-Atlantic on the heels of that deadly tornado outbreak.\par \par \uc2 NBC News investigates after our report on toxic air concerns near a Louisiana school. What the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is now doing about it.\par \par \uc2 And it was 20 years ago we lost a member of our NBC News family covering the war in Iraq. Tonight, my conversation with David Bloom`s widow about the lives his story may have saved.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC Nightly News with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening and welcome. The tears of grief that consume Nashville, Tennessee just 10 days ago over the murders of young children and school staffers are replaced tonight by cries of anger and accusations after an extraordinary move by Tennessee House Republicans to seek the expulsion of three Democratic members for their roles in a gun safety protest on the House floor last week.\par \par \uc2 All day, angry supporters and gun safety advocates crowding the state house where the GOP majority was putting the resolutions up to a vote one by one, declaring the three participated in disorderly behavior when they led supporters in chants demanding stricter gun safety measures. Expulsion, a rarely exercise right of the Tennessee House that critics on the left tonight are calling anti-democratic.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Priscilla Thompson is in Nashville following it all for us. Where do things stand right now, Priscilla?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Lester, just moments ago, a vote to expel one lawmaker narrowly failed. And this after earlier today, another lawmaker was expelled. That third lawmaker is in the chamber pleading his case right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): Outrage tonight in Nashville in reaction to Republican-led efforts to expel three Democratic lawmakers from the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON SEXTON, SPEAKER, TENNESSEE STATE HOUSE: I hereby declare of Representative Justin Jones of the 57 representative district expelled from the House of Representatives of the 113th assembly of the State of Tennessee. The three Democrats in question, state representatives Justin Jones, Gloria Johnson, and Justin Pearson were accused of disorderly behavior following their participation in a demonstration on the house floor last week.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D-TN), STATE REPRESENTATIVE: We need action! We need action! We need action!\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: They joined advocates calling for stricter gun safety measures following the mass shooting at the Nashville private school that left six people dead on March 27th. Their chants with a bull horn from the podium in the house well led to today`s showdown.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We called for you all to ban assault weapons and you respond with an assault on democracy.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: While Tennessee`s Constitution allows for any member of the state legislature to be expelled with the two-thirds vote, it has only happened three times before. Demonstrators today filled the halls of the state capitol. But arguments from Democrats did little to sway the Republican super majority.\par \par \uc2 JONES: This does not seem like America. To expel voices of opposition and dissent is a signal of authoritarianism and it is very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The Republican Speaker of the House says today`s actions were about the rules.\par \par \uc2 SEXTON: It did break rules by going to the well and showing that they were at the well, they were speaking, they pulled out a bull horn, and rolled out of order before there was a recess.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: But protesters here told us they felt today`s vote was a distraction from the urgent issue of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What`s happening in Tennessee right now is abhorrent and our legislators are not listening to what we are saying.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Priscilla, what happens next?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (on camera): Lester, counties will appoint interim representatives to fill the seats of those expelled lawmakers until a special election is set. And that would be set by the governor. And as for those expelled lawmakers, here in Tennessee, they are still eligible to run and could potentially be re-elected for those seats in that special election. Lester?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:07]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Priscilla Thompson tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s other top story, a Biden administration review partially blaming the Trump Administration for the chaotic and deadly U.S. withdrawal from Afghanistan, prompting a backlash from Republicans. Kristen Welker is at the White House with more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, nearly two years after the deadly and disastrous American withdrawal from Afghanistan, those stunning images of Afghans desperately chasing U.S. planes and the airport terror attack which killed 13 U.S. service members. The Biden Administration releasing its after-action review trying to pin much of the blame on Former President Trump who had left office eight months earlier including citing sharp drawdowns of U.S. troops.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: It is also undeniable that decisions made and the lack of planning done by the previous administration significantly limited options available to him.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Though acknowledging the Biden Administration should have started the evacuation earlier. Despite facing withering criticism, President Biden at the time called the withdrawal a "extraordinary success."\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No nation -- no nation has ever done anything like it in all of history.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And today, this pushback from a White House spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: For all this talk of chaos, I just didn`t it. Not from my perch. I just won`t buy the whole argument of chaos. It was tough in the first few hours --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But a former top Trump official tonight firing back.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2  , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: They came in, took the plan that we had, and just botched it. It is outrageous for them to somehow try and blame anyone but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We pressed the White House.\par \par \uc2 Does the president take responsibility for the withdrawal and everything that happened thereafter?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: He`s the commander-in-chief. And he absolutely has responsibility for the operations that our men and women conduct and the orders that he gives. Meanwhile, in \uc2 Taliban\uc2  -controlled Afghanistan tonight, women face new oppression and Afghan interpreters who helped the U.S. are in hiding. We asked Jeff Phaneuf, a veteran trying to get translators out about the report.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PHANEUF, VETERAN: It doesn`t do enough to take full ownership of the continued problem looking forward to save the 160 to 200,000 of our Afghan partners who were left behind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (on camera): Just last month, the CENTCOM commander warned ISIS in Afghanistan could conduct terror attacks against U.S. interests abroad in under six months. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Israel launching new strikes in Gaza after getting hit by a wave of rocket attacks fired by militants in Lebanon. Raf Sanchez is in Tel Aviv tonight. Raf, what more do we know about this?\par \par \uc2 RAF SANCHEZ, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, in the last hour, Israeli jets have begun striking targets in Gaza belonging to the \uc2 Hamas\uc2  militant group. And we are hearing sirens going off in southern Israel which suggests Palestinian militants are shooting back. We don`t yet know if there are casualties, but we do know Israel blames \uc2 Hamas\uc2  for those rockets from Lebanon earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 And all of this comes after violent scenes yesterday between Israeli police and Palestinians at the Al-Aqsa mosque in Jerusalem, the third holiest site in Islam. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Raf Sanchez, thank you. Despite growing China tensions, a U.S. delegation is in Taiwan after talks in California between Taiwan`s President and House Speaker Kevin McCarthy. Courtney Kube reports now from Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, despite threats from China, including this warship seen off the coast, a top delegation of American officials arriving in Taiwan for critical high- level talks. The bipartisan group meeting with Taiwan`s Vice President Lai, and later this weekend, President Tsai after her historic visit with house Speaker Kevin McCarthy in California.\par \par \uc2 TSAI ING-WEN, PRESIDENT, TAIWAN: Taiwan is grateful to have the States of America by our side.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: China blasting the show of support from the U.S. for Taiwan vowing to firmly defend national sovereignty and territorial integrity. China says the island democracy of Taiwan is part of its territory.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): The United States stands by you and will protect you.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Tonight, a top House Republican Michael McCall saying the U.S. needs to arm and train Taiwan`s military. While Taiwan`s Vice President says they are already on the front lines of combating China`s authoritarianism, U.S. intelligence says China`s President Xi has instructed his military to be ready to invade Taiwan by 2027.\par \par \uc2 We met Enoch Wu a former Special Forces Soldier now using his training to prepare Taiwan`s civilians to be ready.\par \par \uc2 ENOCH WU, FORMER TAIWANESE SPECIAL FORCES: It was important for us to stand up a volunteer corps trained in crisis response.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: He believes the threat of an invasion is higher now than ever.\par \par \uc2 WU: If you can`t defend it, it`s not yours. Protecting Taiwan, protecting our home is our own responsibility.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:01]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KUBE (on camera): Despite the sharp comments, some U.S. officials tell me Chinese President Xi Jinping is unlikely to put on a large military show of force while French President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  is in China on a state visit. But once President Macron leaves, President Xi could respond more forcefully. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Courtney Kube in Taipei, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re tracking more severe weather here at home as the storm that ripped through parts of the Midwest now pushes into the Mid-Atlantic. 10 million people are at risk. Damaging winds the main concern. Fortunately, the tornado threat has diminished.\par \par \uc2 We`ll turn to abortion now and what has been a consequential week for both sides of that battle as it plays out across the country. Blayne Alexander has the story for us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It`s been nearly 10 months since the end of Roe v Wade and the beginning of abortion access falling solely under state control, with major developments in four states this week. In Idaho, the governor signed a controversial new law making it a felony for non-parent adults to help a minor cross state lines for an abortion, the first law of its kind nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  is slamming the measure as extreme saying it will put young people in dangerous and isolating situations.\par \par \uc2 What would you say to critics who call this unnecessarily cruel?\par \par \uc2 TODD LAKEY (R-ID), STATE SENATOR: I think again it`s important that we protect the life of the unborn child. When it comes to minors, what the legislation requires is that parents essentially be informed.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: In Florida, protests and arrests after the state senate passed the so-called Heartbeat Protection Act reducing abortion access from 15 weeks to six.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BOOK, (D-FL), STATE SENATOR: If you vote for this ban, things will get worse. Women will die.\par \par \uc2 ERIN GRALL (R-FL), STATE SENATOR: For 50 years, the killing of innocent children has been legal. Legal does not equate to right.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It all comes amid a pair of victories for abortion rights advocates. In Michigan, Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  signed a repeal of the state`s abortion ban on the books since 1931.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): Abortion rights are now enshrined into our state constitution.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And in Wisconsin, abortion was at the heart of Tuesday`s high- stakes election for a seat on the state supreme court now set to be filled by the liberal candidate all but ensuring abortion access in the state.\par \par \uc2 JANET PROTASIEWICZ, JUSTICE-ELECT, WISCONSIN SUPREME COURT: Wisconsin voters have made their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Tonight, several more states are considering legislation that would either expand or restrict access to abortions. Blayne Alexander, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, what residents say is a long-time toxic threat to a school near a production plant in Louisiana, and what the \uc2 EPA\uc2  is now doing about it following our investigation, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Three weeks ago, we first reported on the Fifth Ward Elementary School in Reserve Louisiana located next to a plant emitting what the \uc2 EPA\uc2  has long said was a likely carcinogen. Cynthia McFadden returned with the head of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  who was now taking action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL REGAN\uc2  , ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 EPA\uc2  : Our work isn`t done.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MCFADDEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL AND INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): 18 months ago, the newly appointed head of the \uc2 EPA\uc2  , \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  took what he called a journey to justice tour that brought him here.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: I vowed that \uc2 EPA\uc2  would take action.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Today, he returned to make good on that promise proposing a staggering slate of emission standards for 80 toxic chemicals.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: We`re sending a safety standard that we believe is the lowest you can set that is detectable with technology.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Including chemicals being emitted from the Danka plant here in Reserve. On our last visit here, we met George and his great-grandmother Mary Hampton who`s been battling for years to get the Denka Plant next door to George`s elementary school to stop emitting where the \uc2 EPA\uc2  has long said are unsafe levels of two of those toxic chemicals.\par \par \uc2 One of your neighbors says it`s like sending the kids to a gas chamber.\par \par \uc2 MARY HAMPTON, RESIDENT, RESERVE, LOUISIANA: Exactly, exactly.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: We also met Robert Taylor who had been fighting with her for years. He didn`t have much confidence the \uc2 EPA\uc2  would do anything. So, when the agency called the night our story aired and suggested we meet up with \uc2 Michael Regan\uc2  in Louisiana, we agreed.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that Mr. Taylor said to me the first time I came to visit was that this was a sacrifice zone.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: What I can tell you is there are none of these facilities in Beverly Hills. There`s no coincidence if you look at who`s on the front lines, most of these individuals are Black, Brown, or tribal and or low-income. So, there`s a consistent pattern here. If the new standards announced today are put into effect, they become much more enforceable.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: I think what angers a lot of people is though the Denka plant has been emitting at these historically high levels for years now. Why not shut the plant down while everything else is being sorted out.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: We are using every tool in our toolbox. And if I have more powers, trust me, I would use them.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Denka told us it`s reduced emissions and says it`s breaking no laws denying it`s putting the folks who live here at any increased risk for cancer.\par \par \uc2 REGAN: I wish that we could move more expeditiously to take action to protect George, to protect Mr. Taylor, to do all the things that we want to do yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: As for Bob and Mary, they said today has filled them with hope.\par \par \uc2 What`s the best part of today?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT TAYLOR, RESIDENT, RESERVE, LOUISIANA: We got the attention of our politicians.\par \par \uc2 HAMPTON: It`s not over.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: It`s not over but we`ll be here when it ends. I tell them all the time. I was here from day one. I`ll be there till day zero.\par \par \uc2 MCFADDEN: Cynthia McFadden, NBC News Reserve, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`ll take a short break. Up next, sticker shock, the soaring cost of buying a new car. What`s driving it up in "THE PRICE YOU PAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: If you`ve been shopping for a new car, then you know that prices and monthly payments have been soaring. Miguel Almaguer reports on why and what you can do in "THE PRICE YOU PAY."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For Americans already facing sticker shock on the sales lot, tonight, car payments are also accelerating towards record levels. Admins revealed the average annual percentage rate or APR on a newly financed vehicle has now ballooned to seven percent. The typical payment for a new car in overdrive hitting $730 a month. It could be enough for Sharon Divestern to pump the brakes on buying new.\par \par \uc2 SHARON DIVESTER, LOOKING TO BUY A CAR: If you find something that you like, you just save money.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: As car payments become more expensive than ever, tonight, one in six buyers who finance new are paying more than $1,000 a month. The staggering price to sit behind the wheel rivaling the cost of putting a roof over your head.\par \par \uc2 IVAN DRURY, DIRECTOR, EDMUNDS INSIGHTS: Unless you have a lot of cash on hand to kind of mitigate those APRs, you will be looking at a number you probably have never seen before if you bought a car in the last 10 years.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With more drivers going into delinquency, the average cost of a new car has nearly hit $50,000.\par \par \uc2 Many buyers are no longer in the driver`s seat when it comes to monthly payments. Supply remains tight. Inflation is driving up costs and the Fed continues to raise interest rates.\par \par \uc2 There is some good news though. Experts believe rates will soon stabilize. But for now, it`s a bumpy road ahead for drivers buying new. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And next tonight, I`ll remember a beloved NBC News colleague, David Bloom, and his important Legacy 20 years later.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: This is a somber anniversary for all of us at NBC News and for journalism at large. It was 20 years ago we learned our colleague David Bloom had died while embedded with the front-line elements of the U.S. invasion of Iraq. How David died was a shock in itself and a wakeup call still impacting lives today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID BLOOM, FORMER NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: This is the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  `s third infantry division. We`re with the second brigade, the 315 Infantry.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It`s where we like to be on the big story out front. David Bloom rolling into combat on live T.V. with the Army`s 3rd Infantry Division trailed by a customized satellite truck known as the Bloom Mobile.\par \par \uc2 BLOOM: Basically, what we`ve done is we`ve established the ability transmit live from this vehicle using a pretty sophisticated camera which can be outside the vehicle and take the bumps and the jars.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Paul Nassar was David`s producer on the journey.\par \par \uc2 BLOOM: We`re just rolling along. It`s nighttime now.\par \par \uc2 PAUL NASSAR, PRODUCER: The difference with the Bloom Mobile is it was the first time that you had live television on the move in the middle of an invasion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rocket, rocket, rocket.\par \par \uc2 BOOM: Tom, we`re under attack right now.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: David traveled aboard a cram tank recovery vehicle.\par \par \uc2 What was your level of concern about him going off and working in a battlefield.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE BEAL, WIFE OF DAVID BLOOM: I was very concerned. And what I knew about David though is that`s the only place he would be. He really had a calling to be a journalist.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: His wife Melanie worried about the war, unaware he faced another threat.\par \par \uc2 BEAL: Two nights before his death, he had called me and he said, you know, I just can`t sleep another night in that tank with my knees pulled up to my chest. I`ve been having these leg cramps.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: What no one including David knew he had developed a blood clot.\par \par \uc2 NASSAR: The that he died, he kind of woke up and he said that he wasn`t limping anymore. It had gotten much better. And we were all relieved but what was evident now is that the blood clot had moved from behind his knee to other parts of his body.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Specifically, his lungs were a pulmonary embolism proved fatal. David was just 39 and left a wife and three young daughters behind.\par \par \uc2 After David`s passing, Melanie committed herself to increasing awareness about deep vein thrombosis, DVT, the condition that took David`s life and affects hundreds of thousands of lives each year.\par \par \uc2 BEAL: 50 percent of the time there are signs of a clot in your leg, and those signs can be swelling, redness, pain, cramps in David`s case. But 50 percent of the time it`s silent and you don`t really know that the clot is there.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The message she believes is getting through.\par \par \uc2 BEAL: I think changes have been made. People now are aware when they get on planes to get up and walk around, stretch their legs.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Do you think his story has ultimately saved lives?\par \par \uc2 BEAL: I know that it has saved lives. I really do believe that. And David dedicated his life to informing the American people in his role as a journalist. And I couldn`t think of a better legacy for him than to inform the American people about what took his life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: David and his family in our hearts tonight. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230407ej4600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc31}{\*\bkmkend toc31}\b \uc2 TODAY WITH HODA & JENNA for March 29, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Jenna Bush Hager, Donna Farizan \par \uc2 9,719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 March 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copy