at picks you made.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Well, I tried.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You did it.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Did that make you feel good?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I -- yes, I feel at home.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, good. I hope so. To check out these products head today.com/readwithjenna. And we should mention that TODAY may collect a commission on items purchased through our link.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. We`ll be back right after this.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All good. Every single thing was good.\par \par \uc2 [10:55:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, coming up tomorrow, a performance by Grammy-winning singer, Lauren Daigle.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We love her. Plus, the 75-year-old TikTok twins who are spreading joy to millions.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And get ready, we got your spring style guide and -- oh, we have one more breaking news.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: A breaking news. Sarah?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Sarah Clagett our producer.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Happy birthday, Clagett.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Clagett`s birthday was yesterday. So, we decided to celebrate you a day later.\par \par \uc2 SARAH CLAGETT, LIFESTYLE PRODUCER, TODAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We got -- we got five seconds to say we love you, Clagett.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230329ej3t000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc32}{\*\bkmkend toc32}\b \uc2 The Cancer Survivor`s Journey To Starting A Family That Is Giving New Hope To Other Couples\b0\par\par\uc2 Sheinelle Jones, Carson Daly, Kate Snow \par \uc2 1,417 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 March 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: Back at 8:43 this morning on Your Health. One woman`s journey that`s giving hope to others facing breast cancer while also wanting to have children.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY: NBC senior national correspondent Kate Snow joins us with that story. Kate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good morning Carson. Good morning, guys. For many younger women diagnosed with cancer, the toxic treatment can take away their fertility. But new innovations and increased awareness have given some women the chance to preserve their fertility and build a family even after surviving cancer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHELLY BATTISTA, CANCER SURVIVOR: Hi.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Which one is Nina?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is Nina.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Nina?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, she came first.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Hi, Nina.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And then this is little Margot.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): These days, life is a bit different for Shelly and Robert Battista. They grew from a family of three to five. While the Chicago couples days are now filled with dirty diapers, crying and lots of laundry, they wouldn`t have it any other way.\par \par \uc2 Three years ago, Shelly was diagnosed with an aggressive form of breast cancer and learn she has a genetic mutation that raises her risk for ovarian cancer too.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): How frightening is all of that?\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: Yes, it was terrifying. You know, we had a six-month-old baby at home. We felt like we were just starting our life together and we get this shocking news.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): Shelly needed to start treatment immediately, but the couple had their heart set on a big family and the chemotherapy would destroy her fertility.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: We were so blessed to have Emilia prior to this breast cancer diagnosis. But, you know, you still have dreams of having more children.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): Dr. Kara Goldman specializes in fertility preservation at Northwestern Medicine in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:04]\par \par \uc2 KARA GOLDMAN, PHYSICIAN: Chemotherapy that is used in young patients with triple negative breast cancer is very toxic to the ovaries. This is a tremendous blow for a young woman who a day prior was healthy and had no medical diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But Dr. Goldman told the couple there was hope. Before starting chemotherapy, Shelly and Robert could create frozen embryos.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: We have to do it quickly. We started her fertility preservation journey two days later. She started medications and two weeks later, she had eight frozen embryos.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): Shelly had 12 rounds of chemotherapy, a double mastectomy and had her ovaries and fallopian tubes removed. A year after treatment, she was healthy enough to get pregnant even without ovaries.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: The ovaries and the uterus function very independently of each other. So in Shelly`s case, because she did not have ovaries producing hormones, we were able to provide her the hormones necessary for pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): The first two trials were unsuccessful, they tried a third embryo. And eight days later, Dr. Goldman called them with the news Shelly was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: She called me herself, personally, we were both static and crying, and yelling.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BATTISTA, SHELLY`S HUSBAND: And this was just kind of like the cherry on top. It was awesome. We`re going to have another kid. Shelly`s healthy, like everything was just like behind us at that point.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): But the surprises weren`t over yet. At their first ultrasound, the technician saw something.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: She was like, oh, there`s two of them. And I was like -- we both were kind of like two of what? Like -- she`s like, there`s two babies.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: You didn`t implant two embryos.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: We implanted one embryo and the embryo split. And so they are identical twins.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): The chance of that happening, just 1%. Nine months later, two healthy little girls were born., Margot and Nina, born on the two year anniversary of Shelly being declared cancer free.\par \par \uc2 R. BATTISTA: I think it`s like the best celebration of Shelly`s cancer journey. It`s like you`ve beaten cancer and now here`s this extra, like love you get on top of it.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: It`s loud, it`s tiring. We have lots of laughs, lots of cries, lots of laundry. It`s chaotic but it is -- that`s perfect.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: It`s perfect. Having a baby after cancer treatment is becoming a lot more common thanks to better technology and awareness. Also important to note, guys, the Battistas live in one of just a handful of states that require insurance to cover fertility after cancer. Most states don`t do that, so they live in Chicago, they had an Illinois law.\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is going to grab the attention of so many of voice because there are so many families who are dealing with this exact same challenge.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: By the way, while you`re here, folks, in addition to being our senior national correspondent, we got to talk about this exciting personal project that Kate Snow has taken on. It`s been on your bucket list for years. We`ve talked about this but I want folks to take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You`re thinking all your colleagues, but you thinking also --\par \par \uc2 DALY: For a story?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Why you`ve been keeping this from us is the real question.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: I`m not, I`m not. So that was Nashville Tennessee. I`m in a vocal tracking booth there. You heard the backing band. I met with a whole bunch of great amazing musicians in Nashville through friends of friends. I`ve been a singer my whole life. Carson think it`s funny.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well because we didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I did not see this coming.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: I know. Nobody --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: But you`re putting out songs, like music.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: I have three songs out now, so that was one of them that you just heard.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Just wait till they turn the chairs for you on "The Voice", Kate.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Kate, I love you.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Are they all covers or do you write?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: They all covers.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: So "Nothing Compares to You" is what you just heard, "Because the Night" and "Can`t Find My Way Home."\par \par \uc2 DALY: Oh my God, amazing.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Three of my favorite songs.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it, Kate. Did you just feel like you know what it`s time to - -\par \par \uc2 SNOW: It was -- yes, total (inaudible) like why not? What am I waiting for?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And so, kind of made it happen.\par \par \uc2 DALY: How can people hear the music? It`s all -- the three of them are up on any place you stream music, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , \uc2 YouTube\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: That is amazing, just hearing that. And the range, I was going to say if range is a reporter, you got ranges of musicians.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it. Do it all, my friend.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: You know what --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Do you have a stage name like Lady Snow or something? I mean, people just look up Kate Snow?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Just Kate Snow.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Kate Snow. That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Congratulation.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So proud.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: It`s stress relief too from this job.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Again you can stream her new EP, how cool does that sound --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: It does.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- wherever you get your music.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Look at you. We gave you the City Music treatment there.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Up next, he cannot sing but he can help us find some great travel deals. Spring break season is underway, Eastern is around the corner, our guy Mark Ellwood is here. He`s going to help you plan and save on that next vacation. But first, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 [08:50:09]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230322ej3k000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc33}{\*\bkmkend toc33}\b \uc2 The Today Show for March 20, 2023, 