par \par \uc2 And special celebration.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hip, hip.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hooray.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Bruce Willis` family coming together for a special milestone as his wife speaks out amid the star`s battle with dementia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMMA HEMING WILLIS, BRUCE WILLIS` WIFE: As much as I do it for myself I do it for you because I know how much you love my husband.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Today, Monday, March 20, 2023.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Starting off the week.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Cabot, Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Tamarac, Florida.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Houston, Texas.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: And Springfield, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Shout out to my dad and my brother.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Watching in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Michigan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ohio.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We`re on a girl`s trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating my 40th birthday.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Raleigh, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Ashland, Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And first time in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And welcome to New York City. Look at all those smiling faces.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hello.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Girls trips and birthdays and anniversaries. That`s a good Monday crowd.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST: It`s spring break time.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s all around the country, folks. It`s freezing a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re going to get outside in just moment. We`ve got Sheinelle with us. We`ve got Jacob with us. With Savannah on assignment today. Hoda enjoying time off this week. Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: You know what first day of spring means?\par \par \uc2 JONES: What?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What`s that?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Summer is almost here.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: There you go.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Almost here. Almost here.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s right. We`ll have more on the spring from Mr. Rocker in just a moment. But let`s get right your news at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 In the new and unexpected witness set to testify before grand jury in the Donald Trump hush money investigation. Over the weekend the former president himself predicting he would be arrested as soon as tomorrow. NBC`s Garrett Haake is at the district attorneys` office here in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Garrett, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Craig. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 And yes, this courthouse behind me is going to be the center of the political world later today when grand jurors are expected to hear from what could be the final witness in what`s been a yearslong probe into those alleged hush money payments from Donald Trump going all the way back to 2016. Now, Donald Trump made clear over the weekend, he believes this investigation is going to end with his arrest. He said it would come on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Now, to be clear, there has been no formal word from the district attorney that any indictment is coming, but law enforcement officials here in New York City have been planning for that possibility and planning for the possibility that folks could listen to Mr. Trump`s call for protests if and when that happens.\par \par \uc2 Now, while we wait for the legal fallout, the political fallout is already happening. We`ve heard from speaker of the house over the weekend calling for folks not to protest any decision that may come out of here. And we`ve heard from some of Donald Trump`s political rivals rallying to his defense, calling any prosecution purely political.\par \par \uc2 Now, about that witness today, Robert Costello is his name. He was a legal adviser to the prosecution`s star witness in this case, but he has been called by Donald Trump`s attorneys, according to a source directly familiar. We`ll be watching very closely throw see what comes out of that grand jury room today and what decisions are made in court and on the presidential playing field here as it relates to this over the next couple of days. Craig.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right. Garrett Haake, for us there. Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Now, to some frightening moments for a New York lawmaker and pilot forced to make an emergency landing on a Long Island Beach. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: New York state assemblyman Clyde Vanel says, he was practicing some maneuvers when suddenly his engine went out. So, he had no choice but to land his plane --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- right there on the beach.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Geez.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thankfully, both Vanel and his passenger walked away with only a few cuts and bruises.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Out in L.A., a meteorologist is recovering after a frightening on air moment. She suffered a medical emergency in the middle of a broadcast, leaving her co-anchors and viewers completely stunned. NBC`s Emilie Ikeda is here with that story.\par \par \uc2 Emilie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Jacob, good morning. It was around this time, Saturday morning, when a local \uc2 CBS\uc2  Meteorologist Alissa Carlson, collapsed on live TV. We`re told she`s on the mend and she`s thanking everyone for their support. A warning to viewers, some of these images may be hard to watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NICHELLE MEDINA, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS LA ANCHOR: Let`s start off with a check of your next weather.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): An on-air scare in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 MEDINA: She joins us live in the studio, Alissa --\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): Local CBS Meteorologist Alissa Carlson`s eyes appearing to roll back right before she slowly leans forward and then suddenly collapses to the floor. KCAL`s co-anchors quickly tossing to commercials after realizing something was very wrong.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL KIM, KCAL NEWS WEEKEND ANCHOR: Oh.\par \par \uc2 MEDINA: You know, we`re going to go ahead and go to break right now.\par \par \uc2 KIM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): According to the station, the team took immediate action to comfort Alissa and call 9-1-1.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 78-Immediate dispatch on 4200 Radford Avenue.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): After she was taken to the hospital, Carlson sharing a message of reassurance on \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . Posting, thanks for all the text, calls, and well wishes. I`m going to be OK. This isn`t the first time she has suffered a health scare during a broadcast. Back in 2014, she got sick working as the chief meteorologist for NBC`s affiliate KGET.\par \par \uc2 ALISSA CARLSON, CBS METEOROLOGIST: All of a sudden, during the newscast, I threw up in the weather center.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): Carlson later learned she had a leaky heart valve, a diagnosis that inspired her to embrace a healthier lifestyle and advocate for the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2 . And later, after giving birth to her daughter, a miraculous surprise.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The stem cells from my baby had started to heal my heart.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): It`s not clear why Carlson fainted in this latest incident, but she told us her heart is fine. She`s not the only TV news personality to suffer an on-air medical emergency. Just last year, Julie Chin experienced what was believed to be the beginning of a stroke while anchoring for our Tulsa, Oklahoma, affiliate.\par \par \uc2 JULIE CHIN, NEWS ANCHOR, TULSA NBC AFFILIATE KJRH: -- today at the -- at the -- at the event. The event features live -- I`m sorry. Something is going on with me.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voiceover): Chin lost partial vision while her hand and arm went numb. She was unable to read the teleprompter but managed to toss back to her co-workers on live TV. Chin later reflecting on that harrowing incident on "TODAY."\par \par \uc2 CHIN: And of course, I mean, I want to know what caused it. So, you know, maybe we can prevent it from happening again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): OK. So, the good news is, Alissa Carlson tells us she is out of the hospital and even enjoying some pizza. You can tell she`s incredibly grateful for the support of her co-workers and the thoughts and prayers from viewers. She says, she is recovering from a head injury suffered in the fall and understandably is not sure when she will be back on air. But, of course, we`re rooting for her.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I was going to say.\par \par \uc2 JONES: A quick recovery.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Oh, man. So scary, but glad she`s OK.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Emilie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you, Emilie.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thanks, Emilie.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. That`s it for the news. Do you want to give us a "Boost"?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Hoda`s not here,\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: But I`ll do my best. This morning, a sweet reunion between a little girl and her dog named Leo. It turns out Leo got lost back in January. But last week, their family finally got that phone call they had been waiting for.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, so sweet.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh. You can see the little girl there, overcome with emotion after seeing Leo for the first time in months.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Awe.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thankfully another family was apparently taking good care of him until they were finally able to connect with his owners and she could not wait to give little Leo a big hug.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Leo was happy to see her too.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes, Leo was. And shout out to that family for taking care of Leo.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That was sweet.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: How nice is that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That was good.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: That was a go ahead one.\par \par \uc2 All right. Ladies and gentlemen, just ahead, Adam Sandler honored overnight with one of the most prestigious awards in all of comedy. Carson`s is going to have that story and more coming up on "POP Start."\par \par \uc2 JONES: But first, Vicky is here to kick off our brand-new series, "Money- Saving Mondays".\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR CONSUMER INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good morning, guys. With food costs continuing to rise, how best to preserve your produce from the ideal fridge tempts to which fruits you should be hanging. I`ll have all the food hacks to help you leave no food behind. That`s next, right here on "TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We are back. 8:14 now with our new series, "Money-Saving Mondays". We`re going to start with your food budget.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: I like it. I can eat the props. Prices at the supermarket have increased more than 11 percent in just a year. So, of course, you want those groceries to last. Here with tips to make our food stay fresh longer, we have NBC`s Senior Consumer Investigative Correspondent Vicky Nguyen.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Hello, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. So, let`s take it here.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes. OK.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You say the easy thing we can do right now, first of all, check your refrigerators.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What are we looking for?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: First step, you want to check the fridge temp.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: So, the USDA --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, the temperature. OK.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- says 40 degrees is what they recommend but a lot of food experts say, keep it even lower, between 35 and 38 degrees. And how would you know? Well, if you don`t have one of those built in thermometers --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- you can get one. And what I would say is, check it every day and see it in different regions so you know, is the top of my fridge cooler, is the bottom. But 35 to 38, it will help inhibit the growth of bacteria like salmonella, E. Coli, listeria, and it will keep your food fresher. The other this is make sure your fridge is not too crowded and that it`s not too empty --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s a good advice.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- because that will make it not as efficient.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And then the freezer?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Freezer is zero degrees.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Zero.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: And one sign there is if you have too much frost buildup, that means your fridge or your freezer is too cold.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes. And if you keep it too cold, you`re going to lose moisture and flavor. So, zero degrees is the recommendation.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. All right. You have a few tricks to prevent some of the food we have in the fridge from spoiling.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Let`s start with greens.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Moisture is the enemy of your greens.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: That`s what causes it to wilt. So, after you bring your greens home, you want to store them in an air tight container and just throw a paper towel in there.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I do this.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: This is --\par \par \uc2 JONES: It really works.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, it keeps them fresh longer.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And I will admit, like, even in the bag salad --\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- you just stick a paper towel in there.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Stick it in there. Perfect.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s good.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Very good.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. What about mushrooms?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Mushrooms, OK. So, they sell them with plastic.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: You want to rip that plastic off right away. You can either keep it in a cardboard box or if you want to keep it in a little paper bag.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: This will keep your mushrooms fresh longer. You can actually even keep them out of the fridge if you want, in just a cool, dry, dark place.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Really?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, but don`t keep the moisture in. That is what will cause your mushrooms to rot.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, so that`s -- and I`ve done that.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: But they`re sold in plastic --\par \par \uc2 JONES: And then I`ll -- won`t get to use them.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- so it`s confusing. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: They`re, like, mushy by the time I get to them.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Last but not least, let`s do butter.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes. OK. So, this is really interesting. If you don`t eat your cheese quickly, you can spread butter on the cut side of the cheese to keep that fresher.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: And other key is to wrap your cheese in wax paper before you put it into an air tight bag. Look, we`re using Ziplocs, but I like to use those resealable, reusable silicon bags.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Those are fine too. The key is just keeping the air out of everything.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Is that what butter does to the end of the --\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: The butter will just keep the cheese fresher, longer on the cut end.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow. OK.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, Vick, these are two items that you actually -- I`ve never thought about freezing nuts or herbs. But you maintain that`s the way to make them last longer.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Craig, you`re going to hear it here first.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Freeze your nuts.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Freeze your nuts.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Never would think to do that, but yes, nuts have a very high oil content. They can go rancid within two to four weeks and they`re expensive. So, that`s the key when you think it, but freeze your nuts. This may be my last time on TV with you, Craig. The other thing is --\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: Yay, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- chop up your herbs, this is another thing, a lot of other people don`t think about it. Fresh herbs are so expensive.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: So, you chop them up after you`ve washed them, pat them dry, chop them up, and pre-portion them, spread them on a cookie sheet, put them in the freezer, then you can dump them all into a bag. So, you`ve got here parsley, rosemary, sage. It works with all kinds of herbs.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Frozen herbs, frozen nuts.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Let`s come over here to -- so, these are items that you maintain that you can actually keep keep on the counter --\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: -- or somewhere room temperature.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, absolutely. So, with tomatoes, I never knew this. But if you keep your tomatoes stem side down, that actually helps them ripen in the right way --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- and then the bottoms don`t get mushy from the weight of the tomatoes --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- so they don`t go bad as long. And also keep them out room temperature, cool dark place. The only time you put tomatoes in the fridge is after you`ve cut them and you wrap them in plastic or put them in a container so that you prevent the growth of bacteria.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: OK. And then when it comes to fruit, so, check your fruit often. See, this, like, little old guy here.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: The pear doesn`t look good.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s a bad looking pear.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: This will cause everything else to slowly go bad.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Check your fruit bowl, you know, fairly --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, spoiled fruits can spread?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Didn`t know it.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Because it`s releasing all this gas and it will spread to everything else. I didn`t know this either. So, these two, which we always store together.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: This will -- onions will cause your potatoes to go bad sooner. So, potatoes love apples. Potatoes and apples, apples will keep your potatoes from growing those, like, sprouts on the side.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: That`s fine though, that`s now -- it doesn`t make them inedible. They just cut them out if you want. They just don`t look great. So, kind of, people freak out. But don`t waste your potatoes, you can eat them even after they have sprouted.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You`re blowing our minds, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Speaking of onions, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: We appear to have onions in a pair of pantyhose.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes. OK. So, this is a trick that I thought was --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Nobody is doing that, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: -- like from the older days.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Nobody is doing that.\par \par \uc2 N