evening?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: This evening at 5:26. So let`s see what your spring is going to be like as we start this week off. Plenty of sun in the East, heavy snow in the Great Lakes -- I should say the Rockies. By midweek, we`re looking at a little bit more wet weather getting into the Ohio Valley, some sunshine along the east. Above average highs in the plains, cool and wet out West.\par \par \uc2 And then toward the end of the week, it`s going to be wet strong storms down through the Mississippi Valley and mountain snows out West. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Thank you, Al. Coming up, Molly Hunter is looking into the new wave of royals coming to America. Hi, Molly.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Sheinelle. That`s right. More royals are coming your direction. We will have the latest transatlantic moves and all those juicy details coming up.\par \par \uc2 [08:35:08]\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. But first, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And welcome back. So when \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  made their move here to the United States, a lot of folks were surprised.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But now we`re seeing a growing number of royals from other parts of the world following the suit, the latest announced over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: NBC`s Molly Hunter joins us with details. Hey, Molly. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: Hey, guys, good morning. That`s right. So look, ever since Harry and Meghan left the UK, there`s something in the water across Europe`s royal families as monarchies across the continent slimmed down and modernize there are more and more royals hopping upon come in your direction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voice-over): It`s a move that would once have been unthinkable, young royals packing their bags and coming to America. But a new modern day expansion is underway with royalty, from Japan to Denmark and, of course, the UK, bucking tradition and making the move.\par \par \uc2 The Danish royal family announcing over the weekend, the youngest son of Queen Margherita, Prince Joachim, and his wife, Princess Marie, are set to pack their bags and move to the nation`s capital Washington, DC, while New York City is now the new home of former Japanese Princess Mako Komuro. And now there`s even speculation over here that Prince Andrew`s youngest daughter, Princess Eugenie, may also be considering a move stateside too.\par \par \uc2 EMILY NASH, ROYAL EDITOR FOR HELLO MAGAZINE: Reports are that she and her husband have been looking for a property in California. You know, she`s pregnant with her second child. We`ve all seen how fantastic life looks for children in California, so who could blame her.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voice-over): An official decision has not been made public, but a new house in the Golden State would make her neighbors with cousins \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and Meghan Markel, whom she`s remained close with even after a couple stepped down his working worlds in 2020 and moved to Montecito, blaming intense media scrutiny and a lack of support back home.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN MARKLE\uc2 , WIFE OF \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2 : And I realized that I wasn`t just being thrown to the wolves, I was being fed to the wolves.\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2 , MEGHAN MARKLE`S HUSBAND: And I`ll do everything I could to protect my family.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voice-over): Experts say these latest world relocations are also partly the result of a shift in modern monarchies as they look to slim down their courts. Prince Joachim`s move comes just months after Queen Margherita stripped her four grandchildren of their royal titles, and Princess Mako gave up her royal status in 2021, all to marry her college sweetheart.\par \par \uc2 Harry and Meghan were not the first world`s to move to America. In fact, Princess Madeleine of Sweden settled in Florida back in 2018. And before that, Princess Beatrice lived in New York for two years back in 2013. But Harry and Meghan have been able to capitalize on their American lives like no other.\par \par \uc2 [08:40:05]\par \par \uc2 MARKLE: OK, it`s me. I`m just excited to be myself and talk, and be unfiltered.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER (voice-over): Launching a podcast, releasing a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  docu series, and Harry writing that revealing memoir all amid a royal family feud. Their new neighbors will likely live slightly less public lives. Joachim will hold a new position under the Ministry of Defense at the Embassy of Denmark. While Mako`s husband is registered as a New York City lawyer, and she reportedly volunteers at the \uc2 Metropolitan Museum of Art\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NASH: These are jet setting successful families. I think lots of people see America as a land of opportunity, and so America seems like the perfect option for a new start.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: The land of opportunity. Now, of course, the big question on this side of the pond, will Harry and Meghan be coming back this direction for the coronation? We have reached out to everyone for comment. No one can tell us. We`ll have to wait and see guys.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Come on out to the land of opportunity.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We are.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Molly Hunter, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Take all your royals. We`ll take all your royal.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Isn`t right, they`re on the bottom of the Statue of Liberty?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Royals, yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Up next, the cancer survivor`s journey to starting a family that is giving new hope to other couples. Kate Snow has the remarkable story. But first this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Back at 8:43 this morning on Your Health. One woman`s journey that`s giving hope to others facing breast cancer while also wanting to have children.\par \par \uc2 DALY: NBC senior national correspondent Kate Snow joins us with that story. Kate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW, NBC SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good morning Carson. Good morning, guys. For many younger women diagnosed with cancer, the toxic treatment can take away their fertility. But new innovations and increased awareness have given some women the chance to preserve their fertility and build a family even after surviving cancer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHELLY BATTISTA, CANCER SURVIVOR: Hi.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Which one is Nina?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is Nina.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Nina?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, she came first.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Hi, Nina.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And then this is little Margot.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): These days, life is a bit different for Shelly and Robert Battista. They grew from a family of three to five. While the Chicago couples days are now filled with dirty diapers, crying and lots of laundry, they wouldn`t have it any other way.\par \par \uc2 Three years ago, Shelly was diagnosed with an aggressive form of breast cancer and learn she has a genetic mutation that raises her risk for ovarian cancer too.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): How frightening is all of that?\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: Yes, it was terrifying. You know, we had a six-month-old baby at home. We felt like we were just starting our life together and we get this shocking news.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): Shelly needed to start treatment immediately, but the couple had their heart set on a big family and the chemotherapy would destroy her fertility.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: We were so blessed to have Emilia prior to this breast cancer diagnosis. But, you know, you still have dreams of having more children.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): Dr. Kara Goldman specializes in fertility preservation at Northwestern Medicine in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:04]\par \par \uc2 KARA GOLDMAN, PHYSICIAN: Chemotherapy that is used in young patients with triple negative breast cancer is very toxic to the ovaries. This is a tremendous blow for a young woman who a day prior was healthy and had no medical diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But Dr. Goldman told the couple there was hope. Before starting chemotherapy, Shelly and Robert could create frozen embryos.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: We have to do it quickly. We started her fertility preservation journey two days later. She started medications and two weeks later, she had eight frozen embryos.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): Shelly had 12 rounds of chemotherapy, a double mastectomy and had her ovaries and fallopian tubes removed. A year after treatment, she was healthy enough to get pregnant even without ovaries.\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: The ovaries and the uterus function very independently of each other. So in Shelly`s case, because she did not have ovaries producing hormones, we were able to provide her the hormones necessary for pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): The first two trials were unsuccessful, they tried a third embryo. And eight days later, Dr. Goldman called them with the news Shelly was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: She called me herself, personally, we were both static and crying, and yelling.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT BATTISTA, SHELLY`S HUSBAND: And this was just kind of like the cherry on top. It was awesome. We`re going to have another kid. Shelly`s healthy, like everything was just like behind us at that point.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): But the surprises weren`t over yet. At their first ultrasound, the technician saw something.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: She was like, oh, there`s two of them. And I was like -- we both were kind of like two of what? Like -- she`s like, there`s two babies.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: You didn`t implant two embryos.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: We implanted one embryo and the embryo split. And so they are identical twins.\par \par \uc2 SNOW (voice-over): The chance of that happening, just 1%. Nine months later, two healthy little girls were born., Margot and Nina, born on the two year anniversary of Shelly being declared cancer free.\par \par \uc2 R. BATTISTA: I think it`s like the best celebration of Shelly`s cancer journey. It`s like you`ve beaten cancer and now here`s this extra, like love you get on top of it.\par \par \uc2 S. BATTISTA: It`s loud, it`s tiring. We have lots of laughs, lots of cries, lots of laundry. It`s chaotic but it is -- that`s perfect.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: It`s perfect. Having a baby after cancer treatment is becoming a lot more common thanks to better technology and awareness. Also important to note, guys, the Battistas live in one of just a handful of states that require insurance to cover fertility after cancer. Most states don`t do that, so they live in Chicago, they had an Illinois law.\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is going to grab the attention of so many of voice because there are so many families who are dealing with this exact same challenge.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: By the way, while you`re here, folks, in addition to being our senior national correspondent, we got to talk about this exciting personal project that Kate Snow has taken on. It`s been on your bucket list for years. We`ve talked about this but I want folks to take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You`re thinking all your colleagues, but you thinking also --\par \par \uc2 DALY: For a story?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Why you`ve been keeping this from us is the real question.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: I`m not, I`m not. So that was Nashville Tennessee. I`m in a vocal tracking booth there. You heard the backing band. I met with a whole bunch of great amazing musicians in Nashville through friends of friends. I`ve been a singer my whole life. Carson think it`s funny.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well because we didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I did not see this coming.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: I know. Nobody --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: But you`re putting out songs, like music.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: I have three songs out now, so that was one of them that you just heard.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Just wait till they turn the chairs for you on "The Voice", Kate.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Kate, I love you.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Are they all covers or do you write?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: They all covers.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: So "Nothing Compares to You" is what you just heard, "Because the Night" and "Can`t Find My Way Home."\par \par \uc2 DALY: Oh my God, amazing.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Three of my favorite songs.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it, Kate. Did you just feel like you know what it`s time to - -\par \par \uc2 SNOW: It was -- yes, total (inaudible) like why not? What am I waiting for?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: And so, kind of made it happen.\par \par \uc2 DALY: How can people hear the music? It`s all -- the three of them are up on any place you stream music, \uc2 Spotify\uc2 , \uc2 YouTube\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: That is amazing, just hearing that. And the range, I was going to say if range is a reporter, you got ranges of musicians.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it. Do it all, my friend.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: You know what --\par \par \uc2 DALY: Do you have a stage name like Lady Snow or something? I mean, people just look up Kate Snow?\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Just Kate Snow.\par \par \uc2 DALY: OK.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Kate Snow. That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 DALY: That`s awesome.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Congratulation.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So proud.\par \par \uc2 SNOW: It`s stress relief too from this job.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Again you can stream her new EP, how cool does that sound --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: It does.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- wherever you get your music.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Look at you. We gave you the City Music treatment there.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Up next, he cannot sing but he can help us find some great travel deals. Spring break season is underway, Eastern is around the corner, our guy Mark Ellwood is here. He`s going to help you plan and save on that next vacation. But first, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 [08:50:09]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re back now, 8:52, with TODAY`s Consumer. And if you need a vacation this spring, folks, we`ve got you covered. And yes, there is still time to score some great deals to fantastic destinations. Our guy, travel expert Mark Ellwood. Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 MARK ELLWOOD, TRAVEL EXPERT: Thank you, Craig.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So you`re telling folks that this would be a good time to head north, to Canada perhaps?\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: I am. And I want to say everything I`m going to talk about starts at under $160 a night. So these are all great deals and amazing. And I`m going to send people to Canada --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: -- because the weather has been nice and cold. And if you want to go skiing, I`m going to send you to Banff.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Banff.\par \par \uc2 JONES: To Banff.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh you got two great resorts.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: I have.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Let`s start with Banff.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: We`re going to start with Banff. And we`re basically, Banff has great weather right through May because it`s so high up. But also this month, there`s something called night rise on Sulphur Mountain, which is a sound and light show. Really beautiful. And you`ve of course look at that skiing.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, that does look nice.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: We`ve got a great result there. Again, remember we`re talking under $160 a night starting for everyone.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Meanwhile, if you`ve never been to Niagara Falls, now`s the time.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Have you been?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I have.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Did it blow your mind?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I loved it. Absolutely loved it.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: I think it is mind blowing.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: A great spot for kids too.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Exactly. And everyone acknowledges essentially, you`re better off seeing Niagara from the Canadian side, because the development has been more controlled. I`m going to send you to this \uc2 Hilton Hotel\uc2 , which was just renovated core, just $42 million renovation. You`re right there by the Horseshoe Falls, the American Falls.\par \par \uc2 And I`m going to send you on a little zipline around the falls. So you can get really close. But do take a wet weather jacket because I was caught short and the spray is intense.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Let`s go from Canada to the Caribbean now.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Yes. And the Caribbean is not as expensive as people think. I think people assume if you`re flying to a new country, it`s going to really break the bank.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Often cheaper than a domestic flight especially if you go somewhere like the DR to Santo Domingo, lots of flights to Santo Domingo from many, many gateways. It is the Goldilocks destination this month.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What do you love so much about it?\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Everything`s just right. The weather is just right. The hotel is just right. You`re seeing here right on the beach and you`re near El Malecon, the eight and a half miles sort of adventure playground on the beachfront, bars, restaurants all right to it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Now, I can attest to this next place. This next place is beauty, St. Lucia.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: You have been there, right?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re there recently. It`s a fantastic island.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: It is really emphasizing wellness. That`s a big trend in travel. And St. Lucia is saying come to the outdoors here. We`ve got waterfalls, we`ve got hikes. They`ve also got Sulphur Springs, which are naturally heated pools. Apparently put them on yourself for three days running, purchase negative energy.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Really?\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Yes, I`m going tomorrow. This should get started.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK. So folks who like to vacation and enjoy some time outdoors as well. What do you have for them?\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: I`ve got some domestic ideas.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: I`m going to send you, do you know which state has the most dark sky preserves in America?\par \par \uc2 [08:55:05]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I would guess Utah.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: It is Utah, well done.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: I`m going to send you to the dark sky campground there, just right on the border with Arizona. And dark sky is essentially like LASIK for your night vision. You look up and you think, gosh, the universe is magnificent. And this campground not like your all campground, great facilities, wi-fi everything you need\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And Lake of the Ozarks.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Did you watch "Ozarks?"\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I did.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: So, OK, if you love that show, go there. The hotel that inspired that has just been renovated.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, you can go to the actual hotel.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: You can go to what was the Blue Cat Lodge Resort. You can enjoy the lake. It will be like being in "Ozark."\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And Great North Woods, New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 ELLWOOD: Essentially Northern New Hampshire is rugged and pristine. It`s this incredible untouched backwards, full of maple trees. It`s sugaring season, I`m going to send you to a great hotel in the mountains overlooking Mount Washington. There`s an amazing cog railway there. You can take a three and a half mile -- three and a half hour journey around the mountains.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Also sounds like a great destination for kids as well. Mark, thank you. You`re always bring it. Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on Hoda and Jenna, the music podcasts and more to get you in the spirit of spring. First on the 3rd Hour, actor and singer Dove Cameron live in studio. All of that coming up after your local news, weather and these messages. Well done, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS AND WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230320ej3k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc34}{\*\bkmkend toc34}\b \uc2 The New Study On Pregnant Women That Everyone Should See\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Rehema Ellis \par \uc2 717 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 March 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW: We`ve reported before on the dangers of pregnancy, especially for women of color. Well, tonight, a new study with startling findings that wealthy black women are dying in child birth at rates far greater than all white women. \uc2 Rehema Ellis\uc2  takes a closer look at why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REHEMA ELLIS, NBC NEWS EDUCATION CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This is how Aziza Bowser-Clarke and her husband Jason looked this couple looked in the early weeks of her pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 AZIZA BOWSER-CLARKE: It was a picture of absolute joy.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): But for these two highly successful new Jersey Lawyers, joy soon turned to fear.\par \par \uc2 BOWSER-CLARKE: We were very, very worried about making sure that we had all the information that we had, the very best doctors that we could find.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): In her first trimester Aziza says she was exhausted. When she told her doctor she was concerned about anemia, the response?\par \par \uc2 BOWSER-CLARKE: Of course you`re tired. You`re pregnant. And me, this is my first child.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): But this very athletic 41-year-old says it got worse. By her seventh month she could barely hold her head up and demanded a blood test. The results, she was dangerously anemic and had to have infusions.\par \par \uc2 BOWSER-CLARKE: It felt like my throat dropped into my stomach, because it was there and concrete that I wasn`t imagining this.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): After 27 hours of labor, doctors abruptly decided on an emergency c-section. Her son was born healthy, but she passed out and spent about eight days in the hospital recovering.\par \par \uc2 BOWSER-CLARKE: It was traumatic.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): And she is not alone. A groundbreaking study reveals a disturbing history of unequal care. Researchers analyzed nearly all pregnancies in California over a nine-year period. For the first time factoring in both race and income. The findings? Money didn`t matter in maternal health care. And during child birth, even the richest black women are more likely to die than the poorest white women.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAMEELAH PHILLIPS, NEW YORK OBSTETRICIAN AND GYNECOLOGIST: Take it as an opportunity --\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): Dr. Kameelah Phillips is not surprised.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (on camera): What is this about?\par \par \uc2 DR. PHILLIPS: This is about racism. We are still dealing with institutions, people, and their biases.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS: Some people would say that`s dramatic that, how can you say that it`s racism when black women are going to the same hospitals that white women are going to in large measure and they`re receiving the same kind of care, aren`t they?\par \par \uc2 DR. PHILLIPS: So, I would argue that women of color and in particular black women are not receiving the same type of care, even in the same institutions.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): She says some doctors often don`t take black women`s concerns about their health as seriously as white women`s.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (on camera): They`re not listening, particularly to women of color.\par \par \uc2 DR. PHILLIPS: Particularly women of color. There are terms that often make it into the chart as it relates to black women, difficult, challenging, disruptive.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): That means, early warning signs can be missed, which could lead to negative outcomes.\par \par \uc2 So, what can pregnant black women do right now? Experts say, identify your risk factors with your doctor. Bring someone with you to your OB-GYN appointments, and take notes. Aziza did all that and still says --\par \par \uc2 BOWSER-CLARKE: My advice for black women who are pregnant, make sure your voice is heard.\par \par \uc2 ELLIS (voiceover): A powerful message when starting a family. Race matters. \uc2 Rehema Ellis\uc2 , NBC News, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Powerful story. When we come back, brotherly love. The hero kid giving his big brother a second chance at life.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230322ej3j0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc35}{\*\bkmkend toc35}\b \uc2 The Judge In Texas Who Could Ban The Abortion Pill Nationwide, Even In States Where Abortion Is Legal\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 420 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: In Texas today, a federal judge heard arguments in a lawsuit aimed at banning the most prescribed pill for abortions that`s been \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved for more than two decades. As Dasha Burns explains, the outcome could have consequences nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Anticipation in Amarillo building for weeks as a case in this Texas courthouse could restrict access nationwide to the abortion pill, the most commonly used method for terminating pregnancies in the country. A conservative judge appointed by Trump could soon decide whether to issue a preliminary injunction, blocking access to Mifeprex, generically known as Mifepristone. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved it as part of a two-drug abortion regimen in 2000.\par \par \uc2 Both sides had two hours to present their arguments. The judge peppering attorneys with detailed questions. Now, today was the first public hearing in the case. The judge delayed adding it to the public docket to avoid a "circus-like atmosphere."\par \par \uc2 The note inspiring one group of demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I remember before Roe v. Wade, and now I`m looking at it all over again.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I value life. I think it`s extremely precious.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The plaintiffs are challenging the FDA`s approval of the drug, arguing the agency didn`t adequately evaluate its safety and endangered women further when they made it available through telemedicine during the pandemic. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  says the claims in the lawsuit are unsupported by any evidence and said in court today that a ruling in the plaintiff`s favor would amount to unprecedented action and would cause significant public harm.\par \par \uc2 Since its approval in 2000, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says more than five million women have taken mifepristone with 28 deaths associated with the drug.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL O`LEARY CARMONA, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, WOMEN`S MARCH: What happens here today will be felt by all Americans no matter what your feelings are on abortion.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: A ruling that could radiate nationwide. Dasha Burns, NBC News, Amarillo, Texas.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230316ej3f0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc36}{\*\bkmkend toc36}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for March 6, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Gabe Gutierrez, Tom Costello, Miguel Almaguer, Dylan  Dreyer, Blayne Alexander, Jesse Kirsch, Catie Beck, Yamiche Alcindor, Harry  Smith \par \uc2 3,896 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 March 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS HOST (on camera): Tonight, the urgent search for four Americans kidnapped at gunpoint in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The terrifying video appearing to show the Americans being dragged into the back of a pickup after gunmen opened fire on their minivan. Was it a case of mistaken identity? The reward the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is offering and what we`re learning.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, scary moments in the air. The passenger on a United plane accused of trying to open the emergency exit and stab a flight attendant. A Southwest plane making an emergency landing in Cuba. Smoke filling the cabin. And a passenger dying after severe turbulence.\par \par \uc2 California slammed with even more snow. Desperate residents trapped for more than a week. And the new winter threats we`re tracking.\par \par \uc2 Protesters and police clashing in Atlanta. Nearly two dozen now facing domestic terror charges.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  investigating after another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train derails in Ohio. Our one-on-one with transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg. We asked, should the company be forced to pause operations?\par \par \uc2 Jurors in the Alex Murdaugh double murder trial breaking their silence. What they say about his decision to take the stand.\par \par \uc2 And he was the last original member of a legendary rock band. Remembering guitarist Gary Rossington.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening, everyone. And thank you for joining us. Chilling images tonight appear to show four Americans kidnapped at gunpoint moments after crossing from Texas into Mexico on Friday. Tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  asking for the public`s help, offering a $50,000 reward to help locate the Americans who were attacked by armed men after crossing from Brownsville, Texas into Matamoros riding in a minivan bearing North Carolina plates.\par \par \uc2 One woman seen being dragged into a pickup truck. A U.S. official tells NBC News the attack may have been a case of mistaken identity. Authorities say a Mexican citizen was killed during the confrontation. Gabe Gutierrez reports it happened in an area that has become increasingly dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): These photos shared widely on social media appear to show the kidnapping`s aftermath. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says the four Americans entered Matamoros, Mexico Friday in a white minivan with North Carolina license plates before armed gunmen opened fire and abducted them.\par \par \uc2 A law enforcement official with knowledge of the matter tells NBC News this video depicts part of the kidnapping, appearing to show one woman walking on her own, forced into a white pickup truck. Armed men with bulletproof vests are then seen dragging other people into the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are closely following the assault and kidnapping of four U.S. citizens in Matamoros, Mexico. These sorts of attacks are unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Authorities say a Mexican citizen was killed during the incident. A law enforcement official tells NBC News the Americans did not cross the border for any criminal purpose and that this appears to be a case of mistaken identity.\par \par \uc2 Today Mexico`s president said they`d entered the country to buy medicine, although U.S. officials have not confirmed that.\par \par \uc2 We visited Matamoros to report on migrant tent camps for years, but violence there has escalated. The city of about half a million people just across the border from Brownsville, Texas is home to warring factions of the gulf drug cartel. The shootouts on Friday prompted the U.S. consulate to issue another security alert.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is offering a $50,000 reward for the safe return of the Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Gabe is with me now. Do we know anything about the identities of the Americans?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Well, Lester, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has not confirmed any details but I just spoke with a family member of one of those abducted and she says the group was from South Carolina but had a rental car with North Carolina plates. She says the group crossed the border for a medical procedure. A law enforcement source with knowledge of the matter tells NBC News it was cosmetic surgery. And the family is pleading for their safe return tonight, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to those mid-air scares. The latest, a man arrested for allegedly trying to open a plane`s emergency exit door during a flight, then stab a flight attendant. That coming after a Southwest flight was forced to land after a bird strike. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It happened on a United flight from L.A. to Boston. Federal prosecutors say 45 minutes before landing, a male passenger attempted to open an exit door between first class and coach. When flight attendants confronted the man, he allegedly tried to stab one of them in the neck with a broken spoon. Fellow passengers tackled and subdued the passenger until the plane landed in Boston. 33-year-old Francisco Torres tonight being held on federal charges.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, smoke and panic on Southwest flight 3923 Sunday. The 737 hit by birds as it took off from Havana, Cuba headed for Fort Lauderdale. The engine exploded and on fire and on fire. Terrified passengers donned oxygen masks as the crew circled back, making an emergency landing in Havana.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:28]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Smoke started coming into the cabin, and they just said stay in your seats and we had to start breathing.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Bird strikes are common, but rarely cause serious damage. The miracle on the Hudson landing in 2009 came after a flock of geese took out both of the plane`s engines. Meanwhile, tragedy on board a private plane when a passenger, 55-year-old Dana Hyde of Maryland, was killed as the Bombardier Challenger plane hit severe turbulence. The plane flying from New Hampshire to Virginia making an emergency landing in Connecticut. The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says the pilots were dealing with a trim issue at the time, suggesting they may have hit clear air turbulence in a jet stream moving 50 to 100 miles an hour faster than normal.\par \par \uc2 JOHN COX, NBC NEWS AVIATION ANALYST: Clear air turbulence is not something that radar shows. So, pilots are unable to see it in advance.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Every year, passengers and crew members suffer head lacerations, broken bones and bruises in severe turbulence.\par \par \uc2 SARA NELSON\uc2 , INTERNATIONAL PRESIDENT, ASSOCIATION OF FLIGHT ATTENDANTS: This is exactly why we tell you to wear your seat belt because if you`re not wearing your seat belt when severe turbulence like this happens, it can actually be deadly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): The incidents are rare but they come as the \uc2 FAA\uc2  prepares for an aviation safety summit next week. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Parts of Southern California remain under a state of emergency tonight with many residents still trapped after days of historic snowfall. First responders still trying to reach those stranded with some running out of food and medicine. Miguel Almaguer is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): As blizzard- like conditions blanket California cities already buried under feet of snow --\par \par \uc2 ROBIN UNWIN-BASSETT, RESIDENT, TWIN PEAKS, CALIFORNIA: We have neighbors who are panicking. They`re without, you know, food, power, heat, medications.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: For nearly two weeks now, trapped residents near Lake Arrowhead just two hours outside of Los Angeles have been pleading for help. Today, middle school teacher Steve Gaskell is a literal lifeline. His jeep loaded with meals and insulin for Marisa Kupsak`s uncle who`s stranded at home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 It`s overwhelming because my husband can`t even come home. He`s stuck at the bottom of the hill.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: As the desperate ration food and medicine, mounds of snow are trapping the most vulnerable who can`t dig or plow their way out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s -- it`s tough. It really is. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The National Guard and firefighters have been called in to help. But it`s volunteers, some walking miles through waist-deep snow, making the difference.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think the first responders are overwhelmed with the sheer volume of calls that they`re getting.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With major roads being plowed, hand crews are slowly starting to reach senior citizens like Sidney White.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can`t see myself getting out of here without them.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: But with more than nine feet of snowfall, the weight of these storms was far too great for the only grocery store in the community of crestline. The parking lot now a free food distribution site for residents like the Moncriefs, who were lucky to get past road blocks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People want to go to their homes. Let them home.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Tonight, the sense of desperation spelled out across this community as help finally arrives for some but not all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: So, Miguel, what is the situation right now with power and water?\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): Well, Lester, people without power and water are slowly getting it back. I want to show you a live picture from our drone overhead. It shows another major problem they`re dealing with all across this area. That heavy snowfall is collapsing roofs. Lester, the people in this area, they need help and they need it fast. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Miguel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Dylan Dreyer who`s tracking all this. Dylan, when will the West Coast get some relief?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST (on camera): Well, where Miguel is we`re going to get some relief, but the next storm is already making its way across central and Northern California where over the next several days we`re going to see rounds of snow. So all the way through Wednesday at least, we will see several inches of snow, and in the higher elevations of the Sierra Nevada mountain range we`ll see one to three feet of snow.\par \par \uc2 Then we`ll get a bit of a break before the next big storm system makes its way on shore for Friday into Saturday. We also have a weak little system moving across Lake Erie right now. This is going to produce just enough snow to make things tricky for Tuesday morning. Heaviest amounts will be across north-central Pennsylvania. But even in New York City just north of Philly, we could end up with enough on the roads to make things slippery for tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Dylan, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Alarming scenes outside Atlanta where police say they faced a coordinated attack by rioters at the site of a planned police training facility. Tonight, more than 20 people are charged with domestic terrorism. Blayne Alexander has late details.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In Atlanta, a chaotic clash. \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  released this video that they say shows rioters throwing fireworks at officers and setting construction vehicles and a trailer on fire. In the end, 23 charged with domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 DARIN SCHIERBAUM, CHIEF, ATLANTA POLICE: When you throw Molotov cocktails, large rocks, a number of items at officers, your only intent is to harm.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Police call it a coordinated attack by violent agitators. Of the 23 arrested, only two are from Georgia. While protesters say it all started at a family-friendly music festival and that officials used excessive force to arrest some who were not involved. All of it centered around a planned public safety training center that opponents have dubbed cop city.\par \par \uc2 The planned site is right here, 85 acres just outside of Atlanta. A training facility for both \uc2 Atlanta Police\uc2  and Fire. Now, officers have been stationed nearby but they say they`re going to increase their presence in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Officials tout it as a way to improve community policing.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 , MAYOR OF ATLANTA: This is where your community and neighborhood watch programs, will learn how to keep your neighborhood safe.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But critics say it will do just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 KAMAU FRANKLIN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, COMMUNITY MOVEMENT BUILDERS: 90 acres of the forest are going to be destroyed right away to build cop city. So, we see this as a further militarization of the police.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: This is the latest flashpoint in a fight that has stretched well over a year. In January, a protester was killed. A Georgia State Patrol trooper injured. Tonight, protesters say that this is just the start of a week of demonstrations.\par \par \uc2 Blayne Alexander, NBC News, Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We turn now to Ohio and another derailment by a \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train. This one just a month after that toxic explosion in East Palestine. All as there is growing outrage over the response by the rail company and the federal government. Jesse Kirsch reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  is investigating another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  crash in Ohio, the company`s fourth derailment in this state in less than five months. This Springfield train wreck caught on camera. The company says 28 cars went off the track this weekend with two crew members on board. No injuries or public health threats were reported.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY, CHAIR, \uc2 NTSB\uc2 : 212 rail cars, some of which were tank cars transporting hazardous materials.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  says the cars with hazardous materials were not among those that derailed. Today, the company promising more investment in sensors that detect overheated wheel bearings.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Even one derailment is too many. And yet the average in recent years has been several every single day.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg says railroad standards need to change.\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: But it`s really the entire industry that has been pushing hard to water down or delay safety regulations.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Is it now time to try figuring out a way to possibly pause \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 `s operations until these investigations are completed?\par \par \uc2 BUTTIGIEG: Well, the reality is that our economy depends on a strong freight rail network operating 24/7. We shouldn`t have to shut down the rail system in this country in order to make sure that it is safe.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Still, some residents here don`t fully trust what they`re hearing from officials after last month`s toxic derailment in East Palestine more than 200 miles away.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don`t know if it`s frustrating, but it`s just kind of scary that I`m sure there`s safety protocols in place and they`re not being followed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH (on camera): Bipartisan calls for rail safety reform growing. Meanwhile, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 `s trains here once again on the move. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Jesse Kirsch, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, jurors in the Alex Murdaugh murder trial speak out about their guilty verdict, revealing their reactions after Murdaugh took the stand.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 A lawyer for Alex Murdaugh tells NBC News he is now off suicide watch but remains in isolation in a South Carolina prison. And now we`re hearing from some of the jurors who convicted him of murdering his wife and son. Here`s Catie Beck.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): After six weeks of testimony and tears --\par \par \uc2 ALEX MURDAUGH, FOUND GUILTY OF KILLING HIS WIFE AND SON: Seeing my boys lay face down --\par \par \uc2 BECK: Jurors in the Alex Murdaugh trial breaking their silence about the decision to seal his fate.\par \par \uc2 JAMES MCDOWELL, JUROR, ALEX MURDAUGH MURDER TRIAL: We already know that he`s a lawyer. He`s able to be emotional with cases, he`s able to be emotional with himself, so I think we were kind of able to read right through that.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Murdaugh found guilty and sentenced to life for killing his wife Maggie and son Paul. Now, a convicted murderer behind bars.\par \par \uc2 Alex Murdaugh will remain at the Kirkland Correctional Center for at least 30 days to be processed and evaluated.\par \par \uc2 Murdaugh was on suicide watch for the first 72 hours and is now in protective custody, locked in isolation for 23 hours a day. Jurors say Murdaugh`s fate was close to decided from the first minutes inside the jury room.\par \par \uc2 AMIE WILLIAMS, JUROR, ALEX MURDAUGH MURDER TRIAL: Everyone had questions, but we were able to explain everything.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Jurors say Murdaugh`s decision to take the stand in his own defense hurt his case more than it helped.\par \par \uc2 MCDOWELL: I think that he believes that he`s so convincing that he felt like that was his, you know, last resort.\par \par \uc2 BECK: But for several, the cell phone video from the dog kennels tipped the scales to guilty. Some jurors accept they may never understand Murdaugh`s motive but have no doubt the punishment fits the crime.\par \par \uc2 GWEN GENERETTE, JUROR, ALEX MURDAUGH MURDER TRIAL: He wanted to have control of everything. Some thinking it was more like greed, him being in control.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Catie Beck, NBC News, Columbia, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And next, as we continue tonight, our exclusive reporting on a new legal challenge to the near total abortion ban in Texas.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back with our NBC News exclusive. A new lawsuit being filed in Texas challenging the state`s near-total ban on abortion and seeking clarity on when doctors are allowed to intervene in dangerous pregnancies without a fear of prosecution. Here`s Yamiche Alcindor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): When Amanda and Josh Zurawski found out they were expecting a baby girl, they were overjoyed. The name they picked for her, Willow.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI, HAD AN ABORTION: I mean, it was magical. The first 17 weeks were very easy, very smooth sailing.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But just before week 18, something felt wrong. Amanda`s body was preparing for labor too early. The news from her doctor was devastating.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI: She said pretty quickly, you know, all in one breath, you`re going to lose the baby. She`s not going to survive. I thought what they would do is go in and intervene immediately, but she told me that they couldn`t because the baby`s heart was still beating and I wasn`t sick.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Laws in Texas ban doctors from performing abortions except when a medical emergency endangers a person`s life or major bodily function. Those who violate the law could face up to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 Take me into what it was like to wait.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI: I just existed in sorrow and fear. I was really, really scared.\par \par \uc2 JOSH ZURAWSKI, HUSBAND OF AMANDA: This just isn`t something that you do with any other kind of medical issue, is you have to ask am I sick enough to get treatment. It was just -- it was infuriating.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Three days after the diagnosis, Amanda`s health deteriorated. Doctors performed an abortion, but serious infections led to sepsis, and Amanda spent three days in the ICU.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI: Because of the law, I very nearly died. Nothing about this is pro-life.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Now, Amanda is part of a group of seven plaintiffs represented by the Center for Reproductive Rights who plan to file a lawsuit against the State of Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI: Until there is a legal fight, nothing`s going to change. And something has to change.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But John Seago, president of Texas Right to Life, who helped craft the Texas Heartbeat Act, says the law is clear as written and that doctors like Amanda`s are misinterpreting it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO, PRESIDENT, TEXAS RIGHT TO LIFE: If they are saying they have to delay and wait the days like you`re talking about, that is medical malpractice. But if it is uncertain that this child may survive or may not, Texas is saying we don`t jump to conclusions.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Doctors are saying the law is not clear, and as a result I`m going to act on the side of being cautious, and that means that I might have to wait for a woman to get sicker and sicker before I can perform an abortion that I know she needs.\par \par \uc2 SEAGO: That`s the wrong conclusion.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2  is the president of the Center for Reproductive Rights.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT, THE CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: The issue here is not that the doctors are being too cautious. The reality is without clarification of this Texas law, without a change in the circumstances that are happening today, it is going to come that women are going to die.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Seago disagrees.\par \par \uc2 SEAGO: The law is not putting lives in danger. The law is black and white about what a doctor can do and what he cannot do.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Does it ever give you pause that there are situations that have happened after this law where women`s lives have been put at risk?\par \par \uc2 SEAGO: Yes. Our mission is to save lives. So, the fact that some women`s lives have been jeopardized is something that we take very seriously.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI: Little outfit.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Amanda and Josh are still grieving their loss.\par \par \uc2 J. ZURAWSKI: I know.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI: Damn onesie gets me every time.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: They hope something changes.\par \par \uc2 A. ZURAWSKI: I want nothing more than to have kids. But I`m terrified to be pregnant again.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News, Austin, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`ll take a short break. Up next, remembering one of rock`s legendary guitarists.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, he was the last surviving founding member of a classic southern rock band. Harry Smith now on the legendary guitarist Gary Rossington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRY SMITH, NBC NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Gary Rossington was one of the original members of Lynyrd Skynyrd. He co-wrote Sweet Home Alabama. The Jacksonville, Florida-based band exemplified the blues-fused sound of southern rock.\par \par \uc2 What`s Your Name was one of their biggest hits. For many, Skynyrd was the sound of the `70s. But at the peak of their popularity, the band`s chartered plane crashed, killing bandmates Ronnie Van Zant and Steve Gaines.\par \par \uc2 Rossington was terribly hurt but recovered and kept playing. Rare was the performance where an audience member didn`t raise a Bic lighter and shout "free bird," Rossington`s signature solo.\par \par \uc2 Today, the band posted "Gary is now with his Skynyrd brothers and family in heaven, playing it pretty like he always does." Gary Rossington was 71.\par \par \uc2 Harry Smith, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230307ej3600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc37}{\*\bkmkend toc37}\b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS for March 5, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Hallie Jackson, Pat McCrory \par \uc2 10,579 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:18]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, crime and punishment. President Biden backs down from fighting a Republican effort to block D.C.`s more lenient`s approach to crime.\par \par \uc2 PAUL VALLAS\uc2 , (D) CHICAGO MAYORAL CANDIDATE: We will make Chicago the safest city in America.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just after the perception of rising crime took down Chicago`s mayor.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT, (D) CHICAGO: We fought the right fights.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can Biden`s moves help Democrats from being seen as soft on crime going into 2024?\par \par \uc2 Plus, divide and conquer. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  isn`t officially in the race yet, but he`s eagerly courting Trump`s base.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: I think there`s a strong anti-woke majority out there across the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The primary fight has kicked into high gear as more candidates consider challenging Trump.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: For those who have been wronged and betrayed, I am your retribution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll talk to New Hampshire Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who`s considering his own White House run. And, confronting China, a bipartisan push to face the growing threats from Beijing.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , (R) WISCONSIN: This is an existential struggle over what life will look like in the 21st Century.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tensions continue to escalate as the U.S. warns about supplying arms to Russia and new concern over COVID`s origins.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, FEDERAL BUREAU OF INVESTIGATION: The origins of the pandemic are mostly likely a potential lab incident in Wuhan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In a joint interview, I`ll talk to the chair and ranking member of the House Intelligence Committee, Republican \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2  of Ohio and Democrat \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  of Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson; former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson; USA Today Washington bureau chief Susan Page; and former Republican governor of North Carolina \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press with Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 This may be the week where we saw the 2024 presidential campaign kick into full gear, and not just on the Republican side. President Biden`s decision to block a D.C. crime law caught many Democrats off guard. Because it`s going to be the first time in 30 years that Congress has nullified a law in D.C. And it`s a Democratic president and a Democratic Senate.\par \par \uc2 But if you view it through the lens of political calculation, it may make perfect sense. President Biden decided that neither he nor his party, with a big Senator map to defend with a lot of red areas to defend, could afford to look soft on crime heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 Republicans tried to make crime a central issue in the 2022 midterms, and they certainly had some success in New York State and a few other areas. But the results this week in the Chicago mayor`s race, where voters fired the incumbent, are a reminder that crime and policing are still important to urban voters as well, a huge part of the Democratic coalition.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have been twisted in knots on the crime issue because it`s a problem with swing voters. And Biden is trying to provide them with a way forward. The mirror image of, sort of, being twisted in knots for Republicans is the abortion issue. But no one in the GOP, at least to date, is taking the lead to try and come up with a modified solution to that political problem for them.\par \par \uc2 If anything, the Republicans are more fractured, and not just on the abortion issue but about the general direction of the party. It`s a divide that has been on full display this weekend. But there`s one issue where there is little divide these days, and it is on China, with its rising national security threats.\par \par \uc2 The secretary of state, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , issued yet another direct warning to China about sending lethal aid to Russia for the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE ANTONY BLINKEN: China can`t have it both ways. It can`t putting itself out as a force for peace in public while it, one way or another, continues to fuel the flames of this fire that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  started.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: If we want to dig deeper into this, joining me now are the chair and ranking member of the House Intelligence Committee, appearing here together, Republican Congressman \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2  of Ohio and Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim Himes\uc2  of Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, welcome back to "Meet the Press," and thank you for coming on together.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKE TURNER\uc2 , (R) OHIO & CHAIR OF HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM HIMES, (D), CONNECTICUT & RANKING MEMBER, HOUSE INTELLIGENCE COMMITTEE: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before we start on this stuff, your -- this train derailment overnight happened in your congressional district. I know the shelter in place order is no longer. What -- what more can you tell us? What more have you learned, and is the federal government responding?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Sure. This truly is outrageous. You know, Ohio is really the crossroads of America, both on the road and in the rail. An unbelievable amount of goods travel through Ohio, some hazardous. What we`ve seen recently with the risk to communities is -- is unacceptable. Luckily, it seems we may have missed a bullet in this one, that this train may have been empty, and it looks like hazardous materials is not going to be a threat to the community. We`ll have to see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it clear -- do you think it`s clear that the freight industry isn`t well-regulated and needs to be?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Oh, absolutely. And the fact that we`re having derailment after derailment shows really the lack of investment -- the disinvestment in our infrastructure, and that needs to change.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move to this -- Congressman Himes, I`ll -- I`ll start with this. I know both of you have been getting these briefings. Another warning to China. We heard it from the secretary of state about arming Russia. Our reporting indicates that this actually came from our intelligence inside of Russia, confirmed (inaudible). How serious is this threat, and do we know if China has made a final decision?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, no question it`s a serious threat. We`ve seen the Russian military obviously come apart. The Chinese could reverse in the same way that the Iranians are helping the Russians right now. I think the administration is doing the right things by saying "Don`t do this."\par \par \uc2 Now, it doesn`t surprise me at all that there`s a debate inside China about whether they should do this. Hopefully, wisdom prevails. I -- I couldn`t help but note, in the Chinese proposed peace plan, the 12th point said we need to focus on rebuilding China -- rebuilding Russia. I can imagine the Chinese see a lot of business there. Obviously, if they`re part of the Russian assault on Ukraine, they will not get that business. So, no, I don`t think a final decision has been made, but I do think it`s wise for us to say "Don`t go there."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Turner, you -- you`ve noted that we have a different posture now on our intelligence. We`re releasing more of it. Do you think more of it should be released to make sure the world believes us?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right, well, and obviously you`re seen with the CIA director, even calling out to China on this issue that they`re considering it has a huge shift in policy. And what we`re seeing is the administration using intelligence to try to impact the outcome of -- of policy, right?\par \par \uc2 So they`re not just saying "We happen to know this" in looking to hold someone accountable. They`re trying to thwart them, to stop them from doing this. You know, the problem with China entering this is because, you know, you`ve got the West giving weapons to Ukraine. You`ve got Russia depleting their stores. We obviously, the West together, have an ability to impact Ukraine greater than Russia alone does. It would be an inexhaustible source of weapons if China and its production capacity supported Russia. And that would change the dynamics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What kind of intel do we have on the state of the Russian army, Congressman Himes, meaning, like, we`ve seen some reports that they may be running out of munitions; they have some issues. What does our folks say?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, so neither Mike nor I can get too deep into any particular intel, but, you know, you don`t need to get too deep into intel. Consider the fact that you see every single day on television, which is the entire Russian army cannot take a town that nobody had ever heard of a year ago, the town of Bakhmut.\par \par \uc2 Now, you know, Mike and I have been doing this long enough that we remember when we literally devoted hundreds of billions of dollars and much of our national security apparatus to this beast of a Russian military. Today we see that they can`t, even with the push of the president, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , they can`t take a town. That doesn`t mean they`re not still dangerous. They have nuclear weapons. But they are not what we thought they were.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play something. \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  was at the G-20, Congressman turner. And wait -- I mean, wait until you hear the reaction to something he said here. Let me play it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERGEY LAVROV\uc2 , RUSSIAN FOREIGN MINISTER: The war which we are trying to stop and which was launched against us using the Ukraine...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... Ukrainian people...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAVROV: of course influenced -- influenced -- influenced the policy of Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, there`s nothing like being able to say the phrase, "The world was laughing" at them.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Completely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The world was laughing at them.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Spontaneously.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What should -- what should the West use a moment like that for?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the thing about both Putin, Lavrov and even Medvedev, although he`s supposed to be the new, new face of a new Russia, come from an era where, you know, they controlled Eastern Europe. They could say things that were not true and people believed them because they controlled information. Today information can`t be controlled. So the -- the laughability of what they`re saying, and the ridiculousness of what they`re saying is easily exposed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let me move to the issue of these classified documents. Nobody seems to be satisfied with -- with what you`ve got. Congressman Himes, does that include you? Were you satisfied with the briefing?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: So we have a lot more work to do. Look, I think, as the leaders of the Intelligence Committee, our number one job is to make sure that the DNI, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , whoever needs to do it, does everything they need to do to protect the sources and methods that might have been exposed were any of those documents to have been out in the wild.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a lengthy process to figure out that. And -- and I can`t speak for Mike, but I will tell you that, though we have been briefed, we`ve got a lot more to do to make sure that the government is doing what it should be doing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask specifically, do you know what the Trump docs are, Congressman Turner?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We -- we...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you know what the Biden docs are? And do you know what the Pence docs are?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: No, but we -- there are some things that we do know.\par \par \uc2 And, first off, I want to...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me stop there. No? They didn`t share with you those things in a classified setting?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, first off, we -- in the things that we do know, one of the things we know is that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is not being forthcoming. They`re -- they`re not giving us the information. They`re claiming that it`s going to affect the outcome of their investigation, which of course it can`t because the people who are the targets of their investigation know what`s -- what are in those documents. And we have the clearance and the ability to look at these documents.\par \par \uc2 We also know, from Averil Haines, that she said that -- that she was not consulted prior to the raid on -- on Trump`s Mar-a-Lago home, that in fact there -- it was not a national security basis.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:08]\par \par \uc2 So as we go through this process, as they give us the category of the documents and their assessment as to who had access to them, who did not have access to them, we`re beginning to build an understanding. But the thing that we know is that it`s unbelievable that administration after administration is apparently sloppy and messy in their use of classified documents. And that`s one thing, on a bipartisan basis, we have to address well beyond just this. This has to change, where classified documents are under a certain amount of control.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, Congressman Himes, do you -- do you know -- do you at least know the classification levels of these docs? Do you know -- is there a -- is there a distinction between the Trump docs, the Biden docs and the Pence docs, or not?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: So we have not been shown anything that would allow us to draw that conclusion. And we`ve got to be a little careful here, because what we were shown wasn`t shown in the...\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you weren`t briefed much?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, we were -- we were briefed, but I -- but let`s just say that neither one of us are satisfied that we got enough information to execute our primary responsibility of making sure that sources and methods have been protected. We`ve got more to learn before we can be satisfied on that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So right now you have no idea whether these were the highest level...\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, we didn`t say no...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... or the mid-level.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: We didn`t say no idea.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Again, we can`t get too into the details. We got a flavor for what was there. And I won`t speak for Mike, but I will tell you, having been given a flavor, this is a very serious issue. This wasn`t stuff that we can say clearly does not matter. It matters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In all three cases?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, again...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Or you don`t know?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: We don`t know yet.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: One thing I would say, Chuck, which is important, which is also the symbolism of the fact that Jim and I are here together.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  and Adam Schiff...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... I don`t know would have done this together.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right. We -- we have shifted. And it takes two to shift to a bipartisan basis. And you see it on the Senate side, too, with \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: ... Senator Warner. And the approach to this issue of the Biden docs and the Trump docs and the Pence docs, we`re looking at holistically, what do we have to do to fix this? How do we address this? What were the risks involved?\par \par \uc2 And you have to understand, also, that without Congress having asked, there wasn`t even a risk assessment being done. We were the ones who initiated this. That`s -- that`s part of the concern.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I get the sense that the -- that the intel community doesn`t trust Congress, and you guys -- is that fair? Do you think you guys have to rebuild your -- you have to prove to them that you`re trustworthy with secrets?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I would say it`s more of a tension between the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Congress than it is the intelligence community. Because we`re having...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just more \uc2 FBI\uc2  and Congress?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Absolutely. And I think that`s going to come to a head over the next couple years. You`re seeing it in a number of areas where they`re just not -- you know, they are not special; they don`t have greater privileges than the president does. And they`re continuing to act as if they have some privilege to be able to operate without congressional oversight.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Himes, I`m curious what you -- that piece about, sort of, the tension between main Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  having to do with retrieving the classified documents from Mar-a-Lago. It looks like there was fear going in of retribution. The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , are they more concerned about perception than whether they`re doing their job? Are you worried about this?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Well, two things about the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . First of all, and Mike`s right, they are subject to congressional oversight. Secondly, they do have to preserve the integrity of their investigation. And there are investigations on board right now. So we need to work with them because both of us, everybody, wants them to be able to do investigations in an unsloppy manner. We`ve got too much of a history of investigations in a somewhat sloppy manner.\par \par \uc2 But of course there was consternation. Look, it`s one thing to conduct a raid on an average Joe citizen. To conduct a raid on a former president who, and I hope I don`t insult my good chairman here, who was, sort of, known for his, you know, aggressive retribution against people he didn`t like, I`m sure that got a lot of discussion.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, look, the lesson to be drawn here is, if you don`t want a raid on your -- on your home, don`t take a year to cough up documents that we know are out there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The COVID origin.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Or -- or six years.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Because Biden...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: ... Biden took six\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Fair enough. fair enough.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The one aspect that`s -- that`s important here is that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  did an unbelievable, terrible political miscalculation. He appointed a special prosecutor against Trump, not realizing he was going to end up having to appoint one against his own boss.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: And that`s going to -- how that`s going to play out is going to be amazing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about the -- the COVID origin situation. I think a lot of people were surprised to learn that the Energy Department had an intelligence agency and, in fairness, and I -- you know, some of us know that`s true. They deal with our nukes. There`s a reason for it. But a lot of people didn`t know. But I want to -- they labeled it "low confidence." So how should the public take that?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, it`s not actually been released yet. These are all leaks that are reporting this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Great. All the more helpful to...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... messing up the story, but fair.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So we will neither confirm nor deny that they actually said that. But let`s assume that they did. In -- in all of this, because there`s no direct evidence; we don`t have China admitting it; we don`t have Wuhan lab handing these things over, all this is being assessed by looking at other aspects of the release.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you what -- one thing that`s of great concern is that the 90-day review that was undertaken when it was both a classified report and an unclassified report that came out from the intelligence community, in my opinion they don`t match.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:02]\par \par \uc2 If you read the unclassified version and then you read the classified version, you would have thought that there would have been other things, other conclusions in the unclassified version. And I think that`s what we`re seeing now is beginning to leak out, is that there are people who are saying, "Wait, I disagree with the underlying conclusion of the unclassified version."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman Himes, it -- we`re told this is still a minority view, but the \uc2 FBI\uc2  director basically, it seems, has used the Energy Department leak as a -- he wanted to go out there, and he reaffirmed something that we had already known, that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was leaning more towards leak.\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Yeah, look, I mean, you need to take a step back here, and just as Mike said, we have so few facts because the Chinese regime has obfuscated. Look, this is hard to figure out when you can do it in Atlanta at the CDC, if something goes wrong. We have so few facts that inevitably different agencies are going to arrive at different conclusions. And when an agency slightly adjusts its -- its interpretation, as the Department of Energy may have done, that doesn`t mean that all of a sudden the government has a firm view. It may be forever before we actually know exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Don`t we need -- don`t we need a 9/11 Commission, something like this? I mean, isn`t this necessary?\par \par \uc2 HIMES: We need the Chinese...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HIMES: We need the Chinese to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Don`t we need something like this?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, the speaker, Kevin McCarthy, has appointed a COVID select committee that is bipartisan, that is beginning this process. In addition, there is now a China select committee, looking at, really 360, the issues with respect to the national security threats of China.\par \par \uc2 So those things are moving forward in Congress, beyond just what our committee`s doing and other committees of jurisdiction. There there are now committees that are solely focused on just this issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Congressmen Turner and Himes, this was terrific. I look forward to doing this quite a bit with you guys...\par \par \uc2 HIMES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... over the next...\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, is it still Donald Trump`s Republican Party? Well, it sure looks like it. And if you are one of his opponents, how do you navigate those waters?\par \par \uc2 I`m going to ask Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  of New Hampshire, who`s considering his own run, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Republican presidential candidates were off and running this week, meaning the party faithful and two mainstays of Republican politics. Former President Trump and former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley were among the top hopefuls attending the Conservative Political Action Conference, known as CPAC, while Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  promoted his new book in stops in California and Texas, after speaking to donors at the annual meeting of the Club For Growth, which was also attended by our next guest.\par \par \uc2 Trump, DeSantis and Haley all are going to make their way to Iowa in the week ahead as well. But it was clear from President Trump`s comments at CPAC, the battle for the heart of the base has already begun.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They cannot steer me. They cannot shake me. And they will never, ever control me.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And they will never, ever, therefore control you.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day, anyone else will be intimidated, bought off, blackmailed or ripped to shreds. I alone will never retreat.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining us now, a man who may be going up against the former president for the GOP presidential nomination. It`s New Hampshire Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor Sununu, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 [09:20:01]\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2 , (R) NEW HAMPSHIRE: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Well, you spoke at a donor conference this weekend. It was about national politics, potentially running for president. Are you closer to a decision? And -- and what -- what is holding you back if you`re not?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah, so, no, I`m not really focusing on the discussion right now. There will be plenty of time for that. Right now, my mission is making sure we`re making this party bigger, frankly. You can`t govern if you don`t win. And so I`m really focused on how do we win, no matter who the candidate is? How do we win in November of `24?\par \par \uc2 Well, we have to attract independents. We have to bring that next generation of voter more on the team. There`s a lot of gap between where we are as Republicans and the younger -- let`s call it the 27 and under generation of potential Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So I`m trying to, kind of, steer the message in the right way. I`m trying to get folks a little more optimistic, inspirational, hopeful for this great product that I think the Republicans have. Sometimes we`re just not so great on the messaging.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting to me that you`re at Club For Growth talking about making -- making this your message. The Club For Growth last year supported a lot of candidates that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supported. Now, they`ve broken from him on the presidential, but they -- they spent a lot of times not supporting candidates that you just described, not supporting the candidates that arguably would have tried to grow the party or appeal to the middle.\par \par \uc2 Have you heard any regret at this conference about how Club For Growth worked in 2022?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, look, it`s not about regret. I think the entire Republican Party, hopefully they looked at November of `22, saw we didn`t win the races that we should have won, understand the value of candidate quality, of messaging, of getting out there early, of not letting Democrats define you early. It`s not just about money; it`s about having that collective message that has to go forward about being more optimistic, about being something that folks want to join, not just with their dollars but with their vote, hopefully, in November, and not -- like I said, not let those Democrats divide us.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s just a fundamental change in approach that I think the entire Republican Party is very open to and looking forward to as we hit the November election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand what you`re trying to do. At the same time, you heard the former president at CPAC, and he certainly has a stranglehold on 25 percent to 35 percent of the party. We can have a debate about the specific number. And you know what those folks want. They want to make liberals cry, right, like that`s the message they want. They want that more than they want a big tent. So how do you appeal to those voters?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: You focus on leadership that is results-driven, that gets stuff done. You know, in 2017 and 2018 as a Republican, I was told we were going to get immigration reform. It didn`t happen. I was told about healthcare reform. I was told about balancing a budget and taking care of our debt, not spending more than we actually have, actually working towards free trade and these types of things that can be economic engine and drivers. A lot of that did not happen because -- some good things happened. Don`t get me wrong. I give the president credit where -- where it`s deserved, on regulatory form and his speed of the vaccine, things like that.\par \par \uc2 But a lot of things didn`t happen. If you want to -- if there`s that part of the party that wants, as you said, to make liberals cry, or whatever it might be, you do it by winning and you do it by getting stuff done, passing it through Congress, working on both sides, taking the first steps of securing the border and immigration reform. You do it by getting stuff done, not just through winning a nomination. You`ve got to close the deal, and you`ve got to get it done in `25.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s what we`re doing. We`re looking for the most conservative candidate that can win in `24 and actually work with Congress to -- to, kind of, finish the deal, finish on that obligation, that accountability in 2025.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you may be running to lead a party that did this yesterday. The Texas Republican Party censured a Republican member of Congress, Tony Gonzales, who won a swing district, OK, in an area that, you know, it`s not always easy for Republicans to win. And his two sins were supporting a gun reform bill following the shooting at Uvalde and for voting to codify same- sex marriage protections. And I looked at what he was censured for, and I`m like, "Boy, they haven`t met Governor Sununu yet, in Texas."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, look, there -- there are aspects of the Republican Party, whether it`s the Texas Republican Party, the New Hampshire Republican Party, all the different groups that are out there. They have their own vision; they have their own leadership; they have their own agendas, of course.\par \par \uc2 That`s why, as candidates, we have to be about what we`re about, what we`re going to deliver, the accountability that we believe in, standing up and making sure we actually not just win the election but show up and actually do the job. That`s what ultimately galvanizes support around you.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans cannot win without independents. It cannot happen. So if we just stay in this ultra-conservative extreme lane, which is a very small part of the party, by the way, that`s all we`re going -- I get it, that`s where the headlines are going to be, right? But at the end of the day, if we can get stuff done -- and I think governors do a great job of that; mayors do a great job of that. In Congress, not so much, on either side of the aisle, but governors and mayors have an amazing ability every day to make decisions to impact people`s lives and deliver results. And that`s exactly what folks are going to be looking for in `24\par \par \uc2 TODD: The \uc2 RNC\uc2  chair, \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , says that she wants to have candidates sign a pledge they`ll support the Republican nominee, no matter who it is, if you want to participate in an \uc2 RNC\uc2 -sanctioned event.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:10]\par \par \uc2 Are you comfortable signing that pledge if Donald Trump`s involved?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah, look, I`m a lifelong Republican. I`m going to support the Republican nominee. When you look at what`s coming out of the White House, it isn`t Democrat policies. It`s real left-wing, extreme-agenda type stuff that is not in the best interests of this country. And I have no doubt that any solid Republican is -- would be better than -- than what comes out.\par \par \uc2 As far as former President Trump, I think he`s going to run, obviously. He`s in the race. He`s not going to be the nominee. That`s just not going to happen. And so I think there`s a lot of opportunity to bring forward what the -- the Republican Party, not what we were, not yesterday`s leadership or yesterday`s story, or crying about what happened in November of `22, but what we are going to bring to the table and get done tomorrow. That`s what America is looking for. And so I`m really confident that whoever comes out of the Republican nomination process is -- is going to lead this country and will be able to deliver a win in `24. And I`ll back `im.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, New Hampshire Republicans have a habit of actually always going for the outsider, whatever that moment is, in that moment, who the outsider candidate is. And it`s been remarkable to me that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looks like he`s trying to be the outsider. He was -- he ran the party. He was...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... the institutional head of the party. He`s running as an outsider. And I know you want to be the outsider, but in many ways he`s already lumped everybody against him. Do you think he`s effectively carving out an outsider lane for himself?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I think the former president has his own lane. He doesn`t need to carve anything. He`s an absolute known commodity to every American in this country. Right? There`s very few people that are on the fence whether they`re with him or not with him, or whatever it may be.\par \par \uc2 So I`m not -- I think he just has his lane and then everyone else, which is a vast majority of the party, that`s looking at an alternative.\par \par \uc2 Right now, if the election were today, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  would win in New Hampshire. There`s no doubt about that in my mind. I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  would win in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So he`s -- I think the former president is trying to find a path to be back the leading voice of the party. I think a lot of us are, you know, that potentially may get in the race want to have something to say about the direction of that that conversation.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I -- gain, thank you for your service. We`re moving on. I just don`t believe the Republican Party is going to say that the best leadership for America tomorrow is yesterday`s leadership. That doesn`t make any sense. That is not in our DNA as Americans. It`s, kind of, the antithesis of the American spirit to settle for yesterday`s news. We want the next generation, the next big idea. And that`s what we`re going to deliver.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The abortion issue is one that I think has twisted -- if the crime issue has twisted the Democrats in knots, the abortion issues has twisted the Republican Party in knots. A lot of people dismiss your candidacy by simply saying the Republican Party is never going to nominate somebody who calls themselves pro-choice or has been identified as pro-choice. How do you change that sentiment in the party?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, the whole dynamic around that issue has changed, right?\par \par \uc2 So most governors are taking it upon themselves to either -- if they`re pro-life, they`re moving to ban it. And in some states they`re making that choice. That`s between them and their voters. There are some governors that are looking for this whole, you know, pro-abortion stuff up until the day of birth, and we`ll see where that goes. That`s terrible. New Hampshire has a 24-week rule. Some states have 20 weeks or 16 weeks. That`s where we all are. Right?\par \par \uc2 So we all provide -- the majority of us provide some choice in some fashion. But if you`re pro-life, then those governors are going to truly ban it. If you`re pro-abortion, there -- and the rest of us are, kind of, in the middle here.\par \par \uc2 So I just think the fundamental conversation has changed so much, not that it`s not an -- an important issue, but folks are going to have to really define themselves as to where they are. I don`t -- I don`t worry about it too much because we have a 24-week rule in New Hampshire. It`s pretty much where most of America is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, you said something interesting in one of your previous interviews about, you know, this idea that, if you get in, it could dilute the field and that helps Trump. And you said, "It`s not about getting in; it`s about knowing when to get out."\par \par \uc2 So what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Yeah. Well, that means, look, I -- one thing I`ve learned -- look, I`ve won four times. I`ve been in this political game for a while. I`ve learned that you can`t tell people not to run. If someone really wants to run, they`re going to run, and that`s fine. But, unlike 2016, I`m going to make sure, and I think other folks are going to make sure, that we all have the discipline to get out before it`s too late. And those that don`t I think will be chastised very publicly for doing so.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve got to be honest. I`ve talked to all the candidates. They all understand that. They really do. We`re going to take our time. There`s a lot to play out over the next nine months to see who can really galvanize, to make sure that we have a candidate that`s winning a true majority of the vote. And I have no -- I have full confidence, I should say, that we`re going to get there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, have you ruled out running for governor again?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: No, look, I don`t rule anything out. I really don`t. I`m -- I`m in the -- I just passed -- I didn`t pass it yet, I submitted my balanced budget, with a big surplus and all that kind of stuff, to the legislature.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:03]\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to go through my legislative session.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: So I love being governor. I mean, I love it. It is a job unlike any other. So we`ll see what happens, you know, in the future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you think \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  and Fox News need to do to regain some trust after what we`ve learned about this? And are you at all concerned you can have an honest conversation with \uc2 Fox\uc2  viewers?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Oh, well, I don`t think it`s just Fox. I mean, I`ll put them in there, but all of media, all of television media, and everyone has to own a little bit of the lack of trust, the lack of accountability.\par \par \uc2 It`s okay to get something wrong in the news, but you`ve got to come back and own it. And whether it`s Fox, or CNN, or MSBNC, or, you know, whatever, everyone just has to own it. As a governor, I might try four or five things, and if one or two don`t work I`ll say, hey, that didn`t work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What about intentionally lying to viewers?\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: But I own it. We can acknowledge it, we can pivot the funding.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. Intentionally lying to viewers, though, that to me seemed to cross a line.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Well, look --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can make a mistake, but that wasn`t a mistake.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: So explain to me -- look, I`m not defending anybody because I think you`re all in the same basket, I really do. But I could go to CNN when they`re going to ignore the Hunter Biden laptop story, we could talk about the virus truly coming out of the lab in Wuhan, we could talk about a lot of different things.\par \par \uc2 If you`re not owning that you misrepresented the story, whether it was intentional or not, everybody does it, and that`s the problem. America is losing faith in media, and you guys have a huge opportunity to regain that.\par \par \uc2 But right now, my message to Fox News is build your ratings. Build your audience, go bigger, because if we don`t go bigger as a Republican Party we can`t win in November.\par \par \uc2 So I want them to talk to independents. We`ll not change our values, or who we are, or what we`re talking about as Republicans. But get more opportunistic about the chance to bring more people in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, governor I can promise you this: At NBC News, you make a mistake like that, you would lose your job. I know that here, for what it`s worth.\par \par \uc2 Governor Sununu, appreciate you coming on.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: Not you. You would never do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure, sure.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: I`m not worried about you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I appreciate you saying that.\par \par \uc2 SUNUNU: You bet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Governor, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective with us. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, President Biden frustrated some Democrats but may have given a lifeline to others by trying to look tougher on the crime issue. Was it the right move? The panel weighs in next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:35:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is here.\par \par \uc2 NBC News senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson, former Homeland Security Secretary Jeh Johnson, former Republican Governor of North Carolina \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2 , and the bureau chief of "USA Today", Susan Page.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 Hallie, I want to start with what I think was the first -- you know, we`re talking about the presidential race is gearing up on the Republican side. But President Biden, what he did in, I guess you could call it, a triangulation on crime -- we may be nullifying the first law in D.C. -- I want to play with Muriel Bowser`s reaction was to this. I interviewed her on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Let me play her reaction and then let`s talk about it on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MURIEL BOWSER (D), WASHINGTON, D.C.: The president had issued a pretty direct and very supportive statement of administration about D.C. autonomy. And we wholeheartedly agree, President Biden has been a vocal supporter of D.C. home rule and statehood for Washington.\par \par \uc2 I vetoed the bill. Unfortunately, we live with the indignity of limited home rule in this -- in the District of Columbia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. This is quickly -- I`m going to try to get through this fast. This is complicated. D.C. did a rewrite of part of its criminal code. Mayor Bowser vetoed it. The City Council overturned her veto, 12-1, mind you. And then Congress does have the ability -- the House side does -- and they`re essentially vetoing the overrule of the veto and nullifying this law, or at least they will.\par \par \uc2 What`s interesting here is progressives have been muted in their criticism of the president. It looks like he made the right call here, because the party`s sort of backing off. Muriel Bowser was not ready to criticize him for doing this.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, I mean, I will tell you, there is still a real sense of anger among some Democratic members of Congress who are, in the words of one person that I talked to just yesterday, supremely pissed by what they saw as a surprisingly incompetent move, again -- I`m quoting here -- by the White House to, as you say, triangulate this way.\par \par \uc2 I think there is a sense, and I`ve talked with current and former White House officials, who believe that this is anger that will essentially blow over on the politics piece of it. But I`ve heard questions raised about the credibility that the White House has now moving forward, when there might be other tough votes.\par \par \uc2 Look at what the NRCC, the Republican campaign arm is doing, putting out, you know, these sort of statements saying, hey, the ad scripts write themselves for these dozens of Democrats who took a tough vote on an issue that has been tough for Democrats. I think there is an acknowledgment from Democrats they`re not where they want to be on crime. I`ve been encouraged by Democratic sources to look at it through the lens of the midterms in places like Pennsylvania, where Democrats did well where crime was an issue.\par \par \uc2 That, of course, disregards a place like Wisconsin, where that wasn`t the case.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Or New York.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know what`s interesting here, Jeh Johnson. Look, there were 31 Democrats that did vote with the Republicans on this issue in the House.\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER SECRETARY OF HOMELAND SECURITY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Twenty of the 31 won with 55 percent of the vote --\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- or less on this.\par \par \uc2 The anger coming from House Democrats are folks who have thought, wait a minute, you were going to provide cover -- you`re providing cover for \uc2 Bob Casey Jr\uc2 .? You were going to provide cover? I wish you would have told me. I might have voted differently.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, first, I can`t believe that the chair and ranking of the House Intel Committee have not seen the classified documents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Okay? This is not that hard. I used to be in the middle of those fights.\par \par \uc2 You just simply say to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , look, go to the nearest classified copy machine, put it in a pouch, bring it over here to the Hill, one copy. Let me see it and I`ll give it right back to you. How else is the legislative branch itself supposed to conduct any sort of meaningful oversight on the damage assessment?\par \par \uc2 Okay, so, Democrats and crime. I`ve written op-eds on this. I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Muriel Bowser are absolutely right. Democrats lose elections, particularly presidents and mayors, who are perceived as soft on crime, in blue communities.\par \par \uc2 And it bears emphasis that, in big cities, crime is the uppermost issue. Just ask people on MLK Avenue across the review in Anacostia, Michigan Avenue in South Chicago. Crime is an intensely personal issue, and they want to see their leaders address it.\par \par \uc2 This is how we got \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , you know, in 1993 in New York City.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Speaking of Republican mayors of blue cities, you were one, Pat.\par \par \uc2 PAT MCCRORY, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Absolutely. In the `93, `95, the 2000, Giuliani, Daley, McCrory, \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2  of Dayton. We had mayors, Republicans and Democrats, who were tough on crime because the gangs were taking over cities, drug trade was taking over cities, the homelessness was taking over our city, and we took aggressive action.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, we had large drops in especially the murder rate and the gang activity. And it was coordinated with the federal government enforcing federal gun laws. And then for whatever reason, the left took over the cities, and the Democratic Party nationally, and the crime rate went up because we started letting career criminals back on the street.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: And it`s not --\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: And they`re paying for it right now, and they deserve to pay for it.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Democrats -- it`s not like Democrats don`t have an argument here. Democrats, respectfully, Governor --\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Respectfully.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: -- Democrats are the ones that want to get guns off the streets.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Susan, what`s fascinating here is how the mayors -- these Democratic mayors -- are being pressured -- they`re being pressured on both sides, because these city councils are way to the left of the mayors. We see it here in D.C.\par \par \uc2 I mean, 12 - the veto override of Muriel Bowser was 12-1. This wasn`t -- it wasn`t like there was a big divide. The progressives have made a lot of progress on these city councils, but they haven`t been able to win mayors` races. But it`s really put mayors in the cross-hairs.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, and in fairness you`re talking a lot about social justice issues and police reform and the need for that. And we see the need for that over and over again in some of the shootings of unarmed Black men, which continues across this country.\par \par \uc2 But to make it a choice between crime and social justice is a real problem for Democrats. And I think you see with Joe Biden`s decision on where he stands on this, is his -- that is his reelection announcement. On this issue of crime and on border security he has in recent weeks moved to shore up what would`ve been the strongest issues against him in a re-election campaign.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And so far, the left`s been muted on this, even on immigration.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You hear the complaints in press releases, but you`re not hearing elected officials do it.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Coming out on TV --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: -- doing the whole thing, doing the whole circuit.\par \par \uc2 You know, and I would be curious as to Secretary Johnson`s perspective on this. I would say to your point, though, when it comes to the issue writ large, Democrats I think truly are looking to get on offense on this. You mentioned something that I`ve heard that they`re looking to do, which is talk about, for example, the assault weapons ban that President Biden wants.\par \par \uc2 It has truly pragmatically, virtually no shot in Congress as it is laid out right now -- funding I`ve heard about for the COPS program, et cetera. But you also have Democrats who are pointing to, for example, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  going out at CPAC this weekend and saying, let`s abolish the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , let`s abolish the ATF --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: -- and hoping to use that as a wedge issue as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jeh, very quickly, what do you make so far of what Secretary Mayorkas and the Biden administration have done? It looks like it`s working, but we`ll really find out in May, I guess.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, working off what we did in Venezuela in the fall, where we provided a legal safe pathway, which will discourage people from entering unlawfully, they`ve expanded that. And it looks like it`s working, though it`s way too early to declare victory. The numbers were down in January, but January is typically a low month.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, it takes a couple more months to judge.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to get to the good stuff when we come back. We`re going to talk a little Trump when you guys come back. But first, we`re going to talk about President Biden`s plan to cancel student loan debt and the uncertain future of that. We`re going to show you how many borrowers will be impacted and what`s really at stake here politically.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:45:24]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Data Download time. So this week, the Supreme Court heard arguments on the Biden administration`s plan to provide student loan debt relief for tens of millions of Americans. If the justices, though, strike it down, there are going to be a lot of unhappy voters across the country in 2024, including many in important battleground states. So let`s unpack this. How did we get here?\par \par \uc2 Well, first, let`s look at the cost of college tuition. In over the last 30 years, the cost of a private university`s tuition has gone up 80 percent, tuition at a public university has gone up 125 percent. Those are some big jumps. For what it`s worth, the cost of both housing and cars, those numbers are -- percentages are actually higher than the cost of education.\par \par \uc2 Still, this Biden student debt relief plan, if enacted, would provide up to $20,000 of relief for those that got Pell grants. Up to $10,000 for those with other types of student loans.\par \par \uc2 And guess what, when this was offered, it was popular. Over 26 million people have already applied for this debt relief, even though it is not clear whether it`s going to be allowed to happen. And if you break it down by states, and you know, how we like to do it around here, by battleground states, you see, these are some big chunks of voters who might not be happy if this gets overturned. Over a million in Pennsylvania, over a million and a half in Florida. A million in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 You see where this is going. This is a popular program. If it gets repealed, there are going to be some unhappy voters.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, this week marks the 58th anniversary of Bloody Sunday, when police beat and tear gassed hundreds of protesters in Selma, Alabama who were marching for racial equality and voting rights.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2015, I had the honor of speaking to Congressman \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  on the 50th anniversary of the Selma march about his experience leading the marchers that day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2  (D-GA): We were kneeling. We were knocked down. They started beating us with nightsticks, trampling us with horses, releasing the tear gas. I was hit in the head by a state trooper with a nightstick. I lost consciousness.\par \par \uc2 Fifty years later, I don`t recall how I made it back across that bridge to the little church that we had left from. Apparently, a group literally carried me back to the church.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`d be perfectly understandable if you were bitter. Bitter today, bitter a week later from when it happened, bitter 20 years. Were you bitter, ever, after all this?\par \par \uc2 LEWIS: I was not bitter then. I`m not bitter now. I grew up in a movement to accept the way of love, the way of peace, the way of non-violence, the way of forgiveness, as a way of life, as a way of living.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  died in 2020.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is in Selma today to commemorate the anniversary of Bloody Sunday, and the Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  will be leading the march alongside him.\par \par \uc2 Up next, can \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  stop Trump from the nomination while still courting the Trump voter? We`re about to find out.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:21]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 All right. The Republican presidential primary. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in some ways re-announced, if you will, this weekend at CPAC. Take a listen to his message.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: We are never going back to the party of \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 , Karl Rove and Jeb Bush.\par \par \uc2 We`re not going back to people that want to destroy our great Social Security system, even some in our own party. I wonder who that might be.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, I declared I am your voice. Today, I add, I am your warrior. I am your justice. And for those who have been wronged and betrayed, I am your retribution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2 , "I am your retribution."\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Wow. To see a lot of good Republican candidates who would make great presidents grovel or kiss up to both CPAC and Club for Growth, is, is -- it bothers me because not one of those groups has ever solved a problem. And right now, we have some complex problems in this country that have to be solved.\par \par \uc2 And what Donald Trump`s going to realize is they`re going to start having infighting among those two groups because CPAC`s with Trump, but Club for Growth, who used Trump in primary commercials to push their candidate, are probably going to turn on Trump. So, we have this internal warfare which may result in a general election where the Democrats have their candidate, the Republicans have the last candidate, and it might open up the door for a third-party candidate because the independent voter, which is 40 percent of the registered voters now in America, are going to go, "Is this the only choice we have?"\par \par \uc2 TODD: Susan, we did a little survey of CPAC attendees just on the issue of Ukraine. And it just shows you where the Trump base is on this. And it shows you this is a different lane than what we`re used to. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARRY MORGENSTEIN, CPAC ATTENDEE: Of course, I feel for the people of Ukraine. I would like them to beat the Russians, but we have so many problems. We have open borders here that the money could be going to.\par \par \uc2 AMY SHANK, CPAC ATTENDEE: Well, I think support is okay. But I think we need to be focusing on the people here first rather than other countries.\par \par \uc2 RENE CAMPBELL, CPAC ATTENDEE: Don`t take my taxpayer money and send it over to Ukraine. Enough with this, you know? Enough with this war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Susan, I think what`s remarkable here is Donald Trump`s carving out the outsider lane. I mean, this is not the mainstream Republican position these days on Ukraine. But it is Trump`s position.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: It is, although -- and it`s not the mainstream Republican position, and it`s not Mitch McConnell`s view, for instance. But there is growing public concern, and not just among Trump voters, about the extent and the length and the cost of the U.S. commitment to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:04]\par \par \uc2 And there`s still majority support for continuing the effort, but it is really pressuring the administration to come up with some answers about how this war could end in a successful way and not go on forever.\par \par \uc2 But it -- Trump -- we say it`s not the mainstream Republican position. This is Trump`s party. This is not the Republican Party. It`s the Trump party. Trump`s position becomes the Republican position.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But has he - has he somehow been able to position DeSantis as part of the establishment?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: You said this a couple -- you said this just now. You said it to Governor Sununu. And I wrote it down because it was interesting to me, the idea that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is trying to run as an outsider. Because, you know, from the jump I have been hearing from sources close to his team that they want a position that`s his a la 2016, obviously not as of a la 2020.\par \par \uc2 And somebody said to me this weekend, well, you know, he`s doing better than he was at this point in 2016. And I said, I sure hope so. He was the president for four years.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s almost as though they`re trying to erase the fact that he was in the White House for four years to do what you`re talking about here. Is he going to come up with a new nickname for DeSantis? How is he going to position him as the outsider?\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you what is so interesting to me just in talking with people in the last 24 hours about sort of CPAC and Club for Growth. You know, Trump allies are saying this is the most important gathering of conservatives since -- that will happen in this campaign until the Republican primary debate.\par \par \uc2 And then I heard somebody else, a rival campaign adviser, let`s say, say, well, wait a second. This is a bunch of MAGA misfits who are showing up -- their words, right? And I`m not quoting but paraphrasing here, showing up to be super pro-Trump here. And actually, the adults in the room are going to Club for Growth and talking about what they`re going to do on -- from a policy perspective.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But you know what the difference is between the Club for Growth and CPAC? The Club for Growth - the folks at Club for Growth talk behind the cameras, behind the scenes, not to voters, but to donors, acting like establishment people. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was talking to people. Whatever you think of it, he was talking to the public.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: This could be wild. You could have the major candidate in the party, an indicted criminal defendant, out on bail.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He said he was going to keep running if indicted.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: But do you know what the most interesting thing he said was to me? Because this is a lot of sort of similar grievances we`ve heard. You know what was new? He said people should vote by mail-in ballot.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, yeah. Speaking of new, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  is not running. I assume that has as much to do with \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , perhaps, getting a jumpstart on him.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Well, they`re all looking for a lane. And he probably didn`t see a lane unless it`s a third-party potential lane which, again, could happen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You, you -- look. You`ve been to some meetings with No Labels.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joe Manchin`s been to some meetings with No Labels. He`s been flirting about this.\par \par \uc2 Is this only if it`s Trump/Biden that this gets traction?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: I think if it`s two choices where the majority of people say, the parties have failed us, there`s a better choice to be president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: And you elect -- and you elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because of voters who draw with the No Labels candidate come from the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: And they`re getting on the ballots. They`re getting on the ballots in the states. And you`ve got to remember, ballots is a state issue, not a federal issue. It`s not a party issue. It`s a state issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So their -- that`s their goal, that they can get ballot access to all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Get on the ballot enough to win the presidency.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`ve tried this. Do you buy it?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: It`s an insurance policy. If the majority of people do not agree with the two parties` selection, and right now the majority of Democrats don`t agree with Biden, and the majority of Republicans don`t agree with Trump. So, it`s going to be interesting --\par \par \uc2 PAGE: But there`s a significant fraction of Republican voters who will stick with Trump no matter what.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Oh, I know that well.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: If he`s in jail, they will stick with Trump.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: And that is a kind of diamond-hard support no other candidate in America would have.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Big money -- by the way, you mentioned -- Club for Growth is money talking. It`s big super PAC money.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Speaking of that, I`ve got to stop the talking because I`ve got to end the show.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, even selection Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230306ej3500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc38}{\*\bkmkend toc38}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for February 26, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kristen Welker, Jonathan Alter \par \uc2 8,948 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 February 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:19]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, defending democracy. One year after Russia invaded Ukraine, President Biden promises to stand strong against the threat from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: Ukraine will never be a victory for Russia -- never.\par \par \uc2 But what does victory look like? And are we giving them enough to win the war, or just survive it?\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): If you want victory, we have to do more.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Plus, rising tensions.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): You are approaching Chinese airspace. Keep a safe distance or you will be intercepted.\par \par \uc2 TODD: As the U.S. continues to warn China about sending weapons to Ukraine, tensions are rising in the South China Sea. Is the Pentagon preparing to move more troops to Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 My guests this morning, the national security adviser to President Biden, Jake Sullivan, and Republican Senator Dan Sullivan of Alaska.\par \par \uc2 And, off the rails.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: I sincerely hope that, when all of the politicians get here, including Biden, they get back from touring Ukraine, that he`s got some money left over.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  travels to the site of the toxic train derailment in Ohio to bash the Biden administration for focusing more overseas, creating the first real split-screen moment of the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker; NPR White House correspondent Tamara Keith; Republican strategist Al Cardenas; and journalist and author Jonathan Alter.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. As the war in Ukraine enters its second year, the question is, what will victory look like?\par \par \uc2 It`s pretty clear no one involved, not the United States or our European allies, Ukraine or Russia, can afford for this war to be in the same place it is today going into year three, a year from now. And yet both sides, President Biden in a surprise visit to Kyiv, and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , this week in Moscow, prepared the world for a drawn-out conflict.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: One year into this war, Putin no longer doubts the strength of our coalition. But he still doubts our conviction. He doubts our staying power. But there should be no doubt. Our support for Ukraine will not waiver. \uc2 NATO\uc2  will not be divided, and we will not tire.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA: We decided to conduct a special military operation. Step by step, we will continue to resolve the objectives that are before us. They started the war, and we used the force in order to stop it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, Biden`s visit to Ukraine was the first time in modern history that a sitting U.S. president has visited an active war zone without a U.S. military presence. And Biden has hitched his legacy to the success of this war. Can he afford for it to drag on into the year 2024 and the inevitable presidential campaign?\par \par \uc2 The American public is already divided on providing ongoing support to Ukraine, split 49/47 on whether Congress should provide more weapons and funding. Isolationism among Republicans is growing, with the leading presidential hopefuls out there right now trying to show some distance from the Ukraine war.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, despite Ukraine`s request and a growing bipartisan chorus in Congress, President Biden made it clear he is ruling out providing F-16s, for now. Meanwhile, Russia has sustained nearly 200,000 casualties, eight times higher than U.S. casualties in the two decades of war we waged in Afghanistan. Moscow`s winter offensive has so far delivered just minor territorial gains.\par \par \uc2 The only potential beneficiary of a protracted conflict may be China, which offered a peace plan, by the way, that Ukraine did not reject out of hand. The U.S. did, and it is still leveling accusations that China is considering providing lethal aid to Russia. U.S. officials tell NBC News that intelligence suggests that aid includes artillery and ammunition. But as U.S. officials sound the alarm, on Friday President Biden downplayed the threat from China.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I don`t anticipate -- we haven`t seen it yet, but I don`t anticipate a major initiative on the part of China providing weaponry to -- to -- to Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is the president`s national security adviser, Jake Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 Jake, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Thanks for having me, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to start right there. Secretary Blinken, last week, and you throughout the week, have been very serious -- seriously concerned over what China could be doing. President Biden seemed to downplay that risk there. Is that -- is that his gut, or do you have new intelligence suggesting the Chinese are backing off?\par \par \uc2 [09:05:05]\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Well, we have the same intelligence that we`ve had, that has been behind the comments Secretary Blinken has made and what you just heard from President Biden, which is we have not seen China yet provide military equipment to Russia for purposes of fighting in the war in Ukraine. We haven`t seen it yet. We`re continuing to watch. We`ll stay vigilant, as President Biden said, but so far we haven`t seen it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you have a sense of what their -- why they would make a decision to do this? What would be their strategic reason for doing it if they did it?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: It`s a great question, Chuck, because I don`t think it is in China`s interest to do this. I think it would alienate them from a number of countries in the world, including our European allies. And it would put them foursquare into the center of responsibility for the kinds of war crimes and bombardments of civilians and atrocities that the Russians are committing in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Their weapons would, in effect, be used for the slaughter of people in Ukraine. So I think it would be ill-advised for China to move forward, but of course, that`s a decision Beijing is going to have to make for itself.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Other than saying there would be consequences for getting involved, you or anybody else has not laid out any specific consequences. Why not? Why not go public with what could be the consequences, whether it`s on sanctions, whether it`s more -- it`s weapons or troops to Taiwan? Why not lay it out in public?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Well, we believe that this is better done directly with Chinese counterparts in private. And in fact, Secretary Blinken had the opportunity to meet with China`s top diplomat at the Munich Security Conference just a few days ago. So we have channels to be able to make sure that China fully understands the U.S. position and what would happen were they to move forward with this step. And we don`t see as much profit in microphone diplomacy on this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that. Do you have the Western allies on board with the -- the immediate response that you want to do with China, or is that going to be a slog?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: We`ve actually had intense and very positive consultations with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, with other members of the coalition supporting Ukraine. And in fact, Chuck, they`re taking steps themselves to communicate to Beijing. Senior officials from Europe are directly talking to senior officials in China, expressing their own strong warnings about China moving forward in this regard.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you believe that the severe consequences the U.S. would level, that our European and \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies would go along with them?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Well, in staying away from microphone diplomacy, I will stay away from hypotheticals. All I will say is tjhat we have had extremely effective, constructive discussions with our European allies about this contingency. I think we`re all on the same page about our concern, our alarm, were this to happen. We all see the same thing right now, which is China has not moved forward. And we will deal with the circumstance should it come to pass.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is the -- the peace plan that China put out -- many United States administration officials have essentially dismissed it out of hand. But President Zelenskyy didn`t. What do you make of his decision, and is that a smart move by him?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Well, what President Zelenskyy said were there were parts of the plan he didn`t like and parts of the plan that he thought could be OK. One part of the plan that I particularly liked was point one of the plan. Point one of the plan was respect the sovereignty of all nations.\par \par \uc2 The plan could just stop there, because Russia could end this war by respecting Ukraine`s sovereignty and pulling out. But what President Zelenskyy also said, and this is critical, Chuck, is that he would like to speak to President Xi.\par \par \uc2 China put forward this plan without having had a single conversation since the war began between President Xi and President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Um-hmm.\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: The Chinese have talked to the Russians a lot, but at the most senior levels, they have not talked to the Ukrainians. And it`s very difficult to advance any kind of peace initiative when there is that kind of one-sided diplomacy going on.\par \par \uc2 So from our perspective, the critical thing is that any peace plan offered by anybody has to involve the input of Ukraine. As President Biden has constantly said, "Nothing about Ukraine without Ukraine," and that goes for this initiative as well as any other initiative under the sun.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about some of the -- some of the -- I`ll call it, I guess, criticism, if you will, from some Democrats who believe that, A, there is more aid that needs to be sent to Ukraine and it needs to happen faster, including the F-16s.\par \par \uc2 I want to play an array of folks, Jason Crow, Mike McFaul, former ambassador to Russia, and Jared Golden. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON CROW, (D) COLORADO: They need more aircraft. They need more advanced aircraft. That`s why we`ve been pressing the administration, on a bipartisan basis, to help them advance their air force, to give them what they need to fight and when.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:05]\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, FORMER AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: I think, if you want victory, we have to do more. We`re not giving them the weapons they need for this counter-offensive that they`re planning in the spring. And I think time`s going to run out.\par \par \uc2 REP. JARED GOLDEN, (D) MAINE: Some people have raised concerns about training. Well, last September many of us called on the administration to start training in F-16 or fourth-generation aircraft. And of course that hasn`t -- that hasn`t even begun. So the time to start this process is right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Jake, it`s that last comment I want you to respond to directly. I understand you say the F-16s are not something they need right now. But to Congressman Golden`s point, why not start training Ukrainians on this now so that, when we inevitably do this, or likely do it, they`re ready to use them?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Well, first, Chuck, it`s important for people to understand that Ukrainian pilots are currently flying, day in and day out. They are fighting their Soviet-era fighters, MiG fighters, Sukhois, and the coalition is providing spare parts for those planes to ensure that they can stay in the sky.\par \par \uc2 So, first of all, we are providing a substantial amount of support to the Ukrainian air force for the limited kinds of missions that the current war calls for them to undertake.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, the central focus of all of our efforts right now is to help them take back territory that Russia currently occupies. And the assessment of our military commanders, those giving advice to President Biden, are that what they need right now are tanks and armored personnel carriers and infantry fighting vehicles, artillery and air defense systems up there on the front line. That`s what we`re giving them. And we`re giving it to them fast; we`re giving it to them in large quantities.\par \par \uc2 And the question of F-16s is really a question for another day, for another phase. This phase is about ground combat and being able to have the tools in the hands of the Ukrainians to take the territory back that the Russians are occupying.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just curious, why not take that -- the F-16 talking point off the table, going, "You`re right, we`re going to provide them with that help down the road?"\par \par \uc2 Because you`ve said, "We`re committed to Ukraine`s security in perpetuity."\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: You know, it`s really, at the end of the day, not our job to focus on the talking points question, you know, what the right thing to say on a Sunday show or in some other public forum. It`s our job to figure out, morning, noon and night, what does the Ukrainian army need on those front lines and how do we get to -- get it to them as rapidly as possible?\par \par \uc2 And we have moved with unprecedented speed and at unprecedented scale to get them a massive amount of military assistance so that they have been able to defend effectively and to take back half of the territory so far that Russia previously occupied during this war.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Simple question, and I know it`s got a complicated answer. What does victory look like for Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: That`s up to Ukraine to define. it has been critical to us, and I said it just earlier in this interview, that there be nothing without -- about Ukraine without Ukraine. And so it`s not for the United States to define victory for Ukraine. It`s for the United States to support Ukraine on the battlefield so that they can achieve the victory that they define. That`s what we are determined to do. We believe they can achieve that. And we are going to give them the implements, the tools that they need to be able to achieve that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, if that`s the case, Ukrainian President Zelenskyy did define victory, what he thought victory looked like, in August of last year. He said, "Crimea is Ukrainian, and we will never give it up. This Russian war against Ukraine and against the entire free Europe began with Crimea and must end with Crimea, with its liberation."\par \par \uc2 There`s always been a hesitance among U.S. officials, Jake, and I know you`re included here, about Crimea, specifically, and it`s always been, "Well, it`s up to Ukraine."\par \par \uc2 Well, President Zelenskyy said it. Victory is "all of the territory back." Why don`t we say the same thing now?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Well, we have repeatedly talked about Ukraine`s sovereignty and territorial integrity within its internationally recognized borders. The question for us is how do we put Ukraine in the best position on the battlefield so that they are ultimately in the best position at the negotiating table?\par \par \uc2 And President Zelenskyy, as recently as this week, has said we are going to have to ultimately get to a diplomatic phase of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 So, from our perspective, our goal is to strengthen the hand of the Ukrainians on the battlefield so that they are in the strongest position, with the most leverage, when they get to the negotiating table, to ultimately achieve an outcome that restores Ukraine`s full sovereignty and territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If they want to take Crimea militarily, will the United States help Ukraine do that?\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Chuck, the critical thing right now is that they need to take back the territory in the south and the east that they are currently focused on. And we need to give them the tools to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 The question of Crimea, and the question of what happens down the road, is something that we will come to. Where we are right now is that we need to be focused on the immediate term, because it is critical that we move fast and we move decisively to help them take back the territory across that line of contact that Russian troops are currently occupying.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It sounds like you think the ideal outcome is Ukraine is able to take more territory back; Putin fears that he could lose Crimea and he comes to the negotiating table to see if he can keep Crimea. Is that the best-case scenario you`re envisioning?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Again, I understand why you`re asking the question, but you have to understand that, from the United States` perspective, we are not going to negotiate over the destiny and sovereignty of another country. That is for their democratically elected president to decide.\par \par \uc2 What we are going to do is give Ukraine the tools that it needs, as I said before, to be in the strongest possible position, to ultimately be able to convert battlefield gains into diplomatic leverage. That is the goal here. And that requires us to achieve those battlefield gains through the provision of military assistance to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In the open of our show, people are seeing footage of a Chinese fighter jet warning an American fighter jet over the South China Sea earlier this week. We happened to have a reporter on board of that.\par \par \uc2 It only underscores just all the tension we have with China just in the last couple of weeks. We`ve had a seven-hour meeting with Taiwanese officials. We`re sending more troops to Taiwan. China`s ignoring calls from our defense secretary. Secretary Blinken had to cancel his trip. The president wants to have a call with Xi. There`s no evidence that anything`s been scheduled.\par \par \uc2 This lack of communication, how dangerous is this situation with China right now?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Well, we have said repeatedly that we need to have military communications channels to avoid escalation, to avoid surprise, to avoid mistake. And it is unfortunate that the Chinese defense ministry has declined to take calls from the U.S. secretary of defense. That`s on China. Because, from the U.S. perspective, for the rest of the world, we are acting responsibly. We are prepared to have those -- those lines of crisis communication. We had them during the Cold War, at the height of the Cold War, when the U.S. and the Soviet Union were squaring off,\par \par \uc2 But, Chuck, we do have the -- the ability to speak to China at high levels. And as I mentioned, Secretary Blinken spoke with his counterpart, the top diplomat of China, Wang Yi, at Munich just a few days ago. So it is not that all lines of communication are cut -- are shut off, but rather that we do not have the military-to-military exchanges that we think are necessary to ensure stability.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we have anything scheduled with the president and Xi on a phone call in the near term?\par \par \uc2 J. SULLIVAN: Nothing scheduled at the moment, though I anticipate the two leaders will speak at some point in the not-too-distant future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Jake Sullivan, the -- President Biden`s national security adviser. As always, sir, thank you for coming on and sharing the administration`s perspective.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, there is a growing Republican divide over aiding Ukraine as the war passes the one-year mark. Will those skeptics, including some 2024 presidential candidates, impact future funding for the war?\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator Dan Sullivan of Alaska, the only member of the Senate currently serving in the military, joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:20:41]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. No issue illustrates the shift in the Republican Party better than the war in Ukraine right now, where traditional hawkishness on Russia has been replaced by a deep skepticism of U.S. involvement overseas, particularly when the policy is led by a Democratic president named \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  poll this month, 50 percent of Republicans said the U.S. was doing "too much" to support Ukraine. And that`s up from just 18 percent who said the same thing in April last year.\par \par \uc2 This GOP split was on display this week as a handful of congressional Republicans who are supportive of the Biden policy in Ukraine met with Ukrainian President Zelenskyy in Kyiv, while back here at home most of the declared and likely presidential hopefuls were striking a more skeptical tone about Ukraine, with really one notable exception.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: World War III has never been closer than it is right now. We need to clean house of all of the warmongers and "America last" globalists and the deep state.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Well, they have effectively a blank check policy with no clear, strategic objective identified. And I don`t think it`s in our interest to be getting into a proxy war with China, getting involved over things like the border lands or over Crimea.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.N. AMBASSADOR & GOV. NIKKI HALEY, (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: I don`t think we need to write them blank checks. But they have the passion to fight for their own freedom. Give them the ammunition to do it.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: There can be no room in the leadership of the Republican Party for apologists for Putin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Needless to say, there`s going to be a robust debate on the presidential debate stage there.\par \par \uc2 But joining me now is Republican Senator Dan Sullivan of Alaska. He`s a member of the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2 . He`s also a colonel in the Marine Corps Reserve, the only member right now of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  that does currently serve in the military.\par \par \uc2 Senator Sullivan, welcome back to "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 SEN. DAN SULLIVAN, (R) ALASKA: Morning, Chuck. Good to be on the show.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me just start with the basics. You heard from the national security adviser there. What does -- what do you think victory looks like for Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 D. SULLIVAN: Well, you know, I think, just to begin with, looking at the past year, we need to recognize how we got here, what mistakes were made and what we can do going forward.\par \par \uc2 I think one element that the national security adviser doesn`t talk about, I think it was clearly some of the Biden administration`s weakness on the issues like energy, national defense and clearly the botched withdrawal from Afghanistan that emboldened Putin to undertake the brutal invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I think, though, that now that we are in this battle, it`s strongly in our interest to continue to support the Ukrainians to restore their territorial integrity and their sovereignty without committing U.S. forces.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, Chuck, your interview actually highlighted one of the problems. Jake Sullivan`s talking about, "Well, we`re not going to do F- 16s today; that`s for another time, not right now." That has been a pattern with this administration from the beginning, where they have slow- rolled critical military weapon system. You know, it`s a long list. It`s Patriots; it`s HIMARS; it`s tanks; and now it`s F-16s.\par \par \uc2 And to me, that is a real blunder. We need to get them what they need now and listen to the Ukrainians...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 D. SULLIVAN: ... not, as he said, the policymakers. They`ve proven their ability to fight bravely, and I think we need to do a much better job. It took nine months to get them the Patriots, and I fear the same thing is happening right now with the F-16s. You just saw it from the national security adviser.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you want the administration to speak with more clarity on the -- restoring the entire territorial integrity of Ukraine, pre-2014, which, of course, means Crimea?\par \par \uc2 D. SULLIVAN: Well, I think you saw the unease that Jake Sullivan demonstrated when you were pressing him on that. I do agree that this is going to be up to the Ukrainians and Zelenskyy. We need to give them, like I said, the weapon systems to undertake that. But I think they should be more clear. I think it should be all the territorial integrity of the entire country, which includes Crimea.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, I think they need more clarity on that. And the national security adviser didn`t demonstrate it in his interview with you today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about more support for Ukraine going forward.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:04]\par \par \uc2 In the introduction I highlighted there is a growing divide in your party. Do you think you`re in the minority now, among the Russia hawks in your party these days?\par \par \uc2 D. SULLIVAN: No, I don`t think so. I mean, you look at the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , the members who are on committees like Intel, Armed Services like myself, Foreign Affairs, I think there`s still a strong contingent of Republicans who support -- supporting the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 Now, I do think that there are legitimate criticisms of this administration`s conduct of supporting the Ukrainians. Let me give you one that in particular being home in Alaska really matters.\par \par \uc2 You know, from the beginning of this administration, this administration`s been focused on shutting down the production of American energy. Well, as you know, Chuck, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and Putin fear American energy dominance. And so to have an administration that`s shutting down energy, making it harder to move energy, pushing back on the ability to finance energy for America, this actually directly relates to our efforts to push back against \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , Putin.\par \par \uc2 You know, in many ways their energy policies have been national security suicide. And I think this is what frustrates Republicans, where we see that other policies of this administration aren`t at all helping pushing back on what I refer to as this new era of authoritarian aggression led by Putin and xi Jinping. And it`s a frustration you`re seeing among Republicans, but it`s also a frustration among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But I`m curious of your take on the -- the growing -- look, this isolationist wing of the GOP used to basically be Ron and \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 . It`s bigger than that now. How much of it do you think is -- is, sort of, genuine, sort of, leeriness of interventions because of Iraq and Afghanistan on the right, and how much of it, do you think, it`s simply because Biden`s a Democrat?\par \par \uc2 D. SULLIVAN: Look, I think always -- you`ve always had, on both sides, kind of, the wings of each party, an isolationist tendency. And I think that this war in particular -- you`re right, the polling is starting to show a lack of support, and I think that there are ways that you can address this.\par \par \uc2 A lot of Republicans say, "Hey, we should be doing more on our own border, not the Ukraine-Russia border." My response to that is, "Hey, great powers can do both things." But it is important to address our own border, to secure the border. That would take away one of the arguments.\par \par \uc2 Something else, and I hear from Alaskans all the time on this, we need to do a better job, the administration needs to do a better job of getting our European allies to pull their weight. As you know, Chuck, they`ve had a commitment to spend at a minimum -- and it should be a floor and not a ceiling -- 2 percent of their GDP on defense. The vast majority of the \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies that we have don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 And I think that frustrates Americans, where we see this huge conflict in the heart of Europe, and the Europeans aren`t stepping up enough the way in which they need to. And I think pressing them to do more and having them do more can help push back against some of the doubts that are starting to spread across the country on Ukrainian support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you think the best way is to deter China from helping the Russians right now? And if they do, what should the consequences be?\par \par \uc2 D. SULLIVAN: Well, look, they`re already helping the Russians. I mean, every time you hear the Chinese officials talk about this, they`re blaming us; they`re blaming \uc2 NATO\uc2  for the Ukrainian war. I agree there should be a serious red line with regard to them supplying military equipment to the Russians. And I think that should be in the form of sanctions.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, there`s a broader issue here, Chuck, and I think this is another one where Republicans are actually more united. And it`s -- the war in Ukraine really revealed that we are in this new era of authoritarian aggression, led by \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and Putin. As you know, they`re working together. They are increasingly isolated. They view their Democratic neighbors in a very paranoid way. And I think that this challenge, where they are looking to take aggressive actions against their neighbors, whether it`s in Ukraine, whether it`s Taiwan, I think it`s going to be with us for decades. We need to face it with strategic resolve and confidence.\par \par \uc2 There`s a number of things that we have with regard to our strategic advantages over these dictators that we should promote, if we`re wise enough to use them, our military, our allies, our natural resources and energy, and, of course, our commitment to democracy and liberty.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that -- in many ways, that was what really helped us win the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:03]\par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  and Putin, their biggest vulnerability is they fear their own people. We need to exploit that as we move forward. But I think that can unify Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to follow up on one more thing on China. There`s a report today that another intelligence arm of the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 , this is inside our energy department, has joined the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in concluding that COVID began with a lab leak in China.\par \par \uc2 If we end up determining, if our intelligence community over time determines this is the majority view, and it`s the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 `s view that this was a lab leak in China and that government covered it up, what should be the consequences?\par \par \uc2 D. SULLIVAN: Well, I think we need to have public hearings on this and really dig into it. Think about what just happened over the last three years: one of the biggest pandemics in a century. A lot of evidence that it`s coming from the Chinese, and when other countries even raise it, like Australia, the Chinese use their coercive economic activities to shut people up.\par \par \uc2 So I think we need to do extensive hearings. I hope our Democratic colleagues in the Congress can support that. I know the Republicans in the House are certainly supportive of that.\par \par \uc2 But I think if that happens, we need to make sure every country in the world knows this. Look, this is a country that has no problem coming out and lying to the world. We just saw that with this Chinese spy balloon. It`s the nature of a Communist dictatorship to lie to their own people, to lie to the world.\par \par \uc2 But I think that we need to make sure every country knows that and then look at what the consequences could be. Obviously millions of deaths, huge economic impacts, and it would once again show that the Chinese Communist Party is not only a menace, but the nature of these regimes is to lie to the world. And we need to make that clear to people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think you`re definitely right on those public hearings. At a minimum, we need to know what we know, and the world needs to know what we know.\par \par \uc2 Senator Dan Sullivan, Republican from the great state of Alaska, thanks for getting up very early this morning out there and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 Before we go to break, I want to show you our "Meet the Press Minute". It takes a look at the legacy of President \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , who is receiving hospice care at his home in Plains, Georgia. Over the course of 40 years, Carter appeared on this program 11 times. In December 1974, he joined us the week he announced his bid for the White House to talk about why he wanted the nation`s top job.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP, DECEMBER 15, 1974)\par \par \uc2 THEN-GOV. \uc2 JIMMY CARTER\uc2  (D), GEORGIA: I think we have a nation that is truly great. Not that it used to be great, or certainly will be great again, but one that has an innate character about it that`s not adequately recognized. A stability, a pride in its past, an economic strength that is presently not recognized adequately by the people in this country and around the world. Also, I think there`s a lack of purpose in our country`s government now, which is much more vulnerable than the people deserve. It`s hard to detect what are our goals, what common purpose we work toward, what sort of sacrifices might be expected from the American people.\par \par \uc2 And if I can exemplify the correction of some of the defects that have been brought in our government by politicians and not by the people, and help to restore the greatness of this country, then I`d like to do it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  turned the site of the Ohio train derailment into a campaign stop to highlight Biden`s absence while he was overseas in Ukraine. Our panel is here, as we look into what may be the first split-screen moment of the 2024 campaign.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:31]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, the panel is here.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Chief White House Correspondent Kristen Welker, co-anchor of "Weekend Today"; Jonathan Alter, journalist and author of his very best life -- "His Very Best: \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , A Life". My apologies there.\par \par \uc2 NPR White House Correspondent Tamara Keith, welcome.\par \par \uc2 TAMARA KEITH, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, NPR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Republican strategist Al Cardenas.\par \par \uc2 All right, we really had our first split-screen moment. And I`m curious, Kristen, I`m going to play it here. Essentially, it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , what I thought was a risky move: holding a campaign event in East Palestine. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We will stand with you. Let us move forward with faith and conviction with a Biden commitment to be allies.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: I sincerely hope that when your representatives and all of the politicians get here, including Biden, they get back from touring Ukraine, that he`s got some money left over.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president in an interview on Friday was pretty defensive of the response, basically saying, hey, we were there from the beginning. \uc2 EPA\uc2  was there from the beginning, and all of those things.\par \par \uc2 Behind the scenes, any regret at the optic problems here for them?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: They`re pretty defiant, Chuck. But I have to tell you privately, some allies of the president are concerned that he hasn`t been there yet. And they`re saying he needs to go. But I`ve pressed them over and over again, are there any plans for the president to go? And they say there are no conversations about that.\par \par \uc2 They reiterate what you just said. We were on the ground within two hours of this crisis happening. We`ve gotten all the resources there that are needed.\par \par \uc2 And I do think big picture there`s a question. Optics are important when you`re dealing with a crisis like this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: There`s no doubt about that. We learned that during Hurricane Katrina, for example.\par \par \uc2 The question is will this backfire on President Biden if he doesn`t go? But could it backfire on former President Trump? Because his visit has put in the spotlight the fact that he rolled back 100 environmental regulations. And so, that`s because a part of this conversation as well.\par \par \uc2 So I think that there are risks involved for both of them. And the question becomes will we see President Biden on the ground in the coming weeks? At this point in time, still no plans for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tamara, I sense that almost they don`t want to look like they`ve been pushed into anything by Fox News and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Is that -- are they being stubborn about this?\par \par \uc2 KEITH: And this White House doesn`t like to feel like they`re being pushed into things. They have resisted other calls for him, "He`s got to be there." Though this weekend they`ve told us that the CDC, the \uc2 EPA\uc2 , and FEMA are going door to door, checking on people, you know; the government is here to help kind of a thing.\par \par \uc2 So pushing back on the idea that these people are forgotten, I don`t know if everyone is going to want a knock on their door from three federal agencies, but that`s what they`re going to get.\par \par \uc2 AL CARDENAS, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: You know, President Biden, though, has a habit of showing up late to just about everything we think is significant.\par \par \uc2 In Florida during the campaign, the Florida Democrats were saying, "Hey, these guys are here every week. You haven`t shown up. They`re calling us socialists. You`ve got to come defend us."\par \par \uc2 And he finally showed up like three weeks before the election after one year of being prompted. On the border. He just showed up at the border. Why shouldn`t he have been there long before?\par \par \uc2 Now on this incident, he had a good reason not to be there, but he has his reputation preceding him about being in places too late.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN ALTER, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I just don`t think the split screen hurts Biden at all. He`s on a very successful trip and his messages were the arsenal of democracy, the FDR message.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:03]\par \par \uc2 He`s backing the modern Winston Churchill, Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 What is Trump doing? He`s saying to the people of Palestine, have a good time. He`s handing out MAGA caps. That`s not a very good impression for him.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And I was in Poland with President Biden, and just to add to that point, he was making this broader case. This war is not just about the war in Ukraine. It`s about democracy. It`s about upholding American ideals.\par \par \uc2 And his surprise trip to Ukraine, if you talk to Republicans and Democrats, they say it may be one of the strongest counterpoints yet to this narrative that he`s too old for a second term.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I think that was a big moment for him on his -- on his vibrancy.\par \par \uc2 KEITH: Absolutely. It was not an easy trip. No president has done what he did, which was spend 25 hours in a country at war, a country where the U.S. doesn`t have a base of operations.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that is -- a ten-hour train ride each direction is a remarkable feat for this White House to have pulled off and they are quite happy that they pulled that off.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But I want to talk about the fact that I was thinking about we`ve had three one-term presidents in our lifetimes, essentially. And two of them lost arguably due to foreign policy issues.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, you profiled one of them in Carter. And that was the Iran hostage crisis. George H.W. Bush, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  successfully sort of used a message that Trump was using, which is, hey, he`s so concerned overseas. It`s the economy stupid here.\par \par \uc2 This war can`t drag on into `24, can it?\par \par \uc2 ALTER: Well, I think it very well might.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALTER: The question is whether it will swamp \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  the way events overseas ended up swamping \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 . In his first half of his term, Carter was very successful. But these events kind of really burdened him. The difference is inflation. So basically --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Speaking of the thing that could hurt both of them, right?\par \par \uc2 ALTER: So events overseas with the Iranian revolution in 1979, that kicked off a whole new round of inflation. So Carter was running for reelection with double-digit inflation and double-digit interest rates. And these overwhelmed many of his quite big achievements, which few people remember now.\par \par \uc2 So if Biden can manage to keep inflation in check in the next couple of years, even if there`s a stalemate in Ukraine, I think he`ll be okay.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Al, go ahead, but I want you to address the growing divide in your party. I mean, you`re a hawk against authoritarianism, as somebody that`s - - good South Florida Republican. What`s going on with DeSantis here?\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Yeah, that`s a great question. I mean, here`s someone who`s served in the military after going to Harvard and Yale. I mean, he had the world in front of him with those records, and yet he went into the military.\par \par \uc2 You would think, like Dan Sullivan, that he`d be on Dan Sullivan`s track, not the track of the dissenter. So I -- that`s one I can`t figure it out. Maybe he`s concerned about the base that follows \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And maybe that`s a reason. But that one I can`t figure out.\par \par \uc2 I did want to say that of all of the comments we`ve made about presidents losing elections, the one that didn`t participate in a election but whose circumstances are more similar to Biden was Lyndon Johnson. He`s in the middle of a war with you know Russian-backed people. And his numbers kept falling. And then he said, you know what, I`m stepping down.\par \par \uc2 ALTER: But he had 500,000 troops there.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: My point is I think--\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a big difference.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: -- Biden may be facing the Lyndon Johnson predicament --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: -- before you consider his running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I`m going to have to leave that there. But that`s a fascinating conversation, and the point is if this gets into `24, we don`t know how this is going to play with the American people.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Ohio train disaster was 100 percent preventable, according to federal investigators. Trains are still, though, some of the safest options for transporting hazardous chemicals. I`m going to show you why after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:46:54]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back, Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 That East Palestine train derailment and the chemical spill it caused raised some renewed questions about the safety of rail travel and using it to transport toxic chemicals.\par \par \uc2 Just how safe is shipping toxic cargo by train? The answer is somewhat complicated.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you. Train derailments overall have actually been going down over the last 10 years. We had over 1,300 derailments in 2013, just over 1,000 in 2022, about three a day. Good news is not all these train derailments are trains carrying hazardous materials.\par \par \uc2 Now, though, let`s compare hazmat spills on railway incidents. So we had 667 hazmat spills in 2013 on the tracks -- railroad tracks. It`s down to 355 in 2022, so you can see it`s actually been getting safer by rail. Now, contrast that by hazmat spills on the highways.\par \par \uc2 Look at this: In 2013 we had nearly 14,000. It`s actually been going up. We had over 23,000 hazmat spills by highway, just 355 by train. So clearly it`s safer to do this and better to do this by train, right?\par \par \uc2 Well, you start to look at it financially, it`s a different story. Let`s take 2022 as an example. There were over 23,000 incidents that happened on highways. The cost of those cleanups: $21 million. The 355 incidents by rail? The cost of that was more than double that: $45 million.\par \par \uc2 Now, why is that? It`s mainly due to one large rail spill in May. It was a train carrying petroleum products that derailed in Harmar Township, Pennsylvania. Spilled over 3,000 gallons into a creek, cost over $30 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 By the way, the rail operator of that train? None other than \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 , the same one behind this month`s incident in East Palestine. When you have these derailments, they can be gigantic accidents.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, more Americans believe abortion should be legal in all or most cases. It`s becoming a major political problem for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:38]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party has an abortion problem. That is the unmistakable conclusion of this new national survey from the Public Religion Research Institute. Of 20,000 Americans, it is easily the most comprehensive survey on abortion attitudes we have seen since the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 This was able to do both national polling and 50 separate state polls. Two- thirds of Americans, 64 percent, say abortion should be legal in all or most cases. This is an increase from 2010, from 55 percent. And Republicans right now look like they`re more out of step, just 36 percent favor legal abortion compared with 86 percent of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And look at this number: 68 percent of Independents. The Republican number has not moved since 2010. But support among Democrats and Independents has actually grown by double digits. State by state, you can see here solid majorities favor legal abortion. In the six historically closest presidential states, the battleground states, all of them but one over 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 And in the three most vulnerable Democratic-held Senate seats, look at those states: Montana, Ohio, and West Virginia, also with very high percentages on legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 Al Cardenas, what does the GOP do here? They seem to be in a box on abortion.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Well, look, everybody was thankful for Roe v. Wade. The Republicans and Democrats didn`t want to touch it for 60 years for obvious reasons. Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  decided he wanted to do evangelicals a favor and name three Supreme Court justices who were committed to looking at this issue.\par \par \uc2 And now, we`ve got the political explosion of having made that Dobbs decision. And in my opinion, that`s not the only one they`re going to make. The \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  has become the Democrats` best ally --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: -- when you come to what`s going on, in spite of the fact that Republicans were rejoicing that they --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: -- had a 6-3 majority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tamara, they don`t -- that`s the thing. They don`t know what their position is now as a party.\par \par \uc2 KEITH: That`s correct. Well, because they don`t actually agree. There are some people like former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , very likely presidential candidate, who says, we need to stand strong, stand firm on abortion. We need to take a strong position here.\par \par \uc2 Then you have someone like Trump saying, "You know, Republicans could be in real trouble here with this issue. So it`s one of the many things that probably won`t be settled by 2024.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:01]\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the identity of the Republican Party, it is a challenge, because when you start talking about specifics -- when something has been taken away and then you start talking about specifics, people actually care about this issue. And that`s what showed up in this poll, is just a spike in support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the White House has been, look, I saw Vice President Harris did an event earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, look, I`m going to look back on 2022 and start to think that we`re overrating the impact of the Trump candidates and underrating the impact of the abortion decision.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s right. I mean, we saw the power of Dobbs in the midterm elections. And I`m told the vice president is going to be out front on this issue. You can expect that over the next two years.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the strategy within the Republican Party moving forward, when you talk to Republicans, one person said something so interesting to me, which is that Roe was overturned and then the focus became on banning abortion in the states. Instead of talking about maternal health, talking about adoption, talking about what happens to these families.\par \par \uc2 Another person said, we`re going to focus on the economy. Well, that`s a risky strategy. What is the economy going to look like in the next year and a half? And it`ll be a test. Is the economy as motivating as the abortion issue?\par \par \uc2 ALTER: And the abortion pill is going to be a huge issue. There`s a decision coming out in Texas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh my goodness, if that goes away, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ALTER: So right now, 55 percent of all abortions are medical abortions with the abortion pill, up from 45 percent just a couple of years ago. And if this decision comes down the way people are expecting, and then it`s upheld at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , as people expect, a lot of women who want to use the abortion pill are not going to be able to. And they`re going to be awfully upset about that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m going to bring it back. You`re my Floridian pal here, Al. I sense Governor DeSantis is uncomfortable on what to say about abortion right now, because he has the 15 week, but I notice he doesn`t want this session of the legislature to touch abortion, does he?\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: No, he doesn`t. He`s shied away from this issue. He`s acknowledged from the outset it`s not a winning issue. He hasn`t mentioned the issue.\par \par \uc2 And he`s acting like, hey, I`m Governor from Florida. I don`t need to get involved with this. Let this be a national issue.\par \par \uc2 So far, it`s working for him. Nobody`s really taking him on, primarily because the Democrat Party has imploded in Florida. But if I was them, I would certainly try to put him in a corner, but they haven`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But with the \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2  news this week, and you see those abortion numbers, I finally -- look, I was pretty pessimistic about this Senate map for Democrats. But suddenly you see those abortion numbers and you`re like: I see the path for Tester. I see the path for Brown.\par \par \uc2 KEITH: They can talk about this issue and make it about people`s rights. And not just about --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Make the libertarian argument.\par \par \uc2 KEITH: -- a healthcare procedure. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But, Chuck, I think it`s interesting. Tam, to your first point about the divisions within the Republican Party, one of the biggest ones is about this 15-week national abortion ban. A lot of Republicans are saying Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  should`ve never introduced this, because the whole point of Dobbs was to send this back to the states.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Except he was actually looking for a compromise number here. I don`t know if Democrats will ever get to even 20, let alone 50.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, and now every Republican candidate is going to have to weigh in on that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Before we go, this week on the Chuck Toddcast, I spoke with former ambassador to Russia, Mike McFaul, Washington Post`s Dan Balz. And after the broadcast today we`re going to have a post-game discussion with Jonathan Alter, talk a little bit about \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So subscribe to the Chuck Toddcast by scanning the QR code on your screen right now. Or just go to NBCNews.com/ChuckToddcast.\par \par \uc2 That`s all for today. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230226ej2q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc39}{\*\bkmkend toc39}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for February 25, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jose Diaz-Balart, Dana Griffin, Erin McLaughlin, Jesse Kirsch, Ali  Vitali, Emilie Ikeda, Morgan Chesky, Marissa Parra \par \uc2 3,865 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 February 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:30:18]\par \par \uc2 JOSE DIAZ-BALART, NBC NEWS HOST: Tonight, a historic storms slams the state of California. Millions still under life threatening flash flood alerts after a rare system dumped heavy snow, torrential rain, and caused damaging wind gusts. The daring rescues and dangerous conditions as the storm moves east.\par \par \uc2 Overseas, Ukraine heads into its second year of war. The images of solidarity emerging from across the globe. What the conflict could look like six months from now?\par \par \uc2 Plus today, the CDC is out in East Palestine three weeks after that toxic train derailment as residents come forward with new health concerns\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELISSA BLAKE (ph): They poisoned me. They shouldn`t get away with that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And any day now, a Texas judge could make one of the biggest rulings on abortion access since Roe v. Wade was overturned. Could a popular and \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved abortion pill be pulled off the shelves nationwide?\par \par \uc2 Plus, what we now know about a top college basketball star being linked to a fatal shooting. And the dancing grannies hit the streets during Mardi Gras. Why it was so much more than just a celebration.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Jose Diaz-Balart.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Good evening. We begin tonight with a rare storm that hasn`t been seen in California in decades. As we come on the air, millions are under life threatening flash flood alerts after an historic system pummeled the state. Just listen to this, a blizzard warning was issued for the first time ever in San Bernardino County, where they saw more than five feet of snow. Other parts of the state saw hurricane force winds and torrential rains.\par \par \uc2 Rescue workers were raced against the clock to save stranded drivers. And now that storm system is pushing east. Our Dana Griffin is in Los Angeles with the very latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DANA GRIFFIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Life threatening flooding in the Golden State. In Southern California`s Ventura County, multiple RVs swept into the river. Watch this dramatic rescue, one of several in the region. A driver stuck in raging floodwater flings to a rescuer. They`re both hoisted swinging in the wind before safely landing on solid ground.\par \par \uc2 This morning, near Los Angeles, a dangerous light show. A live wire fell into the water, setting a car on fire. The historic winter weather walloped California with heavy snow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now we`re having of trouble because the snow is making it slippery.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Torrential rain in some areas, nearly an entire month`s worth in 48 hours. And strong wind gusts creating blizzardlike conditions in the San Bernardino County mountains.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: First time seeing snow in a long time.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: With San Francisco seeing its lowest temperatures in over a century. And Los Angeles experiencing its first blizzard warning in more than 30 years. In San Diego, several water rescues today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We just had a vehicle that was in the deep water, unable to drive out, and two occupants of the vehicle that needed to be rescued and brought back to dry land. So, we anticipate doing this a few more times.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The stunning system now headed east toward Oklahoma City, Wichita and Kansas City, where 9 million are at risk for large hail and even tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, more than 400,000 remain without power from an ice storm earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Back in Southern California, tonight, the mess the storm left behind and the dangers this system could still bring to the area.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Dana Griffin joins us from hard hit Los Angeles. Dana, what threat remains even as the storm now moves out?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: While, Jose, those lingering impacts could include mudslides and additional flooding. Just take a look at the Los Angeles River, it is moving so swiftly at such a dangerous pace, and the water level is usually not this high. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Dana Griffin in Los Angeles. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now to the war in Ukraine. The conflict entering its second year with no end in sight. And around the world today, signs of solidarity in support of the war-torn country. Erin McLaughlin reports tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): From New York to Taipei, Toronto to Tbilisi, a sea of yellow and blue.\par \par \uc2 Outside the Russian embassy in Warsaw, protesters blasting the sound of air raid sirens. Ukraine is waking up under the sounds of real Russian bombs and rockets, she says.\par \par \uc2 [18:35:10]\par \par \uc2 A global show of support for Ukraine as the Russian assault enters its second year. The United States now pledging $2 billion more to help the Ukrainians fight. But President Biden Friday ruling out sending Ukrainian President Zelenskyy F-16 fighter jets for now.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: We`re sending him what our seasoned military thinks he needs now. He needs tanks, he needs artillery.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: All things Ukrainian officials agree they need to win this war and quickly.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Where do you see this war in six months?\par \par \uc2 MYKHAILO PODOLYAK, ADVISER TO PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (through translator): If we get the necessary types and quantities of weapons, primarily shells and missiles, I think in six months we will be approaching the finalization of this war.\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: An optimistic outlook as U.S. officials allege China is considering sending lethal aid to Moscow. China denies the claims and on Friday put forward a peace proposal between Russia and Ukraine, a proposal U.S. officials have dismissed. President Zelenskyy, however, says he`d like to meet with the Chinese leader.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the war grinds on in the east and more lives are lost. Among the dead, a \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  veteran, according to his family, 28-year-old Andrew Peters was killed in action last week fighting for the International Legion. Andrew had a strong sense of right and wrong. The family said in a statement, we are all extremely proud of his bravery and selfless sacrifice.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: And Erin joins me from Kyiv. Erin, more than a year into the war, it often seems like a stalemate, what could make a difference going forward?\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: Jose, an advisor to the Ukrainian military tells me that at this point, both sides are running out of ammunition and that the key to victory for Ukraine could be if it receives enough supplies from its western allies. Jose?\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Erin McLaughlin in Kyiv, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back home, we are just learning about another possible \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train derailment, this time in North Carolina. It comes as fears grow among residents in East Palestine, Ohio, about their health. Here`s Jesse Kirsch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JESSE KIRSCH, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): More than three weeks after that toxic train derailment, the CDC in East Palestine today encouraging residents to participate in a survey.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think it`s important for residents to understand that in order for us to get a better idea of what their potential exposures may have been, what might be causing any potential health effects.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: The latest outreach comes as President Biden defended his administration`s response late Friday, saying for now, he has no plans to visit.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I`m keeping very close tabs on it. We`re doing all we can.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The government has got to be responsible and make sure that everything is done.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Already, more than a dozen lawsuits have been filed against rail company \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ERIN BROCKOVICH, FAMED ENVIRONMENTAL ACTIVIST: And everything happens so quickly.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Famed environmental Activist Erin Brockovich is among those organizing legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 BROCKOVICH: You will ask questions, you will demand answers.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: She spoke to hundreds of residents Friday.\par \par \uc2 BROCKOVICH: They are confused. They don`t know who to believe or who to trust. And I have learned in every community I`ve been in they need to trust themselves.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: Melissa Blake (ph) lives less than a mile from the derailment. Two days later, she says she evacuated and went to the emergency room.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): They quarantined you at the ER.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA BLAKE (ph): Yes. They didn`t know what was on me about the fumes and all that, the chemicals. They told me that it was acute bronchitis due to chemical fumes.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: She says she`s now using an inhaler to help breathe and says she talked to a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE (ph): They poisoned me. They shouldn`t get away with that.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCH: \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  says it can`t comment on litigation and promises to do right by the community.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Cleanup continues, and the \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says it`s still investigating. Meanwhile, here in East Palestine, the trains keep moving through town. Jose.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Jesse Kirsch in East Palestine, Ohio. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, abortion rights activists are waiting on what could be the most consequential ruling on abortion access since Roe v. Wade was overturned. A judge in Texas could decide to pull a popular abortion pill off the market nationwide. Ali Vitali has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): A new fault line in the battle over abortion access. This one centered on the so-called abortion pill. Both sides bracing for a Texas judge`s decision that could restrict access to one of two drugs used to induce medicated abortion.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is one part of a two-step regimen, long approved by the food and drug administration and currently the most prevalently used form of abortion care in the United States with overwhelming evidence that it`s safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Most Americans could look in their medicine cabinet, where they will find medication prescribed by a doctor that they use on daily basis. Mifepristone is no exception to that process.\par \par \uc2 [18:40:05]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: The federal judge in Texas appointed by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could render his ruling any day now, setting off a national chain reaction long anticipated by abortion rights advocates and the White House.\par \par \uc2 In some states, particularly rural ones with limited clinics that present a travel barrier to care, limitations on the pill could be keenly felt. In states like South Dakota, where we met Nurse Misty (ph) last year, limits on the pill were already the next battle.\par \par \uc2 MISTY (ph), NURSE: Because it`s not access at all. Claiming that those medications aren`t safe just is so false.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Here on the steps of the Austin federal courthouse, the national divide encapsulated.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): This as much as it`s a small scene, is a very telling one. Like, this is what (inaudible) this is the national debate playing now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is the national debate, but at the same time, the majority of Americans, the vast majority of them are pro-choice and pro- abortion.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE SALAZAR: I believe that chemical abortion pills are the next battle that anti-abortion people have to be fighting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Jose, if the judge rules against the pill, expect the Biden administration to challenge it, a move that could ultimately put this decision before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Ali Vitali in Austin, Texas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now to a brutal attack in a Florida high school where a student allegedly beat a staffer after she took away his video game console. Our Emilie Ikeda has the details. I want to warn you that some of the video may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, this Palm Coast, Florida high school under the microscope after a brutal attack on a teacher`s aide. It happened Tuesday, all caught on camera. You first see her walk into view. Then she`s attacked from behind by a 6-foot-6, 270-pound student. The paraprofessional falls to the ground unconscious. The student stomping on her back and repeatedly throwing punches in a beating that lasts roughly 25 seconds. Even as students and staff try to help her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This could have been homicide. When you push people down like that, they hit their head. You never know the outcome.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The 17-year-old, who is not being named since he`s a minor, has been charged with felony aggravated battery with bodily harm.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Am I going to jail?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, you are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For how long?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: According to the arrest report, the suspect told investigators that he was upset that the employee took his Nintendo switch game away from him and made comments that when he comes back, he is going to kill her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If I`m the prosecutor, I`m going to listen to that evidence and I`m going to ask myself, what does that say about the state of mind of the student when this was happening?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The president of the \uc2 Florida Education Association\uc2  calls Tuesday`s attack a consequence, in part, of understaffing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Florida has been dealing with a massive teacher and staff shortage issues. When you don`t have enough staff, you -- it`s harder to manage students in our schools.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The district superintendent says, creating a safe learning and working environment on our campuses is critical. Violence is never an appropriate reaction. The violence landing that school staffer in the hospital who is now facing a long road to recovery. Emilie Ikeda, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Still ahead tonight, why a college basketball star is being linked to a fatal shooting. Also, hospitals are supposed to be posting prices, so why are patients still shocked at their high medical bills?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:45:58]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Tonight, a college basketball star is under growing scrutiny after being linked to a fatal shooting last month. Morgan Chesky has the details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): On the court he`s a top \uc2 NBA\uc2  prospect, but it`s his role off the hardwood, putting a growing spotlight on Alabama freshman Brandon Miller.\par \par \uc2 KURT HELIN, NBC SPORTS PROBASKETBALLTALK MANAGER EDITOR: They`ve been one of the top teams in the nation all year, and Miller is a large part of that. He is going to be a top five NBC draft pick if these red flags don`t change that dynamic.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: The controversy stems from a murder investigation tying the 20- year-old to the scene of a deadly January shooting.\par \par \uc2 NATE OATS, UNIVERSITY OF ALABAMA MEN`S BASKETBALL COACH: The whole team understands how tragic and serious the situation that we`re dealing with is.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: On January 15, police say 23-year-old Jamea Harris was fatally shot near campus. Investigators arrested Michael Davis and Alabama player Darius Miles, who was subsequently released from the team. Both men are now charged with capital murder, and investigators say Miles admits he provided the gun used in the shooting to Davis. But in a court hearing this week, police testified it was Miller who brought the handgun to his former teammate after getting text messages to pick him up and bring Miles gun. In a statement, Miller`s attorney said Brandon never touch the gun, was not involved in its exchange to Mr. Davis in any way, and never knew that illegal activity involving the gun would occur. Miller hasn`t missed a game since the investigation began. The university is stressing he`s fully cooperating with authorities and is not considered a suspect.\par \par \uc2 OATS: Make decisions based on available facts, and that`s what we did here.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Tonight, the mother of Jamea Harris upset Miller was allowed to play today, saying had there been no gun, her daughter would still be alive.\par \par \uc2 DECARLA HEARD, JAMEA HARRIS` MOTHER: I haven`t stopped being angry. I haven`t stopped long enough to grieve because I`m so angry. She has a five- year-old son that`s still at home waiting on his mom to come home. And she never come home.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Morgan Chesky, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: All right, we are back in a moment with the failure to comply, why some hospitals still are not posting prices for patients despite a law saying they have to.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the deadly crash that killed two bicyclists into Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:49]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: We are back at the scene of a deadly crash in Arizona, where a pickup truck struck a large group of bicyclists riding across a bridge. At this time, there are two confirmed fatalities and eleven others injured. Officials say the driver stayed on the scene and police are investigating the incident.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, laws were put into place that required hospitals nationwide to post the prices of medical procedures so that patients knew how much they would have to pay for treatment in advance. But patient advocates say many hospitals still are not posting prices, and customers are being shocked by the large medical bills. Marissa Parra has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The pressure of paying for medical procedures has haunted Jeff King in Kansas for years.\par \par \uc2 JEFF KING, SURPRISE MEDICAL BILLS VICTIM: I went back to my follow up appointment. They said, how are you doing? I said, well, your medical bill about put me into AFib again.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: But it is his right to know how much a procedure costs in advance, and it has been since January of 2021, when federal price transparency became federal law.\par \par \uc2 CHIQUITA BROOKS-LASURE, \uc2 CENTERS FOR MEDICARE AND MEDICAID SERVICES\uc2  ADMINISTRATOR: It`s so incredibly important.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Chiquita Brooks-LaSure, the Administrator for \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 , also oversees hospital transparency. In a report released last week, CMS says that of the 600 hospitals they sampled, 70% were in full compliance, up from the 27% the year before. But patient advocacy groups are dissatisfied.\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA FISHER, PATIENTRIGHTSADVOCATE.ORG FOUNDER: It is not happening fast enough.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: And they`re challenging CMS`s survey results. PatientRightsAdvocate.org released their own report weeks ago, analyzing the websites of 2000 hospitals within the nation`s largest hospital systems, their findings only 25% were in full compliance.\par \par \uc2 Patient Advocate Cynthia Fisher shows us what patients should see on a compliant hospital`s website.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: It`s showing all of the codes.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: And she showed us what she found on many instead.\par \par \uc2 FISHER: Instead of prices, they put N/As in the field.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: So we took it to CMS.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Would this hospital be considered compliant?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS-LASURE: Yes, because N/A according to our rules means we don`t do the procedure.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Which would mean if they provide these procedures?\par \par \uc2 BROOKS-LASURE: They should be telling us how much it costs.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: In the two years since the law took effect, CMS has only issued two fines given as NBC was pressing the \uc2 HHS\uc2  secretary last summer.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): The buck stops with you. You`re the secretary.\par \par \uc2 (Voice-over): Brooks-LaSure says they`ve given 500 warning letters to hospitals, and now the maximum fines have been increased from $100,000 to $2 million. So we asked, what`s the biggest hold up? CMS blames the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS-LASURE: So they haven`t always had all of the staff to really focus on some of the IT system changes that need to happen.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: The \uc2 American Hospital Association\uc2 , which represents healthcare networks and hospitals, says health systems remain, "deeply committed, despite considerable strains on the hospital workforce over the past three years."\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Who hurts the most?\par \par \uc2 FISHER: It`s the American people. It`s the hard-working.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: In Washington, Marissa Parra, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: When we come back, the heartwarming reason these grannies are dancing through the streets of the Big Easy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:56:45]\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: There is good news tonight about the power of friendship and how love and dancing help heal the pain.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: For the famous Milwaukee Dancing Grannies, this week`s visit to New Orleans was the trip of a life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ladies and gentleman, the Milwaukee Dancing Grannies.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: A chance to perform in one of the city`s Mardi Gras parades.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, they were amazing.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: But for Jan Kwiatkowski and the rest of the group, it was about so much more.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): Jan, what was it like to be in New Orleans for Mardi Gras?\par \par \uc2 JAN KWIATKOWSKI: It was the probably absolute most amazing, profound, exciting lifegiving thing that not only myself, but all of us have done.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: She says the celebration was also a time of healing. That`s because in November 2021, three of their members and a dancer`s husband were killed along with two other people during the Waukesha Christmas parade when a man driving an SUV intentionally plowed through the crowds.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): In that senseless, horrible tragedy, how do you all as a group go forward?\par \par \uc2 KWIATKOWSKI: The reality is you don`t heal unless you`re healing in community.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Community they found in the New Orleans dance group called The 610 Stompers. They call themselves extraordinary men with extraordinary moves. And they invited the grannies to Mardi Gras as a sign of solidarity.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I cannot begin to tell you how much that means to all of us.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING & APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why can`t we just come together and try to heal and --\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: Stompers Chris Second (ph) and Brett Patron just knew their city would welcome them with open arms.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m going to tell you that right now. They are hard to handle. But they were so much fun.\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: The two dance groups now forever friends with joyful memories they`ll never forget.\par \par \uc2 KWIATKOWSKI: It`s just such a tangible way of saying you`re not alone. That meant a lot. I can`t even put into words. I`m going to cry now how much that meant.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DIAZ-BALART: The Grannies hope to have The 610 Stompers join them in a parade sometime this year back in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 That`s NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for this Saturday. Kate Snow will be here tomorrow night. I`m Jose Diaz-Balart. Thank you for t