ferent sounds even for, you know, it`s got nature sounds but for baby. It`s got that shushing or --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, yes, I love that.\par \par \uc2 POST: And what we like about this as you can throw it in your bag. It`s great if you travel with an infant or if you just like a sound machine. And, you know, I think this is a really great option for under $20.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, great. That`ll help with the sleep.\par \par \uc2 POST: OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Now, I`ve heard of the weighted blanket now we`re talking weighted eye masked, like how heavy is it?\par \par \uc2 POST: It`s just one weight that gives a little soothing kind of just --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 POST: -- moment of Zen, right? And you put it right over your eyes and I love it. It is like a little sort of weighted blanket.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow, OK.\par \par \uc2 POST: -- for your eyes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right.\par \par \uc2 POST: And so, it`s got these hypoallergenic beads in there so it`s going to conform to your eyes. It`ll, you know, black out the light, but it`s really about just taking that moment. I mean you can take this to work.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh yes. Take a little nap at work.\par \par \uc2 POST: Yes. This is me time.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I`ve been known to do it.\par \par \uc2 POST: A little nap at work or when you`re traveling. And it`s really soft, it`s got that plush material.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 POST: So, you know, this is under $10, so this is another little something you can do for yourself.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The price is right, Chassie.\par \par \uc2 POST: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you. If you want to purchase any of these items, you can scan the QR code or you can go to today.com/shopthislist. And we should mention that today does earn a commission on purchases made from this segment which solely is featuring products available at Target today. Chas, have a great weekend, my dear.\par \par \uc2 POST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Sleep like the candle, just make a vibe happen. Coming up next, a special coffee shop reaching across generations of Black culture. Blayne Alexander will have the story. But first, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230307ej2o0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc41}{\*\bkmkend toc41}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for February 23, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Catie Beck, Laura Jarrett, Gabe Gutierrez, Al Roker,  Ron Allen, Guad Venegas, Dasha Burns, Keir Simmons, Dana Griffin \par \uc2 3,842 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 February 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the bombshell testimony. Alex Murdaugh taking the stand at his murder trial.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The South Carolina attorney denying he killed his wife and son, but then stunning the courtroom, admitting he lied to police about his alibi that night. The paranoid thoughts he said he thought he was having and the moment Murdaugh broke down as he described finding the bodies. Then the prosecution`s turn. The tough questions he faced under cross- examination.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, a deadly winter storm on the move. More than a million customers without power. In the Midwest, a downed power line killing a firefighter. A parking garage collapsing under snow. The new threat in the west. Rare blizzard warnings in southern California. \uc2 Al Roker\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 New clues in the toxic train derailment in Ohio. The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  saying it was 100 percent preventable. The preliminary report finding an alarm went off just before the wreck. What investigators say about the crew`s actions and the potential cause.\par \par \uc2 The shooting spree in Florida. A TV reporter and a 9-year-old girl killed. New body cam of police arresting the suspect. The new details.\par \par \uc2 And the Emmy-winning star of Abbott Elementary, Sheryl Lee Ralph on the obstacles she has overcome and inspiring a new generation.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good evening. For the defense, accused murderer Alex Murdaugh taking the witness stand today in South Carolina. Dramatic and at times tearful testimony from the man at the center of the country`s most closely followed criminal trial. Murdaugh under questioning from his lawyers saying I didn`t shoot my wife or son. And addressing what has appeared to be some of the prosecution`s strongest evidence.\par \par \uc2 Murdaugh from the witness box admitting he lied about his whereabouts the night his wife and 22-year-old son were shot to death. Shocking at the lies to an opioid addiction and paranoia. Under cross-examination, Murdaugh, a former lawyer also admitted to stealing from clients. The prosecution has maintained the murders were part of Murdaugh`s attempt to escape accountability for financial misdeeds.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with Catie Beck reporting from Walterboro, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CATIE BECK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): After nearly five weeks of testimony, a stunning surprise.\par \par \uc2 ALEX MURDAUGH, ACCUSED OF KILLING HIS WIFE AND SON: I am going to testify. I want to testify.\par \par \uc2 BECK: The trial`s most anticipated witness takes the stand. Alex Murdaugh testifies in his own defense. The first questions aimed at the heart of the case.\par \par \uc2 JIM GRIFFIN, DEFENSE LAWYER: Did you take this gun or any gun like it and blow your son`s brains out on June 7th, or any day, or any time?\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH: No, I did not.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Did you take another blackout such as this and fire it into your wife Maggie`s leg, torso, or any part of her body?\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH: No, I did not.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Quick to confront what is perhaps the prosecution`s strongest evidence, the video taken by Paul Murdaugh at the dog kennels, placing Alex at the crime scene minutes before the murders. He`s told investigators he wasn`t there, but admits now he was lying.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Alex, why did you lie?\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH: As my addiction evolved over time, I would get in these situations or circumstances where I would get paranoid.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Murdaugh says he didn`t trust state law enforcers, and regrets the lie that led to many others.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Did you continue lying after that night, did you not?\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH: Once I lied, I continued the lie, yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Emotional and crying throughout, Murdaugh`s testimony largely rewrites his timeline on the night of the murders, showering and changing clothes before dinner, and describing the moment he discovered the bodies, again saying he checked for signs of life.\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH: I know I tried to turn him over.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: When you say you tried to turn him over, why were you trying to turn him over?\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH: I don`t know. I don`t know. I don`t know why I tried to turn him over. My boy`s laying face-down.\par \par \uc2 BECK: Later in testimony, Alex admits a long-time addiction to opioids, that he stole clients` funds, but denies being overly concerned about getting caught prior to the murders.\par \par \uc2 CREIGHTON WATERS, CHIEF PROSECUTOR: What kind of cases did you normally do?\par \par \uc2 BECK: On cross-examination, prosecutors began by pressing Murdaugh on the fraud cases, where he admits he stole money from his clients and lied to them. And prosecutors suggesting all the stolen money wasn`t going to fund his pill addiction.\par \par \uc2 WATERS: You were generating millions of dollars in fees. That was not enough for you? Would you concede that?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:08]\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH: If by concede that, you mean was I also stealing money that I shouldn`t have, yes, sir, I agree with that. I`ve said that, repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Catie, what do we know about how the jury was taking all this in how they may have reacted?\par \par \uc2 BECK (on camera): Well, Lester, the jury was captivated, so attentive to Alex Murdaugh`s testimony on the stand today. At times, Murdaugh was repositioning his body to face the jurors directly and answer the questions directly to them. At one point, when Murdaugh got extremely emotional, one juror that was sitting close to him actually slid a Kleenex box in his direction. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Catie Beck, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me bring in our Senior Legal Correspondent Laura Jarrett now. Laura, what was the defense strategy here, and how does it appear to be playing out?\par \par \uc2 LAURA JARRETT, NBC NEWS SENIOR LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, this is the defense team going on offense. Clearly, the goal was to try to take the sting out of the damning evidence that the prosecution would raise on cross-examination, and that`s likely why he was forced to admit that he lied to police and to his family, all while trying to plant seeds of reasonable doubt.\par \par \uc2 But testifying at all, it`s a risky gamble. Already the prosecution has tried to highlight his inconsistencies and also pierce through his folksy demeanor on cross-examination to try to show this jury that he is an accomplished lawyer who knows how to cover his tracks. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Laura, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to that deadly crippling winter storm that is stretching right now across the country and a separate threat here in the West. Gabe Gutierrez is watching it all from Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, devastating snow, ice, and wind pummeling even more of the country. This time knocking out power to nearly a million people across the Midwest. Most of those outages in Michigan where a volunteer firefighter was killed when a tree brought down a high-voltage line.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: A volunteer just died in here for free. He gave his life for free.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: In Wisconsin, authorities say snow likely caused a partial collapse of a parking garage at a shopping mall. Today, Minneapolis under a snow emergency.\par \par \uc2 Over the last few hours, we`ve seen a very rapid snowfall here. The road conditions are getting worse.\par \par \uc2 Snow totals not as high as predicted earlier in the week, still around a foot blanketed the twin cities over three days.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is a big one. For all coming down at once, like, yes, this is a pretty big one.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Coast-to-coast, treacherous road conditions. Portland, Oregon recording its second snowiest day on record with more than 10 inches. This 20-car pileup in Southern California injuring at least eight people. A separate system now prompting the first blizzard warnings since 1989 for the mountains near L.A. The nationwide weather whiplash is remarkable even for February. Across the Mid-Atlantic and southeastern U.S., record heat, unusually high temperatures as far north as Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Having a 70-degree day is just such a welcome break.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): But air travel is still dicey. More than 2,000 U.S. flights canceled just today, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And that takes us to \uc2 Al Roker\uc2 . Al, as one storm moves out, another moves in impacting the West Coast.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR: Lester, we have six million folks out west looking at winter weather advisories, winter storm warnings, blizzard warnings, including the San Bernardino Mountains. First blizzard warning ever issued there. Record lows likely. We`re looking at morning lows in Reno in the 20s. Vegas into the 30s. Even Los Angeles into the upper 30s. Salt Lake City in the 20s.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at five to eight feet of snow back through the Sierra, but also the San Bernardino Mountains and the Santa Barbara Mountains as well. We`re also looking upwards of 10 inches of rain through Southern California. Significant roadway flooding and airport delays likely. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, \uc2 Al Roker\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 And new tonight, a preliminary finding about the cause of that toxic train disaster in Ohio as the Transportation Secretary visited for the first time. Ron Allen is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RON ALLEN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, with the scene being cleaned up, new details about what caused the fiery derailment. Federal investigators saying a preliminary investigation shows an overheated wheel bearing likely to blame.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY, CHAIR, \uc2 NATIONAL TRANSPORTATION SAFETY BOARD\uc2 : This was 100 percent preventible.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  says the train passed over two detectors, which showed the bearing was heating up. By the time it hit a third detector, it was 253 degrees above air temperature, triggering an audible alarm to warn the crew, who then slowed and stopped the train.\par \par \uc2 HOMENDY: This is a community that is suffering. This is not about politics. What I care about is figuring out how this happened.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: The \uc2 NTSB\uc2  chair says there is no evidence the crew did anything wrong. In a statement, \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  says our highest priority is the safety of our people and the communities we serve. Adding, it will continue to support the \uc2 NTSB\uc2 `s investigation. While today, Transportation Secretary Pete Buttigieg visited for the first time after criticism from some residents that he should have come sooner, demanding the nation`s rail operators stop resisting safety measures to cut costs.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:19]\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, U.S. SECRETARY OF TRANSPORTATION: Let`s get people from both sides of the aisle to the table and come to agreement on some steps that are needed, even if the rail industry is pushing back.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Meanwhile, near the crash, this Figleys only comes back to the farm to check on things like their chickens. They`re part of a class action lawsuit that attorneys say includes over 500 residents.\par \par \uc2 MARILYN FIGLEY, RESIDENT, EAST PALESTINE: I live out of my car and at my daughter`s house. It`s awful. I feel homeless.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Why won`t you come back home?\par \par \uc2 FIGLEY: Because I don`t know what I`m coming home to, and I`m 70 years old.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Ron Allen, NBC News, East Palestine, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Now to Florida, where a series of shootings left three people dead, including a young girl and a reporter. Two more are hospitalized. A suspect is under arrest. Guad Venegas is there for us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get on your (BLEEP) face!\par \par \uc2 GUAD VENEGAS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, the Orange County Sheriff releasing bodycam footage of the arrest of Keith Moses, the 19-year-old suspect accused of a deadly shooting spree in Orlando.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Roll him on his side real quick.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Three victims killed, including a news reporter and a 9-year-old girl, two others critically wounded.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re getting multiple gunshots wound calls in the Hialeah Street area.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: The first shooting happened Wednesday morning where authorities found 38-year-old Natacha Augustin dead from a gunshot wound. Officials say the victim was an acquaintance of the suspect. Hours later, a news crew covering that incident was ambushed in their car, deputies say. The gunfire killing 24-year-old Spectrum News 13 reporter Dylan Lyons and critically wounding cameraman Jesse Walden.\par \par \uc2 I just happened to catch a bullet. But he kept shooting at me as I ducked behind the wheel of my car, and he walked forward and shot into my car and ended up striking our reporter.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Authorities say the suspect then fled the scene and entered a nearby home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ve got two more victims, 9-year-old female.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Deputies say he then opened fire again, killing a 9-year-old girl and critically injuring her mother. The suspect was arrested a short time later.\par \par \uc2 JOHN MINA, SHERIFF, ORANGE COUNTY, FLORIDA: We recovered a Glock 40, a semiautomatic handgun from inside of his pants. That gun was still hot to the touch, meaning it had just been fired.\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS: Moses is now charged with first-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VENEGAS (on camera): And tonight, officials are still investigating a motive, but they say the 19-year-old suspect had a lengthy criminal history, including eight felony and 11 misdemeanor arrests. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Guad Venegas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, the battle over abortion. How an imminent court ruling about a widely used abortion drug could have an impact nationwide.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It could be the most consequential abortion decision since Roe v. Wade was overturned. A Texas judge will soon decide whether to block access to a commonly used abortion pill nationwide. Dasha Burns reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: We can`t go back!\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Amarillo, Texas now at the center of America`s abortion debate.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: I believe that we will win!\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Here, a group of anti-abortion doctors and medical organizations are suing the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , challenging the approval of Mifeprex, generically known as Mifepristone, part of a two-drug regimen commonly used for abortions that has been approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  since 2000. \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  arguing the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t adequately evaluate the drug`s safety.\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST, SENIOR COUNSEL, ALLIANCE DEFENDING FREEDOM: We`re asking the court to do what is right, to follow -- and ask the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to follow the law by taking these dangerous drugs off the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Presiding over the case, a conservative judge appointed by former President Trump. An injunction would set off access to Mifepristone nationwide.\par \par \uc2 If the plaintiffs win this lawsuit, what will be the effects?\par \par \uc2 [19:15:01]\par \par \uc2 DR. SERINA FLOYD, MEDICAL DIRECTOR, PLANNED PARENTHOOD OF METROPOLITAN WASHINGTON, DC: It will be devastating. Even in places where they`ve worked very hard to secure access to abortion, states like California, New York, here in D.C.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Abortion providers like Dr. Serina Floyd say the lawsuit is based on misinformation.\par \par \uc2 How dangerous is this medication?\par \par \uc2 FLOYD: It`s not dangerous at all. The evidence is solid, the science is solid. The complication rate is less than one percent.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  told NBC News it does not comment on ongoing litigation. But in the court filings said the claims in the lawsuit are unsupported by any evidence. Since 2000, more than five million women have taken mifepristone with 28 deaths associated with the drug reported.\par \par \uc2 Millions of women have taken these drugs safely. Doesn`t that paint a contrary picture to your complaint?\par \par \uc2 DR. CHRISTINA FRANCIS, AMERICAN ASSOCIATION OF PRO-LIFE OBSTETRICIANS AND GYNECOLOGISTS: I`m seeing these women in my own practice. My emergency room physicians, they`re telling me how frequently they`re seeing these complications, that women come in with retained tissue, with heavy bleeding, with serious life-threatening infections.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: In a court brief, the country`s leading group of OBGYN say the mifepristone is "exceedingly safe and effective, calling the lawsuit ideological and not scientific.\par \par \uc2 The result if you win is that abortion access will be significantly limited. Is that the goal of this lawsuit?\par \par \uc2 BAPTIST: No, the goal of this lawsuit is to protect American women and girls from dangerous chemical abortion drugs.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: There is another drug, Misoprostol, that can be used for abortions, but doctors say it can be less effective on its own. Back in Amarillo, Amanda Barnes travelled from Atlanta to protest this case.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA BARNES, ACTIVIST: It`s ridiculous that just because, you know, we were born with a uterus and certain body parts that we should have to be subjected to this treatment.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The next frontier in America`s abortion battle.\par \par \uc2 Dasha Burns, NBC News, Amarillo, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Coming up, our exclusive report in the flow of goods into Russia, despite sanctions.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: As the war in Ukraine approaches the one-year mark, the U.S. is pushing for more sanctions against Russia. But are the ones already in place having an impact? Keir Simmons went inside neighboring Georgia to find out how Russia is getting around them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): If you want to know how the Russian economy is surviving amid punishing western sanctions, you might come here. We`re just across the border in Georgia, and it`s lined with trucks. President Putin`s supply line.\par \par \uc2 You`re going from Turkey to Russia? To Moscow?\par \par \uc2 He`s carrying xerox printer ink, he says. Since Putin`s invasion and unprecedented Western sanctions, satellite images along with international trade data shows this mountain pass becoming much busier. That`s not surprising. At the outset of the war, Russia dropped most import restrictions on things like cars.\par \par \uc2 The Russian border is about 25 miles that way, but police tell us they`ve closed the road because of an avalanche risk. Business has stopped, but only temporarily.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hello!\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Then hidden in the snow, a stunning discovery. Two brand-new American ram pickups.\par \par \uc2 American cars, American trucks?\par \par \uc2 It was never driven, he says.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this. Look, it`s still got the protection on the dashboard. Look, they`re brand-new. Where are they going?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: To the Moscow.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: To Moscow, he tells me.\par \par \uc2 The men tell us they`ve sold both vehicles for around $170,000 each. They say they are moving cars every week.\par \par \uc2 Does it matter that there is a war on?\par \par \uc2 Where is that war, he asks. It doesn`t matter for us.\par \par \uc2 We have obtained one of the vehicle ID numbers and asked the manufacturer who told us it was legally sold to a private customer by an authorized distributor in Abu Dhabi, that they cannot prevent that customer from reselling it, and the company complies with all U.S. and international sanctions.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not only Georgia. Satellite images also show lines of trucks on the roads to Russia from China. President Putin and China`s top diplomat meeting yesterday, pledging to strengthen ties. China already provides crucial economic help to Russia.\par \par \uc2 Back in Georgia, the road reopens, and we pass a construction site where China is helping build a new tunnel through the mountain. The half-billion project was started before the Ukraine invasion. But the plan will be more trade with Russia. Clearly, not every country wants this to be the end of the road for moscow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (on camera): Despite all the sanctions, the \uc2 IMF\uc2  says Russia`s economy is actually set to grow this year, though only by 0.3 percent. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Keir Simmons tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our inspiring one-on-one with actress and singer Sheryl Lee Ralph.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: She is the star of a hit sitcom, an Emmy winner, and recently sang at the Super Bowl. Sheryl Lee Ralph now on the hurdles she overcame and why she is only just getting started. She spoke with Dana Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DANA GRIFFIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With a career spanning nearly five decades in showbiz, Sheryl Lee Ralph is back in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Do you still have pinch me moments, especially now when you see we`re sitting in the middle of Hollywood?\par \par \uc2 SHERYL LEE RALPH, SINGER: Yes. I have real pinch me moments in life, because, you know, I`ve lived most of my life as a very regular normal human being. I will go to the 99 Cent Store, the \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  or whatever. I will do that. And now when I go, I`m like, what in the world are they staring at? And then, I`m like, oh my God, it`s me.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The entertainment icon has had a whirlwind of recent success, inspiring a new generation.\par \par \uc2 RALPH: Now, who took that picture of me?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Last year, she won an Emmy for supporting comedy actress for her role in Abbott Elementary where she plays teacher Barbara Howard.\par \par \uc2 What was that moment like for you?\par \par \uc2 RALPH: First of all, I was -- I was listening to the whole thing, and I was like getting ready to be happy for somebody. And I realized, no, this is very real. She just called my name.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And more recently, the critics choice awards where her message resonated with many.\par \par \uc2 RALPH: When you look in the mirror, you better love what you see.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Her Broadway, TV and film career dates back to the `70s. In 1981, she starred in the original Broadway cast of Dream Girls. Her latest performance, Super Bowl LVII, singing Lift Every Voice And Sing. But stealing the show hasn`t always been easy.\par \par \uc2 RALPH: They used to say you`re too dark. Your lips are too big. Your hair is too nappy. Suppose I listened to them?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: But Ralph persevered.\par \par \uc2 What else do you want to accomplish?\par \par \uc2 RALPH: More. I want to tell stories that deserve to be told, characters whose voices were dimmed or dulled or muted just because they were Black. Now is the time to tell those stories.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Dana Griffin, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thanks for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230224ej2n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc42}{\*\bkmkend toc42}\b \uc2 Clearing Up Some Common Nutrition Myths With The Registered Dietitian\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie \par \uc2 1,341 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH JONES: Welcome back this morning on Your Health. Common myths and maybe misguiding your diet decisions. Nora Minno is a registered dietician and she`s here to clear these things up once and for all. Nora, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NORA MINNO, REGISTERED DIETICIAN: Good morning, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So we have five nutrition myths that we`re going to debunk. I should say there are three of us here because congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She was so worried as nutritionist people are going to be like, oh, she`s got little (inaudible). No, no, there`s a little baby in there.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We are girl, we don`t know, OK. First things first, I should avoid all carbs. This is a big one.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know so many people who just -- they feel like if they touch a carb, they explode.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: I agree. And I have good news for all you carb lovers. I`m pretty sure as everybody body, right? Carbs are your friend. In fact, there are a really important part of a healthy balanced diet. They do a lot for us. They are our body`s preferred source of fuel, same with our brain. They help our digestion and they actually just make us feel good.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So then, what are we doing wrong then? Because a lot of people, you know, I guess obviously probably shouldn`t have carbs before bed, but where are we going wrong?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. So really, when it comes to carbs, it is just about the quality of carbs that you`re picking and the quantity that you`re consuming. That`s really where a lot of people go wrong over consuming things like refined carbs, sweets, white breads, things like that.\par \par \uc2 But if you focus on portions, choose more complex carbs.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What`s a good carb? Give me a couple of example.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Fruits, vegetables, whole grains, oatmeal, things like that, you should be in the clear.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. So myth number two, fresh produce is healthier than frozen or canned. What would you say?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: No. This is not true at all. In fact, sometimes your frozen or canned options can be actually more nutritious. There was a study done that found that vegetables can lose 15% to 55% of their vitamin C by the time they`re picked, by the time they make it to your grocery shelf. So frozen, they typically flash freeze right on the spot. So you`re packing a lot more nutrients. And they`re actually a little bit better for your wallet too.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I was just about to say there`s a cost benefit as well.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. Less food waste, oftentimes they are more budget friendly. And then you just have some good things on hand if you want to make a smoothie, a salad, things like that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely. OK, our next myth, this is a good one. Plant milk is healthier than dairy milk. What say ye?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: All right. Well, this is not always the case. This one really comes down just to preference because you know, now it just seems like there`s a million different plant milks out there. There are options galore, which is great, but cow`s milk is still a really great option.\par \par \uc2 One cup of skim milk packs in 10 grams of protein and a quarter of your daily calcium needs. Whereas plant milk like an almond milk has one gram of protein per serving.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know what it is, I guess also just depends on, you know, whether you`re lactose intolerant, you know, if you have a dietary issue.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. So this one -- one is not better than the other, it`s just going to come down to choice. And if you are picking a plant milk, you want to pick varieties that have low or no added sugar, and ones that are more fortified with things like calcium, vitamin D that you might not be getting if you`re picking that --\par \par \uc2 JONES: I will admit that the plant milks, I didn`t realize some of them do have a lot of sugar.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Some do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I just didn`t realize that.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Some do. So be a savvy reader.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Let`s talk about fat there. Remember for a while everything was low fat this, low fat that.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: So the nutrition or the myth number four all fat is bad. Obviously it`s not.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:05]\par \par \uc2 MINNO: It`s not. And thank goodness that low fat craze in the `90s and thereafter is over because we know now that fats, most importantly, mono and polyunsaturated fats are really good for us. It is still American Heart Month, and we want to get those fats in from things like avocados, nuts, seeds, olive oils, and even our fatty fish to help protect our heart boost that HDL, which is our good cholesterol, and lower LDL, which is our bad cholesterol.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Sounds good. And I think sometimes when you`re eating, you just don`t know which one is right.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. So we do want to avoid certain fats, there are facts called saturated fats, that the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  says we should stay at or below 13 grams a day --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh wait, that`s good advice.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: -- for a hearth healthy diet.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Si when you get a package is something flipped over the back.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And if you see saturated fats, how many grams did you say it should be layered?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: 13, approximately around 13 per day.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Per day.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: So if you have four ounces of kind of lean ground beef, you`re already at that 13 mark. So you want to be a real savvy label reader.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK, all right. Myth number five soy, I`m actually interested in what you`re going to say. Soy increases risk for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes. You know, I hear this one all the time.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Me too.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: And it`s still very common and abundant. So this myth really stems from the fact that soy contains something called phytoestrogens. And we know that estrogen can sometimes be linked to an increase in breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: But the estrogens in soy are very different than mammalian estrogens, ones that we have in our body, and they act differently. And in fact, they can be protective against things like breast cancer, because what we`ve seen in Asian countries where they consume more soy, is that breast cancer risk is actually much lower.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Does it depend on the product or what kind of soy you`re consuming? No?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Not really. And again, the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  said 25 grams of soy protein per day can actually be heart protective. So you can get your tofu, your (inaudible), your soy milk, your edamame at a sushi restaurant, right? Lots of really great options to pack some good soy protein.\par \par \uc2 JONES: There you go. So you don`t have to avoid it. Nora, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: That`s OK. Thank you, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Next, a remarkable couple who made their dreams come true, opening a unique restaurant here in New York during the pandemic. How it`s become one of the hottest tickets in town, we`ll take you there. But first, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230301ej2k000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc43}{\*\bkmkend toc43}\b \uc2 The Today Show for February 20, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Sheinelle Jones, Tom Llamas, Dylan Dreyer, Jacob Soboroff, Maria  Shriver, Erin McLaughlin, Kristen Welker, Sharon Epperson, Molly Hunter \par \uc2 8,709 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 February 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST: It is 8:00 on "TODAY." Coming up, surprise visit. Overnight, President Biden making history arriving in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: I`m here to show our unwavering support for the nation`s independence, and sovereignty, and territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: A major sign of support from the U.S. as the war in Ukraine approaches its one-year anniversary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That`s how long we`re going to be with you, Mr. President, for as long as it takes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: We`re live with the latest.\par \par \uc2 Then, holiday sales. Hoping to save some money this long weekend. We have a look at all the best Presidents` Day deals, everything from furniture to appliances, electronics, and cars. Plus, the three questions you should ask yourself before making a purchase.\par \par \uc2 And royal treatment. The Prince and Princess of Wales joining stars from around the world for the so-called British Oscars. We`re highlighting all the best moments including an emotional tribute to Queen Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HELEN MIRREN, ACTRESS: Cinema at its best does what her majesty did effortlessly, bring us together and unite us in a story.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, what films took home the night`s biggest awards? All that and more. Today, Monday, February 20, 2023.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Spending Presidents` Day.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: On the plaza.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Fort Worth, Texas.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Murrysville (ph), Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Huntsville, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: St. John, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: And New Lenox, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating milestone birthdays.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My 30th.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And my 60th.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Jacksonville, Florida.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Lea`s (ph) turning 18.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Shout out to our NBC family.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: At KOMU 8 News.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: In Columbia, Missouri.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Happy Presidents` Day.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Welcome back to "TODAY." We certainly appreciate you being with us on this Monday morning. And take a look at all of these folks. We are happy to see this happy holiday crowd. This is on -- in our corner of the woods here at Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, they came from all over the country. We always love that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A lot of states represented. Savannah and Hoda, you know them, will be back tomorrow, of course, and it will be a big morning here in Studio 1A.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That is right. Grammy winning superstar Pink will be here. She`s coming out with a new album and heading on tour. So, a lot to talk about. Plus, she`ll treat us to a live performance when she joins us for our 8:00, 9:00, and 10:00 hours. So, join us tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I am not going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I`m going to stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s going to be a party.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: She always brings it. Like, live --\par \par \uc2 JONES: She does.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: -- Pink live is always amazing.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She does. And we`ll get back outside and say hi to all those folks coming up in just a bit. Meantime, it is a busy morning. So, let`s get to your news at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 In an unprecedented show of support for Ukraine, President Biden took a high stakes trip to the war zone overnight. His surprise journey to Kyiv coming just days before the first anniversary of the Russian invasion. NBC`s Erin McLaughlin is in the Ukrainian capital with more on this historic visit.\par \par \uc2 Erin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Sheinelle. President Zelenskyy calling the visit historic, timely, and brave. The visit began here in the capital at the presidential palace. President Biden was greeted by President Zelenskyy and his wife, Olena. He signed a guest book and announced a $500 million military aid package and delivered this message.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: One year later, Kyiv stands and Ukraine stands, democracy stands. America stands with you and the world stands with you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCLAUGHLIN: From the palace, they made their way to St. Michael`s Cathedral. I watched as air raid sirens echoing in the distance. The two presidents walked out this way and paid tribute to the thousands of fallen Ukrainian soldiers in the memorial behind me.\par \par \uc2 From there, President Biden made his way to the U.S. embassy where he met with staff before departing the capital. In Russia, this visit is being seen as a provocation. But here in Ukraine, it is being seen as a remarkable show of support and solidarity by America. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Erin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And as we just saw there, the president`s secret trip showing his unwavering support for Ukraine, even as some in \uc2 NATO\uc2  and in Congress are questioning the ongoing commitment. NBC`s Chief White House Correspondent and Weekend TODAY co-anchor Kristen Welker joins us now live from Poland where the president will be marking this week`s first anniversary of the war.\par \par \uc2 Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT AND "SATURDAY TODAY" ANCHOR: Hi, Tom. Good morning to you. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 This trip was so secret because of the dangers that it posed, that only a handful of the president`s inner staff was aware that he was actually traveling to Kyiv. And that keen message just adding to what Erin said, the president saying, we`re not leaving. But executing on that is going to be more challenging to keep the global community on board and Congress, with some Republicans, already saying they are not going to write a blank check to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So, what happens now? Well, he travels here to Poland. He`s going to be meeting with the president of Poland. He will be meeting with leaders of the critical eastern flank and then he`s going to deliver his capstone address in the same location where he spoke in Warsaw when he visited when the war was just a month old.\par \par \uc2 And I am told in talking to top officials, that part of his message is going to be a condemnation of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  building on what we heard from the vice president over the weekend, that Putin has committed crimes against humanity and should be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 Now, this all comes amid new concerns about China, it`s believed that China is providing nonlethal aid to Russia. The secretary of state, over the weekend, saying that they believe China is considering providing lethal aid as well. The secretary of state warning that would be met with serious consequences, all as this war enters a pivotal new phase. Tom.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Kristen Welker with a lot of new developments there. Kristen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Breaking overnight, North Korea fired two short-range ballistic missiles towards Japan today, it`s second weapons test in the last three days. The missiles landing in the waters off the west coast of Japan. The test prompting Tokyo to call for an emergency meeting of the \uc2 United Nations Security Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend North Korea also launched an intercontinental ballistic missile, and in response, the U.S. military yesterday held bilateral air drills with South Korea and Japan.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. We`ve covered the news. Now, are you ready for a "Morning Boost"?\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. This is a good one. A young Australian basketball fan was in tears after watching his team, the Tasmanian Jackjumpers get knocked out of the playoffs during a game in New Zealand. While on his way back to the locker room the Australian captain noticed the heartbroken fan and knew that he had traveled a long way to come see the game. So, he walked over to comfort him and even gave him a pair of sneakers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How is that, mate?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CLINT STEINDL, TASMANIA JACKJUMPERS BASKETBALL TEAM CAPTAIN: As much as I try to put a smile on his face, he still had the tears. But maybe puts them up in his bedroom or something, gives him something to aspire to. And that`s what we`re trying to do in Tasian (ph), you know, I just try to help the little fella out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, that`s sweet. A little classy move. Keeping in mind that the player has got to be hurting too from that season ending loss. Listen, there are fans young and old, and it hurts when you lose, you know.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes, that`s --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Shout out to the Jackjumpers.\par \par \uc2 JONES: There you go.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Tasmanian Jackjumpers. I like those jerseys too.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Nice.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Learn something everything.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Those are pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, the Prince and Princess of Wales rubbing elbows with the stars of Hollywood. We`re going to take you live to London for an inside look at the big night at the British version of the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But first, expert tips you need to hear to make the most of today`s holiday sales. We`ll get to that in a moment right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: Welcome back this morning on "TODAY`s Consumer". We are focusing on those Presidents` Day sales.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Yes, inflation is putting a strain on your budget, it could be a great day to find a few deals, but, of course, there are things you need to consider.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Joining us now is CNBC`s Senior Personal Finance Correspondent Sharon Epperson.\par \par \uc2 Sharon, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SHARON EPPERSON, CNBC`S SENIOR PERSONAL FINANCE CORRESPONDENT: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Before we get into it, you say, before we get to the deals, you have three questions you want people to ask themselves.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Very important questions to ask.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: First of all, do you need it?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Fair.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Do you love it? And can you pay it off immediately?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: And so, it`s not just can you afford it. Because you`re probably going to put it on a credit card.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: You don`t want to carry that interest on the credit card. You want to pay that off immediately or that savings is really something you`re going to be paying for, for months and months in interest.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, can you pay it within the month before you get that bill?\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: I feel that`s a good life lesson --\par \par \uc2 JONES: It is.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: -- not just for Presidents` Day.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It is. I know. That is good.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Speaking of Presidents` Day, talk about some of the deals.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Well, there are a lot of different deals. A lot of people look at mattresses for Presidents` Day, furniture, maybe electronics. You may think about home appliances, large ones or little gadgets for your kitchen.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Maybe even a new car. So, there are a lot of different deals out there. I think I seem to be getting messages all the time about this deal or that deal for Presidents` Day doesn`t necessarily mean it`s something that I need.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: So, I kind of delete that message.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, you were saying -- what about like kitchen or -- the kitchen appliances, are we talking like coffee pots or like refrigerators, like big ticket items?\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: There are some big ticket items too that are -- that you can get. One of the things to keep in mind when you`re talking about appliances, whether they`re large or small, as you`re often sold a or asked to purchase a warranty, an extended warranty --\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: -- or sometime service agreement.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Important thing to check out there is to make sure that you actually really need that because it`s not going to be lasting that long. And that you could use that money, perhaps, to put into a savings account to buy a new one. What`s the replacement cost of that?\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Do you need to buy the extended warranty or the service agreement.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The service agreement.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: All right. Let`s dig a little deeper here. I know a family shopping around for a TV. Is it -- are electronics good right now? Is it a day to shop for electronics?\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Well, there are large electronics. Some people want TVs, some people want a laptop or maybe something else for their, you know, sound system or something like that. Again, the important thing to think about is, one, not carrying the credit card debt if you`re going to put it on the card. But also, think about how much that energy is costing on your home. I`m thinking about everything I have on in my house right now when I`m not there. Why do I have my TV on --\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: -- or my laptop on --\par \par \uc2 JONES: The energy vampire.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: The energy vampires. The energy vampires can cost $250 a year in your house if they`re sucking energy --\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Oh.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: -- because you don`t use it. So, think about that. As you`re thinking about what to purchase, keep in mind that maybe you don`t need that extra TV or if you get it, don`t have it on all the time.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s fair.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK. That`s good to know.\par \par \uc2 JONES: But if you`re looking for a car, is now the time? A new car.\par \par \uc2 EPPERSON: Well, there are going to be deals out there for new cars. Inventory is not tremendous, so there are going to be special deals for certain models. But again, the thing to think about is how are you going to pay for this car?\par \par \uc2 And shopping around to get the best loan is very important. You`re going to see perhaps six percent or more for a five-year car loan on average. You could go to a credit union and get far less than maybe you pay through the dealer in terms of the loan or even a bank. So, make sure you shop around for a car loan and lo