r new book, "Be an Angel," she shares how to do just that, using favorite quotes and scriptures along with their own personal stories of discovery and growth.\par \par \uc2 (On camera): You actually believe that all of us can actually be angels? What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 ROMA DOWNEY, ACTRESS/AUTHOR: It`s one thing to say I love you, or I believe in God. And it`s a whole other thing to actually take an action around that. So be an angel really is a call to action to be kind.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): She says this starts with how we begin our day.\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: In the morning, I start my day in gratitude, whether you believe in God or you don`t. To think about maybe how can I do something for somebody else today, think of people can do that, that maybe they would feel more hopeful that maybe they would feel more connected.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Do you think the idea of telling people they can be an angel will turn people off? That`s a religious thing and I don`t see myself that way.\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: I think that we`re all a little bit broken, aren`t we? You know, we -- nobody is perfect. This is not about being perfect. This is really just about being kind.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): Here at her home outside Los Angeles, Roma says life is a far cry from her childhood growing up in war torn Ireland, where she first learned the power of kindness from her father.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): We`re so divided in the United States, for example.\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: yes.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: What did he convey to you that helped you not become a person of hate?\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: You know, the minute you see somebody as different than you, it`s I think it`s easier to feel that disconnection or so our dive would encourage us just to find the things that we share the similarities in each other.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: So in a way, you`re doing the same work as your father --\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: I guess so. Yes, I never thought about -- I never thought of it that way.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: -- higher or different in a book.\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: Yes, I suppose so in some ways, you know, maybe we just turn into our parents before we realize it.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER (voice-over): In her book, Roma also shares lessons from her 15 year marriage to Hollywood producer, Mark Burnett. And she talks about the value of getting older as a woman in her 60s.\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: And I`m quicker to say, I don`t really want to do that to things that I don`t really want to do. I realize a lot of my life. I was like, much more of a people pleaser, you know.\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Age has given you the right to say no to things.\par \par \uc2 DOWNEY: I think so. I don`t know about you or anybody watching but I`m a menopausal woman, and sleep and I are not the best of friends anymore. And so some nights I`m lying in my bed, you know, and the things that maybe were like little worries during the day become these Herculean things to wrestle with in the night. Anybody that follows me on social media knows I`m obsessed with the sunrise is a great sort of symbolism of a new day, a new beginning. And if you messed up yesterday, let`s try and do better today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SHRIVER: Roma`s new book "Be an Angel" comes out Tuesday and it`s a great read. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK, Maria, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you. Up next, our fresh veggies really healthier than canned or frozen? We are clearing up some common nutrition myths with the registered dietitian. But first, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 [08:39:54]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Welcome back this morning on Your Health. Common myths and maybe misguiding your diet decisions. Nora Minno is a registered dietician and she`s here to clear these things up once and for all. Nora, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NORA MINNO, REGISTERED DIETICIAN: Good morning, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So we have five nutrition myths that we`re going to debunk. I should say there are three of us here because congratulations.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She was so worried as nutritionist people are going to be like, oh, she`s got little (inaudible). No, no, there`s a little baby in there.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We are girl, we don`t know, OK. First things first, I should avoid all carbs. This is a big one.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know so many people who just -- they feel like if they touch a carb, they explode.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: I agree. And I have good news for all you carb lovers. I`m pretty sure as everybody body, right? Carbs are your friend. In fact, there are a really important part of a healthy balanced diet. They do a lot for us. They are our body`s preferred source of fuel, same with our brain. They help our digestion and they actually just make us feel good.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So then, what are we doing wrong then? Because a lot of people, you know, I guess obviously probably shouldn`t have carbs before bed, but where are we going wrong?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. So really, when it comes to carbs, it is just about the quality of carbs that you`re picking and the quantity that you`re consuming. That`s really where a lot of people go wrong over consuming things like refined carbs, sweets, white breads, things like that.\par \par \uc2 But if you focus on portions, choose more complex carbs.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What`s a good carb? Give me a couple of example.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Fruits, vegetables, whole grains, oatmeal, things like that, you should be in the clear.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. So myth number two, fresh produce is healthier than frozen or canned. What would you say?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: No. This is not true at all. In fact, sometimes your frozen or canned options can be actually more nutritious. There was a study done that found that vegetables can lose 15% to 55% of their vitamin C by the time they`re picked, by the time they make it to your grocery shelf. So frozen, they typically flash freeze right on the spot. So you`re packing a lot more nutrients. And they`re actually a little bit better for your wallet too.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I was just about to say there`s a cost benefit as well.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. Less food waste, oftentimes they are more budget friendly. And then you just have some good things on hand if you want to make a smoothie, a salad, things like that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely. OK, our next myth, this is a good one. Plant milk is healthier than dairy milk. What say ye?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: All right. Well, this is not always the case. This one really comes down just to preference because you know, now it just seems like there`s a million different plant milks out there. There are options galore, which is great, but cow`s milk is still a really great option.\par \par \uc2 One cup of skim milk packs in 10 grams of protein and a quarter of your daily calcium needs. Whereas plant milk like an almond milk has one gram of protein per serving.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know what it is, I guess also just depends on, you know, whether you`re lactose intolerant, you know, if you have a dietary issue.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. So this one -- one is not better than the other, it`s just going to come down to choice. And if you are picking a plant milk, you want to pick varieties that have low or no added sugar, and ones that are more fortified with things like calcium, vitamin D that you might not be getting if you`re picking that --\par \par \uc2 JONES: I will admit that the plant milks, I didn`t realize some of them do have a lot of sugar.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Some do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I just didn`t realize that.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Some do. So be a savvy reader.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Let`s talk about fat there. Remember for a while everything was low fat this, low fat that.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: So the nutrition or the myth number four all fat is bad. Obviously it`s not.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:05]\par \par \uc2 MINNO: It`s not. And thank goodness that low fat craze in the `90s and thereafter is over because we know now that fats, most importantly, mono and polyunsaturated fats are really good for us. It is still American Heart Month, and we want to get those fats in from things like avocados, nuts, seeds, olive oils, and even our fatty fish to help protect our heart boost that HDL, which is our good cholesterol, and lower LDL, which is our bad cholesterol.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Sounds good. And I think sometimes when you`re eating, you just don`t know which one is right.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Exactly. So we do want to avoid certain fats, there are facts called saturated fats, that the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  says we should stay at or below 13 grams a day --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh wait, that`s good advice.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: -- for a hearth healthy diet.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Si when you get a package is something flipped over the back.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And if you see saturated fats, how many grams did you say it should be layered?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: 13, approximately around 13 per day.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Per day.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: So if you have four ounces of kind of lean ground beef, you`re already at that 13 mark. So you want to be a real savvy label reader.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK, all right. Myth number five soy, I`m actually interested in what you`re going to say. Soy increases risk for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Yes. You know, I hear this one all the time.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Me too.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: And it`s still very common and abundant. So this myth really stems from the fact that soy contains something called phytoestrogens. And we know that estrogen can sometimes be linked to an increase in breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: But the estrogens in soy are very different than mammalian estrogens, ones that we have in our body, and they act differently. And in fact, they can be protective against things like breast cancer, because what we`ve seen in Asian countries where they consume more soy, is that breast cancer risk is actually much lower.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Does it depend on the product or what kind of soy you`re consuming? No?\par \par \uc2 MINNO: Not really. And again, the \uc2 American Heart Association\uc2  said 25 grams of soy protein per day can actually be heart protective. So you can get your tofu, your (inaudible), your soy milk, your edamame at a sushi restaurant, right? Lots of really great options to pack some good soy protein.\par \par \uc2 JONES: There you go. So you don`t have to avoid it. Nora, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MINNO: That`s OK. Thank you, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Next, a remarkable couple who made their dreams come true, opening a unique restaurant here in New York during the pandemic. How it`s become one of the hottest tickets in town, we`ll take you there. But first, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DREYER: We are back with this morning`s Plaza Pics. Kathy and her family, they`re visiting us from tower Minnesota to celebrate Kathy`s 63rd birthday.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I love Minnesotans. I had fun getting to know David and his mom Bibi. They`re from Raleigh, North Carolina and joining a mother-son trip to New York. The cutest.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love that. And here I am with Ginny and her son Tyler from Middleton, Maryland. Every morning before school, they watch the TODAY Show together.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Oh that`s so sweet.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, cute.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: OK. We want to turn out to our discover Black Heritage Series. And this morning a young married couple making waves in the restaurant industry here in New York.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Kevol and Ria Graham have a remarkable story when it comes to their Caribbean-inspired restaurant. They opened Kokomo in July of 2020, four months into the COVID locked down, and right before their second child. Can you imagine?\par \par \uc2 [08:50:06]\par \par \uc2 Now, it is one of the hottest restaurants in Brooklyn and I paid a visit to find out their recipe for success.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Kevol and Ria Graham are living a life they never could have imagined.\par \par \uc2 KEVOL GRAHAM, KOKOMO OWNER: I love hospitality. I never thought that I would have my own restaurant. But as we look back, we kind of see that every step that we took was actually leading us to this moment.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Today, they are the parents of three young children and will celebrate five years of marriage. But it`s their journey to this point running Kokomo, a popular pan-Caribbean restaurant with Indian and Asian flavors that combines their passion for food and hospitality.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Clearly, you both had some kind of restaurant background --\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: (Inaudible) on nightlife and then became our roving chefs. The guys that I grew up with.\par \par \uc2 RIA GRAHAM, KOKOMO OWNER: At the time, I was a marketing manager for a Caribbean restaurant as well.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): They were soon married and hesitantly decided their next move together would be a restaurant. So they took the social media documenting everything about acquiring and opening a restaurant. As they were just about to open, the pandemic hit. Against all odds, they officially opened in the summer of 2020.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): How challenging was it?\par \par \uc2 R. GRAHAM: I would say it was like mental warfare, because you really did not know what to expect. But I think because we were so open about our feelings to our social media, people wanted to genuinely support.\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: Something that`s very inspirational, because Ria was a part of every step during pregnancy that motivated me to even work harder.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): The couple describes the menu as elevated Caribbean fare, served up in an atmosphere that feels like the Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 R. GRAHAM: I would say if you`re looking for a vibe, come here.\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: To represent all different parts of the Caribbean. We definitely appeal to all ethnicities, you know, whether it`s Afro Caribbean, Latin Caribbean, you know, Asian Caribbean.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Did you get inspiration for different recipes? I mean, how did you do it?\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: We were very passionate about like bringing out things that we grew up on, you know, tapping into our Caribbean heritage.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Do you feel like you are blazing a trail?\par \par \uc2 R. GRAHAM: Real trailblazers for us are the grandmas, man, because there`s nothing like a Caribbean grandma in that kitchen showing her stuff. And now we`re just trying to modernize it and just make it into something fresh and new.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Some of their hottest Caribbean dishes are on flatbread.\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: (Inaudible) flatbread or any given day, because it`s a -- it`s something that we reinvented because I think everybody comes in and like they can get that anywhere. It serve with shrimp, foxtail, chicken.\par \par \uc2 R. GRAHAM: Once we saw that brick oven, we knew we had to do O to Brooklyn by having a brick oven pizza crust, and then we`d have on top of it a tomato (inaudible) sauteed shrimp with ackee, which is the national food of Jamaica.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): I had to give the food a try.\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: So this is our Rasta balls.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: Crockett fry Italian Alfredo sauce that we infuse with some jerk seasoning. Aioli only on top.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Can I try it?\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: Of course. The flavors bursting in your mouth.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh my gosh. This alone would have me coming back.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): As they embrace this family run business, they reflect on this moment in time.\par \par \uc2 R. GRAHAM: Everything that you need to succeed is within you.\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: We come from very humble beginnings. Our parents struggled, I know how hard, excuse me. I know how hard they worked to leave a path for myself. In heart, we as parents worked for us to be where we are. I take nothing for granted. And you can achieve anything you want if only you believe in yourself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONES: They are two of my new favorite people. So I`ll be texting you guys and you`ll be sick of me.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 K. GRAHAM: Can I come?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Sunday (inaudible) things. So Kev and Ria say their parents also helped them run the restaurant. In the spring, they`re opening up another restaurant nearby. They`re calling it Ox Kale (ph) specializing in innovative Caribbean cuisine that`s healthy. Isn`t that -- I`m telling you, I told them, I`m like if you can make it healthy and yummy --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Is that (inaudible) speaks for the rest of the menu?\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: This is --\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, they also make their own in house coconut rum.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Wow, OK.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Merry Christmas.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 (Inaudible)\par \par \uc2 JONES: So there you go. Cheers. What did you just say?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Chill. I will talk about during the break.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): OK. Coming up on Hoda and Jenna, actress Rosario Dawson plus a manicures to the stars which will be sharing three hot new nail trends.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: But first in our 3rd Hour, a couple doing the impossible making bagels even better.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yum.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: See, I told you.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: The most delicious food you`ve ever had. You`ll have that after your local news.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Cheers.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS AND WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230220ej2k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc44}{\*\bkmkend toc44}\b \uc2 Adrianna Brach Is Here With Products To Cover All Your Travel Needs\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Adrianna Brach \par \uc2 5,224 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 February 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Back now, 8:52. And you`re in luck if you`re counting down through your next vacation. The theme of TODAY`s Shop All Day is just that, and it`s streaming show it`s "Ready, Set, Travel." Adrianna Brach is here with some more. She`s the Shop TODAY editorial director. So let`s put the QR code up, so we know what everyone can shop for.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANNA BRACH, SHOP TODAY EDITORIAL DIRECTOR: If we could only have one piece