c2 . And it`s so cute.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Under $20?\par \par \uc2 BRACH: Yes. It`s like $19.98. It`s so great because this is a throw and go piece. You can wear all season long --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Use as a bathing suit cover up.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: Cover up, jean jacket.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ll get to this in a second but speaking of bathing suits, this the suit you say we need.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: This is the suit.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What is that?\par \par \uc2 BRACH: It`s got tummy control.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: This is a game changer. And you`re going to want to get your bathing suits right now because these sell out all season long. They come in and out of stocks. So just get it now --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Flattens the tummy.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: Flattens, comes in a bunch of sizes. Flattering on all shapes and sizes, really great color assortment. And you can never go wrong with a pair of white sneakers.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: If you`re looking to upgrade, these are also under $20.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: And these are kind of like your three essentials.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: This is also from \uc2 Walmart\uc2 . It`s the same line as the dress.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You found some good stuff.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: Yes, super affordable.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Girl, you`re on it.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re on it, all right. We`re going to have more travel hacks coming up in our next hour. And make sure you check out Shop All Day, "Ready, Set, Travel" that`s on TODAY All Day starting at 10:00 A M. Adrianna, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BRACH: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back after your local news. Have a good day, guys.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS AND WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC TODAY WEATHER ANCHOR: This morning on the "3rd Hour of TODAY," deep sea discovery. New haunting video of the "Titanic" captured nearly 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It was really literally entering a preserved museum, and the deeper you got into the ship, the more preserved it was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Why it`s just now being released and what researchers found inside the doomed ship.\par \par \uc2 Then, later, a "TODAY`s Checklist" for men and women. How to take care of your hair and what to do if you`re losing it.\par \par \uc2 Plus, an icon in the house. NBA legend Kareem Abdul-Jabbar is here. How does he really feel about his all-time scoring record being broken and his important heart health message?\par \par \uc2 And Taye Diggs joining us live in studio with big news for fans of his hit show, today, Thursday, February 16, 2023.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza, this is the "3rd Hour of TODAY."\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST: Good morning and welcome to the "3rd Hour of TODAY." I`m Sheinelle here with our whole team. The gang`s all here this morning. Al and Craig.\par \par \uc2 First of all, can we just say, I was downstairs with everybody in the concourse, is what we call it here, and we look up at the monitor --\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- and you guys were having like a lovefest or something. What was happening?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, it was the "Titanic." You know, Joe Fryer, who`s about to do a story about the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And we had the video of Jack and Rose. And I just -- I was inspired.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I was moved by the spirit.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, did you just -- you got moved by the spirit and you just decided to just --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes, just kind of like --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: It was fun.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: King of the world.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it. Well, so --\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY CO-HOST AND METEOROLOGIST: I mean, you guys have also recreated the pottery scene from "Ghost."\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: The "Ghost" scene.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, "Ghost" scene.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: So --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We need to do a mash-up.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: No, we are not going to recreate the "Dirty Dancing" scene.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: No. No, we`re not.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Come on.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: No.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s -- that would be the trilogy.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I mean, so you`re so light, I could probably do it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You could probably do it now. All right. Well, to be continued. We`ll try to make that happen and do like a mash-up. But there is a reason we`ve been talking about the "Titanic" this morning. Here`s the latest, some never-before-seen footage. It was shot back in 1986. It`s just been released.\par \par \uc2 Now, at the time, this was the first expedition to actually find the Titanic. Look at this.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Look at this.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The ship is so cool. It`s gone unseen at the bottom of the Atlantic for more than 70 years. So, we have NBC`s Joe Fryer here. He`s following the story for us this morning.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, the chandelier.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s like so beautiful. Why are we just now seeing it, you know, now?\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS NOW ANCHOR: Yes. So, there was this tiny little independent film made 25 years ago called "Titanic." Perhaps you heard of it. And it`s just now starting to get some buzz. This is the 25th anniversary of the Titanic. So, it kind of to commemorate that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: This video is finally being released. I mean, let me give you the background here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: First of all, remember, the Titanic went down in 1912. It took decades to find. It wasn`t until 1985 that a crew was able to find it, more than 12,000 feet below the surface. Robert Ballard, who you heard from at the beginning of the show, he was on that crew.\par \par \uc2 So, months later, back then in 1986, they went down, they took this footage. It`s really the first-time humans laid eyes on the wreckage of the Titanic. But the footage, basically, most it has remained private until now. It was not released publicly. It`s 81 minutes. It`s uncut.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: It`s unnarrated. And you can really just stare at it.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Which is really exciting because now so much of it is disintegrating. We can`t see anything new. So, we think all of our new Titanic stuff, we can`t get anymore. And, wow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Boom.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: It`s like you see chandeliers, you see things from 1986 that were still in such good condition.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes, you see that. And the chandelier is really one of the most haunting images.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: But you see the front of the bow of the ship. You know, you see the first officer`s cabin. There are so many details you see. And things that look different, you know, back in the `80s than they even do now because it is disintegrating. There`s this metal-eating bacteria that`s actually sort of turning the remnants of Titanic --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That sounds really sci-fi.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: I know. -- to dust. And so, you get to see all these --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, it turns it to dust.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: -- incredible images, the promenade window. And it really gives you a look into early 20th century life. And this footage probably does a better job of it than some of the high-definition cameras that have gotten down there more recently. Robert Ballard says it was like a tombstone when he remembers seeing it for the first time.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And one of the most haunting images for him, he remembers seeing, you know, a mother`s shoes next to a baby`s shoes. I mean, really just some images that stick with you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Joe, that was haunting.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s interesting. Joe Fryer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thanks, Joe.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. So, another big story we`re watching this morning, 2024 presidential race starting to take shape. We`ve got former South Carolina governor and \uc2 U.N\uc2 . ambassador, Nikki Haley, officially in. Craig.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: In fact, Mr. Roker had the chance to catch up with the former ambassador right after her first campaign event yesterday in Charleston, South Carolina. We talked about her decision, some of the biggest campaign issues, and the support she`s receiving from her family.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): Your former boss said that you had a hard time making the decision, said that you would never run against the president, he did a great job, he was the best president in my lifetime, his words, told Nikki to follow her heart. She`s polling at 1 percent. Not a bad start. Your response to that?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.N. AMBASSADOR: What I`ll tell you is I think he was a good president. There was so much that he did that was good. What I`m talking about --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Well, then why not support him this time?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Because I think we need to have a new generation, for Republicans. We have lost the last seven out of eight popular votes for president. It means Republicans need to do something different.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Is it the message or is it the messengers?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I think it`s always both. It`s always got to be someone who really does listen to the people, that has their pulse on the ground of what real families are going through. But I also think it`s how you communicate. I don`t want just Republicans with me, I want everybody with me because I believe that everybody wants to be proud about America.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Let`s talk about abortion for a moment. A senior senator from South Carolina, Lindsey Graham, has proposed a federal ban on abortion. Would you sign that?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: We need consensus on this. Our goal --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Consensus on abortion? Would you support a federal ban?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I think we -- not a full out federal ban, because I don`t think that`s been put on the table. I think what Lindsey Graham has put on the table is 15 weeks. And I think, if we`re looking at 15 weeks, what we need to understand is that we are not OK with abortion up until the time of birth. And so, we should at least decide when it is OK.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: A decade ago, you supported significantly scaling back Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid. Should those social safety nets be scaled back?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Should we reform those entitlements? Yes. But should we take anything from people who have already been promised? No.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: When you say reform, what do you mean?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You go and everybody that`s been promised money, they get it. We don`t take it from anybody. But these new ones coming in, they know what`s going to be broke by the time they get there. Those are the ones we fix. The younger generation, we go and do a new system on how they`re going to get it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, you would phase out Social Security --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Not phasing --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: and Medicare and Medicaid, as we know it. As we know it now, you would phase it out?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You would change it. You`re not phasing it out.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Well, it seems like semantics, if you`re saying --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You are saying change it out.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, you want to reform it?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I want to reform it, yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): A plan for the future as Nikki Haley looks at a long campaign trail ahead. But she isn`t doing it alone, her husband Michael and kids Rena and Nalin are by her side.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: You can`t do this without your family. You just can`t. And so, to have them there for support, it`s everything.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (on camera): We`ve got a wedding that`s about to happen in the Haley family. So, are you helping plan a wedding and a presidential campaign simultaneously?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: We are. But this has been really sweet. We`ve had a lot of fun with this.\par \par \uc2 RENA HALEY, NIKKE HALEY`S DAUGHTER: We have.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I mean, between like picking songs and picking flowers and all of those things, it`s kind of a sweet spot to be able to do this.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Did you go to your family before you decided to run for president and say, you know what, this is what I think I want to do?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I did.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: How`d that conversation go?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I did. Nalin has always been very involved in everything I do politically. He, of course, was like, yes, you should do this. And Rena`s response was, why do you want to live in someone else`s house again? Literally, it wasn`t what it was.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (voiceover): Haley`s son not only encouraged his mom to run, he`s even working on her campaign this summer.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN (on camera): Do you ever provide unsolicited political advice to mom?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Every day, all day.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Really?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: By text message, by phone call, and everything in between. Nonstop.\par \par \uc2 NALIN HALEY, NIKKI HALEY`S SON: I`m her biggest supporter, but also her biggest critic.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: It`s very true. It`s very true.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: What kind of mom was Nikki Haley?\par \par \uc2 NALIN HALEY: A tough one, but also a very loving one and caring one.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Rena?\par \par \uc2 RENA HALEY: She was tough but loving. She always made sure to show up to any events we had, came in and supported us, but we always had our house rules we had to follow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Were you surprised at all when she said, you know what, I think I want to do this?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HALEY, HALEY`S HUSBAND: No, I wasn`t surprised.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I would imagine from time to time you would see or hear something that`s said or written about your wife and you --\par \par \uc2 M. HALEY: You know, when you wade in this water, you know, it`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I think it`s tougher on them than it is on me. And they do take it personally. And politics is a blood sport. And it`s a shame that it is, but it is. They know that the key is, you know, you have your faith, you trust in the love we have as a family, and this, too, shall pass. That`s the beautiful thing about campaigns, is there`s a start and an end. And the goal is just pray every day and let`s get through it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, you know, I`ve covered ambassador for a number of years now, and when I saw the kids yesterday, it was pretty interesting because I`ve seen, you know, the kids off and on for years. In fact, I think we`ve got a picture from her first inauguration as governor.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, my goodness.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, those are the two kids there.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: All grown up.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All grown.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Look how they`ve grown.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Did you say, I remember when you were this big?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I did. Because I`m that old guy now.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Which kids love that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes. Of course.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know, I know.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: But it`s funny because, you know, you know, she`s obviously planning a presidential campaign, but the ambassador also a wedding as well. Her daughter is getting married in April.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I wonder which is harder?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: She said -- she sort of alluded to the fact that -- at this point, sort of equally.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Equally difficult.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Equally difficult. So --\par \par \uc2 JONES: That was good. It`s a good interview.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: That was great.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Terrific.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, for sure.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: All right. Well, still to come, it`s "TODAY`s Checklist." We`re talking hair health. How to take care of the --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I`m out.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: -- hair you have and what to do if you start to lose it. Is it too late for \uc2 Al Roker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: We`ll find out.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s not true.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: It is.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Then, later, the one and only Kareem Abdul-Jabbar.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: He is here in Studio 1A. He`s going to open up at his own heart scare. And we are going to talk about that historic moment when Lebron broke his scoring record. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JONES: This morning on "TODAY`s Checklist," we are taking a closer look at hair and scalp health with some of the best ways to care for the hair that you have, and to help men and women struggling with hair loss. This is such an important topic. So many people are dealing with balding or maybe they`re thinning a little bit. So, joining us now, we have plastic surgeon and hair loss specialist, Dr. Michael Wolfeld. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 DR. MICHAEL WOLFELD, PLASTIC SURGEON AND HAIR LOSS SPECIALIST: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: This is a hot topic.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: It`s a very hot topic.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. So, let`s first start with -- I mean, even just breakage.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I guess, before you get to those other things, just how do you protect the hair you have? Let`s talk about that.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Right. So, different things may damage your hair. And here`s some very important things to keep mind of. So, if you`re going to brush your hair, be gentle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Anything too rough or strenuous can maybe damage and break your hair.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: And you know there`s a thing going around where they`re telling girls, if you want your hair to be thicker and longer, to brush it. So, kids are just brush, brush, brush. And adults are brush, brush, brush and you`re making it worse.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Overbrushing can be harmful.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: You want to avoid tension on your hair. So, any tight hairstyles are no good.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Avoid any very hot treatments, hot hair blowers are not very good. And if you have split ends, it`s good to trim them so they don`t propagate and affect the rest of your hair strands.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. So, now, let`s talk about Craig thinning.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s an issue.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Sheinelle just mentioned it, thinning, shedding as well.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Some folks, you know, you`re in the shower, look down on the drain, you get a little concern. At what point should you actually go and see a doctor like yourself.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Yes. So, if you noticed your hair thinning and you`re losing more hair, more hair falling out that`s a good time to come in and see a specialist and to see what may be causing that. Now, genetics is one of the most common causes of hair loss.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Is it true -- I feel like years ago I heard that if you lose your hair -- or if your mother`s father was bald, then there`s a great chance that you`re going to be bald.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s true. I`ve heard that.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Is there any truth to that or is that a myth?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: That`s not true. That`s a myth.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: So, it can`t from either side of the family. So, really have to look at both sides of the family to see where you may fall.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, besides genetics, where else contributes?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Yes. So, there`s stress. Any stress around your body, whether it`s recent procedures or illnesses can cause a shedding of hair. There`s autoimmune diseases that can cause your hair to fall out. And also, vitamin deficiencies. That`s something that you should be aware of as well.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Certain bloodwork that folks should be asking for when they go to see their doctors?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Yes, absolutely. So, you know, we look at vitamin D, iron, zinc, but especially hormone levels may be important.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Especially for women, young women and older women. Thyroid deficiencies or overactive thyroid, that can also cause hair loss as well.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK. I`ve already learned a lot. Mr. Roker`s --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Al needs to look.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, yes. I -- well, the basics. You see God only made so many perfect heads. The rest he covered with hair. So, you`ve got to take care of your scalp, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: That`s right. That`s right. So, taking care of your scalp is really important. So, just in your daily life, from the products you may use, from sweating, from just grime from the environment may build up in your scalp so you want to really cleans your scalp.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: You may want to exfoliate your scalp. Massaging can help to really clear your scalp.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Do you really need all of those fancy shampoos and stuff? Can you just -- I mean, what`s the deal?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Really just a simple shampoo that cleanses your scalp and you can massage your scalp. There`s a lot of things available over the counter, in the office. We have something called -- it`s a HydraFacial, which is a facial for your scalp that can -- it`s Keravive treatment that can really cleans the scalp, exfoliate it, and really get your scalp looking -- and your hair looking much nicer.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. Thanks, Mike.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: All right. Do you hit a point where it`s too late or is regrowth a possibility?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: So, when we talk about regrowth, if you have hair and it`s thinning, there are options and treatments that can really help to thicken your hair and help to preserve your hair.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK. What kind of treatments are there?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: So, there`s some over-the-counter treatments and there`s some in-office treatments. So, over-the-counter, there`s hair supplements.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Which we have some here.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: One example is Nutrafol, which is -- this is it right here.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: It`s an over-the-counter vitamin supplement.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, so you ingest it?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: You ingest it.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: It`s oral. It`s all-natural. And it`s -- you can get it over- the-counter and it can work. There`s also Rogaine, that`s -- which is Minoxidil, that`s over-the-counter as well.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: For men and women.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: For men and women.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: It`s a topical solution or a foam. They also have now oral Minoxidil that is a prescription pill. That -- the oral is off-label but it may help. So, if you`re someone that may have other medical illnesses, such as heart problems and blood pressure.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Right. Check with your doctor.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Check with your doctor beforehand.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK. Then there`s injections too, right?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: There are. So, there`s what`s called PRP, which is Platelet- Rich Plasma injections. That`s really a treatment --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, it actually works.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Yes. It`s a treatment in the office.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Look at that.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: And it uses were blood and the growth factors from that and it can really help to thicken your hair.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Does it hurt?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: It`s slightly uncomfortable --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: -- but it`s manageable.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: But --\par \par \uc2 JONES: And do you have to keep doing it for it to work? That`s the only thing with all of these things, like once --\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- you stop, do you lose your hair again?\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: That`s right. So, the key with any of these treatments is that you have to continue using it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: For the rest of your life?\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Well --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: If you want hair.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Well, yes. If you want to keep your hair.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Or if you want to look dashing like Al here, you don`t have to.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Or any of our crew members.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We got lots of them.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Here. OK. Here, here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You know, over here.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Mason`s also --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, if you notice --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, my God, you`re right.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We stress you guys out.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Mason. Hey, look out Taye Diggs. Check out Taye Diggs. And look at Kareem. Come on. That`s the deal here. That`s what we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: It`s the cool thing to do.\par \par \uc2 JONES: There you go.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Dr. Wolfeld, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Dr. Wolfeld, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. WOLFELD: Thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Coming up, the aforementioned legend himself in the house. Kareem Abdul-Jabbar is going to join us live, spreading an important message during this American Heart Month. And we`re going to, of course, talk a little basketball, too.\par \par \uc2 And then, Taye Diggs also here. Fans of "All American," you may want to cover your ears.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Spoiler alert.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes, it`s going to be a spoiler alert. We`re going to find out about the future of Coach Billy Baker when the "3rd Hour of TODAY" continues.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I like that hat.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230225ej2g000dz\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc45}{\*\bkmkend toc45}\b \uc2 Nationwide Marches On Anniversary Of Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Kate Snow, Marissa Para \par \uc2 579 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATE SNOW: Today, the landmark abortion ruling, Roe v. Wade turns 50 years old. But the controversy surrounding last year`s reversal or Roe is heating up once again, with marches across the country. And now, more than six months after that decision, where does the battle over abortion rights stand? Marissa Para reports from Madison, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We will win. I believe that we will win.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, on the 50th anniversary of Roe v. Wade, abortion rights activists drumming up defiance, moving their main women`s march bigger than Roe, from the capitol to the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This time, we move the National March to Madison in order to kick off our newfound campaign to the states, which is where battle is taking place.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (on camera): So, I have the main march here in Madison, Wisconsin, that is because we are just weeks away from a primary judicial election and a state Supreme Court seat is up for grabs. And if it is filled with a pro- abortion rights candidate, then the Wisconsin abortion ban could be overturned.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): Sister marches in hundreds of cities across the country, just days after the National March for life against abortion and just seven months after Roe v. Wade was overturned in a five-four decision, sending both sides of the debate into action.\par \par \uc2 The state of play, a murky patchwork. Roughly two dozen states have either a total abortion ban or are close to it. Some state laws changed immediately, like Wisconsin`s abortion ban. Some, like neighboring Illinois, later passing abortion protection. And last fall, historically red states, Kansas and Kentucky, voted down antiabortion ballot measures. Today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  once again calling to codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , U.S. VICE PRESIDENT: Congress must pass a bill that protects freedom and liberty.\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voiceover): But with Republicans in control of the House, the federal abortion debate remains in a deadlock, making mobilizing on a local level more important than ever.\par \par \uc2 From Madison, Wisconsin, Marissa Parra, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SNOW: Still ahead, a goodbye at Graceland, the moving tributes from celebrities and fans today for Lisa Marie Presley. How her mother remembered her.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:50:00]\par \par \uc2 SNOW: A violent protest in Downtown Atlanta is under investigation tonight. It happened late Saturday. A group demonstrating against a new police training center got out of hand. Police say some people smashed windows and set a patrol car on fire. Others were carrying explosive devices. Six people were arrested and charged with domestic terrorism, arson, and criminal damage.\par \par \uc2 A small plane made an emergency landing northwest of Houston Today. Take a look at this. The single engine aircraft reported that it lost power and it went down, crashing on top of an 18-wheeler. The plane then caught fire. The most astonishing thing about all this, the pilot and the truck driver were not injured.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230209ej1m000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc46}{\*\bkmkend toc46}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for January 22, 2023, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kristen Welker, Peter Baker, Kimberly Atkins Stohr,  Brendan Buck \par \uc2 10,780 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:19]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST (voice over): This Sunday, another document discovery. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched President Biden`s Delaware home on Friday and found more classified materials. Six documents, including some from his time as a senator, were found. Biden insists he did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: I think you`re going to find there`s nothing there. I have no regrets.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Plus, warning signs. American politics teetering on the edge, from the court to Congress, to special counsel probes and rising extremist threats. After losing an election and claiming it was rigged, a Republican candidate is arrested and charged in connection with shootings at the homes of four Democratic lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): An attack on an elected official is an attack on democracy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: New Mexico, just the latest example of election denialism sparking political violence.\par \par \uc2 And, post-Roe. Fifty years after the landmark Supreme Court decision granting a constitutional right to abortion, the debate over reproductive rights has shifted, with the ruling now overturned.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): We didn`t learn anything after overturning of Roe v. Wade. We buried our head in the sand.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But if you think either side is open to finding a middle ground, think again.\par \par \uc2 My guests this morning, Democratic Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia and Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Plus, lasting impact.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT \uc2 WILLIAM JEFFERSON CLINTON\uc2 : I did not have sexual relations with that woman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Lewinsky scandal and its consequences still influencing American political life 25 years later.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker; Republican strategist Brendan Buck; New York Times chief White House correspondent Peter Baker; and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for the \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. Boy, our democracy and our politics, they feel like we`re at a breaking point. We have rising political violence fueled by election denialism, economic brinksmanship in Congress, a Supreme Court relationships between the justices so strained after an unprecedented leak, they have yet to issue an opinion this term. And trust in the court from the public never been lower.\par \par \uc2 And this morning we`re waking up to reports of another mass shooting with at least 10 dead at a lunar new year event in Monterey Park, California. Details are still coming in. No need to jump to conclusions, but, let`s be honest, these events do not exactly bring comfort to our current political system. We don`t handled these well or solve these problems.\par \par \uc2 And if all of this wasn`t enough, adding to this instability, we have both the current and former presidents under investigation. And on Saturday night the White House revealed more classified information was found at Biden`s Wilmington home, after what was a remarkable 13-hour \uc2 FBI\uc2 -led search on Friday of the home of a sitting president. It wasn`t a subpoena; it was a cooperative search. It was done with the president`s legal team acknowledging it. They said they offered to provide prompt access to his home, and that six more items with classified markings were indeed found, including documents from Biden`s time as a senator as well as vice president.\par \par \uc2 And this \uc2 FBI\uc2  search comes just days after Biden said he had no regrets about how he`s handled the issue of classified documents and how he`s talked to the public about it. But it`s likely to prompt more comparisons between the current and former presidents, though of course Mr. Trump and his advisers are also being investigated for obstructing their probe.\par \par \uc2 Then we have the issue of election denialism. That`s been fueled by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And, sadly, it appears it`s given some of his supporters a permission slip to commit political violence. The case in New Mexico this week is just the latest in a string of violent incidents rooted in election denialism, from the assault on Brazil`s capitol by supporters of the former president there, where Trump allies actually helped sow doubts about those election results. We had the attack on \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  just days before the midterm elections as well.\par \par \uc2 And then, finally, we have this manufactured crisis here in Washington, this escalating rhetoric over the debt limit. It`s a reminder of just how fractured right now the Republican Party is, because it`s Republicans wanting to have this fight in an attempt to re-brand themselves as the party of fiscal discipline, if you will, after they oversaw an explosive rise in the national debt under President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So Republicans are trying to use this debate to try to unify them around an issue, cutting spending. Democrats, of course, now see an opportunity to paint House Republicans as extremist. The reality is, of course, at the end of the day, this debt limit is going to get raised. We just don`t know how it gets done. Even Republican Senate Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  concedes this point.\par \par \uc2 SENATE MINORITY LEADER MITCH MCCONNELL, (R) KENTUCKY: America must never default on its debt. It never has and it never will.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Should we be concerned about a financial crisis?\par \par \uc2 [09:05:01]\par \par \uc2 MCCONNELL: No, I would not be concerned about a financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Senator Manchin, welcome back...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Chuck, it`s always good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I want to get into the debt ceiling. I want to get into all this stuff. But I -- we got some developments overnight with this classified documents, an \uc2 FBI\uc2  search. The White House said it was coordinated with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , but we now have an \uc2 FBI\uc2  searching of former President Trumps`s...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now we have an \uc2 FBI\uc2  search into President Biden`s residence.\par \par \uc2 What`s your assessment of how the president has handled this situation?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, I mean, it`s just hard to believe that -- that in the United States of America we have a former president and the current president basically in the same situation. How does this happen? You know, only thing I can tell you, Chuck, is when I go into the SCIF with the secured documents, they always ask, "Are you clean," when you walk out. They want to make sure you`re not carrying anything out. You know, and there might be a mistake. You might just put it in your other papers. But you double-check right there.\par \par \uc2 So that -- to be held accountable and responsible is what we all are. And to put those in unsecured spaces is irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you see similarities or do you see more differences in how President Trump versus how President Biden`s handled this?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I`m not going to make that decision, but I think that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  did the right thing by putting a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You do?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: And I think that we should wait until the special counsel rather than making this a political circus. Let them find out the facts. What, was one more damaging? Are they both about the same, did not cause any problem? Or is one more reckless and irresponsible than the other?\par \par \uc2 I can`t answer that question, but I think the special counsel will do a better job than the politicians and the political circus that`s going to follow.\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Biden said he had no regrets on how he handled this. Do you have any advice for him on how he should handle this...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Oh, I think he should have a lot of regrets, yeah. I would...\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are they?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I would think that. And I`d say, whoever`s responsible -- I mean, if I hold people accountable and I use, whether it be my staff or my -- you know, my -- my staff, that we`re doing this, and I`m looking at it, then I`m going to hold someone accountable. But, basically, the buck stops with me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you think he should be out there, and "Look, I -- I messed up."\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: That`s all. Just say...\par \par \uc2 TODD: "Maybe I didn`t do it" -- just say it?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: "I made a mistake."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just fall on your sword here?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We`re all human.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We make mistakes. I can tell you, I don`t think anyone intended - - he sure didn`t intend for it to fall into wrong hands and use it against our country. I know they didn`t intend that to happen. Could it have happened? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 And, yeah, you just might as well say, "Listen, it`s irresponsible. It was something we should have had a better check and balance on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, former President Trump defied a subpoena. So, in that sense, the way each has handled is different. Do you acknowledge that?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Absolutely...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... much different than the other. One`s -- one`s saying, "OK, I hope I didn`t make any mistakes. I hope no one was compromised. I hope we didn`t hurt our country."\par \par \uc2 And the other says, "Oh, I`m -- believe me."\par \par \uc2 Well, you know what?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 What they said, verify. You have to verify?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Trust, but verify.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Trust, but verify. Let`s find out. And that`s what the special counsel is...\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s what you want here? Both special counsels to, sort of, resolve...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: First of all, every one of us in our life have to be held accountable and responsible for our actions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: People want accountability. And they want, basically, when you`re held accountable, are you responsible or not?\par \par \uc2 If you are, would you -- can you fix that? Did you make a mistake? Fine. You know...\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s what you think, the president needs to get out and there just get in front of it.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Cicero said, "To err is human." You`re a human being. You`re going to make mistakes. Did you intend to make it? Did you intend to harm somebody? Did you intend to basically do an irresponsible thing?\par \par \uc2 I don`t think, hopefully, either one of them did.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: But it sure -- sure turned out to be irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about the debt ceiling. You`re -- as always, you`re trying to find a compromise and middle ground.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- I know your instinct here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 But why should Republicans get the benefit of the doubt on the debt ceiling here, considering that it`s -- that they`re, sort of, manufacturing a crisis that`s a bit unnecessary right now?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, first of all, if one side thinks that the other one`s more responsible for the debt, at $31.4 trillion, that`s -- that is totally not accurate, and it`s deceptive. We`re all responsible. We`ve got a $31.4 trillion debt. It`s a runaway debt, and no one`s holding themselves accountable. And basically, I think you said it, use the budget process.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been here 12 years. We haven`t had a budget yet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, I -- that`s what I don`t get here.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We haven`t had a budget yet.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s what I question...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yeah, you should.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You want to do this special committee here.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I`m...\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I`m sitting here going, why add more bureaucracy? We have a budget committee. We have two budget committees. We have a joint committee on taxation. We have all these different committees that have already been created to deal with this process. Why can`t we use the congressional bureaucracy that exists?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We have 12 appropriations committees that`s supposed to do their job. Why don`t you basically put a time certain on what you can...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: ... and what can`t do and when you do it.\par \par \uc2 I can`t speak for that. I was a former governor of the State of the West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I was responsible for a balanced-budget amendment and basically staying within the realms of my constitution. So, you know, I met every week; every week like clockwork they walked into my office, on a Tuesday or Wednesday, and sat down and go over it. You`re either going to be -- have to make some cuts now, make some adjustments now so we end the year with a balanced budget...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: ... or a surplus. There`s nothing that holds us accountable -- nothing at all. We can say, "Oh, we`re going to do it." As I`ve said before, 12 years, haven`t had a budget. That`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me -- you want to do this, sort of, you and Senator Romney, to have a committee that deals with the trust fund issues.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:01]\par \par \uc2 But right now neither party wants to touch that. I mean, in that sense, you have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  came out, and certainly Democrats, nobody wants to touch Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, first of all...\par \par \uc2 TODD: So how do you separate those two out and deal with our fiscal...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Why would you scare the bejeezus out of people that basically -- I`m going to say, in West Virginia, I`ve got 60 -percent of my population that that`s all they have is Medicare and Social Security. You think I`m going to go down that path and put them in jeopardy?\par \par \uc2 No, but there are so much other things that -- basically wasteful spending that can be corralled in without scaring the bejeezus, depending on what political side you`re on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about wasteful spending. Because one of the -- the three most hypocritical words I hear are "waste, fraud and abuse."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 Right? Everybody says, "Oh, waste, fraud and abuse."\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: And it`s all there. It`s all there.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK, but -- but waste, fraud and abuse isn`t going to balance a budget, OK? At the end of the day, there`s going to have to be choices that have to be made. What is something that ought to be on -- in the discussion of, you know, maybe we`re spending too much?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, we know we`re spending too much because we`re not balancing our budget...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: ... have more debt. The bottom line is, it`s in the eyes of the beholder. That`s the problem that we have, 535 people say, "Wait a minute, yeah, what you`re doing is wasteful, Chuck; I think you ought to cut that."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: And you`re going to say, "OK, Joe, how about yours?"\par \par \uc2 TODD: But -- but your -- your spending that you think is mandatory, another person thinks is wasteful, right, or abuse?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yeah, and just think, for every dollar -- just get it down -- break it down to the dollar. Is there any savings within that dollar you think that there`s wasteful or abuse that we could at least have a target to set. Is it a penny? Is it five pennies? Is it a nickel?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Where is it?\par \par \uc2 TODD: But here`s what gets lost here, is nobody will put anything on the table. Everybody says, "We`ve got to cut spending." Well, what? And nobody wants to...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Chuck, you hit it dead on the head. The process isn`t working. How come we`re not held accountable to have a -- to have the appropriation bills done at a certain time before the end of the fiscal year?\par \par \uc2 TODD: You tell me.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, that`s what I...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, what does \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  say? What does \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  say?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: You know what happens? It rolls -- it rolls over into an omnibus bill at the end, and everything`s thrown into it, and OK, "Here, we`ve got it, guys, that`s it." It makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what should -- so it sounds like you actually think the debt ceiling is a moment we should use to focus on...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, if you`re going to use the debt ceiling for anything except for theatrics, OK, which is what probably might happen for a while -- we`re going to pass the debt ceiling. You are exactly correct. It has to pass. You know, we have the currency of the -- you know, good faith of the United States dollar and the currency of the world. You just can`t let it default and basically hold us in jeopardy from where we stand in -- in the world -- world order.\par \par \uc2 With that being said, is how do you get to it? Do you use this moment? Do you come to a responsibility? What -- what are we paying for interest now? For 10 years, it was zero; it was funny money. We`re not -- you know, it doesn`t put any burden. We`re just raising debt, but we`re not basically harming how we have to meet that debt through our interest payments. Now we`re talking real money on interest rates. We`re almost up to what our defense budget is, paying in interest.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess I come back to, and I don`t think you have the answer, either, which is, what is the moment to force this confrontation?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: This is a moment -- if Kevin McCarthy coming in -- coming in new, says, "OK, this is -- we`re -- it`s serious, and he takes it from the standpoint -- and he knows...\par \par \uc2 TODD: What does he need to do that you would take him seriously on this?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, you know, Chuck...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know what I mean by that, like, how do you know when he`s being serious and how do you know when he`s playing politics?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well -- well, the bottom line is he has a hell -- a heck of a political hand that`s not -- not very good right now. He`s not -- he`s not holding a lock, if you will. And he has 10 or 12 -- it`s pretty much out there. He has to make a decision on how he wants to govern and how he wants this next two years and this 118th Congress.\par \par \uc2 You know, I just -- it was amazing. I just saw the Ohio legislature -- I don`t know if you paid any attention to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I did, yeah.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: The Ohio legislature, which is Republican controlled, basically chose their new speaker, a Republican, with as many if not more votes from the Democrats. Because they wanted someone they can work with. That`s a coalition. Why can`t we put coalitions together here?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that`s...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Some moderate, centrist Democrats, coming over and working, whoever`s the majority, and saying, "You don`t have to -- you don`t have to bow and kowtow to the extremes."\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t have to work about primaries. A lot of your colleagues have to worry about primaries. Isn`t that why this doesn`t happen?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Let me tell you one thing. I`ve got to be honest with you, Chuck. If it`s all about the election, the next election, that`s the worst thing that can happen to us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You just came from Davos.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yep.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There was a moment -- I don`t know if you realize that went viral -- between you and Senator Sinema. I want to show the moment here. I want to ask you about it. You guys are high-fiving. I think we`ll show it again here.\par \par \uc2 And I think -- it was right after she was talking about the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that what you were high-fiving about?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: That was -- I think -- after that, and I didn`t -- I saw her hand go up, and I said, sure. Because here, the two of us are committed to protecting the filibuster, which I think protects checks and balances on the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 So if you have a Democrat, Democrat, Democrat, president, House and Senate, and you have a strong president, basically, who`s leader of the party, then you don`t have a check and balance. Because I can guarantee you the House and Senate will roll to wherever the president wants.\par \par \uc2 I -- and I`ve said this before. I appreciate the Republican senators and -- under the leadership of -- of Minority Leader, at that time, McConnell -- Majority Leader at that time -- with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  every day beating on him to get rid of the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:05]\par \par \uc2 You`ve got 53-54 Republicans. And he would not. And I appreciate that. And I told Harry Reid we should not have done it when we did it in 2013. But to come back now, the checks and balances aren`t there. It makes -- it forces us to work together. Think what we`ve accomplished in the 117th, in the most divided Congress that we`ve ever had. And we did more substantial bills, I think, is going to be transformational.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think those first two years of Biden and this democratic Congress is going to be historic?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I think it`s going to be transformational and historical, yes. Because here you had bipartisan infrastructure bill we haven`t done for years. You had, then, on top of that, the CHIPS Act, which would bring manufacturing back so we don`t have supply chains that we`re dependent on that aren`t loyal and trustworthy, and then we have the Inflation Reduction Act, which is going to give us -- it`s been maligned. Because this administration basically said this is environmental, environmental, environmental. That bill`s designed to be energy security, Chuck, and energy security is exactly what we need...\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you`re frustrated that the White House won`t say the phrase "energy security."\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: They will not use the word and they have -- and I`m begging you all, please, energy security. We have to have fossil. We do it better and cleaner than anywhere in the world, and we can be energy secured for 10 years and also be able to invest in the technology of the future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is this an agenda you can for re-election on in West Virginia?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Oh, most certainly, because we`re seeing -- right now I`ve got a battery plant coming in; I`ve got basically hydrogen coming in that direction. We`ve got expansion, and we`re raising our coal with carbon capture sequestration. We`ve got basically methane capturing using gas. We have people that are fighting continuously, and you have to have the pipelines to move this product. And it`s going to be needed. If not, you`re going to end up like Europe.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why -- I didn`t want to rub it in to them, but Europe took an approach that they`re going to say, "We`re going to have cap-and-trade, and we`re going to have basically charging you carbon tax. I`ve said I am not - - I am not going to support that and vote for it because I think it`s -- it doesn`t work.\par \par \uc2 So what I took the approach, and basically we wrote this bill, was incentives. And it`s working. And that`s why they were all upset. That`s why the chancellor, and that`s why presidents of other countries were very upset on this bill I`m concerned about.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You run for office in 2024, you going to run as a Democrat?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Chuck, I haven`t made a decision what I`m going to do in 2024. I`ve got two years ahead of me now to do the best I can for the state and for my country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are -- what`s on the table? Is re-election on the table?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Everything`s on the table.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is running for governor on the table, or no?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: No, I -- I`ve done that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That you`ve ruled out?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I`ve done that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So everything on the table, there`s basically only one other thing, the presidency. Is that something you would do outside the Democratic Party if...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: The only thing I can tell you is what I will do is whatever I can, when I make my decision, what I think is the best that I can support and represent the people of West Virginia, but also be true to this country and the Constitution of this country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That sounds like some -- somebody that`s looking for a way into national politics.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, you know, every senator is on a national...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that, but you know where I`m going.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I know where you`re going. And, bottom line is, is I`m not...\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re not telling me no.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I`m telling you that I`m going to do everything I can to make sure that, when I make my decision, I make it based on what`s best, what I think I can do to support and best for my country and my state.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you want President Biden to seek re-election?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: He`ll have to make that decision. I think this -- that`s a personal decision...\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you haven`t decided, if he does, you`re not ready to support him?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I haven`t decided on anything I`m going to do until I see what -- what the lay of the land is going to be at that time. Because this country needs to unite. We need to come back together. We`re not coming together. And it needs to bring somebody that can bring this country together. I think he`s done a good job on so many factions, in so many areas. I think there`s a lot more to be done. I think he`s been pulled to the left too far, and I think I`ve told him that. I`ve been up front with that. That centrist -- center is what the country wants. That`s what the people want in America. They want us to work out of the center.\par \par \uc2 Hey, just because you`re on one side or the other, Republican ideas aren`t always bad, and the Democrats don`t -- don`t have all the answers, and vice versa. Work in that. But don`t be pure to where, "Oh, I`m sorry, that`s a - - that`s a Democrat idea and I`m a Republican; I can`t -- no way I can agree with that." To me, that`s foreign to me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Manchin, there`s a bunch of other things I wanted to get to, but...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I`m sure we will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We will keep doing it, fascinating conversation.\par \par \uc2 So when would you make a decision about 2024?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, I`m not in a hurry. As you know, I`m not...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Filing deadline`s early in 2024, so you`ve got to make this decision...\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, not in West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Not in West Virginia. I`m OK.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve still got a little bit of time.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I`ve got time...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, she says her party has been tone-deaf since Roe was overturned. Is there really any middle ground?\par \par \uc2 Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace of South Carolina joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:21:29]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. For decades opposition to Roe v. Wade was a litmus test in the Republican Party. Now, on the 50th anniversary of that decision and the overturning of the constitutional right to an abortion, Republicans are in an uncomfortable decision. Activists are pushing for tougher abortion restrictions, calling for a national abortion ban in some cases, while the swing voters Republicans need to win in swing districts or statewide in general elections increasingly favor keeping abortion legal.\par \par \uc2 It`s an issue that will continue to be pushed into the headlines for the next year or two by the coming Republican presidential primary and by state legislatures, every one of them convening this calendar year, which have already passed abortion bans into law and are expected to be very active this year, both in limiting abortion rights in red states and expanding them in blue states.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Mace, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 MACE: Good morning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you`ve made your position pretty clear on your discomfort with how the national party`s been handling this so far, those congressional bills and in general where you think the party needs to go.\par \par \uc2 But let`s get some specifics here. When you look at the public`s opinion about Roe, and in some ways Roe has become more popular since it was overturned. Is that the middle ground, the public, you know, maybe nobody loved it on the far left and far right, but was that actually the right middle ground for the American public?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, Roe, when you look at the details, would have allowed, you know, abortion up until birth of the baby, which is not something anyone, the vast majority of Americans, support. But I will tell you, living in a swing district and representing a swing district, I polled the issue over and over and over again. I`ve done a number of town halls. In fact, I had my first town hall of this Congress last week. And it is a top issue for swing voters. It`s the number two issue in my district.\par \par \uc2 And the vast majority of people in my district don`t like what happened after Roe was overturned. They support women`s right to choose generally. But I`ve spoken to Republican and Democrat women. And the folks in my district, swing voters are willing to find some middle ground here. Everyone recognizes abortion up until birth is not something that they want. At the same time, banning all abortions with zero exceptions isn`t something people support. And so where is the middle ground? How do we get there?\par \par \uc2 Democrats are at 24 weeks, for example, with gestational limits; Republicans, 12 to 15, on average. Can we -- can we find some middle ground around 20 weeks, for example, if we`re talking specifics on some sort of gestational limitations, when we`re talking about legislation at the state or federal level?\par \par \uc2 I don`t know if we can get there, because both sides are really, really entrenched. And I see that because I`m repeatedly attacked by pro-life groups, even though I am pro-life. I`m at least willing to have some concessions, find that middle ground, because the vast majority of the people I represent, that`s what they`re asking for, not digging in on the far right or the far left on this issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I hear where you`re trying to go, and I think what you`re trying to do. Unfortunately, you`ve got folks that just see it as a -- as a morality issue or as a -- as a personal freedom issue. And so compromising either your morals or your freedoms is very difficult to -- to do.\par \par \uc2 Do you think this is just we`re not ready yet in our politics, that we need to go through a few more cycles? Do you think your party needs to lose more on this issue before more folks look at it the way you look at it?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it`s the reason we didn`t get more of a majority. We should have had a dozen or two dozen-seat majority this legislative session, but we don`t.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:03]\par \par \uc2 Because this was one of the issues that was top of mind for swing voters.\par \par \uc2 And when you`re looking ahead to `24, whether it`s keeping the majority or trying to win the White House, going to the extremes and digging in there isn`t going to work for most people. And so it`s -- when I look at every issue, not just on Roe v. Wade, but every issue, we`re so divided.\par \par \uc2 It`s refreshing to hear Senator Joe Manchin, who I sometimes joke he`s the most popular Republican in the Senate right now...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ... but to hear him talk about these issues in a very refreshing, balanced way, is the way both sides of the aisle need to approach every issue, including abortion. And I`m here waving my hand, being a very vocal person on this, and saying, I`m pro-life, but I`m willing to sit down and talk about how do we balance the rights of women and the right to life?\par \par \uc2 At some point that infant has the right to life. And women`s rights are equally important. So let`s have that conversation; let`s have it in the open; let`s find a way to move together. Because having a divided Congress means we`re supposed to be working together. But both sides are afraid of their primaries. That`s not the way we should be operating, but that`s the way the majority of people in Congress, they vote and legislate out of fear.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think you may get primaries on the abortion issue?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I mean, I got primaries last time and the time before that. I get -- I expect a primary in a general election every time, being in a swing district. But I will tell you, even with the far right coming after me on this issue in the last primary, I won overwhelmingly, by nine points. And then when we had our general election, I flipped 40 percent of Democrat precincts and outperformed the former president in my district as well, in the most conservative precincts. And so I found a way to represent -- try to represent all the voices. I can`t represent one side or the other.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You are on the Oversight Committee. Maybe not a lot of people know that. We got news this morning of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  conducting this search, finding more classified documents with President Biden. I know that the head of this committee, James Comer, wants to spend more time looking at this as well.\par \par \uc2 Should -- how do you think the Oversight Committee -- what role should they play in this particular investigation?\par \par \uc2 And can you separate the Biden classified document issue from the Trump classified document issue?\par \par \uc2 MACE: They`re very similar, and yet there are some differences. It`s hard to say one or the other, because we haven`t actually seen the classified or top secret information they both may have been holding. They`re similar in that they both wrongly took classified information away from the National Archives and away from, you know, where they should have been deposited in a classified space, like a SCIF, that was mentioned earlier today.\par \par \uc2 But the difference between the former president and the current president is that, for five years, the current president, these documents were hidden; nobody knew about them. And so -- and when it was discovered, they sat on it for two months. And it wasn`t until a reporter leaked it out and wrote a story about it did the rest of the country find out about these documents.\par \par \uc2 I am grateful that the president is now being more transparent about it, but he did hide it for two months once it was discovered, and then nobody knew about it for five years. And so any investigation Oversight does, part of that is, you know, getting to the other side of this. There`s very little information. And so we know everything about the former president. We know that they knew about it for two years, National Archives and DOJ, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , they knew where the documents were located. And I do want to see both the current and former president treated equally. I`m glad that the DOJ is investigating it. I want to see transparency and accountability.\par \par \uc2 As was mentioned earlier, the American people are held to a certain standard; so too should the current and former president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask you this. Should the Oversight Committee treat this -- treat both presidents the same? Right now, it looks like that the current chair only wants to investigate the Biden angle of this. What say you?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, I think that`s because there`s no -- there`s very little information about Biden. I mean, these documents were -- were hidden for five years. We have very little information, whereas, with the former president, everybody knows that those documents existed; they knew where they were; they knew where they were located.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, they...\par \par \uc2 MACE: There was information that was presented...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me stop you there. We didn`t know where they were located.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACE: ... for example.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It did take us -- they defied a subpoena. It took a search warrant.\par \par \uc2 MACE: The \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the DOJ and the...\par \par \uc2 TODD: They didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Yeah. Well, there were some -- in some media reports, it was stated that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the DOJ knew where they were located. Now, did they have access to it? Maybe, maybe not. It`s hard to read from the media reports to actually know. But I do want to see, at the end of the day, the special counsel, the DOJ, treat both of them equally.\par \par \uc2 And there was some discussion earlier in the program about intent. Well, the U.S. could have laws -- it`s really not about intent. It`s about knowledge of having these documents in your possession. And so we`ll have to see where the investigation goes. But I do want to make that clarification. Intent is not really -- doesn`t really matter in this case. It`s the knowledge of do you know that you have these documents, and storing them, and not giving them back to the appropriate sources like the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move on to the debt ceiling. Do you believe it`s a valid exercise to use the debt ceiling as leverage to try to negotiate spending cuts?\par \par \uc2 [09:30:02]\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, there`s no time like the present because we have the debt ceiling looming over us to talk about this. We haven`t had a budget since \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  balanced the budget in 1998, a Democrat president doing that. Before that, it was Republican \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  in 1970. Clearly, something is wrong.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at nationwide, 49 of 50 states, they balance their budgets every single fiscal year. And, you know, we need to get there, $31 trillion created by both Republicans and Democrats. This is both parties` faults. Now is the best time to have this conversation.\par \par \uc2 And there are plans out there. For example, there`s the Penny Plan. I filed that particular piece of legislation in the House. Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  has it in the Senate. That would balance the budget in five years and allow the federal government to raise spending by 10 percent every year thereafter.\par \par \uc2 I`m not even asking for that. Can we do it over ten years? Let`s have a negotiation and build some consensus.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, you want to use the debt ceiling for this moment? You think it`s a proper way to use -- to force this. Why not the budget process? That`s the part of this I don`t quite comprehend.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well, it does -- clearly, the budget process isn`t working because we haven`t had a budget in decades. And there is a lot of hysteria around shutting down the government.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, this happened under the previous administration. The government was shut down for 35 days. There was a stalemate. But people still got paid. Accounts still got filled up. And the sky didn`t fall.\par \par \uc2 I would like to see the president -- and so far, he has said he`s unwilling to negotiate with Republicans. That is not how to unify our country. We are very divided right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MACE: We have $31 trillion of debt. The responsible thing to do would be to get to the table with Republicans and negotiate a way. How do we prioritize spending? How do we balance the budget?\par \par \uc2 We`re not even asking to do it one year like \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  did it. Can we do it over ten years, which would be, you know, consensus and be compromised? That`s a reasonable conversation to have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you have one thing --\par \par \uc2 MACE: It`s not --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this: Do you have one thing you`re ready to put on the table as a spending cut that you think both parties can accept?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Well I think -- well, obviously no cuts to Medicare or Medicaid or Social Security. That`s a nonstarter for either side.\par \par \uc2 But otherwise, it`s up to -- I would lean on the agency heads. Whether it`s one penny or five pennies, the Penny Plan does it with $0.05 on the dollar in five years. But we can find some way to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 And I believe we should go to the agency heads and say, where can you find cuts? Where can we do this? And do it over the next decade. That`s a very responsible measure that we should all be discussing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congresswoman Nancy Mace, always a lot more to get to, but I`m always limited by the amount of time I have here. Thank you for coming on and sharing your perspective with us. I always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Any time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, the classified document story. They were found six days before the midterm elections, but it would be 68 days before the public found out. Our panel is here with the thoughts on the Biden White House strategy of staying silent.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:35:57]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is here.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Chief White House Correspondent Kristen Welker, also co-anchor of "Weekend Today"; Peter Baker, the Chief White House Correspondent for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 "; Brendan Buck, former advisor for Speakers Ryan and Boehner; and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, the senior opinion writer for "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 Well, let me set the scene here a little bit. Kristen, I know you have some good, new reporting on this whole document business. We have the president. He`s passed his two-year mark. He postponed his re-election announcement until after the State of the Union. Clearly, it has to do with the classified document probe.\par \par \uc2 And he tried to address it again on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Fully cooperating and looking forward to getting this resolved quickly. I think you`re going to find there`s nothing there. I have no regrets. I`m following what the lawyers have told me they want me to do. It`s exactly what we`re doing. There`s no there there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: No regrets and no there there. We already heard Senator Manchin saying there should`ve been some regrets. This timing issue -- there are a lot of elected Democrats who are a bit frustrated with this White House right now.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: To say the least, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 And I am told that there was this drip, drip, drip, and then that additional set of documents that was found last weekend. And so the calculation inside the Biden world was that, look, we need to stem the bleeding here. We need to stop this. We need to make sure that there are no additional disclosures.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why they invited the Justice Department to do this search inside the president`s Delaware home. But you heard his comments there, there`s no there there.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you, in talking to folks inside and outside of the administration, they were concerned about that language before we learned about the search last night. And then I was getting all sorts of messages saying, this is exactly why statements like that do not age well. They do think that he should be more apologetic and say, look, we`re going to wait for this play out.\par \par \uc2 Politically speaking, this has been incredibly challenging for this administration, and from a messaging perspective as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter, does the But-Trump paragraph work here for Biden?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Only so far. I mean, it clearly, is very different than Trump. I mean, you outlined it very well already.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Trump was trying to defy a subpoena. He knew what he was doing. He seems defiant even to this day that he owns the documents in effect, and could do whatever he wanted. Biden is saying, look, this is an innocent mistake. Certainly didn`t mean to have these there.\par \par \uc2 But it only goes so far. And it is the transparency issue. It is the notion that they went for 68 days without telling the public. Yes, they told the Archives. That`s what they should do. But they hid it from us before the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Their argument is, look, as a matter of strategy, it was more important for us to show that we are Cooperating with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , not litigate this in public, but they`re paying a cost for it, and the cost is that Democrats themselves are frustrated and think that the White House has caused an own goal here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brendan, has this been a boon to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Like, does it actually give him traction again, when I think there were a lot of Republicans hoping this classified document thing would sink him? And they wouldn`t say it out loud, but I -- they thought this thing was going to sink him, and now they think, oh, nope.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yeah. If there`s some action taken against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and not against the president, that plays right into everything the president has always said, that there`s two sets of standards. And you can say until you`re blue in the face that these situations are different, but voters are going to see that there was consequences for one president and not the other.\par \par \uc2 And clearly, they are different -- look, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not the standard. You can`t set the standard by how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  handled things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s Biden`s biggest problem, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: That is. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  himself was attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for the underlying infraction, not just how whether he didn`t give them back, the underlying infraction. Either that matters, or it doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Kimberly?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: But this is a missed opportunity here because at a time when the public trust in institutions is at a very, very low point, this was an opportunity for the Biden administration to say, look, this is how it`s done.\par \par \uc2 As soon as they got the information about these documents, invite the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in then. Search everything. Take a look. This was inadvertent. And then you can show how this is very different from saying, you know, I can un- classify it with my mind, or defying federal authorities and saying, no, I have a right to keep these things. They`re mine.\par \par \uc2 That`s very different from how they`re handling it, and it was an opportunity to show that to the American public, and they missed it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know this is a classic case of lawyers being lawyers and not wanting to get into the PR game, but it really looks like that keeping a close circle here was damaging.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:03]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It has been damaging optically and again from a messaging standpoint. It`s important to point out, and Kimberly, you kind of touched on this, that in terms of this search that happened at President Biden`s Delaware home, the thinking is it`s going to be short term pain with long term gain because they are being transparent in this moment.\par \par \uc2 But Chuck, I`ve been sitting in the briefings day after day after day --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Water torture. Yeah, speaking of short term gain, drip.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And yes, there`s been a drip, drip, drip.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And not a whole lot that the press secretary can say, in large part because the White House Counsel`s Office wants to be very careful about the investigation.\par \par \uc2 Now, they did start to shift their strategy, the White House Counsel`s Office providing a little bit more information. But there are key questions that still remain unanswered, including why it took two months for the world to learn about this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I want to talk about another investigation and another issue of transparency. It`s the Supreme Court investigation.\par \par \uc2 I want to put up there their first when they announced that basically an investigation was done.\par \par \uc2 The leak was no mere misguided attempt at protest. It was a grave assault on the judicial process. And following up on all available leads, the Marshal`s team performed additional forensic analysis and conducted multiple follow-up interviews of certain employees. But the team has to date been unable to identify a person responsible by a preponderance of the evidence.\par \par \uc2 Kimberly, preponderance of the evidence was a phrase of doing a lot of work in there. And to me, including it seemed to be a yellow flag.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Well, listen. I don`t know. It`s a very legal term that we would use to judge a lot of other things.\par \par \uc2 I think the big takeaway from this is that the Supreme Court as an institution has been this insular, insulated institution for so many years and that even the Marshal tasked to try to carry out this investigation worked against that fact. There were no real rules about how to handle decisions before they were publicly released.\par \par \uc2 Eighty-two people had a hold of this draft opinion. And you had it -- it was even unclear in the initial report whether they even spoke to the justices because they are -- you cover that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, that firestorm left and right, and they put out a new statement saying, oh no, we talked to them, too.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: But in that institution, they are almost deified in a way that it seemed like doing so, or even admitting that you might have done so, might have seemed like doing something wrong. It just shows all the ways that --- talk about a lack of transparency -- there`s few places less transparent than the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brendan, there was nothing illegal about the act, so there was only so much the Marshal can do. That was another problem here.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: I look at all this and it just feels like the Supreme Court is starting to be like all the rest of our politics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome to the club, guys.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yeah. Stuff leaks. I mean, it used to be that the Supreme Court, you know, was read. It wasn`t heard. It certainly wasn`t seen. But now you can go to your average progressive, and they probably have a bobble head doll of \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Like, it`s just so political in every way now, it`s just starting to feel like it`s no longer this thing that you can hold aside as, you know, the high integrity institution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The black robes -- they don`t wear black robes anymore, Peter Baker. They`re red robes and blue robes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah. No, that`s right because we do refer to them now as -- in fact, for the first time really in the last few years, you see Republican- appointed justices being consistently conservative and Democratic-appointed justices being consistently liberal. In the old days, it was much more of a mix, right? Some Republicans would be more liberal and some Democrats would be more conservative. Now it feels like, you know, you`re looking at it as R this and D that. And it`s just a very different way of looking at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I do think that one of the key takeaways is that, because this was an internal investigation, it was not subject to the rigors of an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation. Could they have made the argument that a government document was seized? And so that`s the frustration. I think.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, maybe Congress will say that draft Supreme Court rulings are classified until they`re presented to the public, because then the \uc2 FBI\uc2  would`ve been involved.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Exactly. But I do think the takeaway, and we were kind of getting to this, is that what needs to change? There needs to be more security around this. Fewer people may need to be read in.\par \par \uc2 Listen, these people admitted to telling their spouses about the draft document. That`s problematic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Actually though, they all have one thing in common. We don`t think they`re transparent.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Court, the presidency, the Congress, you name it, and guess what and guess what? Zero trust.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s the trust deficit.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve got to earn trust back.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, who is running America`s largest city? One party is now noticeably absent from the big cities.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:12]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Conference of Mayors was here in Washington this week for their annual meeting, and one group was noticeably absent among the biggest players -- Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you. These are the ten most populated cities in America. Nine of the ten have elected Democrats as mayors, and the tenth -- San Antonio - - doesn`t elect mayors by party, but I can tell you that mayor is decidedly progressive.\par \par \uc2 So essentially nobody right of center is a mayor of a top ten city. That`s actually quite a change from 2000. At the turn of the century, four of the top ten -- New York, Phoenix, San Diego, L.A. -- all had Republican mayors. A huge change.\par \par \uc2 And overall in the 25 most populous cities, right now just three have Republican mayors, Oklahoma City, Jacksonville, and Fort Worth, Texas. That is it.\par \par \uc2 In 2000, of course, that number out of 25 was just seven. So less than half of what it was in 2000.\par \par \uc2 Look, these 25 major cities, they total 38 million citizens in those cities. That accounts for 12 percent of the overall U.S. population. And in fact, there are more people in these 25 cities than there are in 21 states. So, when you talk about political power, yes, governors have power, but the mayors also are political juggernauts themselves, particularly on the Democratic side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, this week marks 25 years since news broke that President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  had an extramarital affair with a young White House intern. It`s a scandal that forever transformed Washington`s politics, the media, and the American public. Monica Lewinsky`s lawyer joined this program to discuss the president`s future just after the story broke.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP, JANUARY 25, 1998)\par \par \uc2 TIM RUSSERT, MODERATOR, MEET THE PRESS: Do you believe the president can survive this crisis?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM GINSBURG, ATTORNEY: I don`t know. I don`t want to call myself a country lawyer, but I certainly am not a Beltway lawyer and I`m not a political pundit.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Does it influence you, affect you in any way to know that the fate of the country, the fate of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , is in effect in your hands and in Monica Lewinsky`s hands?\par \par \uc2 GINSBURG: In addition to the fact that I don`t believe that the government of the United States is in Monica Lewinsky`s hands or in my hands, short of that, you bet you it scares me. It scares me that my country could be in this kind of a crisis. I`m not political, but I am a citizen of this country and I do respect the presidency.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, just how much did the Lewinsky scandal change the course of our political history? Trust me, we are still living with the consequences today.\par \par \uc2 Our panel is next to discuss.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:36]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. This week marks the 25th anniversary of an event that arguably launched our current moment in our politics, when Matt Drudge on the Internet broke the news that "Newsweek" was sitting on a story about former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  having an affair with a young White House intern named \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, here`s what happened next -- a presidential lie, an investigation, disappointing Republican midterm, impeachment, Senate acquittal, round-the- clock media coverage and speculation, and the takeoff of a more partisan cable TV news environment.\par \par \uc2 It remains one of the more significant forks in the road in modern political history, and it turns out we`re perhaps still living in that moment.\par \par \uc2 Peter Baker, you and I were both, sort of, younger, very much reporters in this moment. And I think at the time we were all blown away. I guess looking back last week, it just sort of hit me, my God, we`re still stuck in this loop.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah. We are stuck in this loop. That`s exactly right, I was just remembering. I remember walking home at night, at 2:00 in the morning after we published the first story on this in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " at the time, thinking, oh my God. What have we done? What have we unleashed here?\par \par \uc2 This is an extraordinary story. The president of the United States, sex in the White House, investigation of lying under oath.\par \par \uc2 But to your point, I think it changed our politics. It wasn`t just a one- off sex scandal, which we`ve had a number in our history. It was a moment when we made our politics more tribal, in which we decided we were going to make any investigation, any scandal about us versus them.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what we`ve seen time and time again ever since.\par \par \uc2 [21:25:01]\par \par \uc2 We saw it with Trump. We`ve seen it with other presidents. And I think that we redefined our ideologies to suit which side are we on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, before 1998 we had had one impeachment in this country. Since that time, Brendan Buck, we`ve had three. I mean you just can`t -- we live in this moment constantly and the way Democrats rallied around \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , we saw Republicans rally around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And you can`t help but ask yourself -- without Clinton, does Trump ever happen?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yeah. I think the tribalism is the right point. It`s, while I don`t like what you did, you`re my guy and I would never give the other side a win. And I think that`s how everything is viewed. It`s much less, what my team is for, as being against what the other team is.\par \par \uc2 It turned politics into a soap opera. And that`s what we still have today. Most -- I won`t say "most" -- a lot of politicians now are much more entertainers in their own minds than they are public servants. And it corrodes our politics, and there are so many outlets for it. Of course, the media, there are so many more platforms for them to play the role and to entertain, and it leads us to where we are now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, you definitely saw the partisanship of cable exploded. The idea that cable news is a prime time destination didn`t exist until Monica.\par \par \uc2 But, Kimberly, we also know the fact that this was an incredibly misogynist thing that happened. This was a -- you know, when we talk about Me Too -- and I`ve had younger staff. They say when they learn the history of it, they don`t understand why there was no discussion of the power dynamic. And it`s like, that`s how immature our debate was back then.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Yeah. Two decades before Me Too. You know, over the years, I`ve had discussions with people who have said their viewpoint about \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2  then has changed so profoundly than it is now.\par \par \uc2 That someone -- this 22-year-old intern, you think about the kids that you see walking around --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Twenty-two-year-old intern. We all have 22-year-olds walking around our offices.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Right, and the attacks that were done. I mean, just, I couldn`t imagine being a part of that. She seems to have come out of that with a lot of grace and respect now in retrospect, but it really shows the difference in how up until then --aside from \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  -- there wasn`t a sex scandal that was this public. Now, we treat things very differently.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we though?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, and --\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: No. Not differently enough. But at least there is that change.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I do think that that is one of the biggest impacts, this cultural shift. And \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2  herself describes herself as patient zero for Internet shaming. And she has talked about being bullied and what that experience was like.\par \par \uc2 And the way that she was treated by this country, by the world has not aged well. And in many ways, it is the seeds of the Me Too movement. And I think it`s been fascinating to watch her reclaim her story.\par \par \uc2 And as you say, Kimberly, so many people have changed their perspectives about how they view that moment, how they viewed her and how they treated her.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter, the question I wonder: It`s going to take some outside event to shift us to another fork in the road and get out of this doom loop we`re in, but I don`t know what that looks like.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I don`t know what that looks like. I mean, we had 9/11, and we kind of changed --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Temporary respite.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: -- our politics for about a year or so, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: And that didn`t make a permanent difference. And I don`t know what will happen now that will be the equivalent of that.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we have these big crises. We have these big issues. We have the debt ceiling issue.\par \par \uc2 We have a crisis of our democracy. We have the crisis of the war in Ukraine. So many crises, and we can`t come together on things.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Everything is worse since 1998 in this moment. That`s the bottom line, sadly.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, sign up for our free daily newsletter, First Read. You`ll get the best analysis, polling, and political news every morning: wisdom before it becomes conventional. Scan the QR code on screen or go to NBCNews.com/FirstRead.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week; because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020230122ej1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc47}{\*\bkmkend toc47}\b \uc2 On This 50th Anniversary Weekend Of The Roe Versus Wade Decision That Was Overturned Last Year\b0\par\par\uc2 Joe Fryer; Kristen Welker, Marissa Parra \par \uc2 1,848 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:18]\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning and thanks for starting your weekend with us and it might be a good one to stay inside with another big storm impacting much of the country.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: And another one is close behind. It`s January 21st. This is TODAY.\par \par \uc2 Severe weather watch. Millions in the south preparing for a heavy dose of rain today with the potential of flash floods before the same storm heads up the East Coast. Threatening to bring up to a half a foot of snow in the Northeast. We`ll have the full forecast.\par \par \uc2 Battle Cry. On this 50th anniversary weekend of the Roe versus Wade decision that was overturned last year, tens of thousands take to the streets of the nation`s capital as part of the March for Life rally. Where does the abortion fight go from here?\par \par \uc2 Record surge. The government reporting the number of illegal border crossings at its highest level ever since President Biden took office. What can officials do to slow down the influx?\par \par \uc2 Caught on camera. Police take down.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Drop it! Drop it!\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Harrowing moments inside an Indiana \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  captured on police bodycam after a former employee opened fire. This morning, the woman police are credited with helping to stop the bloodshed from getting worse.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus football frenzy. Fans from coast to coast getting ready for this weekend`s \uc2 NFL\uc2  playoffs divisional round.\par \par \uc2 Eight teams facing off including that highly anticipated rematch between the \uc2 Buffalo Bills\uc2  and \uc2 Cincinnati Bengals\uc2 . It all kicks off today, Saturday January 21st, 2023.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome to TODAY and thank you so much for joining us on this Saturday morning. Peter is enjoying the morning off. We`ve got Joe Fryer here. And Joe, you are here for a big weekend in softball, a big weekend for my beloved Eagles taking on the Giants.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: We know what you`re going to be e doing tonight. We definitely know who you`re going to be cheering for.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Fly Eagle fly. I got the whole family in town. We are ready.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: I know you are and I might be jumping on your bandwagon because the Giants beat my Vikings last week and I`m still bitter.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, you know where your allegiances are.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I like that. All right. Good.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Exactly. We`re going to talk football and a little bit. We`re going to be begin though by covering several developing stories this morning. That includes the marches planned this weekend for activists on both sides of the abortion rights debate. We`re going to hear what anti-abortion rights protesters are hoping for next today after that big March for Life rally here in D.C.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And we`ll also tell you about tough times for the tech industry. \uc2 Google\uc2 `s parent company announcing on Friday at slashing 12,000 jobs. They`re joining other companies like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Meta\uc2  making mass layoffs.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: But let`s begin this morning with our top story. The weather as the south prepares for intense rain today and the Northeast gets ready for a round of snow that could bring up to six inches.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Some area saw a preview of that on Friday for the very latest on all of it. Let`s bring in Somara Theodore. Somara, you`ve been tracking this. What should folks be bracing for?\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:05:16]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Somara, thanks. Maybe a good weekend to stay indoors for a lot of people. We`ll get the rest of your forecast in just a few minutes.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: This weekend marks the 50th anniversary of the Supreme Court`s decision on Roe v. Wade. And a half century later the debate over abortion rights still looms large, especially after the Dobbs decision last year overturning Roe. On Friday the March for life took place just one of several marches this weekend on both sides of the issue. NBC`s Marissa Parra was at the march and joins us with more. Marissa, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA PARRA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Joe and Kristen, good morning to you. Another busy day in D.C. as usual, right?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Well crowds for the march we`re in the tens of thousands. And we know that the march was originally started to protest Roe versus Wade. So, there was a big celebratory mood in this new post Roe era, right? This is what they`ve been fighting for. But activists on both sides of the abortion debate are really galvanized in different ways, especially as we get closer to 2024.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PARRA (voice over): This morning, the battle over abortion access far from over. Roughly seven months after Roe v. Wade was overturned, the nation`s capital seeing the nation`s first march for life in a post Roe world.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): And today, we celebrate Roe is gone. It`s important to keep your focus on what the mission is. But every step of the way. It`s also critical that we celebrate victories along the way.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Changing the route to reflect a change country, stopping in front of both the Supreme Court and the Capitol. A symbolic nod to the role they both have and continue to play abortion law.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s up to the states now so we need to raise awareness and each of the states.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Counter protesters reading their own fight to win back lost ground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That is why I`m here. It is to show that we will not let America be bullied out of the right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: The path to a full nationwide victory for either side an uphill battle. It`s been 50 years since Roe v. Wade was first signed into law. But in the months since its reversal last summer, almost two dozen states have either banned abortion or they`re close to it, with several legislative sessions actively debating what their laws will be on a state level. This says federal action fails to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Senator Lindsey Graham`s federal abortion ban proposal failed last fall and the Biden administration`s hopes of a federal right to choose.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We need to codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Unlikely and a Republican-controlled House. And with new \uc2 FDA\uc2  guidance making abortion bills more accessible over the counter, a new abortion battleground is headed to the pharmacy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PARRA: Now that new \uc2 FDA\uc2  guidance was a small victory for abortion rights activists and we know tomorrow is the 50th anniversary of that original decision. So, there are bigger than Roe pro-choice marches scheduled across the country. Joe and Kristen?\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right. Kristen -- Marissa Parra, thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Great to see you, Marissa. Really appreciate it. Well, just one day after the Supreme Court announced it couldn`t find who was behind the leak of that draft opinion of the decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. Former President Trump is calling on the journalists who revealed the leak to be jailed. And this morning the Biden administration is fighting back. NBC`s Monica Alba is tracking all of it from the White House. Monica, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Kristen, good morning. After an eight-month investigation that leaks still could not be pinpointed with former President Trump pushing stricter measures to root out the leaker. The probe led by the courts chief security officer determined hackers were likely not responsible. after interviewing 97 employees. The search found that 82 had access to digital or hard copies of the draft.\par \par \uc2 But now we`re learning more about who else was questioned in the matter. The court investigator revealing Friday she spoke with each of the nine justices several on multiple occasions and all cooperated in the process. Every credible lead was explored and none of them implicated the justices or their spouses according to the court. And soon after the 20-page report came out that couldn`t identify the leak, the former president called for the reporters who broke the news to be arrested, along with possibly their editor and publisher saying they should be put in jail "until the answer is given."\par \par \uc2 Within hours, the White House released a statement defending freedom of the press saying it`s the responsibility of all leaders to protect First Amendment rights. Kristen?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. Just remarkable that that investigation could not determine who was behind the leak. Monica, another big headline you are tracking we`re also in the midst of this debt ceiling standoff between the White House and Republicans but we`re hearing that the President will meet with House Speaker Kevin McCarthy to discuss a path forward. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 ALBA: That`s right, Kristen. While speaker McCarthy did accept President Biden`s offer to meet soon, no date has been set yet by the White House. But a contentious back and forth Friday about the debt ceiling show just how difficult resolving this issue may be.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:07]\par \par \uc2 House Republicans don`t want to increase America`s ability to borrow without promises of future spending cuts. While the President says there`s no room for negotiation when the stakes are so high. Warning of unprecedented financial calamity if the U.S. doesn`t meet its credit obligations for the first time in history. Experts say it could even lead to a recession or stock market crash if it happens, as the treasury secretary is already using extraordinary measures to keep paying the nation`s bills effectively buying time by shifting money around but that will only last until June. Kristen?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: A major test and showdown for all of Washington. Monica Alba at the White House. Thank you, Monica.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Illegal crossings at the U.S.-Mexico border last month surged to a record number since President Biden took office. The government says it stopped migrants more than 250,000 times along the border. Up 40 percent from the prior December. The extraordinary influx came shortly before Mr. Biden introduced measures to deter migrants from Cuba, Haiti, Nicaragua and Venezuela. Republicans say his lacks border policies have led to the flood.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020230209ej1l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc48}{\*\bkmkend toc48}\b \uc2 50 Years After Roe v. Wade, America Is An Abortion Battleground After The Supreme Court Decision Overturning It\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Anne Thompson \par \uc2 639 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2023\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: We have new details on the Supreme Court`s probe into the stunning leak last year, that draft opinion overturning Roe v. Wade. The Supreme Court Marshal revealed today she spoke with each justice in addition to court employees, but investigators were not able to identify the leaker.\par \par \uc2 And this weekend marks 50 years since the Roe v. Wade decision. In the months since it was overturned, the U.S. has become a patchwork of laws varying from state to state. Anne Thompson with more now on the deep impact on clinics.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The days are nonstop at Florida`s Jacksonville Health Center. 50 years after roe versus wade and seven months after the Dobbs ruling that overturned it, demand for abortion at this Planned Parenthood Clinic hasn`t slowed says Manager Jessica Wannemacher.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA WANNEMACHER, MANAGER, PLANNED PARENTHOOD CLINIC, JACKSONVILLE: It`s getting worse.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 WANNEMACHER: The amount of patient load that we need to be able to see more patients.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Four years ago, the clinic offered abortion services once a week. Now, it`s up to five days. Dr. Shelly Tien specializes in high-risk pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Of the 63 patients you`re seeing today, how many of those are seeking abortion services?\par \par \uc2 DR. SHELLY TIEN, PLANNED PARENTHOOD CLINIC: The vast majority.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Has it been like that since the Supreme Court decision?\par \par \uc2 TIEN: It has.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Florida bans abortions after 15 weeks with narrow exceptions. It also requires two in-person doctor`s visits at least 24 hours apart, but the outright bans and heartbeat bills in neighboring states have more than doubled the out-of-state patients here. For opponents of abortion rights like Andrew Shervill, it is a frustrating consequence of the Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW SHIRVELL, FOUNDER AND EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, FLORIDA VOICE FOR THE UNBORN: It`s extremely -- actually, it`s extremely infuriating because here Florida`s leading on so many other issues, but we should not be in this position at all.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: So, what`s next?\par \par \uc2 SHIRVELL: What`s next? Here in Florida, we`re pushing for a complete abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: But at the clinic Dr. Tien believes any restrictions come with dire consequences.\par \par \uc2 TIEN: Women are going to die because of what has happened to reproductive rights in this country.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: A recent \uc2 Commonwealth Fund\uc2  study analyzed data from 2020 and found maternal death rates were 62% higher in states that had abortion restrictions. The battle is now in states like Florida.\par \par \uc2 SHIRVELL: I think the Dobbs decision properly returned the public policy fight to the states, and that for the foreseeable future is where activists on both sides are going to be engaged.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Should medical doctors have a role in crafting abortion law?\par \par \uc2 SHIRVELL: No, I don`t believe so.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Dr. Tien disagrees.\par \par \uc2 TIEN: It must be shared decision-making between the patient, her family and her healthcare team.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Do politicians have a role in this?\par \par \uc2 TIEN: Absolutely not. Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Rulings may change, but the debate has no end in sight. Anne Thompson, NBC News, Jacksonville.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, coming up, it may say organic, but is it food fraud? The new warning and crackdown next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2023 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020230209ej1k0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc49}{\*\bkmkend toc49}\b \uc2 TODAY WITH HODA & JENNA for Dec 26, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Jenna Bush Hager, Hoda Kotb, Donna Farizan \par \uc2 7,201 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 December 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:00:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voiceover): Today, get ready to be inspired. Donna shows up at the door of a beloved neighbor and gives him the ultimate sweet surprise. Plus, writer and poet Yung Pueblo. How his millions of followers are learning and healing from his every word. And the breast cancer survivor whose personal journey is touching and saving the lives of countless women.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voiceover): It`s TODAY WITH HODA --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- & JENNA. It all starts right now.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It all starts right now.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Hey, guys, happy day after Christmas. It`s Monday, December 26th. We hope that you all had a very merry one and a happy one. All the wrapping paper the bows and all that stuff tucked away.\par \par \uc2 HAGER (on camera): Waking up with family still in the house. It`s -- what were they called this day in the U.K.? Boxing Day?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Boxing Day? They do?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes. I`m not sure why.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, just like to decorative --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But let`s celebrate Boxing Day. Why not?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. I like that.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And we`ve got an amazing show today that`s going to leave you feeling inspired and feeling good. Just as this day should.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my God, this is -- this is our kind of show.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It sure is.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Because we love -- loves -- love things that feel like that.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wait, in fact, can you just talk about the way you start your day? And the way you end your day? Because Henry is reading a book called Think Like A Monk by Jay Shetty.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Jay Shetty, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And he says a lot of people think about the morning routine, but people forget about the end.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know what`s funny? The morning routine I packed with inspirational readings and quotes and prayers and writing in my journal and all these kinds of things at night. I usually read and we have Yung Pueblo on today, a couple of lines from his book. And just before I close my eyes. Like last thing in, last thing in. I also try to say -- usually you think of like three things you`re grateful for. Yes, but a twist on that and this sounds vain, but it isn`t. Three things I appreciate about myself. Now, think about for right now, just freeze for a second. In this moment, what are three things you appreciate? Not that you`re grateful for? So, what is it that you`ve done that you appreciated? And it`s only for you, you`re not bragging. But what did you -- what have you done so far that you appreciate? And sometimes they`re small things like I gave my kids space, so that they could say what they wanted to say and then I received? OK, that`s the thing. And so, that`s the way --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes. Or the way you handle situations.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, it`s so -- it`s so interesting, because I think we don`t even realize the negative stuff we say to ourselves, which is constant. Why did you do that? Why did you do that for? Why did you --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m telling why (INAUDIBLE) should --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You shouldn`t have said that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But -- so imagine just filling that time with? Oh, look, what you did there. You know, saying nice things. And I think it really does make a difference.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know, Michelle Obama`s book that`s been out for a little bit is so beautifully written. But she talks about in one part, how we have a rectangular-sized shield that we put between us and the world. And she`s talking about the phone.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, interesting.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And she says, what we`re doing every day is we are missing micro connections. You`re in the line at the bank, but I`m playing Candy Crush, so I don`t even look up to say hello, or I`m here and I`m doing something small. What your day -- she talked about how every day a micro connection and actually matters. It shows that we`re -- we may be different, but we`re human. But if you`re -- if there`s something in your ears and something in front of you, because people keep saying why are we drifting further apart? Tiny interactions that we would normally have.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thank you. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Please, hi on the street.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: The kind of smile that kind of makes you feel good.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wait, do you know -- so, when I first went back to work after having Mila, she`s 9, so about nine years ago, I got assigned the story. And I swear, it was like some sort of gift. Because the story was all about how basically kids are feeling like they`re not interesting. There was this doctor at Harvard who was studying what this -- what this does to a child. So, pretend you`re saying, Mommy, mommy --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hey, mom, mom, I want to tell you something. No, mom, mom, mom?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Hear. Hear. Hear. So, the words --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right. This is more important.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: This is more important than this.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Than you. Than you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And it was the best gift because what I realized is I was putting emphasis on work everything --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I got to get this deal. Very important.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: -- and you`ll (INAUDIBLE) instead of looking into my baby`s eye.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And well, it`s not true. I mean, no mom thinks this is more interesting than their child.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Or, we don`t even realize -- we think how of just doing one quick thing, just one quick thing.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Anyway, that`s -- that could be a New Year`s resolution for a lot of people out there, and it`d be for mine.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, yes. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 [10:05:05]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK. So, there`s research that recently was published in the Economic Times. It says humor at work actually accelerate creativity, motivation, and promoting productivity.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know what, here`s the funny thing. I`m sitting next to this one. So, here`s the funny thing about you. Sometimes, I worry as we`re sitting here, because the things you say are so crazy and funny that I`ll just lose it instantly. But here`s the funny thing. I feel like there are ways -- there are ways of connection.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: For us, we have it. I think we have a deep kind of spiritual connection. But when you make me laugh, I feel so weirdly and crazily connected. Like I am -- sometimes I think about what you say much later in the day, and I`ll literally just be like, and goes what, I got nothing. It was something -- I can`t even explain.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You called me the other day. And you go, all I have to say is pirate and hung up.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know why? Because I thought I was wearing a pair of cute black pants, black boots, but they didn`t meet in the middle. So, Jenna goes, hey, pirate. And literally, I was -- I just laughed, but that`s the whole thing. They say, it`s the whole thing. If you have them laughing, you have them listening.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: That is such a good one.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And teachers who are -- yes, teachers who are -- who make kids laugh, kids lean in, they listen.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Totally. But laughing also is so much fun. I feel like that`s -- I mean, I`m coming up with all my New Year`s resolutions. Somebody, write this down.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What is it?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: My other New Year`s resolution is to laugh more.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. How?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Like why are we too busy? I mean, we laugh a lot because that`s our --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Because we have -- and we also have space too. If we didn`t, if we were rushing through, you know, the whole --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Rushing and doing this, and -- here`s your paper. Like, laugh instead, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Get in the next segment. I know.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Why don`t we just laugh more?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I mean, we do. In life, why don`t we --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We all, collectively. OK, shall we see this video that`ll make us feel good.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, yes. All right, this is the father of a bride in Jersey. So, the wedding videographer mic`d up the dad for his big walk down the aisle. So, let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Nice and slow, honey. Look at his face. You see nothing but smiles. I love you, you know? Take care of her. Give me a hug, too. You`ll be fine, kiddo. I`ll be always by your side. You`ll never be alone, OK?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my gosh.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: There were so few words yet they were so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We were talking about laughing.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s good, now we`re crying. But you know what I think about crying? If you`re crying, you`re listening, too.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, yes, yes. It works for both. All right, coming up, if you love a surprise we got a good one for you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And Donna shows up unannounced to surprise a beloved neighbor right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Now to our series, "KNOCK KNOCK SURPRISE!" And this is a surprise that`s going to fill your heart, for sure.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. So, a few months ago, a viewer wrote in to tell us about her incredible neighbor of 20 years. His name is Ernie Mann. He lives in Rensselaer, New York. So, we sent Donna there to surprise him with something we knew he`d love. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 DONNA FARIZAN, NBC NEWS 