traveling every week on the road.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: After a routine mammogram, the words triple negative breast cancer stopped her in her tracks.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: My doctor said we found five spots on your chest wall. You are now metastatic. You have two years to live. Get your affairs in order. We don`t have anything for you. And I said, well, I can`t really die right now. I have a daughter at Dartmouth. I have to pay for her tuition. So, me, you and gotten some drugs or something. We got to work this out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It was a long road of surgeries, chemotherapy, radiation, and experimental drugs, but it worked. And Ricki was cancer free. She decided to quit her old life and start fresh.\par \par \uc2 [10:25:03]\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I knew I had to get rid of all the cancers in my life. I divorced my husband of 30 years. I quit my business partner of 10 years. I sold my house in Alpharetta, Georgia with a pool and two acres, and I moved to my little one-bedroom condo on the beach.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She also did a lot of research and learned about the disparity in breast cancer for black women.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: This disease for black women is a different disease. And now, we have a growing body of data that validates that a black breast cancer cell looks different from a white one. We have a 41 percent higher mortality rate than white women. We have a 39 percent higher recurrence rate of breast cancer than white woman, which is crazy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So, with her new home, her new fiance and her new way of life came a new purpose, a nonprofit called Touch: The Black Breast Cancer Alliance.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I wanted to change the game on how the world thinks about black women and breast cancer. I want to advance the science. I want to stop the dying. I wanted to bring pharmaceutical companies to the table to say, deal with this. Help us.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: 11 years after she was told she was going to die, Ricki is still cancer free and has been able to watch her oldest daughter get married and have a family of her own.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I work for my granddaughters. I don`t want them to ever think about this disease. I don`t want them to have to talk about it. I don`t want them to -- I don`t want it to be in their existence.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: As for her younger daughter, Haley, Ricky got her wish and watched her graduate college. They now work side-by-side to help other women going through the unthinkable,\par \par \uc2 HALEY BROWN, DAUGHTER OF RICKI FAIRLEY: Seeing my mom firsthand, being sick and dealing with all the things that come with it. And now just the things that even come with survivorship. Just being a support system for these women has been a large part of my mission. And so, what I`m trying to do now for the breast cancer community is also create that safe space.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Looking back, Ricki has advice for her younger, busier self.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: Your piece is non-negotiable. Take care of yourself, check your breasts, give yourself a minute, give yourself a pause. It`s OK to say no.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And that she sits with her family, four generations of strong women, Ricki`s mission is clear.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I`m a miracle. I know I`m a miracle. I shouldn`t be here. So many women that with --that had what I had didn`t make it. And I know that God left me here to do this work to be an advocate for other women. It`s my purpose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Ricki, I mean, we are in awe of you. Your purpose came with that horrible diagnosis. A huge change. You made so many changes that you`re probably too afraid to make your whole life. What -- was there ever a point of trepidation? Should I divorce my husband, change all these things because of this -- of what happened to you?\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: No. There was no trepidation. I knew -- I knew in my spirit that I had to save my life. And I just went for it. I didn`t even think twice about it. And people said, are you crazy? are you sure you should do all this at one time? Do you want to think about it? I said, no, I have to go now.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You know, the best thing about this is that you had the second life and you`re using it to help other women. You -- those statistics that we heard in the piece are unacceptable. You could have just read those and thought, OK, but instead, you`re fighting them. And that just seems like an incredible mission.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I work for my granddaughters. I don`t want them to ever think about breast cancer, and we have a lot of work to do to save black women. And I don`t -- I don`t want anybody to die of breast cancer. But we deserve mortality rate parity.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, I thought what you said after the diagnosis was like you, the doctor, me, God, medicine, whatever, we all do -- we`re all going to have to work together on this one. You sound like your faith was unshakable throughout.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: It was. I -- you know, faith is my spiritual gift. I know that God left me here to do this work. And I`m on a mission to try to change how we think about it.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Well, Ricki, we are impressed with you if that`s not obvious. But also, Thrive Cosmetics, they told us they were so inspired by the work that you do to eradicate black breast cancer. But guess what, they`re donating $10,000 to Touch to continue the efforts that you put in in everything you do.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: Oh, wow, that`s so awesome.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Will that be able to make a difference?\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: That will be awesome. You know, we have this movement called When We Trial. Whenwetrial.org to educate black woman about clinical trials, so we can get better drugs, so I will put it to work today.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, awesome. Ricki, we love you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re unbelievable. You`re unbelievable. Wow, what a gift for us. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Thank you. Thank you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: That`s such a blessing. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow, for us too.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: How brave and awesome is Ricki. We love everything she`s doing especially for the black breast cancer community.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, she is an inspiration.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Coming up next, the author and poet whose book is on Hoda`s nightstand. We talked about him earlier, Yung Pueblo, after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:33:11]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: TODAY Show is all about people who lift us up through their positivity and light. And one of those people is bestselling author, Diego Perez.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You probably know him by his pen name, which is Yung Pueblo. His words have brought me and millions and millions of others comfort and wisdom when we`ve needed it the most.\par \par \uc2 And recently, Donna caught up with him as he released his newest book, check it out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONNA FARIZAN, NBC PRODUCER (voice over): If you`ve ever scrolled on social media, you may have already come across the words of Yung Pueblo. Diego Perez is the poet behind the pen name, inspiring readers with wisdom, like, "realize how short the walk is from gratitude to happiness." And "manage your reactions, but do not suppress your emotions."\par \par \uc2 Since he first began sharing his words of wisdom in 2014, Yung Pueblo has connected with over 2 million followers. And has written two \uc2 New York Times\uc2  bestselling books of poetry on topics like self-love, growth, and relationships.\par \par \uc2 When you first started posting your writing, what was your intent?\par \par \uc2 YUNG PUEBLO, AUTHOR, LIGHTER: I wanted to write about healing being possible. This hopefully inspires someone else out there who also needed to find a way to heal themselves.\par \par \uc2 FARIZAN: Diego and his family emigrated from Ecuador to Boston, Massachusetts, when he was four years old.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: My mom, she worked cleaning houses, my dad worked at a supermarket. So, we were really stuck in a poverty trap. And then, I think a lot of that tension just got embedded inside of me as well.\par \par \uc2 When I got to college, I could not deal with any tension that was coming up inside of me, I would quickly try to hide it with, with drugs, with smoking, with going to more parties.\par \par \uc2 [10:35:02]\par \par \uc2 What was the breaking point for you?\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: It was right after college. This time I pushed my body to the edge. And I took, you know, an assortment of different drugs.\par \par \uc2 I felt like my heart was going to explode. I ended up talking to a doctor after that episode. And she told me, and said -- but I described to her sounded like a mild heart attack.\par \par \uc2 FARIZAN: How has being first generation American affect your overall outlook of life, especially in that point?\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: The first thought is my parents. I saw the immense sacrifice that they made to just give us the chance at a better opportunity.\par \par \uc2 I felt like I was giving all of that up by just filling myself with more and more pleasure just so that I can run away from my pain. I knew that the only way out was to start telling myself the truth.\par \par \uc2 FARIZAN: In 2011, Diego started taking small steps toward building positive habits. These improvements eventually led him to daily meditation.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: So, the meditation has given me a way to process these like really tough emotions. And when something challenging happens, I noticed that I can feel my reaction, but it`s not as overwhelming as it used to be.\par \par \uc2 FARIZAN: And meditation gave you the clarity and creativity to write and share.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Definitely. I mean, I was never creative before meditating, you know. And I didn`t go into meditating to become more creative. When the mind becomes lighter and it`s not as burdened by past hurt that you carry, this creativity bubbles up.\par \par \uc2 Diego`s poems have been liked and shared 1,000s of times. And in his newest book titled, Lighter, Diego shares his own journey and advice on how to achieve personal transformation.\par \par \uc2 What do you hope is the biggest takeaway for people who come to you and look to your words?\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: I hope they take away inspiration. It`s really possible to transform your life. It`s really possible to sort of take that big leap forward in your own evolution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh my gosh. What an incredible journey. Our conversation with Yung Pueblo, right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:40:45]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Before the break, we introduce you to someone whose books I`ve been reading poet and bestselling author, Diego Perez, who goes by the name Yung Pueblo.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: After some difficulties with alcohol and drugs, he found his way to a life of light and inspiration. We got to chat with Diego about his new book, Lighter. And how he finds his inner creativity.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We just saw that amazing piece with you, Diego. And one of the things that struck us was, you said you didn`t realized you were creative until you had a chance -- until you meditated and had a chance to kind of clear all the muck out from your brain, so you could let it out.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Totally. I had no idea. I never saw myself as a creative person. But honestly, after I did about three silent 10-day courses, I felt that creativity bubbling up. And I felt my intuition really telling me, you know, it`s time to write.\par \par \uc2 Even though you`re not fully healed, even though you`re not fully wise, you know, you have a long way to go. But just write about healing being possible, because I noticed a significant differences in my mind.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You know, it`s so interesting, because so many of us are kind of covering pain.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Like -- and I love that you have said, it`s OK to feel --\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Totally. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s OK to have those emotions, and you have to.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember, like, what you used to do -- the before and when you felt --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Oh, before. I mean, as soon as I would feel that tension, whether it was some type of fear or some type of anxiety, I would try to go smoke as quickly as possible, whether it was like a cigarette or smoked marijuana, or just tried to cover up my sensations basically in some way or another.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, we, Jenna, and I love your quotes. And we`ll just share a couple of them. And just maybe talk a little bit about them. Because when I look at you, I know people at home are going well, I want to feel creative.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I want to feel calm.\par \par \uc2 Is it possible for people to feel what you`re feeling, to have that clarity?\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Yes, I don`t think it`s unique to me at all. I think it`s something that anyone can do.\par \par \uc2 And that creativity shows up for people in different ways. So, like whether you`re a presenter, whether you`re a doctor, whether you`re an engineer, if you start deeply healing your mind, and really allowing that unconditioning to happen, you know, because we carry so much within ourselves.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: If you start allowing that to burn away through some process or another, then you`re going to notice a burst of creativity happening in your life.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK. We`re going to share some of our favorites. I feel like this one -- maybe Hoda said to me recently.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I just send to you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: She texted me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s a really nice way to wake up with a text from you, and via Hoda.\par \par \uc2 The undeniable radiance of someone who is not afraid to grow, to be free, and thrive.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Yes, I mean, I think it`s something that it`s pretty common. When people start deeply healing themselves, they radiate in a different way. Like they`re able to just come in contact with how they actually want to show up in the world, as opposed to how their past wants them to show up.\par \par \uc2 Because that`s often what happens is that something difficult situation arises, and your old self will want to show up as opposed to how you want to intentionally show up in the present.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You know what`s funny? There was a woman who was just on with \uc2 Andrew Lloyd Webber\uc2 , and she walked through, and she was like, hey, I just want to say something to you. And she looked at me, and say, I just want to let you know, I`ve been thinking about you and praying for you.\par \par \uc2 And as I looked at her, I felt like this incredible moment. And it was something that wasn`t concrete. I didn`t know her well. It was just -- it was that.\par \par \uc2 It was like she was alive. She had something inside of her that was like luminous to me. And I was like I`m still dazzling.\par \par \uc2 When she left, I was like, wait, where are you going? Because she had to leave, it was time to go.\par \par \uc2 But people do give that off. Here is what we`d like to --\par \par \uc2 Maturity is knowing that when your mood is down, you should not trust the - - you should not trust the way you see yourself.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Like it`s a bad moment, not a bad life?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Yes. I mean, that`s a really important one because as soon as your mood goes down and your emotions feel heavy, you`ll want to just jump into self-critique, and you`ll start telling yourself things that are totally untrue, like, you haven`t actually gotten that far, or, you know, you`re just like, sort of building this narrative that isn`t based on reality at all.\par \par \uc2 So, I tried to, you know, tell myself and tell other people, when your mind feels heavy, just pause -- just pause, focus on treating yourself gently.\par \par \uc2 And when you actually feel better, that`s when you want to take a step back and do some self-analysis. Right? And don`t examine yourself from now to yesterday, right? That`s not a good scope of who you are. Examine yourself from when you began to where you are now.\par \par \uc2 [10:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes. You know, I think when we watched that piece with you, and we saw the pictures of you as a little boy, you know, you talked about sort of this pressure to be successful for your parents, not fail. Was that kind of what weighed on you, and what do your parents think about this incredible man that you become?\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Yes, I think, um -- it was -- it was hard. You know, it was hard seeing my parents go through this immense challenge of trying to keep our family afloat. And we were just -- you know, like I said before, we were stuck in a poverty trap.\par \par \uc2 And it was quite difficult for us to come out of it until I became older until my brother became older, my little sister and now we can support ourselves, help our parents as well. And I think my parents are just they`re really grateful that the gamble paid off for all three of us. Because coming to United States it`s --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: You know, it`s a bit of a risk and but it worked out for us.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, I have your book by my bedside, it`s one of those you can open up read one beautiful line and go to sleep and sleep really well. So --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: She actually goes to bed with your words in her head every night.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And then make her feel lighter.\par \par \uc2 PUEBLO: That`s a big honor.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Big for me too. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And then make her feel lighter. And I think if you want that light, it`s here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. And you got to get the book, Lighter. And also, your other two books are amazing. I like all three, but get this one because it`s new.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: By the way, since that segment aired Yung Pueblo`s book Lighter reached number one on the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  bestsellers list. To check it out, head to today.com/books.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Coming up next, two tennis-playing sisters who truly understand the importance of serving after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:50:53]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We hope you`ve been feeling inspired by the people and stories we`ve shared with you, but we are not done yet.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We want you to meet two sisters Ayanna and Amani Shah, they`re teenagers and they`ve already changed the lives of thousands of kids all over the world. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: 16-year-old Ayanna and 18-year-old Amani Shah loves to serve.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH, CEO, SECOND SERVE: I think more could supply tennis because it`s a phenomenal way to build character.\par \par \uc2 AYANNA SHAH, PRESIDENT, SECOND SERVE: Tennis has taught me so many lessons and I think the biggest one is learning how to push through obstacles.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And since 2019 their nonprofit organization Second Serve has been working to give kids around the world equitable access to tennis.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH: When I was 10 years old was when I really fell in love with the sport.\par \par \uc2 AYANNA SHAH: The relationships that I`ve built through tennis have been just amazing.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH: Historically, tennis has been deemed an elitist sport because of its very high-cost entry.\par \par \uc2 AYANNA SHAH: Second Serve redistributes new and gently used tennis equipment all around the world. Our mission is to create greater access, inclusion, and diversity within the sport. One of my personal favorite donations was to a young girl in Nigeria named Nana Rosie (PH).\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I started playing tennis since I was three years old.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH: Nana spends four to five hours every day playing tennis and her dedication is really an inspiration for us.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Ayanna and Amani tap teams across the U.S. to make this all happen.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH: Our team has grown to over 90 different high school team members all across the U.S. We`ve been able to redistribute over 20,000 items in over 26 states and 14 different countries.\par \par \uc2 EMMA HIGUCHI, COO, SECOND SERVE: What I was drawn to is it was a -- it`s a youth-led organization. We always have monthly meetings. Just hearing their great ideas is really great and hopefully, we can make those ideas come reality.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PRATT, TENNIS COACH, ORANGE GLEN HIGH SCHOOL: The Shah sisters of Second Serve have been an inspiration to me and also to our high school tennis team. It`s a school of 91 percent economically disadvantaged kids. Having a tennis racquet gives them that sense of ownership.\par \par \uc2 DIANNA, ORANGE GLEN HIGH SCHOOL STUDENT: At first I couldn`t really hit but now I can do like everything.\par \par \uc2 PRATT: Now she`s competing. She`s our number one player. I have seen her build a sense of confidence. I stopped.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Inspired to do more in their own backyard, the team started Serve Escondido.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH: We provide free tennis lessons for under-resourced kids in our own hometown of San Diego.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good job.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And in the spirit of giving back, we had a little surprise for Second Serve.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH: Second Serve has done incredible work to make the game of tennis accessible to all. To help continue that work, Wilson is donating 100 tennis rackets and 50 pairs of youth sneakers to Second Serve.\par \par \uc2 AYANNA SHAH: Oh my gosh. That`s incredible. Oh! Second Serves` mission of enhancing the lives of underserved children by fostering a love for tennis is inspirational and empowering, to support that mission Athleta is donating 100 girls` tennis dresses to Second Serve. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 AMANI SHAH: Thank you so much. This is awesome. It`s just really special to see that other people support this vision of greater access and greater diversity for all kids. When I`m on the tennis court, I`ve experienced that what you believe is really what you can achieve. When you`re about to serve when you`re about to hit a ball if you can visualize that ball going exactly to your target. That`s often exactly what happens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: The Shah sisters are going to take over the world. A big thank you to Wilson and Athleta for helping us with that surprise.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, that was awesome. We`re back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:56:58]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Heart. We hope we let you feel good today. Tomorrow we`re going to meet some of our favorite foodies.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, including Chef Tami Treadwell and our Harlem food truck that serving more than just delicious seafood.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So, have a great one everybody. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alt