uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Blast off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`ve got a fun-filled hour ahead with a few surprises along the way. Today, Thursday, December 8, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Best friends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And "TODAY" super fans.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Parkersburg, West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Pullman, Washington.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: And Lynwood, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Hagerstown, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Celebrating our 25th anniversary.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Friends for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From El Paso, Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Here with my grandson.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Mechanicsville, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hello from Woodbine, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: And Cincinnati, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Celebrating Vicky`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Here to experience a magic.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Of Christmas in New York.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Mizpah, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Welcome back. How do we say thank you for coming out --\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: I know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- every morning? This crowd, it`s like six people thick. It`s a big crowd. We cannot wait to get outside and visit with everybody in just a bit. But what do you say we get to our news at 8:00?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: An early surge in flu cases is sending more Americans to the hospital and it`s actually causing pharmacies to run out of both prescription and over-the-counter treatments. NBC Sam brock has the latest on how long this might last and what you should do in case your drugstore shelves are empty.\par \par \uc2 Sam, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hoda, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The mere fact that the flu season has arrived weeks in advance, as you said, has put parents in quite a crunch, pretty much across the board from antibiotics to antivirals. It`s true in Coral Gables, Florida, where I am, in San Francisco, California, Detroit, Michigan. All over the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, the owner of University Pharmacist here tells me that amoxicillin right now, she has, about, maybe four or five patients worth of supplies left here. This is so important, it`s so critical because it`s the preferred antibiotic for kids. It`s a little bit milder, fewer side effects. That`s just one example. She wants to order more, cannot right now.\par \par \uc2 Check out the store shelves. When it comes to decongestants, you see some Mucinex but the rest of the shelf here is empty. Robitussin, Sudafed they were down here, that`s gone as well. As far as anti-inflammatories for kids, Tylenol and Advil, there`s empty spaces on the shelf for that as well. All of it is unavailable.\par \par \uc2 Now, the ASHP is a pharmacy group that tracks short supplies for drugs. They have right now, Hoda, some 260 drugs nationally that are short at the moment that includes Tamiflu, which is the most popular drug for treating the flu for kids, certainly, for adults as well. The issue with Tamiflu says one of their manufacturers, they have enough supply. They said it`s available for pharmacies to buy, but the owner here tells me it now cost three times of what it did just a few weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 So, that anecdotally, is something that we`re seeing on the ground. This problem could go on for a number of weeks, well into the holidays. One of the advice -- pieces of advice doctors have for you, make you`re your pediatricians office contacts the pharmacy ahead of time to see if they have a drug in stock and to ask if there`s a viable alternative if there`s not. But parents right now are having to get creative as they go through this period where everybody is getting sick. Hoda, back to you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, happening in my neighborhood, too. All right. Sam Brock for us. Sam, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: For the first time in four decades, journalists and other employees at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " are staging a large-scale walkout. The 24-hour strike by more than 1,000 members of the news gilded New York started just after midnight. The union says it lost patience with contract talks that have gone on for two years.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the "The Times" denied the talks were at an impasse and said it was disappointing that the union had taken what she called an extreme action. Negotiations have stalled over wages and remote work rules.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: A new study has found that women who`ve been treated for breast cancer may be able to pause their follow-up medications long enough to have a baby. After treatment, patients often take drugs to suppress their hormones for up to 10 years, but those drugs can also make it hard to conceive.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The new research led by Dr. Ann Partridge looked at how to balance the need for drug therapy with the desire to complete a family. And it found that within three years of pausing their medications, women had no greater risk of their cancers returning than those who stayed on the drugs, and about two-thirds were able to conceive and have a child.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, grownups, who can`t recall third grade English lessons, can probably still sing every word of the "Schoolhouse Rock" cartoon that aired on Saturday mornings in the `70s and `80s. Well, George Newall, the man who helped create those classis tunes has passed away.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Conjunction junction what`s your function.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hooking up words and phrases and clauses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I mean, we`re just singing it right here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Those cartoons had a generation of kids about math and science and civics and grammar. Newall was an advertising pro. He composed all of those songs including "I`m Just a Bill on Capitol Hill." Remember that one?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: "Three is a Magic Number", he got the idea when a colleague with children asked about putting multiplication tables to music. George Newall was 88 years old. What a legacy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow. Indeed. Wow.\par \par \uc2 All right. Guys, would you like a little "Morning Boost"?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You got it.\par \par \uc2 As you probably know, a white elephant party, it`s when you get together with your friends and you exchange silly gifts, usually just for kicks. So, \uc2 Baylor University\uc2  volleyball player Lauren Briseno never imagined she would -- what she would unwrap during their white elephant party that her coach hosted for the team.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAUREN BRISENO, BAYLOR UNIVERSITY VOLLEYBALL PLAYER: Hear ye, hear ye. It is with great exclamation that this proclamation be read. Due to unwavering diligence and determination for relentlessness and execution of incredible skills at the highest levels. For you (ph) of this year proclamation has hereby earned yourself a full scholarship.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Lauren is a sophomore, by the way. She was a walk-on on the volleyball team. That was a surprise scholarship. She said she was eternally grateful. I mean, you think you`re getting one of those white elephant gifts.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And you get one of that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Exactly. It sure beats the fridge magnet or whatever.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Guys, next, this is the next best thing to actually be invited to Ina Garten`s house.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She is here. She`s in our house, and giving all of her secrets for throwing a memorable holiday party from the moment your guests walk through the door to a parting gift they will never forget. Ina, the hostess with the most will host this show.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN, BAREFOOT CONTESSA HOST: We are going to have such a great time.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We are, my friend. We`re back after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is a Special Report. Here`s Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning, everybody. We are following breaking news at this hour out of Russia. Nearly 10 months after her arrest and incarceration there, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner is headed home.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Breaking and welcome news. Russia has agreed to a prisoner swap with the U.S. We`re going to go straight to NBC`s Chief Foreign Affairs Correspondent, Andrea Mitchell, she has these breaking details.\par \par \uc2 Andrea, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Savannah and Hoda. Well, according to a senior administration official, Brittney Griner is free after 294 days in Russian captivity. The last month spent in Russia`s notoriously harsh penal colonies. She was traded for Russian arms dealer Viktor Bout who \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has been trying to get back and had served 11 years of a 25-year sentence in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed off on the trade even though it meant leaving another American businessman, Paul Whelan, behind bars in Russia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (voiceover): Over the weekend, Secretary of State, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2 , saying the U.S. has been actively engaged in discussions with Russia about a possible prisoner swap to free Brittney Griner and Paul Whelan.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN, U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: We have to see if the engagements that we`ve had, the discussions that we had produced an actual result.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (voiceover): Griner, the 32-year-old \uc2 WNBA\uc2  superstar has spent nearly 10 months in Russian custody. Arrested at a Moscow airport in February. She admitted to bringing less than a gram of medicinal cannabis into the country but said she never intended to break any law. Her plight sparked an outpouring from her family, teammates and fans. Griner`s wife, Cherelle.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER, BRITTNEY GRINER`S WIFE: I`m frustrated that my wife is not going to get justice. I know you all are frustrated, too, that`s why you`re here.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (voiceover): Sentenced to nine years in prison, Griner appealed, pleading for leniency from behind bars.\par \par \uc2 BRITTNEY GRINER, WNBA PLAYER IMPRISONED IN RUSSIA: This has been very traumatic experience waiting for this day.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (voiceover): But she was denied. President Biden last month.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: I`m telling you, I am determined to get her home and get her home safely. Along with others, I might add.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (voiceover): The other American held by Russia will not be coming home. Paul Whelan, a former marine, a businessman, detained four years ago is serving a 16-year prison sentence after being accused of spying, which the U.S. strongly denies. Whelan`s family later raised concerns about his safety, which I asked the secretary of state about last week.\par \par \uc2 BLINKEN: We`re working to bring him home, to bring Brittney Griner home. But this is, again, what we see Russia doing in terms of abusing the very basic understandings that countries have had when it comes to having access to our citizens who are being detained.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): Those familiar with these very hard negotiations say that the Russians absolutely refused to release Paul Whelan without getting a Russian spy in return. But the U.S. insists it does not have any Russian spies in U.S. custody. So, the administration says, it had no one to trade to meet that Russian demand. And again, the U.S. maintains that Paul Whelan is wrongfully detained. He`s a businessman, not a spy.\par \par \uc2 And just now we also got a statement from the group that has been campaigning for all of these hostages to be released from all over the world, actually, saying Paul Whelan has been let down and left behind by -- at least three times, by two American presidents. He deserves better from his government and our campaign implores President Biden to urgently secure Paul`s immediate return using all tools available. We understand the president will be speaking shortly or later this morning.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. Andrea, thank you very much for the breaking news. Brittney Griner has been freed. Paul Whelan left behind. Let`s go to Chief White House Correspondent Peter Alexander at the White House.\par \par \uc2 What`s the word there, Peter?\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT AND WEEKEND TODAY CO-ANCHOR: Savannah, we do expect to hear from President Biden at 8:30, so 15 minutes from now. And we have just learned that Cherelle Griner, Brittney Griner`s wife, is right now in the Oval Office here at the white house. She`s behind closed doors with the president.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 The two of them were able to speak by phone earlier this morning with Brittney Griner after she was returned to U.S. custody. And then as we understand it, Cherelle was able to have a private conversation with her wife, Brittney, from the president`s study. But this concludes for the Griner family what has just been a very distressing situation over the course of the last 10 months.\par \par \uc2 The president has said this was a top priority. He had exchanged a letter with Griner in the past who wrote him, over the summer, saying that she was terrified she would be there forever in Russian custody. The good news this morning that the White House will certainly be celebrating today is the release of Brittney Griner.\par \par \uc2 And again, to repeat, Cherelle Griner`s, the president`s -- is in the Oval Office with the president right now and had an opportunity to speak alongside the president with her wife just this morning. Savannah and Hoda.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Peter Alexander, thank you so much. We are going to continue to keep a close eye on the story all morning long. We`re going to bring you updates. And of course, we`ll have coverage on "NBC Nightly News" and all day on "NBC News NOW".\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: For now, many of our stations will return to the "TODAY" show in New York. This is Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb. Good day.\par \par \uc2 8:16. We want to turn now to Dylan and get a check of the weather. She is aboard a ship, are you not? Oh, where are you now?\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY CO-HOST AND METEOROLOGIST: I am, but I brought you inside now to show you some of the extravagance here in an atrium. This staircase made of Swarovski crystals, I mean, because that`s what you need on a cruise ship after all.\par \par \uc2 But let`s take a look at what`s going on outside. Here in the northeast, it is a little bit chilly but I`d say seasonable for this time of year. It`s really down through the Ohio River Valley where we`ll see a lot of that rain move in today, heavy at times. And the snow is going to move into the Upper Midwest. Temperatures, still very warm, especially down along the Gulf Coast where we`ll likely break high temperatures today. Although it is going to be quite chilly across the Northern Plains. Highs only in the teens and 20s.\pa