t along the Gulf Coast, temperatures in the 80s, cold across the northern plains and upper midwest, only 10s and 20s with even colder windshields.\par \par \uc2 And out west, looks nice in the southwest but a little messy once again in the northwest. That`s the look at the weather across the country. Now here`s a peek out your window.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 DREYER: So as you know I`ve been on a boat all morning long. I`m aboard the \uc2 MSC Cruises\uc2 ` newest ship it`s the MSC Seascape. And you know, last night Italian icon Sophia Lauren, she is the official godmother of the ship. And she christened it last night with a bottle of champagne that they break on the ship, and it`s a nautical tradition that is really said to bring good luck and safe travels to the boat itself and also to all the passengers.\par \par \uc2 There was also a special performance by musician Matteo Bocelli, who has a new holiday album called "A Family Christmas" with his father, classical music legend Andrea Bocelli. And Matteo spent the night aboard the Seascape. And here you are with us bright and early this morning. How was your night last night? How did it go?\par \par \uc2 MATTEO BOCELLI, SINGER: It was really a fantastic. Actually this is the second time that I`m here with the MSC family. So it`s such a big honor to be -- to have performed here. And yes, let`s hope to have many more coming.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes. And you`ve done the whole \uc2 MSC Cruises\uc2  with your father before, what`s it like getting the chance to kind of travel the world with your dad?\par \par \uc2 BOCELLI: It is very special. I actually experienced this also in 2018 when I did the duet "Follow Me" with him. And so, I`m very happy to be again with him on tour and also with my little sister. So it is very special.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: It`s a family farewell. It`s so nice to have you here this morning.\par \par \uc2 BOCELLI: It was such a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Of course your new holiday album, "A Family Christmas," is out now. And, guys, we have a huge announcement coming up in the 3rd Hour of TODAY, the whole reason why we`re here on this boat you don`t want to miss it. So stick around for that, guys.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right. We look forward to it, Dylan. Thank you. All right. It is now time for our series, Ina`s Holiday Table. And sticking with the theme we`re keeping it simple. We have easy ways to dress up your dining room table with items that you probably already have around the house, right co- anchor?\par \par \uc2 [08:35:10]\par \par \uc2 INA GARTEN, COOKBOOK AUTHOR: Here to show us how is one of my good friends, Erin French, who I adore. She is the founder of the Lost Kitchen in Freedom, Maine, which might be the toughest reservation in America to get. We actually met during the pandemic on Zoom and we`ve had some wonderful dinners together, and we`ve become really good friends. So, what`s you going to show us? How to do --\par \par \uc2 ERIN FRENCH, FOUNDER, LOST KITCHEN: All right. I`m really excited to show you this because it`s very easy. You have a lot of these things at home.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: So first of all, don`t stress. Tablecloth made from simple drop cloth. You can just get it at the hardware store.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hardware store, OK.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: It`s less than $20. And that`s what you`ve draped over the table.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: That`s for the table.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: That`s for the table. You can cut it to size, or you just tuck it underneath.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Tuck it.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: So, the next thing is once you`ve got your simple tables down, you want to make candles. It`s all about candles. You need to have simple things that feel like they were intentional and they were special, special touches. So if you want to help me make some candles.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We love to\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m ready. I`m doing whatever Ina want to do.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: I`m calling this about last night candles, so these are just wine bottles. This is from last night.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Really? That`s so clever. You don`t even need to canisters.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: Yes. So go ahead. You can pick what you want.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. Take a bottle. Put (inaudible) inside.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: Hop it in.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Oh look at it. It smells so good.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: You can make your own. And if you need, you can use a little chopstick to push it down and if you have a hard time with this.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: So a little rosemary.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love it, OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: How cool is this?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`ll use a chopstick to push it in. And then, that`s going to be your candle holder?\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: That`s going to be your candle holder. And now if you just want to take your candle, and you can just use a vegetable peeler.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love that --\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: To do shave --\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: -- grocery store and get things that you can make candles out of.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: This is how you going to make sure that (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can I have a vegetable peeler? I want to try.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: OK.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: Make your own candle.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: So you just shave it so it fits.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: It fits right in.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Usually you`re just trying to jam it.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And then you break the candle. So how clever is this?\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: Bring it on down -- yes, let`s go set the table.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m very busy setting our table, Ina.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: Set the table, put it on on.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: So there you can never have enough candles. So we have a second candle. So what I did is I just use vintage tea cups saucers, and then a tea light. And then just take a little bit of moss if you want to, Ina, go for it. Hoda, make one if you`d like to.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: I love these little dishes. Aren`t they fabulous?\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: These are my grandmother`s. So those (inaudible), they make you feel like --\par \par \uc2 GARTEN: Just so fabulous, yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: So you`re going for a little look like this. So you`ve got some moss in here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hey, guys. Guys, I know this is kind of a crazy day. Sorry to interrupt. It`s kind of -- there`s a break, a lot of breaking news going on. We`re going to take a quick break.\par \par \uc2 FRENCH: You got it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And we`ll be back with more. Of course, this is TODAY on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From NBC News, this is a Special Report. Here`s Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Hi, everybody. We continue to follow this breaking news out of Russia this morning. \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner heading home, a prisoner swap after 10 months. President Biden at the White House, let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Months ago standing together with her (inaudible) in the Oval Office. I spoke with Brittney Griner. She`s safe. She`s on a plane. She`s on her way home after months of being unjustly detained in Russia held under untolerable circumstances. Brittney will soon be back in the arms of her loved ones and she should have been there all along.\par \par \uc2 This is a day we`ve worked toward for a long time. We never stopped pushing for her release. It took painstaking and intense negotiations, and I want to thank all the hard working public servants across my administration who worked tirelessly to secure her release. I also want to thank the UAE for helping us facilitate Brittney`s return, because that`s where she landed.\par \par \uc2 These past few months have been hell for Brittney and for Cherelle, and her entire family and all her teammates back home. People all across the country have learned about Brittney`s story, advocated for release, stood with her throughout this terrible ordeal. And I know that support meant a lot to her family.\par \par \uc2 [08:40:16]\par \par \uc2 I`m glad to be able to say that Brittney is in good spirits. She`s relieved to finally be heading home. And the fact remains that she`s lost months of her life, experience the needless trauma. She deserves space privacy and time with their loved ones to recover and heal from her time being wrongfully detained. Brittney is an incomparable athlete, a two-time Olympic gold medalist for team USA. She endured mistreatment and a show trial in Russia with characteristic grit and incredible dignity. She represents the best America -- best about America, is across the board everything about her.\par \par \uc2 She wrote to me back in July. She didn`t ask for special treatment even though we`ve been working on a release from day one. She requested a simple, "please don`t forget about me and the other American detainees. Please do all you can to bring us home." We never forgot about Brittney, we`ve not forgotten about Paul Whelan who has been unjustly detained in Russia for years.\par \par \uc2 This was not a choice of which American to bring her home. We brought home Trevor Reid when we had a chance earlier this year. Sadly, for totally illegitimate reasons, Russia is treating Paul`s case differently than Brittney`s. And while we have not yet succeeded in securing Paul`s release, we are not giving up. We will never give up.\par \par \uc2 But remain in close touch with Paul`s family, the Whelan family, and my thoughts and prayers are with them today. They have to have such mixed emotions today. And we`ll keep negotiating in good faith for Paul`s release. I guarantee that. I say that to the family. I guarantee you.\par \par \uc2 I urge Russia to do the same to ensure that Paul`s health and your main treatment are maintained until we can able to bring him home. I don`t want any American to sit wrongfully detained. And one extra day if we can bring that person home.\par \par \uc2 My administrations now brought home dozens of Americans who were wrongfully detained or held hostage abroad, many of whom had been held since before I took office. And today we also remember the other Americans that are being held hostage and wrongfully detained in Russia, or anywhere else in the world. Reuniting this Americans with their loved ones remains a priority. A priority for my administration, every person in my administration involved in this. We`re going to continue to work to bring home every American who continues to endure such an injustice.\par \par \uc2 We also want to prevent any more American families from suffering this pain of separation. And I strongly urge, I strongly urge all Americans to take precautions, including reviewing the State Department`s travel advisories before they travel overseas, which now includes warnings about the risk of being wrongfully detained by a foreign government.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake about it. This work is not easy. Negotiations are always difficult. There are never any guarantees. But it`s my job as President of United States to make the hard calls and protect American citizens everywhere in the world, anywhere in the world. And I`m proud that today we had made one more family whole again.\par \par \uc2 So welcome home, Brittney. And now I`d like to invite Cherelle to say a few words with y`all. Of course, she`s not excited at all about this. Cherelle, it`s all yours, kiddo. Congratulations again.\par \par \uc2 CHERELLE GRINER, BRITTNEY GRINER`S WIFE: Thank you. So over the last nine months, y`all have been so privy to one of the darkest moments of my life. And so today I`m just standing here, overwhelmed with emotions. But the most important emotion that I have right now is just sincere gratitude for President Biden and his entire administration.\par \par \uc2 He just mentioned this work is not easy, and it has not been. There`s been so many hands involved. And so, I`d like to take a moment to just specifically mention a few. Vice President Harris, Secretary Blinken, Jake Sullivan, Josh Geltzer from the National Security Council, Roger Carlson and Fletcher Schoen from the Hostage Invoice Office. A special thank you to Governor Richardson and Mickey, the \uc2 Mercury\uc2  players, the WNBPA for your advocacy. And also you guys may not know this, but my family has been tremendously supported by the Wasserman Agency, BG`s agent. Lindsey Colas has just been amazing for me and my family throughout this process.\par \par \uc2 So today my family is whole but as you all are aware, there`s so many other families who are not whole. And BG is not here to say this but I will gladly speak on her behalf and say that BG and I will remain committed to the work of getting every American home including Paul whose family is in our hearts today as we celebrate BG being home.\par \par \uc2 [08:45:16]\par \par \uc2 We do understand that there are still people out here who are enduring what I endured the last nine months of missing tremendously their loved one. So thank you everybody for your support. And today, it`s just a happy day for me and my family. So I`m going to smile right now. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you all very much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (END LIVE COVERAGE)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: From the White House, President Biden, Vice President Harris and Cherelle Griner, the wife of Brittney Griner, BG, who has just been freed from a Russian prison in a dramatic prisoner swap. But Paul Whelan, who often had been mentioned in the same breath as Brittney Griner, was not freed. And Cherelle Griner right there saying she knows it`s a moment of disappointment for his family, but just a smile for the joy that she is feeling as well.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Let`s go to NBC`s chief White House correspondent Peter Alexander with reaction there. Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Hoda and Savannah, good morning to you. We want to pull back the curtain a little bit about what we know happened already earlier this morning. As you just heard from the President earlier this morning, President Biden joined in the Oval Office by Cherelle Griner, who you just heard from, as well as the Secretary of State \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  and the Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who you see there were able to speak with Brittney Griner by phone. And then later Cherelle Griner, Brittney Griner`s wife, was able to have a private conversation by phone with her wife from the President`s study. So the question now is, where does she go?\par \par \uc2 Senior administration officials are telling NBC News that Brittney Griner is being flown to a military medical facility in San Antonio for care and treatment effectively to make sure that she is physically well and to check on her. You can imagine the mental and physical ordeal that Griner would have had over the course of nearly 10 months behind bars. She was there first attain February 17th of this year. We are told that Cherelle Griner will be flying to San Antonio where she will have the first opportunity to greet her wife.\par \par \uc2 But for the White House, this is a major diplomatic achievement. But as you note, it does come with some significant frustration, the inability to be able to get Paul Whelan home as well. Whelan, a US businessman, a former US Marine remains detained in Russia. The US says that his detention is unacceptable and that it is wrongful.\par \par \uc2 And now for the first time, we are hearing from the family of Paul Whelan, his brother, David Whelan, releasing a statement just moments ago celebrating the release of Brittney Griner but saying despite the possibility that there might be an exchange without Paul, our family is still devastated. I can`t even fathom how Paul will feel when he learns.\par \par \uc2 The exchange of Brittney Griner, we are told, came according to US officials, for a man by the name of Viktor Bout, a convicted Russian arms dealer who had already served 14 years. He had seven years left on his sentence. And it`s a significant swap. This was a stinging swap for the US to have to make. We`re told by senior officials here that the US did everything in its power to try to bring Whelan home as well. But the Russians made it very clear that it was going to be Brittney Griner or no one. The US continues to deny that Whelan was a spy, but his espionage case, Savannah and Hoda, at this point, the Russians are not willing to turn Whelan over. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And does it mean -- and Viktor Bout, as you mentioned, obviously a convicted arms dealer referred to by some in the US as a merchant of death. It was his trade that secured the release of Brittney Griner this morning.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, we`re going to turn out to NBC`s chief foreign affairs correspondent Andrea Mitchell who broke this story earlier for us. Andrea, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Good morning, Hoda and Savannah. It is as we say bittersweet because Paul Whelan is not out, but you saw the joy on Cherelle Griner`s face and her words with Griner being out. And I think that it`s really notable in the statement from David Whelan, that he says that he is so glad that she`s on her way home, that as the family member of a hostage, an American hostage, he fully understands the joy of this.\par \par \uc2 And the Biden administration made the right decision in bringing her home, validating the very tough decision that they had to make. And what a senior official just said to me is, we had no choice. The Russians at the end of these negotiations where they were trying to figure out a second person because the Russians wanted what they called parity, two for two, but they wanted a spy and the US said we don`t have a spy in our custody. And so what they said was, according to the senior official, the choice was one, meaning Griner, or none. And they were prepared to cancel Griner`s release if the US had insisted on trying to get Paul Whelan out.\par \par \uc2 [08:50:04]\par \par \uc2 And with the prior notification this time, unlike last spring when there was another false start, with the prior notification to the Whelan family that this was going to happen. And they were, again, going to be disappointed. David Whelan says that that made a big difference. He was able to prepare his parents, who are 85 and 83 years old, but that really will not be coming home for Christmas. But happily, Brittney Griner is.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s an incredibly thoughtful statement from David Whelan and he does express his joy and relief for Brittney Griner, but he calls it a catastrophe for his brother.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about Brittney Griner. We don`t want to miss the moment here, Andrea. She has been freed after nearly 10 months. Can you talk about the type of conditions she may have been kept in? I know at the very end there she was sent to a notorious penal colony in Russia.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Exactly. At the beginning of November after her appeal was lost, she was taken from the detention jail where she had been at least more accessible to occasional visits from US embassy officials and taken to this penal colony. And the conditions, we understand, were terrible. That the rule that they were fed, not nutritious, no hot water, one bathroom for as many as 80 women prisoners, very little exercise.\par \par \uc2 And this woman is six foot nine (inaudible) had already been kept in a cell that was way too small for her. She`s missed, you know, an entire season 10 months of physical fitness and of conditioning, and of her career at the peak of an athlete`s life and career. So she has suffered immeasurable loss. We`re just hoping when she gets to that medical facility in San Antonio that the wonderful doctors who have taken care of all of our service members for so many years of conflicts, that they will know exactly what to do about PTSD, about whatever else she may be suffering from, and about getting back in shape. And that`s the best option. And near nearer to Phoenix than the US than then being in Washington, at Walter Reed, another facility. San Antonio is supposed to be a great facility.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And it`s worth kind of underscoring why she was arrested in the first place. They originally said that she was charged with smuggling drugs. And was she was contending that she just had some cannabis oil that she had medical permission to use for some of her ailments. So that was the genesis of why --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: In the context at the very moment that Russia was launching its war in Ukraine. So many people, Andrea, feel she was swept up in geopolitics.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Absolutely, it was February 17th when they were about to launch the war in February 24th. And the fact is it was less than a gram. There was no testimony. And I watched all of the trial, no testimony in any of these events that it was more than, you know, less than a gram of cannabis oil, medically prescribed by the physicians, the sports doctors in Phoenix. So there`s no question that she was not doing anything that would warrant this.\par \par \uc2 In any case, even if there weren`t -- if she weren`t being held hostage geopolitically, there`s no excuse at all.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And it`s also, Andrea, worth noting this is exactly what she said when she was in trial. I never meant to hurt anybody. I never meant to put in jeopardy the Russian population. This is what she had to say after the conviction. I`ve never meant to break laws here. I made an honest mistake. I mean, that`s what she contended. We have Peter Alexander at the White House.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Peter at the White House and I want to talk about the Whelan family because the statement, which I said is quite lengthy and very nuanced, very generous of course to Brittney Griner. But also it`s tough on the administration, past and present, saying that the family wishes it had been more assertive to get Paul`s release. Does that work continue or is this just a dead end? Russia saying we want you to give us a spy, the USA we don`t have any spies, and that`s the end of it?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, the US war continues as the President himself indicated in his remarks earlier today, but as you see they are facing a brick wall in terms of the Russian Federation right now, their unwillingness to turn over Paul Whelan, who you`ll remember was picked up back in 2018. He is a US businessman, but notably, he is a former US Marine. And it`s on that basis that Russia has been making its case. The US says wrongfully that he was there serving as a US spy. The US has repeatedly denied that.\par \par \uc2 We are now hearing from some of the officials here as this news is breaking. They are saying this is a big day. It is a great day they say but it would sure be a lot better in the words of one of these officials if Paul was coming home as well. So that`s what makes this particularly bittersweet right now.\par \par \uc2 And we should note, I mean, this is the President ultimately who signed off on this. But Victor Bout, he is a significant prisoner that the US had in custody. As we have said a convicted arms dealer, the merchant of death, Savannah, as you noted earlier. There were conversations in the back and forth that took place over the course of the last several months where the Russians appear to have expressed interest into one of their citizens who was an assassin, who was being held in Germany by the \uc2 German government\uc2 . But that was a nonstarter in the course of the back and forth.\par \par \uc2 [08:55:20]\par \par \uc2 But as Andrea indicated, at the end of the day, when the Russians said they would give up Brittney Griner and Brittney Griner, the president of United States made the decision to go with it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Peter, thank you very much. This has been NBC News Special Report covering the release of Brittney Griner. And we`ve learned this morning she has been traded, as Peter mentioned, for a notorious Russian arms dealer who had been convicted and served about three quarters of his sentence, Victor Bout. That trade has secured her release. She is on route to San Antonio where we understand she`ll be receiving medical treatment.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And it was nice to see her wife, Cherelle, up there at the podium saying that at this moment, although there`s still a lot going on, she`s going to take this moment to pause and smile. So it`s important to mark the moment what has happened today.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Absolutely. A lot of work behind the scenes and false starts that we had seen earlier, reports that there would be a prisoner swap, it did not pan out. They continue to work on it. These images coming to us from the White House, from inside the Oval Office this morning. We`ll continue to keep a close eye and it will bring you any updates. More coverage on NBC Nightly News and on NBC News Now. For now, most will go back to TODAY.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS AND WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221208eic80002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc51}{\*\bkmkend toc51}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for November 23, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Cal Perry, Tom Costello, Erin McLaughlin, Gadi  Schwartz, Gabe Gutierrez, Blayne Alexander, Brian Cheung \par \uc2 3,977 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, another deadly mass shooting in America, this time at a Virginia \uc2 Walmart\uc2  just before Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The gunman, a store manager laughing before opening fire in the \uc2 Walmart\uc2  break room according to witnesses and police. At least six killed before the shooter took his own life. The horror unfolding as the store was filled with holiday shoppers. We`re on the scene.\par \par \uc2 It comes just days after the massacre at an LGBTQ club in Colorado. The suspect making a first court appearance today slumped over in a wheelchair with visible facial injuries. What happened at that hearing?\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, the busiest day on the roads as nearly 55 million Americans travel for Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 And the holiday storm threat. We`re tracking it.\par \par \uc2 Russia`s new missile strikes on Ukraine. A maternity ward hit. A newborn infant killed.\par \par \uc2 The first bombings in Jerusalem in years. The deadly blasts being called a terrorist attack.\par \par \uc2 New details in the murders of four University of Idaho students. And now, the other unsolved stabbing case. Police are looking at it. Is there a link?\par \par \uc2 The countdown to Black Friday. Where to find the best deals despite soaring inflation?\par \par \uc2 And the spirit of a holiday. What kids say they`re most thankful for this year.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. We keep ending in the same place, don`t we? Morning victims who could have been any of us, scratching our heads over possible motives, and reciting the growing list. All those places where mass violence has snuffed out or threatened to take innocent lives.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, just days after the Colorado Springs attack, we`re absorbing details of the latest horrific mass shooting in this country. Six people at a Chesapeake, Virginia \uc2 Walmart\uc2  shot to death by a long-time store employee who opened fire with a handgun. Several others were wounded. A survivor is saying the attack in an employee break room was preceded by the gunman`s laughter. The 31-year-old took his own life before police arrived.\par \par \uc2 The killer described by a senior law enforcement official as a disgruntled employee. But what that means as to a specific motive is unclear. In a statement, President Biden speaking with many are thinking tonight, there are now even more tables across the country that will have empty seats this Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Cal Perry is in Chesapeake with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tragedy in Chesapeake, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How many medics do we have left in the city right now?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Currently six medics available.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Go ahead and dispatch all of them.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: 911 receiving its first call to the \uc2 Walmart\uc2  Supercenter at 10:12 p.m. Officers arriving within two minutes. We got a whole scene out here but it`s crazy out here right now. They everywhere. The 31-year-old shooter who police say worked at the store since 2010 came armed with a handgun and multiple magazines. One senior law enforcement official tells NBC News he was a disgruntled employee.\par \par \uc2 BRIANA TYLER, WITNESS: I literally was frozen. I just stood there. I`d like I literally could be dead right now. It didn`t kick in until I looked at my coworker and she was bleeding out of her neck.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Six people were killed ranging in age from 16 to 70. At least four others were wounded before the shooter turned the gun on himself.\par \par \uc2 MARK SOLESKY, CHIEF, CHESAPEAKE, VIRGINIA POLICE DEPARTMENT: The investigation is still ongoing, and we`ll be processing that scene for days.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): The most important thing we need to do is come around families and support them. It`s unimaginable what they`re dealing with today.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: 24-year-old Jalon Jones had been working at the \uc2 Walmart\uc2  for about a year. He`s recovering at a nearby hospital.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY SHUPE, MOTHER OF JALON JONES: He knew he was being shot. He made it to the front of the store. And then when he made it to the front door, he was shot again.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: 28-year-old Jessie says it was just her fifth day on the job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I saw a lot of bad thing that happened to really good people. And I didn`t understand it. And it didn`t feel real.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: She says she was in the break room when the shooter came in laughing and started firing.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He got the gun pointed at me and he looks at me and he puts it up in the air. And all he said was just to go home. I ran. I didn`t look back. I want to look back.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Do you know why he let you go, Jessie? Do you have any idea?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, I know.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: So far, there`s no word on motive, and it`s unclear if the victims were targeted. Authorities today cordoned off this vehicle in the parking lot saying it`s a vehicle of interest.\par \par \uc2 DR. JESSICA BURGESS, SENTARA NORFOLK GENERAL HOSPITAL: It`s heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Chesapeake, Virginia, just the latest community left grappling with yet another senseless shooting.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:01]\par \par \uc2 BURGESS: It was just two days ago that I was contacting one of my colleagues in Colorado Springs wishing her well and checking in to see how she was doing after she had just taken care of shooting victims. So it`s very disheartening that now I`m in the same position with my colleagues from across the country checking in on me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Cal, what are investigators focusing on right now?\par \par \uc2 PERRY (on camera): Lester, investigators are focused on any connection they can find between the victims and the shooter. For example, if any of the victims worked with the shooter, that could go a long way in finding a motive. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Cal Perry tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado Springs today, the first court appearance by video from jail for the suspect in the killing of five people at that LGBTQ nightclub four days ago. The suspect who was subdued and beaten by club patrons was released from the hospital yesterday. The suspect, on public defenders, identify as non-binary person, was ordered held without bail and faces possible murder and hate crime charges.\par \par \uc2 We have been through this so many times this year. By one count, there have been 608 mass shootings defined as four or more people shot. That`s not far from last year`s record. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): On this Thanksgiving Eve, America is reckoning with the violence that is tearing up the national fabric. This afternoon, four high school students shot in Philly. Every week, nearly 13 mass shootings, so many we too often forget those that happened last week or last month.\par \par \uc2 21 children and teachers killed in Uvalde, Texas. 10 people gunned down in a Buffalo grocery store. Five and Raleigh, four in Memphis, four in Tulsa, six in Sacramento. The list is excruciatingly long.\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA, ATTORNEY GENERAL, CALIFORNIA: We know that this is America`s disease, unique in the world.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: David Hogg, one of the high school students from Parkland, Florida where 17 people died, tweeted at 2:24 this morning, I am tired of waking up to texts about another mass shooting in America.\par \par \uc2 DAVID HOGG, PARKLAND SHOOTING SURVIVOR: It`s going to continue happening. And we have to work together to actually stop this from happening.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Nationwide, 49 people die in gun violence every day.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN DETTELBACH, DIRECTOR, ATF: My biggest fear is that somehow as a country, we become callous or to kind of accept or think that this is OK, that this is somehow part of our national story.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Pediatrician Emily Lieberman was with her children in Highland Park, Illinois when a gunman killed seven people on July 4. Now, she`s leading a group of doctors demanding the Senate pass more than the gun safety measures enacted after Uvalde.\par \par \uc2 EMILY LIEBERMAN, PEDIATRICIAN: Now, the number one cause of death of children in this country is gun violence. It`s time to start tackling gun violence as a public health problem and listening to people like physicians who know how to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): The country holding a mirror to itself with politicians expected back in D.C. next week. And the decades-old gun control debate likely to be a dinner table topic tomorrow. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll take a turn out of the busy Thanksgiving travel rush. Almost 55 million Americans expected to travel this year nearly hitting pre pandemic levels. And we`re also tracking storms that could bring heavy rain to the south and Gulf Coast tomorrow. Erin McLaughlin joins us now from Los Angeles. Erin, what`s the traffic like there?\par \par \uc2 ERIN MCLAUGHLIN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, things are looking pretty good here, Lestrer, especially for Los Angeles. Nearly 49 million Americans are expected to hit the road this Thanksgiving. That`s slightly compared to last year with the national average price of a gas, a gallon of gas, just below that $5.00 mark we saw back in June.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, across the nation`s airports tonight, some good news for the most part. Flights have been on time thanks largely to clear skies and good weather across the country. Here in Los Angeles at LAX airport, they`re expecting some 200,000 passengers today alone. It`s the busiest they`ve been since the beginning of the pandemic. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Erin McLaughlin in Los Angeles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Two bombings in Jerusalem today killed a teenager and injured at least 18 others including two Americans. The explosions were near bus stops during the morning rush hour. Israeli police say the bombs were left at those locations and blamed Palestinians for the attacks. It`s the latest in a surge of violence between Israelis and Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 And in Ukraine, Russia launching a new barrage of missile strikes. Ukrainian officials say a newborn infant was killed when a missile hit a hospital maternity ward in the southern part of the country. Much of Ukraine without power after Russians attack the energy infrastructure in Kyiv and several other cities.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, new details from the police in the killings of those four college students in Idaho. Lots of leads and progress reported in the case but still no suspect identified. Gadi Schwartz has the latest.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GADI SCHWARTZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For the 11th day in a row, investigators saying still no suspect, no motive and the killings of four Idaho university students, but trying to assure the public they are making headway.\par \par \uc2 COL. KEDRICK WILLS, IDAHO STATE POLICE: At this crime scene, we collected 103 pieces of individual evidence.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: But frustration is growing among family members who fear investigators fell behind during the first crucial 48 hours of the case.\par \par \uc2 WILLS: No tip is too small and we would urge anybody that has any information whatsoever to please provide that to help us solve this case.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Those who live in Moscow also asking about a recent dog mutilation that happened three miles away from the murders. Jim and Pam Colbert say about a month ago, someone grabbed their dog at 2:00 a.m. and skinned him in a field in front of their home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Right over there. Right over --\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Took his fur but left his unbuckled collar behind.\par \par \uc2 So, somebody actually had to unclasp it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unclasp it right here. See, that stays. And somebody had to undo it. Because if you had a knife, wouldn`t you have cut it?\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: But Moscow police say they don`t think that case has anything to do with the murders. Police also asked about an unsolved murder 300 miles away in Oregon, in which a masked intruder attacked a couple with a knife in their sleep.\par \par \uc2 JAMES FRY, CHIEF, MOSCOW, IDAHO POLICE: Actually, I had a tip come in on that case and I`ve ordered it to our tip line.\par \par \uc2 SCHWARTZ: Meanwhile, the University of Idaho giving students the option of finishing their semester remotely while families of the victims are struggling with the thought of Thanksgiving without their loved ones and praying that the killer won`t strike again. Gadi Schwartz, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Also tonight, the so-called tripledemic, RSV, the flu, and COVID, is already pushing hospitals to the brink. The U.S. facing shortages of pediatric beds, medical staff, and now even some medications. Here`s Gabe Gutierrez.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In Buffalo, some store shelves normally filled with children`s Tylenol are empty. Pharmacists say a spot shortage of the drug in Canada triggered by mounting cases of RSV is spilling over the border.\par \par \uc2 DON ARTHUR, PHARMACIST: Apparently, up in Canada, the liquid forms of Tylenol are unavailable.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: \uc2 Johnson and Johnson\uc2 , the maker of Tylenol, says there`s no overall shortage in the U.S., telling NBC News we are experiencing high consumer demand and they`re doing everything we can to make sure people have access to the products they need.\par \par \uc2 Still, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  currently lists amoxicillin as being in short supply. And it all comes as the healthcare industry braces for a potential tripledemic this winter. RSV, flu, and COVID colliding.\par \par \uc2 In New York, Ella Rose Giam (PH) is home after a week in the hospital.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was surreal. Like, seeing your daughter on a breathing mask and she`s only three and a half.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Adding to the strain on the system, an increasingly dire staffing shortage. More than half a million people in the healthcare and social services industries quit their positions in September.\par \par \uc2 Were you burnt out?\par \par \uc2 Extremely. Yes, sir.\par \par \uc2 Reece Brown in Louisiana is no longer a traveling nurse.\par \par \uc2 REECE BROWN, NURSE: That joy which is literally being sucked out of us. We were doing everything that we could and we just -- it was never enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): As for COVID, the CDC says new data shows the Omicron boosters offer better protection against variants than earlier doses. Still, they are not as effective as the original COVID vaccine was against the initial virus. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe Gutierrez, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, new abortion restrictions and the agonizing decisions they are forcing some families to make. Our in-depth look at the issue coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: The Georgia Supreme Court today reinstated a ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy while it considers an appeal. And while a few states including Georgia make exceptions when a fetus has life-threatening problems, most states with new abortion restrictions do not. Blayne Alexandre has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Jill Hurtle could not wait to become a mom. She still remembers the second she found out.\par \par \uc2 JILL HURTLE, HAD ABORTION: I was like sobbing. I was so excited.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:02]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You felt like a mom.\par \par \uc2 HURTLE: I felt like a mom in that -- in an instant.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Her husband, Matt, dreamed of being a girl dad. They even picked out a name Ivy Grace. Every doctor`s appointment brought good news until the 18-week scan.\par \par \uc2 HURTLE: And our world`s like came crashing down. She came in and she said her heart isn`t what it is supposed to look like. I think in that exact moment, my stomach dropped and I think I truly knew that it was going to be a really severe situation.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It was. A condition known as hypoplastic left heart syndrome, HLHS. Doctor said her baby`s case was especially severe, and her daughter would likely not survive long after birth.\par \par \uc2 HURTLE: I didn`t want her to have to go through so much pain and suffering.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: After more tests and more devastating news, Jill and her husband made the painful decision.\par \par \uc2 HURTLE: If she were to live an hour or 10 days, I didn`t want her to just - - I didn`t want her to know nothing but pain. So, my protective instinct was to do what I knew what was best for her, which was to send her to heaven.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Every year, congenital or fetal anomalies impact one in every 33 babies born in the U.S. and range from minor to life-limiting or life- threatening. Since the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade, at least 16 states have successfully enacted laws further restricting abortion access. Only three have exceptions for lethal fetal anomalies.\par \par \uc2 DR. DAVID LEVINE, NEONATOLOGIST: We`re not dealing with one size fits all. We can`t make laws for one size fits all.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Dr. David Levine is a neonatologist who opposes abortion access and believes the baby should be carried to term even with a grim prognosis.\par \par \uc2 LEVINE: Most families who have decided to spend time with their babies however short it might be, by and large, have benefited from it. They`ve been able to bond with their baby.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Is it therefore a law to make that decision for a parent?\par \par \uc2 LEVINE: There are many decisions that are taken out of our hands. Is it fair for the mother to make that decision to take a baby`s life away from him.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Jill had to leave South Carolina and wait two weeks for an appointment in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 HURTLE: Those two weeks were probably the hardest two weeks I`ve ever experienced in my life. Every time you feel her kick or move, it`s just a dagger to the heart.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: She believes abortion bans should have exceptions for cases like hers, giving women the chance to make a choice, however painful.\par \par \uc2 HURTLE: I am a mom. I`m a mom to an angel in heaven. And I know that she`s at peace.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Blayne Alexander, NBC News, Charleston, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, as the holiday shopping rush begins, top tips on where and how you can save in our series "RACE TO DELIVER."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: As you may have noticed, many retailers have already rolled out their Black Friday deals. So, where can you save the most? NBC`s Brian Cheung has been shopping around for our series "RACE TO DELIVER."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHEUNG, NBC NEWS BUSINESS AND DATA REPORTER (voiceover): Tonight, amid a growing unease in the nation`s economy, the dash for holiday deals is on. With consumer prices up 7.7 percent compared to last season and one in three households saying their finances are worse off from a year ago, shoppers are left with tighter budgets.\par \par \uc2 TRAE BODGE, SMART SHOPPING EXPERT, TRUETRAE.COM: Retailers are aware of the fact that consumers only have so much to spend.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: That`s why even before Black Friday, many retailers are already offering deep discounts vying for your holiday dollars. The most discounted items this season, everything from TVs, laptops, to home appliances, and beauty products. For electronics, consider older models for an even better deal. Many clothing retailers are also offering extra deep discounts this season in part because of excess inventory falling supply chain lock-ups last year.\par \par \uc2 BODGE: We are seeing a lot of overstock still and retailers have been spending the last couple of months trying to sell through that merchandise.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Which could lead to even deeper discounts.\par \par \uc2 BODGE: I do expect to see discounts deeper than usual in categories like casual apparel, TVs, furniture, home decor. And so, in addition to the exciting cyber weekend sales that we`ll see, we`ll also see kind of extra discounts as retailers look to clear out all those overstocks.\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG: Experts say shopping with strategy will save you big bucks. Start by creating a shopping list with a spending cap. Then compare prices online and in store. And check your credit card for additional discounts and offers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHEUNG (on camera): And don`t forget about gift cards which are always a hot item every year. Many can actually be bought at a discount. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Brian, thanks. Up next, what are you thankful for? Some children will tell us right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, a lot of news has been especially grim this week, but we thought you might be uplifted or find a smile in listening to the gratitude expressed by some New York school kids we spoke to about Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): At Manhattan Country School, today`s lesson is all about giving thanks.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you God for a mother.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And after two years of holidays overshadowed by COVID, many students told us they`re grateful for a Thanksgiving that feels a little more normal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m thankful for like, family and friends because like, during COVID, I wasn`t able to see -- I wasn`t able to see like, a lot of my friends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m very grateful that me and my family -- or my family and I have been healthy and happy and able to see each other.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m really thankful for the school and for my teachers and my friends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m thankful for my dog. She`s good at comforting people especially me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m thankful for my family because they give me comfort and I just love them.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Of course, on Thanksgiving --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I love cooking.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Comfort also comes in the form of turkey, stuffing, and potatoes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We bake and we coconut we have this huge feast at the end.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We make an angel fruitcake which I really love to do with my grandma.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m definitely looking forward to eating because we do cook pretty good Thanksgiving meal.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: When you`re in grade school, there`s something else to look forward to.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We get a lot of days off from school.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The first thing I think about it is wrapped up in a blanket drinking hot chocolate and watching a movie, and I just get really excited about that.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A moment of peace with the people you love, to give thanks, isn`t that what the day is all about?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When I think about the holidays, I think about community and spending time with the people that I love and giving back to them and hopefully receiving the same.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And thank all of you for being here tonight. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS. Be sure to tune in for the Macy`s Thanksgiving Day Parade at 9:00 Eastern here on NBC and Peacock. Thank you for watching and Happy Thanksgiving, everyone. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221124eibn00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc52}{\*\bkmkend toc52}\b \uc2 MTP for November 20, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kimberly Atkins Stohr \par \uc2 8,732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST (voiceover): This Sunday, my interview with Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For me, the fuse was lit in mid-November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: His inside account of January 6th and how he broke with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PENCE: All I know is that what the president asked me to do, I would have violated my oath to the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What he thinks should happen now that Trump is running for office again.\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Do you think a crime was committed?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I don`t know if it is criminal to listen to bad advice from lawyers.\par \par \uc2 TODD (voiceover): His thoughts on the midterms.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: We had some surprising and disappointing results.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And who he blames for Trump`s election denial.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I was disappointed in \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 ` performance as chief of staff.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Plus his criticism of Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  for approving the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of Trump`s Florida home.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I have great concerns about his judgment in the leadership of the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And his belief that the Supreme Court`s decision overturning Roe v. Wade was the single most important achievement of the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I celebrate that from really the depths of my heart.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The former vice president opens up, as he considers his own run for the White House.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for inside (inaudible), "New York Times" Chief White House Correspondent Peter Baker, Republican strategist Brendan Buck, Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ," and Anna Palmer, co-founder of Punchbowl News.\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Welcome to Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press" with Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Good Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 We are following the breaking news reports of a shooting overnight at a gay nightclub in Colorado Springs, Colorado. At least five people are dead, and another 18 so far are wounded.\par \par \uc2 Police say the suspect was injured, is in custody, and is currently receiving treatment at a local hospital. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is already on the scene and assisting local law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on their \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page, the club writes, "Club Q is devastated by the senseless attack on our community. We thank the quick reactions of heroic customers that subdued the gunman and ended this hate attack."\par \par \uc2 Do stay tuned to NBC News for more updates on this shooting. You can get those at nbcnews.com as well.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to politics, Republicans have taken back control of the House, squeaking into a narrow majority. But the biggest question after a disappointing midterm performance, the third disappointing election cycle in a row for the GOP, will Republicans rally behind \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  yet again?\par \par \uc2 Seven-hundred-and-twenty-one days before the next presidential election, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became one of a small group of ex-presidents to announce he will again seek another term even after being defeated. Just one of those ex-presidents, Grover Cleveland, has been successful doing this.\par \par \uc2 The timing of the announcement at his gated Palm Beach resort appears to be an effort to get ahead of several federal criminal investigations, and on Friday, in reaction, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  appointed a special counsel to take over investigations he was overseeing into the former president`s role in the events leading up to the January 6th attack on the Capitol, as well as the former president`s decision to retain classified government documents at Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , UNITED STATES ATTORNEY GENERAL: Based on recent developments, including the former president`s announcement that he is a candidate for president in the next election, and the sitting president`s stated intention to be a candidate as well, I have concluded that it is in the public interest to appoint a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The attorney general`s goal is to create some political distance between that investigation and obviously the Biden administration. That`s the reason for this appointment.\par \par \uc2 Jack Smith is a longtime federal prosecutor, who has mostly -- most recently been working at the \uc2 International Criminal Court\uc2  at the Hague.\par \par \uc2 Now if Trump falters in his presidential campaign, there are a handful of Republican hopefuls that are ready to challenge him, including his former vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Pence`s new memoir, "So Help Me God" actually was out perhaps not so coincidently, the same day that his running mate, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , announced. And he describes Trump`s pressure campaign in the book on him to refuse to certify the 2020 elections, which of course culminated when rioters threatened Mike Pence`s life.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to the former vice president at the Reagan Presidential Library in Simi Valley, California. And I began by asking him about his likely run for president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The former president has said it`s disloyal for some of the Republican Party to challenge him, and he`s speaking about not just you but Governor DeSantis, Secretary Pompeo. Do you accept the idea that running against him is disloyal?\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I think the American people love competition. My loyalty is to my faith, to my values, and to America.\par \par \uc2 And I think if we`re a part of that debate next year, and our family will sort that out before the end of the year, Chuck --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- or if we`re just chipping in from the sidelines and others are in that contest, I think a good, healthy debate over the direction of the country is warranted.\par \par \uc2 I mean, since the first days of the Biden/Harrison administration, it almost seems as though we have a new Democratic administration intent on weakening America at home and abroad. Whether it`s the disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan, whether it be the gusher of spending that ignited the worst inflation in 40 years, the war on energy that`s caused gasoline price to go through the roof, our crime wave in our major cities, and of course, after undoing all the successful policies that secured our southern border, we now have the worst crisis at our southern border in American history.\par \par \uc2 And so I truly do believe that Republican primary voters want to hear a strong debate, they`ll want to find our best standard bear (ph) so that having won back the House, which became official this last week, we`ll have a chance to win back America in 2024.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You laid out a reason why you think the Biden administration has led America down a path that you don`t like. Why do you think voters didn`t accept that premise in 2022? They clearly didn`t in the battleground states. Why do you think that was?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, they did in many states around the country. It`s a -- in the state of New York, four new Republican congressmen elected, may well be the margin of the majority itself. And the state of Iowa, for the first time in a half a century, they`ll have all Republican members of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 But, Chuck, I take your point. A win is a win. I`m glad to see Kevin McCarthy will accept the gavel to be the next speaker of the House, but I`d like to see more Republicans elected. And I`d liked to seen Republicans win the Senate and more governorships around the country.\par \par \uc2 But for me, the common denominator had to do with whether or not candidates were focused on the future. As I traveled around the country --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That sounds like that`s code for whether or not candidates were sort of in the Trump spell.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I don`t --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, I think you`re trying to be polite. I understand that. But it does seem that what you`re saying is anybody that was close to Trump didn`t do well.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: But I think it`s broader than that, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 For me, as I travel -- and I went to 35 states in the last year and a half, campaigning for all kinds of men and women, running for statewide office, and for the House and the Senate. And as I sort through the results, there really is a common denominator, candidates that were focused on the issues that people are facing today and solutions for tomorrow, focused on the future did quite well, but candidates that were focused on the past, candidates that were focused on relitigating the 2020 election did not fare as well. When -- and we had some surprising and disappointing results.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You stopped short of saying that the former president is unfit to serve again. But as former defense secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  says he`s unfit for office, another former defense secretary \uc2 Jim Mattis\uc2  says Trump has no moral compass. Bill Barr, the attorney general, he has neither the temperament nor the persuasive powers. Judge Luttig, somebody you admire, I know, Trump and his apologists are a clear and present danger to American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Is he fit to serve as president of the United States for another four years?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I really do believe that`s a decision for the American people. And President Trump is now --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t your opinion --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- you think your opinion matters to the American people? I mean, and -- how about this, the fact that you may run, are you sending that message without saying it?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I`ll keep you posted on whether I`m going to run or not, but I do think we`ll have better choices. And -- but what I won`t do is I won`t join those that want to dismiss the four years of our administration and all that we accomplished for the American people. And as I write in "So Help Me God" the president`s leadership was central to our success in everything that was accomplished, whether it be rebuilding our military, or decisions he made as commander in chief to allow our armed forces to crush the ISIS caliphate and take out their leader, to allow our armed forces to take out \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2 , a man responsible for the death of hundreds of Americans service members during the war in Iraq, and it was the president`s vision to cut taxes and roll back regulation, and his direct engagement on Capitol Hill with members of Congress that saw us pass the tax cuts and reforms that saw 7 million jobs created, unemployment at a 50- year low.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 PENCE: And so the policies that (ph), and energy and securing our border, and three Supreme Court justices are all a great source of pride to me. And in my book, "So Help Me God" I try and make sure and give the president his due. I was proud to be his vice president and advance those policies that worked for the American people against an avalanche of opposition by the Democratic Party from day one, and frankly, by many in the national media, who were preoccupied with controversies, conspiracies and the Russia, Russia, Russia message that dominated for two-and-a-half years.\par \par \uc2 And so I want to give the president his due. And I --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re making an ends justify the means.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, many people that want to draw broad conclusions about the president or anyone else in the public sphere, want to dismiss the balance of the record. In my book I try and make sure I present it, but also, Chuck, I think I`m very clear in saying that the administration did not end well. That I took at strong stand.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- that had to be in public because every other time the president and I differed, as vice president I thought it was important that I kept those things in private. But when it came down --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Did \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  damage the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: When it came down to the peaceful transfer of power and doing my duty under the Constitution of the United States, we had the tragic day of January 6th and its aftermath. All of that, in totality, is the president`s record and the record the American people will decide upon.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you want the public to try to split this. Are you concerned that Donald Trump`s behavior post-election 2022 damaged the Republican Party and is the -- or the 2022 midterms sort of evidence?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I think it really does depend on our candidates, and whether or not Republican candidates around the country will learn the lessons that we learned through that tragic day. Frankly, in many of the elections that took place in the midterms -- I`ll never forget. I write about it in the book, it was the night before January 6th, the president and I were alone in the Oval Office in what was ultimately a tense conversation.\par \par \uc2 I had made my position clear for weeks to him that I -- as vice president, I believed I did not have the authority to return or reject electoral votes when the House convened to count the electoral votes for president and vice president.\par \par \uc2 But there was great tension in the room. And I -- but at the outset, I remember the president looking at me and he pointed out the window at the crowd that was gathering just off the South Lawn and in Ellipse, and he said, did you see that crowd out there? And I said, I did, Mr. President. And he said, those people love us, and I said, those people love you. And he said, well, that`s probably true.\par \par \uc2 And then I looked at him very seriously and I said, can those people love the Constitution, Mr. President? So I believe Republican voters and the Republican Party`s future is ultimately grounded in us producing men and women who will stand up for the ideals enshrined in the Constitution and the Declaration, ideals \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  stood for without apology and that`ll carry our movement forward for generations.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Does his behavior in January of 2021 tell you that he doesn`t respect the Constitution?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, all I know is that what the president asked me to do, I would have violated my oath to the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So that`s not respecting the -- he certainly didn`t respect it enough to ask you to be disloyal to your oath.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The president of the United States in that moment was receiving counsel from attorneys who were telling him, as the Bible says, what his itching ears wanted to hear. He had, beginning in mid-November, dismissed some of the extraordinary and capable attorneys that were serving in the White House and serving in the campaign, and replaced them with a gaggle of lawyers who brought in conspiracy theories and made promises that they never kept.\par \par \uc2 I write in the book about how it would be a year or after that tragic day that one of those lawyers, \uc2 Sidney Powell\uc2 , actually said in a court filing, that no reasonable person would have assumed that what we were saying was factual, now she tells us. But the president was -- was -- he was receiving terrible advice from people who not only shouldn`t have been in the Oval Office, they shouldn`t have been let on the White House grounds.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, you made that point a couple of times. You praised \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  for how he managed that Oval Office, and managed who came in and came out of it. How much -- how much of what happened on January 6th is on the -- on the hands of \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The president has got to rely on his senior team. The term gatekeeper sounds offensive to the American mind. You think of open government and access. But what General \uc2 John Kelly\uc2  did, when he became White House chief of staff, and frankly what all the best White House chiefs of staff have done throughout history, is make sure the only people that get into the Oval Office are people that have the credibility to be there. And they -- and make sure that the president understands what they`re coming to talk about so he`s in a position to make the decisions that only a president can make.\par \par \uc2 General Kelly created that order, and sadly when he left, \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2 , I think, did his level best as an interim chief of staff to bring that about. But -- but I was disappointed in Mark Meadows`s performance as chief of staff, particularly at the end. But from very early on, when it was clear he had talked the president out of White House coronavirus press briefings, and in -- in a very real sense, I think -- I think his tenure as chief of staff did not serve the president well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It sounds like it`s just not on January 6th. Are you saying a lot of the COVID misinformation that sometimes came out of President Trump`s mouth was due to Mark Meadows`s lack of gatekeeping?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, heh, let`s talk about COVID. I have some great chapters in my book.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I do want to talk about that. And I -- and I plan on it. But I want to ask you about it specifically. Did he -- was he a bad gatekeeper then, too?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, no, what -- I -- I`ve always believed that in a public health emergency, more is more when it comes to information.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: We very quickly began press briefings on a daily basis. And I think they served the country well. But -- but once we went through that early difficult period with COVID and -- and the new chief of staff started his tenure, the pressure began a month or so into the pandemic to move away from the briefings.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You imply in that book, that that meeting with the lawyers, the campaign lawyers, and then on the speakerphone is \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and some of these other lawyers, you concluded that in some ways they -- they helped foment the insurrection on January 6th. That that -- that was the moment that you seemed to go down -- that they went down the wrong road. That -- is that how you -- how you intended it to be read?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: For me the fuse was lit in -- in mid-November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In that phone call by \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: No, the Oval Office was filled with advisers. The president`s campaign advisers were there, the -- other senior members of the staff. On the speakerphone were the -- the new lawyers. They were vying for the opportunity to lead the election challenges. Justin Clark, who is an extraordinary attorney and a man of great integrity, was telling the president about the dozens of lawsuits that had been filed challenging election results around the country. And he was painting something of a pessimistic view about the prospects of those.\par \par \uc2 But the campaign had every right to file those -- those lawsuits. And that`s the process that we have. States conduct elections. And if there are questions about irregularities or fraud, they`re to be brought into the courts that can review the evidence and the law. But they were painting a picture to the president that things were likely not going to be successful.\par \par \uc2 But when \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  said over the speakerphone that your lawyers are not telling you the truth, Mr. President, things got very heated. That in that moment, it was a new low.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: There was shouting, there was yelling, and -- but in the aftermath of that meeting, the president made the decision to replace his capable campaign lawyers with this widening circle of outside attorneys who ultimately, ultimately led him to the conclusion that I had the authority to overturn the election, which was demonstrably and historically false.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think the president committed a criminal act in fomenting the insurrection? Do you think a crime was committed?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I -- I don`t know if it is criminal to listen to bad advice from lawyers. The truth is, what -- what the president was repeating is what he was hearing from that gaggle of attorneys around him. And, you know, presidents, just as like all of us that have served in public life, you have to rely on your team. You have to rely on the credibility of the people around you.\par \par \uc2 And so as -- as time goes on, I hope -- I hope we can move beyond this, and beyond that prospect. And this is really a time when our country ought to be healing. And -- and frankly the Justice Department`s actions have been exposed from during our administration where \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents were advancing political agendas, falsifying records, using what came to be found out to be opposition research paid for by the Clinton campaign and the Democratic Party, the so-called dossier.\par \par \uc2 I -- and then this -- this past summer, when we see the -- the Justice Department execute a search warrant against the personal residence of a former president of the United States, I think the American people -- I think the American people join me in hoping we can move past this contention.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But is he above the law? I mean, if he violated the law, should he be held to the same standard that (INAUDIBLE) to be held to?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, Chuck, no one is above the law. But I -- I would hope the Justice Department would give careful consideration before they take any additional steps in this matter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  weighs in on Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , and his decision to authorize a search of Trump`s Florida home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I have grave concerns about his judgment and leadership at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Central to the special counsel investigation will be Trump`s removal of government records, at least 300 with classified markings at his Mar-a-Lago home. In the affidavit used to obtain the search warrant in August, federal prosecutors cited an obstruction of justice statute, because Trump evaded a subpoena for months. Justice was trying to do this without the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ; and as well as a section of the Espionage Act, which makes it illegal to possess national security documents, which could be obtained by a foreign power.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, Trump`s former Attorney General Bill Barr said he actually believes the Justice Department probably already has the evidence they need to indict Trump. But in my sit-down with Pence, taped on Thursday, the day before the special counsel announcement was made, the former vice president struck a different tone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I`ve not hesitated to criticize the president when I think he was wrong. And -- and clearly possessing classified documents in an unprotected area is not proper. But I have to tell you, I was on the you Judiciary Committee for 10 years in the House of Representatives. I -- I know how the Justice Department works. And there had to be many other way to say resolve those issues and collect...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sounds like they tried.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... those classified documents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They tried for a year.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: They didn`t try everything. And I have to tell you, going to the last resort of executing a search warrant against a former president of the United States, I think, sent the wrong message. It was a divisive message in this country. But I think worse, still, it was the wrong message to the wider world that looks to America as the gold standard. And -- and the idea of...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Doesn`t that send a message that nobody is above the law in this country?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... a former president...\par \par \uc2 TODD: That our democracy is so strong...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: It is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... it can handle the idea that, you know, if former presidents commit -- you know, commit a crime...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: But, Chuck, you...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... they don`t get a pass.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: But, Chuck, you follow -- you follow foreign affairs as close as anybody in your business. And it`s much more commonplace in the Third World that when one leader comes in, then they prosecute and jail their previous leader. America should never be associated with that image at all. And -- and I -- I truly do believe and hope that the Justice Department will give careful consideration to any further steps in that light.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You must have really not liked it when the president, when he was in office, he -- how many times he wanted to weaponize the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , he wanted to order (INAUDIBLE) you`ve seen these reports, perhaps he didn`t say it in front of you. But do you think the former president respects these institutions?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I -- I -- I`d let him answer that, but I never had reason to believe he didn`t respect men and women who serve in law enforcement at every level. In fact, everywhere we go around the country to this day, I have people in law enforcement thanking me for the support that we gave them, at a time when the Democratic Party was giving voice to the defund the police movement, and -- and assuming the worst every time a tragic incident happened in a police shooting. That they saw in our administration a reflexive willingness to support law enforcement, to seek the facts, find justice, make sure that justice was served.\par \par \uc2 But in all the time that I served in the Trump-Pence administration, I think it was very clear our administration stood with the men and women on the thin blue line whether they were federal law enforcement or the -- the rank and file serving on the streets of our cities.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. But you did just hit the \uc2 FBI\uc2  pretty hard yourself in some accusations you made about politicization.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, I -- I think the men and women who serve in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the rank and file, are some of the best people in the United States. I put the blame for the decision to execute a search warrant at the personal residence of a former president of the United States on the leadership of the Justice Department. It`s inconceivable to me that that wasn`t a decision that wasn`t made at the highest levels and approved at the highest levels.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The attorney general said so. He came out and he -- he owned it. They did own it.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: That`s right. That`s right. And I think he should have thought better of it, Chuck. There were preliminary steps. I want to -- I want to be very clear on this. There -- if they had additional evidence that there were additional documents, they could have gone to court...\par \par \uc2 TODD: They did.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Everything you`re describing they did before. They didn`t want to do it the way they ended up doing it.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Trust me, trust me. They didn`t do everything they could have done and avoided this incredibly divisive step that sent the wrong message to the country and the wrong message to the world.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: If anything, I want to see the Justice Department -- and I think -- I think a new Republican majority is going to be a part of this. I want to see -- I want to see the credibility of the Justice Department restored after years of politicization during the Trump-Pence administration. I mean, what went on during the time of Jim Comey`s tenure, in the 2016 campaign, the early days of our administration, the conduct of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  with regard to the Russia collusion hoax, on and on it went.\par \par \uc2 And as someone who cherishes the men and women who serve in the Justice Department, and especially the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , I think now is the time for them to be focusing on restoring public confidence in our institutions...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t have confidence...\par \par \uc2 PENCE: ... law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... in \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  as attorney general, Judge Garland?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I -- I -- I have great difficulty with the decision that he made. I -- I have grave concerns about his judgment and leadership at the Justice Department in the wake of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, more of my conversation with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and what he didn`t like about the Biden administration`s COVID response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: President Biden came in and -- and essentially empowered Dr. Fauci, I think -- I think the American people -- I think they recoiled at the approach..\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:33:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 I also spoke to former Vice President Pence about the administration`s record on the coronavirus pandemic. I asked Pence whether it troubled him, given his own role as leader of the White House Coronavirus Task Force, that Dr. Fauci has been so vilified on the right.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I think in the early days of the Coronavirus Task Force, Dr. Fauci was a great source of comfort to millions of Americans. He had an easygoing bedside manner that he communicated at the podium.\par \par \uc2 And as I said, we -- we always had a good rapport in those days. Because Dr. Fauci, in my time leading the task force, always stayed in his lane. He was always the first one to say, I`m the scientist at the table. I`m going to tell you what the scientific perspective is.\par \par \uc2 But he would almost invariably say, I know the president and you have a lot more to consider than just that. You have to think of the broader impact on the American public and on the country as a whole.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, for me, in those early days, he made an enormous difference in the development of a very balanced policy, empowering states, igniting the entrepreneurial spirit of companies around the country to develop the supplies, the masks, ultimately the medicines and the vaccines in record time.\par \par \uc2 But I must tell you that, when -- when the Biden administration came in and essentially turned over the management of COVID to Dr. Fauci, I was not surprised to see -- to see them put all of their emphasis on vaccines. And then, after having promised that they would not mandate vaccines, then seeking to mandate vaccines on Americans. It`s still a mandate on health care workers and military personnel today. That was the doctor approach.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:01]\par \par \uc2 And I think when President Biden came in and essentially empowered Dr. Fauci, I think -- I think the American people, I think they recoiled at that approach. And -- and sadly, the Biden administration lost more Americans in their first year with COVID than we did in our first year, a year where we had none of the tools, few supplies, no testing, ultimately no medicines until the end.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is it on them --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: But I think it was that --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- that people wouldn`t take vaccines? I mean, this vaccine, you were very pro-vaccine. You made sure people saw you get the vaccine.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The reason we had all these deaths was people that wouldn`t get vaccinated. That is, it was a stew that was boiling around in some of these, you know, Internet conservative circles.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I know that`s -- I know that`s the, forgive me for saying, but that`s the cliche in the national debate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s not a cliche.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: It was all about vaccines.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s the numbers, the counties.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: But, again, Chuck -- with respect, Chuck --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I mean, look at the rural counties. No, I understand, but the data is the data.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: The Biden administration focused exclusively on vaccines. Our administration focused on empowering states to provide them with the supplies, countermeasures for nursing homes, making sure that we had therapeutics available and distributed.\par \par \uc2 There should have been an Operation Warp Speed on therapeutics. The Biden administration literally, literally stopped the development of testing and ran short of testing by the spring of their first year. There was no effort to develop lifesaving therapeutics around the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t accept the idea that maybe both administrations struggled with testing? I mean, let`s --\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, no, we didn`t have COVID testing when I was tapped to lead the White House coronavirus task force. With the entrepreneurial -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, CDC blew it with testing.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: -- innovation of American research companies -\par \par \uc2 TODD: They had to go to South Korea for tests.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: We reinvented testing from a standing start. I mean, we had done 8,000 COVID tests at the end of February. But by June, because of American innovation, because of American companies that we turned to and empowered to develop those technologies, we literally were testing tens of millions of people a month.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still, the vilification of Dr. Fauci, was it -- you explained why it happened. Should it have happened? Was it fair? Is it fair what President Trump has said about him and what some critics of him? Do you think this stuff`s been fair?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Well, this is a freedom-loving nation. And early on, we asked the American people to make sacrifices, in those 15 days, to slow the spread so that what was happening in New York and New Jersey and New Orleans and Detroit and Seattle wouldn`t be happening everywhere. That we wouldn`t run out of supplies in our hospitals, that Americans wouldn`t be denied hospital beds. The American people shouldered that burden.\par \par \uc2 But shutdowns were always just a temporary effort to essentially take a knee while we spun up the supplies and the resources to make sure that no American would ever be denied the health care that you`d want any member of your family and mine to have.\par \par \uc2 But the problem was, I believe, that Dr. Fauci ultimately aligned himself with many Democrat governors who took what were temporary policies and made them long-term policies in their state. And the American people love freedom.\par \par \uc2 TODD: These Democratic governors, in their mind, they were just trying to save lives. Do you accept that?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Look, I -- I never judge people`s intentions, but I do judge their policies. And the truth is, we created a framework in those early days to ask for a pause in our economy so that we could make sure that our health care workers would not be lacking in the supplies and resources and masks and gowns that they would need to meet this moment, this unfurling pandemic, worst pandemic in a hundred years.\par \par \uc2 But I must tell you that one of the things I`m proudest of is that our administration, as quickly as possible, by mid-April, laid out a framework to open up America again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you like to see a federal ban on abortion?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I have to tell you, it was such a joy for me. When I was putting the finishing touches on this book to actually be able to reflect on the fact -- the fact that, after 49 years, with three Supreme Court justices appointed in our administration, we gave the American people a new beginning for life.\par \par \uc2 The Dobbs decision not just overturned Roe v. Wade, but it returned the question of abortion to the states and to the American people. And I celebrate that from really the depths of my heart.\par \par \uc2 You know, I -- when I was a young man, the most important decision I ever made was when I put my faith in Jesus Christ.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:06]\par \par \uc2 I opened up the Bible. I read verses like: Before I formed you in the womb I knew you. And I set before you life and death, now choose life so that you and your children may live.\par \par \uc2 I knew in that moment, a decade before I would arrive in Congress, that the cause of life had to be my cause. And I must tell you, Chuck, to have been able to champion life in the Congress and as governor, but to have been a part of an administration that gave the American people this new beginning for life is something I`ll cherish the rest of my life.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that the most important achievement, in your mind, of the administration?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I truly believe the opportunity to restore the sanctity of life to the center of American law may be among the most consequential legacies of the Trump-Pence administration, if not the most consequential.\par \par \uc2 But I would also say, with regard to your question, that when Senator Graham introduced legislation to establish a 15 week ban on abortion, I thought it was useful. If I`d still been a member of Congress I would have voted for it. It was useful in the sense of demonstrating that Democrats` position is the extreme position. The Democratic Party today supports abortion on demand up to the moment of birth, and taxpayer funding of abortion.\par \par \uc2 But I recognize that this issue is likely going to be resolved one state at a time. Some states have already acted to advance the cause of life. And I`ve said to people, when the Dobbs decision was overturned, we haven`t come to the end, we`ve come to the end of the beginning.\par \par \uc2 And, Chuck, it may take as many years to restore the sanctity of life to the center of American law in every state in this country as it took to overturn Roe vs. Wade. But as long as I have breath, I`ll be on that cause.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know you`ve got passion. Are you -- you`re a limited government guy. I know this. And you really are.\par \par \uc2 Are you comfortable with the idea of essentially government-mandated pregnancy? Somebody doesn`t want this child, doesn`t want this pregnancy to go through. You`re now advocating a law that essentially the government is ordering you to go through with this pregnancy. Are you comfortable with a government mandate like that? The folks in Kansas were not.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Our founding documents say we`re endowed by our creator with certain inalienable rights, and the first one mentioned is life. And I think government exists to protect life, particularly defenseless life.\par \par \uc2 And I intend to be a part of this. But I think we have an opportunity, Chuck, to approach this debate in a new and in a renewed way. You know, when the Supreme Court took this issue away from the American people 49 years ago, there were strong dividing lines that have endured now for two generations.\par \par \uc2 But now we have a political debate. And we have the opportunity not just to craft thoughtful protections for the unborn, but also to extend protections and support for women in crisis pregnancy and for newborns. I`d like to see us redouble our efforts to demonstrate the compassion of the American people. To live out the idea that there is no unwanted child, and that in the freest and most prosperous nation in the history of the world, we can make that the reality.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think the unborn have constitutional rights?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I believe in --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They don`t in our law right now, but do you believe they should?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I believe in the right to life. And I believe --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe that an unborn fetus has a constitutional right?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Look, we are protected in this country from being denied life or liberty without due process of law. And I believe those protections should extend to the born and the unborn. But this is a long-term cause. And it`ll be as much moral persuasion in America as political debates and battles.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Shouldn`t that be where it is, though? Shouldn`t it be moral persuasion and not government mandate?\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I think -- I really do believe now that it`s been returned to the political process, Chuck, that we have an incredible opportunity to demonstrate the real heart and compassion of the American people in this cause, to persuade our neighbors and friends to the day that abortion is not only illegal, but it`s unthinkable. And when that day comes, and we`ll someday institute, in the laws of all 50 states, protections.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There are a lot of women uncomfortable with the idea that a politician will make this decision for them.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: I understand that. I do. But I really do believe that restoring the sanctity of life to the center of law, in every state in this country, is at the very heart of achieving a boundless future for the American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still ahead, our panel is here with their thoughts on my interview with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and the new special counsel investigation into \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [09:45:03]\par \par \uc2 Plus, our Meet the Press Minute looks back at how \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  answered the question of how old was too old to serve as president?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:48:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 The day after his granddaughter`s wedding on the White House South Lawn, President Biden is celebrating an important event of his own today. It`s his 80th birthday. Biden is now the oldest serving president in American history. It`s a title previously held by \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In today`s "Meet the Press Minute", we look back to 1974, when then- Governor Reagan was in his early sixties. He was asked whether his age would be a factor in a presidential run.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAMES J. KILPATRICK, MEET THE PRESS: You will be 63 years old on February 6th, and during the campaign period, of course, would be 65. Does age seem to you, realistically, to be a factor that would operate against your candidacy for president?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2  (R), CALIFORNIA: Well, I think that`s got to be a determination the people are going to make. Certainly, Jim, by the time of 1976, if it should be my decision that I am going to be involved in that race, and I think it`s certainly too early to make that decision by a long way, the people will know my age and they`ll make that determination. We have had presidents that have pretty much spanned adulthood as to their age, and it has not seemed to affect the people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: To dig into the Meet the Press archives, scan the code on your screen right now. Visit NBCNews.com/MTP75. It`s the website that is home to 75 of the biggest moments in "Meet the Press" history.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the panel.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:11]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back.\par \par \uc2 Panel is here. Anna Palmer, co-founder of "Punchbowl News"; Peter Baker, chief White House correspondent for "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", he`s also author of the book "The Divider"; Brendan Buck, former adviser to Speakers \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 ; and Kimberly Atkins Stohr, senior opinion writer for "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 Peter, you wrote about the special counsel and sort of, almost the darned if you do, darned if you don`t moment --\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  was in. You know, and you saw it with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in my interview. There`s this -- there are a lot of Republicans that want to let Trump sort of dangle out there, but they`re also not ready to jump on, jump on pushing him off either, just --\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Right. No, that`s exactly right. Look, you`re right. Garland had no choice. Probably under the rules as they are established, he felt that it was an obvious case for a special counsel.\par \par \uc2 But the idea of a special counsel is to make it seem like politics is not involved. And, of course, that was never going to work. Of course, Trump was never going to let that be a nonpartisan figure. He immediately attacks the special counsel as a political, partisan witch hunter, the latest in a long stream, in his view.\par \par \uc2 And therefore, we`re right back where we started, which is that the investigation is seen through the lens of partisan politics.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Anna, it was one of those, I guess if Garland could`ve done this differently, it would`ve been -- had it been a special counsel from the beginning.\par \par \uc2 ANNA PALMER, FOUNDER, PUNCHBOWL NEWS: Right. Was the delay kind of an opening for Republicans in a lot of ways? It wasn`t just Trump. We`ve seen a lot of Republicans in the Senate, in the House saying, all right, now it`s time to impeach \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . Now, you know, there should be a special counsel for \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I`m just saying these are the partisan attacks that you`re going to hear over and over again for the next several months.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN BOEHNER: They`re going to do everything they can to undermine this process. And I will tell you, when I --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Who`s "they" though?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: House Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Newly in the majority, newly with committee hearings that they can call --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is that a good focus?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: It`s absolutely not. But I will tell you, when I was on the Hill working for Speaker Ryan, we spent an incredible amount of time behind the scenes trying to get our members not to undermine the Mueller investigation.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:00]\par \par \uc2 They wanted to do things. They wanted to try to meddle in that.\par \par \uc2 And we had to say, hard no. Like, we can`t do this because this is important. I don`t know that the current House of Representatives is set up to, to be that disciplined.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ATKINS STOHR, SENIOR OPINION WRITER, THE BOSTON \uc2 GLOBE\uc2 : And I know that the DOJ and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  want to keep things unpolitical. But I think that`s one of the things that he should`ve thought about in making the decision, either to do it from the beginning. I think that he didn`t need one at all. The regulations don`t mandate this. He could have continued this investigation, and it should`ve gone a little faster.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Doesn`t it feel like he is a character from the pre-Trump era?\par \par \uc2 ATKNS STOHR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s sort of like what Garland`s -- you know, whatever you think, he`s just sort of, isn`t this the way we`re supposed to do it?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It`s the old fashioned.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you`re, like, it`s not the way in the Trump world, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It`s a new era. Right. A new era.\par \par \uc2 But there is one thing that might be indicated here. \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has made a threshold decision perhaps, that it is okay to charge a former president, or a presidential candidate, if the elements of the law are met, because otherwise, he wouldn`t have bothered to appoint a special counsel. That`s a pretty big step, if in fact that`s what`s going through his mind right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, the other big event this weekend was this Republican Jewish Coalition event. You know, Anna, it was interesting. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he, he ended up satelliting it in because I think he realized \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was stealing the show no matter what.\par \par \uc2 But he gave this rambling answer about the midterms, correctly noting the impact of abortion, but he never said the word. Meanwhile, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is here telling me it`s the greatest achievement of the administration.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: I mean, one of the biggest divides, right? You have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who was pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , ever since I`ve covered politics, this is very core to who he is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Clearly, not afraid that the American public might not be in the same place that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 But that`s one of the few distinguishing factors, right, when you look at the two of them. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  kind of twisting himself into a pretzel in this whole interview, where he didn`t really want to say that, that the former president was wrong, that what happened on January 6th was wrong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brendan, he wants -- he wants to run on the Trump administration more than Trump does.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Pence is running what seems to be leading up to a campaign that would make a lot of sense if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wasn`t still around. He`s the natural next -- next in line. All the great things that we did. I`m separating myself from him a little bit.\par \par \uc2 But he refuses to actually take on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . In reality, you know, he`s running for president. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be a candidate --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s going to take him on.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Trump will take Pence on.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Exactly. And so we have a situation where, once again, you have Republicans sort of just hoping that he goes away, and unwilling to attack him. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to attack \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . He`s going to attack \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And to think that you can just sit there and wait for him to implode I think is a little naive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But is there a little bit of Trump fatigue? I feel like I`m sensing it in the rank and file.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: I think there`s more than a little bit. The midterms were evidence of that. But it`s not fatigue among the people, like including \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who stood right next to --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well because -- because they`re afraid of Trump`s base.\par \par \uc2 ATKINS STOHR: Well, but it`s an opportunity to say, this is waning and we`re going to break free, and we`re going to take a different path.\par \par \uc2 But instead, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  tried to excuse Trump for everything, other than trying to stop the certification of the election. It was -- it was Fauci`s fault that something, it was Mark Meadow`s fault that he talked about disinfectant. It was someone else`s fault. It was lawyers` fault that he --\par \par \uc2 PALMER: But where \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is, is very different, I think where you`re seeing a lot of the Republican Party saying, that was, you know, a three- time loser. You have members --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pence doesn`t want to go there.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: I mean, it`s pretty -- it`s stunning compared to where the rest of the Republicans are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, he`s self-aware in that who else was going to make him vice president? Ultimately, that`s the issue he has.\par \par \uc2 All right, guys. I have to go. Enjoy your Thanksgiving weekend. You guys are terrific.\par \par \uc2 There`s an unedited version of my \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  interview, including approximately 30 minutes that we weren`t able to air here at MeetThePress.com. Please take a look at it.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching. Enjoy the Thanksgiving holiday.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week. We don`t bring leftovers. We`ll be bringing real "Meet the Press" because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020221120eibk00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc53}{\*\bkmkend toc53}\b \uc2 What We Learned From Voters In This Week`s Elections About The Issues That Really Matter To Them And Where The Country May Be Headed From Here\b0\par\par\uc2 Willie Geist, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 1,354 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 WILLIE GEIST: On election day, all of the polls, the punditry, the focus groups, and the tea leaves give way to voters. Energized by a swirl of issues, inflation, abortion, crime, immigration, and the very foundations of democracy, Americans turned out in near record midterm numbers to make a loud statement about what they`re seeing in the country and what needs to change. NBC News Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali looks closer in our "Sunday Focus".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): They were the midterms that few saw coming.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What happened to the red wave, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): History defying results.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We lost fewer seats and House representatives in any democratic president`s first elected midterm, at least 40 years.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Near record turnout and a twist in where the competitive seats really were. Dems holding on in red states like Kansas. While losing key seats in blue New York.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): I don`t like to lose, but my opponent won this race. And he won it fair and square.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): The economy and inflation, the top issue for voters, and a weak point for Democrats who struggled to offer a clear response. But issues like abortion and threats to democracy acted as a dam to the previously predicted red wave for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m out to say where democracy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t think that a body of strangers should tell us what our choices should be.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): In crucial states like Michigan and Pennsylvania, abortion rights surpassed inflation as voters` number one concern, allowing Democrats to notch key governor and Senate race wins and quiet gains on the state level. Dems not losing a single state legislative chamber for the first time in nearly 90 years. Back in Washington, Republicans now grappling with how it could have gone so wrong.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Look, I`m frustrated that it didn`t turn out well for us.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): From once predicting a more than 20-seat margin in the House of Representatives to just being happy with a majority.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): What`s was our mission? To win the majority, to stop Biden`s agenda, and fire \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . All of that is accomplished.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Maybe, but if Republicans do take the House, it`ll be very slim margins. Giving all members more power.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think it`s wonderful because everybody`s voice needs to be considered.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): And GOP Leader Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, I think there`s some questions about leadership.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): His future as house speaker now thrown into flux.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I expect there will be a challenge.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): But amid a week of surprises, one important traditional hallmark, candidates who lost saying so.\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R-WIS) GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Unfortunately, the math doesn`t add up. I just called Governor Evers and conceded. I wish the Evers family well.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): Not necessarily a given. With nearly 300 election deniers on the ballot and a former president insisting they make election denialism a central focus.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-AZ) GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Trump won, you`re right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): That said, more than half of election denying candidates won their races, many coming to Congress now in 2023. And in the aftermath, Republicans also reckoning with the Trump of it all. Many of his hand-picked candidates lost.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Well, I think if they win, I should get all the credit. And if they lose, I should not be blamed at all.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): While pro-democracy candidates won in key states.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: No one party has a monopoly on good ideas.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): One of the Democrat`s strongest voting blocs, Gen Z. young voters showing up in record numbers. Rallying around things like student loan forgiveness. Among them, 25-year-old Maxwell Frost who won his election in Florida, making him the first Gen Zer elected to Congress.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Maxwell. Maxwell.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (voiceover): And in a midterm election full of firsts, another. A record $17 billion spent on campaigns and ads. All for the country to wake up Wednesday pretty much how it went to bed on election night, divided down the middle.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And Ali joins me now live.\par \par \uc2 Ali, good morning. It`s great to see you. So, after the stunning midterm elections, there`s been a lot of talk about Republicans walking away from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but we`ve heard that before.\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: And the former president made it very clear again this week, he will not go quietly or perhaps at all.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, that`s right, because Mr. Trump is expected to announce another run for president this week down at Mar-a-Lago. But it comes as the party that he`s controlled for the last six years has spent the last week actively pondering a future or as "The New York Post" said, a defuture without him.\par \par \uc2 One of the names that keeps coming up for 2024 is Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . But we know that former V.P. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , former Ambassador Nikki Haley, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , also others, they all likely have their eyes on the presidential prize this coming cycle.\par \par \uc2 And while Mr. Trump`s had a bad political week, we have seen this time and again, Willie, Republicans end up going back and sticking by the former president. So, time will tell if this moment is actually any different. Willie.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Yes, see you January 6th for that.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Meanwhile, we wake up this morning Democrats in control of the Senate. And as Steve Kornacki told us again last night, the house still very much in play.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEIST: Extraordinary. Ali, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next a new "Sunday Sitdown" with Daniel Radcliffe on the fun of playing "Weird Al" Yankovic in a new movie and the rocket ride of being "Harry Potter".\par \par \uc2 And then a life well-lived, the pioneering journalist who broke down the locker room door for the women who came after her.\par \par \uc2 And as we head to break, our photo of the week. The iconic Dolly Parton performing at last weekend`s Rock and Roll Hall of Fame induction ceremony in Los Angeles. After initially saying she did not think belonged in that hall, Dolly finally relented to the fans and accepted the honor. She even wrote and performed a new song for the occasion called "Rockin`". Dolly joined in this year`s class by Duran Duran, Eminem, Lionel Richie, Carly Simon, among others. Congratulations to Dolly, our national treasure.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CECILY STRONG, ACTRESS: The Maricopa County officials are incompetent. And it`s my belief that the election is rigged and the results should be thrown out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It sounds like new numbers coming in which has you taking a narrow lead over \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STRONG: Which is why I have always said, this is democracy. Trust the system. Trust the voters.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEIST: That is Cecily Strong as Arizona Republican Gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake on last night`s new episode of "Saturday Night Live." Legendary comedian Dave Chappell hosted the show with musical guest Black Star.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221115eibd000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc54}{\*\bkmkend toc54}\b \uc2 The Issue That Turned Out To Be A Game-Changer In States Red And Blue\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Yamiche Alcindor \par \uc2 501 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: In the wake of the Supreme Court overturning Roe v Wade, the Midterm Elections saw a record number of abortion-related measures on the ballot. And in every state where voters had a direct say, they opted against restricting access. Here`s Yamiche Alcindor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Democrats across the country are celebrating sweeping wins for supporters of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: A woman`s right to choose one.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): I will always, always keep fighting to protect a woman`s fundamental freedom.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): We will make Michigan a leader, a place where women make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: In Michigan, residents voted to enshrine abortion rights into the state constitution, an issue Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and her running mate been a central part of their successful reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: This is a right that we are not going to go backwards on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m proud of my state.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t want the government in my bedroom.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And abortion rights did drive people to the polls. It was the second most important issue to voters behind inflation. In addition to Michigan, voters in California and Vermont codified abortion rights into their state constitutions. And it wasn`t just blue states, voters in Kentucky and Montana shot down measures that could have restricted access.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that nationally?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: I think it`s a sign that the American public has had rights for 49 years. We`re not going to go backward. And so, whether it`s state by state we see this happen or hopefully at some point, you know, a national effort again, I think that this is where the fight is at the moment.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Those against abortion rights not processing the results.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe abortion is first-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Eric Scheidler from the Pro-Life Action League says he`s gearing up to continue their push.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SCHEIDLER, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PRO-LIFE ACTION LEAGUE: If we`ve learned anything over the past 50 years as a movement is that we`ve got to be able to take a punch.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Still, Americans in five states send a clear message about reproductive rights. Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News, Detroit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Coming up, their new mission: training troops to become teachers in "THOSE WHO SERVE."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221112eib9000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc55}{\*\bkmkend toc55}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for November 9, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Welker, Gabe Gutierrez, Vaughn Hillyard,  Chuck Todd, Andrea Mitchell, Ali Vitali, Dylan Dreyer, Sam Brock, Tom  Costello, Yamiche Alcindor, Courtney Kube \par \uc2 4,066 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the Midterm Election cliffhanger. The battle for Congress still too close to call.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The Senate and the House still up for grabs after the Democrats held off a red wave. President Biden at the White House today calling it a good day for democracy. In Georgia, the tight Senate race headed to a runoff. In Pennsylvania, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  defeating Mehmet Oz. And in Florida, a big reelection win for Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  setting up a possible 2024 showdown with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And what President Biden said today about his own 2024 plans. Our team across the country with a race is still in play.\par \par \uc2 Also breaking tonight, Nicole strengthening into a hurricane and taking aim at Florida flooding on the coast. Buildings collapsing into the sea, mandatory evacuations ordered, the new track tonight.\par \par \uc2 The potential major setback for Russia ordering a retreat from the key city of Kherson in southern Ukraine. Why Kyiv is skeptical.\par \par \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner moved to a penal colony in Russia. How the U.S. is responding.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2 `s parent company \uc2 Meta\uc2  laying off thousands. Why \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  is slashing jobs on the heels of concept \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And "THOSE WHO SERVE," the veterans answering a new call in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone, again, tonight from our election headquarters in New York. It was a good day for democracy, President Biden`s first public reaction to yesterday`s Midterm Elections in which Democrats defied the gravity of broad voter dissatisfaction and history itself to avoid massive losses in that widely-predicted Republican wave.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you from the start tonight, much of our reporting this evening is framed by what we don`t know and that is which party will control Congress. At this hour, control of the Senate is still up for grabs with some races too close to call, while the Georgia Senate contest is headed toward a December runoff election. And the House still in play for both sides, though Republicans currently lead.\par \par \uc2 But there is broad agreement that these were not the results most expected, an unpopular president faced with a bad economy managing to avoid the fate of his predecessors. The President`s message to voters tonight, I get it.\par \par \uc2 Our team has fanned out again this evening watching the count and the issues that shaped the vote starting tonight with Kristen Welker at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): 24 hours after ballots were cast, tonight, control of Congress is still up for grabs. President Biden insisting it was a good night for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: While the press and the pundits are predicting a giant red wave, it didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Republicans who had been hoping to take the House in a red wave now just looking for a win. The GOP still favored to take over but with a smaller margin than expected.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): We will be in the majority and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will be in the minority.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Much more suspense in the Senate where Republicans still could gain the one seat they need to take control but Democrats could hang on to with NBC News projecting races that are too early to call in Nevada and in Arizona. And in Georgia, the showdown between Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , NBC News Now projects will go to a runoff.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): We always knew that this race would be close.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I`m like Ricky Bobby. I don`t come to lose.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Democrats` biggest win of the night coming in the critical Pennsylvania Senate race. Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , six months after suffering a stroke, defeating Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: We did what we needed to do.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Republicans with big victories too including Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  easily defeating Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . And in the biggest win of the night, Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  winning reelection in a 19- point landslide, sweeping virtually every county in the state, including heavily Latino Miami Dade.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We reject woke ideology. We will never ever surrender to the woke mob. Florida is where woke goes to die.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: DeSantis seen as a possible 2024 presidential contender where he could face former President Trump who had a tough night. His endorsed candidates in Ohio and North Carolina won but several of Mr. Trump`s other handpick candidates in key races, including New Hampshire, Michigan, and Pennsylvania all lost.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, President Biden was asked what he will do differently with most Americans saying they`re dissatisfied with the state of the country.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:08]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Nothing because they`re just finding out what we`re doing. The more they know about what we`re doing, the more support there is.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And we pressed him about running again.\par \par \uc2 Two-thirds of Americans in exit polls say that they don`t think you should run for reelection. What is your message to them? And how does that factor into your final decision about whether or not to run for reelection?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: What`s your message to those two-thirds of Americans?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Watch me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen, the President is likely facing divided government. Did he talk about working with Republicans how it would go?\par \par \uc2 WELKER (on camera): Well, Lester, he did. The President says he`ll call top House Republican Kevin McCarthy tonight and says he`s hoping for bipartisanship on issues like Ukraine. The President also saying he`ll make his decision on running early next year. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker at the White House, thanks.\par \par \uc2 That pivotal Senate race in Georgia, as we said, is neck and neck. Gabe Gutierrez is there. And Gabe, what can we expect in this coming run off?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, this is what both campaigns and many voters did not want, another month of campaigning. But incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  are already fundraising ahead of the runoff now set for December 6. Neither managed to get 50 percent of the vote. But in the governor`s race here, Republican incumbent, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  easily won reelection over Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . And that suggests some Republicans may have split their tickets in the Senate contest and voted for Warnock.\par \par \uc2 Now, back in 2020, this state had to runoffs that tipped the balance of power in the Senate. And based on ad spending back then, it`s possible that we could see upwards of $200 million dollars in TV ads here over the next several weeks. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez in Atlanta, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Another key state in play. Arizona remains in flux both in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race between incumbent Mark Kelly and Republican Blake Masters and in the governor`s race. Vaughn Hillyard is in Phoenix tonight. Vaughn, where do things stand right now?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Yes, Lester, at least a half a million votes have yet to be counted here in Arizona. And as soon as we may know who wins this race for governor and Senate, may be tomorrow night. Elections officials continue to process and tabulate these mail-in votes.\par \par \uc2 And there`s an important batch of votes that are expected to be released tomorrow. These results are going to be mail ballots that were hand delivered by voters instead of being mailed back. In 2018, these votes, they trended towards Democrats, but in 2020, towards Republicans. Now, one other note that if these margins remain close, this is a situation where if these -- any of these races finish with -- finished within a half a percentage point, that it would trigger an automatic recount under state law that wouldn`t begin until December. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Vaughn, thanks very much for that.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in our political director and moderator of "MEET THE PRESS," Chuck Todd. Chuck, Monday night, you and I stood right here. And we talked about whether polarization could be the guiding factor in the election was it?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS POLITICAL DIRECTOR (on camera): Look, I don`t think -- I think it was because I don`t know if campaigns mattered. Let me show you Ohio. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  ran up almost a perfect race for a Democrat in Ohio. And yet, it didn`t matter how well his campaign win. Look at this. He basically only performed 1.5 percentage points better than Biden. Essentially, the state reverted to its 2020 partisan norm.\par \par \uc2 Let me take you to Colorado, another example but in the other direction. A Republican running a really good race in Colorado, and it got them all of less than a percentage point better than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The point is, it didn`t matter if it was a blue state or a red state. What the partisan makeup was in 2020 seemed to dictate what the result was in 2022.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Chuck, thanks. Let me turn to Andrea Mitchell now who`s been looking at the Biden effect. How much credit does the President get for staving off this red wave?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Lester, President Biden is touting the stronger than expected results. But our exit polls showed more voters believed his policies were hurting the country than helping. So, can Biden take credit for Democrats overperformance?\par \par \uc2 Another one of our exit polls shows President Biden has less of an impact on voters decisions in this midterms than any other President has had in the last decade. That may explain why despite the President`s low approval ratings, Democrats won in states like Pennsylvania and Michigan and avoided the kind of shellacking President \uc2 Obama\uc2  suffered when he lost 63 seats in 2010. This election could be the best Midterm performance for any president`s party in the last 20 years, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT A lot to analyze. Andrea, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 And even though this election is still full of question marks, it`s already setting the stage for 2024 especially for former President Trump and his potential rivals. Here`s Ali Vitali.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Technically, it`s still 2022. But politically --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: -- it`s open season on the 2024 Presidential Race. Launched to the front the Republican PAC Tuesday night, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Thank you for a historic landslide victory.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Or as the New York Post front page blared, DeFuture.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We`ve got so much more to do, and I have only begun to fight.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Supporters in the room chanting for two more years. His decisive win here in Florida drawing attention including from fellow Floridian \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who had his once ally turned likely rival top of mine this weekend.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Trump at 71, Ron DeSanctimonious at 10 percent.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Trump`s handpick candidates suffering a string of losses Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I think if they win, I should get all the credit. And if they lose, I should not be blamed at all.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Who do you think would be the tougher competitor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or former President Trump? And how is that factoring into your decision?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It`d be fun to watch them take on each other.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But it`s not just DeSantis eyeing potential bids.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2  (R-GA): So, give him a big Georgia welcome.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Former VP \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  also mulling a run, publishing a book So Help Me God out next week, possibly the same day Trump himself could cement his intentions to run again.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 " I`m going to be making a very big announcement.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Other former Trump officials like Nikki Haley and \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , Senate allies Ted Cruz and \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , and governors from Arkansas and South Dakota all talked about as possible 2024 contenders.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VITALI (on camera): And Pence releasing the first excerpt from his memoir tonight detailing his harrowing experience at the Capitol on January 6, and underscoring his indelible differences with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over that day. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Ali Vitali, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, Florida is bracing once again for a hurricane. Dylan Dreyer is here. Dylan, Hurricane Nicole is gaining steam.\par \par \uc2 DYLAN DREYER, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST (on camera): Yes, it is a hurricane now as it moves through the Bahamas and will likely maintain that category one status as it moves to the west, right now at 12 miles per hour. And then we are looking for the chance of about eight inches of rain across parts of Florida. And this is going to make landfall tonight into early tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 And then it`s going to race across the state of Florida, perhaps making another landfall, as it moves its way towards the Gulf of Mexico and then it takes this rapid turn to the north. It`s going to bring all of this rain up into the Northeast where we could see about three to four inches of rain.\par \par \uc2 However, it`s the storm surge down through Daytona Beach and the Jacksonville and the Fort Pierce, the three to five-foot storm surge that could lead to more beach erosion and also some significant flooding right along the coastline. The only saving grace Lester is that the storm hits during low tide. It`s tomorrow morning`s high tide that we`ll worry about.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Dylan, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get the view from the ground. We want to head to Florida. That`s where NBC Sam Brock is watching as things turn for the worst. Sam?\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, good evening. The size of the storm is massive just six weeks after Ian. And while many people saw Ian did its damage on the west side of Florida, here on the East Coast, it caused all kinds of erosion. Just look at this beach and how it`s dwindled, exposing the coastline to a direct hit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voiceover): Tonight, it`s a state of emergency across nearly 50 Florida counties as ocean waters swell from the Space Coast to Daytona Beach.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We watched a huge chunk of this backyard just dissolve and fall into the ocean this morning.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Dramatic images show a public restroom sliding into the ocean in Volusia County, while a couple of hours south, the ground literally crumbling beneath a home on Hutchinson Island as Florida getting slammed by the first November hurricane in almost 40 years.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just can`t believe that we`re having a storm in November.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: These restaurant owners taking stock of the situation and considering closing down as plywood adorns the Cocoa Beach Pier along with caution tape.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We boarded up the house and we have our generator.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Moms like Margaret Lever are on the move but not leaving the storm zone even after Hurricane Ian delivered some harsh lessons only weeks earlier.\par \par \uc2 Did hurricane and changed the mentality for anyone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A little bit, not too much.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Instead of passengers boarding, airport gates are boarded up and school districts across the state are closing even as far south as Miami and Broward.\par \par \uc2 WAYNE IVEY, SHERIFF, BREVARD COUNTY: You know you and you know your place where you live and where you take refuge.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: This historically rare event --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The storms are coming later, storms are coming bigger.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: -- ready to potentially wreak havoc on a state still in recovery mode. Sam Brock, NBC News Jensen Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, the major retreat ordered by Russia in Ukraine, a troubling new turn for basketball superstar Brittney Griner in Russia, and what \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  just said about cuts at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 `s parent company.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with a potential major blow to Vladimir Putin`s war effort in Ukraine. After weeks of Ukrainian advances, the Russian military announced it is retreating from Kherson, the first major city that fell after the invasion began in February. Kyiv is skeptical, however, fearing the announcement could be a ruse to lead Ukrainian troops into a trap.\par \par \uc2 And in Russia, \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner has been moved to a penal colony in an undisclosed location according to her attorneys. Griner was sentenced in Russia in August to nine years in prison on drug possession charges. Despite the stalled efforts to free Griner, President Biden said today he is determined to bring her home.\par \par \uc2 And back home, another big tech company announcing layoffs, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 `s parent company \uc2 Meta\uc2 . The social media giant has spent big on virtual reality technology, but with the economy slowing down, so are the advertising dollars. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It was a contrite and remorseful \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  on a video message recorded by an employee today.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZUCKERBERG\uc2 , CEO, \uc2 FACEBOOK\uc2 : I want to say, you know, upfront that I take full responsibility for this decision.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The CEO of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent company, \uc2 Meta\uc2 , announcing the company would be laying off 11,000 employees, a stunning 13 percent of the workforce, saying the company needs to become leaner and more efficient.\par \par \uc2 ZUCKERBERG: It is one of the hardest calls that I`ve had to make in the 18 years of running the company.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The trouble, \uc2 Meta\uc2  ramped up hiring during and after the pandemic while spending billions on Zuckerberg`s vision of a virtual reality future called the Metaverse. But the metaverse may now have to wait Mehta stock is down 70 percent this year, profits down 50 percent in the third quarter. With the economy slowing, \uc2 Meta\uc2  is the latest tech companies squeezed by a sharp decline in advertising revenue.\par \par \uc2 JULIA BOORSTIN, CNBC SENIOR MEDIA AND TECH CORRESPONDENT: This is \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  saying we grew too fast. We assumed that the boom times would continue.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: And they haven`t.\par \par \uc2 BOORSTIN: This is a really tough time right now for any ad-supported platform.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: \uc2 Meta\uc2  employees took to social media of course to vent. "The inevitable has happened to me. I woke up today to news I was being laid off," wrote one. Another wrote, "Gutted, devastated, sobbing all morning."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Meanwhile, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock closed near a two year low today after \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  sold nearly $4 billion dollars-worth of stock following his purchase of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . And tonight, President Biden said Musk`s relationship with other countries should be looked into. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Tom, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the issue that turned out to be a game-changer in states red and blue.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In the wake of the Supreme Court overturning Roe v Wade, the Midterm Elections saw a record number of abortion-related measures on the ballot. And in every state where voters had a direct say, they opted against restricting access. Here`s Yamiche Alcindor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Democrats across the country are celebrating sweeping wins for supporters of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: A woman`s right to choose one.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): I will always, always keep fighting to protect a woman`s fundamental freedom.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): We will make Michigan a leader, a place where women make their own decisions.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: In Michigan, residents voted to enshrine abortion rights into the state constitution, an issue Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and her running mate been a central part of their successful reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: This is a right that we are not going to go backwards on.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m proud of my state.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don`t want the government in my bedroom.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And abortion rights did drive people to the polls. It was the second most important issue to voters behind inflation. In addition to Michigan, voters in California and Vermont codified abortion rights into their state constitutions. And it wasn`t just blue states, voters in Kentucky and Montana shot down measures that could have restricted access.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that nationally?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: I think it`s a sign that the American public has had rights for 49 years. We`re not going to go backward. And so, whether it`s state by state we see this happen or hopefully at some point, you know, a national effort again, I think that this is where the fight is at the moment.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Those against abortion rights not processing the results.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I believe abortion is first-degree murder.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Eric Scheidler from the Pro-Life Action League says he`s gearing up to continue their push.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SCHEIDLER, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PRO-LIFE ACTION LEAGUE: If we`ve learned anything over the past 50 years as a movement is that we`ve got to be able to take a punch.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Still, Americans in five states send a clear message about reproductive rights. Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News, Detroit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Coming up, their new mission: training troops to become teachers in "THOSE WHO SERVE."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, as we approach Veterans Day, how some of our military men and women are preparing for their next careers in the classroom. Here`s Courtney Kube.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON (voiceover): After more than two decades in the Navy, John Altman still wanted to serve. He swapped his years at sea for days in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ALTMAN, U.S. VETERAN: I`m on the frontlines of developing America`s number one natural resource. What`s that? I teach high school.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: John discovered a program called Troops To Teachers supporting men and women leaving the military to become educators, providing money, counseling, and job referrals with a focus on science, math, and special education in low-income communities.\par \par \uc2 What is it that you hope that you instill upon your students?\par \par \uc2 ALTMAN: You got to work hard if you want something. That you can google your way through life.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Life lessons his students at Paradise Valley High School take to heart.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He`s definitely taught me to get stuff done on time and not put it off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: People from the military could basically go and do anything and he chose to come be a teacher.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: With a need for teachers --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The teacher shortage that you hear about is real. I`m in the trenches trying to find teachers.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Programs like Troops To Teachers are more critical than ever.\par \par \uc2 ALTMAN: I`m glad I found the program. I think it`s important for me to be an example just as when I wore the uniform.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: For Altman, a new battle rhythm with the same mission.\par \par \uc2 ALTMAN: I think that`s my duty assignment. I believe in that.\par \par \uc2 Where`s my force?\par \par \uc2 These youngsters are our tomorrow. I really love my Navy and I love teaching.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Courtney Kube, NBC News Paradise Valley, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And that`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself at each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221110eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc56}{\*\bkmkend toc56}\b \uc2 Today for November 9, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Peter Alexander, Gabe Gutierrez,  Vaughn Hillyard, Jacob Soboroff, Steve Kornacki, Hallie Jackson, Craig  Melvin, Kristen Welker, Dylan Dreyer, Ali Vitali, Dana Griffin, Kerry  Sanders, Carson Daly \par \uc2 7,021 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good Wednesday morning. After an election night of midterm surprises for both parties.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: And we are still awaiting many of the results. It is November 9th. This is Today. Too close to call key races still to be decided control of Congress hanging in the balance as votes are still being counted in key battlegrounds this morning. In Pennsylvania Democrats pick up a hard fought Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATOR-ELECT: I`ll be the next U.S. senator from Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: In Florida, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wins big, grossing back to the State House and turning Florida decidedly red.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): The people have delivered their verdict. Freedom is here to stay.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But across the country controversial Republican candidates aligned with former President Trump disappoint.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Definitely not a Republican way that`s for darn sure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This morning suspense over who will control the House and Senate and the results still rolling in today, Wednesday, November 9th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is a special edition of Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, hi, everybody. Good morning and welcome to Today. It is nice to have you with us on a Wednesday morning, the morning after election night.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: One, two, three cups of coffee and one more for you, a long night. But election results by the way, SG, still coming in right now.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Absolutely. Big night for the NBC News team all-nighters but we still have the question that we headed into the night with, will there be a shift in the balance of power in Congress? It`s unknown at this hour so let`s break it down where things stand, 218 seats are needed to claim majority control in the House. And right now our projection for the House is essentially still a tossup. Now let`s go to the Senate, both parties still neck and neck there as well.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, Democrats have flipped one seat. NBC News projecting Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has won his Pennsylvania seat defeating Dr. Mehmet Oz in a closely watched an extremely expensive race.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s go down to Georgia, neither Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  or Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  have met the 50 percent threshold yet necessary to be declared an outright winner. So it could be that they will go to a runoff election in December.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, it`s still too early to call. The Senate battle in Arizona, that`s between incumbent Senator Mark Kelly and Republican Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And in Nevada it is just after 4:00 a.m. and we`re starting to see some results in the race between the incumbent Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and Republican challenger \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 . So put it this way, we get a lot to get to. Our correspondents, they`ve been up all night they are spread out from coast to coast. Let`s get it started with NBC`s Peter Alexander in Philadelphia this morning. Hi, Peter, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Savannah and Hoda, good morning to both of you. What a night. The crucial Senate seat here in Pennsylvania was a top target for both parties. But now the Democrats have secured a huge flip with John Fetterman`s win. Historically in a midterm, the party in power loses dozens of seats. But Democrats this go around defied history and expectations. And with plenty of votes still to be counted, this remains a country that is deeply divided.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): This morning, controlled of Congress up in the air with both the House and Senate still hanging in the balance. Republicans acknowledging the expected red wave never materialized.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Definitely not a Republican wave that`s for darn sure.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): That hasn`t been as big of a wave as I`d hoped it would be.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): While the battle for the Senate may not be decided for days, Democrats are celebrating a critical Senate pick up in Pennsylvania, where Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  just months removed from a stroke, defeated his Republican rival, celebrity T.V., Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Yes, this campaign has always been about fighting for everyone who`s ever been gotten knocked down that ever got back up.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Georgia`s Senate showdown appears headed to a run off next month if neither incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  nor Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  can reach the 50 percent threshold.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: You all just hang in there. I`m feeling good.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I told you he`s going to be tough to beat. You got to roll in Georgia here on.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Democrats showed signs of strength in a number of key races.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So you know, you got hats off to the Democrats. They perform well in a lot of these swing districts.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): Two Democratic senators who`d been considered vulnerable New Hampshire`s \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  and Colorado`s Michael Bennet, both fending off challenges overnight and in some of the country`s most competitive House races Democrats touting narrow victories including in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:04]\par \par \uc2 REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): Thanks to your commitment and hard work we have won this race.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): But some critical wins for the GOP holding on to a key Senate seat in North Carolina. And in Ohio, Republican political novice author J.D. Vance beating Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . Vance was endorsed by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-PA), SENATOR-ELECT: I will never forget where I came from, and I will never forget the great people of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voice-over): A major bright spot for the GOP Florida, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  cruising to reelection. Across the country, voters send a message that they`re dissatisfied with President Biden and particularly worried about the economy, with nearly a third saying in exit polls that rising inflation mattered most to them this year, but abortion rights was the second most important issue and a huge motivating factor for Democrats, now both parties facing a deeply divided electorate ahead of the next presidential election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And here in Pennsylvania, according to the NBC News exit poll, voters were confident in John Fetterman`s ability to serve this state effectively despite his health challenges. Dr. Mehmet Oz fell short of convincing those same Pennsylvanians that he had lived in this state long enough to serve it. Savannah and Hoda?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Peter Alexander leading us off. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, let`s focus on that closely watched Senate battle in Georgia. It remains too close to call it this hour. NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez spent the night in Atlanta. His watching those results still coming in, have we seen any movement overnight, Gabe?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Hoda, good morning. Well, it was a long night here in Georgia. And we`re still at it, no clear winner. And NBC News has not projected this race but a runoff is appearing more and more likely at least that`s according to the Georgia Secretary of State`s office. Now incumbent senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is holding that slight lead over his challenger, football legend \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . But there`s a third party candidate in this race. And according to Georgia State law, if no candidate reaches that 50 percent threshold, we`ll see a runoff here next month.\par \par \uc2 Now this has been a wild race with more than a quarter billion dollars flowing into the state. And now voters in Georgia are preparing for the possibility of another four weeks of campaigning. Voters I`ve spoken with weren`t too thrilled about that. But in a second race, there was a clear victory. NBC News projects Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  defeating his Democratic challenger \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  for the second time.\par \par \uc2 And the vote tally so far is signaling that at least some Republicans may have voted for Kemp but then voted for the Democrat, Warnock. But again, this Senate race is still too close to call here. And depending on how a few other races go across the country. If we do head to a run off, it could be another several weeks before we know the balance of power in the Senate. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Gabe Gutierrez for us there in Atlanta. Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s go to another pivotal battleground, Arizona, the ballots in that state`s Senate and governor`s race still being counted. NBC`s Vaughn Hillyard is up early in Phoenix. Mark Kelly, the Senate incumbent holds the lead there, but we`re expecting these race results to tighten up.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, that`s right, Savannah. There are still hundreds of thousands of ballots to be counted here in this race. And it`s going to be challenger Blake Masters who is trying to take down incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly. And you see that he`s down by about six percentage points right now though, but it`s important to note there is as much as one-third of the vote that is still outstanding, and much of that vote is expected to skew toward the GOP.\par \par \uc2 Now election workers are returning here this morning in the Phoenix area to begin signature verifying these mail ballots that were received in recent days. This is going to be a slow process. We should also note the governor`s race, Kari Lake the Republican who is taking on Democratic \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , she well outpacing Masters. She is also losing at this hour to the Democrat.\par \par \uc2 However, overnight, we saw the gap here shrink, and that is what the Republicans are hoping continuous here by weeks and that they are ultimately able to eclipse their Democratic opponent. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Vaughn Hilliard in Arizona. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: As we mentioned earlier, we`re still waiting for results out of Nevada`s Senate race too. NBC`s Jacob Soboroff standing by in Vegas, Jacob this race was considered one of the most vulnerable for Democrats. So where do things stand this morning?\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It is still on the line, Hoda. Good morning to you. And Savannah to borrow a line from you last night, what happened in Vegas is going to stay in Vegas, at least for now. It`s not going to Washington, D.C. just quite yet. It was a nail biter out here. That`s what they expected and that is exactly what Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  and her Republican challenger, the former state attorney general, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  got. They thought it was going to be a close race. That race is maybe even closer than they thought.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cortes Masto acknowledged as much in a speech to supporters last night. We were there when she told those supporters that this race that hinged on the issues of abortion and the economy is now going to hinge on the pace with which with -- with which, excuse me, those ballots are ultimately going to be counted. And that is because state law here dictates a slow pace of count to ensure a fair and accurate election.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:19]\par \par \uc2 Now the question will be what is \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  do? You`ll remember after the 2020 election here, he came out on behalf of President Trump and preemptively challenged the results of the election with baseless conspiracy theory election claims, saying that the election was stolen. That of course was debunked, proven as false by the Republican secretary of state here. Now that he`s in the lead. He took the lead overnight last night. Will he do the same thing? I guess we`ll have to find out today. Meanwhile, the vote count continues out here, guys.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Jacob Soboroff in Vegas, Jacob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, let`s get to our national political correspondent, Steve Kornacki who`s been working the big board throughout the night. He`s back at it this morning. OK. I don`t think 24 hours ago we would have predicted that we wouldn`t know what is going to happen in the House yet.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: It`s amazing when you consider Republicans only needed a net gain of five seats to get control of the House. That was the closest a minority party was to winning the House coming into a midterm in nearly a century. And yet this morning, as you say, this is what our NBC News house model is showing. Our projection 218 is what you need to get a majority in the House. Our projection right now is that the Republicans will land at 220 critically plus or minus 10 seats. So in other words, there is v