he only one cashing in. More than 11 million tickets won something with cash prizes totaling $98 million including 3 more million dollar winners in the Golden State. The jackpot selling store`s owner Joseph Chahayed will also enjoy a million dollar bonus as we all wait for someone to claim that historic grand prize\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What do you want to tell that person who is probably sitting at home trying to wrap his or her head?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH CHAHAYED, GAS STATION OWNER: I love you before and I love you know and I`m going to love you forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: So Papa Joe as he`s known around here is definitely filling that lottery love this morning. Now the winner has up to a year to claim that ticket. And if you weren`t the lucky winner, no worries. Guess what? There`s going to be another Powerball drawing tonight. Just a little smaller prize at $20 million, guys.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, Dana Griffin, Dana, welcome to NBC News by the way.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Way to go, Dana.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Welcome to the Peacock.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You did great report.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Thank you. Thank you. Happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We won the lottery with her.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: There you go. Welcome to NBC. Let`s turn now to the latest on the new hurricane threat facing Florida this morning.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, six weeks after and now Nicole taking aim at the state expected to gain strength before making landfall. We`re going to get to Dylan`s forecast in just a moment. But first, NBC`s Kerry Sanders he joins us from Daytona Beach shores. Hey, Kerry, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good morning guys. Look, you can see the Atlantic Ocean over my shoulder. It`s kicking up. The winds are picking up. The commercial airports in the area, that`s Orlando, Daytona Beach, Melbourne, they`re either closed or will be closing because of the approaching Tropical Storm that may become a Hurricane Nicole.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:02]\par \par \uc2 The big threat of course here is along the coast coastal erosion. And as you take a look at some of these pictures just leading into the arrival of Nicole, you`ll get a better understanding as to why Ian hammered this area and a lot of the coast was lost to the storm at the same time with the winds coming in. We have the threat to Artemis. OK. That rocket right now is sitting on the pad. It was supposed to launch on the 14th it`s now been pushed back to the 16th. It`s designed to withstand winds of about 85 miles per hour. But the real threat is going to be the rain.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 NASA\uc2  has put out staff to triple check all the hatches to make sure that everything is sealed. Meantime, mandatory evacuations from Flagler County all the way down to Martin County. Those who live in coastal Zone A have been told to leave. And as you saw from those pictures, the biggest threat of course with the coastal erosion is some of the buildings it`s feared by officials could actually have their foundations undermined and they could collapse.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of attention anybody in a mobile home told also to go inland to shelters and then ultimately, tourists in the area who might be caught off guard, they should also find out where those shelters are. They`re open to everybody not just residents, guys.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, good advice. Kerry Sanders for us there. Kerry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK, let`s go to Dylan. She`s got the very latest on Nicole, the rest of today`s forecast in for Al this morning, hey.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Good morning again, guys. And I want to point out that hurricanes hitting the United States in November is pretty rare. We`ve only ever had it happen three times. So this would be the fourth one if this does strengthen into a category one storm. It is still a tropical storm, but it`s very close to being a weak category one so that`s why we do have your hurricane warnings in effect and it`s just north of West Palm Beach where we`re expecting landfall tonight, into tomorrow morning around 10:00 p.m. to about 2:00 a.m. tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 And as this makes its way on shore, we are going to see that push of water. The good thing is it`s low tide when the storm is expected to make landfall but still on the backside of that your next high tide about six hours later, we could end up with a three to five foot storm surge. And we`re also looking for that to make its way up into the Northeast. Rainfall totals about eight inches possible in parts of Florida in the Northeast. We can see about two to four inches of rain. That`s a look at the weather across the country. Now here`s a peek out your window.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And that`s your latest forecast.\par \par \uc2 [07:48:03]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Dylan, thank you. Still ahead, our exclusive look at a new and emotional interview with Jennifer Aniston she`s opening up about the public scrutiny that surrounds her private life and her secret struggles with fertility. You`re going to want to hear what she`s revealing. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We are back as we say good morning to Mr. Daly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Carson.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Hi.\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning everybody. Good to see you. Coming up on PopStart. Jen on Jen, we got the very sweet message that J.Lo was sharing about her husband`s former wife.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, and then the cast of "The Crown" chatting with our Keir Simmons about the highs and lows of creating the hit shows new season. But first your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:11]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221109eib90005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc57}{\*\bkmkend toc57}\b \uc2 The Governor`s Race In Georgia Too Early To Call Between \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  And \uc2 Stacey Abrams\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Blayne Alexander \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Dozens of governors seats are also in play being driven by issues ranging from inflation to abortion. Blayne Alexander is following the key races. Blayne, what should we know?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Lester, when I`ve spoken to voters, both of those issues came up and more. So much of it just depends on the state. And tonight, Lester, many of those key governor`s races are very tight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, it`s not just control of Congress that`s at stake, also what for grabs, 36 critical Governor seats, including here in Georgia, Republican Governor Brian Kemp`s rematch against \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . A win would make her the first Black woman governor in U.S. history. A big focus for Kemp, his early decision to reopen Georgia during COVID.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2  (R-GA): She was criticizing me for just simply giving you the opportunity to go back to work or to open your business.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: Abrams, like many Democrats around the country emphasizing abortion access.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-GA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We know for women in Georgia, this is the lethal decision.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: In Arizona, it`s a marquee matchup between Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  and Kari Lake, a Trump-backed Republican who has repeatedly denied the 2020 election results and is already casting doubt on this election.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-AZ), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We`re so ready for fair elections, aren`t we? I`m worried about tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D-AZ), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: When people like Kari Lake denied the results of the election, we stood firm.\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER: TWO other contests to watch feature Democratic incumbents in tight races. Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in a tough fight against Republican Tudor Dixon and perhaps most surprising in deep blue New York where Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is facing a serious challenge from Republican \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , who is focusing on rising crime.\par \par \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY) GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Voters of all walks of life, Republicans, Democrats, and Independents, they`ve had enough.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 B. ALEXANDER (on camera): And Georgia officials say there have been very few lines at polling places today, they point to a record 2.5 million people who cast early votes. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Blayne Alexander, thank you.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in now our political director and moderator of "MEET THE PRESS," Chuck Todd. Chuck, you`ve been digging into the exit polls. What do voters seem to be most concerned about?\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS POLITICAL DIRECTOR (on camera): Well, it will not surprise you. The number one issue overall, inflation. What`s interesting is that there`s only a five-point difference. Abortion came in second 27 percent. And then you see the other three issues you saw a lot in TV ads, crime, gun policy, and immigration. Inflation-breaking Republican, abortion-breaking Democrat. Crime-breaking Republican. Gun, if you pick guns, you broke Democrat and immigration by Republican.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you by party. As you can see here, among Democrats, the most important issue was abortion. Among Republicans, the number one issue is inflation. And here`s the tiebreaker perhaps, the difference between getting 49 percent and 51 percent in some of the Senate races. Independents picked inflation.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me give you a little teaser about some of the state polls, Lester. In two of our Senate battlegrounds, abortion topped inflation, Pennsylvania and New Hampshire. Just something to keep in mind tonight as we figure out who`s going to control the Senate.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Interesting trends starting to emerge. All right, Chuck, thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 And our Chief Washington Correspondent Andrea Mitchell joins us now with some concerns that figured tough numbers about our exit polls.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Exactly. Well, as Chuck was just pointing out, the top issue was inflation. But abortion was a top concern among a lot more voters than we had expected and crime less than expected. But there`s something else that really stuck out to me.\par \par \uc2 This early exit poll shows that 46 percent of voters say President Biden`s policies are hurting the country. Only 36 percent say they`re helping the country. 16 percent say his policies are not making a difference. Even some of Mr. Biden`s base voters, including Black voters and young voters, are not convinced that his policies are working.\par \par \uc2 The President in the final weeks of the campaign has been saying that this election is a choice between his party and the Republicans and not a referendum on his presidency. But if voters ended up making their decision based on how they feel about his record -- his record, that`s not a good sign for Democrats. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Andrea, thanks for that.\par \par \uc2 Washington could look dramatically different in the days to come. How Congress have the Biden administration are strategizing for a big shift. Plus, we`ll take you inside a vote-counting center. Why it could take a while before the full picture of the elections is known.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221111eib80002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc58}{\*\bkmkend toc58}\b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS for November 6, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Dasha Burns, Vaughn Hillyard, Jacob Soboroff, Blayne  Alexander, Amy Walter, Kristen Welker, Claire McCaskill, Pat McCrory \par \uc2 10,094 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD": This Sunday...\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: Vote. Get out and vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... battle for control.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: I think Republicans are going to win the House and the Senate. I think they`re going to do both.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Republicans need to flip just one seat to gain control of the Senate and only five seats to win control of the House.\par \par \uc2 KEITH PEKAUI, CANDIDATE FOR U.S. REPRESENTATIVE, (R) ILLINOIS: I want you to watch \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  hand me that gavel.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: This morning, the results of our final NBC News poll before the midterms and what it says about the GOP`s chances of taking complete power back on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Plus, democracy at risk.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You can`t love your country only when you win.\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Biden issues a dire warning about election deniers and the risk for our electoral system. But will those warnings from Democrats resonate with economically challenged swing voters in the final push before Election Day?\par \par \uc2 And, very soon...\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: I will very, very, very probably do it again, OK?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Will former President Trump announce his 2024 bid before we finish counting the 2022 vote? My guest this morning, Florida`s Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , the chair of the GOP`s campaign arm; and New York Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , the chair of the House Democratic campaign arm. Plus...\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Are you ready to meet the press?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Seventy-five years, from presidents to prime ministers, dictators to Civil Rights leaders, we mark 75 years as the destination where newsmakers come to make news.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker; former Democratic senator from Missouri Claire McCaskill; former Republican governor of North Carolina \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2 ; and Amy Walter, editor in chief of the "Cook Political Report."\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News headquarters in New York, the longest-running show in television history. This is a special edition of "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, good Sunday morning. On our 75th birthday, from our election headquarters here at Rockefeller Center in New York City, with just two days until Election Day, more than 40 million people have already cast ballots in the first national election since the January 6th attack on the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 By the way, we expect a record midterm turnout, 120 million to 125 million, when all the votes are counted. At stake, all 435 House seats. Republicans need a net gain of just five to take control of that chamber. It is the smallest number of seats that the out-of-power party has needed since 1932. Thirty-five Senate seats are at stake this cycle. Republicans, of course, need just one Democratic seat to flip control of that chamber.\par \par \uc2 There are also 36 governor`s races, including in some key 2024 presidential battlegrounds like Arizona, Florida, Georgia, Michigan, Nevada, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 And in our final pre-election poll, conducted over the last three days, Republicans do appear to have the momentum. Just how big is that momentum? The question is whether Democrats can prevent what former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  once called a "shellacking." The president`s travel in the final stretch says it all about how the Democrats believe the election is going. Outside of the Philadelphia stop with \uc2 Obama\uc2  yesterday, President Biden has spent the final days of his campaign not in purple states but in blue states, California, New Mexico, Illinois and New York, where he`ll be campaigning later today in suburban New York City, Westchester County.\par \par \uc2 And here`s why. Biden`s job approval rating sits at 44 percent in this final NBC News poll. By the way, all-time low with independents. His approval rating is just 28 percent.\par \par \uc2 And let me put this in context for you. What does a job rating mean? Some people believe it`s the single most important indicator to understand where a midterm election is going. Bill Clinton`s 44 percent job approval rating translated into a 54-seat loss for his party. A 45 percent job approval rating for President \uc2 Obama\uc2  in 2010 translated to 63 lost House seats. Forty-six percent for President Trump, 40 House seat loss for his party.\par \par \uc2 So what will 44 percent give us?\par \par \uc2 And here`s, to me, the number you probably ought to care about the most in this poll, the state of the U.S. economy. Eighty-one percent tell us they are dissatisfied. This is the second highest number we`ve ever recorded. The last time it was this high was just before the 2010 election. And you know how that one went.\par \par \uc2 All right. The generic congressional ballot here. We have it dead even, 47- 47. It`s a slight uptick for the Republicans from two weeks ago, when it was 47-46. Here`s something to keep in mind. Essentially a tie race probably gives the Republicans the five or six seats they need.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:06]\par \par \uc2 For every percentage point that they lead the Democrats, that`s probably anywhere from another five to 10 seats that would be added to their total. Just something to watch for on election night.\par \par \uc2 All right. This is key, and this is something that should make Democrats relieved this weekend. Interest in this election, enthusiasm. Democrats have now matched the Republicans, dead even. In 2018 they had a slight lead. And of course the Democrats had the better night.\par \par \uc2 But let me go inside the numbers, and why there`s still some warning signs here for the Democrats. There are two enthusiasm numbers in particular that I want to point -- I want to highlight here, African-American voters and young voters. And as you can see, remember Democrats had a good night in 2018. And right now African-American voters are not quite at their 2018 levels, and young voters are not quite at their -- at their 2018 levels, but look at seniors. Seniors are a little bit more. So this is why Republicans feel good about the end of this.\par \par \uc2 We also asked folks to send us what message -- if you could send a message with your vote to Congress, what would you say with it? And here`s our word cloud response. And as you can see, "Fix Economy," the number one message that was sent from voters. And essentially tied for second here, "End Partisanship" and "Women`s Rights."\par \par \uc2 So essentially the three big issues of the economy, abortion rights and the democracy, all represented here. And you can see the voters are engaged on this.\par \par \uc2 By the way, given what happened to Paul Pelosi, we asked voters about the current climate of political violence. Voters are now more likely to view our political rhetoric as an important contributing factor, rather than seeing what happened as some isolated incident.\par \par \uc2 And when we asked who is responsible for this elevated rhetoric, social media companies come out on top, almost universally blamed for our current political climate. Cable news media nearly matches them, then Trump, then Republican officials, Democratic officials, and President Biden there at the bottom.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn back now to the battle for the House. Because I want to point out some key numbers here for you to keep track of. In the seven times since the Truman era, when the president`s job rating has been below 50 percent in his first midterm election, which is what we`re staring at with this one, that president`s party has lost an average of 43 House seats.\par \par \uc2 And guess what? This cycle, there are 44 Democratic held seats that are rated as lean or toss-up by the Cook Political Report, and there`s just 20 Republican held seats that are in those same categories in the House battleground. In 2018, you can see it was a much more lopsided battleground here, with the Republicans basically defending almost everything and Democrats barely defending anything.\par \par \uc2 In this cycle, the defense for the Democrats includes the district where Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  is running after redistricting. He also chairs the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee charged with getting House Democrats elected and keeping their majority.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman Maloney joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "Meet the Press," two days before the election, sir.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 , CHAIR OF DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSIONAL CAMPAIGN COMMITTEE & (D) NEW YORK: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me start with this. What constitutes -- what constitutes a good night for Democrats on Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: We`re going to hold this majority. That constitutes a good night. We`re going to defend our -- our mainstream democratic values against the threats to our democracy. We`re going to protect women`s reproductive freedom and voting rights. We`re going to give you cheaper prices for gas and groceries, healthcare and housing. And we`re going to give you safer streets by funding good local policing with accountability, but also doing something about gun violence. And that is a plan for the future. And because our candidates have real plans, they`re going to do better than people think on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you acknowledge, though, that all the data seems to point in the other direction?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Oh, look, I think the polls you just showed shows it`s basically tied with the numbers in 2018, which, if I recall, was a pretty good year for Democrats. I think this race is razor-close. And I think everybody who cares about the extremism in this MAGA movement, the racism, the anti- Semitism, the violence, needs to get out and vote. And that`s not just Democrats. That`s independents and fair-minded Republicans who watched the January 6th hearings, who know what we`re up against, who listen to Republicans oppose things like Social Security and Medicare, want to put it on the chopping block every five years.\par \par \uc2 That`s your next guest, by the way. That`s his plan. And we`ve got a plan to cap seniors` out-of-pocket costs in Medicare. There are big differences. I want everybody to go out and vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you lose your race, does that mean this was a wave?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Oh, come on. I`ve always had a tough race, Chuck. I beat a Republican to have this seat. I`m the first gay person ever elected to Congress from New York. I`m raising an inter-racial family in a county \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won in 2016 by 20 points. It has always been improbable that I would serve in the Congress. I have to go out and earn it every two years. And I`ve...\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you don`t believe...\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: I`ve won five times because...\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... if you happen to lose, you don`t think that somehow is part of the national wave that something -- that you got, sort of, rolled out that way?\par \par \uc2 [09:10:10]\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, I guess what I`m not going to do is play hypotheticals with you when we`re two days out from an election, and...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: ... I`ve won times in a district where everybody said I couldn`t. I mean, if I -- if we were having this conversation in 2012, you would have made fun of me for even running, because I was running against a Republican incumbent with a bunch of money, and nobody -- nobody thought I could win.\par \par \uc2 So people like me weren`t supposed to serve in Congress from New York until I did it. So with all due respect, what Democrats are going to do right now is going to go out and fight with everything we have got, for seniors who need costs capped in Medicare; for people worried about gun violence in our schools; for people who want to have a real plan to go forward together, without the anger and the fear and the hatred, fighting for women`s reproductive freedom and voting rights.\par \par \uc2 That`s what we`re going to do. And I`m going to let the pundits, kind of, make predictions. We`re going to let the voters speak on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to talk about a couple of messaging things that we`ve seen pop up. There`s a lot of blue state Democrats on the House level that are in these competitive seats, Oregon, New York, California. And I`m curious, do you think the abortion message doesn`t work as well in blue states because voters don`t believe it will ever be illegal in their state, but it does work pretty well for some of your red state Democrats? Have you noticed this yourself?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, of course. When -- when abortion is literally being taken away by an initiative like in Kansas, of course people are going to pay more attention. But of course Pat Ryan won a race none of you thought he could win in upstate New York, leaning in to protecting women`s reproductive freedom, right here in New York.\par \par \uc2 My own opponent pretends there`s no threat to it in New York, but of course he voted to ban abortion when he was in the assembly. And if he had his way, every state in the country could ban it for any reason without exceptions. So the threats are real. If these guys control Congress, the first thing they`re going to do is they are going to try to pass a national abortion ban. And bear in mind, 75 percent of the Republicans now in the Congress support a national abortion ban. That means California and New York and other big blue states are on the chopping block, too. And so of course they want to disguise it and try to confuse or lull voters into a false sense of security. But when the wolf comes to the door, it`s wearing sheep`s clothing. And that`s what voters in blue states need to understand.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you -- do you believe the Democrats should have worked sooner on getting a better crime message?\par \par \uc2 It`s an argument Stan Greenberg, Bill Clinton`s longtime pollster, has made, and said that Democrats didn`t learn, even though many in the party said the right things about avoiding the phrase "defund the police," that there wasn`t enough done fixing the problems that clearly showed up in 2020 House races.\par \par \uc2 What say you?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, let`s look at the record. I mean, for one thing, we passed the most important gun safety legislation in 28 years, stood up to the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , stood up to the big gun lobbies, and no Republicans except a handful were willing to help. That`s really important.\par \par \uc2 In addition to that, we have passed the most important local police funding, $300 million over the next five years, out of the House. That`s called the Invest to Protect Act.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at our frontline candidates, my goodness, whether it`s Steven Horsford in Nevada, who`s put billions of dollars through his legislation into violence prevention initiatives that are evidence-based, or you`re talking about \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  in Pennsylvania, who`s been a leader in getting cop grants out there to hire and train more police officers.\par \par \uc2 In my own district, I`ve brought back $7 million for local police departments, including new technologies like ShotSpotter technology, like license plate readers, like body cameras. We`ve got a record of delivering for public safety. And we`re proud of that record.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, and there`s a bill, though, that was going to be a pretty big bill that every single national law enforcement group supported. It was called the Community Oriented Policing Services bill. "COPS" was short of it. The -- let me show you the bill`s co-sponsors here because it`s a who`s who of who Republicans are targeting in these House races, \uc2 Tom O`Halleran\uc2 , Arizona 1; \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , Michigan 7; \uc2 Angie Craig\uc2 , Minnesota 2; \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , Virginia 2; and \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , Virginia 7.\par \par \uc2 Was it a mistake not getting this bill passed? You could have gotten it passed. These -- these members of Congress needed it, and they`re all on the front line.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: I just told you, those members were in the lead on passing $300 million of funding for police departments under 125 officers. That`s every precinct -- every town, and cop, you know, in my district is in a department under 125 officers. Those members took the lead. And so the fact is that, together with the gun safety legislation, which the cops demand, because they don`t want to roll up to a scene and have a kid with an AR-15. We have done real things.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s contrast that to what our opponents have done, which is nothing on gun violence, which is nothing on supporting our police. My own opponent cut police budgets in Westchester County when he had the chance.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:02]\par \par \uc2 I mean, my goodness, at some point the record should matter here. And we`ve got a strong record of supporting police.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Final question here. Midterm results are very much always connected to presidential job rating. How much responsibility do you think President Biden has for this political environment?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: I think the president gets a bum rap. By the way, he`s been a leader on public safety his whole career. He`s also fixing our roads and our bridges. He`s bringing jobs back from China. He`s -- he`s capping seniors` out-of-pocket costs in Medicare. He took on the big drug companies.\par \par \uc2 If you want to know how all this dark money`s coming into these races, it`s because we took on the big drug companies. He`s helping our veterans with extraordinary health care, $250 billion. He`s done gun safety legislation. And let`s not forget the rescue plan. It saved every small business and restaurant and live venue in the country. I think the guy gets a bum rap. I think he`s -- he`s working through the damage of the Trump years and the pandemic, and he`s not getting enough credit for it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Now, we`ve got more to do, and he`ll be the first one to tell you that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, you know, based on all of your answers to me, it sounds like you don`t believe Democrats did anything wrong. You believe, if you don`t do well, it`s simply you had -- you had bad messaging or you didn`t communicate it right? Is that -- is that your basic take here?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Well, first of all, neither your or I know what`s going to happen on Tuesday, so what do you say we wait to see?\par \par \uc2 But, secondly, look, what I`m telling you is we`re not perfect, but we are responsible adults who didn`t attack the Capitol on January 6th, didn`t try to whitewash it, don`t pretend we`re for -- we`re for law enforcement and then ignore 140 cops getting beat up on that horrible day.\par \par \uc2 I`m -- we`re not perfect, but we`ve got a plan for gun violence and for bringing jobs back from China. You don`t like it; what`s yours? We took on the big drug companies. The other side won`t do that because they`re awash in big drug company money. No, we`re not perfect, Chuck. We`ve got all kinds of things we can do better. But we are responsible adults who believe in this democracy and we`re fighting for a better future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , chair of the DCCC, who`s on the ballot himself, be safe on the campaign trail, sir. Appreciate you coming on in these closing days to share your perspective. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And now I want to bring in Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida. He`s in charge of getting Republicans elected to the Senate. He chairs the National Republican Senatorial Committee. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Senator Scott, welcome back to "Meet the Press," sir.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, congratulations on 75 years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- I appreciate that, 3,600 episodes, and then some.\par \par \uc2 Let me start with this. You have not been shy about saying you believe Republicans are going to get 52 seats. Can you get 52 Senate seats without Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Oh, I think we`re going to have a great night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, can you do it without Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: We`re going to get 52-plus. We`re going to win Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand you believe you are. Do you...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And Wisconsin...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe you`re going to get to 52 -- can you get it without it?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Oh, absolutely. I mean, look it, here`s where we are right now. We`re going to win -- we`re going to win Wisconsin. Ron Johnson`s run a great race.\par \par \uc2 First off, let`s stop and think about it. We have great candidates. People are showing up to vote. There`s no energy on the Democrats` side. This election is about the Biden agenda. People don`t like high inflation, high crime, open borders, Fentanyl. That`s what we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 So Mehmet Oz is going to win. Ted Budd`s going to win. Hershel Walker`s going to win. (inaudible) is going to win. I think we have a really good shot in Arizona. I think we have a shot, a real good shot in New Hampshire. I think we have a shot in Washington and Colorado. We maybe even have a shot, depends on the voters, in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Think about it, half of the Democrat seats, we are in the hunt to win. So we`re going to get 52-plus.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about Arizona and New Hampshire. You -- you didn`t put them in your win category yet. You put them in there, you think you have a shot there. Coincidentally or not, those are the two seats that Senator McConnell`s Super PAC chose to pull out of and not invest in. In hindsight, was that a mistake?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, we`ve invested. We`ve invested all along. I`ve always believed Blake Masters has every reason to believe he can win. He`s run a great -- great race. We`ve defined kelly as somebody that has voted against the border in Arizona three times, has always voted with Schumer and Biden. And then we`ve got Hassan, who has a very difficult time up there because she`s voted basically 100 percent with the Democrats. And so I think Don Bolduc`s got a really good chance of winning. I think -- actually, if I was -- if I was predicting that, I believe we`re going to win both Arizona; I believe we`re going to win New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you come up short in the Senate and Arizona and New Hampshire are the reason, whose fault is that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I don`t believe we`re going to come up short. I believe we`ve got great candidates. I think the issues are on our side. We`ve defined -- we did our job at the NRC. We explained exactly who the Democrats were. We did it the first day I started in this job, and we`ve done it all along. So I don`t think we have that -- that risk. I think we`re going to win.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let`s assume you`ve got the majority. I`m going to take you at your word. You`re going to get the House; you`re going to get the Senate. What`s the first bill a Republican Congress sends to the president`s desk that you actually think he would sign?\par \par \uc2 [09:20:09]\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think the issues we`ve got to deal with, inflation, so we`ve got to figure out how to spend our money wisely so we don`t continue this inflation. I think we`ve got to do whatever we can to get this crime rate down. So I think we have to look at that. We`ve got to secure the border. I think we`ve got to get rid of the 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. So I think we have to -- we have to address the issues that people are worried about right now. And those are -- they`re worried about those issues, the Fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 So I think that`s what we need to focus on. So we -- we`ve got to make sure our military is focused on being lethal, not woke. So I think those are the things that we have to focus on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, let me ask you, on inflation, you saw that`s the biggest -- that`s the number one issue. People are dissatisfied with this economy. What`s the first bill you guys can pass that you think can impact inflation?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think the thing we have to work on is we`ve got to -- it`s -- on inflation, it`s all tied to reckless government spending. We`ve got to get our -- we`ve got to get our budget in control. We`ve got to figure out how we`re going to balance the budget. So that`s the first thing we have to do. We have to...\par \par \uc2 TODD: It doesn`t really sound like...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... spending under control.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t mean to...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... balanced the budget eight years. We`ve got to keep doing it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, all the experts say legal immigration is the number one thing we need to deal with in Congress in order to deal with inflation. Everything else is on the fed, but that`s the number one issue. Are you guys going to deal with that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Oh, I hope we deal with first securing the border, figuring out how we have legal immigration where people who want to live our dream can come in here. But I think let`s go back -- we have got to figure out how to spend our money better. We`ve got to -- we`ve got to figure out how do we get to a balanced budget and preserve the programs we care about? That`s what we have to do. If we don`t, we`ll never get inflation under control.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask ask you, you`ve been asked a million different ways about Social Security. You know the Democrats want to bring this issue up. Sunsetting the program every five years for renewal -- why do that? Why put Social Security into, sort of, the political -- into the political arena every five years? Why put seniors through that? Why do you think that`s a good idea?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I have no interest in -- in changing the Medicare program. I want to make sure we preserve the benefits of Medicare and Social Security. I don`t know one Republican who wants to change that. In my plan, I said we have got to start being honest with the public that what`s our plan? Medicare is going bankrupt. Social Security is going bankrupt. Here`s what the Democrats did. They just cut $280 billion, every Democrat that`s running right now, in the Senate, OK, and those in the House. They all voted to cut $280 billion out of Medicare, which is going to cut life- saving drugs. They did this. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, you`re doing a math -- you`re playing a math game there, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... in the Senate, and -- an on top of that, he didn`t pay his taxes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sean tor, you`re playing a math game here. You`re playing a math game. They didn`t cut anything on Medicare. It`s cost savings having to do with the prescription drug benefit. Look, I -- I understand that you want to call it something else, but it...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Chuck, if you cut spending in Medicare, you`re probably going to -- it`s probably going to impact the ability for somebody to provide things. When you cut out $280 billion...\par \par \uc2 TODD: I will remember that when you guys argue that a Medicare cut...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: That`s what`s going to happen. It`s...\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I will remember this when you guys claim a Medicare cut isn`t a cut.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Chuck, it`s $280 billion reduced spending -- $280 billion out of the Medicare reduction is going to cut life-saving drugs. That`s bad for citizens. It`s real simple.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I want to ask you something that \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  said at a Trump rally four days ago. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2 , (R) GEORGIA: Under Republicans, not another penny will go to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, is she right?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think we have to continue to do everything we can to support Ukraine, who wants to defend their freedom and stop Russia from continuing to expand. I don`t want to end up in a war with Russia. If we don`t stop -- if we don`t continue to help Ukraine the right way -- we can`t waste our dollars -- then they`re going to be in Poland or some other country where we will be at war because we have -- they`re part of our \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance. So I want to figure out how do we make sure that Ukraine defend their freedom and we stop Russia?\par \par \uc2 TODD: A couple of quick questions about your future. If Republicans gain the Senate majority, do you plan to run for leader?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I`m not focused on anything except getting a majority Tuesday night. Every -- everybody wants to ask me about a bunch of things that are going to happen after Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a non-answer.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: My whole focus is Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So leave it out there; it`s a possibility.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I`m going to focus on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s a possibility. All right. If...\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I`m focused on what I can get done Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Former President Trump has hinted he may announce in eight days. If he`s an active candidate for president, are you supporting him?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: There`s going to be a lot of people probably announcing. We`ll watch what happens. I`m focused on getting a majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And before I let you go, are you going to accept the results of all the Senate elections on Tuesday night?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely. But what we`re also going to do is do everything we can to make sure everybody -- they`re free and fair. And if anybody`s doing -- if there`s any shenanigans, we are ready to make sure -- we make sure we support our candidates to make sure that these elections are fair and every ballot is counted the right way.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:05]\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it sounds like you`re pretty confident that everything is going to be on the up and up, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: We`re going to try -- you know, we`re going to try our best. I`m -- I`m so disappointed the Democrats don`t want to have voter ID. We have it for everything else. They don`t want ballot -- we shouldn`t have ballot harvesting. So -- but I`m going to do everything I can to make sure this election is free and fair. And we`ve got to keep improving our election laws every year that we can.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , Republican from Florida, head of the NRSC.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 TODD: As you travel around the country, please stay safe on the trail, sir. And thanks for that. And thanks for your perspective.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, we`re going to do the battleground states that will determine who controls the Senate. Our correspondents are on the ground with the candidates as they make their closing arguments for E-Day.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We have correspondents on the ground in the four most important battleground states for the Senate, from Georgia to Arizona to Nevada, but of course we`re going to begin in the state of Pennsylvania. Because, frankly, that is where all the presidents seem to want to be. Both parties see the Pennsylvania Senate race between Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz as essentially, probably the most critical state to deicide control of the Senate. It`s certainly one of the closest contests out there. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , all of them were in the Keystone State Saturday making their closing arguments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Your right to vote is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Social Security and Medicare is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 There`s something else on the ballot, character. Character`s on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT BARACK OBAMA: Reason and basic decency are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 Democracy itself is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 The stakes are high.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Twenty-twenty-four, most importantly, we are going to take back our magnificent -- oh, it`s so beautiful -- White House. We`re going to take it back.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And you`re going to be hearing about it very soon -- very, very, very soon.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, my colleague Dasha Burns is in Philadelphia, perhaps the most important county to Democratic hopes of holding the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Dasha?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, Chuck, you said it. Not one, not two, but three presidents, all in this state, all on the same day, underscoring the outsized importance of this state. President Biden and former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  on the campaign trail together for the first time in this midterm election. And their joint effort was here in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 You said it there, too, Chuck, this city and its surrounding suburbs, the turnout here could make or break it for Democrats. And the suburban voters we`ve talking to in this area, Chuck, consistently list inflation and crime as their top issues. And Oz has tried to make a play for those voters, focusing on that issue of public safety, and trying to paint himself as a moderate candidate, which is a pivot from his MAGA approach in the primary that won him the endorsement of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [09:30:10]\par \par \uc2 And the question is: Will that rally he had with Trump last night, will that help or hurt him, especially in his effort to win over these purple suburbs? It`s going to matter, because every single vote won this weekend will be critical in a race that`s become a dead heat in the home stretch here, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No doubt, if Oz comes up short, that attendance with Mastriano and Trump, that rally is going to get questioned.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s go across the country. Let`s go to the state of Arizona. That is where Phoenix`s Maricopa County, two years ago, broke a 72-year streak of voting Republican for president when Biden won that key county two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, Arizona is the center of election denialism, and the cycle`s biggest test of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after Arizona Republicans picked Trump-liked nominees up and down the ticket from governor, to Senate, to secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn Hillyard is on the ground for us in Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn, how is get out the vote, which going in Arizona with, what, weeks for counting?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right, it`s a matter of turnout here at this point. And, Chuck, I think it`s important to note that this is ground zero for election denialism. In that quartet of GOP candidates, they are traveling around this state together, Kari Lake leading the charge. Each of these four individuals would be responsible for certifying Arizona`s election results in 2024.\par \par \uc2 But when you are looking at this, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  said that if she were to win on Tuesday night and beat Kari Lake for governor, the top reason would be because Arizonans rejected the election conspiracy theories. Now, in order to do that, the Republicans have a 4 percentage-point voter registration advantage over Democrats here. Meaning, that these Democratic candidates will need to win over major swaths of independents, like \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  did in 2018 and Mark Kelly did in 2020, as well as winning over some of those more reticent conservatives, those individuals who are reticent to Trumpism.\par \par \uc2 For Mark Kelly and these other Democrats, though, they are pressing that this is more than about the threat to democracy, but also about the threat to women`s reproductive rights, as well as the threat to Social Security -- Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Vaughn Hillyard on the ground for us in Phoenix -- Vaughn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now let`s go just northwest of Arizona to the state of Nevada. When the country gets the cold economically, Nevada usually gets the flu. And it`s in Nevada where Democrats are fighting to hold onto office up and down the ballot. Governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 , Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 , and all three House Democratic incumbents are in some tough reelection challenges. My colleague, Jacob Soboroff, is in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Jacob, another one where it`s all about partisan turnout.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Partisan turnout and also, you know, if Arizona is ground zero for election denialism, Nevada`s certainly one of the epicenters, Chuck, and the stakes could not be higher on that front as well. I was here almost exactly two years ago and watched with my own eyes as Senator Masto`s opponent, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , held that press conference the day after the election with all those Trump surrogates alleging thousands of illegal ballots here in Las Vegas and in the state of Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, that turned out to be completely untrue. But even with President Biden speaking out last week, saying democracy itself could be on the line, as we crisscrossed neighborhoods here in Las Vegas over the weekend with that powerful Culinary Workers Union, the issue we continued to hear over and over again was un-affordability in Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 Nevada, of course, is a state with one of the worst unemployment rates and the highest inflation rate in the nation. But at the end of the day, it`s all going to come down to turnout. And quick story, we were walking the streets here and we met 18-year-old Alberto, he was fixing up his car outside the house he shares with his mom and his dad.\par \par \uc2 And he said, "It is not all the negative attack ads on social media. It`s not all negative attack ads he`s hearing on his streaming service. Ultimately, it`s going to be face-to-face contact with those canvassers that convinced him that ultimately he can make a difference, and that`s going to get him out to vote, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, those are the most important undecided voters, those that are undecided about voting.\par \par \uc2 Jacob, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And finally, let`s go to the State that could keep us wondering about Senate control for a month, good old Georgia. That`s where we also find Governor Brian Kemp`s reelection as a test of whether the Republicans can split from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a reddish state and win. And he may win big. And if Kemp has coattails, can he pull Senate nominee \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  over the finish line?\par \par \uc2 The early vote in Georgia is shattering records.\par \par \uc2 Blayne Alexander is in Atlanta and has been tracking these races, really, since January 5th, 2021 when these Senate races were first decided. Blayne, what do you got?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely, Chuck. And you talk about shattering records. Certainly, we have seen that here in Georgia. In fact, more people have voted early during this midterm election than any other midterm in state history. We`re talking about some 2.5 million ballots already cast, with two very important races on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:02]\par \par \uc2 So talking about Georgia`s gubernatorial race, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is once again on the ballot. Of course, she`s the one who was widely credited with helping flip the state blue back in 2020 for President Biden. The question this time around is can she do it again, now that she is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, leading the Republican ticket is Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . But last time he faced off against \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  was back in 2018. And at the time, he was a Trump-endorsed candidate. But since then, of course, he`s had a rather public split from the former president over the results of the 2020 presidential election here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But still, the latest polls show that Governor Kemp is leading \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  by about six points or so. And that`s certainly good news, could be good news, for down-ballot Republicans who are hoping to get some sort of a boost from Governor Kemp. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we can see that everybody`s in daylight. Get ready, drink your coffee, guys. Dasha, Vaughn, Jacob, and Blayne, it`s going to be a long, long week.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the only thing we know for sure on Tuesday night is that there will be a red moon. But the real question is whether that`s going to translate to a red wave. We`re not kidding about the red blood moon, by the way.\par \par \uc2 Our panel is here, and they`ll be here next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, it`s panel time.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker, also co-anchor of "Weekend Today"; Amy Walter, publisher and editor-in-chief of "The Cook Political Report", she`s going to tell us the definition of a wave; former Republican Governor \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2  of North Carolina, and former Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill of Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Every elected official, Amy, believes a wave is whatever happens when they lose.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: If I win.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Oh, right, either way. It`s always about them, which \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  wouldn`t engage in.\par \par \uc2 Give me your definition of when you would think this is a wave. What is a wave, and how would you define it?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right. I mean, we can define it, you`re right, any way we would like. But look, I think there are three scenarios that we`re looking at. One is what a lot of Republicans feel like is happening right now.\par \par \uc2 The bottom is falling out. Democrats aren`t going to show up, not as many as Republicans. Republicans are fired up. Independents break overwhelmingly for Republicans. That`s a wave. That`s 30-plus seats. I would say that that would be a wave.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the Senate, it`s not just they win one seat, but maybe two or three.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:00]\par \par \uc2 I think we`re somewhere between scenario two and three, which is, as your poll showed, both sides really energized.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Polarization.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Going to show up. We`re totally polarized, so there are not defectors. To me it`s also where do those Independent voters go? When you get a wave election, Independents break by double digits--\par \par \uc2 TODD: And they haven`t broken yet.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: --for the out party. And they`re not breaking yet. So it makes me think either, (1) they are still sitting on the fence but are they breaking and just not telling us, or, (2), maybe they don`t show up. And that is another question. What does that look like, an electorate that`s just D and R intensity?\par \par \uc2 TODD: I do want to remind people -- only twice this century have we had a national election without party control changing of either the White House, House, or Senate.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So keep that in mind.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely. Well, Democrats and those who are close to President Biden say, if we can hold Republicans to 20 seats in the House, that`s a pretty good night. Because look at how many seats former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and former President Trump lost. Well, we`re talking about 60 and 40 respectively.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that that`s part of why you`re seeing the president try to focus on this messaging of democracy, that democracy is on the ballot. Some Democrats scratching their heads, why is he talking about that and not the economy? The pushback is, because that`s what`s going to energize the base and try to mitigate those losses that they`re trying to stave off.\par \par \uc2 It`s a risky strategy, though, Chuck, because as you`ve been talking about with all of your guests, the main issues are still inflation and the economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Claire, I`ve had Democrats quietly say, "Look, it better be only 20 seats." Because they want to be able to get it back in `24. And if it gets too big, and it becomes hard to get back in `24, that`s double for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, here`s the deal. Listen, this time of year is exciting for all of us who are so close to it. But it also gives me a giant headache. I mean, it is like, so ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you`ve been on the ballot. How does it feel when you`re on the ballot?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: I have won when the polls said I couldn`t and I have lost when the polls said I would win, so I am not a big believer in all of the horse race stuff. I think a lot of people are motivated to vote. And I think historically, to call it a wave it would have to be the same size as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost after his first two years in the presidency.\par \par \uc2 We would have to lose more than 40 seats, more than 60 seats, for it not to be lining up with what happens historically. And inflation is really hard, but what most Americans don`t get, this is global. This is not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have some of the least inflation in the developed world. But he has not been able to really get that home, because when bread costs that much and when eggs cost that much, that`s what people vote about.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting, Republicans start at such a high point, though. They only need 20-25 to hit the number that they`ve had in the past on this.\par \par \uc2 Pat, are your sources in North Carolina telling you? What are you hearing? Is it a polarized electorate, or do you hear, do you see a break?\par \par \uc2 PAT MCCRORY, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: In North Carolina, it feels like a wave. But as Claire and I know, I`ve played this game and I`ve been played by the game. The last week or two is all about emotion, no content. In fact, most of this election has been about emotion and no content, which is a very sad commentary.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, do you know that 70 percent of people told us they made up their minds before Labor Day?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: seventy percent.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: If the negative ads haven`t worked by now, they`re not working, if you try to hit emotionally. They`ve heard it all. They`re sick of TV ads right now.\par \par \uc2 But the Dems are trying to play on the emotion of democracy, abortions, cutting Social Security, which is an old tried, sometimes very effective effort, and Trump. Although they haven`t talked about Trump as much as I thought they would.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans I think still have the winners. Inflation, especially related to energy, which Rick didn`t mention.\par \par \uc2 The gas prices, the price of egg, the price of turkey as Thanksgiving`s coming up. That is what everyone`s reminded of every day. That`s why I give advantage to the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Immigration. Biden and Harris are an issue also. They don`t consume a room. They don`t have standing when they walk in the room. Some people do. \uc2 Obama\uc2  does. Biden and Harris don`t.\par \par \uc2 TODD: In fairness, nobody has standing when their approval ratings are in the low 40s. It`s amazing how much your standing gets better when you`re in the 50 percent, in fairness. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was a 1 percent candidate, he had no standing in `08.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Well, I think the issue`s going to be --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, right? In fairness.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Yeah, the issue`s going to be, with Trump going to Ohio and Florida, which is a whole other issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well --\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Biden going, are they going to turn off more voters?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I was just going to say let me bring this up, this was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last night. He is clearly focused on an election.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Wait, wait.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just not this one.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: He`s focused on himself?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, I know.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: No. No, he`s not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And he isn`t focused on this election.\par \par \uc2 Here he is, rattling off poll numbers to his rally-goers the other night. Listen to this from last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: There it is, Trump at 71. Ron DeSanctimonius at 10 percent. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  at 7. Oh, Mike`s doing better than I thought.\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , there`s no way she`s at 4 percent. There`s no way. There`s no way. But we`re at 71 to 10 to 7 to 4.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [09:45:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ron DeSanctimonius.\par \par \uc2 Okay, this idea that we were overhyping a Trump/DeSantis rivalry? No.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: No, we`re not. I mean, he already has a nickname for a potential rival.\par \par \uc2 Chuck, I had a conversation overnight with a high-level source who`s been in constant communication with former President Trump who said he very seriously considered announcing last night or sometime this weekend. His aides, advisors have urged him not to do that. Cooler heads prevailed, I`m told, and he didn`t.\par \par \uc2 He all but said that on the record, by the way. He all but walked up to the line of announcing that he was running for president again by saying, I will very, very, very, he added a fourth "very," probably do it. He said, but I want the focus to be on these candidates.\par \par \uc2 And I am told he is just itching to announce to potentially freeze the field. But the question is, will he?\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve talked to people that said the smartest thing he could do is hurry up and announce. It would freeze the field. But, Claire, if you could have \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  do one thing in the last 48 hours, is it this?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s what I thought.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Absolutely. Go for it Donald.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Oh, Claire.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is the last thing you want him doing, right.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: If I`m Republican, I don`t want Trump to take away from the message that`s going to win. And I don`t want -- if I`m a Democrat, I don`t want Biden out there either. I said that--\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yeah, Democrats are welcoming this. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, our guide to how to watch election night. With election counts that could take days, we`re going to give you some clues about where to look on Tuesday night itself.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 Even though we`re all counting down to Tuesday, let`s be realistic. It`s highly likely we will not know the full results of the midterms on election night. In fact, by 6:00 a.m., the day after Election Day 2020, look at all the battleground states where we were not at 95 percent, let alone 99 percent, right?\par \par \uc2 Look, Wisconsin, Ohio, North Carolina, Georgia and Florida. We should at least know where these are trending, we should know if Georgia`s in a runoff by 6:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 But look at the problem here. Pennsylvania, 78 percent. New Hampshire, just 75 percent reporting. Arizona, Nevada.\par \par \uc2 The states that are going to decide this majority, we`re not going to know the day after the election. But we`re going to get some clues about the House very early on.\par \par \uc2 [09:50:00]\par \par \uc2 Virginia and Indiana, their polls close by 7:00 p.m., and they`re both fast-counting states.\par \par \uc2 So here`s what we`re going to learn. \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , this should be the first district Republicans should be able to pick up. If they`re not picking this up, it`s suddenly a much better night for Democrats than people thought.\par \par \uc2 Virginia 07 and Indiana 01 should be the next two that Republicans flip if they`re going to have as good of a night as they think they`re going to have. Let me add another district here. If for some reason this turns into a red tsunami, keep an eye on the 10th congressional district in Virginia. That was the one \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2  held. If that flips, then you know a huge Republican night. Now, did African Americans turn out?\par \par \uc2 Two places to watch: Milwaukee County and Philadelphia County. Democrats need huge margins here. And if the African American vote shows up and it looks like 2018, then they should feel pretty good. But if those numbers are lower, then you know that there was a problem.\par \par \uc2 How about young voters? Dane County`s a good place to check in Wisconsin, home of the \uc2 University of Wisconsin\uc2 . Centre County`s home of \uc2 Penn State University\uc2 . Will the Democrats get these margins and the raw vote that they need? That`s two places to look for.\par \par \uc2 Now as for Republicans, they want a huge rural turnout. Well, Putnam County in Ohio, look at that. He had a 62-point margin in 2018. Will it get bigger? That`s something to watch there. Chattooga County in Georgia, this is Marjorie Taylor Greene`s district. How big will the Republican turnout be? That should matter.\par \par \uc2 And then also, if Republicans are going to have a really good night, they`ve got to improve their numbers in suburban counties. Delaware County`s a place to look here. Well, this is a place Democrats have been inching and eroding the Republican gains here. Will that continue or not?\par \par \uc2 Cherokee County, sort of an exurb if you will of Atlanta. Democrats have been making some progress there. Let`s see what happens in 2022.\par \par \uc2 So bottom line is this -- those are the places to watch. But it`s going to be election week, not election night.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we mark a milestone for this program, 75 years as the longest- running show in the history of television.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:55:52]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 We are celebrating our diamond anniversary, 75 years of Meet the Press. It was November 6th, 1947 at 8:00 p.m. when NBC Television debuted this program. Now more than 3,500 episodes later, we`ve had 12 moderators. Thirteen presidents have answered questions here, including six while in office.\par \par \uc2 Our legacy has evolved with the times, but the goal remains consistent: a fair exchange of views and perspective that gives our viewers important context on the issues of the day.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look at some of our memorable moments.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIPS)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Sir, are you ready to "Meet the Press"?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I suppose I`m as ready as I will be.\par \par \uc2 FIDEL CASTRO: Democracy is my ideal.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SHIRLEY CHISHOLM\uc2  (D-NY): I understand that I have broken the ice.\par \par \uc2 REV. \uc2 MARTIN LUTHER KING\uc2 , JR., CIVIL RIGHTS LEADER: We confront the fact that the Negro is still a victim.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Were you bitter, ever?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2  (D-GA): I was not bitter then. I`m not bitter now.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CARTER\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Neither I nor the American people would support the sending of an American team to Moscow.\par \par \uc2 FORMER SEN. \uc2 JOHN KERRY\uc2  (D-MA): This war can be ended, and it should be ended now.\par \par \uc2 TIM RUSSERT, NBC NEWS MODERATOR: Full wrath. That`s a very strong statement to the Afghans this morning.\par \par \uc2 DICK CHENEY\uc2 , FORMER VICE PRESIDENT: It is, indeed.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD NIXON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: The Watergate matter should have been handled properly.\par \par \uc2 RONALD REAGAN\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Some people, when you say that word conservative, automatically think you are talking about a monster who eats his young.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: So you want to be president.\par \par \uc2 THEN-GOV. BILL CLINTON (D), ARKANSAS: I do.\par \par \uc2 THEN-SEN. \uc2 JOHN MCCAIN\uc2  (R-AZ): Do \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  and I disagree on a specific issue? Yeah, because we`re both mavericks.\par \par \uc2 COLIN POWELL, FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: He is a new generation and for that reason I`ll be voting for Senator \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: We are going to be able to not just blunt the momentum of ISIL. Ultimately, we`re going to defeat `em.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , THEN-VICE PRESIDENT: Men marrying men, women marrying women and heterosexual men and women marrying one other are entitled to the same exact rights.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: In four years, you`re going to be interviewing me, and you`re going to say, "What a great job you`ve done, President Trump."\par \par \uc2 TOM BROKAW, NBC NEWS MODERATOR: Because if it is Sunday --\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: If it`s Sunday --\par \par \uc2 DAVID GREGORY, NBC NEWS MODERATOR: If it`s Sunday --\par \par \uc2 TODD: If it`s Sunday, it`s Meet the Press.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIPS)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look. I`m going to make you guys toast. You`re not toasting me. We`re toasting everybody.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Cheers. We`re toasting --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you`ve all picked your favorite race that you`re following on election night. We don`t have a lot of time. So, Pat, what is yours?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: My race, my House seat, North Carolina, where I live. Jeff Jackson, Pat Harrigan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: There -- sadly, there`s been violence. And how the public will respond to violence, possibly due to a commercial.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 Claire?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Nevada.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The whole state?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: It`s going to tell us about the state of election deniers, Hispanic voters, and whether or not a really good incumbent can hold on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quick --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Georgia Senate race. Will it go to a runoff?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. Ruin our Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: You took mine. Of course, Virginia 7. This is a seat that went for Biden --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: - in 2020, went for the Republican governor in 2021.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s as swingy as it gets.\par \par \uc2 All right. Before we go, a quick programming note: however you join us on Tuesday for complete election coverage of election night beginning at 6:00 Eastern on NBC News Now and at 8:00 p.m. on NBC and simulcast on NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 That`s all for today and for 75 years of todays. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020221107eib600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc59}{\*\bkmkend toc59}\b \uc2 Today for October 27, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Hallie Jackson, Gabe Gutierrez, Keir  Simmons, Tom Costello, Al Roker, Stephanie Gosk, Sheinelle Jones, Vicky  Nguyen, Jacob Soboroff \par \uc2 6,623 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:16]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. It`s Thursday. Big developments overnight in two key races that kind of decides who`s in charge in Washington.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: With just 12 days to go until the midterms, we`re going to break it all down for you. It is October 27th. This is Today.\par \par \uc2 New accusations, another woman comes forward claiming \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  urged her to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I do not believe that Herschel is morally fit to be a U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: While in Pennsylvania, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  addresses his debate performance.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATOR CANDIDATE: During that debate wasn`t exactly easy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We got it all covered.\par \par \uc2 Crisis mode, hospitals nationwide being forced to take drastic measures to handle the tripledemic surge of flu, COVID, and RSV.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Not only are there a lot of sick kids right now, there`s no place for them to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Just ahead, the new message from health officials all families need to hear.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, a new wave of protests in Iran, despite authorities intensifying a brutal crackdown, this morning the powerful scenes playing out across the country.\par \par \uc2 Under pressure, \uc2 Tesla\uc2  reportedly facing a criminal investigation over its autopilot claims after a string of high profile crashes. So has the popular automaker misled consumers about how safe their cars really are?\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, members only.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Contrary to popular belief, you can be any age to join AARP.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: From AARP to AAA, the surprising clubs you can sign up for to save big.\par \par \uc2 And feeling lucky.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Here are tonight`s winning numbers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: With no winner overnight, the Powerball jackpot jumps to a whopping $800 million, one of the largest prizes ever now up for grabs. Today, Thursday, October 27th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, good morning. It is so good to see you, happy that you`re here joining us. It is a Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, you got it lined up if you want to get those Powerball tickets. The drawing is this weekend so you have time to get your tickets but jackpots up to 800 million.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It is. We`re going to have a lot more on jackpot fever coming up. But we`re going to start with another big countdown. We`re talking about the one to the midterm.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, 12 days to go now, and the spotlight is intensifying on two major races in Georgia and Pennsylvania. What happens there could impact the lives of every American.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Here`s what`s at stake, the balance of power in the Senate and the direction of key issues from the economy to abortion rights to rising crime rates, NBC`s senior Washington correspondent Hallie Jackson has the very latest for us. Hey, Hallie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON, NBC NEWS SENIOR WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Hey Hoda, hey Savannah. You have laid out the stakes and it is crunch time now, right, for candidates in these really important really close races with big guns like former presidents in both parties set to hit the campaign trail in the coming days. And the focus today turning to Georgia, where there`s a new abortion allegations surfacing, and one of the country`s most closely watched matchups.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): With less than two weeks to go this morning, the future of the Senate could hang on to key states with neck and neck races in Pennsylvania and Georgia, where there`s now another twist, Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  who`s campaigned on an anti-abortion platform now denying a new allegation from a second woman who says Walker pressured her to have an abortion in the early 90s.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATOR CANDIDATE: What I`m saying, this is a lie and I`ve said it once and I`ve moved on my campaign.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): The anonymous woman sharing her voice only came forward in a Zoom press conference arranged by Attorney Gloria Allred. The accuser saying after she became pregnant in 1993, Walker encouraged her to have an abortion, adding she first couldn`t go through with the procedure, but that Walker encouraged her to go back driving her to a Dallas clinic where she had the abortion. The woman explaining why she`s speaking up now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am a registered Independent and I voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in both elections. I do not believe that Herschel is morally fit to be a U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): NBC News has not verified her allegations and the woman did not provide evidence Walker was involved in her abortion.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I`m done with this foolishness.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): The Republican candidate and former football star also denied allegations earlier this month from another former girlfriend who says Walker paid for her abortion in 2009. In a statement the campaign for Walker`s opponent, Senator Raphael Warnock says Walker has a problem with the truth, a problem answering questions, and a problem taking responsibility for his actions. That match up, very close with recent polling showing Warnock leading by just five points. And in another closely watched race in Pennsylvania a tossup with polls ranking Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican candidate Dr. Mehmet Oz within the margin of error.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:11]\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: During that debate wasn`t exactly easy, you know, I knew it wasn`t going to be easy after, you know, having a stroke after five months.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON (voice-over): Fetterman appearing at a campaign rally in Pittsburgh after raising $2 million, his team says, in the hours after his one and only debate against Republican Mehmet Oz. Fetterman facing questions about his at times halting debate performance with lingering auditory processing issues after a stroke five months ago. Oz also on the campaign trail hammering the issue of crime and on defense against a new Democratic ad highlighting his debate comments on abortion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is who Dr. Oz wants in charge of women`s health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATOR CANDIDATE: I want women, doctors, local political leaders, local political leaders.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Meanwhile, Hallie, the President hitting the campaign trail today, what do you expect?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: He is all about the economy, Savannah, in this kind of closing argument here trying to highlight his administration`s record on something we know that voters care about the economy plus cost of living. The top issue that voters say matters to them come Election Day. The President is going to be at Upstate New York to argue that Democrats economic policies are better than Republicans, even as many GOP candidates have pointed to high inflation in their midterm campaigns. You`ve also got the president`s cabinet fanning out across the country too. All of it is part of this full core press ahead of Election Day. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Twelve days to go. Hallie Jackson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to growing concerns over the dangerous tripledemic of viruses nationwide flu, COVID, and the respiratory illness, RSV. With cases surging, some hospitals even in areas of warmer weather, are running out of beds. NBC national correspondent Gabe Gutierrez is inside Phoenix Children`s Hospital this morning with more. Gabe, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. It`s only October but doctors here and across the country are dealing with patient numbers that they usually don`t see until much later in winter. And overnight we met one mother who barely heard of RSV until she ended up here with her newborn son.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voice-over): This morning, the latest wave of child respiratory illnesses is spreading prompting more concerns of a tripledemic this winter. In Arizona, RSV cases are up 344 percent over a typical season.\par \par \uc2 CHEYENNE SHIELDER`S (ph), SON DIAGNOSED WITH RSV: Terrifying, this is serious illness. You know, with him being this young. He doesn`t have an immune system to fight it off.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voice-over): Cheyenne Shielder`s (ph) two-month-old son Ryder (ph) was in the hospital overnight.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What would you tell other parents?\par \par \uc2 SHIELDER: Look up the symptoms of RSV and trust her gut to because I was on the border about coming or not to the hospital. And I feel like I probably should have come a little bit sooner.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voice-over): Last year and now this year, Phoenix Children`s Hospital has been dealing with an unusually high number of RSV cases, though the facility is not at capacity, it`s bracing for a rough winter\par \par \uc2 DR. WASSIM BALLAN, PHOENIX CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL CHIEF OF INFECTIOUS DISEASES: COVID-19 combined with a potentially bad influenza season and now we`re seeing RSV going up at the same time, that obviously makes us concerned that we might get into this triple pandemic situation.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voice-over): This year, even western and southern states with warmer temperatures are seeing a significant rise in RSV and flu cases. In Arizona, 87 percent of pediatric hospital beds are occupied, in Texas 91 percent, Seattle Children`s tells NBC News it`s at 200 percent capacity in the ER.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA, WHITE HOUSE COVID-19 RESPONSE COORDINATOR: Under normal circumstances, about 90 percent of kids end up getting RSV at least once, by the time there are two. Because of the pandemic, some of the kind of standard patterns have been disrupted so we`re seeing a lot of RSV at once.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voice-over): Parents now worried sick across the country from San Diego where Fernanda Costanarez (ph) is caring for baby Alejandra (ph).\par \par \uc2 FERNANDA COSTANAREZ (ph), DAUGHTER DIAGNOSED WITH RSV: She was struggling to breathe. She was struggling breathing. And we got scared because she had no color at all on her skin.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (voice-over): To Washington, D.C. where three-year-old Izzy Banki (ph) is recovering.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Nobody wants to like hold their kid down and hold a mask on their face. And so she`s screaming and trying and trying to rip the breathing tubes out. And that breaks your heart as a parent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: And while COVID cases are trending downward nationwide, right now, there are concerns about new strains this winter. And of course, Savannah, concerns about that flu season that is already hitting many states hard around the country. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Gabe Gutierrez, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Meantime, new unrest is rocking Iran with massive protests across the country more than a month after a young woman`s death while being held by the nation`s morality police. Some of those demonstrations turned deadly, emitted ongoing crackdown and that`s led to new sanctions from the United States, NBC`s chief international correspondent Keir Simmons has been following this story for us. Hey Keir, good morning.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hoda, good morning to you. And we`re just getting more incredibly impactful video from the city of Mahabad in north western Iran. Take a look at this. This is a just this morning. You can see protesters targeting a building there. Later they seem to be throwing stones there. Looks like there is tear gas. The Kurdish rights group that posted this video says a number of government buildings have been taken over and that it`s also rounds the funeral of a man who was shot dead during protests yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): This morning, a powerful image seen around the world. A young woman not wearing a headscarf stands on the roof of a car hands in the air as crowds attempt to reach the grave of Mahsa Amini. And near the cemetery, this video from an independent Kurdish human rights organization, captioned the resistance is glorious. Huge numbers across Iran determined to mourn and to protest the death of the 22-year-old, marking 40 days after she died in the hospital, the morality police targeting her for allegedly violating the country`s strict clothing code. In the capital of Tehran, clashes with the security forces and the sound of gunfire.\par \par \uc2 In Mahsa`s hometown, they shouted women, life, freedom. And these demonstrators daring to chant freedom, freedom, enough of the despotism. Then images like this one, smoke rising told them another violent crackdown there. Reports say live rounds were used and tear gas. The Iranian authorities switching off the internet in the city. But a semi official news agency reporting at least 10,000 demonstrated. Thousands of miles away the White House announcing sanctions targeting more than a dozen Iranian officials.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Our message to Iran is very, very clear, stop killing your people and stop sending weapons to Russia to kill Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2  (voice-over): A dramatic change from earlier this year when the Biden administration was trying to negotiate a nuclear deal with Iran. But this morning, there is no sign of compromise in Tehran and no backing down by these young female protesters and the many men out on the streets in support.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : And just underscoring how incredibly tense it is in Iran, Hoda, a news of a shooting that was not related to the protests, 15 worshippers were killed, ISIS claiming responsibility for that. The government blaming foreign terrorists. It just emphasizes how incredible it is that these protesters are coming out on the streets day after day to support Mahsa Amini.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, and worth repeating, too, that as you said, it`s not just women on those streets, it looked like equal numbers of men that we were seeing in those videos all with the same cause. All right, Keir Simmons for us in London. Kier, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Back home, we`ve got new concerns about energy bills this winter. We haven`t even handed out the candy to trick or treaters. But in recent weeks, a lot of us have gotten that early taste of the cold and it doesn`t bode well for what`s to come. NBC`s Tom Costello over in the Bronx with that story this morning. Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Savannah, we are at an oil terminal facility here in the Bronx. So oil comes out of this facility and into homes across the Northeast. And millions of people use heating oil to heat their homes in the Northeast leading oil stocks right now, the lowest in decades. But there is no talk of rationing. It doesn`t matter if you heat your home with heating oil, gas, or electricity, you`re going to pay more to stay warm this winter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): From the Northeast and Midwest to parts of the south, Mother Nature has already given millions of us a sneak peek at what`s ahead. And this morning, the forecast for America`s collective wallet this winter may also be a bit frigid.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a very, very high 