y.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, have you seen this? A Kentucky coal miner who raced right from work to take his three-year-old son to a basketball game, we`re going to hear from him and tell you something really special that came from that photo. But first, your local news and weather.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:21]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:19]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We are back 7:30. What a pretty sunrise in Fort Myers Beach, Florida. It`s been hard times there in the last month, one month since Ian hit. Well, \uc2 Al Roker\uc2  has arrived along with our lend a hand truck. And we are really hoping to do some good there this morning. We`ll see that community really coming together battling to rebuild and it`s happening. We`ve got some special things planned.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: It`s good.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love when Roker arrives. Meantime, inside Studio 1A Sheinelle is here, Craig is on assignment. Shall we get to our 7:30 headlines?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, let`s do it. A Wisconsin man has been convicted of killing six people injuring dozens of others when he drove his SUV through a Christmas parade. It took the jury a little more than three hours to find Darrell Brooks guilty of all 76 charges. Police say he drove his vehicle into the parade in downtown Waukesha last November, despite police warnings to stop. Brooks, he faces his six life sentences, one for each count of intentional homicide.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Three men tied to the kidnapping plot of Michigan Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  were convicted on all charges yesterday. They were found guilty of supplying material support for a terrorist act. That men were part of a -- they were part of a militia group upset over COVID restrictions. Whitmer called the verdict proof that violence and threats have no place in our politics.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Seven people were hospitalized after a steam powered train derailed at an amusement park in Missouri. Video from the scene shows three passenger cars tipped on their sides as you see here. The train runs within \uc2 Silver Dollar City\uc2  which is an 1880s themed amusement park west of Branson. Thankfully, no one was seriously injured in the accident and no word this morning on how the train came off the tracks.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We turn now in a potential new trouble for \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , the automaker reportedly at the center of a criminal investigation this morning.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, NBC`s Stephanie Gosk is on this story for us. Hey, Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Hey, guys, good morning. According to Reuters, the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  reportedly launched the previously undisclosed probe last year in the wake of more than a dozen crashes, some of them fatal involving Tesla`s driver assistance system, autopilot, which was activated during those accidents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Autopilot changes the way you relate to your drive.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): \uc2 Tesla\uc2  famous for its attention grabbing tech innovations has long touted the autopilot feature and its cars.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Designed for highway use, autopilot assists you with the routines of daily driving, whether you`re cruising or crawling.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voice-over): But this morning, the company could be under fire for that technology. Reuters reporting the electric car company is facing a criminal investigation by the Department of Justice for its claims that its cars can drive themselves, examining whether \uc2 Tesla\uc2  misled consumers. According to Reuters, citing three sources familiar with the matter, quote, the \uc2 U.S. Department of Justice\uc2  launched the previously undisclosed probe last year following more than a dozen