ar \uc2 GUTHRIE: Big market for them too. Stephanie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right, still ahead, a long awaited comeback on PopStart Carson has a little taste of Rihanna`s first new music in six years.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. But first, Vicky is here with a helpful guide to all of the memberships that can save you money. And Vicky some might surprise you.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:04]\par \par \uc2 VICKY NGUYEN, NBC NEWS SENIOR CONSUMER INVESTIGATIVE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, absolutely. Young people joining AARP. Good morning ladies. Who isn`t looking for sneaky and not so sneaky ways to save? Coming up, I`m going to have the discounts that may be right in front of you, including ways that you can get free entertainment, that`s right after these messages on Today.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We are back 7:39, Consumer Confidential, our series just about the same time every morning. We focus on the issues that impact the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, well this morning, we are exploring various memberships that can help you save and fight against inflation including places like AARP and they are attracting you guys a young girl audience.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Interesting. NBC`s senior consumer investigative correspondent Vicky Nguyen is here with more. Hey, Vicky.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Hi guys. Yes, you don`t have to be a senior to join AARP. We are all looking for ways to save right now. And sometimes the shortcuts there right in front of us from sharing a club membership to apps that actually give you access to Oscar winning content for free. We have found some really simple and surprising ways to put a few dollars back into your wallet.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:40:06]\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): With rising costs on essential needs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Inflation is awful. Everything costs so much.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): It`s time to get smart about saving.\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN O`KEEFE MERRICK, ADVISOR AT O`KEEFE FINANCIAL PARTNERS: We`re all trying to figure out ways to save money, and to be super impactful with our spend, especially as the holidays approach.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): And the discounts are out there, a simple library card can actually save you from hefty streaming subscriptions. Apps like Overdrive, Hoopla and Kanopy offer catalogs of content for free. And one surprising trend now among young people, signing up for benefits from the AARP.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Contrary to popular belief, you can be any age to join AARP. The benefits are awesome.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): The Wall Street Journal finding 20-somethings as new card carriers in an effort to snag discounts amid inflation, showing it`s not just for the 50 and older crowd.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA SCHWARTZ (ph), AARP MEMBER: In my head, it was always something for senior citizens.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Twenty-eight-year-old Marissa Schwartz (ph) says she learned a lot about AARP benefits through TikTok, and couldn`t help but join in on the savings and even convinced her parents to do the same. In a similar fashion, a AAA membership can also lend you deals from food, entertainment, and travel, while wholesale clubs are a popular way to save in bulk.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I paid about $200. I think there`s way more than $200 worth in this basket.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s just crazy how expensive at all is and it really, it just adds up.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Maximize your wholesale membership by sharing with another person to cut costs. Alone, \uc2 Costco\uc2 `s memberships can run $60 a year, $120 if you`re looking at the executive level, while Sam`s Club is $50 per year, 110 for its Plus membership. And before you sign up, checkout sites like \uc2 Groupon\uc2 , \uc2 RetailMeNot\uc2 , and Slickdeals, for discounts on the membership itself.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK: I think people will love a deal. The idea that the buy one get one, the 10 percent off, the 15 percent off, you really feel like you`re gaming the system.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): But buyer beware of signing up for too many costly memberships. Experts suggest narrowing down the necessities first to determine which ones will be the most impactful and avoid getting overrun by the club fees.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK: You have to be really thoughtful with these memberships. Because if you`re going to spend money to join a membership, then you need to really make sure it`s worth it on the backend.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONES: And Vicky to make things even easier, these memberships can be used and the cards can be used digitally.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, sometimes you don`t want to pack that wallet and carry it everywhere because you might just pop into the store. Well a lot of these cards can be stored right there on your phone. In \uc2 Costco\uc2  for example, they have an app, so your membership information is always with you if you have your phone with you. So it makes easier.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Makes sense.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We already are members of the AARP. Yes, do you guys want to come join?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Sure, we`re in.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Do you think AARP is going to be like, OK, let`s close that little hole?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: No. I think they love the new members, 20-somethings discounts for everybody.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right, all right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And by the way, Vicky, you`re going to be back tomorrow and you`re going to help everybody get ready for Halloween.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, you know what, this is crazy. You don`t think about it, but Halloween can be one of the most dangerous nights of the year. All kinds of accidents happening on the roads, also at parties, haunted houses, even the decorations tripping hazards, so we`re going to talk about everything you need to know to help keep your ghouls and goblins safe.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Vic, thank you. Let`s go down to Al. He`s in Fort Myers Beach, Florida lending a hand this morning. Hi Al.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Hey guys, good morning. And we are going to bring you that in just a little bit, the devastation here just unbelievable. But the spirit is so incredible. So we`ll show you that in just a bit. Let`s show you right now what`s going on as far as your weather is concerned, we are looking at a beautiful day eastern third of the country sunshine from the Great Lakes to the Gulf to the Mid-Atlantic up to the northeast. Snowy and chilly through the Northern Rockies, some afternoon storms developing in Texas. Look for plenty of sunshine along the west coast. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather. Guys, our lend a hand gates rolling in the next half hour.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Looking forward to it for sure. Thank you, Al.\par \par \uc2 [07:44:39]\par \par \uc2 Up next, the sweet story behind a viral photo of a Kentucky coal miner and his young son at a basketball game and what happened after that image blew up on social media. That`s right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Today`s Can`t Miss, you really don`t want to miss.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: This one is a great one. It`s the story of a father`s love and dedication and it was all captured Jacob in one single photo.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I haven`t seen this photo all over the place. You guys have probably seen it too. Check it out guys, this is the picture. You can see a man with coal mine dust sitting on his face. He`s sitting there with his three-year-old son at the Kentucky Wildcats annual blue white basketball scrimmage. It was posted actually by head coach John Calipari getting nearly 60,000 likes in just two days, attention and fame that Michael McGuire says that he`d never ever expected.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCGUIRE, COAL MINER: It`s just a mind blowing. I`ve never imagined that.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): Disbelief and excitement, Michael McGuire and his wife Molly are awestruck at the attention that this one photo garnered in a matter of days. On Saturday, McGuire rushed from the coal mines, a 30 to 40 minute drive to Rupp Arena in Pikeville in order to take his three year old son Easton to his first in person college basketball game.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY MCGUIRE, MICHAEL MCGUIRE`S WIFE: Easton has took a huge interest in sports. So it was the perfect time to buy tickets for Michael and Easton to start that.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): Working long hours underground and up to six days a week, family time is precious to McGuire. With no time to spare not even to clean off the coal mine dust that powdered his face and clothes. He got to the game in the nick of time.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCGUIRE: I didn`t want to miss it. I`ve had to miss quite a few stuff.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:01]\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): The sight of McGuire and his son moved to head coach John Calipari who posted this picture on his \uc2 Instagram\uc2  writing, my family`s American dream started in a Clarksburg, West Virginia coal mine, so this picture hits home. Adding, he wanted to gift the family VIP tickets to Rupp Arena. Calipari wasn`t the only one touched. Comments on the post included, you just won me over coach, being an ex-coal miner myself, I know this all too well. Another user writing, this is Kentucky in one photo.\par \par \uc2 The annual blue white scrimmage is one in U.K.`s biggest fan favorites ahead of the regular season. But it was especially important this year because it raised over $162,000 to help victims of this summer`s deadly floods in eastern Kentucky. Floyd County where McGuire`s from was one of the worst hit areas.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY MCGUIRE: We had some friends that lost a lot, that`s when we looked at each other and was like, let`s do all we can.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF (voice-over): The check was presented by the Wildcats to Kentucky`s governor during halftime. As for the McGuire`s time at the game.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCGUIRE: Easton was dancing and everything.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY MCGUIRE: That`s a fun experience for us as a family.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Such a special game and in so many ways. I didn`t know this but Michael and Molly told us it`s actually pretty normal to see parents covered in coal dust at those practices. They say that kids don`t even bat an eye, coal mining is the dominant industry by Far and Eastern Kentucky. And as for the VIP tickets, you guys, the McGuire`s said they`re going to bring along their one-year-old daughter Lindley to the game. And there`s another game coming up Kentucky is facing off against the Kansas Jayhawks. So I think Sheinelle --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: -- in January.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- basketball --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Is it amazing?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, it`s really awesome.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That was such a beautiful image. Man, I didn`t know the story behind it. I was like I thought that was beautiful but wow.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: -- the Appalachian, up in the Pikeville, a Dateline there. Those are the greatest, kindest people. And I think it`s just so well deserved attention that he`s getting.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That was great.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Thank you Jacob.\par \par \uc2 Well speaking of basketball, checkout who is with us this morning, the Harlem Globetrotters live on our plaza. OK, they`ve got the moves we know that but did you know Hoda has got some moves too. She took the cord with the Globetrotters. And we cannot wait to show you that but first check if your local news, your weather, and these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:10]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221028eiar00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc60}{\*\bkmkend toc60}\b \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Facing A New Allegation Of Paying For An Abortion Of A GOP Senate Candidate Is Responding Tonight\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 280 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Let me turn now to Kristen. Kristen, there`s a new headline in that tight Georgia Senate race, an anonymous allegation against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . What do we know?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, that`s right. This latest allegation comes from an anonymous woman who spoke on Zoom without showing her face in a press conference arranged by Attorney Gloria Allred, an allegation \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is strongly denying tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, the woman claims she had an affair with Walker, the football star turned Republican Senate candidate, and got pregnant nearly 30 years ago. She says Walker pressured and paid for her to have an abortion, but she did not provide any evidence to support those claims.\par \par \uc2 Now, Walker fired back today calling the claims foolishness and a lie, saying his Democratic opponent Senator Raphael Warnock "lost the debate and Democrats will now say anything to win." The woman`s claim comes after another woman accused Walker of paying for her abortion which Walker also strongly denied. The Georgia race is a toss-up that could determine Senate control. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221028eiaq0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc61}{\*\bkmkend toc61}\b \uc2 The Fallout From A High Stakes Showdown In Pennsylvania With Control Of Congress Hanging In The Ballots\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 1,226 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the fallout from a high stakes showdown in Pennsylvania with control of Congress hanging in the ballots.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz facing off in their first and only debate. Fetterman`s health struggles in the spotlight months after he suffered a stroke. Oz under scrutiny for an answer he gave on abortion. The reaction from voters just 13 days before the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  facing a new allegation of paying for an abortion of a GOP Senate candidate is responding tonight.\par \par \uc2 The verdict in the trial of a Wisconsin man who killed six people when he plowed his SUV into a Christmas parade.\par \par \uc2 The deadly school shooting in St. Louis. The warning the shooters family gave to police and they`re pleas to take a gun away from him months before the rampage.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  overseeing nuclear drills as Russia pushes unproven dirty bomb allegations against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And the American fighter killed in Ukraine two months ago finally on his way back home.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals filling up with children infected with RSV. What parents need to know.\par \par \uc2 The critical shortage in a medication millions used to treat ADHD.\par \par \uc2 And he went from homeless to shooting college hoops. The basketball star inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. Just 13 days before the Midterm Elections, and a flood of political ads are saturating the airwaves on the heels of another marquee debate reminding just what is at stake in hard-fought congressional races, power and control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 In the House, Democrats trying to defend their majority with all 435 seats of the body up for election. Dems facing the same challenge in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  with 35 seats are up for election. The balance of power in the Senate could be determined by a handful of tight races, including Pennsylvania where last night`s debate pitted Republican Mehmet Oz against Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  sparring over abortion, fracking, and immigration. But not before Fetterman call out what he described as the elephant in the room, his health.\par \par \uc2 Dasha Burns starts us off tonight with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Pennsylvania Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz back on the campaign trail following last night`s critical one and only face-off.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Pennsylvania deserves better than Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: His opponent, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , not yet making any public appearances after the fierce clash.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: It`s something called the Oz rule that if he`s on TV, he`s lying.\par \par \uc2 OZ: And this is the only debate I could get you to come to talk to me on and I had to beg.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: A debate the Fetterman`s health front and center. Fetterman whose campaign says suffers from auditory processing challenges after a stroke six months ago, requesting these closed captioning monitors in the studio.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I had a stroke. He`s never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate. Mush two words together, but it knocked me down, but I`m going to keep coming back up.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Fetterman did miss some words during the hour-long showdown, at times of struggling to answer questions, including this moment. Fetterman was asked about his changing position on fracking, which he now says he supports.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is that 2018 interview that you said, "I don`t support fracking at all." So, how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking. And I don`t --I don`t -- I support fracking and I stand -- and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Fetterman would not commit to releasing his full medical records.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: My doctor believes that I`m fit to be serving, and that`s what I believe is where I`m standing.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And in one of the biggest clashes of the night going after Dr. Oz on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: He celebrated when Roe v. Wade went down and my campaign would fight for Roe v. Wade, and if given the opportunity, to codify it into law.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And tonight, the Fetterman campaign zeroing in on this response from Oz.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want women, doctors, local political leaders letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 Local political leaders. Local political leaders.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Turning it into a campaign ad against his opponent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oz is too extreme for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But Oz calling Fetterman the extreme candidates throughout the night, especially on the issue of crime.\par \par \uc2 OZ: We have taken away the ability of police to do their job and that son \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The momentous matchup coming just two weeks before Election Day. Voters with strong reactions about Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: His comment about political leaders, you know, determining what a woman -- a woman can and cannot do with her body is -- was very telling.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:09]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just don`t think he`s capable at this time to be in that position. I mean, if you have somebody that can`t even handle a debate that has to, you know, read from this prompter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Dasha, some big names could soon be campaigning there on both sides.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (on camera): Yes, Lester. Plans are coming together for both President Biden and former President Obama to campaign with Fetterman, and the Democratic candidate for governor. And former President Trump will campaign with Dr. Oz and the Republican gubernatorial candidate. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dasha Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Andrea Mitchell and Kristen Welker. Andrea, first to you. What are you hearing there in Pennsylvania about the impact of that debate?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, Democrats here are telling me that they think Fetterman will be OK, given his labor support and Oz`s missteps on abortion and the minimum wage. But they acknowledge that this format did not work for Fetterman. Republicans say they`re pleased with how it went.\par \par \uc2 The fact that this race is even close, though, tells you Republicans have momentum right now and it`s happening nationwide. In the Senate, enough incumbent Democrats could lose, giving Republicans several chances to take control. And in the House Democrats are in trouble in states like Oregon and New York, which are normally safe.\par \par \uc2 Last night there was a debate for New York Governor where Republican Trump ally Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is within striking distance of Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , which is remarkable in a solid blue state. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea, thanks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221028eiaq0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc62}{\*\bkmkend toc62}\b \uc2 Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  And Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz Face Off\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 1,889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. It`s Wednesday, all eyes were on Pennsylvania overnight.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. The first and only Senate debate there with control of Congress hanging in the balance. It is October 26th, this is Today.\par \par \uc2 Center Stage, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz face off on the issues.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: My campaign would fight for Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  picks everything to an extreme and those extreme positions hurt us all.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And ongoing concerns over Fetterman`s fitness to serve.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I had a stroke, he`s never let me forget that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This morning the candidates in their own words and the takeaways in one of the nation`s most important midterm contests.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Rampage, new details on the gunman behind that deadly school shooting in St. Louis, with a letter found in his car is revealing about a possible motive, and the stories of bravery now emerging.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I really credit my students a lot because even with the gunshots that close, they did not scream.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That harrowing firsthand account just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Cooling Off, housing prices falling fast after soaring to record highs. So is now the best time to buy, and what does it mean if you`re in the market to sell. We`ll break it down.\par \par \uc2 All of that plus, Chaos and Controversy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Miss USA is Texas.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The Miss USA pageant embroiled in scandal. Some contestants claiming it`s rigged. The organization`s president now suspended and an investigation underway. The very latest straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 And breaking news, what scientists are now saying about whether you really need to rake those leaves and it could make your weekend a lot more enjoyable. Today, Wednesday, October 26, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning, everybody. Welcome back to TODAY. It is Wednesday morning, nice to have you along with us. This raking story, you want to get back those hours of your childhood raking leaves, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can you imagine -- you go remember all the time your parents said you got to go out and rake the leaves and stuff them into hefty bags. Who knew that we didn`t have to do that?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Science is saying something different this morning. We`ll get to that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. We got a busy morning a lot to get to including we`ve got some rare good news. It`s tied to the economy at least if you`re planning to buy a house.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. Prices are now going down at a record rate. Tom Costello is going to have everything we need to know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Of course, we will begin with last night`s big debate in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This was highly anticipated not only because of questions concerning Democrat John Fetterman`s health after his stroke six months ago, but also because this race could decide which party controls the Senate. And that will affect every single American come next year. We`ll show you how it all played out.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start this morning with NBC`s Dasha Burns in Harrisburg. Dasha, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. This debate was highly anticipated not just by voters here in Pennsylvania but people across the country. It`s an extremely tight race, just a couple of points separating these candidates in the polls. And in a battle against one of the most familiar faces on American television, many tuned in to see how Fetterman would fare after suffering a stroke earlier this year, and the stakes could not have been higher.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): In a race that could help decide the balance of power in Congress, Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz neck and neck in the polls finally face to face on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I`m running to serve Pennsylvania. He`s running to use Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 OZ: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  picks everything to an extreme and those extreme positions hurt us all.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Fetterman acknowledging what he called the elephant in the room, the stroke that nearly killed him five months ago.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I had a stroke, he`s never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate, mush two words together. I`m also fighting for any forgotten community all across Pennsylvania. They ever got knocked down that had to be -- to get back up. And I`ve made my entire career dedicating to those kinds of pursuits.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Fetterman using closed captioning to assist with auditory processing battling Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It`s about supporting and helping young earners -- excuse me, young students to give them a break.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): But experiencing some stumbles throughout the debate particularly during an exchange over fracking challenged by the moderators for past contradictory comments.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking and I don`t -- I don`t -- I support fracking and I stand and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:03]\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Fetterman says he`s fit to serve citing a letter from his doctor, but he would not commit to releasing his full medical records.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I believe if my doctor believes that I`m fit to serve, and that`s what I believe is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): The celebrity TV doctor never directly addressing Fetterman`s health but saying this during an exchange on education.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Visited vocational schools. I`ve been answered but John, because obviously, I wasn`t clear enough for you to understand this.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): One point where Oz has not been clear how he`d vote on a 15 week abortion ban introduced by Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want women doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive, to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): A clear difference from his opponent.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: My campaign would fight for Roe v. Wade, and if given the opportunity to codified into law.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Oz also trying to court moderate undecided voters. But it was former President Trump`s endorsement that helped get him onto the Republican ticket here.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has supported you. He has endorsed you. Why won`t you fully commit to supporting him in 2024?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Oh, I do. I would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he decided to run for president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And, Savannah, speaking to media after the debate, the Fetterman spokesperson said the campaign was thrilled with his performance saying he won "countless exchanges against Oz." And we asked the Oz spokesperson what the Republican meant when he said that decisions about abortion should be made between "a woman or her doctor and local political leaders." The campaign responded that he is "prolife" with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother. And, Savannah, voters that we talked to overnight said that they wish they`d seen more substance and fewer attacks, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Dasha Burns leading us off, thank you. Let`s turn to NBC`s Hallie Jackson. You watch the debate as well, Hallie. I mean, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  said the elephant in the room was his health, his recent stroke, his fitness to serve, how did it play in the room?\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So I talked to some folks are in the room. I was on the phone with a campaign advisor right after the debate last night, Savannah. And here`s where I think it`s coming down at this point for Democrats, who are largely acknowledging that, yes, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , it`s very evident, is clearly still recovering from a stroke. There were accommodations made, as Dasha talked about, the closed captioning, to be able to have him conduct this debate.\par \par \uc2 But I will say that there is a sense among people close to the lieutenant governor that voters will understand that he had a health issue, that he is recovering, that he is putting himself out there. They`re going after Oz for the line about abortion access being between a woman, a doctor and local political leaders. I would expect to see some potential attack ads on that in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 But he also talked with Democratic allies, Savannah, who questioned whether it will actually help that Fetterman was on the debate stage and questions overall on both sides of the aisle on how this is going to move the needle for Pennsylvania voters. This is such a close race. Every vote will matter in his race. It has bigger stakes nationally. And there is still a question mark of how voters will respond and react.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And on the issues, we saw some themes play out that have obviously been -- it`s kind of a microcosm of what`s happening in other races where Republicans are hitting hard on the economy and crime, Democrats turning to abortion and we saw that last night.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It was on full display, Savannah. You`re totally right. When you look at, for example, the abortion access, the answer from Oz. When you look at how Oz went after Fetterman on crime and the economy, we are seeing that not just in Pennsylvania. But you look at other debates that were happening overnight in places like New York, for example, where crime in the economy were also issues that Republicans were really trying to hit hard on Democrats.\par \par \uc2 These are issues that are going to matter to voters in the next 13 days before Election Day. You saw them on stage and they`re going to continue to play out over the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Hallie Jackson, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  says that leak of the draft opinion to overturn Roe vs. Wade endanger the life of justices. Those comments came last night during an event at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . Alito says the leak was "a great betrayal of trust," and it put targets on their backs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL ALITO\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: The leak also made those of us who were thought to be in the majority and support of overruling Roe and Casey targets for assassination because it gave people a rational reason to think they could prevent that from happening by killing one of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: In May, the Supreme Court martial launched in an investigation to identify the leaker. No findings from that probe have been made public.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221028eiaq0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc63}{\*\bkmkend toc63}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for October 26, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Dasha Burns, Andrea Mitchell, Kristen Welker, Miguel  Almaguer, Emilie Ikeda, Tom Costello, Anne Thompson, Gabe Gutierrez, Kathy  Park \par \uc2 3,976 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the fallout from a high stakes showdown in Pennsylvania with control of Congress hanging in the ballots.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz facing off in their first and only debate. Fetterman`s health struggles in the spotlight months after he suffered a stroke. Oz under scrutiny for an answer he gave on abortion. The reaction from voters just 13 days before the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  facing a new allegation of paying for an abortion of a GOP Senate candidate is responding tonight.\par \par \uc2 The verdict in the trial of a Wisconsin man who killed six people when he plowed his SUV into a Christmas parade.\par \par \uc2 The deadly school shooting in St. Louis. The warning the shooters family gave to police and they`re pleas to take a gun away from him months before the rampage.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  overseeing nuclear drills as Russia pushes unproven dirty bomb allegations against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And the American fighter killed in Ukraine two months ago finally on his way back home.\par \par \uc2 Hospitals filling up with children infected with RSV. What parents need to know.\par \par \uc2 The critical shortage in a medication millions used to treat ADHD.\par \par \uc2 And he went from homeless to shooting college hoops. The basketball star inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. Just 13 days before the Midterm Elections, and a flood of political ads are saturating the airwaves on the heels of another marquee debate reminding just what is at stake in hard-fought congressional races, power and control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 In the House, Democrats trying to defend their majority with all 435 seats of the body up for election. Dems facing the same challenge in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  with 35 seats are up for election. The balance of power in the Senate could be determined by a handful of tight races, including Pennsylvania where last night`s debate pitted Republican Mehmet Oz against Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  sparring over abortion, fracking, and immigration. But not before Fetterman call out what he described as the elephant in the room, his health.\par \par \uc2 Dasha Burns starts us off tonight with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Pennsylvania Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz back on the campaign trail following last night`s critical one and only face-off.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Pennsylvania deserves better than Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: His opponent, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , not yet making any public appearances after the fierce clash.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: It`s something called the Oz rule that if he`s on TV, he`s lying.\par \par \uc2 OZ: And this is the only debate I could get you to come to talk to me on and I had to beg.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: A debate the Fetterman`s health front and center. Fetterman whose campaign says suffers from auditory processing challenges after a stroke six months ago, requesting these closed captioning monitors in the studio.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I had a stroke. He`s never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate. Mush two words together, but it knocked me down, but I`m going to keep coming back up.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Fetterman did miss some words during the hour-long showdown, at times of struggling to answer questions, including this moment. Fetterman was asked about his changing position on fracking, which he now says he supports.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is that 2018 interview that you said, "I don`t support fracking at all." So, how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking. And I don`t --I don`t -- I support fracking and I stand -- and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Fetterman would not commit to releasing his full medical records.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: My doctor believes that I`m fit to be serving, and that`s what I believe is where I`m standing.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And in one of the biggest clashes of the night going after Dr. Oz on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: He celebrated when Roe v. Wade went down and my campaign would fight for Roe v. Wade, and if given the opportunity, to codify it into law.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And tonight, the Fetterman campaign zeroing in on this response from Oz.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want women, doctors, local political leaders letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 Local political leaders. Local political leaders.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Turning it into a campaign ad against his opponent.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oz is too extreme for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But Oz calling Fetterman the extreme candidates throughout the night, especially on the issue of crime.\par \par \uc2 OZ: We have taken away the ability of police to do their job and that son \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The momentous matchup coming just two weeks before Election Day. Voters with strong reactions about Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: His comment about political leaders, you know, determining what a woman -- a woman can and cannot do with her body is -- was very telling.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:09]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just don`t think he`s capable at this time to be in that position. I mean, if you have somebody that can`t even handle a debate that has to, you know, read from this prompter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Dasha, some big names could soon be campaigning there on both sides.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (on camera): Yes, Lester. Plans are coming together for both President Biden and former President Obama to campaign with Fetterman, and the Democratic candidate for governor. And former President Trump will campaign with Dr. Oz and the Republican gubernatorial candidate. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dasha Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Andrea Mitchell and Kristen Welker. Andrea, first to you. What are you hearing there in Pennsylvania about the impact of that debate?\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, Democrats here are telling me that they think Fetterman will be OK, given his labor support and Oz`s missteps on abortion and the minimum wage. But they acknowledge that this format did not work for Fetterman. Republicans say they`re pleased with how it went.\par \par \uc2 The fact that this race is even close, though, tells you Republicans have momentum right now and it`s happening nationwide. In the Senate, enough incumbent Democrats could lose, giving Republicans several chances to take control. And in the House Democrats are in trouble in states like Oregon and New York, which are normally safe.\par \par \uc2 Last night there was a debate for New York Governor where Republican Trump ally Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is within striking distance of Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , which is remarkable in a solid blue state. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let me turn now to Kristen. Kristen, there`s a new headline in that tight Georgia Senate race, an anonymous allegation against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . What do we know?\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, that`s right. This latest allegation comes from an anonymous woman who spoke on Zoom without showing her face in a press conference arranged by Attorney Gloria Allred, an allegation \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is strongly denying tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now, the woman claims she had an affair with Walker, the football star turned Republican Senate candidate, and got pregnant nearly 30 years ago. She says Walker pressured and paid for her to have an abortion, but she did not provide any evidence to support those claims.\par \par \uc2 Now, Walker fired back today calling the claims foolishness and a lie, saying his Democratic opponent Senator Raphael Warnock "lost the debate and Democrats will now say anything to win." The woman`s claim comes after another woman accused Walker of paying for her abortion which Walker also strongly denied. The Georgia race is a toss-up that could determine Senate control. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin this evening, the man who plowed his car through a Christmas parade guilty on all counts, bringing his chaotic trial to an end. The charges included multiple counts of intentional homicide after he killed six people. Miguel Almaguer has more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We the jury find the defendant Darrell E. Brooks guilty of first-degree intentional homicide.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The verdict was a clean sweep for prosecutors. Darrell Brooks guilty of all 76 criminal charges including intentional homicide, reckless endangering safety, and hit and run causing death.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are those your true verdicts?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: After just three hours of deliberation, the chaotic three-week trial came to an end as Brooks sat slumped over.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN OPPER, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, WAUKESHA COUNTY: We are satisfied that this defendant has been held accountable for his actions.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: It was last year at this Christmas Parade in Waukesha, Wisconsin when Brooks plowed his car through the crowd, killing six and injuring dozens. Among the victims, the elderly and children.\par \par \uc2 TYLER PUDLEINER, SURVIVOR: Were stronger than him and it`s been proven today. Just another step forward in the process.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Maintaining his innocence --\par \par \uc2 DARRELL BROOKS, DEFENDANT: I am a lot of things. A murderer is not one of them.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Brooks, who represented himself, showed a range of emotions in what became a circus-like trial, deciding to plead not guilty by reason of mental defect.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER DOROW, JUDGE, WAUKESHA COUNTY CIRCUIT COURT: Mr. Brooks, you`re interrupting me yet again.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: No, you weren`t just speaking, so --\par \par \uc2 DOROW: All right, Mr. Brooks, you are now going to be removed to the courtroom.\par \par \uc2 BROOKS: I don`t concent to that.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Brooks repeatedly interrupted the trial, at one point removing his shirt and staring down the judge who removed him from court.\par \par \uc2 DOROW: I need to take a break. This man right now is having a staredown with me. Frankly, it makes me scared.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER (on camera): In Wisconsin, conviction of first-degree intentional homicide carries a mandatory life sentence. Darrell Brooks will be formally sentenced in the coming weeks. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Miguel Almaguer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, new details emerging about the 19-year-old gunman who killed two people and injured seven others at a St. Louis high school on Monday. Emilie Ikeda has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Months before storming a St. Louis high school with a long gun and 600 rounds of ammunition, police said the accused killer`s family worked with law enforcement to have his gun removed from their home.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SACK, INTERIM COMMISSIONER, ST. LOUIS METROPOLITAN POLICE DEPARTMENT: It`s -- and it`s hard to tell when somebody is going to be violent or act out or if they`re just struggling.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: His family played an active role in getting a medical help involving therapy, medication, and committing him on several occasions according to police.\par \par \uc2 Was the family aware of the arsenal of ammunition he had?\par \par \uc2 SACK: They were aware that he had acquired a firearm.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The ATF is still tracing where he originally bought the gun and how it landed back in his possession after law enforcement had transferred it to another adult.\par \par \uc2 SACK: How he acquired it after that, you know, we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: It`s believed to be the same AR-15-style weapon used to gun down sophomore Alexandria Bell and teacher Jean Kuczka, both of whom Ashley Wrench knew well.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY WRENCH, TEACHER: And you can hear just pop, pop, pop, pop.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The longtime math teacher walking by her classroom for the first time since the shooting spree.\par \par \uc2 What do you remember from Monday?\par \par \uc2 WRENCH: Everything. The terror, the intruder trying to get in my room two, three, four times.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: That day, the school doors were locked, and seven unarmed security guards were on site. But authorities say the government managed to avoid the school`s checkpoints.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell us if he had to break glass to get into the building though?\par \par \uc2 SACK: You did have to force entry. And that`s good for us because that buys us time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: And Lester, we`re learning tonight authorities believe the gunman was targeting the school and certain individuals. But the rapid police response and a potentially not functioning gun helps prevent even more bloodshed. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And the details that keep coming out are stunning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 A show of force in Russia. President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  observing annual drills led by Russia`s strategic nuclear forces as Russia continues with unfounded claims that Ukraine intends to use a dirty bomb in the war there.\par \par \uc2 Also, today, Russian authorities returned the body of an American man killed in August while fighting for Ukraine. The remains of 24-year-old Joshua Jones were handed over to Ukrainian officials in a Russian- controlled part of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn to that big increase now in cases of RSV infections in children, with many hospitals saying they`re in crisis mode now. Tom Costello has the warning signs for parents.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): 3-year-old Izzy Bankey is today almost back to her old self, but she`s been through rough few weeks. Homesick, a two-hour wait in an urgent care clinic, a second urgent care visit, an ambulance ride to Children`s National Med Center as she struggled to breathe. Then, admitted with RSV-caused pneumonia.\par \par \uc2 KATE FOSTER-BANKEY, MOTHER OF IZZY BANKEY: Nobody wants to like, hold their kid down and hold a mask on their face. And so, she`s screaming and trying -- and trying to rip the breathing tubes out. And that breaks your heart as a parent.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: RSV is an ever-present respiratory virus but especially serious in kids with low immunity after isolating during the pandemic. Now, hospitals nationwide say they`re overwhelmed with sick kids. 74 percent of pediatric hospital beds nationwide are occupied. 89 percent in Missouri, 91 percent in Texas, 99 percent in Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 Seattle Children`s tells NBC News it`s a 200 percent capacity in the ER. At \uc2 UW Health\uc2  Kids in Madison, Wisconsin, the chief medical officer is urging parents to stay on top of the symptoms before kids have to go to the ER.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOSHUA ROSS, PEDIATRIC EMERGENCY MEDICINE PHYSICIAN, \uc2 UW HEALTH\uc2 -KIDS: Suctioning the nose, nasal passages, staying well hydrated, using anti- fever medications usually do the trick. We really want to pay attention to as kids under about 18 months of age that can develop more significant respiratory symptoms.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Seek immediate help if a child is in respiratory distress, breathing fast, nose flaring, using their shoulders or pulling under their ribs to breathe, and turning blue.\par \par \uc2 DR. RICK PLACE, PEDIATRIC MEDICAL DIRECTOR, INOVA L.J. MURPHY CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL: If you think your child is in danger, you need to go to the E.R. I mean that`s a flat-out message.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Trust your gut, say doctors.\par \par \uc2 Tom Costello, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, the nationwide shortage of Adderall, a key medicine that helps kids and adults with ADHD. What`s behind it and what families are doing to bridge the gap.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with a disorder that impacts so many Americans, including more than six million children, ADHD. For those who treat it with Adderall, a nationwide shortage has them scrambling to fill prescriptions. Anne Thompson explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Giovanni is the center of Lyon and Kandy Flood`s world.\par \par \uc2 KANDY FLOOD, SON HAS ADHD: Oh, nice.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: It turned upside down by the Adderall shortage.\par \par \uc2 When they told you the prescription wasn`t available, I mean, did your heart sink?\par \par \uc2 K. FLOOD: Absolutely. My anxiety went up and my heart sank, and I was at a loss.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: For a week, the third-grader had no Adderall manage his ADHD.\par \par \uc2 Giovanni, what was it like for you to go without your medicine?\par \par \uc2 GIOVANNI FLOOD, HAS ADHD: Uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 LYON FLOOD, SON HAD ADHD: His teacher came to us, threw her hands up and said, can you do something because he was way out of control, doing cartwheels in the class, and rolling on the floor.\par \par \uc2 K. FLOOD: Rolling on the floor, yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. LENARD ADLER, PSYCHIATRIST, NYU LANGONE: We`re fielding several phone calls a day.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Dr. Lenard Adler leads the adult ADHD program at NYU Langone Health.\par \par \uc2 ADLER: I`ve had a patient having to call about 15 pharmacies before they finally got to --\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: 15?\par \par \uc2 ADLER: 15 pharmacies before they got their prescription filled.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: This month, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  revealed the shortage blaming increased demand for the control substance and manufacturing issues at \uc2 Teva\uc2 , the largest drug maker. In a statement, \uc2 Teva\uc2  says the shortages are temporary and it expects inventory recovery in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 ADLER: If you`re at the end of your prescription, I wouldn`t suggest doing anything on your own. I would have a discussion with your physician. Sometimes that means trying a new pharmacy. It can be. If necessary, trying a new medication.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Kandy`s daily phone calls to her pharmacy paid off. They got a month`s refill, but there`s no guarantee they won`t face this again and soon.\par \par \uc2 Anne Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, the verdict in the case against three-man accused of supporting the plot to kidnap Michigan`s governor. Plus, voter intimidation and efforts to combat it in our series "POWER OF THE VOTE."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In Michigan today, three men were found guilty in state court for supporting a plot to kidnap Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  out of anger over her COVID restrictions. Two other men were convicted on federal conspiracy charges in August. Whitmer said, today`s verdicts are quote further proof that violence and threats have no place in our politics.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, less than two weeks to the midterms, Arizona election officials are stepping up security after what they call vigilantes were spotted near ballot drop boxes. Gabe Gutierrez is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Amid complaints of armed masked groups wearing tactical gear and recording voters, the sheriff in Maricopa County, Arizona has now stepped up security near ballot drop boxes.\par \par \uc2 PAUL PENZONE, SHERIFF, MARICOPA COUNTY: It troubles me that suddenly others feel compelled to act out in a way that is potentially criminal intimidation or criminal threats, trying to undermine democracy under this cloak of patriotism. And in doing so, what they`re doing is they`re destroying the most important institution in our nation, you know, the freedom and the right to vote.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: He also says his office is investigating this incident captured on cell phone video where a woman tried to take a picture of one of their license plates and was pushed. We spoke to the woman. She asked us not to use her name or show her face for fear of backlash.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You know, a lot of people are intimidated. And when people are standing around, you know, with handguns on them and sometimes tactical gear, you know, it really does scare the average person.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: At least six complaints of alleged voter intimidation here have now been referred to the State Attorney General`s Office and the Department of Justice. Across the country, election threats are ramping up ahead of the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH CHAPMAN, ACTING SECRETARY, PENNSYLVANIA COMMONWEALTH: A lot of these threats are fueled by missing disinformation.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Law enforcement Nationwide is on alert.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The Justice Department has an obligation to prevent to guarantee a free and fair vote.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Back in Arizona where early votes are already been counted, Republican election officials have called these armed drop box watchers uninformed vigilantes.\par \par \uc2 As a Republican, how frustrating is it to you that some of these groups have latched on to conspiracy theories?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN RICHER, MARICOPA COUNTY RECORDER: Well, it`s been an interesting last two years. And you know, for the party of facts and logic, at times, we have fallen short from, in my estimation, on facts and logic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Voter rights organizations have now sued to temporarily block at least some of these groups from monitoring these ballot drop-off boxes. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Gabe, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, at $700 million, it`s one of the biggest Powerball jackpots in history. Could there be a winner tonight? Plus, he was homeless before he found his place on the court. "INSPIRING AMERICA" is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight`s Powerball jackpot has grown to a massive $700 million, the fifth largest in the game`s history. No one has won since August. The odds are one in 293 million. If you bought your tickets, good luck to you.\par \par \uc2 And finally, the student-athlete beating formidable odds with a former \uc2 NBA\uc2  point guard coaching them on. Kathy Park with tonight`s "INSPIRING AMERICA."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KATHY PARK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Jeremiah Armstead picked up a basketball at 14, the same age he realized life wasn`t a game.\par \par \uc2 JEREMIAH ARMSTEAD, BASKETBALL PLAYER: I felt like I wasn`t in at 14. That`s what I feel like. Because that`s when they really picked up homeless and stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 PARK: For years, Jeremiah says he moved from shelter to shelter with his mom and two siblings.\par \par \uc2 ARMSTEAD: I was living off food stamps.\par \par \uc2 PARK: What would you say were some of the darkest times in your life?\par \par \uc2 ARMSTEAD: Sleeping at bleachers. People walking by, looking in the car. Stuff like that builds character, so I can`t be mad to stuff like that.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Homelessness pushed Jeremiah to fight harder for his goals.\par \par \uc2 ARMSTEAD: Can`t is not in my vocabulary.\par \par \uc2 PARK: He excelled as a student in California and as an athlete, a winning combination that got him into \uc2 Fisk University\uc2  in Nashville, and a spot on the basketball team.\par \par \uc2 KENNY ANDERSON, FORMER NBA PLAYER: The grit definitely sets him apart.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Former \uc2 NBA\uc2  point guard Kenny Anderson now coaches a rising star and sees a lot of himself in Jeremiah.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: My best year of my high school career, I was -- I have the way to live. We got evicted.\par \par \uc2 PARK: The \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend beat the odds and believes Jeremiah can too. Meanwhile, his cheering squad couldn`t be prouder.\par \par \uc2 MINDY BROOKS, MOTHER OF JEREMIAH: This is a child who deserves to just show what he can do.\par \par \uc2 PARK: What`s the end game for you?\par \par \uc2 ARMSTEAD: My first goal which is get my mom and my sister out to shelter that they`re in.\par \par \uc2 PARK: Family first while making moves on and off the court.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Park, NBC News, Nashville.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Good for him. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Wednesday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Goodnight, everyone.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc64}{\*\bkmkend toc64}\b \uc2 The Today Show for October 26, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Craig Melvin, Al Roker, Carson Daly,  Dasha Burns, Hallie Jackson, Emilie Ikeda, Tom Costello, Stephanie Gosk,  Vicky Nguyen \par \uc2 6,343 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:17]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. It`s Wednesday, all eyes were on Pennsylvania overnight.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. The first and only Senate debate there with control of Congress hanging in the balance. It is October 26th, this is Today.\par \par \uc2 Center Stage, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz face off on the issues.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: My campaign would fight for Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  picks everything to an extreme and those extreme positions hurt us all.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And ongoing concerns over Fetterman`s fitness to serve.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I had a stroke, he`s never let me forget that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This morning the candidates in their own words and the takeaways in one of the nation`s most important midterm contests.\par \par \uc2 Inside the Rampage, new details on the gunman behind that deadly school shooting in St. Louis, with a letter found in his car is revealing about a possible motive, and the stories of bravery now emerging.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I really credit my students a lot because even with the gunshots that close, they did not scream.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That harrowing firsthand account just ahead.\par \par \uc2 Cooling Off, housing prices falling fast after soaring to record highs. So is now the best time to buy, and what does it mean if you`re in the market to sell. We`ll break it down.\par \par \uc2 All of that plus, Chaos and Controversy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Miss USA is Texas.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The Miss USA pageant embroiled in scandal. Some contestants claiming it`s rigged. The organization`s president now suspended and an investigation underway. The very latest straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 And breaking news, what scientists are now saying about whether you really need to rake those leaves and it could make your weekend a lot more enjoyable. Today, Wednesday, October 26, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is TODAY with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning, everybody. Welcome back to TODAY. It is Wednesday morning, nice to have you along with us. This raking story, you want to get back those hours of your childhood raking leaves, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can you imagine -- you go remember all the time your parents said you got to go out and rake the leaves and stuff them into hefty bags. Who knew that we didn`t have to do that?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Science is saying something different this morning. We`ll get to that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. We got a busy morning a lot to get to including we`ve got some rare good news. It`s tied to the economy at least if you`re planning to buy a house.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. Prices are now going down at a record rate. Tom Costello is going to have everything we need to know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Of course, we will begin with last night`s big debate in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This was highly anticipated not only because of questions concerning Democrat John Fetterman`s health after his stroke six months ago, but also because this race could decide which party controls the Senate. And that will affect every single American come next year. We`ll show you how it all played out.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start this morning with NBC`s Dasha Burns in Harrisburg. Dasha, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. This debate was highly anticipated not just by voters here in Pennsylvania but people across the country. It`s an extremely tight race, just a couple of points separating these candidates in the polls. And in a battle against one of the most familiar faces on American television, many tuned in to see how Fetterman would fare after suffering a stroke earlier this year, and the stakes could not have been higher.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): In a race that could help decide the balance of power in Congress, Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz neck and neck in the polls finally face to face on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I`m running to serve Pennsylvania. He`s running to use Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 OZ: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  picks everything to an extreme and those extreme positions hurt us all.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Fetterman acknowledging what he called the elephant in the room, the stroke that nearly killed him five months ago.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I had a stroke, he`s never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate, mush two words together. I`m also fighting for any forgotten community all across Pennsylvania. They ever got knocked down that had to be -- to get back up. And I`ve made my entire career dedicating to those kinds of pursuits.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Fetterman using closed captioning to assist with auditory processing battling Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It`s about supporting and helping young earners -- excuse me, young students to give them a break.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): But experiencing some stumbles throughout the debate particularly during an exchange over fracking challenged by the moderators for past contradictory comments.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking and I don`t -- I don`t -- I support fracking and I stand and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:03]\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Fetterman says he`s fit to serve citing a letter from his doctor, but he would not commit to releasing his full medical records.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I believe if my doctor believes that I`m fit to serve, and that`s what I believe is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): The celebrity TV doctor never directly addressing Fetterman`s health but saying this during an exchange on education.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Visited vocational schools. I`ve been answered but John, because obviously, I wasn`t clear enough for you to understand this.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): One point where Oz has not been clear how he`d vote on a 15 week abortion ban introduced by Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want women doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive, to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): A clear difference from his opponent.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: My campaign would fight for Roe v. Wade, and if given the opportunity to codified into law.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voice-over): Oz also trying to court moderate undecided voters. But it was former President Trump`s endorsement that helped get him onto the Republican ticket here.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has supported you. He has endorsed you. Why won`t you fully commit to supporting him in 2024?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Oh, I do. I would support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he decided to run for president.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And, Savannah, speaking to media after the debate, the Fetterman spokesperson said the campaign was thrilled with his performance saying he won "countless exchanges against Oz." And we asked the Oz spokesperson what the Republican meant when he said that decisions about abortion should be made between "a woman or her doctor and local political leaders." The campaign responded that he is "prolife" with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother. And, Savannah, voters that we talked to overnight said that they wish they`d seen more substance and fewer attacks, Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Dasha Burns leading us off, thank you. Let`s turn to NBC`s Hallie Jackson. You watch the debate as well, Hallie. I mean, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  said the elephant in the room was his health, his recent stroke, his fitness to serve, how did it play in the room?\par \par \uc2 HALLIE JACKSON NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So I talked to some folks are in the room. I was on the phone with a campaign advisor right after the debate last night, Savannah. And here`s where I think it`s coming down at this point for Democrats, who are largely acknowledging that, yes, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , it`s very evident, is clearly still recovering from a stroke. There were accommodations made, as Dasha talked about, the closed captioning, to be able to have him conduct this debate.\par \par \uc2 But I will say that there is a sense among people close to the lieutenant governor that voters will understand that he had a health issue, that he is recovering, that he is putting himself out there. They`re going after Oz for the line about abortion access being between a woman, a doctor and local political leaders. I would expect to see some potential attack ads on that in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 But he also talked with Democratic allies, Savannah, who questioned whether it will actually help that Fetterman was on the debate stage and questions overall on both sides of the aisle on how this is going to move the needle for Pennsylvania voters. This is such a close race. Every vote will matter in his race. It has bigger stakes nationally. And there is still a question mark of how voters will respond and react.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And on the issues, we saw some themes play out that have obviously been -- it`s kind of a microcosm of what`s happening in other races where Republicans are hitting hard on the economy and crime, Democrats turning to abortion and we saw that last night.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: It was on full display, Savannah. You`re totally right. When you look at, for example, the abortion access, the answer from Oz. When you look at how Oz went after Fetterman on crime and the economy, we are seeing that not just in Pennsylvania. But you look at other debates that were happening overnight in places like New York, for example, where crime in the economy were also issues that Republicans were really trying to hit hard on Democrats.\par \par \uc2 These are issues that are going to matter to voters in the next 13 days before Election Day. You saw them on stage and they`re going to continue to play out over the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Hallie Jackson, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  says that leak of the draft opinion to overturn Roe vs. Wade endanger the life of justices. Those comments came last night during an event at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . Alito says the leak was "a great betrayal of trust," and it put targets on their backs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAMUEL ALITO\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: The leak also made those of us who were thought to be in the majority and support of overruling Roe and Casey targets for assassination because it gave people a rational reason to think they could prevent that from happening by killing one of us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: In May, the Supreme Court martial launched in an investigation to identify the leaker. No findings from that probe have been made public.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Also this morning, new details are emerging from the investigation into that deadly high school shooting in St. Louis. Police say a letter left behind is shedding light on what may have led a former student to carry out that attack. NBC`s Emilie Ikeda has more on the story joins us from St. Louis. Emilie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. Investigators say one of the most revealing pieces of evidence was found in the suspect`s car, a hand written note where the teenage gunman called himself an isolated loner.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Armed with this AR-15 style weapon and an arsenal of ammunition, police say the St. Louis school shooter`s rampage could have caused far more bloodshed had officers not arrived within minutes,\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SACK, ST, LOUIS INTERIM POLICE COMMISSIONER: But appears that he came into the building with more than 600 rounds of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): The chilling details emerging as police examined the suspect`s trail of evidence, including a handwritten notebook from the accused killer Orlando Harris`s car. Police say the 19-year-old wrote, "I`ve been an isolated loner my entire life. This was the perfect storm for a mass shooter."\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY RENCH, MATH TEACHER: You heard boom, boom, boom, boom.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Math teacher Ashley Rench recalled the sounds of gunfire right outside her classroom.\par \par \uc2 RENCH: I really credit my students a lot because even with the gunshots that close, him pulling on the door trying to get in, they did not scream. They just huddled together.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): Rench knew both of the victims. St. Louis Public Schools identifying teacher Jean Kuczka who`s being hailed a hero for reportedly standing in front of students to protect them and sophomore Alexandria Bell, dying just weeks before her 16th birthday.\par \par \uc2 KACY SHAHID, PRINCIPAL, CENTRAL VISUAL AND PERFORMING ARTS HIGH SCHOOL: They very bright student, very charismatic, beautiful spirit, a wonderful dancer.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (voice-over): This morning, a growing memorial outside the school still riddled with grim reminders of a terror the community will never forget.\par \par \uc2 In a now viral tweet, parent Michael Bishop said when he learned of an active shooter at his daughter`s school, I jumped in my car and drove 100 miles per hour down the wrong way of Kings Highway.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): In those moments you didn`t hear from her? Can you describe what that`s like?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BISHOP, PARENT: It`s heart wrenching that these kids are here trying to get educated and trying to build their future, and this is what they have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says it`s seen an uptick in threats since Monday. School shooting authorities warn even jokes will be met by serious action. This morning, law enforcement encouraging people to report concerning behavior or comments underscoring the importance of see something, say something. Savannah?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Emilie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. We have a lot more to get to, 7:12. A little bit of good news, I guess, if you`re looking to buy a house.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes. It depends on where you are along the side of the coin. Savannah, Hoda, good morning. Good morning to you as well. We`ve got some important economic news to talk about.\par \par \uc2 While Americans continue to struggle with soaring inflation in those higher interest rates, a key measure now shows that home prices are declining at a record rate. So, what does that all mean for you? NBC`s Tom Costello joins us with some answers. Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Craig, good morning. So, if you bought your home more than a year ago, chances are you`re still in the money. You`ve still made money on that home if you were to sell it, but the gains are starting to shrink at the fastest rate on record. Experts call this the real estate rebalancing. It all comes because mortgage rates have skyrocketed doubling this year alone, and that affects people on both sides of the equation, buyers and sellers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Even with inflation burning red hot keeping prices up on just about everything we buy, there`s one sector, housing, where costs are actually cooling down. While nationally home prices in August were 13% higher than last year, that`s actually down more than 2% from the month before, the largest month over month decline since the index started 35 years ago.\par \par \uc2 SKYLAR OLSEN, ZILLOW CHIEF ECONOMIST: A pause and a breath is really what the housing market is experiencing.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): The biggest price drops are in the West, San Francisco down 4.3%, Seattle down 3.9%, San Diego down 2.8. The driving factor, of course, rising mortgage rates now nearly 7% Pushing many prospective buyers out of the market.\par \par \uc2 OLSEN: Mortgage payments are now 75% higher than they were last year.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Rob Mebach (ph) is a realtor in California`s Bay Area. He says this climate is allowing buyers to be a bit more selective.\par \par \uc2 ROB MEBACH (ph), REALTOR: Before they would want to try to get into a house no matter what. But now their motivation to jump into a house that is imperfect is gone.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): But some 