y this race could help decide the balance of power in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Then tripling down. New warnings about that oncoming tripledemic, COVID, flu and RSV. What experts are saying this morning about keeping you and your loved ones safe.\par \par \uc2 Plus, all in the family. Jenna sits down with the Queen Consort Camilla`s nephew Luke Parker Bowles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LUKE PARKER BOWLES, QUEEN CONSORT CAMILLA`S NEPHEW: I have this name. And if I use it for something that helps people, then all the better.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: What brought him to the United States and what he hopes to accomplish here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, HODA & JENNA HOST: If somebody had told you when you were 18, you are going to be in New Jersey when you get older, what would you say?\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: I`d say what`s New Jersey?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Today, Wednesday, October 26, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Vernon, Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Celebrating our golden anniversary.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Friends for 35 years.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Visiting from Irvine, California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Girls trip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To the "TODAY" show.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Shout out to my daughter.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: In Merrimac, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: From Lake Charles, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m turning 70.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Aw.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: Aw.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Aw. Hello. Good morning. Welcome back to "TODAY." What a great crowd out there. A beautiful fall morning. Relax. You don`t have to rake your leaves today.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That`s what we`ve learned this morning.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, far. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Happy birthday, Lake Charles. Cool.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s get to the news, 8:00 on a Wednesday morning.\par \par \uc2 With control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  at stake, all eyes on that Pennsylvania rates last night. And the only debate, hitting Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  against Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz. And it comes amid questions concerning Fetterman`s health after his stroke six months ago. NBC`s Dasha Burns joins us with some of the key moments last night.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Dasha. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS REPORTER: Hey, Savannah. Good morning. This debate was highly anticipated, not just by voters here in Pennsylvania, but across the country. This is an extremely tight race. Just a couple of points separating these candidates. And in the battle against one of the most familiar faces on American television, many tuned in to see how Fetterman would fair after suffering a stroke earlier this year and the stakes could not have been higher.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): In a race that could help decide the balance of power in Congress, Pennsylvania Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz neck-and-neck in the polls finally face to face on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I`m running to serve Pennsylvania. He is running to use Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE NOMINEE: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  takes everything to an extreme. And those extreme positions hurt us all.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): Fetterman acknowledging what he called the elephant in the room. The stroke that nearly killed him five months ago.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I had a stroke. He`s never let me forget that. And I might miss some words during this debate, mush two words together. I am also fighting for any forgotten community all across Pennsylvania that ever got knocked down that had to be made -- to get back up and I have made my entire career dedicating to those kinds of pursuits.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): Fetterman using closed captioning to assist with auditory processing, battling Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: It`s about supporting and helping, you know, young earners, excuse me, young -- young, uh -- young, you know, students to give them a break.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): But experiencing some stumbles throughout the debate, particularly during an exchange over fracking, challenged by the moderators for past contradictory comments.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking and I don`t -- I don`t -- I support fracking and I stand -- and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): Fetterman says he is fit to serve, citing a letter from his doctor. But he would not commit to releasing his full medical records.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I believe if my doctor believes that I`m fit to serve and that`s what I believe is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): The celebrity TV doctor never directly addressing Fetterman`s health, but saying this during an exchange on education.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I visited vocational schools. I have an answer. But, John, because you -- obviously, I wasn`t clear enough for you to understand this.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): One point where Oz has not been clear, how he would vote on an abortion ban introduced by Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 OZ: I want women, doctors, local political leaders leading the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): A clear difference from his opponent.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:00]\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: My campaign would fight for Roe V. Wade. And if given the opportunity to codify it into law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS (on camera): And, Savannah, and speaking to media after the debate, the Fetterman spokesperson said the campaign was thrilled with the Democrat`s performance saying he won, "Multiple exchanges against Oz." We also asked the Oz spokesperson about that line you just heard there where Oz said that decisions about abortion should be made between, "A woman, her doctor, and local political leaders." The campaign said that Oz is, "Pro- life with exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother." Savannah.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Dasha Burns, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: With fears of a winter tripledemic growing, President Biden is urging Americans to get their flu and their COVID boosters as soon as they can. The president, who tested positive for COVID twice this summer, got his updated COVID shot yesterday. He noted that the virus is constantly changing but said that nearly all COVID deaths can now be prevented if enough people get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, three quarters of all pediatric hospital beds around the country are now full because of an outbreak of RSV among children.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Clorox is recalling eight of its Pine-Sol disinfecting cleaning products because they could contain harmful bacteria. The recall covers 37 million containers of Pine-Sol cleaners. They were made at a plant in Georgia. The CDC says the bacteria can cause blood and lung infections in people with compromised immune systems. For more information on the recall, go to our website, today.com.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The International Space Station had to do defensive driving this week to avoid a potential collision with a piece of Russian satellite debris. \uc2 NASA\uc2  says the ISS had to fire its thrusters for more than five minutes on Monday. That raised its altitude a bit and allowed it to dodge the space junk.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That maneuver did not affect normal operations. The debris was created by Russia`s test-firing of a missile last year that destroyed one of its old out of commission satellites.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Space is really getting crowded.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Really getting crowded.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Guys, we got the news covered. Now, how about a boost?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. So, a UPS driver in South Dakota is the hero of the neighborhood because he made one incredible shot after he made a delivery. So, he dropped off a package, heads back to his truck, he`s walking past a driveway basketball game. The ball rolls towards the street, picks it up, and he fires off a one-handed shot.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No way.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Oh.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my gosh. After a brief celebration, the delivery man just climbs back in the truck, off he goes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What a nice moment.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That`s impressive.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s cool.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So happy about the ring cameras sometimes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I know.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I mean, otherwise, they`d be like, no. But, seriously, I was away.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: He did it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I did it.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We got lots more ahead on a Wednesday morning, including our first look at a holiday special from the Marvel Universe. I can hear \uc2 Al Roker\uc2  screaming now. We`re going to show you the beloved star who is going to make a great cameo.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But first, Jenna`s one-on-one with an extended member of the royal family, the queen consort`s nephew who lives right here in the northeast, New Jersey. Sharing his story for the first time. And Jenna, we`re looking forward to that conversation. Right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And welcome back. Welcome back. In the wake of Queen Elizabeth`s death, the eyes on the world are on the new monarch, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  and Queen Consort Camilla.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, I learned something new today. One of their biggest supporters lives right here in the U.S., very close to us. Camilla`s nephew Luke Parker Bowles.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And Jenna met up with him to hear his intriguing story. Who knew he lived in Jersey.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, HODA & JENNA HOST: I mean, he -- you would have thought you had to go across the pond.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I just had to go in the hole and tunnel. After growing up of Parker Bowles, he`s began a new life right here in New Jersey. He is smart, charming, lovely. And now he is sharing his story for the first time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): It`s an unlikely story, an unofficial British ambassador for the royal family living among the rolling hills of picturesque Montclair, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 LUKE PARKER BOWLES, QUEEN CONSORT CAMILLA`S NEPHEW: This town is so much about film and culture.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): I met with up with Luke Parker Bowles, nephew of queen consort Camilla, while he was busy promoting the Montclair Film Festival.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): I never really thought I`d find a little slice of England in New Jersey. But tell me how you came here.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: I came to America, I met a beautiful woman who is now my wife. And I was in England making movies there. One of Hugh Grant`s films, "Love Actually" and everything, and I moved over here and started doing independent film.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): The father of three has lived in the United States for more than a decade. Lured away from the U.K. by the bright shiny lights of Hollywood and big dreams of an acting career.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: And after a while I -- it would -- it became very clear that I did not have the skill set, but I loved film more than anything. And so, I realized I wanted to be behind the camera.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): Far different from his life in London. He grew up the nephew of Camilla Parker Bowles, by her first marriage to Andrew Parker Bowles. His last name often casting a large and dramatic shadow as his aunt was in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): I read that when you were younger, you were actually sometimes bullied.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: It was a rough time. It was not a great time to be a Parker Bowles. But it hasn`t scarred me.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): His family focused on using its name for good.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: All of us, I think. My cousins, my brother, and myself, we`ve always have been involved in charity. It`s much more important to be doing charitable things, not to be holier than thou, just because it gives you a focus.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): One of the charities close to his heart is the Queen Elizabeth II Garden in Lower Manhattan which pays tribute to his countrymen lost on 9/11.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: His majesty, very kindly become our trustee and helped us get this garden going, and then her late majesty allowed us to name it after her. And it`s a place for people to go down and reflect and remember.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): Your heart is here. Your kids are here. Your wife is here. Your job is here. Part of you is still there. And by giving back here, you are keeping that part of you alive.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: I think my shrink would say there is some guilt involved, maybe, that I`m not over there. It goes back to the fact that I have this name. And if I can use it for something that helps people, then all the better.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): He says, it`s an example set from the very top. He admired Queen Elizabeth and her dedication to service, working for the British people up until the very end.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): Watching the outpouring of love --\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): -- for her majesty, Queen Elizabeth, we were in Scotland. So, to get to see little girls lining the street with hand- colored papers for her.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: I think, for the most part, she left this world feeling that everything was in its right place.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): And she was such an example --\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): -- of service.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): -- and dedication.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: I mean --\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: And flinchingly and never seeking thanks or approval. It was her job and she took it very seriously.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): Now, with the next generation leading the monarchy into a new area, Parker Bowles says he is optimistic and excited for the future.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: It`s been a big change for the U.K. of late. You know, we are in a lot of trouble with our financial situation, with our political situation.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And I think that it`s no surprise that the one ray of light, at the moment, is -- I have to get used to the queen.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: Is his majesty and my aunt, and also Kate and William.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: You know. I think that the queen, before she died was very smart about making sure that the four of them were front and center to the public and were showing that they actually are working.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): Parker Bowles now focuses on his business called Cinema Lab, restoring old movie theaters in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: And it was really spurned on by seeing my daughter and her friends and my friends` daughters and sons, all hanging out at CVS car parks, or whatever it may be.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): There was no movie theater for them to go?\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: There`s no center. We want to bring back the community.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (voiceover): That need of community and a place to come together is universal, whether it`s in London or across the pond in a small town in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER (on camera): If somebody had told you when you were 18, you are going to live in New Jersey when you get older, what would you have said?\par \par \uc2 PARKER BOWLES: I`d say, what`s New Jersey, probably? Truly. But I -- it`s the best decision I ever made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Don`t you love that, what`s New Jersey?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: It just shows you never know where life is going to take you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: But he talked about how important giving back was to his family. That his dad had three rules. One of them was to be kind. One was to not get caught, what he said, that one that we couldn`t break. And the other one was to do service. And I think that those were good rules to take back to our kids. But do not get caught.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN:\par \par \uc2 CARSON DALY, NBC NEWS HOST: Two out of three ain`t bad.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Yes, two out of three is pretty good. But he is a very charming, funny guy. And he said that Camilla is the same way, the queen consort.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: Is that same way -- has that funny --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wit.\par \par \uc2 BUSH HAGER: -- not humor which -- you know --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, I was going to say I know someone who 