r \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. We have a lot more to get to, 7:12. A little bit of good news, I guess, if you`re looking to buy a house.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes. It depends on where you are along the side of the coin. Savannah, Hoda, good morning. Good morning to you as well. We`ve got some important economic news to talk about.\par \par \uc2 While Americans continue to struggle with soaring inflation in those higher interest rates, a key measure now shows that home prices are declining at a record rate. So, what does that all mean for you? NBC`s Tom Costello joins us with some answers. Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Craig, good morning. So, if you bought your home more than a year ago, chances are you`re still in the money. You`ve still made money on that home if you were to sell it, but the gains are starting to shrink at the fastest rate on record. Experts call this the real estate rebalancing. It all comes because mortgage rates have skyrocketed doubling this year alone, and that affects people on both sides of the equation, buyers and sellers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Even with inflation burning red hot keeping prices up on just about everything we buy, there`s one sector, housing, where costs are actually cooling down. While nationally home prices in August were 13% higher than last year, that`s actually down more than 2% from the month before, the largest month over month decline since the index started 35 years ago.\par \par \uc2 SKYLAR OLSEN, ZILLOW CHIEF ECONOMIST: A pause and a breath is really what the housing market is experiencing.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): The biggest price drops are in the West, San Francisco down 4.3%, Seattle down 3.9%, San Diego down 2.8. The driving factor, of course, rising mortgage rates now nearly 7% Pushing many prospective buyers out of the market.\par \par \uc2 OLSEN: Mortgage payments are now 75% higher than they were last year.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Rob Mebach (ph) is a realtor in California`s Bay Area. He says this climate is allowing buyers to be a bit more selective.\par \par \uc2 ROB MEBACH (ph), REALTOR: Before they would want to try to get into a house no matter what. But now their motivation to jump into a house that is imperfect is gone.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): But some buyers say they simply feel locked out right now, like Eric Basil (ph) and Anna Palmer Marinko (ph) in Arlington, Massachusetts, they`ve been looking to upgrade from their two bedroom apartment.\par \par \uc2 ERIC BASIL (ph): We`re just finding less on the market and prices are still incredibly sticky.\par \par \uc2 ANNA PALMER MARINKO (ph): After being in this for a year, I feel like we are even more motivated to just find what we`re looking for.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): And many current homeowners are deciding to just stay put for now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don`t know that we could afford the house we`re living in today if we had to go buy it today.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Still if you`re looking to buy it could be the right time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You do a short term sacrifice of paying a higher rate now for maybe the next 12 to 18 months. Once the rates go down and you can refinance, your payment is going to go low and then instantaneously your equity is going to go up.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:10]\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, Tom, buyers can be a bit more selective right now. What does this mean for people who are looking to sell right now?\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Well, you know, a year ago or so the sellers would say, listen, we got a lot of competition and take it or leave it. They weren`t going to incentivize. So they were going to make repairs, they weren`t going to do any additions for example, no remodeling. Today that`s changed. Sellers are now saying, OK, we`ll make the repairs. We`ll offer you the incentives. We`ll fix the garage, whatever the case may be.\par \par \uc2 And buyers are saying, OK, well now we do want to run an inspection on this house. A year ago, they were going without. By the way, $400,000 house, 20% down, 7% interest. You`re talking about a $2,100 mortgage before taxes and insurance.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Tom Costello for us this morning. Tom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Tom does math really well. I was impressive, yes. I mean, like --\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Next level.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- a calculator, OK.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): I think he was a business correspondent once too.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): He was at CNBC that`s why he understands. We`re impressed, Tom. Mr. Roker, what you got?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: We`re always impressed with Tom. Well, we are watching a system that`s going to be a double barreled system basically. We`ve got one area of showers and thunderstorms stretching to the Great Lakes all the way down into the Appalachians. We got another system that`s going to be coming up the eastern seaboard. It`s the remnants of that.\par \par \uc2 Invest Area, we had 94-L but we`ve also got this cross country storm that`s bringing rain and snow back behind it. We`re looking at showers along this frontal system. So as the day wears on today, we`re going to be looking at wet weather stretching from the Midwest into the Northeast. And this former tropical system going to be bringing really heavy rain into New England. Some places in parts of Eastern New England could see upwards of three inches of rain or more.\par \par \uc2 We`re also looking at some mountain snows out west where they could use all of that, plenty of sunshine, cooler conditions down through Southern California and from the Great Lakes all the way down into Texas. We are looking at gorgeous weather and sunshine returns into the southeast. We`re going to get to your local forecast in the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL