B: Did your parents make you?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Absolutely. And in fact, I did -- when I was in high school, I did a whole thing. A whole research project to convince my father that we did not have to rake leaves. And that we just take the leaf catcher, that lawnmower, and mulch it (inaudible). And I`ve talked to the Queens` cooperative extension guy. He said it`s fine. I was -- oh --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You were ahead of your time. Did you rake leaves?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We raked, that was a definite chore.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I grew up in the desert, we didn`t have leaves.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Yes. But would you leave -- they`re saying you should leave all the leaves on the lawn, it`s good for it. They break down, it`s natural --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Is this a great show for people because, first, kids find out that the video games are good for them. Now they don`t have to rake leaves anymore.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Keep watching kids.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s like no school, candy for breakfast, what`s next?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Did you`re dad but it?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes, yes, he did. Because he wasn`t doing it, you know --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: -- for a while\par \par \uc2 DALY: But I love it after the leaves have been raked, and then just a few kind of scattered on the green lawn, how beautiful that looks.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, it`s cool.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I`m more likely to compost.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You`re welcome kids. You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 DALY: How long can you hold your breath for? You`re not going to believe what the stars of the Avatar sequel learn to do for the upcoming movie. We`ll have that and a whole lot more. I`m assuming some Taylor Swift news too coming up.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Jenna, she`s got a great conversation with the Queen Consort`s nephew on his famous aunt and life is built right here in New Jersey. But first your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS AND WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221026eiaq000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc66}{\*\bkmkend toc66}\b \uc2 Missed Cases Of Breast Cancer: The Detection Delayed In Dozens Of Patients And The Hospital At The Center Of The Controversy\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 987 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: There`s no question that mammograms save lives were the early detection of breast cancer, but a review of cases at one hospital showed many cancers were initially missed even though they appeared on the scans. Kristen Dahlgren has our investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Jim and Pat Ratliff had a 54-year love story.\par \par \uc2 JIM RATLIFF, WIFE HAD BREAST CANCER: We were attached at the hip.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Through ups and downs, including Pat`s breast cancer more than a decade ago, which is why she was so diligent about screening and concern when she noticed changes in her other breast. She had imaging at a nearby hospital, part of NIH Northern Arizona Health Care.\par \par \uc2 And they told her what?\par \par \uc2 RATLIFF: They couldn`t find anything.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Eventually, Pat saw breast surgeon Beth DuPree.\par \par \uc2 DR. BETH DUPREE, BREAST SURGEON: I literally couldn`t believe what I was seeing. It was screaming cancer.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Advanced breast cancer. Two years later, Pat was gone. Dr. DuPree had just moved to Sedona to develop a state-of-the-art Breast Center at \uc2 NIH\uc2 . Pat was just the first patient she says who came to her with a cancer missed on previous scan.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: These were misses that are not subtle.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The hospital has an exclusive contract with Northern Arizona Radiology. DuPree who says she continued to flag misread mammograms push them to hire dedicated breast imagers, including Mike Ulissey who couldn`t believe some of what he says was missed.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ULISSEY, BREAST IMAGER: I said my god, Beth. That`s the size of a tangerine. I don`t understand how they can call it normal.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The hospital requested the radiology group have those new breast imagers review more than 6000 patients whose scans had initially been read by a particular general radiologist. Ulissey says they found cancer in an additional 25 women.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE GALLO, HAD BREAST CANCER: The recommendation was returned to me here.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Among them Christie Gallo (PH).\par \par \uc2 GALLO: My question was, how can I go from normal to having three tumors in one year? That didn`t make sense to me.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Dr. DuPree believes the number of patients with missed cancers was much greater than the 25 detected in the review. She made a poster with all of her patients whose diagnoses she says were delayed, hoping to show it to the hospital board. We`re only showing those who gave us permission.\par \par \uc2 It must be hard to look at their faces.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: It is.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: NBC News obtained an email from one of the breast imagers to the radiology group sent before the review was complete, warning of the gravity of the situation. After the review, there was still concern.\par \par \uc2 ULISSEY: We thought that there were a significant chance that additional patients were in harm`s way. We need to expand our investigation.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But Dr. Ulissey says the radiology group did not. In his statement to NBC, \uc2 NIH\uc2  points out the radiology group is a separate entity and says in part, "The hospital quickly acted to address these concerns. We ensured these mammograms were reread specifically by a team of breast fellowship-trained radiologists, saying very few required any follow-up. And since this review, all mammograms are read by specialized breast radiologists."\par \par \uc2 The hospital`s Chief Counsel spoke on a Zoom with community advisors.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, the quality process works. There was an error. We`re humans. Doctors are human.\par \par \uc2 GALLO: They messed up and they don`t want to fess up to it.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Northern Arizona radiology called the review results well within national standards. In a statement, it says, "All radiologists are board certified and meet all requirements, including those under the Mammography Quality Standards Act. DuPree and three out of four new breast imagers including Dr. Ulissey have now left.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has learned the general radiologist who was the subject of the review is now working in another state. As for Gallo, her cancer is in remission, but she`s not sure the questions will ever go away.\par \par \uc2 GALLO: Maybe if they had caught it sooner, it could have been a just a lumpectomy instead of a whole mastectomy choice, but I didn`t get that choice.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News, Sedona, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next for us, late news about today`s earthquake in California. Plus, top brands cut ties after antisemitic comments by music and fashion mogul Kanye West.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back with the new troubles for Kanye West. Adidas ended its deal today with the music mogul and designer who now goes by Ye over his recent antisemitic comments. Hours later, Gap said it is immediately removing West`s clothing line from its stores. \uc2 Forbes\uc2  reports that without his lucrative Adidas deal, West is no longer a billionaire.\par \par \uc2 Some scary moments in Northern California today when an earthquake struck near San Jose. The quake had a preliminary magnitude of 5.1 according to the \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2 . Seismologists say it`s the biggest quake to hit the Bay Area in more than eight years, but there are no reports of injuries or significant damage.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the dramatic rescue. Tourists trapped hundreds of feet underground at a cavern in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221027eiap000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc67}{\*\bkmkend toc67}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for October 25, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Emilie Ikeda, Tom Costello, Andrea Mitchell, Kristen  Welker, Blayne Alexander, Kristen Dahlgren, Miguel Almaguer \par \uc2 3,836 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the chilling new details of the deadly shooting at a St. Louis High School.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): Investigators revealing today the 19-year-old gunman had an AR-15-style rifle and 600 rounds of ammunition. That disturbing handwritten note he left behind. What we`re learning about the victims, the stories of survival, and what police a prevented even more bloodshed.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight the spike in children hospitalized with RSV as flu season ramps up and President Biden getting his Omicron booster. His warning to Americans ahead of another potential winter COVID surge.\par \par \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner losing her appeal in a Russian court. Where does that leave negotiations for a prisoner swap? What the White House is saying?\par \par \uc2 Just two weeks until the Midterms and the high stakes showdown tonight in Pennsylvania. Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz facing off in their first and only debate with control of the Senate on the line.\par \par \uc2 Adidas dropping its deal with Kanye West after his antisemitic comments caused a firestorm.\par \par \uc2 NBC News investigates the breast cancer diagnoses missed in more than two dozen women. How did it happen?\par \par \uc2 And the mission to rescue tourists stranded in the Grand Canyon Caverns 200 feet underground.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. We will never know exactly how much higher the death toll would have been in yesterday`s school shooting in St. Louis had police not killed the attacker. But today, there is chilling new evidence the 19-year-old killer intended it to be a bloodbath.\par \par \uc2 Today, police revealing the gunman used an AR-15-style rifle to shoot his victims. A teacher and a student were both killed, four others were wounded. In addition, three people were injured during the chaos at Central Visual and Performing Arts High School. Authorities also saying the shooter, a former student, was carrying 600 rounds of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 But the most revealing evidence may be what police say they found in his car, entries in a notebook that may offer a disturbing roadmap into his state of mind. Emilie Ikeda is in St. Louis tonight with new details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, a trove of evidence shedding light on what may have driven a 19-year-old to storm his alma mater, gunning down a sophomore and teacher and injuring at least seven. Investigators discovering a letter in the suspect`s car parked outside Central Visual and Performing Arts High School.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SACK, INTERIM CHIEF, ST. LOUIS METROPOLITAN POLICE DEPARTMENT: He spoke about his desire to engage in this incident to conduct the school shooting.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: The handwritten note reads in part, "I don`t have any friends. I don`t have any family. I`ve never had a girlfriend. I`ve never had a social life. I`ve been an isolated loner my entire life. This was the perfect storm for a mass shooter." A mass shooter that claimed the lives of beloved health and physical education teacher Jean Kuczka and sophomore Alexandria Bell. The student`s father calling her death a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE BELL, FATHER OF ALEXANDRIA BELL: No matter how I felt, I can talk to her and it was -- it was alright. That was my baby.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Armed with an AR 15 style weapon and 600 rounds of ammunition, police say the suspect Orlando Harris` shooting rampage could have been even worse.\par \par \uc2 SACK: Oh, this could have been a horrific scene. It was not by the grace of God and that the officers were as close as they were.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Officers who already happened to be in the area for another event, said they arrived on scene within five minutes of the first 911 call, and made it to the government on the third floor within another eight minutes. The suspect shot dead. Police would not say how the team broke into the school or obtained a long gun plus a stockpile of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 When you got there, what did you see?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BISHOP, STUDENT PARENT: It was chaos.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Student parent Michael Bishop applauds the police`s rapid response, but knows the trauma will follow his daughter and her classmates.\par \par \uc2 BISHOP: She`s going to second guess going into any school, any building with people for who knows how long and it`s heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Emilie, there`s going to be a lot of looking back at this point. Were there any red flags in the gunman`s history?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA (on camera): Lester, police say the suspect did not have a criminal history but may have been suffering from a mental illness. Law enforcement underscoring the importance of see something, say something, encouraging people to speak up if someone makes concerning comments or is struggling. Tonight, a community still in shock as school officials say that it may be weeks before students can return for in person instruction here. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Emilie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, pediatric emergency rooms around the country say they are beyond capacity because of that surge in children with respiratory viruses including RSV. It comes as they deal with COVID and flu as well. Here`s Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:16]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In Northern Virginia, the respiratory virus known as RSV has made its way into 4-year-old Nova Louis`s lungs.\par \par \uc2 How are you feeling?\par \par \uc2 NOVA LOUIS, HAS RSV: Sick.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Struggling to breathe with an existing heart condition, her mom rushed her to the ER this morning.\par \par \uc2 SUMMER LOUIS, MOTHER OF NOVA: It`s very troubling and worrisome because everyone`s getting sick, and she just had COVID this summer for the first time.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The medical director at the Inova Children`s ER says they`re in crisis mode.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Three days of fever and cough congestion.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Overwhelmed like many hospitals with a dangerous viral tripledemic. The start of the flu season, the ongoing COVID threat, and RSV infecting large numbers of children who spent much of the pandemic isolated and virus free bubbles. Already, this children`s er has broken its yearly record for treating patients./\par \par \uc2 DR. RICK PLACE, PEDIATRIC MEDICAL DIRECTOR, INOVA L.J. MURPHY CHILDREN`S HOSPITAL: We`ll have 70 patients in the department at any one time in the evenings.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: With just 21 beds.\par \par \uc2 PLACE: With 21 beds.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: It`s not unique to this hospital. Nationwide, 73 percent of pediatric inpatient beds are full right now. Here in Virginia, 76 percent.\par \par \uc2 Many hospitals are now out of pediatric ER beds.\par \par \uc2 PLACE: Not only are there a lot of sick kids right now, there`s no place for them to go.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Meanwhile, the country is still averaging 367 COVID deaths per day, but only 8.5 percent of Americans have received updated COVID boosters to cover the latest variants.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden received his new COVID Booster while urging Americans to get both flu and COVID boosters by Halloween.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Your old vaccine or your previous COVID infection will not give you maximum protection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (on camera): Here at Inova, they`ve now moved to an emergency operation status and they`ve added this tent outside because if the patient count continues to grow at this pace, they`ll be forced to move triage outside. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Tom Costello, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A court in Moscow today rejected \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner`s appeal, meaning she will face a possible eight more years in prison for drug possession. Andrea Mitchell has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Brittney Griner today appearing via video link from behind bars, pleading for leniency against long odds.\par \par \uc2 BRITNEY GRINER, WNBA PLAYER: This has been very traumatic experience waiting for this day. I just beg that the court takes in all the states that were overlooked during the first court and reassess my sentence here.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: But the judge has quickly ruled she must serve another eight years for smuggling less than a gram of cannabis, worse than she`d feared.\par \par \uc2 MARIA BLAGOVOLINA, ATTORNEY FOR BRITTNEY GRINER: I think Brittany herself is quite -- is quite pessimist about the outcome.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Griner`s former Olympic coach after seeing her today.\par \par \uc2 DAWN STALEY, BRITTNEY GRINER`S FORMER OLYMPIC COACH: You hear the hopelessness in her voice. You know you hear her trying to be strong, probably trying to be strong for her wife, Cherelle, and her family.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: On Griner`s 32nd birthday last week, she wrote from her cell, "Thank you everyone for fighting so hard to get me home." Secretary of State Blinken now tweeting, "Today`s result is another failure of justice compounding the injustice of her detention." Her agent calling Griner, "A hostage, a political pawn." Her last hope, a prisoner swap. President Biden tonight saying they`re in constant contact with the Russians.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: So far, we have not been meeting with much positive response, but we`re not stopping.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL (on camera): The President could make the case to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  at next month`s G-20 hopefully before \uc2 Grindr\uc2  is sent to one of Russia`s notorious penal colonies. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea Mitchell tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just two weeks until the Midterms, and tonight, the only debate in that crucial Senate race in Pennsylvania, as our new poll shows Republicans gaining momentum nationwide. Kristen Welker has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, just hours before the critical one and only debate, Pennsylvania Democratic Senate Candidate John Fetterman`s campaign, with a striking attempt to lower expectations, declaring Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz has a huge built-in advantage saying, Fetterman is dealing with a lingering audio processing challenge after his stroke in May. And writing Fetterman is going to win this race, even if he doesn`t win the debate.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s debate is unprecedented with Fetterman requesting closed captioning monitors like the ones he used in an interview with NBC News. Fetterman with this explanation over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: It`s the elephant in the room having a stroke. There`s a monitor right now using captioning so I can fully, you know, understand everything in terms of from hearing.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The debate comes as Dr. Oz has been gaining in the polls.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: The debate we`re having tomorrow night is going to reinforce John Fetterman`s radical positions.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:03]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Our latest NBC News poll showing Republicans with momentum to him in their fight to take back control of Congress. 71 percent of Americans now saying the country is on the wrong track, and Republicans leading in congressional preference among likely voters, 48 percent to 47 percent. Republicans with a nine-point enthusiasm edge.\par \par \uc2 While in Florida, a fiery debate between Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Democrat \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 . Chris pressing DeSantis who is seen as a likely presidential contender.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2  (D-FL), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Will you serve a full four- year term if you`re re-elected governor of Florida? It`s not a tough question.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Well, listen, I know that Charlie is interested in talking about 2024 and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but I just want to make things very, very clear. The only worn-out old donkey I`m looking to put out to pasture is Charlie Chris.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen, you mentioned veterans challenges. Dr. Oz also faces challenges tonight.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s right, Lester. After Dr. Oz`s campaign was criticized for attacking Fetterman`s health, there will be a lot of scrutiny on his tone. Plus, he`ll have to answer those charges that he`s a celebrity candidate who`s not from Pennsylvania. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker in our Washington studio, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we look at the essential role of poll workers who help voters get their ballots they find themselves under increasing pressure and in some cases are deciding to quit. Blayne Alexander reports in our series "POWER OF THE VOTE."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Angie Jones never thought she would become a poll worker.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE JONES, POLL WORKER: I voted, you know, as most Americans do, but I`ve never really been involved in the political process.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But the 2016 election changed all of that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: After that election happened, that I needed to be part of the solution.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Her solution, training to become a poll manager in Fulton County, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 People who come to your polls and say, I don`t trust this process. What do you want them to know about the work that you do?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, first of all, come join us. Take the training. If people would understand the process more, I feel like they would be less fearful that their votes are not secure.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Angie has worked every election since 2018, but none like Georgia`s 2021 runoff that gave Democrats control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 After the 2020 election, did you notice a change?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Immediately I noticed a change. I had a lot of voters that came in angry, distrustful.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And election workers were increasingly targeted by conspiracy theories like Shaye Moss and her mother Ruby Freeman, who also worked in Fulton County.\par \par \uc2 SHAYE MOSS, FULTON COUNTY POLL WORKER: It turned my life upside down.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: According to the Brennan Center for Justice, one in five election workers say they are unlikely to serve through 2024. One in six have personally experienced threats. Angie says she will never forget asking a friend to sign up.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She said, no. She was afraid. And I remember she said to me, people will say things to me that they would never say to you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Because she`s a woman of color.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Because she`s a woman of color.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Georgia was at the center of efforts to overturn the 2020 election, something election official \uc2 Gabriel Sterling\uc2  called Dangerous.\par \par \uc2 GABRIEL STERLING\uc2 , CHIEF OPERATING OFFICER, GEORGIA SECRETARY OF STATE: It has all gone too far. All of it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Are you concerned that there could be similar threats this time around?\par \par \uc2 STERLING: I`m sure there will be. You got to be aware of it, but you can`t over-prepare for it.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Do you ever think about just walking away?\par \par \uc2 JONES: No, never. Never. My responsibility is to do everything that I can do within my power at that precinct to protect the vote.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Blayne Alexander, NBC News, Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Shining some light on important work and important people.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, missed cases of breast cancer. The detection delayed in dozens of patients and the hospital at the center of the controversy. Our investigation is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: There`s no question that mammograms save lives were the early detection of breast cancer, but a review of cases at one hospital showed many cancers were initially missed even though they appeared on the scans. Kristen Dahlgren has our investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Jim and Pat Ratliff had a 54-year love story.\par \par \uc2 JIM RATLIFF, WIFE HAD BREAST CANCER: We were attached at the hip.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Through ups and downs, including Pat`s breast cancer more than a decade ago, which is why she was so diligent about screening and concern when she noticed changes in her other breast. She had imaging at a nearby hospital, part of NIH Northern Arizona Health Care.\par \par \uc2 And they told her what?\par \par \uc2 RATLIFF: They couldn`t find anything.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Eventually, Pat saw breast surgeon Beth DuPree.\par \par \uc2 DR. BETH DUPREE, BREAST SURGEON: I literally couldn`t believe what I was seeing. It was screaming cancer.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Advanced breast cancer. Two years later, Pat was gone. Dr. DuPree had just moved to Sedona to develop a state-of-the-art Breast Center at \uc2 NIH\uc2 . Pat was just the first patient she says who came to her with a cancer missed on previous scan.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: These were misses that are not subtle.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The hospital has an exclusive contract with Northern Arizona Radiology. DuPree who says she continued to flag misread mammograms push them to hire dedicated breast imagers, including Mike Ulissey who couldn`t believe some of what he says was missed.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ULISSEY, BREAST IMAGER: I said my god, Beth. That`s the size of a tangerine. I don`t understand how they can call it normal.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: The hospital requested the radiology group have those new breast imagers review more than 6000 patients whose scans had initially been read by a particular general radiologist. Ulissey says they found cancer in an additional 25 women.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIE GALLO, HAD BREAST CANCER: The recommendation was returned to me here.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Among them Christie Gallo (PH).\par \par \uc2 GALLO: My question was, how can I go from normal to having three tumors in one year? That didn`t make sense to me.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Dr. DuPree believes the number of patients with missed cancers was much greater than the 25 detected in the review. She made a poster with all of her patients whose diagnoses she says were delayed, hoping to show it to the hospital board. We`re only showing those who gave us permission.\par \par \uc2 It must be hard to look at their faces.\par \par \uc2 DUPREE: It is.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: NBC News obtained an email from one of the breast imagers to the radiology group sent before the review was complete, warning of the gravity of the situation. After the review, there was still concern.\par \par \uc2 ULISSEY: We thought that there were a significant chance that additional patients were in harm`s way. We need to expand our investigation.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: But Dr. Ulissey says the radiology group did not. In his statement to NBC, \uc2 NIH\uc2  points out the radiology group is a separate entity and says in part, "The hospital quickly acted to address these concerns. We ensured these mammograms were reread specifically by a team of breast fellowship-trained radiologists, saying very few required any follow-up. And since this review, all mammograms are read by specialized breast radiologists."\par \par \uc2 The hospital`s Chief Counsel spoke on a Zoom with community advisors.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So, the quality process works. There was an error. We`re humans. Doctors are human.\par \par \uc2 GALLO: They messed up and they don`t want to fess up to it.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Northern Arizona radiology called the review results well within national standards. In a statement, it says, "All radiologists are board certified and meet all requirements, including those under the Mammography Quality Standards Act. DuPree and three out of four new breast imagers including Dr. Ulissey have now left.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has learned the general radiologist who was the subject of the review is now working in another state. As for Gallo, her cancer is in remission, but she`s not sure the questions will ever go away.\par \par \uc2 GALLO: Maybe if they had caught it sooner, it could have been a just a lumpectomy instead of a whole mastectomy choice, but I didn`t get that choice.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News, Sedona, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next for us, late news about today`s earthquake in California. Plus, top brands cut ties after antisemitic comments by music and fashion mogul Kanye West.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back with the new troubles for Kanye West. Adidas ended its deal today with the music mogul and designer who now goes by Ye over his recent antisemitic comments. Hours later, Gap said it is immediately removing West`s clothing line from its stores. \uc2 Forbes\uc2  reports that without his lucrative Adidas deal, West is no longer a billionaire.\par \par \uc2 Some scary moments in Northern California today when an earthquake struck near San Jose. The quake had a preliminary magnitude of 5.1 according to the \uc2 U.S. Geological Survey\uc2 . Seismologists say it`s the biggest quake to hit the Bay Area in more than eight years, but there are no reports of injuries or significant damage.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the dramatic rescue. Tourists trapped hundreds of feet underground at a cavern in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, the tourists stranded 200 feet underground and the mission to rescue them from the depths of the Grand Canyon Caverns. Here`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The four-hour rescue unfolded more than 200 feet underground. This narrow and dark elevator shaft the only way up for four tourists trap 21 stories down after the elevator malfunctioned at the base of the Grand Canyon Caverns.\par \par \uc2 FELICIA JIMENEZ, TRAPPED AT THE GRAND CANYON CAVERNS WITH HER FAMILY: That`s when we start realizing like this is real.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Suddenly stuck, Felicia Jimenez and her family needed help from the local sheriff`s department in Peach Springs Arizona who used manpower and a series of ropes and levers to winch up the stranded from the cavern floor. The steep stairway to the popular tourist attraction not an option for the four older members of Jimenez`s family with mobility issues.\par \par \uc2 JOHN PAXTON, COCONINO COUNTY SHERIFF`S OFFICE: If you can picture a fire escape on the side of a tall building, you know, like I think -- we think of New York, and you`d have to do that for 21 floors.\par \par \uc2 JIMENEZ: We`ve all agreed on this. Grateful for our family members because things could have gone way worse. When they got hoisted out, somebody could have fell.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The stranded stuck for 26 hours did have access to this single hotel suite run by the company who operates inside the cave, but not by choice.\par \par \uc2 JIMENEZ: I just feel a lot of love for my family. It makes you not want to take anything for granted.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: After a 30-minute tour became nearly a 30-hour ordeal, tonight, everyone is back above ground after a rocky start to a deep excursion. Miguel Almaguer, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Quite a drama and quite a rescue. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Tuesday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221026eiap00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc68}{\*\bkmkend toc68}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for October 23, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Kristen Welker, Ashley Parker, Cornell Belcher,  Danielle Pletka \par \uc2 9,966 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [09:00:19]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD": This Sunday, polarized and energized.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: The election is not a referendum; it`s a choice.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sixteen days until Election Day. Our new NBC News poll reveals voter interest is at an all-time high, with the potential for record turnout.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, HOUSE MINORITY LEADER & (R) CALIFORNIA: I want this election to be about ideas.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But both sides now see the opposing party as a threat to America as we know it.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: The American people are going to grab the reins back.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT BARACK OBAMA: Don`t sit this election out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Majorities of Americans are worried about the economy and believe the country is heading on the wrong track. While GOP enthusiasm is growing, voters are evenly split on which party should control Congress.\par \par \uc2 Plus, ordered to testify, the January 6th Committee officially issues a subpoena for former President Trump to testify, as his former advisor \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is sentenced to prison for defying a January 6th subpoena.\par \par \uc2 (UNKNOWN): Lock him up! Traitor!\par \par \uc2 TODD: My exclusive guest this morning, Republican congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  of Wyoming, vice chair of the January 6th Committee.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LIZ CHENEY\uc2 , VICE CHAIR OF THE U.S. HOUSE SELECT COMMITTEE ON THE JANUARY 6 ATTACK & (R) WYOMING: The solution for conservatives cannot be that we`re going to torch the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll ask her about the direction of the Republican Party, growing threats to the democracy and her own political future.\par \par \uc2 Joining me for insight and analysis are NBC News chief White House correspondent Kristen Welker; \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  senior national political correspondent Ashley Parker; Democratic pollster Cornell Belcher; and Danielle Pletka of the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press" with Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A good Sunday morning. Just 16 days until elections. Seven million Americans have already voted, and we have some brand-new NBC poll numbers to share with you that indicate interest in this election is at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 Look at this. We record 70 percent of voters saying that interest in this election`s a 9 or 10. That is higher than 18, higher than 14, higher than 10.\par \par \uc2 And what`s significant about 2018? That was the highest midterm turnout in 100 years. This indicates we`re going to be even higher than that. Overall, the battle for congressional control, it`s a dead-even 47-46. But if you go under the hood of our poll, this brand-new poll, you will see that, among likely voters, Republicans have an advantage. And among the final group of persuadable voters, Republicans have the advantage. That is why it feels as if the wind has shifted a little bit here.\par \par \uc2 We asked them, "What message would you send with your vote to Congress?"\par \par \uc2 Democrats, the number one message clearly is "Protect women`s rights." It goes along with all of those abortion rights ads we`ve been seeing among Democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 When you ask Republicans what message would they send, it`s a bit mixed, quite a few of them. One that does stand out, though, is "Fix the economy/ reduce the cost of living," on that front.\par \par \uc2 Now, presidential job approval is one of those numbers that seems to be indicative of which way the wind is blowing. So President Biden sits at 45 percent. Where does that stand contextually? It`s two points lower than Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  in 2010. It`s two points lower than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2018. Both were wave elections against the party in the White House.\par \par \uc2 Digging deeper here, we`ve got some all-time midterm highs that should serve as red flags for the Democrats. "The wrong track," 71 percent, all- time midterm high since we`ve been polling.\par \par \uc2 How about presidential disapproval on the economy? Sitting at 57 percent. Again, we hadn`t recorded that ever that high in our poll. And the third one, the direction of the economy, in the next year is it going to get better or worse? Fifty percent say it`s going to get worse. Again, an all- time high in midterm polling.\par \par \uc2 Going further here, this interest in the election. We told you, it`s high on both sides. This 69 percent number for Democrats is astonishing. It`s 78 percent among Republicans. That is an extra-astonishing number, if you will.\par \par \uc2 And the big gap here? African-American interest among Democrats, this is why you`re going to see a lot of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in the next two weeks. If the Democrats don`t get interest in the election up among African-Americans, they`re going to have some problems, particularly in some of those key Senate races.\par \par \uc2 There is one lone bright spot here for Democrats. Biden is seen as a bit more -- his agenda a bit more agreeable than Trump`s, though everybody is negative here. Do you agree with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  most of the time, 45 percent, mirrors his job rating; 50 percent disagree with his agenda most of the time.\par \par \uc2 How about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Thirty-six percent agree; 56 percent disagree most of the time. If you want to call that a bright spot, there is one there for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Here`s another one here. Most important issue facing this country, number one, for the second poll out of three, threats to democracy.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:02]\par \par \uc2 Number two and three are both economic-related, jobs and the economy and cost of living, but this issue of threats to democracy rose to number one this summer after the start of the January 6th hearings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Our institutions only hold when men and women of good faith make them hold, regardless of the political cost. We have no guarantee that these men and women will be in place next time. Any future president inclined to attempt what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did in 2020 has now learned not to install people who could stand in the way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me now is Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  of Wyoming, vice chair of the Select Committee investigating January 6.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Great to be back with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t want to presume anything, but is your number one issue threats to democracy, as a voter?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: It is. I think that, when you look at the extent to which we`re facing challenges now that threaten to unravel the fundamental institutions and structures of our election system and process, that -- that is the basis and the foundation on which we can have all of these other debates. So it absolutely is the number one issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the Wyoming ballot, we know of at least two election deniers, the woman that defeated you in the primary, and the -- a gentleman by the name of Chuck Gray, running for secretary of state. I assume they did not earn your vote?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: No, they will not. And I think no one of any party should be voting for people who are election deniers. And I think we have to be clear what it means to be an election denier. It means, in the case, for example, of Kari Lake and Mark Finchem in Arizona, they have both said, "We`ve looked at all of the facts; we`ve looked at the results of the election in 2020; we`ve looked at the law; we`ve looked at the fact that these -- the courts all ruled against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; we`ve looked at the audits and the recounts; we are willing to ignore all of that, and we are saying we would not have certified that election." They`re telling you that they`ll only certify an election they agree with. And that -- that -- there`s not much graver threat to the democracy that you can imagine than that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play for you something. You brought up Kari Lake. I want to play for you, Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin was campaigning with her. He was specifically asked by my colleague Garrett Haake about your critique of Republicans that were campaigning with Kari Lake. Here was his response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA: I believe that every state deserves a Republican governor, and Arizona deserves another Republican governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: A lot of Republicans in the last three weeks, that I would describe as empathetic to what you`ve been doing, have suddenly found themselves deciding, "Hey, party over country." What do you make of those decisions?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: You know, I think they -- they are really indefensible decisions. And, you know, I`ve said I think that Glenn Youngkin has done a good job as governor of Virginia, but nobody should be out advocating for the election of people who will not honor the sanctity of our elections process. And, you know, people who do that are in fact putting politics ahead of the Constitution and ahead of the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve noticed Dan Cox is the Republican nominee in Maryland. It`s been pretty easy for Republicans to ignore him. It seems like it`s been harder for them to ignore Kari Lake in Arizona, a battleground state, even -- you`ve seen some even campaign with \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania. It - - this -- this "Win it, whatever it takes to win, winning trumps everything," how do you -- how do you blow that up in the party?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, I think you`ve got to remind people that everybody has an obligation -- an obligation to defend the Constitution, an obligation to do what`s right.\par \par \uc2 And so right now you`ve got a lot of Republicans, in particular -- Democrats do the same thing; it just happens that our candidates are more dangerous right now. But you have got a lot of Republicans who are saying, "You know what? It just -- I`m going to ignore the threat posed by a former president who attempted to use force to stop -- to overturn an election, to stop the count of electoral votes; I`m going to ignore that; I`m going to ignore these people who embrace him; I`m going to ignore the danger and I`m just going to focus on the near term, is the Republican Party going to prevail?"\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you think more Republicans can`t -- look, I just came back from Georgia. \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , the secretary of state; \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , the governor, both beat back Trump challenges. Now, they didn`t use anti-Trump rhetoric to do it, but they won. And they look like they`re pretty -- they`re going to be in better shape. Why do you think other Republicans can`t look at that and say, "Oh, it`s good politics, not bad politics?"\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: You know, I -- my hope is that ultimately our party will come back to that. I think it may take a couple of election cycles to do that. But I think, you know, people need to understand they`re not bystanders. They`re accountable for their actions. The people who are excusing and appeasing and enabling are also responsible for the -- the impact of that.\par \par \uc2 And -- and words matter. And when you support and endorse somebody who`s said that they only will honor results if they win, then you are responsible and accountable for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, somebody who has been walking this line has been \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . On one hand, I think on Trump, you and him see eye to eye on some things, the danger of Trump and all of this. But I want to bring up an excerpt in a book that Jonathan Martin and Alex Burns wrote.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:03]\par \par \uc2 And that -- this is about McConnell. "McConnell found the whole \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  saga confusing. In his mind, she was committing a cardinal sin, relinquishing power. Why, he wondered aloud, would Cheney willingly jeopardize her leadership post by continually condemning Trump?\par \par \uc2 "Just ignore him like I do," he said.\par \par \uc2 What`s your response to McConnell? And I think you`ve probably had this conversation.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Yeah, look, I think, obviously, the idea that we could simply ignore \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the threat would go away is clearly wrong. And I think that, you know, Leader McConnell and Leader McCarthy obviously have taken slightly different approaches to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Leader McCarthy is embracing him; Leader McConnell has thought we can ignore him and -- and go forward as a party without him continuing to have power and authority. That`s clearly not the case.\par \par \uc2 And my view from the beginning has been, you know, we have to, as a party, reject insurrection; we have to reject what he stands for. I don`t think this is an issue about which you can make a political calculation. I think it matters too much.\par \par \uc2 So, clearly, you know, Leader McConnell and I do not see eye to eye on this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He had a -- he had a chance to vote to convict \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Do you think -- how much of a mistake do you think that vote -- his decision not to vote to convict was?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, I think that it was a mistake. I think it was the wrong decision. I think, you know, the fact that -- that all of the Republicans who did not vote to convict -- some did, but not enough -- you know, we were in a situation where I would have liked to have seen that trial take place immediately. I don`t think that the article of impeachment should have been held. I think there were a number of people responsible for the delay in that trial, but that was a mistake. And I think clearly this was an impeachable offense for which \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should have been convicted.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Has it made you think less of McConnell?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I -- I don`t -- he and I do not agree on this issue, and I -- I would not like to characterize it beyond that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let`s talk about a Speaker Kevin McCarthy. You clearly think this is a mistake, that he will -- you are concerned about his speakership. What specifically concerns you?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, look, the speaker is second in line to the presidency. And at every moment since, frankly, the aftermath of the election in 2020, when Minority Leader McCarthy has had the opportunity to do the right thing or do something that serves his own political purpose, he always chooses to serve his own political purpose.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, that extends to what we`ve seen just in the last few days with this comments about aid to Ukraine, the idea that somehow the party is now no longer going to support the Ukrainian people, which, you know, for somebody who has a picture of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  on the wall of his office in the Capitol, the notion that now Kevin McCarthy is going to make himself the leader of the pro-Putin wing of my party is just a stunning thing. It`s dangerous. He knows better. But the fact that he`s willing to go down the path of suggesting that America will no longer stand for freedom, I think, tells you he`s willing to sacrifice everything for his own political gain.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think at all this is just gamesmanship; he just wants the Democrats to do it in the lame duck, or do you think this is a reflection of an isolationalist streak that`s actually taking hold inside the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: We certainly have isolationists inside our party. We have isolationists inside the Democratic Party as well. But -- but leaders have to lead. And when you have the leader of the Republican Party suggesting that -- that we can play with the fire of isolationism, suggesting that somehow the American people will not support the fight for freedom, which is the front lines of freedom, right now, happening in Ukraine, the battle between Putin and Zelensky, and the notion that he would be willing to embrace that, to enable it, tells you he`s just not fit for the office.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you a little bit more about what`s going on. We have Iran now on the ground in Ukraine helping the Russians.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: At the same time, Iran sits on OPEC. Our supposed ally, Saudi Arabia, who doesn`t like Iran, is sitting here making decisions that essentially are helping the Russians, helping the Iranians, hurting the West. What kind of -- what should our foreign policy be to Saudi Arabia right now, consider the current circumstances?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, look, I think, first of all, we ought to absolutely and clearly walk away from the negotiating table with the Iranians. The notion that the Iranians have -- are now providing these drones to the Russians to use in Ukraine, if nothing else, has convinced us yet to walk away from the table, that should.\par \par \uc2 It also tells us the Russians are having a hard time replenishing their supplies. And so the sanctions are working. We ought to do more. We ought to do more with respect to sanctions against Iran. But across the board, you know, I think that there -- there are too many people around the world who no longer think that they can count on the United States; they no longer think that they can trust us, that we`ll stand with our friends and that we will ensure that we`re standing against our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I hear you. The Middle East, though, is really -- it looks problematic from a U.S. -- when some of our closest allies are, kind of, neutral in this war, right?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Israel has been, sort of, neutral, kind of, helping late, obviously Saudi Arabia. How much of a problem do you view this?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, it`s a big problem, and I think it`s a problem of American leadership. We need to be doing more, faster, in terms of what we`re providing to the Ukrainians. We need to be very clear -- you know, when Kevin McCarthy suggests that the Republicans are not going to support aid to Ukraine, that is incredibly damaging to America`s standing in the world. It`s damaging to the effort that the Ukrainians are engaged in. We need to demonstrate that the United States of America recognizes we have to be a leader in the world. We`re not going to go back to the days of isolationism, which has been a threat in this country, you know, ever since the end of World War II.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before we go -- on the other side of the break, we`re going to go deep into January 6th and your investigation. But I`m curious. For four years you publicly were supportive of the Trump administration. You were never a big Trump fan. That was pretty clear. And you would disagree with him on things when you thought it was time. But you voted with him 93 percent of the time. Do you look back and think of any moment, and think, "Boy, maybe I should have spoke out sooner, maybe I should have confronted him sooner?"\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: At the time, I was confronting him on the policies when I disagreed with him. They tended to be mostly national security policies.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I`m a conservative. As you said, I voted with him 93 percent of the time. But I think that there`s no question that his election as president in 2016 began something that`s been very dangerous for this nation. And we now see he`s willing to do everything he needs to do, everything within his power, to stop a peaceful transition of power. And he can never be president again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let me pause our conversation here. When we come back, we`re going to go deeper into the January 6th Committee, which officially issued a subpoena of the former president to testify and hand over documents. Why did they wait until now?\par \par \uc2 We`ll have more with the congresswoman, after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. On Friday \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was officially subpoenaed by the January 6th Committee, ordered to turn over documents related to the insurrection by November 4th. And he`s ordered to appear before the panel by November 14th.\par \par \uc2 All this came just hours after \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , the president`s former adviser, was sentenced to four months in prison for contempt of Congress, for disobeying a similar subpoena. The sentence was stayed, pending appeal.\par \par \uc2 The congresswoman is back.\par \par \uc2 So let me start with the subpoena. I`ve got to start with \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  first. You both successfully showed that your subpoenas were enforceable. He`s going to serve time. But he`s still not going to turn over documents, is he?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, I think \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  is one of a number of individuals who clearly have something to hide. You know, he actually, as you`ve seen, was convicted of contempt and has been sentenced to -- to prison. Others have come in front of the committee and taken the fifth. And I think it leads the American people to ask, "What is it all of these people, including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , are attempting to hide about January 6?"\par \par \uc2 [09:20:07]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`ve issued the subpoena. And I -- I assume you -- you`re going to let it play out. So what happens on November 5th if you don`t have any materials?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: So we are anticipating that the former president will understand his legal obligation, will comply with the subpoena. We`ve made clear in the subpoena a number of things, including that, if he intends to take the fifth, that he ought to alert us of that ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 We also -- I would also encourage everybody to go to our committee`s website and -- and read the letter that accompanies the subpoena. Because understanding what a grave and serious situation this is, the committee took great -- made a great effort to lay out in the letter itself the specific information we`ve already gathered about Donald Trump`s personal and direct role in managing and overseeing and coordinating this sophisticated, multi-part plan to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is the -- is the committee open to his supposed offer, or at least behind the scenes offer, of going on live television?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: So the committee treats this matter with great seriousness. And we are going to proceed, in terms of the questioning of the former president under oath. It make take multiple days. And it will be done with a level of rigor and discipline and seriousness that it deserves.\par \par \uc2 We are not going to allow the former president...\par \par \uc2 TODD: A television spectacle, it`s not going to be?\par \par \uc2 He`s not going to turn this into a circus. This isn`t going to be, you know, his first debate against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the circus and the food fight that that became. This -- this is a far too serious set of issues. And we`ve made clear exactly what his obligations are, and we are proceeding with -- with that set out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you don`t get cooperation from him, do you have time for this legal fight?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: We have many, many alternatives that we will consider if the former president decides that he is not going to comply with his legal obligation, the legal obligation every American citizen has to comply with a subpoena.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There was some news this week from a judge having to do with John Eastman`s e-mail and what e-mails need to be turned over. And there seems to be one extraordinarily damning piece of evidence. You know, some would argue that you guys have made a circumstantial case about the president`s culpability here. But this may be something where he signed -- knowingly signed something that was false. How important is that e-mail and that piece of information?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, I would say first that the -- what we have laid out is not circumstantial. What we`ve laid out in a number of instances includes, you know, the extent to which the president himself, people who were in the room with the president at the time, his own most senior officials testifying to the committee about his personal and direct involvement.\par \par \uc2 We know, for example, from -- from his own actions and his own inactions what he did and failed to do while the attack was under way. It appears from Judge Carters`s latest decision that the president himself signed information that was false, that he knew to be false and submitted that to a federal court.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s one more piece that we`ve seen of a president who fundamentally does not believe that he has an obligation to abide by the law or to abide by the rulings of the courts, and demonstrates again how serious this is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m not asking you to influence \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . I`m not asking you to influence any U.S. Attorney. What crime do you believe he committed?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Look, I think that there are multiple criminal offenses. The committee -- I don`t want to get in front of the committee, but that we are looking at. And I think it`s very important for everybody to recognize that, when you are faced with a set of facts, when you are faced with evidence as clear as this is -- and some have said, "Well, you know, we don`t know what his intent was; maybe he really thought he won the election."\par \par \uc2 We actually know that that`s not the case. We`ve put on testimony that showed that he admitted that he lost. But even if -- even if he thought that he had won, you may not send an armed mob to the Capitol. You may not sit for 187 minutes and refuse to stop the attack while it`s under way. You may not send out a tweet that incites further violence.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve been very clear about a number of different criminal offenses that are likely at issue here. If -- if the Department of Justice determines that they have the evidence that we believe is there and they make a decision not to prosecute, I think that really calls into question whether or not we`re a nation of laws.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I am -- you know, they`re -- they`ve got a lot of issues that they`re dealing with that may be criminal, when it comes to the former president. If they make the decision to charge him on the Mar-a-Lago classified document situation, because perhaps it`s an easier case to prosecute, and they don`t choose to go down this road, do you find that to be a mistake?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I -- I have confidence in the professionals at the Department of Justice. I have confidence in the attorney general, that they are taking very seriously their obligations with respect to every aspect of the potential criminal conduct by the former president.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:05]\par \par \uc2 And let`s just have the American people pause on that, that we are talking about multiple instances of -- of criminal conduct by the former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s separate criminal investigations right now in the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious about something in history that I think may have both mistakenly guided how people of our generation view history, and that was Gerald Ford`s decision to pardon \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If he doesn`t pardon \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  and \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  is put -- had faced criminal prosecution, do you think the country would be more open to dealing with Trump this way?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I think that it was the right thing for President \uc2 Ford\uc2  to do. I do not think that that would be the right thing for President Biden to do. Some have suggested that. President \uc2 Ford\uc2  pardoned President Nixon after President Nixon had resigned from office, had left office. I think that it was the right decision to make, in terms of the national healing there. President Trump continues to this day to glorify those who attacked the Capitol on January 6. He continue to this day to say things that he knows caused violence. And so I think that the two things are very different.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 , though, never admitted culpability, although \uc2 Gerald Ford\uc2  insisted to death that he doesn`t issue that pardon if he -- that the pardon was not...\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, he resigned from office.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I mean, but look, we`ve never in our history had a president do what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did and -- and frankly continues to do. And -- and we have to take that very seriously.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You, among politicians, have you had a unique experience. There was a time you were extraordinarily demonized by the left. There`s been a time...\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I don`t remember that time, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Really?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Then you`ve been extraordinarily demonized by the right.\par \par \uc2 What has that -- what has that experience been like for you?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Look, I think that we have to deal with, sort of, the facts that are in front of us. And my view is the job that I have to do right now and that I have had to do, especially since January 6th, is so important that that really is my focus. I think that we have seen, across the country; we`ve certainly seen on our committee, a coming together of people despite partisan differences and policy differences, to say, look, the most important issue is the defense of the republic. And we have to do that on a non-partisan basis.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been extremely disappointed and very sad by the response to the vast majority of my colleagues on the Republican side to this. I really believed, growing up in the family that I grew up in, that, you know, when the chips were down, people would do the right thing. And it turns out that not very many people do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You have said you`re going to do whatever it takes to stop \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from getting back to that Oval Office. Define "whatever it takes."\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Look, there are very few certainties in politics. But one thing that you can absolutely count on is that there are tens of millions of Americans who will do everything we need to do to make sure \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is never the president again. The threat he poses is too great. He`s demonstrated his willingness to use force to attempt to stop the peaceful transition of power. And there are simply many, many millions more Americans who, despite any party affiliation, understand how dangerous that it and will make sure he`s never in the Oval Office again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you bring this country together, though? Because, as I said, you`ve both had a unique experience in seeing what partisan hatred, partisan anger directed at you individually, and we see this divide; you see it in our own polling. And it seems very difficult. How would you try to bridge this divide to a national audience?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: You know, I -- I experience a unity every -- every day, as I travel around the country, as I work with colleagues on Capitol Hill. There are responsible and rational and sane people in both parties who want what`s best for our country and who want to election politicians who are going to do the right thing, who want to see elected officials engage on the basis of substance and policy differences, not -- not minimize those differences but really engage on the basis of that.\par \par \uc2 And so I -- I`ve been very heartened by the unity that I`ve seen, so many people who understand we have to say "Stop, we cannot go over this abyss, and we have to come together to solve the great challenges our nation faces."\par \par \uc2 TODD: If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  ends up the nominee in 2024, you`ve said you`re not going to be a Republican anymore. So it implies you think the Republican Party can be saved?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: I think that the party has either got to come back from where we are right now, which is a very dangerous and toxic place, or the party will splinter and there will be a new conservative party that rises. And if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the nominee of the Republican Party, the party will shatter, and there will be a conservative party that rises in its place.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was with a voter group this week who both were exhausted by the January 6th Committee and wanted to see you run for president. What would it take to get you to run for president?\par \par \uc2 [09:30:05]\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Look, I`m -- I am going to be very focused on all of the things that we`ve been talking about. And I care deeply, as I know you do, as millions of people do, about this nation, and about the blessing that we have as a constitutional republic. And so, you know, whether that means helping other candidates, whether it means helping to educate people around the country. I have been on a number of college campuses and am very inspired by those young people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: But I`m focused on what we`ve got to do to save the country from this very dangerous moment we`re in, you know, not right now on whether I`m going to be a candidate or not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There are some people who suggest if you were a third party candidate, it would be enough to stop Trump.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, we will do whatever it takes, as I said. He will not be the President of the United States again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before I let you go, this investigation has an expiration date if Republicans take control of Congress. There`s no report out yet before the election. I know you on one hand want to take politics out of it, but shouldn`t this be on the ballot? Shouldn`t the decision whether this investigation continues be on the ballot in November?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Look, I think it`s very important to take politics out of it. The committee is investigating and proceeding in a way that is not partisan at all. I think that, as people go in to vote, they need to recognize that there are certain candidates who are anti-democracy. They need to recognize that election deniers are anti-democracy, and they should not vote for those people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Where does this investigation go on January 4th?\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, you know, if we were in a nation where our politics were operating the way they should, the investigation would proceed no matter what. I think that the Republicans have made very clear that they`re not interested in getting to the bottom of what happened or holding people to account. And I think that also ought to be something that Americans across the country are paying attention to. Why would you not want to understand how this happened? TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Believe it or not, I still have questions for you. But I am out of time.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Well, thank you for all the time. I appreciate it. Thanks for having me, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, and be safe. Stay safe on the trail as you go out there.\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Before we go to break, this week on "Meet the Press Reports", my colleague Yamiche Alcindor took a deep dive into the growing threat to American democracy and what can be done to secure the vote. Al Schmidt served on the Philadelphia County Board of Elections as a Republican in 2020.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Schmidt was targeted for not echoing the MAGA conspiracy theory that the election was being stolen, and for not questioning the integrity of the vote count.\par \par \uc2 AL SCHMIDT, PHILADELPHIA COUNTY BOARD OF ELECTIONS: Well, I was a Republican before \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a Republican. It`s important that we, we stand up against these lies and that we not surrender the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can watch the full episode of "Meet the Press Reports: Securing the Vote". It`s available on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , Roku, AppleTV, wherever you get NBC News.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, the midterm momentum shifted. Our panel is here to discuss whether it`ll shift again.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:35:46]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back with the panel.\par \par \uc2 NBC News Chief White House Correspondent Kristen Welker, also co-anchor of "Weekend TODAY"; Ashley Parker is the senior national political correspondent for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 "; Cornell Belcher is a Democratic pollster; and Danielle Pletka is the senior fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right. Let`s talk midterms.\par \par \uc2 I want to start, Kristen, with the person you cover every day, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in his own way acknowledging the shift in momentum. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think that we`re going to see one more shift back to our side in the closing days. And let me tell you why I think that. We`re starting to see some of the good news on the economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ah. There it is. They`re looking for this good news on the economy again, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They seem to acknowledge that things turned against them in the last couple weeks.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: They have acknowledged that. And a lot of Democrats think that the president is being overly optimistic there, by the way.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things that are notable. What is he trying to do to shift things back in the other direction? He`s trying to put the spotlight on Roe in this very fascinating way of almost trying to turn back the clock and say, remember the outrage that you felt on the day that Roe was overturned?\par \par \uc2 Because what`s happened is there`s a sense that that outrage is now baked in the cake, right, that these polls that we`re seeing get much closer, and with voters largely focused on issues like threats to the democracy and the economy, that there`s not a whole lot more wiggle room there, and also putting the focus on the economy. He had that announcement this week, that he was going to release 15-million barrels from the strategic petroleum reserve.\par \par \uc2 That, by the way, capped an announcement that he had already made. So I think the impact on gas prices is going to be pretty minimal. It allows him to show, though, that he`s working on this problem.\par \par \uc2 And I think to send a message to Democrats more broadly, we can`t just be talking about abortion. His message to them is, we need to talk about the economy, and also, Chuck, crime which has been a big issue in some of these very close races, particularly Pennsylvania Senate race.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ashley, I`ve got to put up another quote here from \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  from the beginning of this week in an interview with Punchbowl News.\par \par \uc2 We`ll have to message it better in the next three weeks ahead. I think we`re in great shape. Other people don`t want to believe that.\par \par \uc2 She was talking about the economy. What are you -- what are you messaging better in three weeks?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY PARKER, NBC NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, this has been the challenge that has been bedeviling the administration since last fall when they started experiencing inflation, right? We reported today that there was a big debate in the administration about how they should talk about it.\par \par \uc2 And some people argued to sort of try to not address inflation, at least early on, head-on. You may recall when they kept on calling it transitory. Of course, it was not transitory. And they wanted to focus on the good economic indicators, like the low unemployment rate. And there are those.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, what voters feel, the number that matters most when it comes to the economy, and this is why messaging is a challenge, is inflation, it`s that 8.3 percent number from September. Because regardless of all of these other positive indicators, and they do exist, when you drive to work or you drive your kid to a soccer game and you pass gas stations, each sign is a billboard of how much gas costs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: When you go to the grocery store, milk and eggs. So, yes, they absolutely do need to message it better.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: But it`s not something they`ve been able to figure out this entire year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, the other thing that happened this week, Dani, is that it just feels like, Oh. The midterms look like traditional midterms again. There`s always the but-for. In this case, it`s but for Trump, and but for abortion rights. But everything else looks like a traditional midterm.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE PLETKA, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I mean, I think we`re going to see, aren`t we, soon enough, thank God. I cannot wait.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t want anymore ads? No?\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re tired of the ads?\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: No. I don`t want anymore, or email, or anything else.\par \par \uc2 No. Look. I mean, I think all elections are traditional elections in the sense that, you know, most of the base votes for the guy they were going to vote for in the first place. It`s always the question of the swing.\par \par \uc2 I do think that what`s different in this instance is that we do have these overhang issues. We have the question of the president and his unpopularity. We have the question of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his insanity.\par \par \uc2 And we have this unbelievable, as Ashley said, overweening inflation that a lot of us haven`t experienced in, for me at least, 40 years, for some never in their life. I just went to Giant, our supermarket, and I couldn`t believe how fast I got to $100 bucks.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was nothing I bought. People feel that before they feel outrage about abortion, before they feel outrage about democracy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh. I think the housing crash is actually having a huge impact in the last two weeks, the fact that mortgage rates surged to 6 percent.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:03]\par \par \uc2 Cornell, I guess the big question is: If this is a more competitive election, it`s going to mean the Democratic Coalition turned out, what are you concerned about?\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Look. I think broadly a lot of you are wrong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But --\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Especially you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You may be -- we`re going to find out in 16 days or 25,000 years, however long it takes to count the ballots.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Look. You know, momentum shifts back and forth. I don`t know if we`ve got as much momentum shift, or we are actually shifting momentum in our narrative. Look. Look. What was the NBC`s News generic Democratic support in October of 2018? It was 48 percent. What is it right now? It`s 47 percent. Shift? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Right now, and I think -- and the NBC polls show it, we have an enthusiasm like we`ve never seen before. And what we already know in some of these battleground states, we have more people early voting than we saw in 2020. This is not going to be your regular midterm election by any chance.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But, Cornell, the thing about our poll that really struck me is how familiar it looked. It looked like the Virginia exit poll when you looked at enthusiasm: rural versus the suburban. Suburban was good. Rural was through the roof.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yeah. But you need more people, right? You know where we`ve been losing population since 2010? Rural voters.\par \par \uc2 Rural voters surged in 2018 and 2020 also. As Jay-Z would say, you need more people. And, look -- underneath -- and look, I think a lot of the polling numbers, the top lines may not be accurate because it`s all about the universal. And we don`t know what the universal voters are going to be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well speaking of the universal voters, and the fact -- and the one thing I pointed out was the fact that African Americans were not at the same level of interest in this election as other parts of the Democratic Coalition. The Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  campaign schedule is about to get busy, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It is about to get very busy. He is going to be in Georgia. He`s raising money. Milwaukee, Las Vegas, as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m still waiting. Where is Philadelphia on here?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, good question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I assume he is going to Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Good question. Well, we`ll have to see. But that race, for obvious reasons, they`re being very careful about who they invite.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s worth noting we`re seeing a lot of former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  on the campaign trail, not as much of the current president. And he`s really sort of breaking with the past, Chuck, because he`s holding these sort of issues-focused events where he can talk about what he thinks are his successes, but not those traditional big rallies. I`m told that`s going to change in the final week. But it`s notable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m old enough to remember when a sitting Democratic President \uc2 Obama\uc2  needed to look to a former Democratic president named \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  to help.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The Explainer in Chief. The Explainer in Chief. That`s right --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s funny how the former presidents all seem to look better in hindsight except for one current former president these days.\par \par \uc2 Anyways, up next: It`s the one issue both parties -- both parties do agree on. But it makes any hope of coming together nearly impossible.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:45:39]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 A little bit more from our important NBC News poll. When you look at the most important issues of this election, a picture emerges of just two sets of voters and two Americas. And there`s really only one issue both parties agree on, and it may be the most polarizing of them all.\par \par \uc2 Let me just show you, most important issue by party.\par \par \uc2 Among Democrats, the number one issue is threats to democracy, abortion rights comes in second, cost of living third, climate change fourth and if you look at television advertising, really abortion rights is what you see more than threats to democracy.\par \par \uc2 Among Republicans, the top three is jobs and the economy as one, immigration and the border, which you actually see in advertising more, is number two. Cost of living is really the only issue that is in the top four among both Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the real issue that both parties actually agree on. Will the other party`s agenda destroy America? Eighty-one percent of Democrats believe this about Republicans. Seventy-nine percent of Republicans believe this about Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now ask yourself, how`s anybody going to bring this country together when right now this is what the two parties think of each other.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, why political chaos is not a uniquely American trait these days.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LIZ TRUSS, BRITISH PRIME MINISTER: I am a fighter and not a quitter.\par \par \uc2 I am resigning as leader of the Conservative Party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:50:45]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back.\par \par \uc2 So Brexit and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Dani, it all came about the West at the same time. I think we have to thank our founders, because our system of government has prevented the chaos that the U.K. has gone through.\par \par \uc2 But what are the similarities between the U.K. and here? You were talking about it`s conservative politics.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: Right. Well, I think much more than a crisis of democracy in the U.K., what we see a crisis of conservatism. This is the Tory Party actually having no idea what they stand for.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sounds familiar, actually.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: Well, yes. And it`s not just here. It`s here. It`s Australia. It`s all over Europe. And, of course, it`s in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, we`re just seeing it all out in the open. What did Byrd used to say? You know, watching democracy is like watching sausage being made? Right. And it`s a particularly gross sausage in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 So we don`t even know who`s going to be prime minister at the end of this week. And God knows, we don`t know who`s going to be prime minister next year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Can I quick point here?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: From what I know, hanging out in the Kwant Bar in London. But what I actually think is durable, I actually look at their democracy and I go, there`s a durable democracy. You know why? Because you can`t imagine Republicans forcing Trump or forcing the leader out here in America. You cannot imagine that. Why I think they`re more durable --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s actually a good point.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: - is that Tories forced their leader out. We can`t imagine that happening in our country.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: Well, except it`s not a freaking Pez dispenser. I mean, forcing them out every week is perhaps--\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sausage and Pez dispensers.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: I know. I`m an analogy woman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I love it. I love it.\par \par \uc2 I want to pivot back to domestic politics. Kristen you had, believe it or not, it was less than a week ago that you interviewed \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I had to remind myself. I know --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. Must feel like months ago.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It seemed a lot longer ago. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We asked a question about sort of moral failures of if you -- if there`s somebody -- would that drive you away from supporting that candidate if your party`s candidate has a major moral failure? Sixty-three percent of Democrats and 67 percent of Republicans said they`d still vote for that candidate.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-two percent of Democrats and 16 percent of Republicans said they would probably skip the race or vote third party. And just 4 percent of Democrats and 6 percent of Republicans would actually vote the other party. It is a small slice. Basically, jersey color still matters more than moral fiber.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Absolutely. And that was very clear being on the ground in Georgia. And have to stress the fact that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  denies all of these allegations that he paid for an ex-girlfriend to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 However, it was notable, Chuck, because not only did he take a page from the Trump playbook, very defiant in the face of these allegations, I think in this final stretch, he almost feels emboldened in part because of numbers like that, in part because I think he had better-than-expected showing at the debate.\par \par \uc2 And if you talk to voters on the ground in Georgia, I think because of the name recognition, because of who \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is, some of that is baked in. But I think it`s notable, your point about the fact this cuts to Democrats, too. This is not just Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Actually, at least they were honest. Because we`ve seen it, right? \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  got a pass from Democrats. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  basically got a pass from Republicans. So why wouldn`t \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  get a pass, right?\par \par \uc2 PARKER: Well, what was so striking is right after those allegations broke I was talking to Republicans in Georgia. And one Republican operative said to me -- they would not put this on the record -- but they basically said, I don`t care if \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  performed the abortion himself. I`m still voting for him. Which, again, was quite honest. But that sort of is the reality.\par \par \uc2 That said, when you look at the \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  campaign and some of the ads they are running, they are sort of betting that they can give some Republicans, some independents a permission structure to say, I find this so abhorrent that I`m going to vote for the Democrat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look. We`re going to stay in Georgia. I`m going to play -- speaking of different ways that Mr. Warnock is trying to appeal to independents, take a look at this ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AD ANNOUNCER: Things work surprisingly well together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Pizza with pineapples.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED BOY: French fry and frosting.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Ted Cruz?\par \par \uc2 AD ANNOUNCER: That`s right. \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  partnered with Republican Ted Cruz to extend I-14, connecting military communities in Texas and Georgia, which will help create jobs from Columbus to Macon to Augusta.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [09:55:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Cornell, in our polarized environment sometimes I wonder: Is the benefits of that, does it -- could it hurt you with any base voters who don`t want to see you working with the other side at all?\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: No, I think -- that`s a really good ad. There`s two points here.\par \par \uc2 One: The broad swath of Middle American voters, what we hear all the time - - you`ve heard them say it -- I want to see them together. Please work together and accomplish something real.\par \par \uc2 He`s showing that he`s worked together and actually accomplished something real. So he`s above the partisan fray. The other quick point about this is that, you know, if you`re talking about six or seven points of Republicans who won`t vote for a morally problematic candidate, you`re talking about the difference in most of these elections.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a fair point.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right? And, listen, last time -\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s not a lot of people, but that matters in our polarized environment.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Right. And last time a Democrat won Alabama, what happened? Right? So.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay.\par \par \uc2 PLETKA: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? I have absolutely no idea. I mean -- no. I mean, you were telling me before.\par \par \uc2 It`s impossible to know. There`s no crystal ball. I do think \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has a good chance, and I don`t think anybody cares about morality anymore. It`s -- perhaps is worthy of much more serious than a 30-second conversation.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that we don`t care -- and I know we do -- but the fact that we as a nation don`t care about this anymore by two-thirds majority says something not simply about our candidates, not simply about our system, but about our democracy as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and this is the question of I don`t know how we truly are brought together, because if the two parties think the other side is a threat to the nation, you know -- the whole point of politics is to avoid violence. Sometimes you wonder how we`re going to do it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Right. Well, and that`s what makes the Warnock ad so notable, this attempt to almost bend over backwards to look like he can reach across the aisle. He was asked at the debate if he would support President Biden if he runs again in 2024. He wouldn`t answer that question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That felt slippery. That felt like a politician.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well. And, again, being on the ground in Georgia, Chuck, I can tell you the voters didn`t like that answer because they didn`t buy it. And so I think there`s --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They didn`t believe it. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: -- an authenticity factor as well that voters are very tuned into.\par \par \uc2 PARKER: And there`s also a somewhat cynical calculation. And, again, it`s not just one party, but you look at, for instance, with former President Trump. You had Republicans, you had evangelicals sort of hold their nose and vote for him and say, I like what -- I don`t like how he`s lived his life, but I like what he`s going to do for the courts. And he ended up appointing three justices. That is how Roe got overturned, and that`s a calculation when we get to the morality that a lot of voters in both parties are willing to make.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But isn`t this bringing about basically a worse type of candidate? It now opens the door for them to go, why not? I can run. We`re going to populate our elected offices with a lot of morally compromised officials.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yes. And we`re seeing it. It`s happening all over the country. One of the things I do want to go back to is level set for a moment around Biden. Because I worked for a guy who a lot of people in 2010 didn`t want him either, right? And you go back to 2006, a lot of folks didn`t want George Bush either on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 And I think you`re going to have 10 million jobs. You`re going to have inflation going in the right place. You`re going to have more manufacturing jobs. I think his arc -- he`s going to have something to run on well in 2024.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if it`s a Republican Congress, maybe something to run against.\par \par \uc2 BELCHER: Yeah, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we`ve seen that before as well.\par \par \uc2 All right. Before we go, we`re pretty excited to tell you about the 2022 "Meet the Press" Film Festival. It`s at DOC NYC. That`s right. We`re going to New York, baby.\par \par \uc2 On November 15th, we`re showcasing the best-in-class short documentaries of the year in New York City. So if we can make it there, this film festival makes it everywhere.\par \par \uc2 Tickets are on sale now. Scan the QR code right there. You can find more at NBCNews.com/MTPFilm.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching. Come on, Packers. Beat the \uc2 Washington Football Team\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week, because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020221023eian00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc69}{\*\bkmkend toc69}\b \uc2 Today for October 20, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Cal Perry, Al Roker, Tom Costello,  Kristen Welker, Natalie Azar, Craig Melvin, Kelly Cobiella, Vicky Nguyen \par \uc2 7,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:16]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good Thursday morning from Washington and New York. Our top story, all eyes once again on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Tension there building after new attacks and new threats. It is October 20th. This is Today.\par \par \uc2 Leave immediately, this morning, thousands of residents in southern Ukraine being forced to flee their own homes. Mass evacuations underway after a warning of imminent attacks and martial law declared by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And this morning, mounting concern over power outages ahead of a harsh winter, we`ll have the very latest and a live report straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 On the road, President Biden hits the campaign trail today in the closely watched battleground of Pennsylvania. Can he move the needle for the undecided voters with less than three weeks to go?\par \par \uc2 Feeling the freeze, another day of bitter cold across much of the country, parts of the south waking up two record lows, but Al says, hang in there, a big warm ups on the way. They`ll have your full forecast.\par \par \uc2 Consumer alert, the new warning from police after a string of robberies in cities across the country tied to popular online marketplaces.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CMDR. RODNEY G. HILL, CHICAGO POLICE DEPARTMENT ENGLEWOOD DISTRICT: Nine times out of 10, if you see a deal that is too good to be true, it is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: This morning what you need to know before you click.\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, "The Crown" and the controversy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is with regret, the Prince and Princess of Wales are separating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The growing backlash against the hit show`s upcoming storyline. Why an Oscar winner wants it to come with a disclaimer and how "The Crown`s" creator is responding.\par \par \uc2 And smash hit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At the wall looking up, see you later.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The Astros defeat the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  in game one of the ALCS while the \uc2 Padres\uc2  rally to tie up their series with the \uc2 Phillies\uc2  in a game for the record books.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Aaron Nola against Austin Nola, little brother against big brother.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: The battle of the brothers going down in history, today, Thursday, October 20th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie live from Washington, D.C. and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well good morning. Good to see you. So happy you`re joining us on a Thursday morning. Savannah is joining us in D.C. And SG, can you imagine being the mom and dad you`re like, I`m cheering for both my sons. You go, no you go. It`s got to be a tough one.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know. Yes, moms are always like, no, I don`t have a favorite. No really, I don`t. Well, a lot more of those highlights in just a bit. Plus nearly two thirds of the country waking up to another morning of bitter cold, I know you`re feeling it there in New York, here Washington too. Record low temps expected in the south today. Al is tracking it and he has some good news on when it`ll feel a little more like fall.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But we are going to start this morning in Ukraine where new evacuations are being ordered. It comes as residents are being told to charge their devices after drone strikes knocked out key power stations, rolling blackouts now common. And this is just as the brutal Ukraine winter is approaching. NBC`s Cal Perry is in Ukraine for us. Cal, good morning. Give us the latest.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good morning to you, Hoda. Fresh strikes overnight, the target once again that vital energy infrastructure of Ukraine. It is what has prompted the mayor here to tell people turn off your electricity, save power. All the while as you mentioned, the bitter cold from the winter has arrived, the temperatures plunging overnight, all the while the strikes continue across Ukrainian cities.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERRY (voice-over): This morning, the people of Kherson are caught in the middle. Thousands in the port city that has been under Russian occupation for months, now being ordered to evacuate by Russian installed leaders. Some getting text messages that read, dear residents, evacuate immediately. There will be shelling of residential areas by the armed forces of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The evacuations and apparent reaction to Ukraine`s counter offensive in the region, recapturing villages and farmland in recent weeks, Ukrainian officials accusing Russia of trying to create hysteria. It comes on the same day Russian`s President Putin declared martial law in the four Ukrainian regions currently occupied by Russia.\par \par \uc2 I have signed a decree, he said, introducing martial law into these four entities of the Russian Federation. Under the order Russia can, it says, legally seize Ukrainian homes and property, move civilian populations and create local militias, meaning Moscow may try to force Ukrainians to fight against their own country.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It seems his only tool available to him is to brutalize individual citizens in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PERRY (voice-over): From Mykolaiv and the south to Kyiv and the north, the Russian bombardment continues. In just over a month, at least 239 drones have been shot down by air defenses, according to the Ukrainian military. The ones that make it past those air defenses are frequently striking infrastructure targets, 30 percent of power plants have been destroyed.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:17]\par \par \uc2 Other times, civilians pay the price. Here in the capital of Kyiv, eight dead in 24 hours, all the while the threat of a nuclear attack looms. Children drill in a nearby school here in Kyiv. And while bulletproof vests are necessary on the battle front, swim caps and goggles must do here on the home front.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Now Ukraine is asking countries around the world to help with its air defense system. Israel the latest to offer hands saying it will help provide that very important early alert system. But holding back on the ammunition needed to actually shoot down the drones. Officials here are hoping to get that from the United States. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re counting those young kids with swim caps and goggles, it`s just -- it`s also unsettling. Thank you so much. We appreciate your report there.\par \par \uc2 Let`s move on back here at home. We`ve got another morning of cold temperatures and a lot of the country, record setting in parts of the South. Let`s bring in Mr. Roker to get a first check of the forecast. Good morning, Al.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: All right, good morning, Hoda. Good morning, everybody. The good news is if you are one of the 76 million people under these frost advisory, freeze warnings, we`ve got some relief coming for you, so hold on. For this morning though, Memphis 39 degrees, Tallahassee right now 32, Orlando 55 degrees, Norfolk 39. But we are going to see a little bit of a change.\par \par \uc2 For today though, it is going to stay cold but we are going to see a little relief. But for today, about 12 degrees below average in Buffalo behind where the Jetstream dips to the south. We`re looking at Pier 75 degrees, that`s 15 degrees above normal, Boise, Idaho 77. For tomorrow, those temperatures still a little on the chilly side from New York, Charlotte on into Jacksonville. But the warmth is coming, Little Rock 81, San Antonio 86, Dallas, it`s going to be 88 degrees.\par \par \uc2 And as we get into early next week, temperatures moderate, look at this, we`ll be in the 60s in New York right on into early next week, 70s in Raleigh and in Cincinnati, Chicago as well. And hot springs Arkansas into the 80s. We have that record warmth out west, record cold in the east. However, it`s because of this jet stream that`s really been amplified.\par \par \uc2 The good news is if you`re here in the east, we`re going to see a flip, cooler air with rain and snow, which is good news for Seattle and Portland. They have right now the worst air quality in the world because of all the heat and the wildfires. They`re going to see temperatures drop 10 to 25 degrees below normal here in the East, much warmer as we get into next week with temperatures 10 to 20 degrees above normal. So Savannah, we`re all getting some good news back to you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, I like that Al. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, this morning, new data is out. And it reveals more about the effects of inflation on families all across this country, and the Federal Reserve`s efforts to get it under control. This is some top business leaders are voicing big concerns about the months ahead. NBC`s Tom Costello here with us in Washington tracking it again. Not good news that we`re hearing from business leaders.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Not good news. And we`ve been talking for months about persistent sky high inflation. And now, the head of one of the country`s biggest banks, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  is warning about a possible volatile period as we head into 2023. Listen, when Goldman talks, people listen, for the average family, there`s less concern right now about whether we are entering a recession or already there, but rather, when will things improve and how do we cope right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): From Main Street, USA to Wall Street, Americans are still staring down red hot inflation that shows no signs of cooling off. Now some top executives are warning of potentially tough times in the months ahead.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SOLOMON\uc2 , GOLDMAN SACHS CEO: I think it`s a time to be cautious. That doesn`t mean for sure that we have a really difficult economic scenario. But in the distribution of outcomes, there`s good chance we could have a recession.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): And \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , also issuing a warning earlier this week tweeting, the probabilities in this economy tell you to batten down the hatches.\par \par \uc2 With less than three weeks before the midterm elections, President Biden denies that the move to continue tapping into the nation`s emergency oil supply is politically motivated, calling on oil companies to increase production and their refining operations.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You still make a significant profit. Your shareholders will still do very well. And the American people catch a break they deserve and get a fair price at the pump as well.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): But gas is just one part of the global inflation picture. In the U.K. inflation is running even hotter, above 10 percent. Food up a staggering 14.6 percent while the average American family now spends nearly $450 more per month to buy the same goods as last year. The head of the country`s largest retailer, \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , tell Savannah it`s hitting people in different ways.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Do you feel like consumers are hitting that point where they`re done paying those high prices?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FURNER\uc2 , WALMART U.S. CEO: I think it depends on the personal situation that consumers are in, really different depending on where you are around the country.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): For Stephanie Lane`s (ph) family of six living off a single income in Pennsylvania, it`s meant cutting back across the board.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE LANE (ph), PENNSYLVANIA RESIDENT: It`s a hard pill to swallow when you have to really change everything you`ve done. And do it everything differently because you can`t afford it.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Despite the challenging times, Stephanie (ph) says she and her family are looking forward.\par \par \uc2 LANE (ph): We don`t know when it`s going to get better. But I do know it will. I have faith that it will.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Tom, we`ve been talking so much about the Fed`s interest rates hikes and how it`s going into effect. And sometimes it takes time to see if they`re working. We`re already seeing some leading indicators right now turning up and affect in the economy.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Last week, you and I talked about a lagging indicator. The unemployment rate is a lagging indicator of what the economy is like right now. We have a new leading indicator, this is concerning. New home building fell 8 percent last month, the lowest since the earliest days of the pandemic, that`s a concern. Mortgage rates, of course, now up to 7.2 percent on a 30 year, 