f abortion. Democrats thought they had a lot of momentum with that issue right on the heels of the Dobbs decision in June. Now, it doesn`t seem that it`s having as much of a sway with independent voters.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That`s right. And you look at the swing that we`re seeing among independent women in particular, who said that they favored Democrats in September. Now, huge swing over to Republicans, you saw abortion as an energizing factor in races in Kansas in special election in New York in the primaries, but I think Democrats are concerned that they need to be having a broader appeal.\par \par \uc2 You saw President Biden talking about the issue of abortion earlier this week. He said remember, basically the outrage that you felt on the day that Roe v. Wade was overturned, trying to turn back the clock in essence, Savannah, but there`s a real awareness and I think a message from the White House. Yes, we need to talk about abortion. But we need to talk about issues including the economy and crime that voters care about in this election cycle, too. So that`s why the President is going to be in Pennsylvania talking about infrastructure today, for example.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, the polls have shown that the Republicans appeared to have some momentum going into these midterms. The control of the Senate, control of the House is on the table, Pennsylvania, where the President will be today is a key Senate race and it`s tight.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It`s very tight. It`s basically a statistical dead heat, Savannah. And what`s notable about the President stop in Pennsylvania today, he`s going to be talking about infrastructure. And here you can see these polls between \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Oz. He`s going to talk about infrastructure and then hold a fundraising event with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So not the typical rally that you would usually see a President hold in these closing weeks of a campaign. Why? Well, his approval ratings ar