 the majority of people, it causes a mild cold like illness. So we`re talking runny nose, a low grade fever, a cough, sneezing.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: How long does it last?\par \par \uc2 AZAR: Usually lasts about one to two weeks, sometimes the symptoms can peak at day five.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 AZAR: But in certain individuals, when it goes from the upper respiratory tract into the lungs, it can actually cause more severe disease. And I think it`s very important, especially for caregivers out there of young ones who can`t communicate, the symptoms of more severe disease would be things like poor feeding, irritability, wheezing, or shortness of breath. Those are the kinds of symptoms and signs that you`d want to reach out to your pediatrician immediately.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So sometimes you wouldn`t know if your child has a cold or has this but either way, like, how do you treat it? What do you suppose to give them?\par \par \uc2 AZAR: Well, that`s the thing. And so you mentioned a very good point, it`s actually indistinguishable, you know, from cold or flu or things like a COVID, you would actually need to get tested. You have to get tested, though in the doctor`s office, there are no home tests for this. In terms of treatment for the majority of people, the treatment is supportive. So that means rehydrating, managing the airway.\par \par \uc2 There is a treatment, however, for people who are at risk, and that group includes premature infants, kids under the age of six months, heart lung problems, weakened immune systems, older folks. It`s a monthly injection that can actually prevent severe disease.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Just a last quick thing. There was a headline in -- here in New York that said COVID cases in some pockets of New York were up 20 percent. Is that a bellwether? Are we expecting like this big bounce in numbers?\par \par \uc2 AZAR: Right, Hoda. So we`re talking about new strains, and they are descendants of the Omicron strain. This is not unexpected. This is what we expect the virus to do. At this point, it doesn`t appear that it`s causing more severe disease. But remember, more infections could lead to more hospitalizations, and then the convergence of flu. We worry about hospital capacity and of course, cases of lung COVID. So we`re certainly keeping an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And that 20 percent is pretty alarming even in --\par \par \uc2 AZAR: It`s a pretty significant doubling time. That`s what`s making experts concerned.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Dr. Azar, thank you. We appreciate it. It is 17 minutes after the hour. Hello, Craig.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hello, good morning, Hoda. Good morning Dr. Natalie as well and good morning to you. October best part of the year for baseball fans, major league baseball playoffs underway right now. The \uc2 Houston Astros\uc2  came out swinging in game one of the ALCS.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Drive into right, well hit, Judge on the run, and Chas McCormick, opposite field home run.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And there you have it. Chas McCormick with Houston second home run of the inning there, they would go on to beat the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  four to two. Game two is tonight. Over to the National League now, the \uc2 San Diego Padres\uc2  scoring five runs in the fifth inning to take the lead there. They would hold on to that lead to win it, eight to five. That series all tied up at one game apiece.\par \par \uc2 There was some history made in that game as noted at the top of the show here, \uc2 Phillies\uc2  pitcher Aaron Nola is facing his brother Austin Nola, who is a catcher for San Diego. You see their parents looking on there to the farm, right. It`s the first time in MLB postseason history that a pitcher faced his brother at the plate. Austin Nola getting the big RBI single there. That was smart the \uc2 Padres\uc2  in the fifth inning. And I heard