ap. What you need to know to stay safe. That`s coming up next on Today.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:42:21]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re back 7:42 with Today`s Consumer Confidential, it`s our series that we do around this time each morning and we focus on everything that`s impacting your bottom line.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes, this morning, an urgent warning for people who use some of the really popular sites to buy and sell things online. Authorities say criminals, criminals are on those sites as well and they`re looking for victims.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, NBC`s senior consumer investigative correspondent Vicky Nguyen is here to tell us how we can sit stay safe. Hey, Vic.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Hey. Good morning, Hoda and Craig. Those online marketplaces they sell everything from clothing to cars. Here`s how it works. You see something you want, you agree to meet up with cash to make that purchase. But police say beware, it can be a bait and switch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): For holiday shoppers in a season of high inflation, online marketplaces can be a great place to find bargains. Unfortunately, police a peer to peer sites like \uc2 Facebook\uc2  marketplace and OfferUp are also being used by scammers and violent criminals.\par \par \uc2 HILL: Nine times out of 10, if you see a deal that is too good to be true, it is.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Chicago police are investigating dozens of robberies over the past few months. The suspects using online listings to lure in would be buyers.\par \par \uc2 HILL: The offenders will contact them via cellphone and tell them to go to another location. And when they go to that area, four multiple offenders will show up, display guns and take not only the money that they have in their pockets, but sometimes vehicles, watches, and wallets that type of thing.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): It`s a trend law enforcement officials say has been growing nationwide. In Houston, police releasing this video of a robbery earlier this year. They say the suspects posed on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  marketplace as potential car buyers. In Florida and Detroit, similar cases of suspects accused of robbing individuals selling Air Jordans and iPhones. Authorities say criminals are most likely to target high end items like designer purses, all-terrain vehicles, scooters, \uc2 Apple\uc2  iPads, and watches. To stay safe, officials they always use safe exchange locations, including in some cities inside the lobby of a police station.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): And the police are OK with that?\par \par \uc2 HILL: That`s what we want them to do. Especially now, the holidays are quickly approaching, we don`t want anyone to trade their safety for a great deal.\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN (voice-over): Experts say online marketplaces are also targeted by hackers and scam artists who use fake accounts and listings to steal your money. Common red flags include the seller refusing to meet in person or let you see the item before purchase. The seller`s profile shows the same item look catered in different states, the seller asking for gift cards instead of cash. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  says it takes the threat of scams seriously and has specialized detection tools and trained enforcement and review teams working to remove bad actors from the platform.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:16]\par \par \uc2 OfferUp says it also has a team of investigators who looked to remove bad items and users from its site and provides in-app tools for identity verification. \uc2 Facebook\uc2  also allows users to set up a meeting plan within the app that can be shared with a group of trusted friends, all to ensure your hunt for deals doesn`t put you in danger.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow, we could always use one more way to protect ourselves, what do you have?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Yes, Hoda, when it comes to buying anything online, if you are going to use a payment method, always use your credit card because that offers another layer of fraud protection and of course, if you`re seeing a deal that is way below market, that`s your first red flag. And when you`re doing this in person, police say, anytime you feel something is off, trust your gut, leave, get out of that situation.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right. They can tell you, meet around the corner then you should not. All right, thank you Vic.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Vic. Mr. Roker, it looks like a relatively quiet day in the weather.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: It really is. We`ve got that still pesky low pressure system now over Canada brings some light snow and rain around the Great Lakes. But we`re not looking at a risk of thunderstorms anywhere in the country. It is rare to see that and not unusual during winter but we like it. We`re also looking at those cool temperatures through the Great Lakes on into the Northeast, warming up as we get out west. We`ll see that change later in the week. For the day, today, sunny and warm out west, easing chilly conditions here in the East, sunshine from the plains down to Texas. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That is your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next, guys, you`ll like this one. Pick your favorite flavor. We`re talking about pop tarts, the great debate over pop tarts, what`s yours? We`re going to talk about it and ours coming up right after this.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Not PopStart?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Pop tart.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: No, pop tarts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:45]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Can you just pick a normal flavor?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, those are my favorite. I gave you a flavor and you said that`s basic. I gave you another, you said, you didn`t like 