e`ll immediately move to pass national abortion rights. Will it be enough with polls showing voter concerns focused on the economy right now?\par \par \uc2 More than 25 years after the disappearance of Kristin Smart, the verdict in the murder trial or for former college classmate.\par \par \uc2 Blackouts in Ukraine after officials say Russia destroyed nearly a third of its power stations in little over a week. Growing fears for an Iranian climber after she competed without a hijab. The worries about her safety tonight.\par \par \uc2 The deadly crash. A plane slamming into a car dealership.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  pushing to take a controversial pregnancy drug off the market. Why some doctors and moms say it`s a mistake\par \par \uc2 And the Wikipedia warrior fighting to get some remarkable women the recognition they deserve.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC Nightly News with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. In a year defined by some of the most extreme heat we`ve ever seen, along with one of the strongest hurricanes to hit the U.S. mainland, tonight, making headlines for unseasonable cold and early snow. In the East, autumn interrupted by a sudden and dramatic detour into winter, a climate change whiplash leaving 86 million people currently under freezing alerts from the northern tip of Texas across the south and up into the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 More than 40 record lows possible this evening across 18 states. Several cities in the south could see their earliest freezes on record. \uc2 Al Roker\uc2  will lay it out for us in a moment but first let`s go to Maggie Vespa feeling the big chill in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Less than a month into fall, a frigid blast blanketing swaths of the Midwest in white.\par \par \uc2 Did you have to dig out the winter coats?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We did. We just need to go for a walk tonight. We got to go home.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: And tonight, leaving at 6 million Americans across 25 states under freeze alerts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`ve never seen anything like this. This is super early.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: In particular, parts of northern Michigan and Wisconsin slammed with more than a foot of snow. Whitecaps crashing on the Great Lakes. The storm knocking out power for thousands and closing schools. It`s paralyzing impact stretching into Illinois and Indiana where roads today were impassable. Snow falling as far south as Kentucky and Tennessee. The system`s powerful punch landing more than two months before the official start of winter. East of the Rockies, temperatures are 10 to 25 degrees below average. Even seasoned locals shutter.\par \par \uc2 And is it a birthday gift or is it a problem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Countless families forced to crank the heat just as heating bills are expected to hit a 10-year high, leaving some left to wonder whether this unseasonably severe cold snap is a sign of brutal winter weather to come.\par \par \uc2 Is any part of this painful given that we`re like mid-October?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s going to be a long winter. A really long winter starting this early.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Maggie joining me now. I know the timing of this story made the impact much worse.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (on camera): Yes, Lester, that`s right. Here in the Upper Peninsula where you can see snow is still falling, utility crews are reportedly finding a lot of downed trees that hadn`t shed their leaves yet, meaning they were likely toppled by snow because this early in fall, they`re that much heavier. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Maggie Vesper, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get right to \uc2 Al Roker\uc2  who is tracking this cold wave. Al, what can we expect for the rest of the week?\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS WEATHER ANCHOR: Lester, we`ve got this storm system over the Great Lakes. It`ll be moving out but probably dump another one to three inches of snow, so that`s out of here. But still here, 86 million of us are going to be under some sort of frost advisories, freeze watch, freeze warning from the plains all the way to northeast, down to the Gulf. We got that Jetstream dipping way down. Records possible in 18 states from Texas, Kansas, on into Kentucky, in the east southeast and down to the Gulf.\par \par \uc2 And then tomorrow, temperatures still below average anywhere from 10 to 15 from Philadelphia, Baton Rouge, St. Louis. Those will start to moderate late this week. And then next week, warmer in the east, colder out west.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Al, thanks.\par \par \uc2 21 days before the Midterms, President Biden trying to keep abortion rights front and center, but polls show voters worrying most about the economy and soaring inflation. Peter Alexander reports on the new clashes between candidates in key battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:09]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): With just three weeks until the Midterms and Republicans slamming Democrats over the economy and record inflation, President Biden tonight trying to amplify the politically divisive issue of abortion rights, urging voters to keep Democrats in control of Congress.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Here`s the promise I make to you and the American people. The first bill that I will send to the Congress will be to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: But some Democrats fear the potency of that message may be waning. Tonight, a new poll shows 44 percent of likely voters cite the economy or inflation as the number one problem facing the country while just five percent list abortion as their top issue. And now, Republicans have a four-point lead when voters are asked which party`s candidate they`d support.\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, one of several crucial races that`ll determine control of the Senate. Republican candidate J.D. Vance hammered Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  over skyrocketing prices in a heated final debate.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH) SENATE CANDIDATE: That rising energy price that people see at the pump, that they see in the utility bills, that was the direct result of policies enacted by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and supported 100 percent by \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: While Ryan slammed Vance for embracing former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): You were calling Trump America`s Hitler. Then he kissed his ass --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It`s not true.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: It is true. And then you kissed his ass, and then he endorsed you. And you said he`s the greatest president of all time.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: There are critical governor`s races too in the battlegrounds of Arizona and Michigan and a rematch in Georgia between Republican Governor Brian Kemp and Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . Their one and only showdown last night.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-GA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We live in a state of fear. And this is a governor who for the last four years has beat his chest but delivered very little for most Georgians.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Kemp touting his early moves to reopen the state during the pandemic and going after Abrams.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): Miss Abrams is going to lie out my record because she doesn`t want to talk about her own.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter, President Biden is going to pivot to the economy tomorrow focusing on high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Yes, Lester, that`s right. Looking to lower those prices, the President is going to announce that the administration will move forward with the release of as many as 15 million additional barrels of oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. It`s a controversial move that comes amid Russia`s war in Ukraine and after Saudi Arabia snub the President`s request to boost oil production. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter Alexander at the White House, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There`s breaking news and a trial involving a murder they went unsolved for 25 years. A California man convicted late today for killing Kristin Smart, a college student who disappeared in 1996. Paul Flores, a former classmate of Smart`s wasn`t arrested for the crime until last year. Flores` father who was accused of helping move Smart`s body was found not guilty. Smart`s remains were never found.\par \par \uc2 Worldwide concern tonight about what will happen to a celebrated female athlete from Iran who competed without a headscarf as the country deals with unprecedented anti-government protests driven by women. Andrea Mitchell has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, another Iranian woman in the spotlight. Elnaz Rekabi, a 33-year- old elite rock climber, the first female athlete on an Iranian team known to have competed without her country`s mandatory headscarf or hijab. During international competition in South Korea, Rekabi`s long hair flowing uncovered, prompted concerns for her safety.\par \par \uc2 Hours later, a post on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account read, I encountered a problem with my cover during the final. Adding, she was returning to Iran with her team as planned. The circumstances under which it was posted remain unclear. Human rights activists are worried about her fate and around where women and girls have been beaten and jailed for not following the dress code.\par \par \uc2 What are your concerns about the fate of the woman climber who took off the hijab in competition in South Korea?\par \par \uc2 NAZANIN BONIADI, IRANIAN-BORN ACTRESS AND ACTIVIST: We`re very concerned that she`s going to end up in solitary confinement and be tortured into making a false confession. And we need to internationally stand up for her.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: U.S. leaders are voicing support.\par \par \uc2 What can America do to stand by the women of Iran?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Well, we can make sure everyone is paying attention to it. This is organic. It`s initiated by the women, and we should all take great pride in that. And these women in Iran are just so remarkable.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Tonight, the resistance in the face of the regime`s furious crackdown undiminished. Andrea Mitchell, NBC News Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A building materials company that operates across the U.S. agreed to plead guilty today to supporting terrorists who killed Americans. Officials say it`s the first U.S. prosecute mission of a company for supporting terrorism. Here`s Justice Correspondent Ken Dilanian.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:06]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): One of the world`s largest corporations admitting tonight it funneled millions of dollars to ISIS.\par \par \uc2 LISA MONACO, U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL; Corporate crime that has reached a new low and a very dark place.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: French cement company \uc2 Lafarge\uc2 , which operates plants in dozens of countries and 43 U.S. states pleading guilty to paying terrorists in Syria between 2013 and 2014 so it could keep a cement plant running as ISIS and other groups fought for control. Payments made at the same time the terror group was capturing and beheading U.S. hostages, including James Foley and Steven Sotloff.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say the company sent ISIS and the al-Qaeda-linked \uc2 Nusra Front\uc2  more than $10 million, then tried to cover it up.\par \par \uc2 BREON PEACE, U.S. ATTORNEY, NEW YORK EASTERN DISTRICT: \uc2 Lafarge\uc2  made a deal with the devil, foreign terrorists who pledged to in fact did harm the United States, its people, and its national security.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: The company agreed to pay a settlement of nearly $800 million to the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . French authorities in 2018 charged eight former \uc2 Lafarge\uc2  executives with financing terrorism. The company now owned by a Swiss firm said today it deeply regretted what happened, adding that the executives involved no longer work there and that none of the conduct involve us employees.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN (on camera): In a disturbing twist, court documents show \uc2 Lafarge\uc2  was doing business with ISIS and al-Qaeda at the same time, its concrete was being used to build One World Trade Center on the site where terrorists destroyed the Twin Towers in 2001. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Ken Dilanian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine tonight the crippling impact of Russia`s attacks over the last week. President Zelenskyy warning today that 30 percent of Ukraine`s power stations had been destroyed, causing massive blackouts. New airstrikes today left hundreds of thousands of people without power or water. Concern is growing as cold weather sets in.\par \par \uc2 Now to our reporting from inside Russia and the impact of President Putin`s partial draft. We`ve seen the traffic jams of young men fleeing the country to avoid it. Keir Simmons with some who have left.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, as Russia`s President Putin attempts to force Ukrainians into capitulation with deadly drone strikes, he`s under pressure at home with many Russian men deciding to leave. We traveled to Kazakhstan to meet some of the thousands who came here to escape the partial draft.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I don`t want to kill people. I don`t want to die.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : With their possessions on their back, these men not wanting their faces on camera.\par \par \uc2 This is the bag that you left Russia with?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : Just this?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Dimitri, not his real name, is homeless and heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I feel very sad about it because I feel a connection with my motherland.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : You love your country.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Of course. Of course, I love -- I love Russia.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : We met a group of Russian artists putting on street performances as a thank-you to the people of Kazakhstan for welcoming them.\par \par \uc2 Alexander`s friend got his draft papers, and they decided to leave that same day, just grab anything they could. And what happened on the border?\par \par \uc2 We flew, then found a car, he says. But there was 10 miles of traffic at the border. So, we walked. No longer facing life on the front line, but their lives changed forever.\par \par \uc2 What do you see in the future?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can tell you nothing about my future because I see like only in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Back in Russia, the only independent pollster here tells us support for President Putin remains strong but the partial mobilization is having an impact.\par \par \uc2 DENIS, VOLKOV, DIRECTOR, LEVADA POLLING CENTER: More than half our population feel anxious about it.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : What are they anxious about that?\par \par \uc2 VOLKOV: That the husbands, sons, might be recruited.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : The Kremlin says the partial draft will soon be over with 300,000 enlisted. But ultimately, it`s President Putin`s decision. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Keir Simmons, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, companies making it tougher to earn those customer loyalty rewards. The well-known brands making you spend more in the "PRICE YOU PAY."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with the "PRICE YOU PAY" and the loyalty points you earn for everything from your morning coffee to your hotel stays with inflation running hot. Many companies are making big changes. Tom Costello explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): For Americans earning reward points for every gallon of gas, rental car, airline ticket, and cup of coffee, loyalty may not be worth what it once was, as companies rewrite their reward rules, often requiring more purchases, not fewer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m very disappointed in Dunkin.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: \uc2 Dunkin Donuts\uc2  has faced a social media backlash after raising the cost of earning a free cup of coffee from $40 to $50 and doing away with free birthday drinks. There is drama in the Dunkin world right now tweeted one customer. I no longer run on Dunkin. But Dunkin says its revamped program gives customers more options and a wider variety of food and beverages.\par \par \uc2 Chipotle is also requiring more purchases to earn a free burrito. P.F. Chang`s now offers extra reward points for a monthly fee. As inflation drives up restaurant costs, forcing many to trim expenses.\par \par \uc2 R.J. HOTTOVY, HEAD OF FINANCIAL RESEARCH, PLACER.AI: We`ve seen from Chipotle. We`ve seen it from Dunkin. We`ve -- probably we`ll see it for many others before we`re all set and done.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: For many customers, the points make the difference.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, big difference. They save -- they save a lot of people a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Meanwhile, you can now earn a mile on \uc2 Delta Airlines\uc2  for each dollar you spend at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , just as Delta will require passengers to spend more on tickets to earn top tier status and free upgrades. The advice if you`ve been gathering points --\par \par \uc2 TED ROSSMAN, SENIOR INDUSTRY ANALYST, BANKRATE: You don`t want to hoard these just for hoarding sake. You want to earn and burn strategically.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: Use them or lose them. Tom Costello, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, a terrifying scene. The deadly plane crash slamming into a car dealership. And the controversy over a drug meant to prevent premature births. Why the \uc2 FDA\uc2  wants to pull it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Fiery images out of Ohio today when a small plane crash into a car dealership parking lot in Marietta. Investigators say the pilot and a passenger were both killed when the plane went down about 30 minutes after takeoff from Columbus. No injuries were reported on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, the battle over a controversial pregnancy drug. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  wants to take it off the market but some doctors and moms say it`s a lifesaver for babies born prematurely. We get more now from Ann Thompson.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): They are the littlest Americans. Nearly 400,000 children born prematurely each year, 10 percent of all births, 14 percent in the African American community. Tony Blake delivered her first two children at 28 and 32 weeks.\par \par \uc2 TONY BLAKE, MOTHER OF PREMATURE BABIES: I didn`t want another kid in the NICU. Like you just don`t want that because you want to have that bonding time.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: So, her doctor prescribed McKenna for her third pregnancy, a synthetic version of the hormone progesterone. She carried her daughter Rain to 33 weeks.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE: It helped me to not actually go into labor.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Now the \uc2 FDA\uc2  wants to pull McKenna from the market saying trials show Tony story is the exception, not the rule.\par \par \uc2 DR. PATRIZIA CAVAZZONI, DIRECTOR, FDA CENTER FOR DRUG EVALUATION AND RESEARCH: Based on the evidence available today, McKenna is not shown to be effective. Its benefit-risk profile is unfavorable, and it shouldn`t be withdrawn from the market.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Once fast track for approval, regulators say, along with no benefits, there are risks to mothers, including blood clots and depression. But Dr. Kristin Lyerly wants to keep prescribing McKenna to her patients.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTIN LYERLY, OB/GYN: Why not leave it on the market while we are investigating further? Because the potential to reduce preterm birth has an incredible impact on patients and their families for their entire lives.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Especially, she says, for African American moms with higher rates of premature birth. Others disagree.\par \par \uc2 Is there any evidence that African American women benefit from this drug?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 MICHAEL CAROME\uc2 , DIRECTOR, PUBLIC CITIZEN HEALTH RESEARCH GROUP: The available evidence based upon data reviewed by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  doesn`t support that the drug is effective for those patients.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: McKenna`s manufacturer says the drug should be kept on the market while additional study is undertaken. The advisory committee will vote tomorrow, but the final decision is still months away. Anne Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, raising the profiles of women and minority scientists. Her mission to tell their stories.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Millions turn to Wikipedia for answers but as Stephanie Gosk reports, when one physicist noted there were few entries on women and minorities in science, she got writing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): From the Boxer Rebellion to the history of the banana. Wikipedia has instant information on everything. At least that`s what British physicists Jess Wade believed until she noticed a gaping hole in the online encyclopedia`s content.\par \par \uc2 JESS WADE, BRITISH PHYSICISTS: Women is so underrepresented, and people of color are so underrepresented.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: And then, the 33-year-old got to work.\par \par \uc2 How many entries do you have in Wikipedia?\par \par \uc2 WADE: 1757 or 1756, but who`s counting?\par \par \uc2 GOSK: She`s dedicated on top of a day job spent in the lab.\par \par \uc2 WADE: I get home, I have my dinner, and I have my designated Wikipedia time where I just kind of research and then write and then read and then edit and then write some more.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Writing bios on women like Gladys West.\par \par \uc2 WADE: She was born in Virginia in 1930. She went to a historically black College and university. She studied Math. She became a math teacher at high school and then she worked for the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  on GPS.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Jess Wade plucked her story out of forgotten history, leading to West`s 2018 induction into the U.S. Air Force Hall of Fame.\par \par \uc2 It`s nice to think that you were able to give her just a little bit of nudge.\par \par \uc2 WADE: Like, I don`t -- I didn`t do anything, right? She`s the magic. I just -- I`m just that person who just kind of taps it closer to the goal line, and then she shoots, and she scores.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Wade says society is doing better getting young women interested in math and science but it`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 WADE: We`re not doing enough to retain them in the system. And part of that is because we`re not celebrating them.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Now, with her help, everyone can type in a search and give a round of applause. Stephanie Goss, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Tuesday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc72}{\*\bkmkend toc72}\b \uc2 TODAY for October 18, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Maggie Vespa, Al Roker, Richard  Engel, Meagan Fitzgerald, Steve Kornacki, Kerry Sanders, Sheinelle Jones,  Sam Brock, Carson Daly \par \uc2 6,730 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:16]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. It`s Tuesday. Hope you have those winter coats handy.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, and in several cities, they`re already breaking out the snow shovels. It is October 18th. And this is Today.\par \par \uc2 Deep freeze, nearly 100 million people waking up to brutally cold temperatures from Colorado to New England all the way down to Florida, some places in between coping with the earliest snowfall in years. It`s going to be a long winter. We are alive where the flakes are falling and Al will have your full forecast.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, cities across Ukraine facing a new barrage of Russian airstrikes, this time, cutting power and water supplies to tens of thousands of civilians, the very latest in a live report from the front lines.\par \par \uc2 Growing concern, an Iranian athlete in the international spotlight after competing in a high profile event without her hijab, so was it a show of support for women protesting on the streets back home, her new message overnight and what she`s facing as she returns to Tehran.\par \par \uc2 Three weeks to go, with the midterms fast approaching candidates from coast to coast clashing in closely watched debates overnight, just ahead, we will break down the key races where they stand and the biggest issues on the minds of voters.\par \par \uc2 All that, plus, burgers, tacos, fries, oh my, those sky high prices now hitting a staple for millions of Americans, fast food, what you need to know and how to save on your next meal.\par \par \uc2 And just for kicks, the Los Angeles Chargers use a big Monday night rally to collect your field goal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The kick is good. And the Chargers win it in overtime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And knock off the \uc2 Denver Broncos\uc2  in a nail biter, today Tuesday, October 18th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, good morning. Good to see you. It`s a Tuesday morning. We`re so happy that you`re with us.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, yes, and burr, that cold snap affecting much of the country, we started talking about it yesterday. Well today, it`s here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: More specifically it is here. The snow really whipping up this morning in northern Michigan near the shores of Lake Superior and while it may not be snowing where you live, the chances are it is getting cold.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes nearly 100 million Americans under frost and freeze warnings this morning with that winter like weather extending from Colorado all the way up and down the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And that early Cold is a cause for concern for many Americans who are already bracing for higher heating costs. Al is standing by. He`s got his forecast. But first, NBC`s Maggie Vespa joins us now from a snowy Michigan. Maggie, we can`t believe what we`re seeing in there.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: I know guys, good morning. This is crazy, right? You wouldn`t know it based on this snow globe of a live shot. But we are less than a month into fall right now. And obviously this looks like a winter wake up. And to be clear, we`re in the Negaunee, Michigan like Upper Peninsula Michigan, so a dusting of snow this time of year is not unusual. But look at our handy dandy tape measure. Here in Negaunee, we`re at roughly eight inches and counting. And again, this chill is not just affecting the Midwest, this cold snap is stretching all the way to Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): Overnight, fall quickly turning to winter in the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Is a part of this painful given that we`re like mid-October?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s going to be a long winter.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): And early season storm dumping more than a foot of snow in northern Wisconsin and Michigan. And making for a treacherous drive in Indiana. The weight of the heavy snow knocking out power to tens of thousands, Monday, and causing trouble for plows.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We have hard times for our plows to get through because the trees are hanging over the roads.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): Autumn leaves blanketed in white as Chicago saw its first flurries of the season. The October chill bringing the first freeze of the season to 89 million people this week. Today Kansas City Omaha and dozens of other cities waking up to record breaking cold as low as the teens. Americans forced to crank the heat just as heating bills are expected to hit a 10-year high. The frigid blast arriving nearly a month ahead of schedule and also surging south, sending temperatures tumbling from Tennessee to the Florida Panhandle this week. Alabama and Mississippi set to feel even colder than Boston Wednesday. While in South Carolina farmers are scrambling to harvest before the frost.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It isn`t fun to lose crops. We will harvest what we can and we`ll continue to harvest tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 VESPA (voice-over): A change of season and a taste of winter come early.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What is it looks like this when your trip trick or treating?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m going to freeze my butt off.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:05:03]\par \par \uc2 VESPA Yes, guys, the stakes are high. All right so back out here live, again, we have this handy dandy thermometer now. And you can see it`s right around freezing, so just hold it up for the snow to keep piling up. And for reference, by the way, the first day of winter isn`t until December 21st. And for those of you down south, it sounds like you`ll be waking up to your coldest morning tomorrow morning, your coldest temperature is tomorrow morning. The good news is it sounds like you won`t need those jackets for long. Hoda, I`ll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Maggie Vespa for us, Maggie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She`s got all the gadgets in her tricks. And Al has got even more.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I know she doesn`t have a big wall like this.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, come on.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: But we can show you all that snow that`s going to be coming in and it`s going to be hanging around a bit. We`re going to talk about that later. But let`s get to that cold. As you can see stretching from the Rockies all the way to the northeast down to Mobile, Alabama, almost 90 million people impacted by frost advisories, freeze watches and warnings. Right now it`s 22 in Des Moines, 39 in Cincinnati, Fort Smith, Arkansas at 40 degrees, Pittsburgh 38.\par \par \uc2 The Jetstream dips down to the south the eastern half of the country. Out west, they`ve got some record warmth in the Pacific Northwest. But for this afternoon, only 46 in Detroit, that`s 14 degrees below average, Memphis 53, 20 degrees cooler than average, Knoxville 50, that`s 10 degrees cooler.\par \par \uc2 And then tomorrow morning, we look for possible records in Raleigh Savannah, Knoxville, Tupelo, Little Rock Tulsa, on into Austin and Baton Rouge. However, we will see temperatures start to temper just a bit as we move toward the end of the week by Friday at 60 in New York City, 68 in Cincinnati on Friday, Memphis will be 77.\par \par \uc2 And by next week, we`re going to flip the script, out west colder than average. And here in the east, it`s going to be warmer than average. So just hang in there. Good news is coming guys.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Cool. All right, Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, a new wave of attacks across Ukraine, explosions have been felt across the capital of Kyiv and other cities, even as the death toll rises from yesterday`s barrage of Russian strikes. NBC`s chief foreign correspondent Richard Engel has the very latest. Hey, Richard, good morning.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Hoda. Russia is now attacking Ukrainian cities daily and with impunity. This was the main flower market here in the city of Mykolaiv was hit by a Russian missile overnight. And just down the street, Russia attacked an apartment building killing at least one person according to Ukrainian officials. And Ukrainian say by attacking sites like these that have no military value, Russia is trying to break the Ukrainian spirit.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL (voice-over): Russia is flattening Ukraine, one market at a time. Death by a thousand cuts. For Irina Shipchenko (ph), whose flower shop was destroyed overnight in Mykolaiv, what Russia is doing is nothing less than state terror. We clean up and continue living. What else can we do and we will win, she says.\par \par \uc2 Over the last two weeks, Russia has intensified attacks on Ukrainian cities, claiming to be destroying the country`s infrastructure and its ability to wage war. But Russia is firing directly on civilians attacking an apartment building overnight in Mykolaiv, too. Officials say Russia launched a guided missile, killing one person.\par \par \uc2 The strikes came hours after an airplane mysteriously crashed in Russia. State media reporting that a pilot on a training mission took off from an occupied part of Ukraine and ejected, the aircraft smashing into a building and killing four Russians. Ukrainians wonder if it was an accident or sabotage from within. President Putin personally dispatched investigators to the scene.\par \par \uc2 Discontent is growing in Russia after the Kremlin imposed the draft, rounding up conscripts across the country. And with precision weapons in short supply, Moscow is increasingly relying on Iranian made dive bomber drones, launching them in swarms at Ukrainian cities including Kyiv with deadly effect.\par \par \uc2 Overnight in his daily address, President Zelenskyy called for more air defenses. Secretary of State Blinken condemned Iran for supplying Russia with weapons. The United States gives Ukraine billions of dollars in military support, but not long range weapons, trying to find a perhaps impossible balance of supporting Ukraine without antagonizing Moscow excessively.\par \par \uc2 In a sign, both sides seemed to recognize this will be a long war. Russia and Ukraine exchanged prisoners 108 Ukrainian women for captured Russian soldiers in a one to one swap.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: Over the last several days, we`ve been talking a lot about how Russia is using those Iranian made drones. The drones are highly effective. They have a long range but they don`t carry very much explosive power. Certainly not enough to cause damage like this or to dig a crater in the ground anything like this. You see it can even expose the city`s earlier foundation. And President Zelenskyy today commenting on this attack says it is more evidence why his country needs better air defenses. And he wants Russia to be held accountable. Hold?\par \par \uc2 [07:10:14]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Richard Engel for us there in Ukraine. Richard, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: There is international concern this morning for the safety of an Iranian athlete after she represented her country in a recent climbing competition without wearing the customary hijab. NBC`s Meagan Fitzgerald has that story for us this morning. Meagan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Savannah, good morning. Elnaz Rekabi competed on Sunday with what appeared to be a headband her hair uncovered which is a violation of Iran strict dress code. Now this morning, an apology on her \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page, but there`s still growing concern for her safety.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (voice-over): This morning, concerns for a young female Iranian athlete after she competed without wearing a hijab. Elnaz Rekabi, a prominent rock climber, took part in a competition in South Korea this weekend, notably climbing with her head uncovered. In the past, Rekabi has worn the traditional headscarf while climbing. The hijab has been a focus of recent extraordinary protests in Iran, sparked by the death of 22-year- old Mahsa Amini who was detained by the morality police for breaching the dress code. She died three days later.\par \par \uc2 Women across Iran have protested by cutting their hair and appearing in public with their heads uncovered. After the appearance in South Korea, Rekabi is headed home. The Iranian embassy in Seoul tweeting she departed yesterday. Human rights groups have been concerned about pressure she may face from authorities.\par \par \uc2 MAHMOOD AMIRY-MOGHADDAM, DIRECTOR OF NGO IRAN HUMAN RIGHTS: I think they will put her under tremendous pressure. There is fear of torture, ill treatment, psychological pressure. They want to set an example both for other athletes in the same situation and also for the Iranian girls so that they don`t get a role model in Elnaz.\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (voice-over): Rekabi this morning posting on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  that her job had issues before she was called to climb. While the statement was posted on her account, the circumstances under which he was posted remain unclear.\par \par \uc2 Now back in Iran, the regime has been under pressure as protests led by young women continue, many watching Rekabi`s returned to the country with concern.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD: Now the International Federation of sports climbing says it`s in contact with Rekabi. They also say they`re in contact with the Iranian Climbing Federation. They released a statement that I want to read to you. They say athlete safety is paramount for us. The IFSC fully supports the right of athletes, their choices, and expression of free speech. But Savannah, of course, this is a story that we`re going to be watching closely.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Meagan Fitzgerald, thank you very much. And we turn now to the midterms with just three weeks to go early in person voting is now underway across a number of stakes. And at stake, control of the Senate and the House. And on the minds of voters major issues like the economy, crime, abortion rights. NBC`s national political correspondent Steve Kornacki is here with some of the things we`re watching three weeks out. And let`s start right there with the issues that voters say matter to them. And what that`s telling us about what might happen here and these elections.\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, interesting, Savannah. We`ve talked so much all year about the economy and inflation being important issues. How about this? This is a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll brand new here asking voters what`s the top issue on your mind this election? I haven`t seen it this dramatic in a poll. Add the economy and inflation together. That is 44 percent. We`re getting close to half of all respondents in this poll saying that`s their top issue.\par \par \uc2 Nothing else even cracks 10 percent right now. And we`ve talked about the Republicans when it comes to the economy and inflation having a pretty clear advantage in the polls.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And that brings us to the next question on the poll. They call it the generic ballot, you know, they just asked, would you like a Republican or a Democrat to control Congress? And there`s been some movement here.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes. And typically, this has been a pretty good indicator of where things end up in midterm elections. So this is the average of the generic ballot right now. And you can see the Republicans with a lead here. That is a 2.2 point lead for Republicans now. It`s close, generally speaking, but we were here a week ago taking you through the generic ballot. And a week ago, this number was inside of one point. You had that inflation news over the last week certainly didn`t help the Democrats. And it does seem that as the economy moved to center stage, Republicans are improving their chances here.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And a few weeks ago, it was the Democrats who had the advantage there. So you see the momentum in these final weeks. Let`s talk about the issue of crime, not high on the list of voters concerns and yet it seems to be making an impact in certain races.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: It`s fascinating because we showed you the top issue voters have, crime doesn`t rate high there, doesn`t mean it`s not on their mind. So let`s show you a place where I think it is having an impact. This is Wisconsin, one of the key Senate races, Ron Johnson to term Republican incumbent. He leads in the polling average over his Democratic challenger. Now Johnson has been leaning heavily on crime.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Interesting poll that came out of Wisconsin this week they asked things a little bit differently. They basically asked, are these issues important to you? That`s what`s being asked here. Inflation tops the list. But look at crime. It`s close to 90 percent. And I think also striking, abortion is under 80 percent. Republicans running heavily on crime, Democrats running heavily on abortion, and it looks like in this Wisconsin race, crime may have more resonance than abortion does.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Making a big difference in Pennsylvania Senate race as well, it appears. But let`s talk about Georgia, because it may just be Georgia, Georgia, Georgia to quote our friend Tim Russert. That might be the decision maker in terms of who controls Congress.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, I mean, again, we saw this two years ago, Georgia is a runoff state. If nobody gets 50 percent and here`s that Senate race, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Warnock leading but nobody had 50. If nobody cracks 50, it`s a December 6th runoff, could have control of the Senate on the line. They did have a debate last Friday. We`re waiting to get a readout from some polls haven`t really seen it yet if that change anything there.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Real quickly, the governor`s race is interesting because it doesn`t look like people are at least telling pollsters they`re going to vote straight down party lines.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: We thought this type of voter was extinct, the split ticket voter. But look in Georgia, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , the Republican governor, pretty comfortably ahead outperforming the other Republican, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . What pollsters say they`re seeing here is there`s a suburban voter outside Atlanta who doesn`t like Biden, doesn`t like the Democrats but also doesn`t like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And, you know, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  went to political war with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and he survived in that voters eyes. Kemp`s OK. Walker`s got some convincing to do.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re going to be spending a lot of time together, Steve Kornacki as we get close to election day, thank you so much. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right guys, let`s turn to Monday Night Football. Four quarters not enough for the Chargers and Bronco so the game went into OT and this is how it ended.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s good. Scott puts it down. That`s good. The kick is good. And the Chargers win it in overtime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: There you go. That was just in Hopkins, a fourth that field goal of the night, his foot was hurting but he kicked it anyway. The Chargers win it 19 to 16.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, we got to check the weather from Mr. Chilly Al Roker. What`s up?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: There you go. Hey, well, we`re finally going to have some sunshine today in the Northeast. They`re going to get that Yankees Cleveland Guardians game in around 4 o`clock. Weather shouldn`t be a problem. Record lows as we`ve mentioned in through the planes all the way down to Texas. Plenty of sunshine though. And record highs into the Pacific Northwest today with even more sunshine. That`s what`s going on. We`re going to get to your local forecast in the next 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that`s your latest weather. Guys?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Al, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, the search for the winners of a lottery jackpot where the half a billion dollars, and Kerry, one of those tickets sold in a city that was so hard hit by Hurricane Ian.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: You know, Hoda. It was the worst of luck when Hurricane Ian went into Fort Myers. But now somebody in the region, in the zone has the best of luck because they`re holding one of two tickets for that close to $500 million jackpot. We`ll have that coming up.\par \par \uc2 [07:19:10]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`ll come in real handy. Plus, something we`re sure a lot of folks are noticing, those rising prices now hitting fast food in a big way but fear not, we`ll show you how you can still snag a deal. There`s that. First this morning, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Coming up, have you seen those videos on TikTok. There`s a diabetes drug. It`s going viral. And it`s also in short supply. Some are saying it`s been helping them lose weight.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And we`re going to take a closer look and answer the biggest questions and concerns that people are raising after checking your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:21]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:18]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, it makes me feel fine, some breeze, Tuesday morning, 7:30. Why are we showing you this beautiful picture? Because it`s cold outside. And why not just dream of warmer days. That`s Jacksonville Beach, Florida looking pretty this morning.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: We have a long way to go if you`re dreaming a warmer --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good morning, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning. Good to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, let`s get right into our 7:30 headlines. Dozens of people were left without a home yesterday after a fire ripped through a warehouse in Evansville, Indiana. You can see those massive flames, huge plumes of smoke pouring from that building. The fire was so big, it was even detected on weather radar in the area. About 75 people who live in a residential portion of that building were displaced. Thankfully, somehow nobody was hurt and investigation into the cause of that fire is underway.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: New York City police are searching for three people caught on camera stealing over a half million dollars-worth of high end jewelry from a Manhattan store, it happened over the weekend. The thieves used a sledge hammer to break into the store and then smash the jewelry display cases. They stuffed the goods into the bags and took off. Police are offering a $3,500 reward to anybody with information that leads to an arrest.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Now to some frightening moments in Connecticut over the weekend. A 10-year-old boy was hospitalized after a black bear attacked him in his grandfather`s yard. Neighbor Jonathan Digimus (ph) heard the boy screams and ran next door. He found the boy`s grandfather trying to fight off the nearly 250 pound animal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN DIGIMUS (ph), NEIGHBOR: I picked up the pipe and I threw it at the bears hard as I could. I struck him in the head. My neighbor and my neighbor`s wife brought the little boy, their grandson, inside. He was bleeding from his foot and he had claw marks all over his back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: But thankfully, the boy`s injuries are not life threatening. The bear was captured and euthanized. Authorities say it is rare for black bear to attack a person. They are urging the public not to leave bird feeders out and not to take the trash out until pickup day.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, well, this morning, a lottery mystery is unfolding after the latest Mega Millions jackpot where the half billion dollars.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Two winning tickets were sold one in San Jose, California, the other at a 711 in Fort Myers, Florida. One of the area`s left battered by Hurricane Ian last month.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, NBC`s senior national correspondent Kerry Sanders. He`s on the case, Kerry. Do we know who won it?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: We all want to know who won it. Look two tickets were close to a half billion dollars. And one of those tickets may be from a survivor in Hurricane Ian who possibly lost it all and now is sitting on a fortune.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): With so much chaos in the hurricane zone and life literally turned upside down for so many, some are wondering if the winner even knows they hit half of a $500 million Mega Millions jackpot and are rich beyond their wildest dreams.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Good luck.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): The winning numbers drawn on Friday night, one winning ticket sold in San Jose, California. The other right here in Fort Myers, where for weeks conversations have been about rebuilding and recovery. But now, folks are talking about who has the golden ticket.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I play last weekend but not at this store unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): But with so much pain and suffering here, it`s clear everyone is rooting for a feel good story.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: After Hurricane Ian and devastations, it`s kind of nice to know that somebody was able to win that kind of money.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Even lottery officials jumping on board saying, this one feels slightly more meaningful. We`re hopeful that the prize will have a positive impact. For those who didn`t win, there is a different hope that a generous winner will spread the wealth for those who desperately in need of help right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would donate to a local charity to help out the community because we were all affected in some way. So it would be a nice thing to do, nice gesture.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s best to