hter in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So excited to see him.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I`m back Gracie.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes, looking good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Looking good, walking and talking after back surgery. Carson can`t wait to catch up with you. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:18]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Boy, we`ve been waiting for this moment, the long awaited return of Carson, tanned, rested, and ready with a new back.\par \par \uc2 DALY: We will still do PopStart --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It`s still a thing.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Jacob Soboroff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: Thank you, Sheinelle. And you guys, great to see you. I`m great. I had back surgery like real hardcore spine fusion surgery. Seven weeks ago today, it`s been quite the recovery. I would have been here last week, but we shot the voice in LA. So I had to go back to work last week. It works. It`s great.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: You look rested.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Well, yes, I had some time to sit around and do that. But it feels great. There`s a long road ahead --\par \par \uc2 JONES: I was just about to ask you, so what`s the prognosis? I mean, because before you were dealing with pain, and people didn`t realize it.\par \par \uc2 DALY: Well, I hope I get a chance to do a story on it because what I realized now it`s like the iceberg effect. I mean, this is just a story about people who have back pain, and they how do you fix it? There`s a subtext underneath it all. It`s very complex that so many of us have to deal with. When you`re in pain, you`re looking to just feel good. And that changes your relationship with food and drink and your life.\par \par \uc2 And then when they fix the pain, you`re left with these other complex areas of your life that you need to deal with and realign relationships with. So when I say I`m getting better, I`m getting better in a multitude of ways, which is great, which is really great.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And one of the parts of your recovery is walking. So you`ve been doing this like long walk about it from your neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 DALY: I`m the Forrest Gump of Long Island. It`s crazy. I walk -- I`m like, yes, you would be so proud now. Inspired but I would love that.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Let`s do that. And so how far -- how long before since it`s the Tiger Woods surgery, before you going to be going for the green jacket?\par \par \uc2 DALY: So he says I can ship and put a 13 week so I`m still another six weeks out from that. And then I`ll probably get a condo in Alaska -- in Augusta, Georgia first, start visiting and then I`ll start, you know, getting to win master`s hopefully in maybe four years.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Carson we have --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: It`s great to be back. Thanks for all the love and time.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DALY: All right, I have to do this again now.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DALY: PopStart still on. We got a big one here loaded with news on a host of long awaited sequels from the "Marvel Universe" to "Twister" and whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, "Twister," wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Then guys, a diabetes medication is exploding in popularity for an off label use, weight loss. We`ll talk to Dr. Azar about the pros and cons and controversy too but first your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:10]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc73}{\*\bkmkend toc73}\b \uc2 Conversation With Elizabeth Heiskell About Her Breast Cancer Journey\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie \par \uc2 3,550 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: We are back. 8:13. The "Pink Power TODAY". And this morning story hits close to home for us.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yes, our beloved "TODAY" food contributor Elizabeth Heiskell was diagnosed with stage two breast cancer this past summer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Like a lot of women this can happen, too. She was in the best shape of her life. She has no family history. She says breast cancer wasn`t just -- well, not on her radar. Elizabeth, we love you. You are here with us.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH HEISKELL, " TODAY" FOOD CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We are going to talk to you in a second. OK. But first, let`s check out her story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: I was diagnosed with stage two breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Elizabeth found a lump by chance.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: I went to just scratch my chest and I found a lump. I thought that I had had a mammogram eight months ago. I wasn`t that concerned, honestly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): But she had the lump checked by her doctor.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: She felt around and she started to look very panicked. We got to get a mammogram. We got to get an ultrasound. We need to get a biopsy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Elizabeth thought she was up to date with her mammogram screenings but the doctor informed her otherwise.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: She said, Elizabeth it`s been two years, almost two and a half years since you had it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): The news she had breast cancer came as a complete shock.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: When my doctor did call me and told me that I had invasive ductal carcinoma, I didn`t know invasive or ductal meant but I knew carcinoma. I knew that was cancer. It was like the wind had been knocked out of me, like somebody punched me. I was not prepared for this. I was in the best shape of my life. I had just lost a good amount of weight. Breast cancer was not on my radar at all. It took me to my knees.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 I have no family history of breast cancer. That was another reason I was so blindsided by this. As hard as it is for somebody to tell you that you have cancer, as hard as that is to hear, it is impossible to say the words to your family. Honestly, I didn`t think I was going to be able to process it to say that sentence to my family. It took weeks and weeks for it to even sink in.\par \par \uc2 I just finished my third chemo treatment. I am just now grasping the fact that I have cancer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Cooking brought some comfort.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: I must have made meat loaf, mashed potatoes, and sort of green peas with cream sauce. This was what -- what my mother made for me. I don`t even know how many times I made that meal because it did, it brought me such comfort. I was diagnosed three weeks before mama passed away. So, it`s been a lot.\par \par \uc2 We are going to do these great easy salads that we can --\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): As a well-known chef and "TODAY" contributor, Elizabeth says she is passionate about cooking and taking care of others, but cancer has forced her to focus on herself.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: It`s not my nature. I bring the casseroles. I bring the food. I am the doer. I am the one that makes sure that everyone else is OK. And it`s easy to let that take over and you forget to take care of yourself. You just have to take it step by step, day by day. But you will, you will get there. You will get to the top of the mountain.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Yes, you will. Yes, you will, Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We are so happy that you are sitting here on this couch. We want to know all about it. But just in this moment, like, how are you -- tell me the prognosis. How are you feeling today?\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: You know, I am so unbelievably grateful. So, we went back to MD Anderson and that little tumor has shrunk 78 percent.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow. Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Come on.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: That was only -- that was after three chemo treatments.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: I mean, it`s unbelievable. And I`m just -- I am -- I`m so grateful that I`m almost -- I feel guilty at times, you know, because I have the most amazing support system. I have you all. I have family and friends. And, you know, an incredible health care. And, you know, it`s good. It`s good.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So, you`re halfway through your chemo. You`ll have your surgery. But -- I mean, to have that kind of, it`s working?\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: The feeling must be such, such a -- you know, just an inspiration?\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: It was unbelievable. And the lady that did my ultrasound, she -- her -- my mother`s name was Sheila (ph). Her name was Sheila (ph). She spelled it just like my mother did. And then her daughter`s name was Elizabeth and we have the same birthday.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: So, if you don`t tell me that my mama wasn`t all up in that room.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: But you know, the thing is, is that this is -- it is a journey. You know, it is a climb. And I`m leaning in and I`m learning. You know, I`m learning so much. And, you know, it`s -- I had worked myself up. When I was getting ready for that first chemo treatment, honey, fear had taken over. It was driving the bus. You would have thought that I was going to go in there and they were taking off all my fingers, all my toes. Like, I was petrified.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: And so, I got through that day and Luke was with me and I literally -- everything they did, I said, oh, that wasn`t so bad.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Well, I mean, that wasn`t so bad. So, by the time I got home, I called my girls and I said, look -- I said, fear is not going to drive this bus. It -- to dream, it`s -- you use the same exact brain, the same exact creativity to make those horrible nightmares as you do to dream. And I am not doing it any more.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: I am not holding back. You all think that I`ve always been 100 percent. There is something that I have been holding back and I`m not going to do it anymore. I am not fearing.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You said there are things that cancer could -- is teaching you that nothing else could have taught you. Because everyone knows that adage of God only gives you as much as you can handle.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You actually don`t believe that?\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: No, I don`t think that`s the case. I think it`s time for you to lean in and learn something new. And, you know, the lessons that I have learned -- I mean, it`s look for silver linings even in this mess.\par \par \uc2 There was this sweetest lady when I was at the clinic. And, you know, they give us these warm robes. So, it -- kind of, for five minutes you feel like you are at a spa, until they take your boob and they stick it into a vice grip. For that minute, you know -- and so, as she looked at me and she is sitting there with her bald head and sunken eyes, and she said, oh -- she said, aren`t these robes just the silver lining?\par \par \uc2 And I had just been diagnosed, and I thought silver lining my butt. We are sitting here, you know. And then it came to me. And then she said, you know, where you live? And I said, I live in Oxford. She said, bus -- but my sister-in-law lives here in Houston so I have a place to stay. She goes, another silver lining.\par \par \uc2 And it was almost like she was catching butterflies. I am not kidding you. And so, if you stay there in that silver lining, that silver lining is going to be the thing that you`re going to wrap yourself in. That`s all you`re going to see is the silver. And I`m telling you, these are the lessons that I would have -- I mean, and I know we talk about it and we say, you know. But to really believe --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Believe it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: -- and every day you are looking for the silver lining butterflies.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: One of your silver linings is standing over there. You talked about your tribe.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Your people. Who do we have?\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: So, I have my daddy, who I love more than life itself. And it`s his birthday tomorrow. And then I have Mindy and my sweet husband, Luke who -- let me tell you something, Luke looked at me in my first cancer treatment. And he looked over and I was sitting there and he had tears in his eyes when they went to put the first chemo drug in my body. And he looked at me and I know he was thinking, if only I could just take this and put this in me right now. He has been everything. You cannot even imagine.\par \par \uc2 And my girls. Oh, my gosh, and my friends.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Is that one of your brothers?\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: And my friends -- no. And that`s Molly. And we have Margie (ph) and Peter. And -- you know, I do. I have a whole tribe. I have a little boy that every day when he goes to school, he tells his dad to honk the horn and -- so that I know that they are praying for me that morning.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there is -- you cannot imagine. I have somebody who sent me a night light because I`d said that when the fear was taking -- driving the bus, I was up late nights. I mean, it was tough. And so, she sent me a night light that I could turn on when I was up scared at night. I mean, these are the people that I have.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I hope that people that, if you know somebody that has breast cancer or cancer or that`s struggling, please go out of your way. It doesn`t have to be much.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: A honk of the horn or a night light, anything.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And sometimes people think, well, you know what, they may want their family time. They may want their privacy. But reach out, right?\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Lean into them.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Lean in, yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Get under them.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Hold them up.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Hold them.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: And carry them up that mountain. I mean --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Call, don`t text.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Yes. And they did call me. You all they called me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Of course.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: And of course, when I was driving back from Memphis, we had been to the oncologist when you called, and I was like, oh God. Lord. Look, Hoda`s on the phone. Stop the car. Because we were about to go through a dead area. I was like, Hoda never calls me. Just stop the car. And then Savannah called. And it was just amazing.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, we --\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We love you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re our family.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You are our family.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re part of your tribe, too.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Well, that -- I have been looking forward to being back here with you all so much. And you are -- I mean, I am. I`m back with family. I`m with Debbie and my producer, who I -- who really, I mean, she is my family.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, we adore you. You need to -- all of this is, like, the most inspirational.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know. It really is.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I`m sure you helped a ton of people today. We love you.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: Go get your mammograms.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: I literally thought it was eight months. It had been two years people.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: It had been two years people.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You got it.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: You put it on your calendar right now. You stop what you`re doing. I`m serious.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We love you. We love you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We love you. By the way, Elizabeth wrote a beautiful and personal essay, shares more of her story, and you should read it. Check it out today.com. And we just love you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEISKELL: I love you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Al, over to you.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Silver lining butterflies. Oh, I`ll remember that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We love that.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That and hotter than the hinges of hell.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right. Let`s show you what we got going on. Heavy snow in the U.P. of Michigan today. Some showers in the northeast. Look for some wet weather also down through the Florida Peninsula. Heavy rain down through Southern Texas. Warm and dry out west. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: I`ve got to say, that was the best part of the morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: My goodness, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: But, yes. We love you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We really do.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Anyway, "POP Start."\par \par \uc2 KOTB: "POP Start" too.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK.\par \par \uc2 JONES: "POP Start" is good too.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. We`re going to try to give you some good stuff today.\par \par \uc2 First up, Robbie Coltrane. Tributes are pouring in after the beloved actor died at the age of 72 over the weekend. He was best known for his role as Hagrid in the "Harry Potter" movie franchise. His agent shared in a statement that he would, "Be best remembered for decades to come as Hagrid in the Harry Potter films. A role which brought joy to children and adults alike all over the world. Prompting a stream of fan letters every week for 20 years."\par \par \uc2 Coltrane`s "Harry Potter" co-star Daniel Radcliffe also paid tribute in a statement writing, Robbie was one of the funniest people I`ve met and used to keep us laughing constantly as kids on set. I feel incredibly lucky that I got to meet and work with him and very sad that he has passed.\par \par \uc2 All right. Next up, Carrie Underwood. As we mentioned on Friday, the country superstar is hitting the road for her "Denim and" -- I was going to say diamonds -- "Rhinestones" tour. Highlighting her greatest hits and new music from her latest album. Underwood kicked off her tour with a sold-out show in Greenville, South Carolina, over the weekend. Here`s a look at one of her epic opening night performances.\par \par \uc2 [08:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARRIE UNDERWOOD, SINGER: Yes, I`m one of those crazy angels. Tilted halo with a neon shine. Call me one of those crazy angels.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: Pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: She said she wanted to do something new and she certainly did. Underwood plans to make stops in 43 U.S. cities. That`s a lot of shows. Next up is Indianapolis, Indiana. You can catch the "Denim and Rhinestones" tour through March 20th through the 23rd.\par \par \uc2 All right. Next up, Savannah and Hoda. Rumor has it our favorite duo had some fun over the weekend, taking the stage at this year`s BravoCon. The fan of it celebrates some of Bravo`s biggest TV shows and reality stars. And you guys, you sat down with our pal, Andy Cohen, to ask him all the burning questions submitted by Bravo fans. Andy even recruited you to do the infamous shotski. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY COHEN, RADIO AND TELEVISION TALK SHOW HOST: OK. One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: How was it?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: How many did you do?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Just one.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: One, two, three. One, two, three.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I mean, it wasn`t even, like -- well, I don`t know. We`re like, yes, sure. Let`s bring it on.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Because it`s Friday and that`s the way --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: There you go. I love that.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: New "Steals & Deals" just ahead. But first your local news.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: We are back, it`s 8:30. It`s a beautiful Monday morning. We`re greeting our Rockefeller Center Plaza crowd. So many smiling faces, so many birthdays, anniversaries. We`re so happy that y`all came and that you`re joining us today.\par \par \uc2 AL ROKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: All right. And just ahead, Sheinelle, you got to go back to Philadelphia --\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: I did.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: -- home of the Eagles, Savanna`s Eagles, and saw some of the work you did during last night`s game.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I was so nervous. I`m like please win, please win. So they won, yes. So before their win over the Cowboys, I joined their team of groundskeepers and learn what goes into creating the perfect field. There`s truly an art to it. I can`t wait to share this story with you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Cool.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: All right. Also ahead awesome, guys, it`s cozy season already. Jill Martin wants to make sure we`re all prepared. So she brought Steals and Deals to keep warm inside and outside of your house.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And then Harry Smith, he`s taking us inside the new home of the iconic New York Philharmonic, how a few changes are making the sound even better for musicians and their audiences.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And then just ahead on the 3rd Hour of Today, our new Consumer Confidential about scams being on the rise. From Social Security to student loans, those bad guys are getting creative and getting their hands on our money. We`re going to tell you what to look out for and how we can protect ourselves.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Al, let`s check the weather. Looks like it`s like --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes it does.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: (Inaudible) some sprinkle, some wet weather here, but let`s show you what we`re looking at week ahead. Winter chill around the Great Lakes today, some winter storms coming on in, look for heavy rain down through Texas, warm out west. Midweek period rain and snow around the Great Lakes, record lows down to the south, record highs out west.\par \par \uc2 And then as we get toward the end of the week, we are looking at a morning chill in the Northeast in New England, nice and mild from the plains all the way down to Texas, some showers much needed rain moving into the Pacific Northwest. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Guys, we have a little giveaway. So, does anybody want tickets to Carrie Underwood?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Me, me. Me, me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I think I see a girls` trip here. Ladies -- is it your birthday?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The 28th.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: 28th? Well, happy early birthday. And where`s Allison? Allison, what do you think about some Carrie Underwood tickets?\par \par \uc2 ALLISON, AUDIENCE: I would love to.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 ALLISON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What`s your favorite?\par \par \uc2 ALLISON: "Temporary Home," Jesus Take the Wheel," (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She knows them all. Y`all from Tennessee?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED SPEAKERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. We are going to arrange some Carrie Underwood tickets for you guys, all right? Enjoy your time. Happy birthday.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We love you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I love it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We love you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You guys (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We got the real pic.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I mean, look at yourself. We`re happy for you. We love you, honeybunch. Oh my god, what a great day. What a great day.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Good. (Inaudible) rhinestones out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 COHEN: Ready to go.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up next, Sheinelle rolls up her sleeves. She goes inside the game in a big way, the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2  ground`s team. Sheinelle is now a member, and she`ll never think of a football field the same way again. But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221020eiah0008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc74}{\*\bkmkend toc74}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for October 16, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Amy Walter, Eugene Daniels, Maria Teresa Kumar, Pat  McCrory, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 9,567 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:17]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST (voice over): This Sunday, the battlegrounds. With the midterms now just 23 days away, the candidates in the Senate races that will determine party control are making their final pitch to voters and facing off on the debate stage, in Georgia...\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , GEORGIA REPUBLICAN SENATE NOMINEE: That was a lie, and I`m not backing down.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 , (D) GEORGIA: My opponent has a problem with the truth.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... Pennsylvania...\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ, PENNSYLVANIA REPUBLICAN SENATE NOMINEE: We should have had a debate already.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D) PENNSYLVANIA: I actually have a record in fighting crime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON, (R) WISCONSIN: We have to keep violent criminals in jail.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. MANDELA BARNES, (D) WISCONSIN: It is absurd when people say that I`m soft on crime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ll speak with two independents, Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  of Vermont and Utah`s independent Senate candidate, Evan McMullin. Plus, Trump`s testimony.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2 , (D) MISSISSIPPI & CHAIR, SELECT COMMITTEE ON THE JANUARY 6 ATTACK: He must be accountable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The January 6th committee issues a subpoena for former President Trump to testify about his role in the attack on the Capitol...\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING & VICE CHAIR, SELECT COMMITTEE ON THE JANUARY 6 ATTACK: We are obligated to seek answers directly from the man who set this all in motion.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... while new video reveals congressional leaders pleading for help...\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK & MAJORITY LEADER: Why don`t you get the president to tell them to leave the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... and new questions emerge on how the Secret Service handled advance warnings of the threat.\par \par \uc2 I`ll talk to Democratic congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  of Florida, who sits on the committee.\par \par \uc2 And political polarization, why the state of Wisconsin best represents the deep divide playing out in our national politics. My reporting on the ground behind what`s driving the red and blue divisions.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are Amy Walter, editor in chief of the Cook Political Report; Politico "Playbook" co-author Eugene Daniels; former North Carolina \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2 ; and Maria Teresa Kumar, president of Voto Latino.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. There are just over three weeks to go until Election Day. Nearly 2 million Americans have already voted, just another 120 million to go.\par \par \uc2 So with Republicans still favored to win control of the House, both parties are actually keeping their attention and money right now focused on the battle for control of the Senate, in particular these nine battleground states. These are the nine closest Senate races in the country because they`re trying to keep their top targets in play.\par \par \uc2 In order to win back control, Republicans have to flip one of the Senate seats currently held by the Democrats, Georgia, Arizona, Nevada or New Hampshire, while also holding onto all of their seats that are in play here. Pennsylvania, Ohio, North Carolina are open seats. Florida and Wisconsin feature incumbents.\par \par \uc2 On the Democratic side, Nevada Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is arguably the most vulnerable Democratic incumbent. Republicans are also trying to pick off Georgia Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . But they`ve struggled a bit as allegations about domestic abuse and now abortion seem to saddle Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Georgia now, by the way, has the highest price tag in the country of any mid-term race. More than $140 million has been spent on ads since the primary. By the way, that total is more than what both Bush and Gore spent in the year 2000, if you want to see how much funding has grown exponentially. So the number here will get even bigger, especially if this Georgia race goes into another overtime, as it happened two years ago. This time the runoff would be on December 6th.\par \par \uc2 On Friday night the candidates met in what may be -- may be their only debate, where Walker again denied that he paid for the mother of one of his children to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: As I said, that`s a lie. And I said that was a lie, and I`m not backing down. And you have Senator Warnock, people that would do anything and say anything for this seat. But I`m not going to back down because this seat is too important to the Georgia people for me to back down right now.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: You`ve been vocally pro-life, supporting a ban on abortions without exceptions. Would you support a complete ban on a national level? Thirty seconds.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: First of all, see, that`s not true, either. I said I support a -- a heartbeat bill, and I said that has exceptions in it.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: Do you believe there should be any limitations on abortion set by the government? And you have 60 seconds.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: I think that the women of this country and the women of this state woke up one summer morning and a core protection that they`ve known for 50 years was taken from them by an extremist Supreme Court. And I stand where I`ve said I stand in the past, that a patient`s room is too narrow and small and cramped a space for a woman, her doctor and the \uc2 United States government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, early voting in Georgia starts tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to a few of the Democratic opportunities here. Just two Republican incumbents, as I noted, are in battleground races.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:02]\par \par \uc2 That`s Wisconsin and Florida. But nominees that essentially were hand- picked by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in those three open races have made those states more competitive as well. The cycle`s second highest ad price tag is in the state of Pennsylvania. More than $95 million has been spent on ads just since the primary in May.\par \par \uc2 My colleague Dasha Burns had exclusive interviews this week with both Senate nominees. NBC News agreed to Democrat John Fetterman`s request to use a transcription program during the interview because he`s still experiencing some auditory processing issues from a stroke he had in May. Outside, though, of releasing a letter from his cardiologist on June 3rd, the campaign has denied additional requests for his medical records.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Don`t voters deserve to know your status now?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Being on -- in front of thousands and thousands of people and having interviews and getting around all across Pennsylvania, that gives everybody, and the voters decide, you know, if they think that it`s -- it`s really the issue.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz likes to make fun of me that I might miss a word, but, you know, he`s missed, you know, two words, and that is "yes" or "no" on the national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: One of your campaign staffers said that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  wouldn`t have had a stroke if he had, quote, "ever eaten a vegetable in his life." You`re a doctor. You`re a heart doctor. You of all people understand what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is going through, better than most. Why would you allow your campaign to mock him like that?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I have tremendous compassion for what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is going through. I mean, not only do I as a doctor appreciate the challenges, but I know his specific ailment because it`s a specialty area of mine.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Doesn`t the buck stop with you? Isn`t that what a leader accepts?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Oh, I accepted responsibility. And -- and I -- you know, I deal with issues as they come up. But he has his own set of issues. We should have had a debate already.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But would you ever talk to your patients like this?\par \par \uc2 OZ: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: In Wisconsin the race between Republican Senator Ron Johnson and Democratic Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes comes in at $76 million spent on ads, in just two months since the primary. This puts Wisconsin third on the overall spending list. Many of the ads have focused on the issue of crime, at least the ads coming from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And in a debate this week, those attacks by Johnson continued.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We need law enforcement. And the problem with the whole Defund movement, which he has been a big supporter of, it dispirits law enforcement. They`re having a hard time recruiting members.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: No police officers in this country were more dispirited than the ones who were present at the United States Capitol on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I immediately and forcefully and repeatedly condemned the violence on January 6th, unlike my opponent...\par \par \uc2 (AUDIENCE BOOING)\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: Audience, please.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: ... who basically just ignores the 570 riots, the 2,000 law enforcement officers injured during the summer riots. And he incited the Kenosha riots.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: He called those folks "patriots." He called them "tourists," the people who were beating up police officers in the United States Capitol, the people that were there to protect him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mandela Barnes was endorsed early in the primary by Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 . It helped vault Barnes to lead in that primary. Meanwhile Senator Sanders has been cautioning Democrats to stay as focused on economic issues in the final weeks of the race as the party has been on the issue of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 And Senator Sanders joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, I want to focus on your -- this was an op-ed you wrote about a week or so ago. And you`ve said this in other -- other programs. So walk me through what you`d be doing right now, you`re -- if you`re in one of these battleground races. You`ve said abortion rights should be front and center. How would you be talking about the economic issues?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, number one, what the Supreme Court did was totally outrageous. It is women who should control their own bodies, not the government.\par \par \uc2 Number two, too many people have fought and died to defend American democracy. We cannot let right-wing extremists undermine what is so important to this country, and that is the right of all people to participate in the political process.\par \par \uc2 Thirdly, and terribly important, we live in a moment, Chuck, in which we see an economy where the people on top are doing phenomenally well, while working people are struggling. You`ve got more income and wealth inequality today than any time in the history of the United States, three people owning more wealth than the bottom half of the people in our society. That is insane.\par \par \uc2 So what I think is Democrats should talk about the economy. I think they should contrast their views with the Republican positions. I believe, and most democrats believe, that at a time when half our people are living paycheck to paycheck, we should raise the minimum wage to a living wage. No Republicans support that.\par \par \uc2 I think we should make it easier for workers to join unions. Republicans don`t support that. I believe that, when you have billionaires start paying a nickel, in some cases, in federal income taxes, yeah, we should demand that the rich and large corporations start paying their fair share.\par \par \uc2 Now, what Republicans are saying, Chuck, which is quite amazing to me, is that, in the midst of these difficult economic times for seniors and for other people, you know what they say? "We`ve got to cut Social Security; we`ve got to cut Medicare; we`ve got to cut Medicaid."\par \par \uc2 [09:10:12]\par \par \uc2 I think that that is grotesque. And I think Democrats have got to hold them accountable for those reactionary positions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, do you accept the criticism that the American Rescue Plan passed in early 2021 contributed to the current inflation issue we`re dealing with now?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No, I don`t. Inflation right now, as I`m sure you know, Chuck, is an international problem. In Germany it is 10 percent; U.K., it is 10 percent; Canada, it is 7 percent. Inflation globally is caused by the pandemic and the break in supply chains. It is caused by, in my view, the war in Ukraine, obviously.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And it is also caused by incredible corporate greed.\par \par \uc2 And I hope everybody understands that, when you go to the gas tank, you fill up your car today, the oil companies are making huge profits.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: The food companies are making huge profits. They -- prescription drugs are high. Pharmaceutical industry is making huge profits. We`ve got to deal with that issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you think...\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And Republicans won`t, by the way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We have a majority of economists now that believe a recession in the next 12 months is likely. First of all, do you think the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is currently helping or hurting the situation?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I think they`re hurting the situation. I think that it is wrong to be saying that the way we`re going to deal with inflation is by lowering wages and increasing unemployment. That is not what we should be doing. This inflation thing is a real issue. It is a global issue. But at a time when working families are struggling and the people on top are doing phenomenally well, I don`t think you go after working people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you wouldn`t be raising interest rates anymore, at this point? The damage has already been done, in your view?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move to a couple of races, and I want to get your take on a few things. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in Pennsylvania, look, you had -- you had some health issues on the trail during your presidential race. You did -- you were asked to release more of your medical records. You didn`t release all of your records, but you did release quite a few letters from your doctors. And frankly, you were on the trail being who you are and going all over the country. I think folks saw that your health was in good shape.\par \par \uc2 Do you think Mr. Fetterman should be releasing more of his medical records, since he`s asking for a six-year term here?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, Chuck, you know, I don`t know enough about John`s medical situation. But I think the contrast is very clear. I do know \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . I`ve known him for years. And John is somebody who is going to be a real fighter for the working class of this country. He is prepared to take on the greed of corporate America. And I hope very much he joins us in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: In Wisconsin Mandela Barnes getting hit hard on the issue of crime. This -- you`ve heard the phrase "Defund the police." He`s not alone. A lot of Democrats are being hit with these ads. How would you be handling these attacks?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, what -- you know, obviously it`s -- it`s not true, the attacks on him. I know that Mandela believes very strongly that we need good law enforcement, non-racist law enforcement. And I agree with him on that. I think, at the end of the day, if we`re going to solve the crime problem; if we`re going to solve the terrible drug problem that we`re seeing all over this country, we`ve got to get at the root causes of those problems. And that means we have a better educational system, that we make sure that our young people have the decent jobs that they need.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Democrats are not spending a lot of resources and time on two races in particular, Ohio and Florida, those Senate races. Do you think that`s a mistake?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, I think that Ryan -- Mr. Ryan in Ohio is running a very strong campaign. And I`ve known him for years. And this is a guy who is prepared to stand up, and has been prepared to stand up for working-class people.\par \par \uc2 And again, to me, Chuck, all over this country, the issue is pretty clear. Are you going to support a party that wants to give more tax breaks to the rich, cut Social Security, Medicare and Medicaid, or are you going to support people who are prepared to stand up for working people?\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m not here to tell you that the Democrats are perfect. Believe me, they are not. But on virtually all of the issues, including climate change, the choice is pretty clear. Democrats are far, far more preferable in Florida, Ohio, all over this country. And I hope very much that we can retain control of both the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Senator, you have not -- during 2016 and in 2020 you made a big deal about wanting to court Trump voters. I`m curious your perspective on this now, in an era where a majority of Republican voters think the 2020 election was stolen and that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is somehow an illegitimate president.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:10]\par \par \uc2 Are these voters still worth courting, in your mind?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, I think there are some extreme right-wing voters who are racists, who are sexists, who are homophobes, xenophobes. No, and I don`t think you`re going to ever get them. But I also think there are millions of people in this country, working class people who look at Washington and they say, "You know what, I`m falling further and further behind; I can`t afford health care; I can`t afford to send my kids to college; I can`t afford the outrageous cost of prescription drugs. Who is listening to me?"\par \par \uc2 And I think what we need is a Democratic Party that has the guts to stand up to them and say, "Yeah, we`re going to take on the greed of the insurance companies and the drug companies and Wall Street."\par \par \uc2 And I think, if we do that, some of those people -- I`m not saying all -- will say, "You know what, I`m going to stand with the Democratic Party because, on these economic issues, they`re far preferable to right-wing Republicans."\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , the independent senator from Vermont, who does caucus with the Democrats. I imagine we`re going to see you on the campaign trail, a little bit, in the next three weeks. Maybe we`ll catch up with you again?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Yes, you will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let me turn now to the unexpectedly close Senate race in Utah, where another independent here named Evan McMullin, he`s taking on a two-term Republican senator, \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a poll this week, Lee is up by just 4 points, 41/37. A large number of voters are still undecided. It`s been that way in this polling, really, for months, in this race. Lee and McMullin will square off in their only scheduled debate tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 Unusually, Lee went on national television earlier this week begging Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , the other senator in Utah, to endorse his re-election. Romney has been pledging neutrality.\par \par \uc2 Evan McMullin joins me now. And for what it`s worth, we invited Senator Lee on the program as well, but he declined to appear for this Sunday. We hope to ask him again.\par \par \uc2 Mr. McMullin, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN, INDEPENDENT CANDIDATE FOR UTAH SENATE: Good to be with you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with what we heard from your opponent this week. Let me play what Senator Lee said on Fox News with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2 , (R) UTAH: I`m asking him right here again tonight, right now. Mitt, if you`d like to protect the Republican majority, give us any chance of seizing the Republican majority once again, getting it away from the Democrats who are facilitating this massive spending spree and a massive inflationary binge, please get on board; help me win re-election. Help us do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Have you spoken to Senator Romney about his neutrality? And do you expect him to stay neutral in this race?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Well, I haven`t spoken to him about it, but I deeply respect Senator Romney`s leadership in the Senate for Utah and for our country. And I respect and appreciate his decision to stay out of this race. And, you know, that`s about all I can say. I don`t know what his decision will be going forward. But, yes, I would expect him to stick with the decision that he`s made. But I haven`t spoken with him about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Lee is implying essentially that a vote for you is a vote for the Democratic -- Democrats to keep their majority. Is he right?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Look, no, because I am running as an independent. And look, I`m building a coalition of Republicans, Democrats, independents and members of third parties to better represent Utah and to help lead our country forward. And I`m not going to Washington to join a party or to caucus or to play the party power game. I`m going to represent our coalition. And that`s a commitment that I`ve made to our coalition, that I`ll maintain my independence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All six years? If you serve all six years, the entire time you`re in the Senate, you will not join either party`s caucus, giving them officially your vote?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: No, Chuck, I will not. I will maintain my independence. I will not caucus with either side.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Chuck, I think that this will give Utah a -- a -- added value of influence in the Senate that it just doesn`t have. Certainly, we have, in Senator Romney, someone who has gotten a lot done for our state and our country, working across party lines to get things done. With Senator Lee we get none of that. He sits on his hands until it`s time to vote no, and then he goes and complains about our country on cable news.\par \par \uc2 And I`m just not going to do that. I think that we`ve seen well enough over the past year or two especially that the senators in the chamber who are willing to act with greater independence, serving their constituents, standing up to party bosses, standing up to extremist factions and special- interest groups, they have the most influence in the chamber. They`re more influential, I think, even than the party bosses. And I want that for Utah. And I know we`ll do a great deal of good with it serving this way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, there`s easily a scenario that could happen where you win your seat; Republicans are sitting at 50 -- have 50 Senate seats; Democrats have 49; you`re the one. And I want to ask you this question. You tweeted this in January of 2021.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:01]\par \par \uc2 You said, "Not only should \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  never be majority leader again, he shouldn`t be minority leader, either. It`s time for something new."\par \par \uc2 This eventuality may be in your hands. The scenario I painted may make it where you decide whether \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is majority leader or minority leader. If you`re in that scenario, are you just going to stick with being independent and let the chips fall where they may and know that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is going to be majority leader?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Well, look, the parties are going to decide who they choose to lead them. I won`t be part of that as an independent. And whatever scenario, Chuck, the parties in Washington and others, they`re going to have to figure out what this means for them on their own. I`m committed to maintaining my independence. I`m building a cross-partisan coalition.\par \par \uc2 I also believe that our country just needs more independent leaders who will stand up to party bosses on both sides, the special-interest groups who own too many of our politicians, and also to the extremist factions that I think have far too much influence in Washington. I`m committed to doing that. I`ve made a commitment to this coalition that I`ll maintain my independence. And as I said, for the parties and the bosses in Washington, they`ll have to decide what this means on their own.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A couple of bills I want to ask you about, if you were in the Senate. The Inflation Reduction act. You have said there are some good things in it and there are some things you didn`t like. But at the end of the day, you`ve got to vote for -- sometimes you`ve got to vote on a compromise. Would you have voted for that legislation or not?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Not as written. There are things I like in it and other things that I didn`t. I didn`t like the fact that I don`t think it did much to actually reduce inflation, especially in the short term. And I don`t think our leaders are doing enough on that front. Here in Utah our inflation rate is worse than in most other states. My family and most others here are being crushed by inflation, and we need leaders and need a Senate who is going to focus on actually delivering more results to lower inflation.\par \par \uc2 I did like the fact that it -- it started to allow Medicare to negotiate on prescription drugs. We pay far more for prescription drugs than we need to in America. That comes as a result of people like my opponent, Senator Lee, taking lots of money from big pharma special interest groups and then preventing taxpayers from negotiating lower prescription drugs in a free market. And that`s got to end, and again, why I`m running to replace Senator Lee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the issue of abortion, in 2016, when you were running for president, you had said you`d want to see -- you wanted to see Roe v. Wade overturned. When you and I talked on a podcast a couple months ago, your concern was that the overturn was creating some extreme laws, and you were worried about those.\par \par \uc2 Do you think there needs to be federal legislation that deals -- that sets some sort of minimum standard? And would you support Lindsey Graham`s bill that I guess one would argue is attempting to do that, banning abortion after 15 weeks but allowing it up until then?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Well, look, for every -- any piece of legislation that has a chance of passing the Senate, I`ll give it a close look. But the reality is that most of these bills, if not all of them being introduced since the Supreme Court`s decision, have been messaging bills that have either been intended just to make a political point or, worse, to divide Americans.\par \par \uc2 Chuck, I`m committed to finding a more constructive way forward on this issue. It`s tearing the country apart, where extreme bills are being passed around the country, some of which my opponent has supported, which would not allow exceptions for the victims of rape or incest, even, for example. I think that`s wrong. And we`ve got to stand up to those extremes. But more importantly, we`ve got to find a more constructive way forward. And that`s about making contraception more available, doing more to support women, children and families, and improving education on this matter. And that`s where I`ll focus my time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I want to just close -- one more time, there is no scenario where, if you win this Senate seat, you will caucus with the Democrats, if it means they would be in the majority?\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I will not caucus with Democrats or Republicans. I`m going to maintain my independence because I think our country needs that, and certainly our state needs that. I`ve made that commitment. And for party bosses and others in Washington, they`re going to have to figure out what this means for them. But I`m committed to maintaining my independence, and that is what I`m going to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Evan McMullin, the independent candidate from Utah, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us. Stay safe on the trail.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, the January 6th Committee voted to issue a subpoena to former President Trump. I`m going to speak with a committee member, Congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  of Florida, about why they waited until now to call him to testify.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:26:40]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. In its final hearing before the midterms, the January 6th Committee voted to subpoena former President Trump, and it prompted a 14-page response, which didn`t include whether he was actually going to comply with the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2  of Florida is a member of the committee, and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman Murphy, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2  (D-FL): Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to start with something you said yourself on Thursday. I`m going to put it up on screen. You said this, "The big lie, the pressure campaigns against state officials, the pressure campaign against the Department of Justice and his vice president, the fake electors, summoning the mob -- all of this demonstrates President Trump`s personal and substantial role in the plot to overturn the election."\par \par \uc2 You said this on Thursday. But, arguably, by the end of July, your committee had proven all of this, and it, sort of, raises the question, why wasn`t the Trump subpoena issued at the end of the summer, in July or August? Why now?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, as you know, we have been very methodical about our investigation. And we`ve been gathering additional information. And we`ve gotten new information, even since the July hearing. And we have always pursued this investigation, calling in whoever we need to, as the information and the facts detail. And so that`s why you see that we have asked the central figure in the effort to overturn the 2020 election to turn over documents as well as provide sworn testimony.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If he doesn`t comply, are you going to ask the full House to -- to send a criminal referral to the Justice Department to force him to comply?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I won`t engage in any hypotheticals at this moment, as the subpoena hasn`t yet even been served. But what I will say is that, with previous subpoenas, what you`ve seen the committee do is be very deliberate and take the response to our subpoenas on a case-by-case basis. And I imagine that we will also do that, because we understand the seriousness of the charge of our committee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why not subpoena Vice President Pence?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think that`s comparing apples to oranges. The former president was the perpetrator, the central figure in orchestrating the efforts to overturn the 2020 election, whereas Vice President Pence was somebody who was a key figure in stopping that effort from being successful. And he, through his counsel, has been engaged with the committee. And we will continue to engage with him and make a determination, if necessary, in the future, as it relates to the former vice president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, when you think about former President Trump, former Vice President Pence, I assume, if you had Pence`s word on what happened, in the conversations between him and the president, that he`d be a bit more of a credible witness than the former president. So why not put him under oath?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, we`ve had quite a few people who worked for former Vice President Pence appear before our committee and provide us the information they had of those conversations.\par \par \uc2 At this point, we are interested in hearing from the former president so that he may respond. What I will also note, though, is that whether people supply with the subpoena and come before our committee or not, it`s not as if they are absolved of their role in the January 6 violent attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:06]\par \par \uc2 It`s just that other people will articulate what their actions were and what their motivations were.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Secret Service -- for me in your hearing this week, the Secret Service obviously did not come out looking very good. Do we need an additional special investigation, special inquiry into the actions of the Secret Service, not just in and around January 6, but the decision -- when the former head of his detail, Mr. Ornato, became the former president`s deputy chief of staff -- I guess in short, did the Secret Service become a rogue agency?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: As a part of our investigation, we`ve looked at all of the law enforcement agencies` response to January 6th. And as you know, we are still going through tens of thousands of documents that the Secret Service have turned over recently.\par \par \uc2 I look forward to us calling back in some of those Secret Service officials who have knowledge about what happened and putting them under oath this time, now that we have additional information, to gather their perspectives. And then as far as recommendations, that would be included in the report.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are we going to see this report -- I know we`re not going to see the report before Election Day now. And we could have a debate about whether the voters should, sort of, see this before Election Day. But are we going to see a report before the end of the calendar year? I mean, you`re under a clock here, as I assume you have to turn in something to us, right?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: You will see a report before the end of the calendar year.\par \par \uc2 But let me just say that this isn`t about the next election this fall. This is about whether the sanctity of American elections here on out will be marred with violence and with fraud, because in a case where that happened, these people were not held accountable. And so, that`s the purpose of this committee, is to ensure that we tell the full truth, allow government officials to make changes to the system, to improve our guardrails, allow the American people to make better decisions about who they elect, and also to allow DOJ to -- encourage DOJ to do their job.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Realistically, how much more time do you think the committee would need to finish this investigation up? Look, January 3rd is January 3rd, I get that. But if that deadline weren`t there, how much more time would the committee like?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, there is so much information out there. And if we were allowed to proceed, I think that we should. Because there are a lot of processes and institutional changes that need to be made so that we can guard against anybody, irrespective of political party, ever attempting to overturn a U.S. election again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So bottom line is this investigation isn`t done even if the committee is forced to end on January 3rd?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: I think that we will present a report. And what I look forward to is seeing our recommendations be implemented. And if you`ll note, the 9/11 recommendations took several different administrations, as well as different congresses, to be implemented across almost over a decade. And I imagine and I hope that that will happen with the recommendations that come out of the report we produce before the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Of course, we hope we can implement these changes before the democracy itself is at risk again.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 , Democrat from Florida. Thanks for coming out and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, the issues of crime and abortion. They are dominating the debates and the airwaves in the contests that are going to decide Senate control. The panel is next to discuss.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:21]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 And yes, it`s a good one. Amy Walter, the editor-in-chief of "The Cook Political Report". We have "Politico Playbook" co-author Eugene Daniels here. Former North Carolina Governor \uc2 Pat McCrory\uc2  is joining us for the first time as an official NBC News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Welcome aboard, sir.\par \par \uc2 PAT MCCRORY, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Maria Teresa Kumar is also an NBC contributor, and she`s president of Voto Latino. But this is not your first rodeo with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. I actually want you guys to, I want to -- each party thinks they`ve identified the Achilles` heel of the other party. Crime, Republicans hitting Democrats. Abortion, Democrat hitting Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So I want to look at the defenses. Here`s how -- in the three big races -- how Democrats are handling the crime issue in debates and interviews in Wisconsin, Pennsylvania and Georgia. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): I have supported our police officers. I`ve called them and I`ve prayed with their families, like those officers lost in Cobb County. One thing I have not done, I`ve never pretended to be a police officer, and I`ve never, I`ve never threatened a shoot-out with the police.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. MANDELA BARNES (D), WISCONSIN: One hundred forty officers injured, one crushed in a revolving door, another hit in the head with a fire extinguisher, another stabbed with a metal stake. So this talk about support for law enforcement, it`s not real. LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA: We funded the police. We funded the police and created a strong partnership between the police and the community. So I`m the only candidate that I`m actually running on my record on crime, whereas Dr. Oz has never done anything about crime except going around and running a bunch of commercials, lying about my record.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Amy, what`s interesting to me is that there are three different ways that they are handling the crime attacks there, right? Mandela Barnes going to January 6, Warnock sort of hitting a little bit at Walker, and then Fetterman going, "Hey, I was the mayor. I`ve actually fought crime."\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Right, I`ve actually been ---\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah, who`s doing it best?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Which is consistent -- which is consistent also with their themes of their campaigns, right? In Wisconsin, it`s, Ron Johnson`s not who you think he is, or, he`s looking out for interests that are not your own. He says he`s protecting you. He`s really looking out for himself. Or in the case when law enforcement needed his help, he wasn`t providing it.\par \par \uc2 So it`s part of a bigger, stronger theme. But Democrats have known that the crime issue was a challenge for them since the very beginning of this election. They talked about it a lot after 2020. The very first ads run in a special election in New Mexico, special election for the House in a very Democratic seat, what did the Democrat do? One of her first ads was having sheriffs and police officers standing behind her.\par \par \uc2 So this was -- they`ve been playing defense on this for quite some time. And they`re hoping that in the last few weeks of this election, people are going to be spending as much time looking at the weaknesses of their Republican opponents than on the challenges that they have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Eugene, who`s done this best? I feel like Warnock`s been more aggressive at inoculation here, really than Fetterman and Barnes, which may be why those two races are closer.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah. I think Warnock, right? You look at Warnock, his ability to stick to his guns, so to speak, on this issue. And as Amy was saying, it connects with everything else that he`s been talking about, right?\par \par \uc2 When he said, I call and I pray, that is a reminder to people that, I am a reverend at Ebenezer Baptist Church in Atlanta, Georgia, right? That is a callback, and it`s on purpose.\par \par \uc2 And that is one of the reasons why he has been winning the whole time. Even before the abortion allegations for Walker, he has been winning this race. That`s where that`s going to go.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Maria Teresa, Mandela Barnes -- I think, arguably you could`ve seen this coming -- he didn`t do as much inoculation as Warnock did.\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: He didn`t. But I do think that he was reminding folks in his last debate -- I don`t think enough people watched -- but in his last debate, the difference between he and Ron Johnson.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:04]\par \par \uc2 One of the conversations we`re not having is that this is an opportunity for the Democrats to own police enforcement. And I say this because Ron Johnson on that stage said that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was planting evidence against him. And he`s talking about the larger picture of when the \uc2 FBI\uc2  went into Mar-a- Lago and, you know, legally took away all of President Trump`s archives.\par \par \uc2 And all of a sudden, these Republicans and Republican leadership flipped on it, and said it was an \uc2 FBI\uc2  sting job. That is not the party of law enforcement. And if anything, it`s undermining a really basic institution.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: But let`s be -- let`s really clear. Like, Fetterman, and in the race -- why am I blanking on the candidate?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes, thank you -- Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, they both have records that are liberal. And they can be used to make them look out of sync. This is not random.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to bring Pat in here, because we had a mass shooting in Raleigh over the last three days. And you and I were talking before the show. You said, you know, what`s been interesting is you haven`t seen Cheri Beasley, the Democratic nominee, she hasn`t hit Budd on guns or some of these things, where maybe there`s some vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Well, as a former mayor, it`s one of the issues I ran on for 14 years as mayor of Charlotte. And I split crime into three areas: one is the riots, mainly by anarchists who want to destroy things, the small groups. And at the time, Republicans had the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Until January 6th?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: That`s what I`m about to bring up.\par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and them were bailing out Hollywood stars, bailing out rioters who were burning down police stations.\par \par \uc2 But then January 6th, Trump wants to pardon rioters who attacked police. We break even on that.\par \par \uc2 Then we have the mass shootings. I`m shocked that the Democrats aren`t bringing up guns. But everyday crime, the Democrats are on their heels. And they deserve to be on their heels. You look at in cities, in LA, San Francisco, my own city, the Democrats are weak on DAs and enforcing crime.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We could keep going on crime, but I want to pivot to the Achilles` heel issue on the other side, and it is abortion.\par \par \uc2 Here`s some answers how Walker, Johnson and Oz are handling the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE NOMINEE: I`m a Christian, but I`m also representing the people of Georgia, and that`s who I represent. So what the people of Georgia stand for, I`m going to stand with them.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): I proposed a one-time, single-issue referendum to decide at what point does society has the responsibility to protect life?\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE NOMINEE: I`m giving you a bigger answer than yes or no. I`m telling you I don`t want any federal rules limiting what states do with abortion. It should be up to the states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Eugene, we`re all over the place here. Even Evan McMullin kind of like, maybe I`ll do -- I mean, nobody knows on this issue what to do if you`re on the -- what was originally the pro-life side of this issue.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Right. That was always going to happen. We talked about this on "Meet the Press" last year when we were talking about this issue. And it was very clear that they had caught the car. They were going to catch the car, and what was going to happen after?\par \par \uc2 And this is what happens, kind of this hodgepodge of different ideas of what voters might want to hear at that time. And you know, when you look at abortion versus economy, versus crime, versus -- this is a very choose your-own-adventure election, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Isn`t it? Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: People can just pick and choose whatever they want, even within the issue themselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The five swing voters, what do you think they care about: crime or abortion?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right. Although what we`re seeing too, the Kaiser Foundation had polling on this that found if you`re in a state with abortion restrictions --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You care about it more.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: - you are much more motivated than if you`re in a state that doesn`t.\par \par \uc2 And so if you`re in a traditional blue state, Oregon, California, et cetera, you`re less motivated by it than some of these other states.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I`m just going to pause the discussion. We`re going to come right back.\par \par \uc2 Up next, though, why the state of Wisconsin best represents our deep divide that plays out in our national politics. A little taste of my reporting from the ground and what`s driving the red and blue divisions in a state that I like to call patient zero for polarization.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:46:49]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 It is no secret the country is polarized. All you have to do is look at Washington by the numbers.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  here, 50/50. The House is almost as close, 220-212. And here`s a number that should really be a bit disturbing to folks -- believe it or not, 44 states have voted for the same party for president in five of the last six elections. Take a look at the six states that don`t fit that category.\par \par \uc2 So to better understand these national divisions, I traveled to Wisconsin, a state that truly represents political polarization in our country, and what we`ve undergone in recent years. I sat down with two former Wisconsin governors of opposite parties who both knew how to win big in this divided state back in the `90s and the early aughts.\par \par \uc2 In 1994, incumbent governor \uc2 Tommy Thompson\uc2 , he won by more than 35 percentage points and all but one of Wisconsin`s 72 counties. And this came in between two presidential wins in the state for a Democrat -- \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  at the time. In 2002, Democrat Jim Doyle`s margin was narrower than Thompson. But he still carried 43 counties, including many in the rural north of the state, something that would be basically unheard of in this current political climate.\par \par \uc2 So what`s changed? What does it tell us about the nation as a whole?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOMMY THOMPSON\uc2  (R), FORMER WISCONSIN GOVERNOR: Jim and I, you know, are friends, but I would never -- in modern-day politics -- I would not want to debate him. I wouldn`t even want to talk to him because I`m afraid somebody from my right would run against me. Jim would feel the same way. Somebody from the \uc2 George Soros\uc2  left would come against him.\par \par \uc2 So we have become paralyzed because we have redistricted to such an extent that a Republican state`s going to be a Republican state, a Democrat - except on a statewide ballot -- but individual districts. And that has become the partisan parochialism that it is today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sir, would you disagree with anything he just said there?\par \par \uc2 JAMES DOYLE (D), FORMER WISCONSIN GOVERNOR: No, I agree. But I would add this: One of the reasons Wisconsin is so much the center of this now is you look at the demographics of the state and the big divisions that have happened in the country -- rural and small town against cities; big, expanding economies like Madison against rural economies that are struggling -- those have happened all over.\par \par \uc2 It`s just in Wisconsin when you add those up, it`s a 50/50 state. So, you know, I was the attorney general when Governor Thompson was governor, and we certainly had our fights. But there were times that he would win 60 percent of the vote at the top of the ballot and then the next line down would be me and I`d win 60 percent of the vote. That is never going to happen again--\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Fifty-nine percent. You`ve never gotten 60.\par \par \uc2 DOYLE: I got 60 in that --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s now, there are no swing voters. It`s two states in one. That`s what Dan Balz calls it. It`s really two states, right?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: It has become so partisan and so divided that I would have to say it`s almost two states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So to give you an idea of where things stand today, check out Wisconsin`s nationally-watched race for governor. It is almost dead even.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, you want more of my reporting from Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 [09:50:01]\par \par \uc2 Check out our latest episode of Meet the Press Reports, "Wisconsin: Ground Zero for Polarization." It`s on demand right now on Peacock, \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , or MeetThePress.com.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, we`re going to look at the role former President Trump is playing in the midterms and beyond.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Well, Mr. Trump had quite a few legal challenges this week. He chose to respond to the subpoena the January 6th Committee hearing. He didn`t say what he would do, but he did write this, complaining about a lack of one focus of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 He said: It was indeed one of the largest crowds I`ve ever spoken before -- talking about his speech on the Ellipse.\par \par \uc2 It was a very wide swath, stretching all the way back to the Washington Monument. The massive size of this crowd and its meaning has never been a subject of your committee.\par \par \uc2 It is true, Amy, that they have never investigated the size --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: The size.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- of the January 6th crowd.\par \par \uc2 I -- you know -- it`s sort of, it`s sort of I guess not shocking that he came into office talking about his crowd sizes and he`s still complaining about his crowd sizes.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Well, he would like the 2022 election to continue to be about him, which, of course, Republicans would like it not to be about him. And I think the only thing that is helping Republicans right now is that while he is still trying to put himself in the center of the conversation, he`s really not. This is in the background.\par \par \uc2 I think January 6th, especially what happened this week, didn`t generate the kind of headlines and the kind of wall-to-wall coverage --\par \par \uc2 [09:55:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Subpoenas wasn`t a headline enough for you--\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Subpoena, no. I think voters expect, of course, he`s going to get subpoenaed. If they said, we`re indict -- we believe that he should be indicted. We know that the real issue here is, until there is some legal action taken, this is still all background noise.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We got defamation testimony this week in that defamation lawsuit that apparently the former president, Jean Carroll, that, that he`s worried about. That happens Wednesday, Eugene.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yeah. I mean, it`s just over and over and over and over again.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Right?\par \par \uc2 It`s January 6th. It`s Mar-a-Lago. It`s this defamation case.\par \par \uc2 We talk to Republicans, and you probably would feel the same way, it`s that they`re like, it`s just too much chaos, and, we don`t want -- we want you gone. Please go away, and he wants to be at the center of attention all the time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Well, I`ve got to play for you what \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  said earlier this week, because he said something that both seems obvious and yet I want to say naive at the same time.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL RYAN\uc2 , FORMER SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: I think Trump`s unelectability will be palpable by then. We all know that he`s so much more likely to lose the White House than anybody else running for president on our side of the aisle. So why would we want to go with that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m old enough to remember, Pat, when that was Scott Walker`s argument in 2015 about why people need to rally around somebody other than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Well, there are about 20 Republican -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: - candidates for the presidency that are running right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Who really believe this too, right?\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Who really believe this, too, and they can`t say it because they don`t want to upset the core 30 percent who are with Trump regardless.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, the Democrats are running away from Biden in this election, too. They do not want -- the Democrats don`t want Biden on the stage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, no, no, no. Oregon Democrats do.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Yeah, and the Oregon governor`s race is now close--\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know. Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Democrats don`t want Biden or Harris either, and Republicans frankly don`t want Trump. And they`re the ones trying to consume the TV time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I don`t think Biden`s actually --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- White House seems to be fine --\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Yeah. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- with this. I`ve talked to folks at the White House like, hey, we`re all on the same message. We`ll go to Oregon and Florida? Okay.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: It doesn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: You tell us where to go. But, I also think that with Biden, his challenge is that he hasn`t been able to communicate to the American people what he`s done. I mean, we can say that the difference between \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the difference within Biden is Biden is a policy president. So he has - - he`s going to make fundamental changes for a generation.\par \par \uc2 And we`re talking about student loan relief. We`re talking about bringing back manufacturing. And the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 But the difference with Trump is that his consequence is not policy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: It`s his legacy of undermining elections.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Biden --\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: And it`s not just him, it`s not him, but he actually has a cadre of individuals right now seeking office that are election deniers.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: But -- but -- but --\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: And that legacy is far more dangerous for a modern-day president than any other thing --\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: But Biden can`t go on--\par \par \uc2 TODD: Last word.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: -- because it believes in undermining our democracy.\par \par \uc2 MCCRORY: Biden can`t go on TV because 401(k)s are down 20-25 percent, because the price of eggs is more than we`ve ever paid in our lifetime, because of gas is going up. The Democrats don`t want Biden around.\par \par \uc2 Republicans right now, the last four weeks, they just want the super PAC ads--\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Democrats do want one Democratic president campaigning for them. It`s the former Democratic president, who we`re going to see a lot of I think in the last three weeks anyway.\par \par \uc2 Terrific panel, terrific show. Just 23 days.\par \par \uc2 You ready, Amy? Are you ready?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Always ready. Always ready.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Remember, you can start voting in many states.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So get out there and vote. We`re expecting over 110 million, if not more.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020221016eiag00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc75}{\*\bkmkend toc75}\b \uc2 The World-Famous Hollywood Sign Gets A Touch Up Just In Time For A Huge Anniversary\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Peter Alexander, Steve Patterson \par \uc2 1,656 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER: Well, if you`ve ever traveled to Los Angeles, you`ve probably looked high on the Hill to see that world famous Hollywood sign.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER: You almost don`t have to travel to Los Angeles to have seen that sign.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is right, everywhere.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s like ubiquitous. A big anniversary is approaching for the sign and now it`s getting a pretty nice touch up.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Steve Patterson grabbed a paintbrush and got to work.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE PATTERSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Every great city has a symbol, representing the ideals of its people. In Hollywood, it`s a sign. Nine giant letters spelling out one iconic notion. This is where dreams are made.\par \par \uc2 Believe it or not, the Hollywood sign is about to turn 100 years old, it was meant to last just 18 months.\par \par \uc2 The sign went up back in 1923 as Hollywoodland, a billboard to sell real estate, but it caught people`s attention and stuck around.\par \par \uc2 In the 40s, it was donated to the city of Los Angeles and the land was removed.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ZARRINNAM, HOLLYWOOD SIGN TRUST CHAIR: The sign came to mean something different. It came to mean the Hollywood industry as a whole.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Through the years, the old wooden sign decayed, even losing one of its O`s. So, in 1978, the old one was knocked down and a new wood rose in its place. Now, it`s getting a fresh coat of paint ahead of its big birthday.\par \par \uc2 ROGER MARTINEZ, DUGGAN AND ASSOCIATES SUPERINTENDENT: It honestly feels like you know, you`re kind of like one of the stars up here. All right, let`s go.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Roger Martinez, he`s overseeing the repainting process. He took me up to lend a hand.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Ready to help with paint?\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Use the roller yourself.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: I would never dream that I would be putting paint on the Hollywood sign.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Dreams come true.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Dreams do come true.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Remember, this is Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: This is Hollywood, this is where it happens, right?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: I`m the guardian of the Hollywood sign.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Jeff Zarrinnam runs the Hollywood sign trust.\par \par \uc2 ZARRINNAM: People have grown accustomed to seeing the sign. But it also has a meaning in their hearts. And they relate to the sign to their favorite actor, their television show, their movie, and that`s what it means to people around the world.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: In a place where dreams of stardom are made and dashed, the message high up here endures. A symbol of the stars shining down on Tinseltown for generations to come.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:10]\par \par \uc2 For TODAY, Steve Patterson, NBC News, Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Talk about dreams coming true. So cool Steve got to help paint.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, some people dream of painting the Hollywood sign, others dream of being in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE) Still to come, it`s time to shop by. Retailers are starting their holiday sales now and how you can take best advantage of all those deep discounts. We are back with that after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still to come on TODAY, the passion for Pickleball, why everyone including \uc2 NFL\uc2  and \uc2 NBA\uc2  MVPs are getting in on the game.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Plus, our first look at the new season of "The Crown". How will season five play out? That`s coming up in POPStart.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Those stories and more after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice-over): Good morning. Debate duel. Democratic incumbent senator Raphael Warnock going toe to toe with Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  last night in Georgia, the two battling each other on everything from the economy to policing. Walker also facing questions over reports he paid the mother of one of his children to have an abortion and pressured her to have a second.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I`ll be a senator that protects life. And I say that was a lie. And I`m not backing down.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): We`re live in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Ready, set, holiday shop. It`s only October but with inflation at near record highs, worried retailers are busting out those deep discounts nice and early this year. Hoping to get you in the door well before Black Friday. The deals you should take advantage of now.\par \par \uc2 And Pickleball for all.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED MALE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED MALE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED MALE: Oh.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): The game once thought to be for the senior set now being picked up by new legions of fans, even pro sports players. What`s behind the craze that`s sweeping the nation?\par \par \uc2 Today, Saturday, October 15, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hi (INAUDIBLE) from Bloomington, Illinois. We`re sending out our love on the "Today Show."\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED MALE: To our kids in New Jersey, Missouri, Illinois and Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED FEMALE: We love you.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED MALE: Coming to you from our home the Bolts house.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED FEMALE: Here in New York City on "Today."\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED MALE: Go Air Force.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED FEMALE: Go Tampa Bay.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED SPEAKERS (in unison): Hello, (INAUDIBLE) with on "Today Show."\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good out for that, crew, huh?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Join there Friday night.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And then Saturday morning --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: -- as we get started.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That first there.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Thanks for being with us. So, we talked pickleball, we`re not talking fermented cucumber here. OK, folks, this is going to be -- this is like 40 percent more people who play in this sport that just a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, and you have celebrities and \uc2 NBA\uc2  stars and \uc2 NFL\uc2  stars. So.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`ll break up the rockets later.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. All right. We`ll see how you do.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: That`s (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. But first, we do want to get to today`s top story.\par \par \uc2 The first and only debate for the Senate candidates in Georgia, incumbent Democrat Raphael Warnock facing off against Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . The fate of this race could determine whether the Democrats retain control of the Senate or the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ellison Barber is in Savannah, Georgia with more. She was at the debate last night.\par \par \uc2 Ellison Good morning. What were the big takeaways?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, good morning to both of you. Both campaigns are declaring victory this morning. Walker`s campaign saying their candidate dominated Warnock saying there showed Georgians why they elected him in the first place. This was a highly anticipated debate, and there were quite a few heated moments on the stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): Incumbent Senator Raphael Warnock and Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  trading blows on a range of topics in their first and likely only debate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: He says no exceptions, even in the case of rape, incest, or the life of the mother. I think that`s extreme.\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): Walker who once said he`d support an all-out ban on abortion, attacking Warnock for being a pastor who supports abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: And instead go abort and obeyed Him. Why are you not baptizing those babies?\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): Clarifying his current stance.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: -- supporting the Georgia heartbeat bill because that`s the bill of the people from Governor Kim. And I say that has exceptions in it.\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): And denying accusations that he paid for the mother of one of his children to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: And I`ll be a senator that protects life. And I say that was a lie. And I`m not backing down.\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): Throughout the debate, Warnock tried to emphasize his work in Washington.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: I passed the single largest tax cut, contrary to what my opponent is suggesting for middle and working class families in American history.\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): And paint Walker as unqualified.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: I`ve never pretended to be a police officer. And I`ve never, I`ve never threatened a shootout with the police.\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): Walker pulling out what appeared to be some sort of badge, only to be admonished by the moderator.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have a prop.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNDENTIFIED FEMALE: That is not allowed, sir. I asked you to put that prop away.\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): Walker tried to focus on the economy.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: You have to blame this administration and Senator Warnock, because within two years, this inflation has gotten worse.\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): And tie Warnock to a president whose approval rating among Georgia voters is 37 percent.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Can you tell me why he voted me \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  96 percent of the time if you`ve seen it for Georgia?\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voice-over): Every topic Georgia voters have the final word and early voting begins Monday and race that could determine which party controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And Georgia and ordered to avoid a runoff a candidate has to pass a 50 percent threshold and recent polling. These candidates are in a statistical tie. So there is a chance we might not know who wins come election day. Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 [08:05:13]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, early voting gets underway on Monday. Ellison Barbara, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221019eiaf000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc76}{\*\bkmkend toc76}\b \uc2 Georgia Is On The Minds Of Voters Everywhere With The Senate Candidates Facing Off In Their First And Likely Only Debate Last Night\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Ellison Barber \par \uc2 1,110 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:00]\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning, thanks for starting your weekend with us.\par \par \uc2 Georgia is on the minds of voters everywhere with the Senate candidates facing off in their first and likely only debate last night.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER. NBC NEWS ANCHOR: The high-stakes race there could decide control of the Senate. It`s October 15. This is "TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voiceover): A Bruising Night In The Peach State, Georgia`s Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  face to face with his Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the two battling one another on everything from the economy to policing. Walker`s own personal life also under scrutiny with questions whether he paid for an ex to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: I`ll be a senator that protects life. And I say that was a lie and I`m not backing down.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Demanding Answers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think I heard three or four gunshots,\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hear people screaming.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: New 911 calls released overnight reveal the chaos and confusion during a shooting rampage that left five people dead in Raleigh, North Carolina. A 15-year-old suspect in custody as policed and grieving family members try to figure out why this tragedy happened.\par \par \uc2 Cat Scratch Fever. Some conservative political candidates and elected officials under fire this morning for spreading the false claim that schools are providing kitty litter boxes for students who identify as cats, this morning, dissecting misinformation.\par \par \uc2 All that plus there`s no Hogwarts without Hagrid.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE COLTRANE, SPANISH ACTOR: Rubeus Hagrid, keeper of keys engravings at Hogwarts.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Robbie Coltrane, the actor best known for playing the lovable giant in the Harry Potter movies died on Friday at 72. How his co-stars are remembering him?\par \par \uc2 Today, Saturday, October 15, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is "TODAY" with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Welcome to today. Thanks for joining us on this Saturday morning. I think there is a generation of kids and dare I say adults who feel like they grew up with that gentle half-giant Hagrid. Robbie Coltrane is going to be missed.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: He sure is. Those who knew him described him as boisterous and eccentric. And we will look back at his life at his legacy. Yes. Well, we do have a lot to cover this morning, especially in the world of politics with questions swirling about whether former President Trump will answer that subpoena to appear before the January 6 committee Mr. Trump did respond with a scathing letter slamming the committee but never actually said if he would appear. Meantime, the Republican Senate candidate in Georgia backed by President Trump, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , appeared on stage with incumbent Democratic candidate \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  for their only debate overnight.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Georgia is one of at least three crucial battleground states with very high-stakes Senate races at stake there. They could determine whether the Democrats retain control or if the Republicans will take it away. So we want to begin this morning with NBC`s Ellison Barber. She`s in Savannah with a wrap-up of last night`s big showdown, Ellison, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Peter. This is a closely watched race. This debate was highly anticipated. This morning, both campaigns are declaring victory. It wasn`t just Herschel Walker`s first time debating Senator Warnock, it was his first time debating ever, which led to some fiery moments on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voiceover): Incumbent senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  trading blows on a range of topics in their first and likely only debate.\par \par \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 , DEMOCRATIC SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: He says no exceptions, even in the case of rape, incest, or the life of the mother. I think that`s extreme.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Walker, who once said he`d support an all-out ban on abortion, attacking Warnock for being a pastor who supports abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: And instead of aborting those babies, why you`re not baptizing those babies?\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Clarifying his current stance.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I support the Georgia heartbeat bill because that`s the bill of the people from Governor Kim. And I said that has exceptions in it.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And denying accusations that he paid for the mother of one of his children to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: And I`ll be a senator that protects life. And I say that was a lie and I`m not backing down.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Throughout the debate, Warnock tried to emphasize his work in Washington.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: I pass the single largest tax cut, contrary to what my opponent is suggesting for middle and working-class families in American history.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And paint Walker as unqualified.\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: I`ve never pretended to be a police officer. And I`ve never -- I`ve never threatened a shootout with the police.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:00]\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Walker pulling out what appeared to be some sort of badge only to be admonished by the moderator.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have a prop.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That is not allowed, sir.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: It is.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I asked you to put that prop away.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Walker tried to focus on the economy.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: You have to blame this administration and Senator Warnock because, within two years, disinflation has gotten worse.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And tie Warnock to a president whose approval rating among Georgia voters is 37 percent.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Can you tell me why he voted with Joe Biden 96 percent of the time if he was standing for Georgia?\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Every topic, Georgia voters have the final word, and early voting begins Monday in a race that could determine which party controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: In Georgia, a candidate has to pass a 50 percent threshold in order to avoid a runoff. In recent polling, Walker and Warnock are in a statistical tie, Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Just a few weeks away until those crucial midterms. Ellison Barber there in Savannah, Georgia, Ellison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221019eiaf000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc77}{\*\bkmkend toc77}\b \uc2 TODAY for October 15, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter Alexander, Kristen Welker, Ellison Barber, Monica Alba,  Priscilla Thompson, Cal Perry, Somara Theodore, Joe Fryer \par \uc2 7,042 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning, thanks for starting your weekend with us.\par \par \uc2 Georgia is on the minds of voters everywhere with the Senate candidates facing off in their first and likely only debate last night.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER. NBC NEWS ANCHOR: The high-stakes race there could decide control of the Senate. It`s October 15. This is "TODAY."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (voiceover): A Bruising Night In The Peach State, Georgia`s Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  face to face with his Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the two battling one another on everything from the economy to policing. Walker`s own personal life also under scrutiny with questions whether he paid for an ex to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: I`ll be a senator that protects life. And I say that was a lie and I`m not backing down.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Demanding Answers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think I heard three or four gunshots,\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I hear people screaming.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: New 911 calls released overnight reveal the chaos and confusion during a shooting rampage that left five people dead in Raleigh, North Carolina. A 15-year-old suspect in custody as policed and grieving family members try to figure out why this tragedy happened.\par \par \uc2 Cat Scratch Fever. Some conservative political candidates and elected officials under fire this morning for spreading the false claim that schools are providing kitty litter boxes for students who identify as cats, this morning, dissecting misinformation.\par \par \uc2 All that plus there`s no Hogwarts without Hagrid.\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE COLTRANE, SPANISH ACTOR: Rubeus Hagrid, keeper of keys engravings at Hogwarts.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Robbie Coltrane, the actor best known for playing the lovable giant in the Harry Potter movies died on Friday at 72. How his co-stars are remembering him?\par \par \uc2 Today, Saturday, October 15, 2022.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News, this is "TODAY" with Peter Alexander and Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Welcome to today. Thanks for joining us on this Saturday morning. I think there is a generation of kids and dare I say adults who feel like they grew up with that gentle half-giant Hagrid. Robbie Coltrane is going to be missed.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: He sure is. Those who knew him described him as boisterous and eccentric. And we will look back at his life at his legacy. Yes. Well, we do have a lot to cover this morning, especially in the world of politics with questions swirling about whether former President Trump will answer that subpoena to appear before the January 6 committee Mr. Trump did respond with a scathing letter slamming the committee but never actually said if he would appear. Meantime, the Republican Senate candidate in Georgia backed by President Trump, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , appeared on stage with incumbent Democratic candidate \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  for their only debate overnight.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Georgia is one of at least three crucial battleground states with very high-stakes Senate races at stake there. They could determine whether the Democrats retain control or if the Republicans will take it away. So we want to begin this morning with NBC`s Ellison Barber. She`s in Savannah with a wrap-up of last night`s big showdown, Ellison, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Peter. This is a closely watched race. This debate was highly anticipated. This morning, both campaigns are declaring victory. It wasn`t just Herschel Walker`s first time debating Senator Warnock, it was his first time debating ever, which led to some fiery moments on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER (voiceover): Incumbent senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican candi