worse.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: And tie Warnock to a president whose approval rating among Georgia voters is 37 percent.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Can you tell me why he voted with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  96 percent of the time if he was standing for Georgia?\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Every topic, Georgia voters have the final word, and early voting begins Monday in a race that could determine which party controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: In Georgia, a candidate has to pass a 50 percent threshold in order to avoid a runoff. In recent polling, Walker and Warnock are in a statistical tie, Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Just a few weeks away until those crucial midterms. Ellison Barber there in Savannah, Georgia, Ellison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And this morning, there`s new drama surrounding the legal battles of former President Trump on the Mar-a-Lago documents case. The Department of Justice is asking a federal appeals court to throw out the appointment of the Special Master who`s looking at the classified records cease during the search. This, as Mr. Trump, has yet to say whether he will comply with a subpoena to appear before the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: NBC`s Monica Alba is here with us in studio with the latest on all of it. Monica, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MONICA ALBA, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Peter and Kristen. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  responded to the January 6 committee`s unanimous subpoena with a lengthy letter of baseless election claims but didn`t say whether he`d actually comply with the request. And it`s not the only open question for the former president, now facing another legal twist in a separate investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALBA (voiceover): This morning, it`s up to a federal appeals court to decide if it should stop the work of an independent reviewer known as a special master evaluating thousands of documents seized at former President Trump`s Mar-a-Lago state. The Justice Department filed a brief on Friday, arguing there`s no legal basis for an outside review. The latest legal development, as lawmakers wait to see whether the former president might appear before the January 6 committee which plans to subpoena him in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): We are obligated to seek answers directly from the man who set this all in motion.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Mr. Trump hasn`t said whether he will, either way, instead releasing a scathing 14-page statement Friday, full of falsehoods and disproven claims of widespread election fraud, repeating that he won certain states in 2020 that he clearly lost and including photos of the crowd at the Stop the Steal rally he headlined on January 6.\par \par \uc2 In the letter to Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , the former president also defended the rioters as great American patriots and called the January 6 committee a witch hunt of the highest level. Just hours after the panel showcase never before seen video featuring congressional leaders of both parties huddled together pleading for the National Guard to be deployed as the attack unfolded.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL, (R-KY): Get in there in one hell of a hurry, you understand?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY): We need them there now whoever you got.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: The new footage film by Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s daughter Alexandra, a documentary filmmaker. In another exchange --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA): Hi, Mr. Vice President.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Speaker Pelosi reaches then-Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , expressing her concern for his well-being.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I worry about you being in that Capitol building. Don`t let anybody know where you are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Now, it`s still possible the committee could subpoena the former vice president. They haven`t rolled that out yet. But there`s not that much time left for the group to do its work since it could be disbanded if Republicans take control of the House in the next midterm elections. Next step, the committee will finish its final report and that is expected to be released sometime in December, Peter, and Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right a lot to watch, Monica. Thank you. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ALBA: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: On November -- excuse me, a North Carolina community is grieving and looking for answers this morning after the latest mass shooting in this country that killed five people. The suspected gunman is just 15 years old. NBC`s Priscilla Thompson is in Raleigh with more. Priscilla, you spoke to the fiance of one of the victims, what did he tell you?\par \par \uc2 PRISCILLA THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Peter, Kristen, good morning. I spoke to Robert Steele, the fiance of Mary Marshall who was killed and he told me that his world has been completely destroyed. He wasn`t ready to talk on camera, but he said that yesterday he went through their home looking for the wedding bands. They were set to exchange later this month. And he found the one that Mary had gotten for him and saw that she had it engraved, writing You`re My Favorite Place. But now, instead of preparing for a wedding, he`s planning her funeral.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think I heard three or four gunshots.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: OK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I heard people screaming.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voiceover): This morning, that newly released 911 calls paint a harrowing picture of a gunman on the loose in the North Carolina neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We heard some shots outside and I looked at my window and the neighbor is on the ground, I think.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The serenity of this normally quiet neighborhood shattered as the community mourns the five who were killed. 16-year-old \uc2 James Thompson\uc2 , a high school junior. 29-year-old Raleigh Police Officer Gabriel Torres killed while heading to work. 35-year-old Mary Marshall just two weeks shy of her wedding day, 49-year-old Sue Karnatz, a mother of three and avid runner, and 52-year-old Nicole Connors remembered by her husband, Tracey Howard.\par \par \uc2 TRACEY HOWARD, WIFE DIED IN NORTH CAROLINA MASS SHOOTING: Nicole, she was a, you know lovely, you know fun loving person.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Their front porch is still riddled with bullet holes, an ever- present reminder of his painful loss.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD: I see her and the dog laying on this porch right here.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: The shooting began in the streets of Raleigh`s Hedingham Neighborhood, Thursday evening. The suspect then fled onto the Greenway trail, continuing his deadly rampage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s going to be an active shooter at this time.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Police identified the alleged shooter as a 15-year-old white male but won`t name him because he`s a minor. The teen is currently hospitalized in critical condition, officials say. The investigation is still ongoing with no word on the motive or how the teenager got the gun. Thursday`s violence marks the 25th mass killing this year, the seemingly random nature of this attack leaving so many in this community shaken.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Three days ago, I was in that -- we were in that exact same spot on the Greenway. It would have been us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And people have begun coming to the entryway of this neighborhood leaving flowers and candles to pay tribute to those victims, and there remains, a 59-year-old woman who was in the hospital in critical condition fighting for her life. I also spoke with the district attorney here who tells me she doesn`t tend to charge the shooter as an adult, Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Priscilla Thompson, we appreciate your reporting. Priscilla, thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, the U.S. announced overnight that it will be sending another $725 million in military aid to Ukraine as Russia`s president defends his decision to invade more than seven months after the war began. And this morning, one of Putin`s close allies is issuing an ominous warning. NBC`s Cal Perry is in Kyiv this morning. Cal, good morning. What`s the latest there?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, good morning, Kristen. We`re here at the day after Defenders Day. It`s the time of year when Ukrainians remember those who have fallen and served in the armed forces, perhaps never a more important defenders day because this is once again a city under fire. Fresh attacks this morning hitting infrastructures on the outskirts of town, it follows a week of renewed airstrikes by the Russian military.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERRY (voiceover): As the war in Ukraine rages back into the capital city for the first time since June, more talk of nuclear threats and no sign of peace on the horizon. At a conference in Kazakhstan, the Russian president telling reporters there`s "no need for talks with the American President." Well, Putin ally and president of Belarus \uc2 Aleksandr Lukashenko\uc2  sat with my colleague here, Simmons, for an exclusive interview, and a warning.\par \par \uc2 ALEKSANDR LUKASHENKO\uc2 , BELARUSIAN PRESIDENT: Speaking in a foreign language.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you drive a person or a country into a corner, there`s only one way out from them that is to move forward. That is why red lines should not be crossed. You just shouldn`t cross them.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: That red line, he says, an attack on Russian soil.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Russia has clearly outlined its position, if there is to be, God forbid, an attack on the territory of the Russian Federation, in that case, Russia can if necessary, use all kinds of weapons.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Here in the Ukrainian capital after repeated threats from Moscow, many fear nuclear weapons could be used at any time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We can do anything about the nuclear war, but we can live our lives as we want. We don`t want to be afraid. We just want to live happy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have to be ready just in case, but you have to live your life and like go on like as usual.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: For Ukraine, it`s not just the threat of nuclear weapons, the government accusing Russia of nuclear terrorism, occupying the largest nuclear power plant in Europe, and torturing the Ukrainian staff.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s very hard for them to operate the plant in these conditions because they`re actually periodically captured, tortured, and beaten.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: So the Russians are torturing the workers of the plant?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes. They just from time to time.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Another threat hanging over a country taking stock after a week of overwhelming airstrikes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Now, there have in the past week been explosions across the border in the Russian city of Belgorod. Ukraine does not take credit for any of these explosions. Perhaps an indication they continue to walk that fine line between defending Ukraine and not wanting to widen the war, Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Cal Perry in Kyiv, Cal, thank you so much for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Also related to this, war news this morning from the Pentagon about what`s called the Starlink satellite network that`s helping Ukraine fight the Russian invasion.\par \par \uc2 [07:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Officials say that Elon Musk`s company \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  has asked the government to take over funding for Starlink. Musk tweeted Friday that providing Starlink costs his company about $20 million a month and that he`s already spent 80 million. Meantime, Musk`s SpaceX Dragon capsule returned from the International Space Station, Friday, splashing down off the coast of Florida, brought home three Americans and an Italian astronaut from a six- month stay.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, two of the nation`s largest grocery store chains have agreed to merge in a deal they say will help them better compete with \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Costco\uc2 , and \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . Ohio-based \uc2 Kroger\uc2  will pay $20 billion for \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 , which is based in Idaho and also includes the \uc2 Safeway\uc2  brand. If antitrust regulators approve the deal together, the stores will control about 13 percent of the U.S. grocery market. That`s still a distant second to \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , 22 percent share.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Time to get a first check of the forecast right now. For that, our friend Somara Theodore has her eyes on the skies. Somara, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, CNN METEOROLOGIST: Good morning. It`s a bit of a chilly start for some of our friends. We`re going to head to the Great Lakes this morning. Areas like Chicago coming in at 55 degrees. So not bad, but we are below the average for this time of year, about eight degrees below the average in Chicago. Areas like Indianapolis coming in at 59 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Heading a little bit farther west into parts of the Upper Midwest, Grand Forks, 41 degrees for a high there, Duluth coming in 11 below the average, and this cool weather spreads to the east coast as we head through the top of the week. Temperatures will actually really struggle to make it out of the 40s -- or out of the 30s should I say in areas like Pittsburgh by Wednesday. Highs around 43 degrees, good morning, New York, high temperatures to on Monday we`ll be in the mid to upper 60s really going to have a hard time getting out of the 50s by Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Now on the West Coast, it`s a bit of a different story. It`s feeling a lot more like summer. And we`re talking record-breaking heat here, 91 degrees in Medford. Redding, you could break a record with temperatures getting pretty close to 97. Medford is going to be 20 degrees above the norm for this time of year. It`s October, but it`s not really feeling like it. Tomorrow starting to level out a bit, temperatures will be in the upper 80s in Medford up towards Portland, 81 for your Sunday. Areas like Boise is 77 degrees.\par \par \uc2 That`s the look at the weather across the country. Let`s see what`s going on in your neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: And that`s the weather in your neighborhood, Kristen Peter.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Somara, thank you very much. And still to come right here. Maura Barrett is digging into the dangers of misinformation during this busy election season.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Politicians are talking about kitty litter -- kitty litter in school bathrooms. It`s just the latest rumor with nine lives that just won`t die. That`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Plus, why it`s so important for women under 40 to speak up for themselves when it comes to breast cancer screenings. We`ll explain. We are back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:20:00]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We are back on a Saturday morning with a "WEEKLY DOWNLOAD," our look at the week`s other big stories.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Our friend, NBC News Now anchor Joe Fryer is in New York with the latest on all of that. Joe, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, Peter, Kristen, good morning to you. This week we covered some big developments and some high-profile court cases.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER (voiceover): Two major school shooting verdicts this week, the Florida jury spared \uc2 Nikolas Cruz\uc2  from the death penalty. Cruz shot and killed 14 students and three staff members at a high school in Parkland. Family members are outraged.\par \par \uc2 FRED GUTTENBERG, DAUGHTER KILLED IN PARKLAND SCHOOL SHOOTING: This jury failed our families today.\par \par \uc2 LINDA BEIGEL SCHULMAN, SON KILLED IN PARKLAND SCHOOL SHOOTING: We have a legal system, but is it just?\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Cruz will get life in prison without parole.\par \par \uc2 In Connecticut, a jury ordered Alex Jones to pay $965 million in damages to victims` families for repeatedly saying the mass shooting at Sandy Hook Elementary was a hoax.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: While the verdict was being revealed, Jones was on air mocking what he called a show trial.\par \par \uc2 ALEX JONES, RADIO SHOW HOST: Yes, never said their name on who they are.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Jones`s attorney vows to appeal.\par \par \uc2 Baltimore prosecutors dropped charges against Adnan Syed after he spent 23 years behind bars for the killing of his ex-girlfriend. The decision comes weeks after he was released from prison and new DNA testing excluded Syed. Syed was sentenced to life in prison for the 1999 murder of his high school girlfriend, Hae Min Lee. The trial and it`s now well-documented airs chronicled in the podcast Serial and an \uc2 HBO\uc2  documentary.\par \par \uc2 NASA\uc2  announced its mission to change the path of an asteroid in outer space was a smashing success, demonstrating they could help save Earth if necessary in the future.\par \par \uc2 BILL NELSON, NASA ADMINISTRATOR: Now this is a watershed moment for planetary defense.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: These three men set out to fish off the coast of Louisiana but rough waters capsized their boat. One man`s iPhone was almost out of battery power but was able to snap a photo of his location and text it. They were rescued by the Coast Guard after surviving shark-infested waters for 28 hours. Two of the rescued man and some first responders reunited on today.\par \par \uc2 LUAN NGUYEN, RESCUED FROM SHARK-INFESTED WATER: I just remember him picking me up pull me out of the water it`s like well I made it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You made it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Some of the week`s most dramatic moments "CAUGHT ON CAMERA." In Kyiv, a young Ukrainian was filming when she narrowly avoided a rocket blast. Police in San Antonio, Texas released body camera footage showing the moment when an officer opened this car door and started shooting this teenager. The teen is in critical condition. The officer has been charged with assault and fired from his job according to the police department.\par \par \uc2 And a new star emerged during the Major League Baseball playoffs. This goose landed on the diamond during the Dodgers-Padres game and refused to move even as the game continued. Workers were finally able to grab it during a pitching change. And finally, this Labrador Retriever named Maverick froze for more than a minute when he came across all these cardboard cutout cats on the lawn. Eventually, Maverick`s owners were able to calm him down and get him moving again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: So, Peter, Kristen, I`m doing my best Maverick impression here. How am I doing tonight?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Strong.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Very strong. I think I want to be Maverick for Halloween. That`s the cutest thing I`ve ever seen.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Maverick was like catatonic. That was crazy.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I know. He just completely froze. Well, I guess credit to those cats. They really got him to hold.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, that`s good stuff, Joe.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I like Joe`s (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, good efforts.\par \par \uc2 Still to come on "TODAY," Hollywood gets a facelift. We help give the iconic sign a fresh coat of paint.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Plus, remembering the beloved actor who brought Hagrid from the Harry Potter books to life on the big screen. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:30]\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Nice shot. We are back on this Saturday morning October 15th, 2022. And that was pro golfers Jordan Spieth and Scottie Scheffler taking on \uc2 NBA\uc2  legend Dirk Nowitzki and pro tennis player John Isner earlier this week as part of the Pro Pickleball Associations celebrity tour.\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: So, not for nothing, Isner and Nowitzki are 6`10" and seven foot, right?\par \par \uc2 WELKER: They have an advantage.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Talk about -- talking about a tough team to face off against. If you haven`t heard, Pickleball is certainly picking up speed these days.\par \par \uc2 Coming up a little bit later, we`re going to take you to the Major League Pickleball tournament. Apparently, you can be a Pickleball pro that`s taking place this weekend.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I actually liked Pickleball. Have you tried it?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: I have tried it. Yes. My daughter is nine and has beat me twice.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, well, we do have a busy half hour. Let`s begin with a check of the headlines.\par \par \uc2 Rescue workers in Turkey are searching for survivors this morning after a coal mine explosion there on Friday. The death toll rising this morning to at least 28. 15 people are either trapped or missing. The cause of the blast is still under investigation. There were 110 miners working in the shaft at the time of the explosion.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Passengers on a \uc2 United Airlines\uc2  flight from Chicago to Miami had a big scare on Friday. This cell phone video shot by a passenger shows flames shooting out from the back of the engine after the plane hit a bird just after it took off from O`Hare International Airport. The pilot immediately turn the plane around and fortunately was able to make a safe landing.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, the Major League Baseball Playoffs continue with four games today after three exciting games on Friday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They all pitch from Hader to Thompson.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: \uc2 Padres\uc2 `s fan saw their first postseason win in San Diego in a quarter century, topping the Dodgers two to one. The \uc2 Padres\uc2  lead the series two games to one in a run out makeup game at Yankee Stadium.\par \par \uc2 Cleveland broke a tie with New York with two runs in the 10th inning, winning four to two, evening the series at a game apiece. And the Phils crush the Braves in Atlanta, nine to one. Philadelphia taking a two to one lead in that series.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yankee fans worried about Aaron Judge.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Oh, for sure.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The guy has got to get hot again for those New Yorkers.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes, that`s for sure. All right. Well, also this morning, a new warning about the dangers of misinformation spreading online making its way into political races.\par \par \uc2 And this latest claim, absurd as it may sound, that some school districts are providing cat litter boxes for students who identify as cats.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: So, of course, this is not true. But that is not stopping some conservative politicians from talking about it as if it was fact.\par \par \uc2 An effort to raise alarms by saying that this is where protections for LGBTQ students ultimately will lead.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Maura Barrett is helping follow this one for us. Again, an effort to sort of dissect this misinformation. Maura, good morning,\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Peter and Kristen, good morning. This story is just the latest example of how misinformation no matter how ridiculous it may appear, can spread like wildfire on social media.\par \par \uc2 And when political figures like \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  or influential podcast hosts like Joe Rogan amplify that bad information, it can have a dangerous impact.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARRETT (voice over): The first sign of the rumor in the U.S. appears to have been this Michigan School Board meeting last December.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I heard that at least one of our schools in our town has a -- in one of the unisex bathrooms, a litter box for the kids that identify as cats.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Where it was discussed by a parent without proof, then shared on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  by a chairwoman of the Michigan Republican Party, referring to furries, a small group of people who are interested in and may dress up as animals with human like features. The false story sneaking its way across the country.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE DRAZKOWSKI (R-MN): They think they`re a cat, a cat. They put tails on and they demand that they have a litter box in the school.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JANICE BOWLING (R-TN): Obviously it`s very disruptive to the learning process.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: And now, into Republican stump speeches. It`s seemingly the latest battle by conservatives and the culture war over gender identity. At least 20 conservative candidates and elected officials made these claims this year about schools placing litter boxes on campus according to an NBC News Review of Public Statements.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There are no litter boxes in schools.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These conspiracies are insulting.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: Every school districts named by those 20 politicians said these assertions are untrue.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:01]\par \par \uc2 BEN COLLINS, NBC NEWS DIGITAL REPORTER: This is about stirring up more panic and more fear. Even the trolling and mocking of this was a way to get the word out there about this lie.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: The cat comments often brought up in conjunction with school policies around transgender or non-binary students. Experts increasingly concerned about the impact the extreme and baseless rhetoric could have on LGBTQ Plus Youth.\par \par \uc2 NADINE BRIDGES, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, ONE COLORADO: The sensationalizes, some community they know that some people don`t know anything about. This is a tender and vulnerable time and putting out headlines so that young people can be click bait is not that how we do this.\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: The misinformation still spreading, falsely fueling the culture wars with less than a month until the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARRETT (on camera): Of all the schools called out for having kitty litter, NBC found only one that actually has it on campus, in Jefferson County, home to Columbine High School. But the litter is part of an emergency supply kit in case students are stuck in a classroom too long during a school shooter situation. Not the reason politicians are sharing. Kristen and Peter.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Maura Barrett, thank you so much for that report. And I think that Maura makes the key point here, which is that this is about spreading fear.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, the fear in this era is when people get their information from within an echo chamber. If that echo chamber is spreading lies, it has the potential to be spread more widely than that.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn the table now and check in with our friend Somara Theodore. She is watching the forecast for us. Somara, you look great this morning. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SOMARA THEODORE, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Thank you. Good morning. Good morning. We are headed to the tropics. Let`s take a little trip, shall we?\par \par \uc2 So, here`s the latest. You may have heard about Karl, right now, it`s currently a post tropical cyclone.\par \par \uc2 At this moment, not really bringing much but heavy rain to parts of Central America. But we are -- in the Yucatan, I`m sorry. But we are seeing some rain in Cancun. So, if you have any vacation plans over there, keep an eye on the tropics.\par \par \uc2 Now, we are keeping a close eye on this tropical disturbance, nothing organized yet, about 10 percent chance of developing but we`ll keep a close eye on it. It`s still off the coast of Africa right now.\par \par \uc2 A little closer to home. We are tracking some flood alerts, we`re going to head down into Southern California, parts of Arizona. Five million people impacted by this from Phoenix out towards Blythe, El Centro. We could see some heavy rain out that way. And some severe weather we`re going to talk about coming up in the 8:00 hour, so stick around for that.\par \par \uc2 For now, let`s see what`s going on a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 CLAY ANDERSON\uc2 , STORM TEAM4 METEOROLOGIST: And good morning, everyone. Right now, we`re looking at 50 degrees with the winds from the southeast which will continue to bring the warm air across our area.\par \par \uc2 I know we`re waking up to temperatures in the 30s and 40s and 50 degrees in town. But that will change.\par \par \uc2 So, for today, starting out rather cool and crisp, but the afternoon, we rise to the 70s and this evening will be nice as well.\par \par \uc2 In fact, for the weekend, we stay nice and dry. A chance for rain but not until sunset on Sunday. Enjoy your weekend, everyone.\par \par \uc2 THEODORE: And that`s the forecast in your neighborhood. Peter, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right, Somara. Thank you and just ahead, the importance of early screening and detection as the number of breast cancer cases in young women rises.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We`ll get to that, but first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:41:14]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: We are back down with an important message during this Breast Cancer Awareness Month about the importance of advocating for yourself especially when it comes to your health.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Doctors have seen a rise in severe breast cancer cases for women under the age of 40 and age group which typically doesn`t get screened.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Anne Thompson has more on the risks and what you can do about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CHIEF ENVIRONMENTAL AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Breast Cancer used to be only a job for Sadia Zap (PH), leading the communications team for the Breast Cancer Research Fund. This summer, it became personal\par \par \uc2 SADIA ZAP, COMMUNICATIONS TEAM LEADER, BREAST CANCER RESEARCH FUND: With a hard, round lump.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: She was diagnosed with breast cancer that spread to her lymph nodes. Sadia is only 36 and feared for her husband Ben and 4-year-old Sanjay (PH).\par \par \uc2 ZAP: It`s really amazing what you realize when you get news like that and at instant, everything falls away.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Just months earlier, she says she pleaded with two different doctors for early screening because of her high-risk factors. Sadia has started her period before age 11, had her first baby after 30 and her maternal aunt had breast cancer at a young age.\par \par \uc2 ZAP: I asked for a mammogram at that time.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: And what were you told?\par \par \uc2 ZAP: You don`t need screening, then let`s not worry about it. Don`t worry about it.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Doctors did perform genetic testing.\par \par \uc2 ZAP: It said negative in big bold letters.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: But Sadia still worried.\par \par \uc2 ZAP: They were making recommendations based on sort of the big -- the population, right? And they didn`t look at me as a person.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Now, Sadia is undergoing chemotherapy with surgery and radiation ahead. She believes her cancer could have been caught earlier if screening young women was the rule and not the exception.\par \par \uc2 ZAP: We have a cure for \uc2 breast cancer now\uc2  and that is catching up early and young women don`t have that luxury. We`re not being screened annually.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Today, early detection for women under 40 is rare.\par \par \uc2 Why do younger women get more aggressive cancers?\par \par \uc2 DR. ANN PARTRIDGE, YOUNG AND STRONG PROGRAM FOR YOUNG ADULTS WITH BREAST CANCER: We think that`s because young women are more likely to not be screened. And so, their tumors are bigger by the time we find them.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Dr. Ann Partridge directs Dana-Farber`s program for young women with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 PARTRIDGE: They tend to present with more aggressive types of breast cancer. And it tends to be more advanced, or more bigger tumors or more involved lymph nodes, which requires a bit more aggressive approach in general.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: In fact, for women 25 to 39, the rate of metastatic breast cancer, cancer that spread elsewhere is up 32 percent since 2009. And a recent study found breast cancer death rates among women under 40 have stopped declining.\par \par \uc2 If mammograms were required for women under 40, would it make a difference?\par \par \uc2 PARTRIDGE: That`s a really tricky question. It`s not clear that more mammograms for this population is the right answer for screening. It`s really more about finding better tests and then selecting the patients who might warrant more sensitive tests than mammograms.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Until technology catches up, advocates say young women need to know their risk factors, be alert to changes in their bodies, and not be afraid to seek a second opinion.\par \par \uc2 ZAP: The onus of diagnosis shouldn`t only be on the patient. And that`s where it is right now.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: So, women younger and older can benefit from early detection.\par \par \uc2 For TODAY Anne Thompson, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: What an incredibly important story by Anne and Sadia is so brave for speaking out and sharing her own personal battle.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And you hope that there are families who watched this, men and women alike, who don`t just think about those over 40 anymore, but remind those under 40 that they to need to be paying close attention to their health.\par \par \uc2 [07:45:05]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So important.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still ahead right here, the world-famous Hollywood sign gets a touch up just in time for a huge anniversary.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And our corresponding got to help, we`ll show you, that`s right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, if you`ve ever traveled to Los Angeles, you`ve probably looked high on the Hill to see that world famous Hollywood sign.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You almost don`t have to travel to Los Angeles to have seen that sign.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is right, everywhere.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: It`s like ubiquitous. A big anniversary is approaching for the sign and now it`s getting a pretty nice touch up.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Steve Patterson grabbed a paintbrush and got to work.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEVE PATTERSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Every great city has a symbol, representing the ideals of its people. In Hollywood, it`s a sign. Nine giant letters spelling out one iconic notion. This is where dreams are made.\par \par \uc2 Believe it or not, the Hollywood sign is about to turn 100 years old, it was meant to last just 18 months.\par \par \uc2 The sign went up back in 1923 as Hollywoodland, a billboard to sell real estate, but it caught people`s attention and stuck around.\par \par \uc2 In the 40s, it was donated to the city of Los Angeles and the land was removed.\par \par \uc2 JEFF ZARRINNAM, HOLLYWOOD SIGN TRUST CHAIR: The sign came to mean something different. It came to mean the Hollywood industry as a whole.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Through the years, the old wooden sign decayed, even losing one of its O`s. So, in 1978, the old one was knocked down and a new wood rose in its place. Now, it`s getting a fresh coat of paint ahead of its big birthday.\par \par \uc2 ROGER MARTINEZ, DUGGAN AND ASSOCIATES SUPERINTENDENT: It honestly feels like you know, you`re kind of like one of the stars up here. All right, let`s go.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Roger Martinez, he`s overseeing the repainting process. He took me up to lend a hand.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Ready to help with paint?\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Use the roller yourself.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: I would never dream that I would be putting paint on the Hollywood sign.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Dreams come true.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Dreams do come true.\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: Remember, this is Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: This is Hollywood, this is where it happens, right?\par \par \uc2 MARTINEZ: I`m the guardian of the Hollywood sign.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Jeff Zarrinnam runs the Hollywood sign trust.\par \par \uc2 ZARRINNAM: People have grown accustomed to seeing the sign. But it also has a meaning in their hearts. And they relate to the sign to their favorite actor, their television show, their movie, and that`s what it means to people around the world.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: In a place where dreams of stardom are made and dashed, the message high up here endures. A symbol of the stars shining down on Tinseltown for generations to come.\par \par \uc2 [07:50:10]\par \par \uc2 For TODAY, Steve Patterson, NBC News, Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Talk about dreams coming true. So cool Steve got to help paint.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, some people dream of painting the Hollywood sign, others dream of being in Hollywood.\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE) Still to come, it`s time to shop by. Retailers are starting their holiday sales now and how you can take best advantage of all those deep discounts. We are back with that after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still to come on TODAY, the passion for Pickleball, why everyone including \uc2 NFL\uc2  and \uc2 NBA\uc2  MVPs are getting in on the game.\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Plus, our first look at the new season of "The Crown". How will season five play out? That`s coming up in POPStart.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Those stories and more after these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221015eiaf000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc78}{\*\bkmkend toc78}\b \uc2 Dr. Mehmet Oz On Crime And His Campaign`s Attacks On Fetterman`s Health\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Dasha Burns \par \uc2 696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Just 25 days before the Midterms, tonight, we speak exclusively with Dr. Mehmet Oz, the Republican facing off against Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in that critical Pennsylvania Senate race. Our Dasha Burns pressing Dr. Oz on crime and his campaign`s attacks on Fetterman`s health.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Pennsylvania Republican Senate candidate and celebrity doctor, Mehmet Oz, has focused much of his campaign on crime and blasting Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  on his votes as the head of the state`s Board of Pardons.\par \par \uc2 He says he believes in second chances, the former president with his First Step Act, also was about giving second chances to prisoners who deserve it. Why don`t prisoners who prove good behavior, who are modeled prisoners, why don`t they deserve second chances?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: The First Step Act had nothing to do with convicted murderers being released from prison, especially if they`ve been sentenced to life in prison. It`s a whole different game. When \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is asked, if you could wave a magic wand, what`s the one thing you would do, the one thing? And he says why get rid of life in prison?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, I asked him that. He actually said, it would be codifying Roe versus Wade and abolishing the Filibuster.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Well, he`s answered it the way I just described in the past as well. And too often, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  seems to pay more attention to the feelings of the criminals than the innocent who were hurt.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: We also asked about his campaign`s attacks on Fetterman`s health. He was off the campaign trail for months after suffering a stroke.\par \par \uc2 One of your campaign staffers said that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  wouldn`t have had a stroke if he had "ever eaten a vegetable in his life." You`re a doctor. You`re a heart doctor. You of all people understand what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is going through better than most. Why would you allow your campaign to mock him like that?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I have tremendous compassion for what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has gone through. I mean, not only do I, as a doctor, appreciate the challenges, but I know his specific ailment because it`s a specialty area of mine.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But would you ever talk to your patients like this?\par \par \uc2 OZ: No.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: He criticizes Fetterman for not releasing his medical records, while Fetterman accuses Dr. Oz of not being transparent on abortion rights. This is what he told us this week.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Dr. Oz likes to make fun of me that I might miss a word. But you know, he`s missed, you know, two words. And that is a yes or no on the national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So, we asked him about Republican Senator Lindsey Graham`s bill that would ban abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 If you win, you`re going to be a colleague of his. You`re going to have to vote on bills like this. So, can you tell us right now, yes or no on that bill?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I`m giving you a bigger answer than yes or no. I`m telling you. I don`t want any federal rules limiting what states do with abortion. It should be up to the states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Lester, there was more we wanted to discuss. We had to ask for the same amount of time Fetterman gave us about 30 minutes, but the campaign said they had time constraints, so we only spoke for about half that time. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dasha Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programing copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221020eiae0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc79}{\*\bkmkend toc79}\b \uc2 Behind The Scenes Of Carrie Underwood`s Epic New Tour\b0\par\par\uc2 SHEINELLE JONES \par \uc2 11,943 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: I am excited for this. So, this morning, we have a "TODAY" exclusive. A first look at what to expect when country superstar Carrie Underwood hits the road for her Denim and Rhinestones tour. It`s her first in three years since she took me back stage in South Carolina where it all kicks off.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARRIE UNDERWOOD, SINGER: It`s a good time. A lot of hard work has gone into the show and very, very proud of it. People are going to have fun.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): It`s been a few years since a Carrie Underwood tour has made its way across the country. And no one seems more excited than the superstar herself.\\\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): What is the one thing you miss about touring?\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: The crowds. I love to sing. When I`m on stage, I feel like I am at home. And just to feel the energy from people and you look out at all these seats and every single person that is there.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): The eight-time Grammy winner embarking on a 43-stop arena tour, kicking it all off with a huge show in Greenville, South Carolina. And yours truly is given an all-access pass.\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): This gives me like the feels.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: It`s pretty awesome. It looks bigger. I feel like from up here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: From here?\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): The stage for her "Denim and Rhinestones" tour lit in dramatic purple will include pyrotechnics and even Carrie performing some high-flying aerial stunts.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: I am always looking to do things I`ve never have done before.\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): Like fly?\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Like fly.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): Carrie, taking a page out of her CMT Awards performance.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: And it`s important to me to make sure like everybody gets a good view. So, we want to figure out some cool ways to get from point A to point B.\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): How exciting is that?\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): It`s all part of her goal to bring something new to the arena tours.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: I want everything to feel different. I don`t want somebody to be like, no, I saw a Carrie Underwood show, you know, four years ago and I`m good.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): Something definitely worth the price of admission, Carrie`s stunning wardrobe.\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): Like this is --\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: It`s a lot. There`s a lot going.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s amazing. Look how beautiful it is.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: The last part is adding more rhinestones.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I don`t know if you could add any more rhinestones.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: You always can. I mean --\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): But something no amount of rhinestones can replace, Carrie`s time with her kids on the tour and at home.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:00]\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: They will be out to visit. This tour, I`m going home a lot more than I ever have before. There is a way to get me home even if it`s just for the day.\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): So, are your kids aware of what mommy does? I mean, they`ve been on tour with you before but they are young.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: I feel like Israel who is seven and a half, he is becoming more aware that it`s not normal, probably because of people around him. So, he`ll go to school and somebody will be, like, I saw your mom on TV last night.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): One of the Carrie`s few must-haves, a mobile gym.\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): This is good. Do you ever work out to your own music?\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: No.\par \par \uc2 JONES: No.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: It`s weird.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): A U.S. arena tour, a long way from Carrie`s "American Idol" days.\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): Let me take you back.\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It was in 2005. I was a reporter in Tulsa, Oklahoma, and I had the assignment to meet you at the door. The whole town was going nuts because a local girl made it on "American idol." And you walked in and your hands were like -- and I grabbed your hand. I was like, are you ready? If you look at this picture, like this girl right here, do you remember what you were feeling in those early days?\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: At that point, it was all very new, very fresh. And I am, like, OK, you know, let`s enjoy this and have fun and, you know, keep trying to, you know, move forward, but it could all go away at any second. And I think it`s kind of important to treat everything like that, you know, because it just makes you grateful. So, I still -- I`m waiting for the moment where I`m like feeling like I belong.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 JONES (voiceover): And while I had her, I just had to ask.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC PLAYING)\par \par \uc2 JONES (on camera): One of your fellow "American Idol" winners, you know, Kelly Clarkson, does her Kellyoke. Have you heard her sing "Before He Cheats?"\par \par \uc2 UNDERWOOD: I have. I have. I feel like she`s one person that you`re like, please, don`t sing my song because you`re going to make me look bad.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Are you joking? You all are both like powerhouses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s funny. I was asking her, you know, if she stays in touch with some of the -- you know, the folks who she, you know, was on "Idol" with years ago. And she has -- great. She says she still talks to Simon, you know, that whole thing.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, that`s great.\par \par \uc2 JONES: One thing I will say about Carrie is she is so humble and it`s not lost on her. Because I`m like, oh, my god, look at all these clothes and look at all of this. And she is like, yes. You know, she just -- it keeps it --\par \par \uc2 DREYER: It seems like it hasn`t gone to her head.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: She`s walking around like, can you --\par \par \uc2 JONES: She is still saying, can you believe it?\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 JONES: The tour kicks off tomorrow and tickets are on sale now. There will be pyrotechnics. She flies, as we talked about. She even -- she`s like, I am humble and even --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I want to see you get up on the flying thing.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I loved it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: With that old hairstyle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: All right. Coming up, another icon is here with us in studio. She`s --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: There she is.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: \uc2 Stella McCartney\uc2 . You`re going to fashion, family and so much more, right?\par \par \uc2 STELLA MCCARTNEY\uc2 , FASHION DESIGNER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: OK. When we come back.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Gorgeous. Gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Nice done. Nice walk off.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:40:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: We have our life therapist here. She`s also --\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Really have a good time\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- a fashion royalty with us this morning here in studio.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: \uc2 Stella McCartney\uc2  has made a name for herself designing clothes for celebrities like \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2 , Amal Clooney, Beyonce Johnson, so, so many more.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: She launched her brand in 2001, pioneering animal free and ecofriendly fashion.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And Stella joining us now fresh off her Paris fashion show.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Good morning, Stella.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 STELLA MCCARTNEY\uc2 , FASHION DESIGNER: Hi.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, you made this a part of your mission, you know, sustainable ecofriendly. I mean, Bella Hadid`s Met`s dress in the Paris fashion show you making a big splash. How important is that to you and why did you decide so early on? I mean, you were really ahead of the curve on this.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: You know, I grew up on an organic farm in England as a vegetarian and I basically wanted to be a fashion designer and I was lucky enough to be one and I thought, I can`t compromise my ethics for my career. I don`t want to kill animals. You know, billions of animals get killed for handbags and shoes and leather jackets. So, I was like, I can`t do that. I need to find an alternative. And it started there really. And, you know, the best thing about today is that actually doing sustainable fashion and being ethical and conscious is actually on trend.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s in.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Which is a miracle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Right.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Is it hard to do?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: It`s impossible to do.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: It`s a miracle that I am here today. It`s really, really hard. You know, you have to work with your suppliers so deeply. But it`s this kind of sexiest thing, I think, in fashion. It`s the future of fashion. I think if people don`t start adopting this way of working, then, you know, they`re going to go out of fashion pretty quick.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Thanks in no small part to everything that you have done. And Al mentioned, by the way, at Paris Fashion Week, I want to ask you about that because saw your dad was there, your sister Mary was your photographer.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: A real family affair. How was it? You guys have a good time? You guys always go together as a group?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: No. I mean, I`m always there. But, you know, my dad is a busy guy. So, when he can come, I`m always so excited. And, you know, family, the most important thing, right? Let`s face it. Family and friends. And, you know, the fashion industry is a tough cookie and to have your family there supporting you. You know, my family happened to be fabulous. So, it adds a little diamond.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Understatement.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s fair to say.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I saw -- speaking of your dad, that he posted -- he is almost 80 and he`s doing -- still doing head stands. Here it is.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Wait. Guys, look in the corner of the TV screen. He is watching MS and there a teaser for our documentary series. So, shout out --\par \par \uc2 JONES: Oh, look at that, Jacob.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Shout out to Mr. McCartney.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, that`s why you --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: There you go.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Do you do that too?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: I will be sending a check in the post.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Yes. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: And you are going to owe him a couple of pounds.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Are you a head stander?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Yes, I can do the head stand.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Really?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: But my dad is very religious about it. He does eye exercises. You know, I mean, he just headlined Glassenbury (ph) a couple of months ago.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: A week after turning 80 to 250,000 people. So, he must be doing something right.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Should all we do one now?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: No. No, thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You know what, earlier this year you were awarded -- somebody in my ear goes, yes, please. Are you joking?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Earlier this year you were awarded the CBE. So, it`s the Commander of the Order of the British Empire by the late Queen Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: I know.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Amazing.\par \par \uc2 JONES: For your services to fashion and sustainability. Your father, of course, is knighted. And you also designed the dress that \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  wore at the queen`s funeral?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: I did.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What an honor.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:00]\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Yes. Well, you know, it is a real honor, actually. It`s funny. I don`t know. You know, it`s hard to not kind of pinch yourself when these things happen. And yes, I have another award coming up. I`m really excited. I`m --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Do tell.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: It`s the -- oh, God. This is when I stop -- start to -- like that.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Tell us, tell us.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: It`s the other after the CBE.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: The Next One?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Is Next One.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s -- so, Next level up, so this week?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: The Next. I am going up as opposed to going down, which is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: So, what is your title then?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Don`t ask me. I`m terrible. I am like the worst person in the world.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Oh, we don`t know. We were talking to America. We don`t know any of this.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: You can put it out audience. You can have the community search of what I am going to be next.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: You guys, you can win a \uc2 Stella McCartney\uc2  plant-based handbag. How about that?\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Oh, really?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: If one of you tells us.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Can I submit an answer? I wouldn`t mind that.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: You can do it.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I`ll start Googling.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I know.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Let`s also talk about your skin care line. This too keeps the environment in mind. I mean, it`s got to be just as hard to do in the skin care industry as well.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Oh, God. Yes. It`s really, really tough. You know, there are so many less ingredients, really, when you`re trying work in a sustainable and natural way. But my new skin care range is coming soon. It`s coming here. And I`m so excited.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: I am so proud of it. It took me three years to do. It doesn`t harm animals. It doesn`t harm the planet. It`s refill-based. So, that`s really, really exciting.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Oh, that`s --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: You know, with --\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s the way of the future.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s -- everybody is doing that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: And it`s great. You can throw in your bag. It`s delicious. Like -- and I think the best thing about what I tend to do in luxury fashion and sustainability is you shouldn`t compromise in any way the quality and the luxury element of what you -- you know, you`re investing in. So, the skin care feels like luxurious, it smells delicious and it`s just the results are amazing.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: And yet, your skin is.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: And yet, it`s learning to nature.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: I`m so close to you right now and your skin is --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Yes. Look at that. Look at that. Smell that.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: OK. That`s not a little creepy. OK.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Stella, it is always great having you here.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Let`s do some head stand.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTNEY: I love doing it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Well, up next, we are sticking with the fashion and beauty theme. We`re going to show you some smart products to add to your routine, like a chill pill for your face. That sounds good. I need a chill pill for my face.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: I could use that.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:50:00]\par \par \uc2 DREYER: This morning, we are upgrading our beauty routines with smart products designed to make our lives a little easier. And here to help us is Julee Wilson, Cosmopolitan`s beauty editor-at-large. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JULEE WILSON, COSMOPOLITAN`S BEAUTY EDITOR-AT-LARGE: Hi. How are you?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Hey, Julee.\par \par \uc2 SOBOROFF: Hey, Julee.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love it when you come.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: I just love being here.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Well, we love having you. And we`re starting off with blush.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Because blush, I feel like you go with the wrong color, you are kind of throwing things off.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: Yes. But this one is super smart. It`s from Youthforia. It`s green, you see here.\par \par \uc2 DREYER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: But when you put it on your skin, it works with your skin`s natural pH. And it turn -- look it. Do you guy