d boy. What was the shooter`s motive?\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, the new move in the battle of her documents seized at Mar-a- Lago. What the DOJ is asking an appeals court to do regarding the special master.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sending a 14-page letter to the January 6 Committee after it voted to subpoena him. What it says?\par \par \uc2 And the video from the day of the attack, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  saying she wanted to punch Mr. Trump out.\par \par \uc2 Our NBC News exclusive with Dr. Mehmet Oz, GOP Senate candidate in Pennsylvania. Does he stand by his campaign`s attacks on the health of his Democratic rival \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 ? Does he support a federal ban on abortion and his anti-crime message?\par \par \uc2 The holiday shopping season, why it`s off to an early start. Where to get big bargains right now?\par \par \uc2 It`s America`s fastest-growing sport with fans like Tom Brady and LeBron James. How Pickleball is going pro.\par \par \uc2 And remembering the actor who brought up a beloved Harry Potter character to life.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. We are going to begin with the tragic and frightening story that was breaking in North Carolina as we came on the air here last night. We now know five people died allegedly at the hands of a teenage shooter who held a Raleigh neighborhood in fear, forcing residents to barricade themselves inside their homes.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina`s Governor calling it the nightmare of every community. Authority say the alleged shooter left a two-mile trail of victims and crime scenes behind him. Tonight, the 15-year-old suspect is being held and is in critical condition after a long standoff with police. The dead include an off-duty police officer and range in age from 16 to 52. Two other people were injured.\par \par \uc2 And eyewitnesses are now describing to us their own harrowing encounters with the accused shooter. Blayne Alexander is in Raleigh and has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The crime scene stretched more than two miles from his quiet Raleigh neighborhood to a nearby walking path, a literal trail of terror all left by one gunman who police say is just 15 years old.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Officer down. We have an officer down.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: The first chance came just after 5:00 p.m. as neighborhood kids were getting off the school bus. Nayesha Royle (PH) was at home and heard screams outside.\par \par \uc2 NAYESHA ROYLE, RALEIGH RESIDENT: A neighbor`s kids were knocking banging on the door. I`m telling her like open the door. Something`s going on. And you hear the kids say he has a gun. He has a gun. There`s a gun.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: And when you open the door, what did you see?\par \par \uc2 ROYLE: Chaos. Chaos.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: She also saw the gunman and frantically started pulling neighbors and schoolchildren into her house.\par \par \uc2 ROYLE: If you could have seen a fear in their eyes -- like, the little girl was like, he pointed the gun at me. And they`re kids. They`re kids.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: For more than four hours, police combed the area, bringing dogs and helicopters and telling residents to stay inside. Finally, they say they found the gunman hiding nearby. He was taken into custody and to the hospital in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 MARY-ANN BALDWIN, MAYOR, RALEIGH: There are several families in our community waking up this morning without their loved ones.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Among those killed according to officials, 29-year-old Gabriel Torres, an off duty police officer headed to work, 16-year-old James Roger Thompson a high school junior, the Sue Karnatz who was out for a run. Her husband says that was her biggest passion outside of their three boys. Nicole Connors, a caregiver at heart dedicated to her husband and their dog Sammy. And 35-year-old Mary Marshall set to get married in just two weeks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Blayne, none of it makes sense. Did police said anything about a possible motive?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): Unfortunately, not yet, Lester. In fact, police are not saying what led up to that shooting, only that the entire thing is still under investigation. But we are learning more about charges. Tonight, the DA`s office confirms that even though the suspect is only 15 years old, he will be charged as an adult. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Blayne Alexander in Raleigh tonight, thank you. This evening, former President Trump`s response after the January 6 Committee voted to subpoena him as new video from inside the Capitol on that day shows the fear and frustration of congressional leaders. Kelly O`Donnell has late details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Former President Trump used 14 pages to vent his anger, disappointment, and complaint at the January 6 Committee for not investigating his claims of election fraud rejected by courts. With crowd photos, he embraced rioters as hard-working American patriots, but no answer on whether he will comply with a subpoena to testify.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:22]\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): You know, the Capitol has been totally overrun. Answer my question.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: NBC News obtained 42 minutes of documentary footage shot by Speaker Pelosi`s daughter. CNN obtained additional clips that show congressional leaders of both parties.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Get them there in one hell of a hurry. Do you understand?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Scrambling to get guard troops sent to the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: We need them there now, whoever you got.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: In another exchange --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Hi, Mr. Vice President.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Speaker Pelosi reaches \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I worry about you being in that Capitol building. Don`t let anybody know where you are.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: From concern, to fury at President Trump.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I want to punch him out and I`m going to go to jail, and I`m going to be happy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kelly O`Donnell joining us now. And there are also new legal developments, Kelly, in the search of the former president`s Mar-a-Lago estate.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL (on camera): That`s right. The Department of Justice is asking an appeals court in the Mar-a-Lago documents case to end the use of an independent reviewer of those materials seized that Mr. Trump had requested. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just 25 days before the Midterms, tonight, we speak exclusively with Dr. Mehmet Oz, the Republican facing off against Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in that critical Pennsylvania Senate race. Our Dasha Burns pressing Dr. Oz on crime and his campaign`s attacks on Fetterman`s health.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Pennsylvania Republican Senate candidate and celebrity doctor, Mehmet Oz, has focused much of his campaign on crime and blasting Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  on his votes as the head of the state`s Board of Pardons.\par \par \uc2 He says he believes in second chances, the former president with his First Step Act, also was about giving second chances to prisoners who deserve it. Why don`t prisoners who prove good behavior, who are modeled prisoners, why don`t they deserve second chances?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: The First Step Act had nothing to do with convicted murderers being released from prison, especially if they`ve been sentenced to life in prison. It`s a whole different game. When \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is asked, if you could wave a magic wand, what`s the one thing you would do, the one thing? And he says why get rid of life in prison?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, I asked him that. He actually said, it would be codifying Roe versus Wade and abolishing the Filibuster.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Well, he`s answered it the way I just described in the past as well. And too often, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  seems to pay more attention to the feelings of the criminals than the innocent who were hurt.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: We also asked about his campaign`s attacks on Fetterman`s health. He was off the campaign trail for months after suffering a stroke.\par \par \uc2 One of your campaign staffers said that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  wouldn`t have had a stroke if he had "ever eaten a vegetable in his life." You`re a doctor. You`re a heart doctor. You of all people understand what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is going through better than most. Why would you allow your campaign to mock him like that?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I have tremendous compassion for what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has gone through. I mean, not only do I, as a doctor, appreciate the challenges, but I know his specific ailment because it`s a specialty area of mine.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But would you ever talk to your patients like this?\par \par \uc2 OZ: No.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: He criticizes Fetterman for not releasing his medical records, while Fetterman accuses Dr. Oz of not being transparent on abortion rights. This is what he told us this week.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: Dr. Oz likes to make fun of me that I might miss a word. But you know, he`s missed, you know, two words. And that is a yes or no on the national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So, we asked him about Republican Senator Lindsey Graham`s bill that would ban abortions after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 If you win, you`re going to be a colleague of his. You`re going to have to vote on bills like this. So, can you tell us right now, yes or no on that bill?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I`m giving you a bigger answer than yes or no. I`m telling you. I don`t want any federal rules limiting what states do with abortion. It should be up to the states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Lester, there was more we wanted to discuss. We had to ask for the same amount of time Fetterman gave us about 30 minutes, but the campaign said they had time constraints, so we only spoke for about half that time. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Dasha Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the migrant crisis. The Biden administration announcing this week it will start expelling more Venezuelan migrants to Mexico. It comes as President Biden is under pressure to do more after a record number of illegal border crossings. Gabe Gutierrez reports now from Yuma, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Each night, they rushed to turn themselves in, group after group of asylum seekers crossing the Arizona border.\par \par \uc2 GISSELLE PALACIO, CUBAN MIGRANT: It`s beautiful coming to a free country.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Gisselle Palacio and her 3-year-old daughter came home from Cuba.\par \par \uc2 Why did you come?\par \par \uc2 PALACIO: I want to be free. I want to buy some food -- some food for my girl and work and deliver progress. American dream, you know?\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: This year alone is seeing hundreds of asylum seekers each night.\par \par \uc2 And in just the last year, there have been more than 300,000 illegal border crossings in the area around Yuma. That`s three times the city`s entire population of nearly 100,000. It`s an all-time record.\par \par \uc2 Is that sustainable?\par \par \uc2 [19:10:13]\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS NICHOLLS, MAYOR OF YUMA, ARIZONA: It`s not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Douglas Nicholls is Yuma`s mayor.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLLS: I definitely do consider this a crisis. It`s a situation where numbers are multiple times higher than anywhere else in history.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: The only hospital here estimates that over six months, it`s doled out $20 million in uncompensated medical care to migrants.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBERT TRENSCHEL, CEO, YUMA REGIONAL MEDICAL CENTER: We have to stop that border flow. If you`re going to have the borders open like they are, then there has to be a payment source for these individuals.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Officials here say they`re not getting enough help from the federal government. Arizona`s governor has spent $6 million to bring in these shipping containers to close gaps in the border fence. The state is also spending $83,000 per bus to send migrants to Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 FERNANDO QUEROL, AID VOLUNTEER: Politics play a big role.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: But aid volunteers like Fernando Querol say there is no chaos. There`s immigrant rhetoric in an election year.\par \par \uc2 QUEROL: None of these individuals are overwhelming, are overrunning the city.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Still, he views it as a humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 PALACIO: We spend like 25 days coming here.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: One that brought a desperately Gisselle Palacio and her family to U.S. soil in search of a dream enduring a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): Local officials say they expect border crossings here to rise again over the next few months as temperatures cool. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  addressing the situation in Ukraine while a key Putin ally, the president of neighboring Belarus also speaking out. Keir Simmons spoke with him exclusively.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, after bombarding Ukraine, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  suggesting he could cause more destruction.\par \par \uc2 There`s no need for massive strikes, he said, at least for now. As for the future, we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 We met her key Kremlin ally, Belarus President \uc2 Alexander Lukashenko\uc2 , who insisted his crews also film our exclusive interview, but with no restrictions on questions.\par \par \uc2 Do you think President Putin would use a nuclear weapon?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER LUKASHENKO\uc2 , PRESIDENT, BELARUS (through translator): If you put a person or a country in a corner, there is only one way out from there, to move forward? As far as the subtext of your question is concerned, never. Putin and the leadership of Russia never set the goal of using a nuclear weapon in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : How would you describe President Putin state of mind right now?\par \par \uc2 LUKASHENKO (through translator): Is that what you think in the western United States? He`s an absolutely adequate, healthy, athletic man.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : President Lukashenko was supported by President Putin after a widely discredited election in 2020. And this week, Lukashenko supported Russia, at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Civilians being targeted, women and children. How can Belarus justify voting against the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . resolution to condemn the Russian offensive?\par \par \uc2 LUKASHENKO (through translator): You know, there is an information war going on. The Ukrainians talk about mass graves, and the Russians similarly reveal mass crimes from the Ukrainian side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS\uc2 : And tonight, President Putin insisting his actions in Ukraine are correct and timely. They`re calling the situation unpleasant. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Keir, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, holiday sales already kicking off as spending cools down. How stores are getting you by now.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Spending by Americans remain flat in September which is bad news for retailers but it may be good news for holiday shoppers already being lured with deep discounts. So, is Black Friday a thing of the past? Here`s Miguel Almaguer with tonight`s "PRICE YOU PAY."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is Black Friday now.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): At some of the nation`s biggest retailers --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Black Friday deals for days.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: It`s beginning to look a lot like Christmas two weeks before Halloween. To jumpstart consumer spending that`s falling flat as inflation hits a 40-year, high big box stores like \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 , Target, and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  are tonight trying to unload excess inventory kicking off holiday sales earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:14]\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY PALMER, PERSONAL FINANCE EXPERT, \uc2 NERDWALLET\uc2 : Black Friday has already started because the sales have begun, the discounts have already been launched.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Some of the deepest discounts could come on electronics, appliances, and computers, deal that 50, 60, and even 70 percent off by the real Black Friday may be the price retailers have to pay to get consumers to buy.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If there`s a deal, of course, I`m just going to swipe that up.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: As families tighten budgets amid high-interest rates, steep gas prices and rising rents, more than 40 percent of Americans say they aren`t earning enough to cover the cost of holiday gifts this year.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Basically, retailers know that consumers are really stressed out financially by spreading out those sales over a multi-week, multi-month period. It makes it easier for consumers to keep spending.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: The best advice say experts, do your holiday shopping now and wait for the perfect sale. Last year, 50 percent of shoppers did their holiday shopping before Thanksgiving. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Miguel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, they`re a pivotal group of voters changing the political map. The race to connect with Latinos in our series "POWER OF THE VOTE."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with our series the power of the vote. And tonight, the battle over the all-important Latino vote. As the political landscape rapidly changes, Tom Llamas takes us to South Florida where some long- standing expectations about Latino voters are being put to the test in the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 [19:20:16]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In a state known for hurricanes, in 2020, a political earthquake hit Florida.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS POLITICAL DIRECTOR: This Latino thing is an issue here for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the presidency but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took nearly half of the Latino vote in Florida.\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2 , FORMER REPUBLICAN CONGRESSMAN FROM FLORIDA: Republicans figured out how to connect with Latino voters culturally. This is not about policy. This is about people`s view of the country.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A new NBC News Telemundo poll shows while Latinos lean Democrat and prefer Democratic control of Congress, support for the GOP is up 10 points over five midterm cycles. It`s why some are calling the Latino voter The Sleeping Giant.\par \par \uc2 One of the tightest races in the country is happening in this Florida congressional district that includes parts of Little Havana. More than 70 percent of the voters here are Hispanic and this election as two Latinas going head to head.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Maria Elvira Salazar\uc2 , a daughter of Cuban exiles and former news anchor, flip this district read in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The GOP has made so many gains when it comes to Latinos. Why do you think it`s happened?\par \par \uc2 REP. MARIA ELVIRA SALAZAR (R-FL): Well, because we`re not stupid. And we want the same thing as other Americans, security, good jobs, good economy.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Her opponent, State Senator Annette Taddeo grew up in Colombia and fled after terrorists kidnapped her father.\par \par \uc2 Do you think Democrats take Latino voters for granted?\par \par \uc2 ANNETTE TADDEO (D-FL), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Sometimes, yes, absolutely. As elections get closer, a bunch of money comes in at the last minute. I would like to see us all the time.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Part of the answer, recognizing there`s a diverse mix of Latino voters, many who fled socialist regimes.\par \par \uc2 What are the things that are -- the issues that are on the minds of voters right now in this district?\par \par \uc2 SALAZAR: Inflation, gas prices, good jobs. Number one is like, things are really bad.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: Democrats are also hoping anger over abortion rights drives voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 TADDEO: We all come from places where the government tries to tell you what to do. And the last thing we want is the government or politicians making decisions that are so difficult and private.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: And Taddeo is critical of her opponent for not speaking out about Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` decision to transport Venezuelan refugees to Martha`s Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 Do you think Governor DeSantis treated those refugees with dignity?\par \par \uc2 SALAZAR: I think Governor DeSantis, just like any other governor, has no other -- has -- they are desperate and their -- the choices on the table are very small.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: You think it was OK to ship. refugee families to Martha`s Vineyard where 50 people had one bathroom in a church and they were not prepared for them?\par \par \uc2 SALAZAR: I am not OK with what`s happening on the border period.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: A passionate fight for the Latino vote which could mean winning beyond this year`s Midterms. Tom Llamas, NBC News, Miami.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Up next, it`s being embraced by some of the biggest stars in sports. How Pickleball is going from a pickup game to pro.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBBIE COLTRANE, ACTOR: You`re a wizard, Harry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Sad news for Harry Potter fans. Robbie Coltrane, the Scottish actor who played Hagrid, the beloved half-giant gamekeeper at Hogwarts has died. Coltrane had a long career as an actor and comedian. He appeared in two James Bond movies and started the British crime series Cracker. He was 72.\par \par \uc2 And finally, what do Tom Brady and LeBron James have in common besides being sports legends? Like a growing number of Americans, they`re obsessed with pickleball. Emilie Ikeda explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANNA BRIGHT, PICKLEBALL PLAYER: The ball has to bounce.\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): 23-year-old Anna Bright is a product of Pickleball`s pandemic boom, picking up the game just a year ago. Now, she`s dominating the sport as a professional pickleballer.\par \par \uc2 BRIGHT: I probably netted between 100 and $125,000 since late February.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: So, you`ve made six figures within less than a year of playing pickleball.\par \par \uc2 BRIGHT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Once viewed as a pastime for the older athletes among us, pickleball, with its noisy pops of plastic, is somewhere between tennis and ping pong and proving it has no bounds. This is Major League Pickleball complete with color commentary.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Taddeo is flashing it all right now.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Today, the first of a three-day tournament in Columbus, Ohio. 12 teams, 48 players. The top prize, $100,000. Pickleball is considered the fastest-growing sport in the U.S. with nearly five million players picking up a paddle last year.\par \par \uc2 ANNE WORCESTER, STRATEGIC ADVISER AND BOARD MEMBER, MAJOR LEAGUE PICKLEBALL: There will be 40 million people playing pickleball by 2030.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: And some of the biggest names in sports are paying attention.\par \par \uc2 TOM BRADY, NFL STAR: All right, Pickleball.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Tom Brady, LeBron James, and Kim Clijsters are among the growing list of superstar pro athletes buying up professional pickleball teams.\par \par \uc2 What makes pickleball appealing as an investor?\par \par \uc2 KIM CLIJSTERS, FORMER PROFESSIONAL TENNIS PLAYER: The growth margin. I think it`s really cool to see that there is still so much potential. The reason why I did MLP is because it`s equal pay. Men and women are starting on an equal playing field.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Major League money is following the fans as professional pickleball takes its swing. Emilie Ikeda NBC News, Columbus, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It sounds like it`s time to get in the game. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221015eiae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc81}{\*\bkmkend toc81}\b \uc2 TODAY WITH HODA & JENNA for October 14, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Jenna Bush Hager \par \uc2 8,001 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [10:00:16]\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice over): Today, a Hollywood star who is doing it all, actress, entrepreneur, musician and mom, Zooey Deschanel.\par \par \uc2 Then, you`ll meet the breast cancer survivor showing her pink power to help other women of color.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the always funny Iliza Shlesinger gives us her take on motherhood marriage and more.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (voice over): It`s TODAY with Hoda and Jenna, it all starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Hi guys, it`s Friday, October 14th, middle of the month.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Smack in the middle of October.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Halfway to Halloween.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, halfway to Christmas. No? But we`re closer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Don`t you love a Friday?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s feeling like fall in the northeast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I know. Have you looked at the leaves yet?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You did?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Did you look at the leaves?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I saw them the other day.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: They`re changing.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Sometimes you get busy and you miss the most obvious thing in the world.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Don`t miss -- by the way, they`re right behind you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: They`re changing.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: They`re right behind us and they`re changing but yes, a Friday feeling.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, we hope you guys -- we hope you guys have a great weekend, savor and linger. Let your weekend last.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And look at the leaves.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Roll it out. Yes, enjoy.\par \par \uc2 All right, so, there is a gentleman in his 40s who is turning heads because he looks fine like wine.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, we`re talking about Chris Pine. He has a new hairdo.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, look at his before picture.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Which was kind of blonde.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Shoulder length.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Blondish.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But if you look deeply into the picture on the left, it`s very gray with blonde ends.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And he decided he was going to just kind of --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Go for it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Go for it.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Shave it all off.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. Wow. What do you think of -- do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: First of all, he`s not my favorite Chris. I know there`s a lot of Chris`s. Who`s your favorite again?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I can`t remember.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: There`s so many.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: There`s too many Chris`s. There`s so many.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: There`s too many Chris to even count. But anyway, now with that hairdo, he`s in the top two, or three.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: He`s moved. He was that (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I don`t know who else there is. There`s Chris Hemsworth. There`s Chris Stapleton.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Chris Pratt.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Chris Pratt. Anybody else from -- Chris Evans.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Chris Evans, of course. Chris Hemsworth.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: There`s a lot of Chris`s.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: There`s so many. Wouldn`t you hate to be a Chris? There`s just too many cute ones.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Kris Kringle.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK, you`re really getting weird as usual.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, I feel like Chris was that