`m sure I had these smock dresses that my grandma and my aunt would make. And I would always be wearing dresses. I liked dresses, and I liked the smocking with the little, you know, all those little like Christmas dress.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Can I see that picture one more time? Because I am noticing something else that`s similar, bangs. You`ve had bangs your whole life. Is that -- has that been your signature since you were little?\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: I mean, you know, when you`re a little kid they just cut your hair, right? And that`s how it was cut and then it just kind of looks better on me. I grew them out once and it didn`t look as I want to.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It wasn`t for you?\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: No. I look better with the bangs.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That was like no.\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: I looked into the mirror. I`m like nobody recognizes me with --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Proof that I have a forehead.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got really cool parents.\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: I do.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You hit the parent jackpot. How many years have they been married?\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: 50. Yes, they just had their 50th wedding anniversary this summer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh my God. So, what -- watching them, I mean, they`re obviously teaching you things about a relationship and how to keep a relationship work. What do you think you`re learning or you`ve learned from them?\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: We have like family dinners all the time. Yes, it`s, you know, they`re just a fantastic couple. And they both have their own independent things that they do. They`re both very independent people. But they always come together. And you know, they just like being together. So, I think that`s the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: There is an app that you are associated with that you -- that you`re crazy about, tell me about it.\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: Yes. So, I`m a co-founder of this app called Merryfield. And it`s kind of like couponing. But it`s easy. I feel like couponing is kind of hard. And you have to be good at math.\par \par \uc2 [10:25:00]\par \par \uc2 So, this is just specifically for better for you brands. When I had my first kit, I got really into, you know, health and wellness. And it really kind of upset me that it seemed like, you know, it was kind of difficult to eat healthy on a budget, and it felt oh my gosh, it was so expensive. I`m like, wait, why is it expensive to buy fresh food that`s healthy for you?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Right.\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: So, I started at a company called Lettuce Grow, and we do like hydroponic growing devices. So, I was like, I want to do 20 percent of my growing home, like grow food.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: But then what about the other 80 percent? So, Merryfield is like the solution for the other 80 percent. We give rewards and cashback for buying better for you, or ecofriendly brands.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So, if you buy healthy, something healthy you get?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, better our brands, and our brands are all vetted through like a very strict set of standards.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And yes, it`s been amazing. The response has been incredible. A lot of people who are saying, you know, these brands were kind of out of their reach, you know, they couldn`t afford them before now. Now it allows them to afford these Better For You brands and we`re helping people eat healthier.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: They feel better in the end.\par \par \uc2 DESCHANEL: I know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Zooey, it`s so good to see you. Congratulations on what`s happening in your life right now. I`m really happy for you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Jenna and I are going to sit down with a breast cancer survivor who`s inspirational stories touching so many lives, coming up after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:29:57]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: First, a look at how she created a whole new life for herself with a very meaningful mission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICKI FAIRLEY, FOUNDER, TOUCH, THE BLACK BREAST CANCER ALLIANCE: I tried to like let everything go, and just paddle, and just feel the water. And see to feel the sun or the wind. Feel God around me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voice-over): Ricki Fairley starts most of our days like this, alone, and at peace on the Chesapeake Bay. But this mother of two didn`t always know how to slow down. Like most caregivers, Ricki was constantly on the go.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I was a crazy busy typical mom, working mom. I actually had been married for 30 years, and I was the breadwinner for our family, traveling every week on the road.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: After a routine mammogram, the words triple negative breast cancer stopped her in her tracks.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: My doctor said, we found five spots on your chest wall. You are now metastatic, you have two years to live. Get your affairs in order. We don`t have anything for you.\par \par \uc2 And I said, well, I can`t really die right now. I have a daughter at Dartmouth. I have to pay for her tuition. So, me, you, and gotten some drugs or something, we got to work this out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It was a long road of surgeries, chemotherapy, radiation, and experimental drugs. But it worked. And Ricki was cancer free. She decided to quit her old life and start fresh.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I knew I had to get rid of all the cancers in my life. I divorced my husband of 30 years, I quit my business partner of 10 years. I sold my house in Alpharetta, Georgia with a pool and two acres. And I moved to my little one bedroom condo on the beach.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She also did a lot of research and learned about the disparity in breast cancer for black women.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: This disease for black women is a different disease. And now, we have a growing body of data that validates that a black breast cancer cell looks different from a white one.\par \par \uc2 We have a 41 percent higher mortality rate than white women. We have a 39 percent higher recurrence rate of breast cancer than white woman, which is crazy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So, with her new home, her new fiance and her new way of life, came a new purpose, a nonprofit called, Touch, The Black Breast Cancer Alliance.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I wanted to change the game on how the world thinks about black women and breast cancer. I want to advance the science. I want to stop the dying. I wanted to bring pharmaceutical companies to the table to say deal with this. Help us.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: 11 years after she was told she was going to die. Ricki is still cancer free and has been able to watch her oldest daughter get married and have a family of her own.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I work for my granddaughters. I don`t want them to ever think about this disease. I don`t want them to have to talk about it. I don`t want them to -- I don`t want it to be in their existence.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: As for her younger daughter, Hayley, Ricki got her wish and watched her graduate college. They now work side by side to help other women going through the unthinkable,\par \par \uc2 HAYLEY BROWN, DAUGHTER OF RICKI FAIRLEY: Seeing my mom, firsthand, being sick, and dealing with all of the things that come with it. And now, just the things that even come with survivorship. Just being a support to some for these women has been a large part of my mission.\par \par \uc2 And so, what I`m trying to do now for the breast cancer community is also create that safe space.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Looking back, Ricki has advice for her younger, busier self.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: Your peace nonnegotiable. Take care of yourself. Check your breath, give yourself a minutes, give yourself a pause. It`s OK to say no.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And as she sits with her family, four generations of strong women, Ricki`s mission is clear.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I`m a miracle. I know a miracle. I shouldn`t be here. So many women that with that had what I had didn`t make it. And I know that God left me here to do this work. To be an advocate for other women. It`s my purpose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Ricki, I mean, we are in awe of you. Your purpose came with that horrible diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: A huge change, you made so many changes that you`re probably too afraid to make your whole life. What -- was there ever a point of trepidation? Should I divorce my husband? Change all these things because of this -- of what happened to you.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: No, there was no trepidation. I knew -- I knew in my spirit, that I had to save my life. And I just went for it. I didn`t even think twice about it. And people said, are you crazy? Are you sure you should do all of this at one time? You want to think about it? I said, no, I have to go now.\par \par \uc2 JENNA BUSH HAGER, NBC ANCHOR (on camera): You know, the best thing about this is that you had the second life and you`re using it to help other women.\par \par \uc2 You -- those statistics that we heard in the piece are unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: You could have just read those and thought, OK. But instead, you`re fighting them.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And that just seems like an incredible mission.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: I worked for my granddaughters. I don`t want them to ever think about breast cancer and we have a lot of work to do to save black women. And I don`t -- I don`t want anybody to die of breast cancer. But we deserve mortality rate parity.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, I thought what you said after the diagnosis was like you, the doctor, me, God, medicine, whatever.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We have a lot of work to do.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We all -- we`re all going to have to work together on this one.\par \par \uc2 FAIRLEY: Right, right.\par \