u have --\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Yes, all Latina-owned, Hispanic-owned businesses, incredible small businesses that all celebrate their culture and our culture and heritage.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK, let`s start with Hoda`s favorite category the candle.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love a candle.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Candle as a candle. So, this is Albisa. The owners is a husband and wife duo Albert and Isa -- Isabel. That`s why the name comes from Albisa. Cute, right? Everything is inspired by their Cuban heritage and that nostalgia. So, these are all scents that we grew up with. So over here, there`s caficon niche (PH) candle\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Violetas.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Violetas is there`s a very popular perfume I wear as a baby.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Beautiful, smell it.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: It`s a violet perfume. So, it`s really cute. So these are -- isn`t it amazing? All the sorts of things that when you smell them, they kind of bring you back to home. Absolutely wonderful.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: OK. Let`s talk about these gorgeous ceramics.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Yes. So, Franca ceramics. This is owner Jazmin de la Guardia. She grew up in Paraguay to Cuban and Uruguayan parents. And the name of the brand comes from lingua franca. So, it`s that common language that we all speak. Her partner Sierra, they met in New York. And this just kind of was the perfect way of sort of bringing that together, bringing their cultures together. And these are absolutely gorgeous. Everything`s handmade in their Brooklyn studio.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: They`re gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I know. And I love the chunky mug. It`s like the perfect size, beautiful.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: And they do everything. They do tabletop, they do for the garden, for the home.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But also, to support local artists like this is very cool.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Exactly. I wish you would love that.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Shall we move to sodas?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: You guys a little thirsty? Yes. Agua Bonita, so this is by Kayla Castanedathat, Mexican American. She lost her job during a pandemic and decided to go back to her roots. Agua Fresca is a very popular Mexican beverage --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Yes. And it`s made with flowers and herbs. And what she did was -- is it feel good, right?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes, really good.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: So, these are recipes that were kind of inspired by her grandfather, her grandfather had diabetes. And so, she made this 70 percent less sugar. So, that everybody can kind of enjoy them. She`s also all about not letting anything go to waste so they --\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Is that a mango?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Yes, mango.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, my god, a mango.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: We`re like too distracted by the deliciousness. But yes, it`s so fantastic because it really -- everything`s in recyclable cans. All about doing good things for the earth, celebrating her culture and her heritage and her family.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Slightly healthy, right? OK, let`s move on to the beauty department.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: I`m a beauty. We love a beauty here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Male section.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: I know. So, this is Lights Lacquer. So popular beauty YouTuber Kathleen Lights. Kathleen Fuentes is her real name. And this is all about nail polish. She`s got press (INAUDIBLE). I`m a press on (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Are you wearing press ons right now?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: For like the past seven years that I`ve been here, I`ve always worn press ons.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: But your nails have always looked amazing.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: My nails have always been press ons. Yes. Because that`s amazing. So, you can have a beautiful manicure without going to the salon.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: How long does the press on last?\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: So, they can last for a few days up to a week or two? It really depends on what you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: How long to these last? Like a week --\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: These last me about a week.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, it`s cool.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: And then I love this. So, this is her Zodiac collection. You know, everybody wants to know their horoscope and all of that now. So, this is super cute. And this is all really inspired by her Miami, her Cuban background. It`s all a very sort of -- yes. Very colorful, very fun. You like it?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, I love it. All right.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, what about this makeup?\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: OK, so this is Alamar Cosmetics. So, the founder, Gabriela Trujillo, she was a makeup artist who always had a bag filled with other people`s makeup. And she was like, I want to start my own company. I want to do something that`s my own. So, she did this. Everyone was like, it`s a saturated market. She still made it happen. And all of these things against celebrate. The names are all kind of Spanish, Spanglish. It`s really all about kind of celebrating and things. I love the glasses.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: They feel good too.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: (INAUDIBLE) vibrant cool (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And yes, I like (INAUDIBLE) too it`s kind of little -- yes. All right let`s talk about jewelry.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Speaking of vibrant and exciting. Jam And Rica. So, this is Lisette Scott. She`s an afro-Latina woman. That name comes from Jamaica and Puerto Rico. So, she`s celebrating both sides of her culture. I mean, they`re just festive. They`re beautiful. I love the colors. I love the quality of them. And everything you can tell it`s just made with a lot of love. And it`s all about celebrating, again, our culture, our traditions, because that`s what Hispanic Heritage Month is all about.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: These are awesome products --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: By the way, awesome. These are beautiful.\par \par \uc2 RAMOS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: So, if you want to check out all of these awesome items, go to TODAY.com/Shop.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And we`ll be back right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [10:56:50]\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Next week, guys, music stars Brandi Carlile and Tanya Tucker.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Wait, what? Oh, my gosh. Plus, a big performance from Betty Who.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And I am so excited for Arden Cho, one of my favorite shows Partner Track.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: What?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She`s here.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Oh, gosh. It`s going to be a week. All right, have a good weekend, y`all.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Bye. Oh, time to go.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221015eiae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc82}{\*\bkmkend toc82}\b \uc2 Top Republicans Traveling To Georgia To Campaign With \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Amid His Abortion Controversy\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Peter Alexander \par \uc2 424 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Just four weeks until the Midterms, today`s Senate Republican heavyweights rally behind \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as he tries to weather the abortion controversy now overshadowing his campaign in all-important Georgia. Here`s Peter Alexander.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, in Georgia, Republican reinforcements arriving to pollster embattled Senate nominee \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): It`s especially good to be here with the next United States Senator from Georgia \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Key senators Tom Cotton and Rick Scott standing by the former football great as he faces an allegation that he paid for an abortion for an ex-girlfriend more than a decade ago, while now campaigning on his opposition to abortion rights. Walker has strongly denied the accusation, but today for the first time publicly acknowledged he now knows who the woman is. The senators tonight insists the controversy will not cost Walker the race.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): Inflation, crime, parents not being involved in schools. That`s what people are going to vote on.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Still, some state Republicans have criticized Walker. Lieutenant Governor Geoff Duncan writing, Walker has not yet earned my vote. He has four weeks left to change our minds. While, overnight, another critical clash over Ohio`s open Senate seat. In their first debate, Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , accusing Republican J.D. Vance of lavishing praise on former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): Ohio needs an ass-kicker, not an ass-kisser.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: While Vance attacked Ryan for voting 100 percent of the time with President Biden who remains unpopular with Ohio voters.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: We`re getting close to Halloween and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is put on a costume where he pretends to be a reasonable moderate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:10:07]\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER (on camera): \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  also tried to distance himself from President Biden saying he does not believe the President should run for reelection in 2024. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221014eiab000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc83}{\*\bkmkend toc83}\b \uc2 [09:00:19]\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Yamiche Alcindor, Jen Psaki, Brendan Buck \par \uc2 10,349 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:19]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, GUEST HOST OF NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): Our issues this Sunday, October surprises. In Ukraine President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  faces a huge setback after an explosion damages a bridge to a critical supply line to Crimea, as President Biden warning the risk of a nuclear attack is at its highest level since the Cuban missile crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We are closer to a potential use of a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: In the Senate race in Georgia, Republican hopeful \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  denies allegations he paid for an ex-girlfriend`s abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , (R) GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: The abortion thing is false. It`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Abortion rights now taking center stage in a race that could determine which party controls the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 , (D) GEORGIA: The people of Georgia need a senator who will stand with women.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And gas prices could spike ahead of Election Day after \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cuts oil production, putting Democrats on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: I was able to bring down gasoline down well over $1.60, but it`s inching up because of what the Russians and the Saudis just did.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I`ll speak with Republican Congressman \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  of Nebraska and Democratic congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  of Michigan.\par \par \uc2 Plus, presidential portrait.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD J. TRUMP: It helps when you`ve done nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Maggie Haberman of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and author of the new book "Confidence Man" is here with her new reporting on the former president`s document fight and an in-depth look at his rise to power, including Donald Trump`s changing views on abortion, reversing the position he once made right here on this program.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I`m very pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are Yamiche Alcindor, moderator of "Washington Week" on \uc2 PBS\uc2 ; former White House press secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 ; and Republican strategist Brendan Buck.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press With Chuck Todd."\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Good Sunday morning. I`m Kristen Welker, in for Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 Election Day is just 30 days away. And that means 30 more days of October and November surprises. The term "October surprise" was coined during the 1980 presidential campaign. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  worried \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  would engineer a last-minute release of U.S. hostages held in Iran. Carter, of course, did not, but the term stuck.\par \par \uc2 There are at least three stories unfolding that may produce a surprise this cycle, first the nuclear threat from Russia. President Biden warned in a private fund-raiser this week that "We have not faced the prospect of Armageddon since the Cuban Missile Crisis" in 1962. Then there are rising gas prices, now averaging just under $4 a gallon, which Democrats worry will be a sign to voters that there is trouble in the economy.\par \par \uc2 But it is of course the Georgia Senate race which produced the October surprise of the week, since it could determine the control of Senate. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the Republican nominee who`s been running as an anti- abortion candidate, opposing abortion even in cases of rape or incest, spent the week denying allegations by a woman that he paid for her abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 She now tells the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  he urged her to terminate a second pregnancy two years later. They ended their relationship when she refused. The woman, who is also the mother of one of his four children, shared a $575 receipt for her abortion procedure and a copy of a check from Walker reimbursing her, as well as a "get well" card he allegedly signed.\par \par \uc2 Over the week, Walker`s explanations have evolved.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never paid for an abortion. And it`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Here`s the card. And you signed it. Is that you?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: That -- that`s why I`ve said (inaudible) that looks like my signature. First of all, I never just put an "H" on anything. I never have.\par \par \uc2 If that had happened, I would have -- I would have said it. You know, there`s nothing to be ashamed of there. You know, people have done that. But I know nothing about it.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You said that, if this did happen, there`s nothing to be ashamed of. How do you square that...\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I never said -- I never said...\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: You said it this morning on (inaudible) show.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No, what I said -- I was talking about something totally different than did it happen. I said, when I -- with my ex-wife, in my past, nothing to do with what this woman said.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: So you`re saying that...\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I said this -- this here, the abortion thing is false; it`s a lie. If anything happened, that`s nothing to be ashamed of because my ex-wife and I have been the best of friends with her husband and my wife.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Republicans have largely stood by Walker, as Texas Senator Ted Cruz told my colleague Vaughn Hillyard, "I want every Republican running for Senate in this country to win."\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Republican Congressman \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2  of Nebraska, whose toss- up race for re-election is one of NBC`s most consequential races to watch.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Bacon, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. DON BACON (R-NE): Well, thank you, Kristen. Thanks for having me back on.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It is good to see you. I do want to start right there in Georgia, the headlines this week.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:05]\par \par \uc2 Given everything that we have seen, do you support \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, I sure do, more for the policy positions he`s going to take. Senator Warnock is one of the most liberal, progressive folks. He wants abortion on demand until birth. Only one in five voters support that.\par \par \uc2 Now, Herschel needs to be -- come clean and just be honest. We also know that we all make mistakes. And it`s just better if it -- if this actually did happen, say "I`m sorry" and ask for forgiveness. But this is also...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BACON: ... about positions.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But, Congressman, let me ask you.\par \par \uc2 BACON: And...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let me ask you this, though, Congressman. by supporting \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , given these allegations, is the GOP, are you sending a message that Republicans are willing to win at all costs?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, I think people mistakes. And if people acknowledge them and ask forgiveness, none of us are perfect. And also, we`ve got to realize that Senator Warnock has his own allegations.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But he hasn`t acknowledged that this is one of his mistakes. Do you want to hear more from him on this issue? It sounds like you still have questions.\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, I think it`s just best to be candid. I`ve learned that in my own life, be candid; be honest. But in the end this is going to be about policy positions. Senator Warnock is one of the most liberal people in the Senate. He wants abortion on demand until birth. Only one in five voters support that.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But do the allegations...\par \par \uc2 BACON: There`s also allegations against Senator -- well, let me get a word in here, or two, on this. Senator Warnock has his own allegations. What about spousal abuse? There are allegations out there, too. So I hope the media does just as much attention on these allegations on Senator Warnock and what his wife, his ex-wife, said he was -- she was abused by him. So I hope you`re showing some equal opportunity here with both these candidates.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And those are allegations that he, of course, has denied as well. But let me ask you more broadly speaking. Does this undercut the Republican Party`s broader message of being anti-abortion, when you have these conflicting things happening with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BACON: I think you`ve just got to stand on principle here. When I -- when I look at our own race right now...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But does it undercut that principle, Congressman? Does it undercut that principle?\par \par \uc2 BACON: It -- it can undercut. You want to walk the talk and talk the walk. You want to have cohesion with your message. But people also make mistakes. I`m surely not -- I`m not a flawless person by no means. I have made my own mistakes in life. And Herschel has, too. We all have. I think it`s better just to be honest.\par \par \uc2 But in the end, Americans do not want abortion on demand until birth. And I think that that`s the principle tjhat we need to focus on as a Republican Party. The average voter -- and I have seven different polls -- would like to see restrictions at about the 15-week point. I -- that`s where Europe is at right now; Germany`s at there -- is there. Our policies at the federal level are aligned with China and North Korea. And that`s where Senator Warnock`s at. That`s where my opponent`s at.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let me follow up with you on that point then. Can you say point- blank that you support Senator Lindsey Graham`s proposal for a federal ban on abortion after 15 weeks?\par \par \uc2 BACON: On principle I do. Because I think most voters support a 15-week ban. It`s where Europe is at. Most free countries have gone there. But the reality is the Senate will not be able to pass that because of the 60-vote -- you know, the filibuster. So most of these decisions are going to be made at the state level. Because that`s where decisions can be made. Whether you have a pro-abortion majority in the House and Senate or a pro- life majority in the Senate or House, you`re not going to get back the 60- vote threshold in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well...\par \par \uc2 BACON: So the reality is most of it`s going to be done at the state level.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But, Congressman, and I hear you, but I think a lot of people would be interested in the fact that you support this 15-week ban at the federal level, because Republicans have been arguing for 50 years now that this should be a states` issue. Now you`re going against that?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, the voters get to decide and they get to decide by selecting representatives at the state and the federal level. But on principle, I believe in the humanity of the unborn child. I think the science has taken us way beyond where Roe v. Wade had us. That`s why -- Roe v. Wade prohibited states from going the 15-week restrictions, for example. And most of the free world has -- has left America behind. The free world has seen the science of the unborn child and they`ve moved more towards a more reasonable policy of 12 to 15 weeks. I do believe America should go the same direction.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, let me follow up with you on that, because you co-sponsored the Life at Conception Act, which would effectively have given a fetus constitutional rights from the moment of fertilization. So why now support legislation that would allow over 90 percent of abortions to occur?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, on principle I am pro-life. And I`m always going to defend the humanity of the unborn. Now, the...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But isn`t supporting a 15-week ban in contradiction to also supporting the idea that a fetus has constitutional rights at conception?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, I want to -- I want to move us forward. You know, we have too many people in Washington that say "I want -- I demand 100 percent," and they won`t take 50 percent or 40 percent. I believe I want to move us in the right -- we want to move the right direction here. And I do believe in the personhood of the unborn.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:01]\par \par \uc2 However, this same law doesn`t prohibit state legislatures or the federal government to put in various restrictions or exceptions. It just says on principle the unborn child has humanity. And I believe that. But it still gives legislators the right to restrict or provide exceptions. But the American voters right now are at 12 to 15 weeks. I accept that reality and I want to move us forward.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congressman, let`s talk about foreign policy. This week -- of course you serve on the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 . You are a decorated Air Force veteran. We all learned this week that President Biden, at a closed-door fund-raiser with Democrats, warned of a nuclear Armageddon. Is that consistent with your perspective?\par \par \uc2 Is that your assessment of the threat right now?\par \par \uc2 BACON: I do think \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is a cornered animal. I think he is, right now, unpredictable, unstable. He is getting beat in Ukraine, piece by piece. And he`s -- he`s being embarrassed. His country`s being embarrassed, not only by being defeated but the barbaric behavior of his country in Ukraine, stealing their grain, sexually assaulting their women, murdering just innocent people. This is the view the world has towards Russia and Putin.\par \par \uc2 But I think he`s a cornered animal right now. He`s unstable. But you can`t let this guy threaten us and bully us around. Because if you cave out of fear, he`ll do it again.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let me turn to...\par \par \uc2 BACON: So we just -- we`ve got to stand firm with him...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Go ahead. Go ahead, finish, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, we`ve just got to stand firm. We`ve got to stand firm towards him. You can`t let a bully push you around.\par \par \uc2 I also do think President Biden`s got to be more careful, though. Throwing down the "nuclear Armageddon" was a little too flippant. President of France Macron chided him on it. And I think the president needs to be more cautious.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let me ask you about another campaign issue. Obviously the issue of crime has loomed large. I want to ask you about some comments that we heard from Senator Tommy Tuberville overnight at a rally with former President Trump. Take a listen. I`ll get your reaction on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOMMY TUBERVILLE (R-AL): Some people say, well, they`re "soft on crime." No, they`re not soft on crime; they`re pro-crime. They want crime. They want crime, because they want to take over what you`ve got. They want to control what you have. They want reparation, because they think the people that do the crime are owed that. (Bleep).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Are those comments appropriate for a sitting U.S. senator, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, I wouldn`t say it the same way. But there is a problem in our country with crime. Major cities have seen a 40 to 50 percent increase in violent crime. And the primary reason, in my view, is we have these far- left prosecutors, county attorneys and mayors who are releasing violent criminals back on the street.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But, Congressman...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congressman, there is a different way to say that. I mean, most people heard those comments...\par \par \uc2 BACON: I would be more polite.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: ... and felt -- not just polite, but a lot of people heard those comments as being racist.\par \par \uc2 BACON: Yeah, I`m -- that`s not the way I present things, but we`ve got to be honest that we have a crime problem in our country...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But -- but do you feel as though those comments...\par \par \uc2 BACON: ... prosecutors releasing...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: ... crossed a racial line, Congressman? Do they cross a racial line?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Oh, I don`t -- I don`t -- I`m not going to say he`s being racist. But I wouldn`t use that language, be more polite. But the fact is we can`t ignore we have a 40 percent to 50 percent violent crime increase. And it`s because Democrat politicians and prosecutors are putting violent criminals back on the street early. And it`s unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 When a guy commits a crime the first time, shame on them. When society releases that same criminal and they create more victims, that`s shame on us for allowing that to happen.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let me ask...\par \par \uc2 BACON: And too many Democrat politicians are releasing these criminals on the street.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let me ask you very quickly about what is happening inside your own party. According to our NBC News political unit, 219 House Republican candidates have actively questioned or outright denied the 2020 election results. Would you be comfortable serving alongside them?\par \par \uc2 BACON: Well, as you know, I certified the election. I supported the bipartisan commission. I condemned the violence that day. And so I just stand on my own record. You know, districts get to elect their own people. We have had people on the other side of the aisle call the 2016 election illegitimate. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  did that. We have the -- the lady for Georgia who lost her election still has not conceded defeat from two years ago, or four years ago.\par \par \uc2 So both sides are -- are guilty. I don`t like the hypocrisy, though, of focusing on just one side on the elections. I believe in protecting our elections. I have voted that way. I`ve spoke that way. But we tend to, and our media tends to focus on just the Republican part here, when you have \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  also questioning the legitimacy of the 2016 election.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congressman -- all right, well, let me ask you...\par \par \uc2 BACON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: ... last time you were on this show, you told Chuck that you would not support former President Trump for the Republican nomination in 2024. But I want to ask you, if he is the party`s nominee, will you vote for him?\par \par \uc2 BACON: We`ll get - when we get to that point, I`ll -- I`ll let you know. In the meantime, we`re going to have this election...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You`re up for re-election...\par \par \uc2 BACON: ... in 30 days.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: ... right now, Congressman. A lot of voters want to know where you stands on this issue. Will you supports former President Trump if he`s the nominee in 2024?\par \par \uc2 [09:15:17]\par \par \uc2 BACON: I have made my point for the primary. That`s as far as I want to go right now. In the end, we`ve got to win in November and take back the House and the Senate, to stop Joe Biden`s policy. We need to force him back in the middle. That is where my focus is at.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. Congressman Bacon, thank you so much...\par \par \uc2 BACON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: ... for joining "Meet the Press." Really appreciate it. And Democratic Congresswoman...\par \par \uc2 BACON: Yes, ma`am.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: ... \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  of Michigan joins me now. Her race is also rated a toss-up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISSA SLOTKIN (D-MI): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So I want to ask you about the political drama that is unfolding in Georgia. I was just talking about it with Congressman Bacon. And I want to ask you about the response from the Democratic incumbent, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , who has decided not to directly attack \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , to address, really, these claims repeatedly on the campaign trail. Given the high stakes of Georgia, is that a mistake, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: I don`t know. I mean, I think what Mr. Walker is doing himself is enough for voters to see, right? He`s being accused of something. He`s not admitting it, or he`s dodging. People are saying that he -- you know, people make mistakes, but he`s not admitting that mistake. I think just -- I understand the instinct to just let him stand in the spotlight and answer for his own record and not take that as a moment to just, kind of, double down on the attack.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: OK, well, abortion is obviously an issue that you care a lot about. You have talked quite a bit about it. And abortion is actually on the ballot in your state. In fact, the ballot proposal would enshrine a right to an abortion. And opponents argue the problem with it is that it`s too wide-ranging, that there are no limits on third trimester abortions.\par \par \uc2 So let me put this question to you directly, Congresswoman. Would you like to see limits on third trimester abortions?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: Yeah. I mean, the ballot initiative that we have says that you can have the right to an abortion up to the point of viability, and then afterwards it has to be because of the health of the mother deemed by a medical professional. So it`s not abortion on demand. It`s not through the ninth month, all these talking points that the Republicans are using because they know they`re on their heels on this issue.\par \par \uc2 So I support that ballot initiative. I think it`s written correctly, and it codifies Roe in a state where, without that, we`re facing a 1931 ban.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But, again, just on that issue of third trimester abortions, should there be restrictions to that?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: Yes. I -- I agree with the proposal, which says, up to viability, you have the right to make that decision on your own, but afterwards you need the input of a medical professional. I think that`s the right place for our state and, frankly, I think, for the country.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You have prided yourself on being a moderate Democrat. And I wonder if you think there is room within the conversation around abortion for Democrats who are anti-abortion, by John Bel Edwards or Representative \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: Yes. I mean, I think, for me, I represent a district that`s Republican-leaning. The vast majority of my constituents identify as pro- life. And I`ve been at plenty of events, as recently as yesterday, where people will say, "Look, I am a pro-life Democrat."\par \par \uc2 But I think the distinction is that they`re not trying to impose their will on this issue on other people, on other women, right? They have a personal conviction that they feel very firmly about, and I respect that, but they`re not trying to say that their will has to be imposed on the rest of all women. And I think that`s -- they have a place in this party. But I think they also will say very openly they don`t believe the government should make that decision for women.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let`s talk about one of the top issues for voters in races all across this country right now, Congresswoman. Of course that is the economy. Republicans, including your opponent, have accused you and Democrats of overspending, whether we`re talking about the COVID relief bill or the latest Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to that criticism, that Democrats have actually made inflation worse by overspending?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: Well, first of all, I think we can`t dance away from the fact that inflation is a real issue that`s affecting every single person in this country. I don`t know one family that isn`t making different decisions because of how bad inflation. And we don`t win any points by trying to pretend it`s not there. It`s there. I think, for me, there`s almost, like, a gleefulness about some Republicans, including my opponent, about how bad inflation is. There`s a gleefulness when prices of gas are going up. And I think that`s, just as a leader...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But, Congresswoman, I guess I`m asking, do Democrats bear some of...\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: ... not the right approach.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: ... of the responsibility? But do Democrats bear some of the responsibility? You control the White House, both chambers of Congress. Why shouldn`t voters hold Democrats responsibility for the economy?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: I -- I think they do. I think, while I think inflation is obviously a worldwide phenomenon, I am open to the fact that we have to make decisions here in the United States. Democrats are in power. We`re in the White House, in the House and the Senate, even if it`s slim margins.\par \par \uc2 So, sure, I do think that there is a rightful skepticism about particularly the last COVID package. And I think that`s why you`ve seen a lot of us in Congress really shy away from another COVID package, even when the White House was proposing one this past summer.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:05]\par \par \uc2 So I -- I own it, but I think it has to be reflected, you know, across the world, that it`s going -- it`s a post-COVID phenomenon that we`re trying to deal with the best we can.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Let`s talk about student loan debt. Are you comfortable with President Biden`s plan to forgive student loan debt, even though it`s likely going to add $400 billion to the debt?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: I did not support the proposal that the president put forward or the action that the president took forward. It`s not that I don`t understand that we have a crisis of college affordability. I represent Michigan State and five universities and colleges. But I thought it was a Band-Aid that didn`t get to the underlying problem. I was vocal about that. I -- I want to get to a better place on college affordability, but a one- off like this that doesn`t help, like, a current freshman at \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2 , to me is not -- it`s not the way I would have gone.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congresswoman, given what we saw from \uc2 OPEC\uc2  this week, the decision to cut 2 million barrels of oil per day, do you believe that President Biden`s trip to Saudi Arabia, that fist-bump diplomacy, do you think that was a mistake?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: You know, you have to try. I -- I appreciate his willingness to try. And there`s nothing more important right now to inflation than the price of gas. So you have to do it. But I think the Saudis have made a really strategic error. And I can`t say whether, you know, the \uc2 Mohammed bin Salman\uc2 , the Crown Prince of Saudi Arabia, over-promised to the president or if he just absolutely threw that promise out the window. Either way the Saudis are going to be living with this decision for a long time, right? We`ve been partners for 70 years. They had a strategic decision to make about whether to help the world economy and put more oil in there or to cut it back.\par \par \uc2 At best, they made a decision that didn`t help the rest of the world. At worst, they aligned themselves with Putin, so...\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congresswoman...\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: ... we have a problem.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Congresswoman, let me ask you, a little bit, about politics. You have said that Democrats will support President Biden if he runs again in 2024. But I want to ask you personally, do you plan to support President Biden if he runs again in 2024?\par \par \uc2 SLOTKIN: He`s the sitting president. If he decides to run again, I`m going to support him; the party`s going to support him. That`s -- you know, that is -- has a long history in our country.\par \par \uc2 But I have been very vocal, including with my own leadership in the House, that we need a new generation. We need new blood, period, across the Democratic party, in the House, the Senate and the White House. I -- I think that the country has been saying that. And, you know, I`m one of just a handful of members of Congress who hasn`t voted for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  as speaker. And I`ve said I think we need new leaders. I would love to see some Midwestern leaders in there, right? That`s been important to me, is to reflect the middle of the country. We`re here, too. And -- but I do think new blood is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 But if the sitting president of the United States decides to run, we`re going to support him.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: All right. Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , thank you so much for joining "Meet the Press," really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a reporter who has covered \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for decades, Maggie Haberman of the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , is here with fresh reporting and her new book "Confidence Man."\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:25:17]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : Under the Presidential Records Act, everything should come back, and there is no crime. You negotiate. Whatever you negotiate is fine. It`s a process. But they make it sound like the greatest crime in America was committed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome back. The Justice Department suspects former President Trump still has documents that belong to the government and possibly classified material beyond what was seized at Mar-a-Lago. Sources tell NBC News that the top DOJ counter-intelligence official recently communicated that concern to Trump`s legal team. The Justice Department has signaled in court filings that there may be documents missing. \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents found 48 empty folders marked "classified" when they searched Mar-a-Lago in August.\par \par \uc2 Author and \uc2 New York Times\uc2  senior political reporter Maggie Haberman has been covering Trump since her days at the New York Post" in the 1990s, her new book, "Confidence Man: The Making of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Breaking of America."\par \par \uc2 And Maggie is here. Welcome back to "Meet the Press." Here is the book. Maggie Haberman, congratulations.\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It`s so good to have you.\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So I`ve read it cover to cover.\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Fantastic book. And we are going to delve into it. But as we are sitting here, as you`ve just completed this, you`re still reporting on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And your headlines in recent days include the fact that DOJ officials believe that there are still classified documents at Mar-a-Lago, and that Mr. Trump may have been trying to cut some type of a deal.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  understands how much legal jeopardy he`s in right now?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: I actually do think he understands how much legal jeopardy he`s in. And then -- and then I think he convinces himself that it`s not that bad.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that he spent $3 million on a retainer for a lawyer, whose advice he has sometimes not listened to, I should note -- but the fact that he was willing to do that, I had people who worked for Trump in the 1990s reach out to me to say this is the biggest legal retainer he has ever paid, as far as they knew. So I think that speaks to how concerned he is.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, I think it`s a significant point you make, because you really detail all of the legal battles that he has had, going back to the 1990s. And this moment, you`re making the point, feels different.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about one of the passages in your book that relates to this current moment. It relates to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who Trump hand-picked to run for Senate in Georgia. You asked him about what you called a complicated personal history that worried Republicans from the bat.\par \par \uc2 So this was a conversation from a year ago in your book. Former President Trump says to you, quote, "I don`t think it`s a problem today."\par \par \uc2 You asked, "Why is that? Why do you think that`s changed?"\par \par \uc2 The president responds, "Because the world is changing."\par \par \uc2 You write, he did acknowledge that it was changing. He didn`t acknowledge that it was changing because he had helped change it.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, Maggie, a lot of people have compared this moment to the "Access Hollywood" moment from Trump`s candidacy. Is that a fair assessment, do you think?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: I think it`s an understandable benchmark that people are looking toward. And I should note that, when we had that conversation, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Senate minority leader, was still not on board with Walker. I think he, sort of, threw in the -- the towel eventually and got behind him. I don`t know that everything is the same as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s what we`re going to find out. There is -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I still believe, there are people who can follow his playbook; there are people who can try to be like him. More often than not, they are people who are celebrities, either in the world of film or television or sports. But Trump, I still think, may be sui generis in that he was part of the pop culture fabric for so many decades. And also, the nature of these allegations are not the same as the "Access Hollywood" tapes. And that was one of a number of controversies that Trump had survived. This feels a little different, but we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: One of the things that makes your book so fascinating to read is that you do go back to the beginning. And you describe a portrait of a man who has wrestled with the truth since his very early days. And you have this anecdote, when he goes to the dedication of the Verrazano Bridge, with his father. He`s a freshman in college at the time. And then, years later, he talks to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporter about it and he gets all sorts of details wrong, the weather, things that were said at that dedication.\par \par \uc2 And you write, "He revealed himself to be an unreliable narrator of his own history from its early moments. His comments went unchecked for years, but for good reason. Why would anyone think such details might be untrue?"\par \par \uc2 Where do you think that impulse comes from, Maggie, from the former president, to not tell the truth about things?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: It`s a great question, but it has been with him for a very, very long time. I think there are parts of it that he absorbed -- this is before Roy Cohn, his famous fixer and lawyer, was in his life. I think some of it is what he absorbed from his father, honestly, who knew -- you know, was a very effective businessman but knew how to cut corners and knew how to handle things certain ways. Trump just takes everything to an extremely different level.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And so exactly why it is that he seems to get- have this need and get this charge out of saying something that isn`t true, or other times convinces himself something is the case because it feels better. And then still other times, it sounds better.\par \par \uc2 And I talk about that in the book, that sometimes he tells people, say XYZ. It sounds better. It`s all of the above.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And of course, we saw that play out in the oval office, as well, as he struggled with the truth over and over again. Now you have 219 candidates who fall into the category of election deniers. Do you think even Mr. Trump understood that he would have that impact on the party?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: I don`t. I mean, I think that he looks at everything in terms of what works for him in short, ten-minute increments of time. I think that the effect that it could have on other people, I think he delights in it. I think he likes feeling like he`s powerful and like he`s shaping the party. But I don`t think that`s the reason, and I don`t think that`s the long-term strategy.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: You also talk about his relationship with race over time. Goes back to his father. You also have this anecdote about how he pitches this idea to have an apprentice show that`s divided along racial lines, African American candidates versus Caucasian candidates. And he even confuses some Democratic staffers of color who are in the oval office with some of the serving staff. How do you think Mr. Trump has influenced race in America?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: I think if you look at that clip that you played earlier in the show of Senator Tuberville saying what he said, I can`t imagine someone -- he`s a former high school football coach. I can`t imagine someone saying that a few years ago. I think he has created a permission structure for people to say what used to be known as the, quote/unquote, quiet part, in race-baiting politics, out loud. And now it`s just the doors are blown open.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: I want to talk to you about yourself, Maggie Haberman the reporter. This has been covered, but I do want to delve into this, which is that the former president has said of you, I love being with her. She`s like my psychiatrist.\par \par \uc2 You`ve kind of dismissed those comments. You`ve said that`s flattery. He says this to a number of different reporters.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, he does actually reach out to you more directly, arguably, than most other reporters. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: I think he`s fixated with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ". And I think that I am the person who covers him more often for the paper and who he identifies with the paper more. But it really is about this decades-old outer borough New York City guy trying to make his way in Manhattan and saw "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " as synonymous with the elites who looked down on him.\par \par \uc2 He had this line that he said to the publisher of "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", and we made this public back in 2019 when he was president, over the Resolute Desk, he`s talking about our- he`s complaining about coverage, which as you know, every president does. But in Trump`s case, he was complaining, I think I`m entitled to a good story from my paper, and if you want the clearest distillation of how he views "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ", that`s it.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And very quickly, you have said you think it`s more likely than not that he will run again in 2024. Is there anything other than health that you think prevents him from doing so at this point, Maggie?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: I think a fear of losing and I think a disinterest in the process the way it is now. You know, there is a bitterness around him and a quickness to anger. That you know, he was always pretty quick to anger, but it has gotten more intense.\par \par \uc2 Those are the reasons I could see. You know, something unforeseen, like something related to one of these cases, these legal cases, maybe. But I think he has backed himself into a corner and has to run.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Fascinating, fascinating stuff. It`s a fascinating read, "Confidence Man".\par \par \uc2 Stick around, because you`re on our panel. We have a lot more to discuss with you, Maggie.\par \par \uc2 Up next, with 30 days to go until the midterms, the battle for Senate control is heating up as the candidates finally debate the issues. Our panel is here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:36:28]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: We are back now with the panel. Maggie Haberman is still with us.\par \par \uc2 And joining us are: Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News Washington correspondent and moderator of "PBS Washington Week"; Brendan Buck, former adviser to Speaker`s \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 ; and \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , former White House press secretary and an NBC News contributor.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to all of you for being here.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche, let`s start with what`s happening on the ground in Georgia. You were there for many days. Is this \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  controversy resonating, or do you think he survives it?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, certainly a lot of people in Georgia are talking about it. But people, based on my reporting, are already in their camps. If you`re someone who didn`t like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  like Lisa Gordon, this African American woman, who told me she had never even considered \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  because he was facing a host of domestic abuse claims. She didn`t think that he had the character to serve in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But I didn`t meet one Republican voter who was changing their mind. And that means, everyone that I talked to said, I don`t like this. Or maybe it`s made up. Maybe he`s telling the truth. Either way, it`s not changing my vote.\par \par \uc2 And I`m thinking about this one voter; her name is Linda Oviot. She`s an elderly white woman. She told me, 100 percent I believe these allegations. I think \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is lying right now.\par \par \uc2 She told me, I`m still voting for him. People make mistakes. He`s now someone who is an opponent of abortion rights. And as a result, I`m going to continue to stick with him.\par \par \uc2 And really, I think this is about people seeing politics as tribal, seeing \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  as a means to an end, and that end being more restrictions on abortion, maybe a better economy, maybe harsher policies on crime. Name it. That`s what Republican voters are telling me.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Well, and they also see it as a way to win the Senate. And Brendan, top Republicans are standing by him.\par \par \uc2 I want to play a little bit of the sound that we heard from top Republicans this week and then get your reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you stand by \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): I believe Herschel Walker`s going to be the next senator. I`m proud to support \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: But do you want them to elect that Republican?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Hell yes, I want every Republican running for Senate in this country to win.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): Herschel`s going to win. So they`re going to throw everything under the sun at him. Herschel is a great candidate. He`s focused on the right issues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And NBC News has now confirmed that Scott and Tom Cotton are going to be campaigning with him this week.\par \par \uc2 You`re from Georgia. You`ve been talking to your sources down there. What are folks telling you about how big of an impact this is having and whether this type of full-throated support may be risky for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN & BOEHNER: Well, they`re certainly circling the wagons, and that`s because of what`s at stake here. This seat could decide who controls the Senate. So it`s not surprising at all that \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , whose job is to get Republicans elected, is backing him.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not hard to run into a \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , Republican governor, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  voter in Georgia. And that`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think this is going to change a lot of things. I think it is relatively baked in. But you have to remember, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was losing before this happened. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  needs to make up ground in the last month. People are going to be starting to vote very soon in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And the reason that the Republicans have been doing poorly in Georgia recently is the northern Atlanta suburbs. I know Yamiche spent some time in Marietta, Alpharetta, Roswell, these areas where Republicans have been losing educated women particularly. And this is a candidate who is raising the issue of abortion. That`s not going to help him get back to where he needs to be to get over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And 1 or 2 percent percent, it makes all the difference in Georgia, right? Let`s also remember, all you need is that to make -- to win this seat. So that`s even if people are baked in. One or two percent of women changing their mind and Warnock wins --\par \par \uc2 WELKER: It makes a big difference.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: But there`s also a potentially very long way to go here because this in all likelihood will go to a runoff in the beginning of December. And so while we`re looking at this last week and questioning, is it a game changer? Is it not a game changer?\par \par \uc2 Frankly, I think Senator Warnock is probably doing the smart, strategic thing as of this moment, which is kind of letting \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wallow in his muddy pit of scandal.\par \par \uc2 [09:40:02]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: So you don`t think he should take the gloves off at this point?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I actually think, for the moment, and you always have to reevaluate in campaigns. You evaluate week by week.\par \par \uc2 But again, this is -- could be a long call. What he is going to have to depend on, Senator Warnock, that is, is whether there`s going to be big spending to keep this in the minds of voters over the next month to two months. And then he may have to decide that he may have to take the gloves off, at some point.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t think he needs to do that now. And I think he`s doing the right thing by letting \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  just wallow in his muddy scandal pit on his own here.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Maggie, one of the things that makes this moment so interesting is that it`s sort of like celebrity candidates on display, a lot of them handpicked by Trump. We talked about obviously \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , but also Dr. Oz is another one.\par \par \uc2 Is this a key test of celebrity candidates, and does it underscore kind of the challenge with it if you`re not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: I think there`s a baseline challenge to that. Number one, he has been in public life as a celebrity for a very long time, dealing with the national media in a way that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was not, but in a way that Dr. Oz was more, right? I mean, so it just depends on which race you`re talking about and the specifics.\par \par \uc2 The Oz-Fetterman race is very different than what`s happening in Georgia. I think that you`re going to have to isolate out these specific races. I don`t think that there is a blanket takeaway from this. I do think you will see more celebrity candidates, particularly among Republicans jumping in. And that is the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  effect.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: That is a very good point.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about the battleground map broadly, now. It runs through nine key states. In order to win back control, Republicans have to flip one of the Senate seats currently held by Democrats in Georgia, Arizona, Nevada, or New Hampshire while holding on to seats in Pennsylvania, Ohio, North Carolina, Florida, and in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, Republican Senator Ron Johnson, like many Republicans, is trying to put the focus on crime.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this recent exchange from a debate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Whenever I see a police officer, I go up to them and I say, thank you for your service. And if I have time, I say, please, don`t be dispirited by the loud few who are trying to defund you.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. MANDELA BARNES (D-WI): I`m sure he didn`t have the same interaction with the 140 officers that were injured during the January 6th insurrection. Let`s talk about the 140 officers that he left behind because of an insurrection that he supported.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Brendan, what do you make of that point/counterpoint?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, crime is a big issue. And we talk a lot about inflation; we talk about abortion. Most Republican candidates are running on crime. And particularly in Wisconsin, it is working to devastating effect on Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 Republicans -- Ron Johnson is someone who should be very vulnerable, but he has been hitting this crime issue. And in some places, it`s just going to work better than others. And in Wisconsin, it is working.\par \par \uc2 I asked somebody, a plugged-in political operative in Wisconsin, why is it working so well there? He said, one word, Kenosha.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Jen?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: I would say I agree with Brendan that crime is an issue that Democrats have to speak to. I think there was a missed opportunity there for Mandela Barnes. When he talked about the insurrection, I think that was a little disconnected from what people in Wisconsin are feeling.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Milwau -- crime is up in Milwaukee. If I were him or advising him - -which I`m not -- I would say, you should have made it about the use of gun violence and the fact that your opponent has done nothing to address gun violence. And that is what is showing rising crime in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Because that`s what people are thinking about. Their fears when they go out their door, less the insurrection, honestly.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: There has not been a more potent political issue that I can remember in recent years than Defund the Police. And while Mandela Barnes may have not said he wants to defund the police, he does have a record of wanting to redirect funds away from police. And that is going to hurt, and that is showing up in the polls.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But here`s the interesting thing. We had a country where a man was murdered, strangled, George Floyd. We had a country who then debated race relations and policing, and that backfired.\par \par \uc2 So defund the police, while Democrats have continuously said --most Democrats have said -- we don`t support that, it stuck to them, because some people in this country were deeply uncomfortable with the fact that we were talking about the fact that Black people get killed at disproportionate rates than white people.\par \par \uc2 We should also remind people that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just this week said, murders are up. Violent crime is down. So a lot of this is about fear. But it`s fear on both sides because Republicans are saying, you should be fearing crime. You should be fearing the border.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are saying, you should be fearing your personal privacy, and you should be fearing threats to democracy.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Yes. It`s certainly the closing arguments that we are seeing.\par \par \uc2 All right. Stick around, guys. We do have a lot more.\par \par \uc2 But when we come back, Elon Musk`s potential \uc2 Twitter\uc2  takeover and the surprising reality about how much it influences our politics.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:17]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome back. It`s looking more and more like Elon Musk, the richest man on the planet, is going to purchase \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , kicking off conversations about what that could mean for the social media platform that plays a significant role in our country`s political discourse.\par \par \uc2 That role is thanks in part to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who tweeted over 26,000 times as president before being removed from the platform after the January 6th Capitol insurrection. But although \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has played a dominant role in American politics, its reach is actually much smaller than you might think.\par \par \uc2 Less than a quarter of Americans say they ever use \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . That`s a small fraction of some of the more dominant social media platforms like \uc2 YouTube\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2 . And of Americans who actually use \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , data shows a minority of very active users are producing the overwhelming majority of tweets.\par \par \uc2 The top 25 percent of American \uc2 Twitter\uc2  users are producing 97 percent of tweets. The other three quarters of the platform`s users are just simply reading them.\par \par \uc2 Data also shows that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  users lean pretty far left. About 32 percent of adults who say they are Democrats or that they lean Democratic say they use \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . That compares to 17 percent of Republicans, creating a 15- point partisan gap.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  comes closest to having a balanced audience. Musk has indicated he plans to reinstate Donald Trump`s account, relax some of the platform`s content moderation rules -- which would likely allow a lot more disinformation -- and enhance its paid subscription option.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, if Musk succeeds, how will his changes affect \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as a platform and its relevance to American politics today?\par \par \uc2 When we come back, a look back at Donald Trump`s changing views on abortion, reversing the position he once made right here on this program.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: Well, look. I`m very pro-choice. I hate the concept of abortion. I hate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:52:52]\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 And the panel`s still here with me. I want to start this discussion by talking about President Biden and his wild week from the decision by \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to cut oil production, Jen, to these revelations at the end of the week in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " that investigators believe they do have enough evidence to charge \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s not clear he`s going to be charged, but how large is this looming over the president, over Democrats broadly?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, it was a big week here in Washington. So much news. I don`t even know how you decided what to talk about this morning, Kristen.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, you know, I looked at a bunch of local front pages this morning. And if you look at the front page in Nevada, they`re talking about Trump`s rally there and what it means for candidates in turning out to vote.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the front pages in Pennsylvania, they`re talking about, Mastriano, does he have a grassroots campaign or movement going? And obviously, Georgia is quite focused on every latest development in \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So the truth is: As much as there was so much news happening in Washington this week, it doesn`t always translate and often doesn`t translate to what voters are talking about in states. And I think that`s what we`re seeing currently.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: What will probably translate what happened this week is with gas prices. One of the --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: True, the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  decision.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: One of the big pieces of news this week was that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  is cutting back production at the worst possible time for Democrats. And gas prices -- if you want to figure out the direction of politics, watch gas prices. And Democrats did better during the summer when they started to tick down.\par \par \uc2 It`s very likely they`re about to start going back up, and that`s bad news for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And I was just talking to a voter who told me, at the end of the day, it`s the party in office that`s going to be blamed for this.\par \par \uc2 And we know what White House officials have told me, that they look at this every day. And it`s a neon sign on the roads across America. What is the price of gas?\par \par \uc2 My friend in- I have a friend who`s in Arizona. Just sent me a screenshot of $7 gas prices there, just talking about how worried she is. She`s not someone who`s deeply into politics, but she`s someone who really notices when the economy is going one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Administration officials say, first thing they do is look at the gas prices in the morning.\par \par \uc2 I do want to play this sound bite and pivot back to former President Trump. This is from 1999, right here on this program in October with Tim Russert and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [09:25:01]\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIM RUSSERT, FORMER NBC MODERATOR: Partial birth abortion, the eliminating of abortion in the third trimester. Big issue in Washington. Would President Trump ban partial birth abortion?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, look. I`m very pro-choice. I hate the concept of abortion. I hate it. I hate everything it stands for. I cringe when I listen to people debating the subject.\par \par \uc2 But you still -- I just believe in choice. And, again, it may be a little bit of a New York background because there is some different attitude in different parts of the country. And, you know, I was raised in New York and grew up, and work, and everything else in New York City. But I am strongly for choice, and yet I hate the concept of abortion.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: But you would not ban it?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No.\par \par \uc2 RUSSERT: Or ban partial birth abortion?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: No, I would -- I would -- I am -- I am pro-choice in every respect and as far as it goes, but I just hate it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Maggie, one of the things that hits me when you watch that clip, of course, it is former President Trump who appointed the three conservative Supreme Court justices who made it possible to overturn Roe v. Wade. And you were the first to report that Mr. Trump realizes the political place that that puts Republicans in in these midterm elections and possibly 2024.\par \par \uc2 HABERMAN: There`s no question that Republicans are the dog that caught the car on this issue. And you see it with any number of candidates who are having trouble talking about it. Because, while I don`t think it is going to rise to the same level as the economy in midterms, it is animating a lot of Democrats to vote.\par \par \uc2 What strikes me actually, listening to that clip, and I`ve listened to it several times, is he`s taking a very liberal position. He`s basically saying no limits on abortion. And this was, you know, 20-some-odd years ago. But he`s also just not answering the question. I mean, he`s basically trying to answer the question by saying, "Pro-choice, hate abortion," and Russert is trying to push him on specifics, and Trump is evading that.\par \par \uc2 And that was part of how Trump was successful in 2015 and 2016, was he, you know, people would present him with this menu of options because he was so vague and jumping all over the place, and so much of his political career and frankly all of his career was about sort of bluffing and faking his way through things. And that was another example.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: And, Brendan, obviously that was one of the things that made Republicans so nervous when he first announced his candidacy. And one of the points that Maggie makes in her book is actually, there was a lot of buzz about whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was going to run for the office of the presidency going back to 1999, right? But this issue has loomed very large over him.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Sure. And I think you can look at this issue narrowly at abortion, or you can look at it I think, which is probably the better way to describe where Republican politics is right no, and it`s a culture war.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of people out there who believe that if you don`t support abortion, if you`re a Christian, that there are people out to get you, there are elites in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has very successfully taken the mantle of the person who`s there to defend. And it speaks to what Maggie spoke about earlier. He saw himself as an outsider in the outer boroughs trying to get into Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 And so, this rich billionaire, how is he the working man`s person? He`s very successfully said, I am fighting for you. And people will go -- they really don`t care what he says at this point. They are going to stand with him because of that.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: But, what you`re seeing on the other side, which is also interesting, and it reminds me of the Affordable Care Act debate, is that when something is taken away it makes people very angry. And that`s what you`re seeing among Democrats.\par \par \uc2 But also you`re seeing in places like New Hampshire -- which is a fascinating place in the abortion debate to me -- it`s more people saying, like, get out of my uterus. Live free or die. We live in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 So this debate, I think, has become much more politically palatable for Democrats, and I think we even thought when I was sitting in the White House when these leaks were happening back in the spring.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And quickly, you also have the fact that women in this country, they had all these labels. But now, those labels don`t make sense anymore because some people want restrictions and some want more limits.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: What a great conversation. We are out of time. Thank you all.\par \par \uc2 And thank you for watching. Chuck Todd will be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020221009eia900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc84}{\*\bkmkend toc84}\b \uc2 Embattled Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Defiant In The Face Of New Accusations From The Ex-Girlfriend Who Claims He Paid For Her To Have An Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Kristen Welker, Allie Raffa \par \uc2 708 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER: All right, well, now to several new twists and what is arguably the most closely watched Senate race of next month`s midterm elections. Overnight, Georgia`s Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , speaking out to NBC News and standing by his claims that he didn`t know an ex-girlfriend had an abortion more than a decade ago. This is the same woman now says Walker wanted her to have a second abortion years later but she refused. NBC`s Allie Raffa is following all of these developments from Atlanta for us this morning, Allie, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:00]\par \par \uc2 ALLIE RAFFA, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Kristen, good morning. Democrats controlled the Senate by just the thinnest of margins and keeping that hold could easily come down to this crucial Peach State Senate race where in just a few days has gone from too close to call to downright roiled in allegations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RAFFA (voiceover): This morning, all eyes on Georgia, the crucial Senate race between Trump-back to Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and incumbent Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock roiled in controversy after bombshell allegations this week by The Daily Beast that Walker paid for a former girlfriend`s abortion in 2009. And a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report Friday that Walker pressured this same woman to have another abortion two years later, but she refused.\par \par \uc2 Walker, who`s running on a staunch anti-abortion platform, repeatedly denying both claims.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: I`m not going to back down. These things are way, way too high.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA: But in a new twist overnight, the former football star confirming to NBC directly that his wife and the woman making the allegations revealed to be the mother of one of his children, were in contact Friday morning, the woman telling Walker`s wife that he paid for her abortion. Walker sharing copies of the text exchange with NBC News and saying "I`m not saying she did or didn`t have one. I`m saying I don`t know anything about that. I don`t know."\par \par \uc2 Walker telling NBC this was the first time the woman ever mentioned the abortion to him or his wife. But the woman showed the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and The Daily Beast a copy of a receipt from an Atlanta abortion clinic. A check that she claimed was reimbursement for the procedure. And the get well card she claims came from the former football star. We should note we have not seen this documentation.\par \par \uc2 Walker telling NBC News "I don`t remember any card or check or anything. But I was dating her. I could have sent some money. I could have sent a card. But not for the reasons she is saying." Despite the spectacle, most congressional Republicans are still backing Walker.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ, (R-TX): \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to win that race. This is an effort from Democrats to hold on to power.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA: While state Republicans` frustrations only grow.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. GEOFF DUNCAN, (R-GA): Even the most staunch Republicans I think are rattled at the continued flow of information.\par \par \uc2 RAFFA: Just 32 days before voters have their say.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RAFFA: As of now, Walker doesn`t have any plans this weekend to meet with voters. But ultimately the choice is theirs. Republicans here tell me they don`t think this controversy will have any impact on this Senate race, while Democrats say this is just another reason they don`t think Walker is fit for Office, Kristen, Peter.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Allie Raffa, at the center of the political universe right now in Atlanta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221011eia80002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc85}{\*\bkmkend toc85}\b \uc2 The New Twist In The Herschel Walker Firestorm\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Yamiche Alcindor \par \uc2 643 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:05:15]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: With a little over A month until the Midterms, a growing firestorm in Georgia of a Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . the woman who says Walker paid for her abortion is now making a new allegation which Walker is strongly denying. Yamiche Alcindor is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): After a campaign event in Georgia, tonight \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  defiant.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: And I`m not going to back down. The stakes are way, way too high.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Following the latest twist and the controversy surrounding the Republican Senate candidate and former football star. The Daily Beast reporting the unnamed woman who the legend Walker paid for her abortion more than a decade ago says she`s also the mother of one of his children.\par \par \uc2 Previously, she provided the news outlet with a receipt from an abortion clinic as well as a check and a get-well card she says are from Walker. She has not revealed her name citing privacy concerns, and NBC News has not verified her story nor the documents. Walker, an abortion rights opponent, again strongly denying the accusation.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I know this is untrue. I know nothing about any woman having an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But Walker`s son, Christian, has fiercely criticized his father.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER, SON OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Don`t lie on the lives you`ve destroyed and act like you`re some moral family man. Y`all should care about that, conservatives.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Today, we pressed Walker.\par \par \uc2 What`s your reaction to people so close to you, your son, a mother of your -- someone who says that she`s the mother of your kid saying that you`re lying. What`s your reaction to people so close to you saying don`t trust you?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Just like what I just said. Democrats are desperate for this seat. This seat is important.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: They`re your family.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: They`re very desperate for to seat. I love my family. I always love my family. I`m going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: The Daily Beast says the anonymous woman, a registered Democrat, who says she`d had a years-long relationship with Walker thinks his denials are quote ridiculous. Many National Republican standing by Walker slamming it, and said it`s Democratic distraction from issues like inflation and crime.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to win that race. This is an effort from Democrats to hold on to power.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But some state Republican starting to voice their unease.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. GEOFF DUNCAN (R-GA): Even the most staunch Republicans I think are rattled at the continued flow of information.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Meanwhile, voters in Georgia sounding off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s 100 percent true right now, but we don`t know a whole story. I`m voting for Herschel. I mean, he made a mistake.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The only reason \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is on his ballot right now is because he supports Trump. If you support Trump, you can have any kind of morals in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Today, Walker`s opponent, Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock called these allegations, "disturbing." And he`s reiterated his support for abortion rights. Next week, both men are scheduled to face off in a debate. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Yamiche, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221007eia6000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc86}{\*\bkmkend toc86}\b \uc2 THE NIGHTLY NEWS for October 6, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kelly O`Donnell, Yamiche Alcindor, Ken Dilanian, Sam  Brock, Cal Perry, Miguel Almaguer, Gabe Gutierrez, Emilie Ikeda, Maggie  Vespa \par \uc2 3,893 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the historic move from President Biden toward decriminalizing marijuana.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voiceover): The President announcing he`s pardoning everyone federally convicted of simple marijuana possession, impacting thousands of Americans. And what he`s ordered top cabinet officials to do, signaling a major shift in U.S. policy on pot.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, news on the president`s son \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . New reporting that the feds believe there`s enough evidence to bring a criminal case against him. What charges could he face.\par \par \uc2 The new twist in the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  firestorm. The woman who said the GOP Senate candidate paid for her abortion now saying she also had a child with him. How the former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star is responding.\par \par \uc2 The deadly stabbing rampage outside of Las Vegas casino. The search for a motive tonight.\par \par \uc2 The massacre at a daycare killing at least 36, most of them preschoolers in Thailand. What we`re learning about the attacker.\par \par \uc2 Two Russians fleeing Putin draft escaping to the U.S. and requesting asylum. How they got to Alaska.\par \par \uc2 That major U.S. city taking drastic measures to combat its police shortage. What it`s hiring civilians to do.\par \par \uc2 And at age 100, he`s showing no signs of slowing down. The Marathon Man inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening, everyone. With recreational marijuana use now legally in 19 states, it has been easy to forget pot is still very much illegal under federal law where the drug is classified right alongside LSD and heroin. But tonight, President Biden declaring it`s time to right the wrongs of what he called a failed approach to marijuana, announcing he is pardoning all prior federal offenses of simple possession of marijuana, and asking states where most pot convictions take place to do the same, potentially impacting thousands.\par \par \uc2 It`s an effort to end what the President sees as unfairness in simple marijuana possession cases where he says Black and Brown people are more likely to be prosecuted. And he also wants a hard look taken and how the government classifies pot, which for now is regarded as more serious than fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 Kelly O`Donnell has details.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today, President Biden changed the lives of thousands of Americans 33 days before the Midterm Elections.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No one should be in jail just for using or possessing marijuana.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The President granted a full pardon for simple possession of marijuana as a federal crime. Weldon Angelo has been advocating for this clemency.\par \par \uc2 WELDON ANGELOS, MUSIC PRODUCER: Having this pardon is going to allow these individuals to move on with their lives.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: But there had been no outward sign this dramatic move was coming. The president held unrelated events in New York State. Aboard Air Force One, he used his pen to wipe out these prior federal convictions. An estimated 6500 people convicted from 1992 to 2021, and thousands more who live in the District of Columbia. Angelos previously had his own drug convictions pardoned and met with Biden officials on this policy.\par \par \uc2 ANGELOS: The society has determined that cannabis is not a crime. And so, the federal government needs to catch up with the rest of the nation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Advocates say these convictions lead to real-world barriers in jobs and housing that have been a greater burden in minority communities. Candidate Biden in 2020 promised to take action on marijuana convictions\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: -- of a marijuana conviction. Automatically, that should be expunged, so when you get asked a question, have you ever been convicted, you can honestly say no, because you have to put it down now on a job application.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Laws and attitudes have changed. 19 states and the District of Columbia permit marijuana use for adults. Medical marijuana use is legal in 38 states and DC. The President is urging governors to grant similar pardons. And his administration will review whether marijuana should still be classified with drugs like LSD under federal law.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kelly joins me live now. Kelly, the President is not decriminalizing marijuana possession with these pardons, right?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s right. He doesn`t have that power. Congress would have to change federal law on marijuana possession. And the President is clear that there must still be strict laws on trafficking, marketing, and selling marijuana to underage users. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly O`Donnell, thank you. A prominent member of the Proud Boys pleaded guilty to seditious conspiracy today for his role in plotting the January 6 attack. Jeremy Bertino was the first Proud Boys member to plead guilty to that charge. Officials say Bertino has agreed to cooperate with the Justice Department`s investigation.\par \par \uc2 [19:05:15]\par \par \uc2 With a little over A month until the Midterms, a growing firestorm in Georgia of a Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . the woman who says Walker paid for her abortion is now making a new allegation which Walker is strongly denying. Yamiche Alcindor is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): After a campaign event in Georgia, tonight \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  defiant.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: And I`m not going to back down. The stakes are way, way too high.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Following the latest twist and the controversy surrounding the Republican Senate candidate and former football star. The Daily Beast reporting the unnamed woman who the legend Walker paid for her abortion more than a decade ago says she`s also the mother of one of his children.\par \par \uc2 Previously, she provided the news outlet with a receipt from an abortion clinic as well as a check and a get-well card she says are from Walker. She has not revealed her name citing privacy concerns, and NBC News has not verified her story nor the documents. Walker, an abortion rights opponent, again strongly denying the accusation.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I know this is untrue. I know nothing about any woman having an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But Walker`s son, Christian, has fiercely criticized his father.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER, SON OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Don`t lie on the lives you`ve destroyed and act like you`re some moral family man. Y`all should care about that, conservatives.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Today, we pressed Walker.\par \par \uc2 What`s your reaction to people so close to you, your son, a mother of your -- someone who says that she`s the mother of your kid saying that you`re lying. What`s your reaction to people so close to you saying don`t trust you?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Just like what I just said. Democrats are desperate for this seat. This seat is important.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: They`re your family.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: They`re very desperate for to seat. I love my family. I always love my family. I`m going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: The Daily Beast says the anonymous woman, a registered Democrat, who says she`d had a years-long relationship with Walker thinks his denials are quote ridiculous. Many National Republican standing by Walker slamming it, and said it`s Democratic distraction from issues like inflation and crime.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to win that race. This is an effort from Democrats to hold on to power.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But some state Republican starting to voice their unease.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. GEOFF DUNCAN (R-GA): Even the most staunch Republicans I think are rattled at the continued flow of information.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Meanwhile, voters in Georgia sounding off.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s 100 percent true right now, but we don`t know a whole story. I`m voting for Herschel. I mean, he made a mistake.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The only reason \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is on his ballot right now is because he supports Trump. If you support Trump, you can have any kind of morals in this country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Today, Walker`s opponent, Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock called these allegations, "disturbing." And he`s reiterated his support for abortion rights. Next week, both men are scheduled to face off in a debate. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Yamiche, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to new reporting that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  could potentially face federal criminal charges. Ken Dilanian explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, NBC NEWS JUSTICE AND INTELLIGENCE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, new signs Hunter Biden could face criminal charges. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reporting federal agents believe they have a plausible case to charge the president son with tax crimes, and also with lying on a gun application when he said he wasn`t using illegal drugs citing people familiar with the case.\par \par \uc2 The final decision about whether to file charges rests with the top federal prosecutor in Delaware who was appointed by President Trump. The Justice Department declined comment. NBC News has not confirmed the report. Biden`s lawyer saying prosecutors, in this case, are diligently and thoroughly weighing not just evidence provided by agents but also all the other witnesses.\par \par \uc2 Hunter Biden`s business dealings have repeatedly come under fire including taking a lucrative position on a Ukrainian energy companies board while his father was vice president. An NBC News analysis of information on his laptop showed he and his firms were paid $11 million from 2013 to 2018 including by Chinese and Ukrainian firm. Last year, \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  insisted he did nothing wrong.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN\uc2 , SON OF PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I will be cleared of any wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: His lawyer said he paid a $2 million tax debt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN (on camera): President Biden has repeatedly said he was not involved in his son`s business dealings. The White House tonight is declining comment. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Ken Dilanian in our Washington newsroom, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Terrifying moments today along the Las Vegas strip where police say an attacker went on a stabbing rampage with a large kitchen knife in front of a Wynn Casino. At least two people were killed, six others injured, three in critical condition tonight. A suspect is in custody. Police say the motive is under investigation but it appears, they say, unprovoked.\par \par \uc2 Now to the disaster in Florida and is still-rising death toll from Hurricane Ian. Many people remain desperate for help more than a week after the storm hit. Sam Brock is there for us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Eight days in, and the havoc wreaked by Hurricane Ian is still hard to comprehend. Sheriff`s deputies canvassing this trailer park in Lee County with mailboxes but no residence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What we need right now is some kind of housing temporary.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:o6]\par \par \uc2 BROCK: The death toll now sits at more than 130, the highest from any Florida storm since 1935, more than five times the U.S. death toll from Hurricane Andrew, a category five 30 years ago. Ian`s rapid storm intensification residents remaining behind, and massive population growth in southwest Florida, all likely playing a role. 49-year-old Liz Maguire was among those lost.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There`s going to be a huge hole.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Her mom, Susan, says she was a foundation of love for her family.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Has it sunk in yet?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. We`re sitting here in her house and she`ll never be here again. I`m looking at her things. Yes, it sunk in.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Liz was a mother and a grandmother.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She just enjoyed everything.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: She suffered a medical emergency during the storm and called for help. But first responders couldn`t reach her. She was found with her cell phone in her hand. Susan last spoke with her daughter that afternoon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She always ended every conversation with I love you. I love you, mom. I love you too, sweetie. If you can pick anything to be the last thing you say to a person, that would be it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK (on camera): Back where Susan lives, on hard-hit Pine Island, supplies are still being brought in by helicopter. And the conditions are only expected to get more challenging. Many of the bridges that serve as a lifeline for Southwest Florida are unusable. On a typical day, some 80 truckloads worth of materials and supplies would pass right through here. But now, that won`t happen for months. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Sam Brock, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Thailand, the deadliest mass killing ever in that country. A former police officer with a gun and a knife attack a daycare center, killing 34 people, 24 of them children. Then he fatally shot himself at his home where his wife and son were also found dead. Authorities say he was fired previously for drug possession.\par \par \uc2 Now to the war in Ukraine. In the extraordinary lengths, some Russians are taking to avoid President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is draft. Two Russian citizens sailing hundreds of miles to U.S. soil to request asylum. And inside Ukraine, our NBC News exclusive tonight with a top official leading the U.S. effort to help rebuild. We get more now from Cal Perry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, more Russians fleeing Putin`s war. Following that crush of traffic along Russia`s borders, now two Russian men arriving in Alaska by boat requesting asylum saying they too are fleeing military service.\par \par \uc2 On the battlefield, Ukrainian troops press their offensive while Russia lashes back once again targeting civilians. Ukraine saying a rocket attack killing at least three people in a residential building in Zaporizhzhia. While in Kyiv, \uc2 USAID\uc2  Administrator \uc2 Samantha Power\uc2  here to spearhead the effort to rebuild.\par \par \uc2 The challenges must be immense to rebuild a country while it`s fighting a war.\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA POWER, ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 USAID\uc2 : Yes, and I hear a lot of build back better. It`s interesting. You start to see this government, and really they`ve done this from the beginning but increasingly walking and chewing gum at the same time.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Even during our interview, the sounds of war break through.\par \par \uc2 POWER: We have provided now more than $25 billion in a combination of military and security assistance.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: All of it coming after that startling report in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  about this deadly car bomb attack in Russia back in August. The victim Darya Dugina, the daughter of a prominent Russian nationalist. According to the report, U.S. intelligence agencies believe parts of the Ukrainian government authorized her assassination and fear it could widen the war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY (on camera): NBC News has not confirmed the Times` report. And tonight, a top Ukrainian official suggesting that Russia was behind the attack. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Cal Perry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, soaring crime and understaffed police departments, a controversial plan to have civilians pick up the slack in one major city next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Tonight, investigators are poring over a grisly crime scene. Four members of a family, including an 8-month-old baby were killed after being kidnapped. Miguel Almaguer is following this. Miguel, what do we know about this?\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Lester, authorities have not officially released a motive but say it`s possible the suspect may have known the family and that the family`s ATM card was used after the heinous crime. On Monday, investigators say Jesus Salgado forced two brothers out of their business office at gunpoint in \uc2 the city of Merced\uc2 , zip tie in their hands behind their back before returning for a young mother and her 8-month-old daughter.\par \par \uc2 The family was then driven away in a truck. All of their bodies were found two days later in a central California orchard. Investigators have not said how the family was killed, but the suspect is talking to police. The sheriff says the suspect has a long criminal history and deserves the death penalty. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Just an awful crime. All right, Miguel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 New Orleans is one of many cities seeing a dramatic rise in murders is now taking a controversial approach to combating its shortage of police officers, hiring civilians. Gabe Gutierrez is there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Seventh grader Byron Kelly was just 13 years old when he was shot and killed in New Orleans earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 CARLA THOMAS, MOTHER OF BYRON KELLY: I just been praying. And I keep telling myself, don`t cry, don`t cry.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: His mother, Karla Thomas, says it`s been hard to get a hold of the police officers working the unsolved case.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: I have called them, I have sent them text messages. I have like, none, nothing.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Police did not respond to our interview requests. The homicide rate in New Orleans is soaring, up 40 percent just this year. Now, the Big Easy is taking drastic measures hiring civilians to help with policing, including detective work like going to crime scenes to gather evidence.\par \par \uc2 SHAUN FERGUSON, SUPERINTENDENT, NEW ORLEANS POLICE: This is also to reduce some of the workload currently placed on our patrol and district personnel.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Good idea?\par \par \uc2 MIKE GLASSER, PRESIDENT, POLICE ASSOCIATION OF NEW ORLEANS: No, not really.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Mike Glasser, who heads up one of the city`s police unions blames plummeting morale for a crushing shortage of about 600 officers from Seattle to St. Louis, Atlanta, to Philadelphia. Major cities across the country are also reporting a cop staffing crisis.\par \par \uc2 GLASSER: If we`re going to send civilians in there that are unarmed, inexperienced, untrained for what it turns into, that`s dangerous for them and it`s dangerous for the public.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Highlighting the crime problem, during our brief interview today, someone broke into a car in the Police Union`s own parking lot.\par \par \uc2 Is the city safe?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL, MAYOR OF NEW ORLEANS: So, \uc2 the city of New Orleans\uc2  is one that is grappling with violent crime like many other cities throughout the country,\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Democratic mayor \uc2 Latoya Cantrell\uc2  blames the pandemic slowing down the court system as well as progressive prosecutors for the spike in crime. But her critics also blame her and are mounting a recall effort.\par \par \uc2 CANTRELL: Well, the recall organizers in my view are absolutely unfair but are demonstrating their democratic rights. I have led on key issues that no other mayor has.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ (on camera): The mayor recently suggested that the officer shortage could impact Mardi Gras. She has since walked that back, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Gabe Gutierrez in New Orleans, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Next, retailers overstock a much, much different picture from last year. How you can save this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: It is not even Halloween but the holiday shopping season is already here and it`s far different from last year when just about everything seemed in short supply. Here`s Emilie Ikeda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): As we enter the holiday shopping season, a new story is emerging at America`s ports as companies worldwide experienced economic whiplash. With sky-high inflation cutting into shoppers buying power, some shipping companies are now canceling trips. U.S. shippers say they`re seeing a 20 percent drop in ocean freight orders. A sharp contrast to last year when big retailers hired private vessels to transport goods.\par \par \uc2 LORI ANN LAROCCO, CNBC SENIOR EDITOR: Based on the items on the water right now and what is being processed in, retailers are anticipating a customer that is not going to be spending quite as much.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: This week, the \uc2 WTO\uc2  warning global trade is losing momentum.\par \par \uc2 NGOZI OKONJO-IWEALA, DIRECTOR-GENERAL, WORLD TRADE ORGANIZATION: The picture for 2023 has darkened considerably.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Still, there`s room for the consumer to benefit this holiday season.\par \par \uc2 Major retailers are now trying to clear their shelves that this year are overstocked. \uc2 Nike\uc2  says its North America inventories up 65 percent compared to last year. That means steep discounts for bargain hunters. Everyone is looking for a deal right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re trying to stretch our money as far as we can possibly stretch it.\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: To luring cautious customers, retailers including Kohl`s and Target jumpstarted their holiday deals today. And for the first time, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  will hold a second prime day-like event within one year. So, when sorting through the savings, shopping experts say, compare prices online. Look for retailers who offer price matching and use a cashback credit card to stretch your dollar the furthest as inflation curbs the power of your paycheck.\par \par \uc2 Emily Ikeda, NBC News New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, the 100-year-old Marathon Man inspiring America.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, we want to introduce you to a 100-year-old who has truly gone the distance and shows no sign of slowing down. Maggie Vespa with "INSPIRING AMERICA."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Runners often strive to outpace time.\par \par \uc2 MIKE FREMONT, 100-YEAR-OLD RUNNER: The nice thing about running at this age --\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Mike Fremont defies it.\par \par \uc2 FREMONT: -- is that there`s very little competition.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: At 100, the retired Cincinnati engineer and great grandfather of four logs five to 10 miles three times a week.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You are so sweet.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: His 14-minute mile pace only slowed by starstruck fans.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You`re an inspiration to a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Does that feel weird to hear that you`re a celebrity?\par \par \uc2 FREMONT: Absolutely embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: With 60 marathons under his feet, Mike boasts four world records, fastest marathons for his age at 80 and 90. Fastest half marathons at 90 and 91. Running started his therapy in his 30s after the loss of his first wife.\par \par \uc2 FREMONT: So, starting to run and it worked.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Decades later, after battling cancer, Mike went vegan and with the encouragement of his second wife, Marilyn, ran marathon after marathon.\par \par \uc2 What do you think is fueling it?\par \par \uc2 MARILYN WALL, WIFE OF MIKE FREMONT: I think he can`t quit.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Proving her point, his other hobby, canoeing. Of course, racing goes to.\par \par \uc2 For people who think at 100, a person should be slowing down. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 FREMONT: I think they ought to be speeding up.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Proof no matter your age, your best time may still lie ahead. Maggie Vespa, NBC News, Cincinnati.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And he just keeps going. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Thursday. Thank you for watching. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Goodnight, everyone.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221007eia600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc87}{\*\bkmkend toc87}\b \uc2 Republicans Rally To The Defense Of Georgia Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Facing Devastating Abuse Accusations From His Own Son And A Report He Paid For An Ex-Girlfriend`s Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Meantime, the midterms just over a month away and a firestorm has erupted in a key Senate race in Georgia. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the former football star now Republican nominee denying a report that he paid for his ex-girlfriend`s abortion. NBC`s Kristen Welker joins us with details on this sordid tale. Hey, Kristen, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Hoda, good morning to you. Well, campaigns always brace for the possibility of an October surprise. Well, here it is in the critical Senate race in Georgia with a new controversy surrounding Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . Now GOP sources are telling NBC News that leading senators are preparing to stand shoulder to shoulder with him on the airwaves and on the campaign trail. But will it be enough with just five weeks until the midterms?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): This morning Republicans are closing ranks around former football star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , their embattled Senate nominee in Georgia, a swing state that`s critical to Republicans fight to win control of the Senate. After "The Daily Beast" reported Walker in 2009, urged to then girlfriend to get an abortion after he got her pregnant, then reimbursed her for the procedure and sent her a get well card. Walker who says he wants to ban abortion after 15 weeks without exceptions, is denying the allegation.\par \par \uc2 H. WALKER: It is a flat out lie. I can tell you right now, I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never pay for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): "The Daily Beast" says the woman asked not to be identified citing privacy concerns, adding she backed up her story by providing the get well card from Walker, an abortion clinic receipt, and a bank receipt with an image of a $700 check signed by Walker. NBC News has not independently verified the documents.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER, HERSCHEL WALKER`S SON: Everything has been a lie.\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): Walker is also facing fierce criticism from his own son, Christian, a budding conservative influencer who says the family urged him not to run quote, because we all knew some of his past.\par \par \uc2 C. WALKER: Family values people. He has four kids, four different women, wasn`t in the house raising one of them. He was out having sex with other women. Do care about family values?\par \par \uc2 WELKER (voice-over): \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  later tweeting, I love my son no matter what. Walker is locked in a tight race with incumbent Democratic senator Raphael Warnock who won a special election by just two percentage points last year. Many Republicans are rushing to Walker`s defense led by former President Trump who`s endorsed him now saying Walker is being slandered and maligned by Democrats. It`s not the first controversy to rock his campaign. He`s falsely claimed he was a member of law enforcement and acknowledged fathering three children out of wedlock. Walker`s ex-wife has accused him of holding a gun to her head and threatening to kill her in incident Walker has blamed on his struggle with mental health.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now his challenger again incumbent Democratic senator Raphael Warnock, not directly responding to the controversy. Instead, he`s stressing his long standing support for abortion rights. The big question here, will all of this make a difference? It`s just too soon to tell. But in close races like this one, even a few votes in either direction, could have an impact. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, those are some stunning allegations there. Kristen Welker for us there in Washington. Kristen, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Former President Trump has taken his battle over those documents seized at his Florida Country Club, all the way to the Supreme Court filing an emergency request, Mr. Trump asking the court to declare that the 103 classified documents should be reviewed by that independent observer, the special master. Well, the government is arguing that even the special masters should not see those classified documents that were kept at Mar-a- Lago because of national security. The emergency request is being handled by Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who has given the Department of Justice until Tuesday afternoon to respond.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221006eia5000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc88}{\*\bkmkend toc88}\b \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , The GOP Candidate Who Has Called For A Ban On Abortion, Denying That He Paid For An Ex-Girlfriend`s Abortion More Than A Decade Ago\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kristen Welker \par \uc2 577 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT: Just 35 days before the Midterms, a new allegation is rocking that very tight Senate race in Georgia. Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  strongly denying an accusation that he paid for a former girlfriend abortion. Here`s Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Republican candidate and former football star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  forcefully denying an allegation that`s rocking the critical Georgia Senate race. A new accusation that Walker who supports an abortion ban after 15 weeks without any exceptions, paid for his then-girlfriend to have an abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you know the woman that is making this allegation?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I have no -- I had no idea but it is a flat-out lie.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The explosive report appearing in The Daily Beast which did not name the woman citing privacy concerns. She says she and Walker agreed she would have the abortion. The Daily Beast publishing a photo of a get-well card she says this from Walker. The news outlet also says she gave them a receipt from an abortion clinic and an image of a $700 check she says his from Walker. NBC News has not seen nor independently verified any of the documents.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never pay for an abortion and that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But tonight, Walker is facing fierce criticism from his 23-year-old son Christian saying Walker`s family urged him not to run because we all knew some of his past.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER, SON OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Don`t lie about your life at the expense of me, my mom, and all of the people that you`ve affected throughout your life. You don`t get to pretend you`re some moral family guy.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  tweeting I love my son no matter what. And blasting the allegations as a last-minute distraction by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: There`s inflation, the border wide open, crime. They don`t want to talk about that. So, they`re making up lies now because they need this Georgia seat.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now, top national Republicans are rallying around him from former President Trump to Senator Rick Scott tonight saying Democrats know that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is winning so they have cranked up this smear machine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Incumbent Democrat Senator Raphael Warnock not weighing in on the controversy, but saying he`s been a consistent abortion rights supporter. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker, thanks.\par \par \uc2 It appears that Elon Musk`s plan to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is back on tonight. A lawyer for the billionaire tells federal regulators that Musk is willing to go forward with a $44 billion deal if a Delaware court ends \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s lawsuit against Musk. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  file that soon after Musk put the deal on hold last spring.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221006eia4000ji\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc89}{\*\bkmkend toc89}\b \uc2 Conversation With Clea Shearer, About Her Breast Cancer Journey\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb \par \uc2 3,605 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [08:10:00]\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: We are back. 8:12 with "Pink Power TODAY". Our special series for breast cancer awareness month. And this morning, this morning we`re checking in with our friend Clear Shearer.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yes, "The Home Edit" co-founder was diagnosed with breast cancer earlier this year and she has decided to share every step in her journey, including her recent milestone of finishing chemo.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Yes, throughout it all, though guys, there`s been a group of women she`s been leaning on. They talk every day but they had never met face to face until two days ago. They`re amazing women. They all came together from all across the country for a very special conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLEA SHEARER, "THE HOME EDIT" CO-FOUNDER: Look, all of your toys are so accessible.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Clea Shearer finds joy in being a helper.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Will you guys help me?\par \par \uc2 JOANNA TEPLIN, "THE HOME EDIT" CO-FOUNDER: I can`t get up there fast enough.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TEPLIN: Like, show us the closets.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): She`s created a career out of it. Taking on clutter and organizing people`s lives as one half of "The Home Edit" team.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Hey, guys. So, by now you probably read or heard that I have breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): But after she shared her breast cancer diagnosis earlier this year, Clea found herself asking for help from those who personally knew the fight she was facing.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: As you`re going through something so brutal, like chemo, everyone in your life wants to help, but there`s nothing quite like someone who`s actually been through it or going through it at the same time.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): A friend connected her to Maria Boschetti, a three-time breast cancer survivor who lives in California with her husband. The 42- year-old, who works in public relations, was first diagnosed when she was just 25.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: She`s taken so many copious notes. So, if I just say -- if I whip out any side effects she`s like, boop, boop, boop --\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Got it.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: -- page eight. She`s like, you`ll get over that real soon.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Amy Jethway was 42 years old when she found a lump while on vacation with her boyfriend. The biotech sales account manager diagnosed with breast cancer just weeks after Clea. Their mutual friend passed along Clea`s number to Amy who lived hundreds of miles away in New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): These are strangers? People you`ve never met.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I`ve never met them.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Just on text. Hey, girl?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Well -- yes, literally. And I -- there are some of, you know, these women I talk to every day. If not every day, every week, and, you know, just to check in. They`re like my dear close friends.\par \par \uc2 KOT (voiceover): A community connected through tough times. Dear friends who have never met. Meeting up in person for the first time.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Well, I think it`s a good time to meet them because it`s fun to text, but it`s more fun to see people in person.\par \par \uc2 [08:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Oh. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 AMY JETHWAY, BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR: I can`t believe we get to see you. You`re real. You`re real. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: It`s so good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: I`m going to cry. Oh, my gosh. You`re so tall.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): This is so cool in this moment to be sitting here.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I can`t believe.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): First of all, you haven`t met in person yet you --\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: No. Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): -- but you know each other intimately. What did you think, Maria, when you first started connecting with Clea?\par \par \uc2 MARIA BOSCHETTI, BREAST CANCER SURVIVOR: I was shocked she was so positive, right? Because it`s a terrifying time in your life. You were wanting to talk about it. You had questions but also you were -- wanting to help others.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Others like Amy.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: I reached out to you during your fourth AC --\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: -- when you felt really horrible.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: And she was so available to me. Like, that was really overwhelming to me because I was about to start my chemo journey.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Did that make you scared or did that make you prepared?\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: I was so grateful for how open you were and how honest you were about things because if this is what`s going to happen to me, then this is going to happen. I wound up having a very different reaction with my fourth.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I know. I was, like, so sorry. I scared you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Oh, you were like, I`m cool.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: She was like, fine.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Meanwhile, this whole time I was, like, guys brace yourselves. It`s going to be awful.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: Like, it aches everywhere.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Clea`s openness about her hair loss empowering Amy as she started to lose her own.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: I told my boyfriend, I`m going to shave this. Like, we`re -- we can do this. We can take this head on. And kind of, whatever parts of the journey you can own, you now, that`s something I got from you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): That`s important -- that`s something you got from Clea?\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): What is that?\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: Like, owning what you can in the journey.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Were you reluctant, Clear, because there are parts that you want to share and then there are parts you want to keep. But it sounds like you were very --\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Oh, I don`t keep much to myself.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): You don`t?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: No. Hoda, you know me. I am a barrier soul kind of girl.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Not only did I tell everything pretty much to these ladies but, I mean, I would also tell our \uc2 Instagram\uc2  followers what was going on. And it made me feel understood, you know. And it`s also nice not to feel like an anomaly.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): When you`re having a crummy day, you need someone who gets it. How important has that been, Maria, to have that?\par \par \uc2 BOSCHETTI: It`s so important. I -- you know, I was so young the first time I was diagnosed. And I didn`t have anybody to talk to. You don`t process those emotions when you don`t have someone to talk to or ask question and that friendship. And now, you know, I was just texting Clea last night. I feel like you are this light of sunshine that`s even bringing the last eight years back in me.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I`m sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): By the way --\par \par \uc2 BOSCHETTI: Don`t make me cry. I`m not a pretty crier.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): But that`s so beautiful. That`s a really important point.\par \par \uc2 BOSCHETTI: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): It like -- sometimes feelings lay dormant.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BOSCHETTI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Until someone says, let`s unearth those.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (voiceover): Clea, Maria, and Amy finding strength in each other and finding peace in the small things.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Everybody has their spot where they go to when they want to just, like, cleanse and clear and you all are walking in mine. This is Central Park. What`s your place, Clea? Where do you --\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: My back porch.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Your back porch?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I have a chair. I read there. I listen to the rain there. I greet my kids after school there.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: I just -- it makes me so peaceful.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): What about for you, Amy?\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: I have my one bench right on the Hudson --\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Oh, that`s so nice.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 JETHWAY: -- and that just go out.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Doing meditation, Maria, or anything that like --\par \par \uc2 BOSCHETTI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): You`re a meditator?\par \par \uc2 BOSCHETTI: Yes, meditation and beach.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Meditation and beach?\par \par \uc2 BOSCHETTI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): All right. So, this is a sisterhood.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): This is something that you believe, Clea, is not for this moment.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: Uh-huh. It`s for life.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): How do you know?\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: You go through life and certain people enter it for a reason. I consider you one of those people, too, Hoda. Because I don`t lose friends. I only make them. So, we`ve been through something really incredible together. And also, and a little earth shattering.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHEARER: A lot earth shattering. So, you`re stuck with me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB (on camera): We`re stuck with clea.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Wow.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So. sweet. And by the way, if you wonder if that friendship`s rear - - real, they`re planning a vacation October together. Also, Joanna, you know, Clea`s partner, was standing in the background the whole time. Weeping. Weeping. Like these friendships, the new old friendships or old friendships are all keeping her in. She also said about her hair, she talked about it. She said it`s very kind of peach fuzzy. She`s like, I`m not sure if I`m going to cover it. I think I might just -- finally, I`m free of all that stuff.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS 3RD HOUR OF TODAY, CO-HOST: I think she looks beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She has posted this week, actually, about her -- why she was wearing a wig to her kids` pickup.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: She didn`t want to embarrass them.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And -- this is going to make me cry. Then her daughter said, mommy, you don`t embarrass me, I think you`re beautiful. So, she started -- yes. And you took them to your special place --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- in Central Park when you went through breast cancer?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, that was my spot. It was very calming to me. It`s like -- you know, there`s no green space here but that was mine. So, I used to go there and feel very peaceful. And everyone had their little spot where they felt the warm hand. So, it was beautiful.\par \par \uc2 I want to say thank you, of course, to Clea. She`s amazing and Maria and Amy. We also want to thank the Kent, that`s a beautiful space in New York. We had a great conversation there. So, we want to say thank you to them.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That was wonderful.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I`m so weepy today.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: That was wonderful.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right. Mr. Roker, take us away.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Oh, special. OK. Guys, well, we are looking at a rainy, windy day here in the northeast and parts of New England. Sunshine through the southeast on into the Gulf where it will be nice and warm. Even up to the Great Lakes, we`re looking at a flood risk through the southwest. We`re going to continue to track that. And beautiful sunshine from Seattle to San Diego.\par \par \uc2 [08:20:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Don`t forget, if you`re heading out the door, head out to "TODAY" show Radio Sirius XM Channel 108. But right now --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: What time is it?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: -- best time of the morning.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: In for Carson.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Come on, Sheinelle.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We have good stuff for you this morning.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. We`re ready.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK. So, we`re going to start with "Black Panther