ight. Well, this is another good one. So, next up "The Crown". Calling up all Prince Harrys. A recently released casting announcement that Netflix is looking for an actor to play the adolescent royal redhead. According to the online note, they`re seeking someone with a strong physical appearance, if you guys know anybody, to play Harry from 16 to 20 years old.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what`s surprising to a lot of people. Apparently, no previous professional acting experience is required because they want him to really look like him. So, if you look like the Duke of Sussex or you know someone, you should throw your hat into the ring. Thank us later. The application closes next Friday and filming is scheduled to start in November. So, you`ll have to take off from or something, I don`t know. So, the clock is ticking. Do you know anybody?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: They have to have a posh English accent too.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I mean, I bet you there`s somebody who we don`t know. Who -- this could be their next thing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: A Harry doppelganger.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: For sure.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Next up, Paul and Linda McCartney. Yesterday, "Rolling Stone" co-founder, \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2  stopped by "The Howard Stern Show" and revealed how he played matchmaker with one of his magazine`s photographers and a young superstar Beatle.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOWARD STERN, "THE HOWARD STERN SHOW" HOST: You didn`t realize Linda was a new hired to be a "Rolling Stone" photographer. She said, I want to go meet \uc2 Paul McCartney\uc2 . I want to photograph --\par \par \uc2 JANN WENNER\uc2 , "ROLLING STONE" CO-FOUNDER: When she said, I want to (INAUDIBLE). I said, well, we don`t really need to spend the money, you know, sending you to London and cover. Why do you want to -- why do we need more Beatles. And so, just -- well, I want to meet Paul. I say, OK. You got the assignment. Go.\par \par \uc2 STERN: Wow. Nice.\par \par \uc2 WENNER\uc2 : And then I started getting postcards from her every couple of weeks saying, you know, how she`s getting closer and then finally I got one said, we went out last night and had the most wonderful time. Thank you forever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: How cool was that? And the rest they say is history.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That`s \uc2 Jann Wenner\uc2 , by the way.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK. Cool.\par \par \uc2 JONES: All right. Next up, Lizzo. We still can`t believe this history- making moment from last week when Lizzo was invited by the \uc2 Library of Congress\uc2  to play President James Madison`s 200-year-old crystal flute. Well, the presidential invites keep coming.\par \par \uc2 Now, Madison`s Montpelier Estate is asking the Grammy winner to perform at the president`s home turned museum in Virginia. Writing on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , thanks to classically trained singer/songwriter, Lizzo. The world had the opportunity to learn about James Madison`s crystal flute and hear his beautiful music. They go on to say, we are crossing our fingers that she would consider adding us to her schedule, once home to Madison and the now famous crystal flute. So, they loved it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It`s classical --\par \par \uc2 JONES: It was controversial but they thought, you know what --\par \par \uc2 ROKER: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You were there last night, right?\par \par \uc2 JONES: It was phenomenal.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Did she play --\par \par \uc2 JONES: She puts on a phenomenal show.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Did she play the flute?\par \par \uc2 JONES: She had it on a crystal stand. I mean, it was just --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 JONES: -- she had the whole place on a cloud.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Oh, awesome. Awesome.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes, we love it. We love it when she comes here to "TODAY."\par \par