 KOTB: But first, this is Today on NBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221006eia4000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc90}{\*\bkmkend toc90}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for October 4, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Kerry Sanders, Kelly O`Donnell, Kristen Welker,  Andrea Mitchell, Stephanie Gosk, Tom Costello, Jake Ward, Kristen Dahlgren \par \uc2 3,871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight, the desperate search in Florida as the death toll from Hurricane Ian rises even higher.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The race against time. Rescuers going door to door looking for survivors nearly a week after Ian hit as a powerful category four storm. The death toll climbing to at least 108. Our team on the ground tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also, this evening, the new move by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his battle over classified documents seized from Mar-a-Lago. His emergency request to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The explosive report of the Georgia Senate race. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the GOP candidate who has called for a ban on abortion, denying that he paid for an ex-girlfriend`s abortion more than a decade ago. Walker`s son lashing out calling his father a liar. How Republicans are reacting.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk`s about-face reviving his $44 billion bid to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . What the company is saying.\par \par \uc2 The major new provocation from North Korea launching a ballistic missile over Japan for the first time in five years. How the U.S. responded with a show of force.\par \par \uc2 NBC News investigates how did a driver busted with a record amount of fentanyl escaped from the feds.\par \par \uc2 And from coal miner`s daughter to the Queen of Country Music. Remembering the iconic Loretta Lynn.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (on camera): Good evening. Now, six days after Hurricane Ian`s devastating impact in Florida, a grim acceptance is setting in about the fates of those yet to be found. Rescuers know the most critical hours to find the trapped or injured alive have passed. But still tonight, the search goes on and the death toll ticks upward now more than 100 in the U.S. almost all in Florida which took Ian`s mightiest blow.\par \par \uc2 Power there is being restored. The number of homes and businesses in the dark now dropping below 400,000. But for many who survived the storm and are now left with nothing, these are harrowing and trying days of another kind. In some communities, there was a growing sense of neglect. Help they say has arrived for so many in southwest Florida, but not for them. Kerry Sanders continues our reporting tonight from the disaster zone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Search and rescue.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Today`s search teams move door to door again and again. The death toll climbed. More than 100 people killed by Hurricane Ian, about half of them in Lee County. The search and rescue now shifting to mostly recovery.\par \par \uc2 CARMINE MARCENO, SHERIFF, LEE COUNTY, FLORIDA: More towards the second and third phase.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Which is --\par \par \uc2 MARCENO: It`s possible there are still people out there, possible, but most likely we`re on recovery now. And you know that`s very difficult.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: FEMA has been on the ground but miles from the coast in predominantly Black and Hispanic neighborhoods, some say Help has not arrived.\par \par \uc2 Do you feel ignored here?\par \par \uc2 RUBY WILLIAMS, HURRICANE IAN SURVIVOR: Yes, very much.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No Red Cross, no FEMA, no nothing.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Though the damage here is not as dramatically visible, Ruby Williams says her community was also hit hard.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: We have water up to here. I say look, it looked like the Gulf of Mexico in front of her house just that quick.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Williams used an extension cord, all she could find in that moment, to tie herself to her niece so they wouldn`t get swept away.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It was the grace of God we survived.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Now they`re concerned they won`t get the aid they need. A recent study shows communities with large populations of Black, Hispanic, or Native American residents often receive less money from FEMA than mostly white communities. FEMA says it considers several factors when deciding aid requests, including property value, which is often lower in minority neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 ARMANI BACKMAN, GRANDDAUGHTER OF RUBY WILLIAMS: They work just as hard. They work just as hard as the people on the beach do and they`re not getting the attention here because it`s not the beach. It`s not a seaside home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because we are Latino, Black, poor people, working people. That`s the way it is.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: In the hurricane zone, one equalizer, the misery.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What are we going to do for the ones who fought through the storm and are still alive? Because if they don`t get the resources, they are going to die.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kerry, I`m curious to know what females responses to getting aid to minority communities.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (on camera): Well, Lester, tonight, FEMA tells NBC News they`re working to make -- reach the immediate needs of survivors while at the same time expanded initiatives to get out to those vulnerable and underserved communities. At the same time, tomorrow, President Biden will visit this area to see the devastation for himself. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kerry Sanders in Florida tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Late this evening or late today, former President Trump asked the Supreme Court to intervene in his battle over those classified documents seized from his Florida home. Kelly O`Donnell is at the court. Kelly, what`s at the heart of this appeal?\par \par \uc2 [19:05:16]\par \par \uc2 KELLY O`DONNELL, NBC NEWS SENIOR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Well, Mr. Trump is making an important -- emergency appeal here to where he appointed three justices, asking them to wipe out a victory that the Department of Justice had won. He wants those 100 classified documents that were seized from his Mar-a-Lago home to go back to that special master for him to review.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump says that the Department of Justice has criminalized a dispute over documents. He also says that the Department of Justice needs to give those back. And here at the Supreme Court, they have the opportunity to simply reject the claims made by the former president. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And Kelly, today, I know the justice has heard a key case about voting rights. What`s at stake here?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, the issue here is in Alabama and their congressional district and whether they have violated the landmark Voting Rights Act by denying Black voters equal opportunity to participate in the political process. In the state, Black voters represent 27 percent of the population, but are a majority in just one of seven congressional districts.\par \par \uc2 The State of Alabama said it used race-neutral criteria to draw the map. But liberal Justice Jackson said the state should be race-conscious in trying to protect the minority rights of its citizens. And the court here has already rolled back some of the Voting Rights Act and has allowed Alabama to use this new map in the upcoming Midterm Elections. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kelly O`Donnell at the Supreme Court, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just 35 days before the Midterms, a new allegation is rocking that very tight Senate race in Georgia. Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  strongly denying an accusation that he paid for a former girlfriend abortion. Here`s Kristen Welker.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, Republican candidate and former football star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  forcefully denying an allegation that`s rocking the critical Georgia Senate race. A new accusation that Walker who supports an abortion ban after 15 weeks without any exceptions, paid for his then-girlfriend to have an abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you know the woman that is making this allegation?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATE CANDIDATE: I have no -- I had no idea but it is a flat-out lie.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: The explosive report appearing in The Daily Beast which did not name the woman citing privacy concerns. She says she and Walker agreed she would have the abortion. The Daily Beast publishing a photo of a get-well card she says this from Walker. The news outlet also says she gave them a receipt from an abortion clinic and an image of a $700 check she says his from Walker. NBC News has not seen nor independently verified any of the documents.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never pay for an abortion and that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: But tonight, Walker is facing fierce criticism from his 23-year-old son Christian saying Walker`s family urged him not to run because we all knew some of his past.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN WALKER, SON OF \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Don`t lie about your life at the expense of me, my mom, and all of the people that you`ve affected throughout your life. You don`t get to pretend you`re some moral family guy.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  tweeting I love my son no matter what. And blasting the allegations as a last-minute distraction by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: There`s inflation, the border wide open, crime. They don`t want to talk about that. So, they`re making up lies now because they need this Georgia seat.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Now, top national Republicans are rallying around him from former President Trump to Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  tonight saying Democrats know that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is winning so they have cranked up this smear machine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Incumbent Democrat Senator Raphael Warnock not weighing in on the controversy, but saying he`s been a consistent abortion rights supporter. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Kristen Welker, thanks.\par \par \uc2 It appears that Elon Musk`s plan to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is back on tonight. A lawyer for the billionaire tells federal regulators that Musk is willing to go forward with a $44 billion deal if a Delaware court ends \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s lawsuit against Musk. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  file that soon after Musk put the deal on hold last spring.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the anxious moments in Japan. People scrambling after North Korea`s most provocative missile test in years. Our Andrea Mitchell has that story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Panic in Japan. Early morning sirens warning people to take cover as a North Korean missile flew over Japan for the first time in five years at a range long enough to reach U.S. territory in Guam after for shorter-range missile launches in just the last 10 days. Kim Jong-un`s latest launch from a site near North Korea`s border with China flying for 22 minutes for 2800 miles before landing in the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR CHA\uc2 , NBC NEWS KOREAN AFFAIRS ANALYST: They`re really pushing the envelope here. This is the 23rd missile event of the year.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: In response, the U.S., South Korea, Japan scrambling fighter jets. South Korea and the U.S. running bombing drills to demonstrate their combined ability to retaliate.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:02]\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS COORDINATOR, NSC: We`re going to make sure that short of diplomacy which is obviously the path that we are ready from a military perspective to protect interests and those of our allies in the region.\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: Just last week, Vice President Harris was at the Demilitarized Zone. The Biden administration has repeatedly offered talks over the last year and a half. Kim Jong-un`s answer launching increasingly threatening missiles.\par \par \uc2 CHA: They really seem to be bent on following this path of establishing themselves as a nuclear weapon state before they`re willing to talk to anybody else, including the Chinese. North Koreans don`t appear to be listening to anybody right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: The U.S. is calling for a \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  meeting saying today`s launch is reckless and dangerous. Outside experts say, perhaps Kim Jong-un is testing us resolve as it focuses on the war in Ukraine. But now, the U.S. and its allies worry that Kim`s next move could be his first nuclear test since 2017. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Andrea Mitchell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, to our NBC News investigation. It was touted as a record bust, agent seizing more than 100 pounds of fentanyl. So, how did the alleged drug mule who was transporting it escaped from authorities? Here`s Stephanie Gosk.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GOSK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): This is what 114 pounds of fentanyl looks like, enough the DEA says to kill 25 million Americans seized back in June. DEA agents touted the bust and their fight against the deadliest drug on the streets.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BESSER, SPECIAL AGENT IN CHARGE, DEA DENVER: Behind the scenes, there is aggressive and tenacious police work being done. We are not asleep at the wheel.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: It was the single largest fentanyl seizure ever on an American highway and it happened here on I-70 in Colorado, one of the main drug trafficking routes across the country. State police stopped and driver weaving in and out of traffic dangerously, and a quick-thinking State Trooper realized something wasn`t right.\par \par \uc2 This state arrest affidavit obtained by NBC News laying out the details of the entire bust. The driver, David Maldonado, seemed nervous. He said he had to go to the bathroom really bad. At a nearby exit, the Colorado State Patrol discovered what was really behind those nerves, bags and bags of fentanyl stashed under the car`s floorboards. State law enforcement told us what happened next was a debacle.\par \par \uc2 According to the affidavit, once he was in custody, a DEA agent struck a deal with Maldonado. He would continue on to South Bend, Indiana where he said he was headed and lead them to the drug dealers. Maldonado hit the highway. He then promptly ditched a tracking device and the DEA and vanished.\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Clearly there`s been a failure of operational planning and execution.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The DEA did not respond to our request for comment but in a July statement to a Denver newspaper, the agency confirmed Maldonado is a fugitive, saying the defendant was offered the opportunity to work with the DEA to further the investigation. Subsequently, after agreeing he changed his mind and was able to elude law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: We`ve got a record amount of fentanyl involved here. In fact, enough fentanyl to kill everyone in the state of Colorado. The individual who was delivering that amount of fentanyl is now in the wind.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: \uc2 U.S. Marshals\uc2  are now leading the manhunt.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (on camera): There`s no way to know for sure if the drugs were headed here to South Bend but we decided to come and see if the fentanyl problem was an issue. And like so many cities and towns in this country, fentanyl is killing people.\par \par \uc2 How bad is the problem right now?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN VIDA, DIRECTOR OF HEALTH OUTREACH, PROMOTION AND EDUCATION, ST. JOSEPH COUNTY HEALTH DEPARTMENT: How bad is it? It`s everywhere.\par \par \uc2 GOSK (voiceover): Recently, seven overdose deaths in a single week all suspected to be from fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 VIDA: All of a sudden in July of 2021, I began to notice almost all of my death certificates had some form of fentanyl listed on it.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: Robin Vida works for the County`s Department of Health.\par \par \uc2 If there was a big bust, if someone was arrested who was dealing fentanyl in the city, would it help you guys?\par \par \uc2 VIDA: Yes, hands down. We need to stop dangerous drugs from entering into our community.\par \par \uc2 GOSK: The DEA says fentanyl is their top priority. Letting a suspect like David Maldonado slip away isn`t going to help. Stephanie Gosk, NBC News, South Bend, Indiana.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: In 60 seconds, how inflation is driving millions to change holiday travel plans. When to buy your tickets next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: After more than two years of pandemic-related stress and a surge in unruly passengers, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  has announced a new rule guaranteeing flight attendants more rest. We get more from Tom Costello.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): They`re on the front lines in the nation`s skies dealing with safety, in-flight emergencies, and bad behavior. For years flight attendants have complained that they`re overworked and overstressed as federal rules allowed them to work 14-hour days with nine hours of rest between shifts. Today, the \uc2 FAA\uc2  order flight attendants must get a minimum of 10 hours rest.\par \par \uc2 BILLY NOLEN, ADMINISTRATOR, \uc2 FAA\uc2 : They need just as much rest because flight attendants are the foundation of aviation safety`s culture starting with cabin safety.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: It comes after airlines asked all crew members to work extra shifts during the summer travel surge.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just the full flights and the overtime. Flight attendants continue to deal with this increase in unruly, sometimes even violent behavior onboard their planes.\par \par \uc2 SARA NELSON\uc2 , PRESIDENT, ASSOCIATION OF FLIGHT ATTENDANTS INTERNATIONAL: This has been the most challenging time in the history of our aviation.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: While some airlines have already adopted the new rest rules, they must all comply after the new year. Meanwhile, 43 percent of Americans are planning to pay more to travel this holiday season. But a new Bankrate survey finds nearly eight out of 10 are changing their plans due to inflation. Roughly a quarter say they`ll travel fewer days and shorter distances, drive instead of fly, and select less expensive destinations or hotels. A third say how will they travel plans will strain their budgets.\par \par \uc2 TED ROSSMAN, SENIOR INDUSTRY ANALYST, BANKRATE: They still want to travel. They still want to have the big holiday meal and all the gifts under the tree. But they`re going to try to cut some corners and do it differently.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: The time to buy airline tickets say travel pros is now. Tom Costello, NBC News, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next for us tonight, fighting a crime wave. One city makes it easier for police to use private video surveillance. We`ll tell you about the controversy tonight.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And now, our look at a controversial move by San Francisco on fighting crime. The city is now giving the police power to monitor private surveillance video as part of a new pilot program. Jake Ward explains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JAKE WARD, NBC NEWS TECHNOLOGY CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Soon after Michael Su opened his San Francisco shoe store, it was robbed.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL SU, STORE OWNER: The perpetrator used the blowtorch to get in to bypass our alarm system.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Thieves have hit the store three times.\par \par \uc2 SU: When somebody breaks in, it`s like somebody`s breaking into your home.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Now, a San Francisco ordinance gives police access to live private footage like Su`s. They need the owner`s permission, but they do not need a warrant.\par \par \uc2 SU: I want to be the first one in line to give them access to my camera.\par \par \uc2 WARD: San Francisco has seen a surge in retail thefts, including by this 2021 group that evaded police while robbing downtown stores.\par \par \uc2 LONDON BREED, MAYOR, SAN FRANCISCO: Some of that potentially could have been avoided if they were able to watch the situation in real-time.\par \par \uc2 WARD: San Francisco banned the use of facial recognition in 2019 and has championed alternative policing. But voters recalled its progressive DA this year as crime became more visible. Police data shows arrests are made in fewer than 10 percent of reported cases. But Mayor Breed says the city has to try something new.\par \par \uc2 BREED: Sadly, people believe based on understanding the way the laws work in San Francisco that in most cases they can get away with something like this, even if they`re on film.\par \par \uc2 WARD: You believe that state law is too lenient.\par \par \uc2 BREED: I think it`s just a combination of things. And then when it does happen, we want to hold the perpetrators accountable.\par \par \uc2 WARD: This ordinance requires special circumstances and internal clearance before officers can get footage. But those who voted against this ordinance say we are not seeing the bigger picture.\par \par \uc2 CONNIE CHAN, SUPERVISOR, SAN FRANCISCO DISTRICT 1: Do they really help make us safer, you know? And that is the question. And when those video footages are already readily available to our law enforcement and yet we still cannot deliver safety results, where does this lead us?\par \par \uc2 WARD: Michael Su says he`ll hand over access but within limits.\par \par \uc2 SU: Obviously, they wouldn`t need to really see the inventory room. Cameras, I`m willing to share whatever it`s needed, you know, for the police to do their job.\par \par \uc2 WARD: Jake Ward, NBC News, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, we`ll remember the queen of country music.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, Loretta Lynn rose from humble beginnings to become one of the biggest country music superstars of all time. Here`s Kristen Dahlgren.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN DAHLGREN, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): She was the Queen of Country Music who started as a coal miner`s daughter. Loretta Lynn was born in 1932 in Butcher Hollow, Kentucky, one of eight children with a hardscrabble upbringing that later fueled her music.\par \par \uc2 LORETTA LYNN, SINGER: I write about my life. I mean, every song I`ve written.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: She was just a teen when she married 21-year-old war veteran Oliver Doolittle Lynn and started having babies. Eventually, the lullaby she crooned to her children led her to give professional singing ago. The song Coal Miner`s Daughter after catapulting her career became a best- selling book, then an Oscar-winning film starring Sissy Spacek and Tommy Lee Jones.\par \par \uc2 Her marriage fraught with hardships. She sang about her husband`s cheating.\par \par \uc2 LYNN: He says if it`s true, write it. So, that`s what it is.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: She tackled sensitive topics people just did not talk about like birth control.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Loretta Lynn.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Lynn was the first woman to win the \uc2 Country Music Association\uc2 `s Entertainer of the Year. And her Grammys we`re joined by the Presidential Medal of Freedom. One of country music`s most enduring icons who never forgot her humble roots, or her father.\par \par \uc2 LYNN: I`d give anything in the world if he would have been here when I recorded the Coal Miner`s Daughter. But I think he hears me. And one day, I will sing it to him.\par \par \uc2 DAHLGREN: Kristen Dahlgren, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Loretta Lynn was 90. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020221005eia400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc91}{\*\bkmkend toc91}\b \uc2 How To Identify Your Personal Breast Cancer Risks And The Steps You Can Take To Reduce It\b0\par\par\uc2 Sheinelle Jones, Al Roker, Craig Melvin, Jill Martin-Brooks \par \uc2 12,567 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES: We are back with our "Pink Power" series to mark the start of Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And this morning, we have a checklist of important information all women need to know from risk factors to screening and more. So, joining us now is Dr. \uc2 Marisa Weiss\uc2 , director of breast radiation oncology at Lankenau Medical Center in Pennsylvania. And Dr. Weiss is a breast cancer survivor herself. So, she certainly knows of what she speaks.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 DR. MARISA WEISS, CHIEF MEDICAL OFFICER AND FOUNDER OF BREASTCANCER.ORG: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, you and I we were just talking, it was 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes. 12 years ago.\par \par \uc2 JONES: That you were diagnosed?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes. I am very grateful. I benefitted from early detection. And I am here today to share important information to help save other lives.\par \par \uc2 JONES: I love that. So, with that, let`s dig in and get right to it. How do women assess their personal risk for breast cancer? I am hearing about women who have no family history getting breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Exactly. So, breast cancer is the most common cancer to affect women and 75 percent of the time, there is no family history.\par \par \uc2 JONES: 76 percent?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes. No inherited gene abnormality. But if you do have in your family breast cancer, ovarian cancer, prostate, melanoma in your family, you could be at higher risk of breast cancer because of an inherited gene.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: So, that`s why it`s so important to share that information with your doctor, to assess your risk, you know, to take and make a plan to lower your risk.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Absolutely. What do you mean by background?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: And consider genetic testing.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Background, well, you heard from Katie Couric about dense breast tissue?\par \par \uc2 JONES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Putting you at higher risk, you might need extra test to find it. Also, black women are more likely to get breast cancer at younger ages at more advanced stages and have a more serious type of breast cancer. Jewish women are more likely to inherit an abnormal breast cancer gene. And age, our risk goes up as we grow older. But keep in mind, 20 percent of breast cancers happen before the age of 50.\par \par \uc2 JONES: My goodness, which is why screening is so important.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: So important. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN-BROOKS: Speaking of that, Dr. Weiss, earlier detection.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN-BROOKS: And we talked a little bit about this in the green room. So, I have a history in my family.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN-BROOKS: They told me 45 you start, this was five years ago. Now, is it 40? When should you start for these yearly mammograms?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes. I say, please, everyone, get your yearly mammograms starting at age 40. And if you have --\par \par \uc2 MARTIN-BROOKS: Even if you don`t have family history?\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: If you don`t have family history, that`s right. And if you let things slip and slide because of the pandemic, call today to schedule your appointment. You also want to make sure that you get a comprehensive breast exam by your doctor or nurse practitioner once yearly and then, examine yourself on a regular basis just to make sure that you know what`s normal, what may have changed.\par \par \uc2 And if you are at extra risk because of a strong family history, an inherited gene, or many you have dense breast, you might need extra tests like adding an ultrasound to your mammogram, then getting an MRI six months later and then, repeating that overtime.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN-BROOKS: It`s really important information.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: It really is. Hey, Al.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, Dr. Weiss, what should women be looking for, you know, when you talk about self-exams, things like that?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes. Well, as it turns out, most breast cancers don`t produce signs or symptoms. They are found by mammography. But people regularly feel things or see things like a lump or an area of thickening in the breast or the armpit, area of swelling or a bulge or the skin might be dimpled or retracted, you might notice a nipple discharge or you might have, let`s say, pain or discomfort in the breast.\par \par \uc2 If you have any of those symptoms that are new and persist, check them out. Call your doctor. Don`t wait. You want to find breast cancer as early as possible when it`s most treatable.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Dr. Weiss, what are some things that folks who are watching or listening can do to reduce the risk?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes. Well, the good news is the same steps that reduce your risk of breast cancer improve your overall health.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Like getting to and sticking to a healthy weight, exercising regularly, even 10 minutes a day makes a big difference. You want to eat mostly a vegetarian-based diet with like meat, chicken, fish more as a sideshow. And then, reduce your alcohol use, since the more you drink, the higher the risk. And stop smoking because it increases the risk for all kinds of things. I say start with one step, then go to a next step. And before you know it, you will be in a healthier place.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: So, let`s just say you go to the doctor, you get the diagnosis. You get the dreaded C word.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You hear it.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Life is not over. How do you manage that part?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: It can really make -- I mean, I have been there. I am a doctor and a patient as it were. Well, it`s so important to get answers to your medical questions to reduce uncertainty and to know what to expect. Put your questions and concerns on a list and put the most important ones on the top and call, e-mail or text your doctor to get answers to your questions.\par \par \uc2 Hey, don`t try to do it all. We women carry the weight of the world on our shoulders, right? So, delegate and reach out for help with like childcare, grocery shopping, you know, housework, all the things that don`t matter as much. Save your precious energy for your treatment.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Lean on your friends and family.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: I want to ask you, you mentioned something a few moments ago, I want to follow up on.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Genetic testing. Who should consider that?\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Anyone who`s got breast cancer who is -- can be -- can get genetic testing. But if you`re diagnosed at age 50 or younger, if you have a history of ovarian cancer in the family, there are men in the family with breast cancer, there`s a history of breast, ovarian, pancreatic cancer, melanoma, you should ask to get genetic testing.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: If you`re of Jewish background, you should ask for genetic testing.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And you mentioned men, doctor.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Exactly. If you are a man who gets breast cancer, you are more likely to have an inherited genetic mutation, like BRCA1 and BRCA2. And you want to make sure, if there are any men in the family -- because men get breast cancer just as -- not as often as women, like about 1 percent of the time, but that goes along with higher risk of an inherited gene abnormality.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: All right.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It`s important that we`re talking about it.;\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Dr. Weiss, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DR. WEISS: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And for more information on breast cancer awareness, we put so much on our website. Just go to today.com/health.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: When we come back, former ambassador to the United Nations and governor of the greatest state in the country, South Carolina, Nikki Haley is here like. We`re going to talk about leadership, we`re going to talk about powerful women and of course, we`ll also talk about 2024.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, she lives in New York State, which is -- anyway. Then multi- talented actor and producer Kate Del Castillo is joining us in studio to talk about bringing back her international hit series. "3rd Hour of TODAY" will be right back live from New York State.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JONES: We are back on this Monday morning. And we are reminded of a famous quote, this is from \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 . She said, if you want something said, ask a man. If you want something done, ask a woman.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Former South Carolina governor, former ambassador to the United Nations under President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Nikki Haley took that advice to heart. In fact, it`s the basis of her new book. There`s the title, "If You Want Something Done...: Leader Lessons from BOLD WOMEN."\par \par \uc2 In the book, she profiles that obstacles and accomplishments of 10 powerful women throughout history and weaves them in with her own story. And the former ambassador joins us this morning.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER SOUTH CAROLINA GOVERNOR, FORMER AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS UNDER \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  AND AUTHOR: Great to be with both of you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Welcome back. Welcome back. I just told you in the green room, I started reading the book this weekend. It`s a fantastic concept. We`re going to dig into the book in just a moment. But let`s start with -- because, I mean, the reality as you write this book about accomplished women and there has been some talk, perhaps you have heard, 2024, that maybe this accomplished woman would for president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 I know you have said you haven`t made a decision. How are you going to decide? What factors will you consider and what`s your timetable?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I mean, I think, first of all, our focus is November. You know, everybody`s looking at the November elections and I have been all over the country working on that. And then, after, you know, the first of the year, Michael and I will sit down. We`ll figure out if there is a place for me. If there is a place for me, I`ve never lost a race, I am not going to start now. We will put 1,000 percent in and we will finish it.\par \par \uc2 If there`s not a place for me, I have fought for this country as long as I can remember and I will do it until my last breath.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And you -- I think you said last spring that if your former boss, Former President Trump, if he does decide to run, that you will sit this one out, you won`t run. Is that still the case? And if so, why?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I mean, we`ll see if he runs. You know, we`ll see if he runs. He hasn`t said that he is going to run. And so, you know, we will make a decision after the first of next year and figure it out.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Would you ever run against him?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: It`s not something I think about. I was proud to serve with him in the administration. It`s something that, you know, I can tell you that I`m thrilled with the accomplishments we did together. We had a great working relationship. But right now, we will see what he does.\par \par \uc2 JONES: You have been very involved in the midterm elections this year. As you know, some Republicans, you know, the candidates running have denied the results of the election. Do you think that`s a disqualifier?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Well, I mean, I think that the candidates I`m helping, they are really focused on the economy. They are very focused on crime. They are focused on education. Those are the things people are talking about.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 I mean, do I think that the elections were real? Yes. Do I think that Joe Biden is the president? Yes. Unfortunately, I am not happy with that, but that is what it is. And the candidates that we`re talking about, real people, they are talking about the fact that it cost more money to go to the gas station, and that`s how they go to work. It cost more money at the grocery store, and that`s how they feed their family.\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, they deny those election results though, you don`t think that`s a disqualifier?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I don`t know that I`ve talked to anyone that`s denied the election results. Everybody that I`m helping acknowledges the fact that the elections, you know, were real.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Let`s talk about the book. What was the impetus? How did it come about?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: You know, it`s -- this is a book about ordinary women that did extraordinary things. And if you are a parent, you know that if you raise strong women, they become strong -- if you raise strong girls, they become strong women and strong women become strong leaders.\par \par \uc2 And so, I was a fan of \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2 . And loved how she was very confident in herself. She believed in Britain but believed had herself. And then, when I went to the United Nations, I learned more about Jeane Kirkpatrick, who was my predecessor. And she was someone who was unapologetic about loving America.\par \par \uc2 But then, I met people like Nadia Murad, who was in Iraq. She was part of the Yazidi community. They were overtaken by ISIS. She was gang raped. She was -- her family died and she came out, won the Nobel Peace Prize, fought for her community. Or people like Cindy Warmbier, where, you kno, her son, Otto Warmbier, we know was tortured in North Korea. But you find these stories that are just fantastic of women who had sheer will and knew that they were meant for great things, and they deserve to be in the room.\par \par \uc2 And I hope that this is a reminder to every parent who has a child that we want those kids to know they deserve to be in the room. If you are someone who has had a female mentor or a leader, give them this as a thank you and say, thank you for believing in me. And these are inspirational stories. I hope it will inspire a lot of women.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: One of the things I found interesting in the book is this idea -- and I never knew this -- you, throughout the course of your career, you send notes to women who inspire you. Who have you sent notes to?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I mean, you know, any time I see a woman make a leap. You know, I think I sent one to the head of the New York Stock Exchange. I send it to the first female head of city. You know, I just sent it to the new female anchor at Fox News. You know, any time I see a woman do something, I think we have to give that and push them and say, go get them. You know, because women second guess themselves. And any time we can go and just push them a little further and say, you`ve got this, it`s important.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JONES: And finally, listen, we just -- you just heard us talking about breast cancer awareness, we were talking so much about health and wellness for women. I was just reading that you`re running now. Did you just finish a half marathon?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I finished a half marathon in December.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Last I told you, I picked up running.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I finished a half marathon in December.\par \par \uc2 JONES: What did (INAUDIBLE) finally?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: You know, it`s a great way to tune out the noise. Like, a lot of people don`t realize I don`t live in the politics.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I have a regular life. But when you get out and run, it`s a great way to just kind of center your mind. You can take God with you and you just plan your day out and you can kind of figure out what really matters. It`s a great way to kind of forget the world for a while. So, it`s --\par \par \uc2 JONES: So, you`re just going to keep running? Marathon next?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: I ran seven miles yesterday. I`m going to keep on going.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: OK.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Tell Michael hello. Is he running with you?\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Love it.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Good deal.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right. Thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HALEY: Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Congratulations again on the book. Again, it`s called, If You Want Something Done...: Leadership Lessons from BOLD WOMEN." It`s out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you. All right. Coming up, Kate Del Castillo joins us live. We`re going to find out about the highly anticipated return of her hit show.\par \par \uc2 And then, later, we are stepping in our October "Start TODAY" walking challenge, speaking of fitness. And Stephanie Mansour is going to show us some moves to add to our walks. What are you guys doing?\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Way to go, Jill.\par \par \uc2 JONES: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Way to go, Jill.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 MARTIN-BROOKS: This morning, we are catching up with the incredibly talented Kate Del Castillo. You know her from blockbusters, of course, like "Bad Boys for Life" and the animated film "The Book of Life."\par \par \uc2 JONES: And now, Kate is starring in the long awaited third season of Telamundo "La Reina del Sur" returning as anti-hero Teresa Mendoza.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Kate`s character is taking on the male dominated cartels of South America, once again picking up four years after the events of season two.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, Kate joining us now. Kate, good to see you. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Hey, Kate.\par \par \uc2 MARTIN-BROOKS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KATE DEL CASTILLO, ACTRESS: Thank you so much for having me. Good morning, everyone.\par \par \uc2 ROKER: So, you have been playing this character now. How is Teresa changed from when you first started playing her?\par \par \uc2 DEL CASTILLO: Oh, my God. It`s been -- now, it`s been 12 years since we started the first season. It`s only three seasons. But, you know, they go for long. And now, is just more mature Teresa Mendoza. But she is always -- you know, she`s raw and she is bad spoken, and she is an anti-heroin. So, she drinks a lot, you know. Likes tequila a lot. But she steals the show. So, she`s still the same Teresa. It`s only that now she has a kid and her daughter is