ancer Research Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And yes, it`s obviously something that needs to be addressed and is going to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We talked about how Elton John`s done, you know, so much work. When we think of him, we think of HIV AIDS. And when we think of you, I mean, you`ve been part of this campaign for decades.\par \par \uc2 HURLEY: 27 years.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: 27 years.\par \par \uc2 HURLEY: Well, I came to the TODAY show for the first time 27 years ago.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And what is -- why is it so important to you?\par \par \uc2 HURLEY: Well, I think it`s because in each intervening year, more -- I know, more and more women who`ve been diagnosed, it`s still one in eight women will be diagnosed in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 So even though, because of the research because of the treatments, because of the prevention, you know, mortality rates have decreased by 43 percent since the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 But still, a lot of women are dying. And that`s why you know, even though we`ve been here, 30 years, we will be here as long as it takes, I think until they find a cure, which they will if there`s more money.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You`re right, you`re right. And we should point out that the Empire State Building and you`re in tonight, is it going to be in dazzling pink?\par \par \uc2 HURLEY: It`s going to be pink, this first building we ever let pink in the year 2000.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 HURLEY: And it`s an annual thing. Of course, we haven`t done it for two years because of COVID. So, we did it remotely.\par \par \uc2 So, this is very important for us to go back here today. And you know, remind everyone that we`re here. We care. And that one day there`ll be -- there will be a cure for breast cancer. Women will stop dying. It`s just about the research.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: What you do is so important.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We`re so happy you`re here. Thank you so much for joining us today. We appreciate it.\par \pa