o cute.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Cute.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: So, cute.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Is that leather?\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: It`s -- so, it`s faux leather.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Faux leather.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Right.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Go figure. So, we have like kind of two trends in one in this coat which is great. I love the cropped silhouette of this, because a puffer jacket you get, I think it`s big and oversized.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: But this really makes it feel super modern.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Now, is that warm too?\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: I love it.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: It is? You love that?\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: It is. It`s nice and cozy. It`s a great --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s a super flattering --\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: I know, and it is great link (PH).\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I love it. It`s flattering.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: She is like, I think I like this. I`m like it looks really good (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Now, let`s bring in the Queen Phyllis (PH), because --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: The Queen Phyllis.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: We need Phyllis.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: -- no fashion show is complete without our Phyllis.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Of course. So, I love nothing more than an ivory coat for fall and winter.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Look at Phyllis.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: It`s so -- I know. She loves --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: She`s gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: It`s so classic, it`s stylish, it`s chic, it`s polished, it`s refined. And again, what`s great about a long coat like this is that you really don`t have to worry about what you`re wearing underneath, because it`s almost --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAGER: It`s a statement.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Right, and it`s --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: But look -- but look at her boots.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Of course. Well, we had to bring your (INAUDIBLE) game, right?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: We had fun boots here. And again, we just kept the rest of the outfit fairly simple, but a classic ivory coat, you can never go wrong with it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And what`s the price point is -- yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: So, these are a little bit like, on -- I think, this one`s a little bit on more expensive aside from Abercrombie.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, high. OK.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: Not crazy expensive.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, looks good.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: But this is an investment piece that you will have forever.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: And does not feel warm enough to wear in all winter?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 PHYLLIS, MODEL (PH): Exactly. Warm and I look cute.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: You look really cute, Phyllis.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Phyllis, remember when used to work for the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PHYLLIS: Yes, I do. I do.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: OK.\par \par \uc2 PHYLLIS: Long ago.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: And she retired, and now, she`s a best model.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: I know.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: She is a model.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: I know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: How cool are you.\par \par \uc2 GARCIA: I know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, to check out these cozy faux coach, you can head to today.com/shop.\par \par \uc2 HAGER: Coming up next, Colin Hanks is going to tell us about his true crime thriller, right after this.\par \par \uc2 COLIN HANKS, AMERICAN ACTOR: Here we go --\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221006eia30005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc92}{\*\bkmkend toc92}\b \uc2 An Exclusive Interview With Katie Couric\b0\par\par\uc2 Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie \par \uc2 3,411 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: All right, meantime, we are marking the start of breast cancer awareness month with a story that hits close to home.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: Yes, our friend Katie Couric is with us. She has long been a cancer advocate using her platform, including her time here at Today to inform and educate the public. Well now she`s gone from cancer advocate to cancer survivor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voice-over): Last week in a personal essay on the Katie Couric Media website she wrote, June 21st was the first day of summer, my eighth wedding anniversary, and the day I found out I had breast cancer. Like more than a third of women, Katie, was not up to date on her annual screening writing, had the pandemic given me a skewed sense of time? Had it messed with my memory?\par \par \uc2 Six months overdue, Katie, went in for a mammogram and a breast ultrasound noting something suspicious in her left breast. Her doctor did a biopsy and it was cancer. After having a lumpectomy and 15 days of radiation, Katie, is sharing her story to educate others and once again save lives. In 2000, two years after her husband, Jay Monahan, died of colon cancer, Katie aired her own colonoscopy right here on Today. As a result, there was a 20 percent increase in screenings with the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  dubbed, the Couric effect.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Katie that smile right here please.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE (voice-over): Now, she`s on a mission again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And she`s right here with us. Hi, Katie, it`s so good to see you.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Hi, look at us all in pink.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We are in pink. We got the memo. It is breast cancer awareness month. And the first question has to be, how you`re feeling? How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 COURIC: I`m feeling great. I`m just getting over a cold. That`s why I wouldn`t give you all a hug this morning to protect you but I`m feeling just fine. You know, I finished radiation last week. They said it made you tired. I was actually not too tired from it. I had a lumpectomy in July. And my next stop I go on something called aromatase inhibitors which suppress estrogen because I have a hormone receptor positive, HER2 new negative tumor that they removed when I had my lumpectomy. But I just feel super lucky that it was diagnosed when it was that I went even though I was late that I went when I did.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Can you take us back because I think I mean, you are a cancer advocate, obviously but it`s a whole different ballgame when you go in, you think you missed your mammogram by six months, which is probably like most people at home right now. So you went in for your checkup? Tell us about that moment.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Well, I went in with my phone camera and I was going to share it with my followers and people who read my newsletter and go the website to say don`t forget, I forgot, I don`t want you all to. So when my rady -- breast radiologist Susan Rossmann said, can you turn off your phone? I was like, uh-oh, what does that mean. And she said I think there`s something we really need to biopsy and I want to do it today.\par \par \uc2 [07:35:11]\par \par \uc2 So I thought, oh, my God, you must be kidding me. And then which I found out the next days, she called me, I was pretty stunned. And I think those words, it`s cancerous or you have cancer, do stop you in your tracks. But she told me it was treatable. We needed to have a plan. So I went from feeling shocked to not that shocked, given my family`s history, and the, you know, and to relieved because my exposure to cancer with Jay and Emily and my mother in law, Carol Monahan, you know, they were all advanced, and the prognosis was really tough.\par \par \uc2 So, I felt so grateful, honestly. And that`s why I`m so thrilled to be able to talk to you all about the importance of screening. And I know, Hoda you know about that, because Hoda and I had, you know, you have the same kind of tumor I had when you were in your 40s. When you talk about early detection, it was so funny, because when I was diagnosed here, I remember I sat with Corvo, David Corvo. And I told him what had happened. And he said, I`m going to tell you something. I said, OK. He said, I know a lot of friends who have breast cancer, and they all have one thing in common. And I said, what`s that? He said, they`re still here. And that was about just like, for you and for me to once you get the early detection, some of it, then it just becomes, now it`s medical.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Right, exactly. And there have been so many strides made.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: But likes -- I mean, as you said, a third of all women, I didn`t even know that number, didn`t get screened during COVID. And a lot of screening places shut down. I think our, the cadence of our lives changed. And I think we just got off track. And that happened to me as well.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Before we get into some of the particulars, because I know you are a woman on a mission, and you have a really important message. I just, I immediately thought of Carrie and Ellie, your daughters, they`re in their 20s now.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Yes, Ellie is 31, if you can believe it, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know, they experienced so much loss at a young age, you know, their father, their grandmother, their aunt? And I`m sure it was, that was a hard call to make even knowing that you, you know, had a really good prognosis.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Yes, it was -- I was nervous about it. I waited a few days, so I could process and really understand what we were dealing with and I FaceTime each of them. And Ellie is in LA. And Carrie never calls me back, so I basically had to FaceTime both of them. She`s here in New York. And, I told them, but I was very reassuring. But I saw in their faces, you know, it`s just hard to deliver that news, no matter how you do it. But, you know, I assured them that I was going to be fine. And Carrie came with me, when I got my lumpectomy, when I was being wheeled into the operating room. She was singing the "Arms of an Angel."\par \par \uc2 She`s so funny. But anyway, but they`ve been incredibly supportive. And of course, I was worried about that. But again, just so grateful that they caught it early enough, so it could be treated. And by the way, you know, even when it`s at later stage than mine, I`m stage 1-A, there are so many treatments available. And a lot of women are living with breast cancer. But we need to do better. We need to come up with better treatments, and we need to get more women screened, I was surprised to see only 70 percent of women actually get breast cancer screening. And that number went down to 65 percent during the pandemic, but I think it`s probably went even lower.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Here`s a weird thing that we just saw on the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  website. It says, and I think we have a graphic, women 55 and over should switch to mammograms every two years. They`re kind of giving you that window. You don`t need to go each year, you can go every two.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Yes, I mean, I think the recommendations have been all over the map. I mean, I`m not a doctor, and I`m trying to play one on T.V. but, you know, I think people should go every year. You should talk to your doctor. I think women have to advocate for their own health. And that`s why I`m so, you know, so excited to be able to talk about dense breasts, in particular.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I wanted to ask you about that because you were someone that you`ve learned you have dense breasts. First of all, what does that mean, I thought I knew, I just asked you, you`re like, no, that`s not it. And what does that mean in terms of the kind of screening you need?\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Well, this is my message, so thank you for asking that. It`s 45 to 50 percent of women in this country have what are considered dense breasts. This is not something you can tell by feeling your breasts if your breasts are lumpy. It doesn`t necessarily mean you have dense breasts. It`s indicated on a mammogram. So you have to ask your radiologists or radiologists ideally should be telling you, you have dense breasts and then you often need secondary screening. So my radiologists compared it to try and defined snow balls in a field of snow.\par \par \uc2 [07:40:02]\par \par \uc2 I got a 3D mammogram which is definitely superior to standard mammograms and my breast radiologists Susan Rossmann said it was made it much easier for her to see it. But it was key to get a breast ultrasound to confirm that I had a tumor that needed to be biopsied. And a lot of women don`t know this, 38 states require doctors to say, hey, Hoda, you have dense breasts, but your mammogram is fine. So what are you supposed to do with that information? You don`t necessarily know. You need additional screening.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And by the way insurance doesn`t necessarily pay for that additional ultrasound screening.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: So two issues, yes, first with the notification, Savannah. So the \uc2 FDA\uc2  said the language needs to change. All states need to require notification with much more specificity about what you`re supposed to do with that information. And then you`re right, only 14 states in the District require insurance to pay some or all of breast ultrasound screening for women with dense breasts. And often the reimbursement is very small. So I`ve been working with Congressman Rosa DeLauro of Connecticut, and she`s introducing legislation this month that would require insurance companies to cover. No, no, you know, cost on the patient.\par \par \uc2 Breast ultrasounds for women who have dense breasts, because it`s criminal, all these breast cancer diagnoses would happen much earlier if in fact, women with dense breasts had breast ultrasounds.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow, you`re already -- so you already spoken to the congresswoman, that`s already on the move.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You know what, guys, she`s on a mission. So just go ahead and do what she ask.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: But I just really want to get screened. I`ll put it off. I`ve heard from so many who said your story made me make my appointment. But also, if you have dense breasts, find out what more you need to do.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Where your kids do any genetic testing? Do you want them to?\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Yes. Ellie already has. And what`s also -- I`m so glad you asked me this too, because I`ve been, you know, learning and reporting on this myself. So basically, you know, I used to look for the BRCA gene, BRCA1 or BRCA2, which you could increase your risk of breast cancer up to 80 percent. But the panel has increased to almost 30, or I think more than 30 gene mutations that give you a slightly higher risk, sometimes as high as 40 percent higher for breast cancer, genetic testing has gone into the mainstream, insurance companies cover it.\par \par \uc2 And it really is a great tool to help you understand what you need to do vis-a-vis screening and how often you need to do it. And you shouldn`t be having a conversation with your healthcare provider about breast cancer as early as 25 just to start the conversation. And black women have a 40 percent higher mortality rate. So if you`re black or you have Ashkenazi Jew heritage, you should start discussing it at age 30 because it might change your screening protocol.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Well, Katie, you had such an impact earlier in your career after Jay`s passing and I believe you`ll have that impact, again, we have our homework cut out for us. I did make my appointment after hearing --\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Good, good. I`m so glad. I`m so glad because I really -- I just want to share what I`ve learned with other women and hopefully keep them healthy and happy.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, we love you, Katie, for --\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Well, thank you. I feel like my little textbook is going in here. But I`ve really studied this and I have the facts to back it up. But thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you Katie, we adore you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We`re delighted to have you and looking so good and feeling well.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: I`m getting there still a little sniffling.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: We might hug you, anyway.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Craig, over to you.\par \par \uc2 [07:43:38]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thank you, Katie. Thank you. Thank you. Still ahead here on a Monday morning, some more news you can use with Halloween on the way and record spending expected. We got some advice for saving money without skipping on your family celebrations. But first, this messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: And welcome back. Time now for another check of the forecast, how are we looking Mr. Roker?\par \par \uc2 ROKER: Well, all in all, we`ve got a fairly quiet day at that, again, remnants of Ian off the northeast coast of Delmarva Peninsula. So we`re watching some heavy rain there. Sunny and mild from the gulf coast all the way up into the Great Lakes. We do see some showers and more monsoon moisture coming into the southwest. So we`re going to be watching that sunshine up and down the West Coast. That`s what`s going on around the country. Here`s what`s happening in your neck of the woods.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 ROKER: And that is your latest weather.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right, Mr. Roker, thank you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on a Monday, could solar panel, solar panels could that be the best answer to high electricity bills especially with the cold of winter on its way.\par \par \uc2 [07:48:05]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Vicky Nguyen will be here to walk us through the process of having them installed, how soon you could expect to save too, but first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Still ahead, just call them the Duke and Duchess of Waco. Our first, look at Chip and Joanna Gaines new royal size project.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: That more on PopStart. Wow. But first, your local news and weather.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:24]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [08:00:00] SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: It`s 8:00 on "TODAY. Disaster zone. Round-the-clock rescues in Florida after Hurricane Ian tore through the State.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What`s your name?\par \par \uc2 RICK, FLORIDA RESIDENT: RICK.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Rick, we`re going to get you out of here, alright.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: This morning, new questions being raised about whether evacuations could have been ordered earlier. Plus, a look at the pets displaced by the storm. How shelters are finding these animals forever homes.\par \par \uc2 Then tricks to save on treats. We`re rounding up tips to help you celebrate Halloween on a budget from costumes to candy. How to celebrate spooky season without breaking the bank.\par \par \uc2 Plus, "Start Today". We`re kicking off our walking club`s October`s challenge with more than 100,000 members around the world getting in on the fun. How you can join us as Al leads those us first steps into the month.\par \par \uc2 And Queen B and Tiffany. Beyonce teaming up with the iconic brand sporting some eye-popping jewelry and releasing a new video and as part of the collab. We`ve your first look today, Monday, October 3, 2022.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hi to our sons Greg and Jeffrey.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Watching in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We love you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Love you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Plymouth, Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`m turning 10.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: From Waukee, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s Clara`s (ph) 13th birthday.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From Reno, Nevada.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Visiting the plaza.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because I`m the station`s biggest fan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Here from.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Ketchikan, Alaska. Hello.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Brought my mom from California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To meet \uc2 Al Roker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Hi, everybody. Welcome back to "TODAY" on this Monday morning. Thanks for joining us. We think it`s cold out there. We got friends from Alaska.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Right.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: They`re like, summertime, you know.\par \par \uc2 They picked a good day to meet \uc2 Al Roker\uc2 . He`s back.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS TODAY, CO-ANCHOR: They picked a good day to meet \uc2 Al Roker\uc2 . He`s back.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: He`s back.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: I know.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. Cool.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: So, cool to have Al here.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Let`s get to your news, it`s 8:00.\par \par \uc2 And five days after Hurricane Ian struck Florida, the death toll there is still rising. Search and rescue teams are working nonstop. And this morning, the mayor of one hard-hit city questions confronts questions about the timing of evacuation warnings. Our coverage starts with NBC`s Sam Brock in Cape Coral.\par \par \uc2 Sam, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning. The scene here in Matlacha is basically apocalyptic. There used to be a road over my shoulder. That`s right gone. This is the entry point to Pine Island.\par \par \uc2 As you hear all of the construction sounds going on right now, they`re getting ready to start the process of building a road where one used to exist. The asphalt just layered over itself right there as residents are about to build a make-shift plank just to get out to Pine Island.\par \par \uc2 In Lee County, that`s where we are, there have been somewhere in the neighborhood of 800 rescues so far. 2,000 in total since Hurricane Ian made landfall. The death toll at this point is 87, roughly half of those are coming from Lee County. And all of the attention this morning right now on when officials in this county issued a mandatory evacuation order, that came out Tuesday morning when on Wednesday evening, the National Hurricane Center said that there was life-threatening storm surge expected in Lee County, expected in Fort Myers, and still there was a delay in the process of actually getting people out. Here is the mayor of Fort Myers beach explaining why that was the case.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR RAY MURPHY, FORT MYERS BEACH, FLORIDA: In the optimum and best-case situations, everybody wants the most warning they can get. But I`m not in a position right now to challenge those warnings or what have you. As I say, that`s still knock on the county level and the state level. I`m going to let them fight that out right now. We`ve got much more work to do here on the ground that we`re busy with.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right. Sam Brock there in Florida. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221006eia300001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc93}{\*\bkmkend toc93}\b \uc2 Meet the Press for October 2, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Blayne Alexander, Julio Vaqueiro, Symone Sanders- Townsend \par \uc2 9,080 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:17]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, HOST, NBC`S "MEET THE PRESS WITH CHUCK TODD" (voice over): This Sunday, disaster zone.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Some of the damage was, you know, almost indescribable.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hurricane Ian`s punishing path across Florida leaves a staggering scale of destruction, entire neighborhoods wiped out.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: It`s not just a crisis for Florida. This is an American crisis.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now the race to recover begins. But how should homeowners rebuild in the face of more extreme weather? And who should foot the bill?\par \par \uc2 I`ll talk to Florida`s Republican senator, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , and North Carolina`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, Putin`s land grab. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  announces the annexation of four regions of Ukraine, escalating the conflict and drawing widespread condemnation from around the world.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: America is fully prepared, with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, to defend every single inch of \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ukraine responds by asking to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . So how real is the nuclear threat from Russia?\par \par \uc2 I`ll talk to NATO`s secretary general \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, split issues. The two parties are divided when it comes to the major issues to focus on before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R) KENTUCKY & MINORITY LEADER: Inflation, crime and open borders.\par \par \uc2 TODD: While Democrats take a different path, centering on abortion.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISSA SLOTKIN, (D) MICHIGAN: A cold, heartless, violent approach to women`s health.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Plus, new numbers from the NBC News/Telemundo poll. How decisive will Latinos be in deciding which party controls Congress?\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are Telemundo news anchor Julio Vaqueiro; USA Today Washington bureau chief Susan Page; Symone Sanders-Townsend, former chief spokeswoman for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ; and Stephen Hayes, editor of The Dispatch.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is "Meet the Press" with Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. For many it`s been a tough week. With 37 days until the midterm elections, it is a tale of two campaigns right now. For Republicans the midterm campaign that they`re trying to run is about inflation, immigration and crime. For Democrats, the campaign they`re trying to run is about access to abortion and the growing extremism of the Republican Party represented by a singular figure in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But with both parties so sure what they want the midterms to be about, it is worth keeping in mind Tom Brokaw`s favorite reminder to many of us during campaign seasons past, "Watch for UFOs." In this case -- it`s "The UnForeseen will Occur. It`s a guarantee," he says. Well, this week the unforeseen was represented by two major events.\par \par \uc2 In Russia \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  announced the illegal annexation of 15 percent of Ukraine, after a series of Russian setbacks on the battlefield. Putin`s war is already threatening to drive Europe into recession, soaring energy costs. And this week a massive explosion created four separate leaks in that critical natural gas pipeline between Russia and Germany. President Biden accused Russia of a deliberate act of sabotage.\par \par \uc2 Then there`s Hurricane Ian. This is one of the five worst hurricanes in America`s recorded history. Ian is also one of the costliest storms ever to hit the United States. Early estimates suggest it could cost up to $47 billion just in insured losses alone. And as more extreme weather events hit the United States, there are some big questions about who should pay to rebuild, and more importantly where we should be rebuilding.\par \par \uc2 Blayne Alexander is in hard-hit Fort Myers for us this morning. And, Blayne, I know the destruction is awful. The question is, are we going to find more people safe and alive?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Unfortunately, that is the case. They`re still searching this morning, Chuck. And, you know, it`s scenes like these that really tell the story of Ian`s wrath. This entire area where I`m standing was once a marina. It was full of boats and shops. Now all of that is gone. And when you widen out and you take a bigger look at this entire area, unfortunately you just see more of the same.\par \par \uc2 Now, where I`m standing right now is truly one of the hardest hit areas. But, Chuck, what`s even more devastating is the human toll. At least 77 people have lost their lives due to Ian. And, unfortunately, that number is only expected to rise. Right now crews are still sifting through the rubble. They`re trying to see if there are any lives that can still be saved. And they`re recovering those who unfortunately did not make it. This morning there are a number of people who are still unaccounted for.\par \par \uc2 And, Chuck, making things only more complicated, there are at least two islands that are completely cut off from the mainland. The road was destroyed in the storm, so they`re using boats to bring people back and forth. You know, I`ve talked with countless people, and they tell me the reason they stayed is that they simply underestimated the power of this storm, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, we`ve learned that President Biden is going to come here to Florida to tour the devastation on Wednesday. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Blayne Alexander in Fort Myers for us.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:02]\par \par \uc2 Blayne, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And joining me now from Naples is Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  of Florida. He`s also a former governor.\par \par \uc2 Senator Scott, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): Hi, Chuck. Boy, it`s a tough day for Florida and been a tough week for Florida.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure has. Let me start with this. Look, in the first 72 hours, the people on the ground never feel like the recovery is coming fast enough. So I understand that. But tell me what you`re seeing. I know you`re in Naples. You`ve probably taken a look firsthand at Fort Myers. How`s the recovering going from your perspective, in the first 48 hours?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yeah, I`ve -- -- I`ve -- I`ve toured; I`ve been in Collier County, Naples. I`ve been up in Fort Myers, Lee County, and I did and aerial tour of all the areas and talked to first responders. I`ve been up to Sarasota, Charlotte -- Charlotte, where we got a lot of damage. People don`t realize that. And, actually, I was in Kissimmee yesterday, where there was actually quite a bit of flooding.\par \par \uc2 So here`s -- here`s -- here`s what`s happening. We`re still working on rescuing people. It`s -- I mean, this is just horrible. The people have lost their lives. It`s horrible that people are still possibly stuck in rubble. But I`ve been talking to the sheriffs and first responders and they`re trying to get to these people as quickly as they can. They`ve been working to evacuate people that stayed on in places like Sanibel and Pine Island and Fort Myers Beach. There`s areas that, in -- up in Charlotte County, where people were traded.\par \par \uc2 So they`ve been trying to rescue everybody. But there`s still work to do. So people are working hard. They`re trying to get the power back on, make sure your phones work. But the biggest thing right now is just continuing to make sure we find everybody, and anybody that needs help, they get -- they get to the hospital and get whatever care they need. We still have a lot of water outages. So there`s -- there`s a lot of work left -- left to do. So it`s really -- my heart goes out to everybody, though.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You brought up Charlotte County. I`m glad you did. That`s a county whose -- has a population -- I think a majority of the population`s over the age of 65. They were having trouble just getting bottled water into Charlotte County yesterday. Has that improved?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: It`s -- everything keeps getting better. One thing -- I think one thing that surprised everybody yesterday was the -- 75 being closed, with the flooding on 75, and actually 41 and State Highway 70, they all flooded, which impacts your ability to get things in. But, you know, I`ve been talking to the sheriff and doing everything I can to -- you know, when I hear things, where there`s something, I pass it on to the right person to make sure they get the resources there. I`ve been talking to FEMA. I know they`re absolutely committed to get all the resources here that they can, and at every level. But our sheriffs, our first responders, you really have to thank them for what they`re doing. Our utility workers, they`re working hard to get power back on and get people back to a normal life.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hurricane Ian is the 17th hurricane that has first made landfall in Florida since 1992. And we have a graphic on our map. Some people in Pensacola are going to say, "Hey, you`re missing some hurricanes." These are ones that hit Florida first, not Alabama first and then impacted Florida. And it`s been a total cost over -- recovery cost of $213 billion in losses.\par \par \uc2 So the question is, as we rebuild, things like mobile manufactured housing, should that even be legal in the state of Florida anymore?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think -- I think what we -- we have to do is take is every experience -- and, Chuck, if you go to Charlotte County in particular, when I did the flyover there, there`s a lot of mobile home parks that got unbelievable amounts of damage. So you`re right. We have to go back and say. every time, "OK, so how do we make sure that we don`t -- the biggest thing is we don`t lose a life." That`s number one. What do we have to do? What laws do we have to change? What building codes do we have to do?\par \par \uc2 We did it after Andrew. We did it while I was governor. We`ve got to look at it every time. What do you do to make sure this doesn`t happen again? You don`t ever want these things to happen again. So I think every county`s going to have to look at that and say, does that make sense for their county?\par \par \uc2 I know -- I`m in Collier County. I`m not sure that they would do any new manufactured housing here, but every county is going to have to look at that. At the same time, you know, people that -- they want to live in Florida. They want to live in the Sunshine State. And, you know, the more expensive housing you have, it makes it difficult for people to -- to live there. So it`s -- I guess it`s a balance.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I guess -- I hear you. But, you know, at what point is affordability -- you know, I get that you have to err -- you want to err on the side of affordability. At the same time, you want to get to safety. And the fact of the matter is these mobile manufactured homes, I understand it`s a county by county decision here, but should the state step in?\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s a reason why you can`t get property insurance right now in many places in Florida, and this might be one of the reasons.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yeah. The -- I think the state has to look at all of these things. I think that the -- you know -- I mean, look, Chuck, I should know; I grew up in a poor family, so I always think about the cost of things...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: ... because, you know, you impact the poorest families every time you raise the cost of something.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:05]\par \par \uc2 So -- but you also want to keep people safe. So we also have to always look at how do you make sure people evacuate, get out of harm`s way as quickly as possible. I know it was a big issue that I focused on when I was governor. You`ve got to -- it`s, sort of, all of the above, Chuck. Because you do have to look at what should -- what housing should we allow to make sure people are always going to be safe?\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s the best way to get insurance companies to do business in the state of Florida? Is it stricter building codes?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think -- I think it`s, sort of, you have to have stricter building codes. You have to make sure that, you know, you learn from every -- every storm. But then, on top of that, you`ve got to make sure that there`s no fraud. We`ve got to -- we`ve got to really crack down on fraud. When I was governor, we worked hard -- every year you had to work on making sure that there wasn`t fraud in the insurance market, because who pays for that? The consumers do. And the poorest families end up paying for it the most, because somebody takes advantage of the system.\par \par \uc2 So what you`ve got to do is, every year, what we had to do was we had to look at what`s going on in the insurance market, where there`s fraud, and how do we eliminate it? And that`s something the state`s going to have to do very aggressively. Because, as you know, property insurance has gotten too expensive, and we`re losing the insurance companies that want to write here. Insurance companies are going out of business because they can`t afford to write here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You and Senator Rubio have already sent a letter requesting additional funds and resources for the state of Florida. This is probably not going be a controversial vote. But when Governor DeSantis was Congressman DeSantis, he voted against a similar bill to help out the victims in the states of Superstorm Sandy.\par \par \uc2 And at the time he said, "I sympathize with the victims of Hurricane Sandy and believe that those who purchased flood insurance should have their claims paid. At the same time, allowing the program to increase its debt by another $9.7 billion with no plan to offset the spending, with cuts elsewhere, is not fiscally responsible. There are going to be some other members of Congress who don`t want to support this supplemental. What`s your message to them, and -- who may have the same mindset that then Congressman DeSantis had?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I think what you have to do is you have to -- you have to, you know, spend the money wisely. But you do need -- I mean, the federal government is a partner in this. And I learned that as governor. The federal government is a major partner in helping families, helping businesses, helping governments get back to normal. But you don`t want to waste money. As an example, I -- I put a lot of effort into cracking down on the significant fraud in the debris pickup market, in what happens after a hurricane.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve been actually trying to get a bill passed since I`ve been up there -- I`ve been up there almost four years -- to fix that. Because there is -- there`s abuse in this. But we do have to provide disaster aid, and whether that`s for a hurricane or whether that`s for flooding, or whether that`s for wildfires, we`ve got to do that. And, you know, I hope people will continue to support, and -- and -- and I think we ought to be doing -- we`ve got to come up with a way to do this where it`s -- it`s a longer-term strategy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re 37 days away from Election Day. You`ve got, another part of your job is Senate Republicans, trying to get them to win the Senate. Do you believe the Republicans are on track to get control of the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, most of my effort right now is on the hurricane relief -- this hurricane. But, absolutely, we`ll -- I think we`re at 52 seats plus. If you look at Biden`s numbers are really bad. People have rejected the Biden agenda. The -- we have great candidates. The Democrats have to defend what Biden has done. So I think -- I think it`s going to be a good November for Republicans. But, you know, every -- you know, we`ve got to keep working hard every day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Was it a mistake for \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  to offer up that abortion ban at 15 weeks? Did that complicate your efforts to win the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Chuck, you know, everybody is -- you know, Democrats have talked a lot about that this year. So every candidate has been talking about what their position is on abortion. So I think -- I think it`s important that people tell people what their position is. And it`s being done state by state all across the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you don`t think it should be done at the federal level?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, I mean -- I mean, look, you can -- there`s arguments to do it at the federal level. Right now, there`s -- all the candidates are taking positions. They`ve been taking positions since, you know, the Dobbs decision, when the first leak came out. So I don`t know what Lindsey put out changed the conversation. But it is an important issue for the country. I`m pro-life, as you know.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think -- I think we ought to have reasonable restrictions. I think a lot of people are comfortable with 15 weeks and exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother. But every candidate gets to make their choice.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , Republican senator from Florida, also the former governor, from Naples this is morning. Everybody, I think, is a Floridian this morning, and we`re all hoping for the best as recovery efforts continue. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yeah, pray for all the families, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: President Biden approved an emergency declaration in North Carolina on Saturday as Ian moved through that state. Four people have died in North Carolina and more than 200,000 remain without power in a state that also has its own history of deadly storms.\par \par \uc2 And North Carolina governor, Democrat \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Governor Cooper, let me just start with, look, North Carolina was spared compared to Florida, but the fact is you had four fatalities. You`ve got some power outages. What`s the situation? What`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ROY COOPER\uc2  (D-NC): Well, we had a peak of over 400,000 power outages. And now we`re at about 33,000. We`ve got about 48 roads closed. Our hearts go out to the people who lost their lives. And certainly we have avoided the worst of it. And we sympathize with the people in Florida. We`ve offered help to them. We already have some logistical help on the ground in Florida. And since the storm has passed North Carolina, we are already in discussions with Florida officials to try to make sure that we help them.\par \par \uc2 This is a time when we all have to pull together to make sure that people are safe. A lot of times people lose their lives in the aftermath, trying to repair homes, running generators. We had a man die because he was running a generator inside a garage and died of carbon monoxide poisoning. So the after-effects of these disasters are always critical, and we need to pay attention to it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s talk about rebuilding from these disasters. North Carolina has had to do it. Florida has had to do it quite a few times. Is it time to put more strings when it comes to rebuilding, so that every dollar that`s spent is done for resiliency, so that it`s better for the next time? And who should pay for that?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: North Carolina has had a front-row seat when it comes to the effects of climate change. And we are making sure that we become a clean energy safe haven and that we are paying attention to resiliency. I`ve done a climate risk assessment and resiliency plan. You have to make tough decisions when you rebuild. We`ve had two 500-year floods within 23 months of each other. And we know that that`s not true anymore. We know that these areas are vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 So what we`re doing is making sure that we are using strategies like elevation and even buyouts. We`ve gone into local communities that have gotten hit several times. It has just become better to make sure that we create green space in the place where homes and businesses used to be, to soak up water that may come from a river flood, and then to relocate people. Those are tough decisions. But they`re going on right now.\par \par \uc2 We also need to make sure that our electric grid is more resilient. And I`m thankful for the federal help that`s come in that arena. We know that we`re going to be working on updating our grid, making sure that we are more resilient into the future.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, should flood insurance be mandatory in the state of North Carolina at this point, if you own a home? There`s -- obviously, you guys have been dealing with a lot of flooding risk.\par \par \uc2 And should we be banning mobile manufactured housing?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Well, we think that mobile manufactured housing, built in the right way and sometimes elevated, can be good for people, to make sure that they have an affordable place to live. We are pushing and encouraging people to buy flood insurance. We know that, particularly in these areas that are hit time and again, that we`ve got to be more resilient. So I think all of those issues have to be on the table.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Like Senator Scott, you have a secondary job as well, as an elected official. You head up the \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2 . You`re the campaign arm to help Democrats either win governorships or get re-elected. Let me ask you this. You, a year ago, thought you would expand the number of Democratic governorships. Are you still confident of that this November?\par \par \uc2 I still think we can. But -- but remember, we are swimming upstream when you look at history; the party of the White House doesn`t do well in the first midterm. In fact, I think in 2010 Republicans flipped 11 governors` seats after two years of the President Obama administration. And in 2014 I think they flipped four seats. So we`ve got a lot of incumbents to defend, because in 2018 Democratic governors did well.\par \par \uc2 And it`s really important that we have Democratic governors across this country, particularly when you look at the effects of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . What we thought were constitutional rights and freedoms are now going to be tossed to state capitals and state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: And it matters who your governor is.\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 So we think that we have a good chance even for pickups, not just defending our area but for pickups as well, even in a historically difficult time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Your organization, the \uc2 DGA\uc2 , has spent money to promote election deniers in Republican primaries. You did it in Illinois, Maryland and Pennsylvania. Are you comfortable with that?\par \par \uc2 These election deniers won, and Pennsylvania`s poll numbers are close. If one of them wins, are you going to regret it?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: First, there were no Liz Cheneys running for governor across this country. Second, these were big front-runners and won by big margins. And it was -- is important for the \uc2 DGA\uc2  to make sure that voters are reminded of these candidates` extreme positions, even during the primary. Because what you are seeing now is some of these candidates trying to moderate their positions. And the \uc2 DGA\uc2  was running the same message during primaries that they are in the general election, about the extreme nature of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 The goal here is to defend our democracy, to make sure we defend rights and freedoms, that we get competent people as CEOs of our state. We know how important it is. And I think it`s critical that people look closely at their candidates for governor, as we approach this midterm, because those governors are going to be making big decisions that affect everyday people`s lives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Governor \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democratic incumbent governor who`s not on the ballot this time -- you guys are always on the presidential cycle in North Carolina -- head of \uc2 DGA\uc2  but also dealing, I hope, hope, hope, your death toll stays as it is and we get power on soon. Thank you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thanks, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if you want to help the victims of Hurricane Ian, you can donate to these organizations that we`ve isolated here, Volunteer Florida, the Red Cross and of course \uc2 World Central Kitchen\uc2 . They`re delivering meals right now to communities impacted by the storm.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Vladimir Putin`s dangerous escalation of the war in Ukraine, a rising nuclear threat and Russia`s attempts to try to pull \uc2 NATO\uc2  into the conflict. I`m going to talk to the \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary general next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Now to the other big story. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is warning he will use all the power and all the means at his disposal to defend the Ukrainian territory Russia has illegally taken.\par \par \uc2 On Friday Putin said the United States created a precedent for using nuclear weapons by using them in World War II. And just hours after Putin`s speech, Ukrainian President Zelenskyy responded by announcing its country was submitting a fast-track application to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NATO\uc2  secretary general called Putin`s land grab "the largest attempted annexation of European territory by force since the Second World War." And \uc2 NATO\uc2  is warning it will retaliate for any attacks on the critical infrastructure of its 30 member countries. That statement follows the massive explosion which produced leaks in the Nord Stream pipelines between Russia and Germany.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:04]\par \par \uc2 President Biden echoed that warning himself on Friday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOSEPH R. BIDEN, JR.: America`s fully prepared, with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, to defend every single inch of \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory -- every single inch. So, Mr. Putin, don`t misunderstand what I`m saying -- every inch.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And NATO`s secretary-general, \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 , joins me now. Mr. Secretary, welcome back to "Meet the Press."\par \par \uc2 Let me start with...\par \par \uc2 JENS STOLTENBERG\uc2 , SECRETARY GENERAL, \uc2 NATO\uc2 : Thanks so much for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, let me start with the president`s comments, "every inch." I`ll use, internationally, "any meter." Does the Nord Stream pipeline -- if that is a Russian attack, does that count as an attack on a \uc2 NATO\uc2  nation?\par \par \uc2 STOLTENBERG: Let me first express my condolences to all those effected by the hurricane that has created so much damage in the United States over the last days. Then, on -- on the pipelines, what we have seen is a very serious situation, where two pipelines have been damaged. All evidence points against that this is sabotage, deliberately done by someone against these pipelines.\par \par \uc2 What is important now is that we support the ongoing investigation so we get the best possible picture of what happened and are able to establish all the facts. Any deliberate attack on critical \uc2 NATO\uc2  infrastructure will be met with a firm and united response from \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you concerned -- do you think \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is trying to coax \uc2 NATO\uc2  into this conflict?\par \par \uc2 STOLTENBERG: He has tried again and again to -- to tell a story that \uc2 NATO\uc2  is party to the conflict and that \uc2 NATO\uc2  caused this conflict. That`s absolutely wrong. First of all, this is a war that President Putin has started. It`s a war by -- by his choice. Second, NATO`s not part to this conflict. What we do is that we provide support to Ukraine, an independent sovereign nation in Europe that has the right to defend itself against the war of aggression. And this is a right actually enshrined in the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . charter. And therefore we will continue to provide support to Ukraine to defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What`s your level of concern about the nuclear threat right now?\par \par \uc2 STOLTENBERG: The rhetoric by President Putin, the nuclear rhetoric, is dangerous; it`s reckless. But it`s actually something we have heard several times before. But that doesn`t change the fact that this is dangerous. That`s also the reason why we have so clearly conveyed to President Putin that, in the use of nuclear weapons, without severe consequences for Russia, it will -- it will, of course, totally change the nature of the conflict. And we have also made it clear that a nuclear war cannot be won and must never be fought. And this is a message that \uc2 NATO\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies convey clearly to -- to Russia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You said something on Friday that got our attention. You said this. You said, "Inaction is a great risk, because that will create a world where Putin will see that, with impunity, he can use military force, invade the neighbor, and then establish a sphere of influence."\par \par \uc2 And this is your reaction to his annexation, here, on this. Inaction, what is your fear, that -- is the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance not going to respond to this illegal annexation? And should the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance respond to it?\par \par \uc2 STOLTENBERG: The best way to respond is to do exactly what we do now, and not least in the United States, demonstrating leadership in providing support to Ukraine. And we will continue to support Ukraine with military equipment, economic, financial support. And I welcomed the just recent announcement by President Biden of further U.S. support.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend we also have new announcements from Germany, from France, Denmark, Norway, the (inaudible) Republic and many others. So allies are stepping up their support to Ukraine. And that`s the best way to ensure that these territories, they are part of Ukraine and that Ukraine is actually able to liberate and retake occupied territory.\par \par \uc2 [09:30:10]\par \par \uc2 And over -- this weekend again, we have seen they were able to take a new town, Lyman, and that demonstrates the Ukrainians are making progress, are able to push back the Russian forces, because of their courage, because of their bravery and skills, but, of course, also because of the advanced weapons that the United States and other allies are providing, and this is making a difference on the battlefield every day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ukraine`s \uc2 NATO\uc2  application, they fast track it. Will NATO fast track Ukraine into the alliance?\par \par \uc2 STOLTENBERG: The \uc2 NATO\uc2  has an open-door policy, and every nation, including, of course, also Ukraine, has the right to choose its own path, including what kind of security arrangements they want to be a part of.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, any decision on membership has to be taken by consensus, all 30 allies have to agree to make such a decision, and the main focus, the top priority now among the allies and partners is to support Ukraine. They need more support. They need continued support, and that`s the message I conveyed to President Zelenskyy when I spoke to him a few days ago. And that`s the message from \uc2 NATO\uc2  leaders, and, again, that is from the United States, which really makes a difference and matters when we see the war going on in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 , the secretary general of the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, appreciate you coming on and sharing the \uc2 NATO\uc2  perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the momentum has shifted a bit in the midterm races. Our panel is here to discuss it.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We are back.\par \par \uc2 And let`s bring in the panel. Susan Page, Washington bureau chief for "USA Today"; Julio Vaqueiro, anchor of Noticias Telemundo; Stephen Hays, editor of "The Dispatch"; and Symone Sanders-Townsend, host of "Symone" on MSNBC, and the former chief spokesperson for Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 All right. Let`s set the table here.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:01]\par \par \uc2 One thing we`ve seen, Susan Page, that a lot of people said let`s see what happens, which is, there`s going to be a lot of money spent in September, so let`s see where the midterms look at the end of September when Democrats were feeling really good at the beginning.\par \par \uc2 And a bunch of the key races have move. Look with -- Pennsylvania here I think is the first one that we have up. In August, I believe, we had a big lead for Fetterman. It has now shrunk to just four points, according to a Fox News poll.\par \par \uc2 Go to Wisconsin. Our friends at Marquette had Barnes up seven in August. Now, he`s down one in the most recent poll.\par \par \uc2 And even in Georgia, we`ve seen while Raphael Warnock still leads, his lead has narrowed.\par \par \uc2 So, money has mattered, but what`s the bigger story, that Republicans have caught up in some of these races? Or that Democrats haven`t fallen back?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : You know, I think one thing we found abortion issues helped Democrats, now crime is an issue that is increasingly helping Republicans, along with the issue of inflation.\par \par \uc2 And one thing we`ve seen for instance in Wisconsin is that the Democratic candidate there, Mandela Barnes, has been caught up in the same phenomenon that some Republican candidates have found, which is things you said in the past, maybe when you weren`t running for this particular office, have resurfaced and portray as extreme.\par \par \uc2 In that race, Johnson has not improved his favorable ratings.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: He has improved -- he`s made higher Barnes` unfavorable ratings, now more than 40 percent see Barnes as too extreme, just like more than 40 percent see Johnson as too extreme.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Julio, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 JULIO VAQUEIRO, ANCHOR, NOTICIAS TELEMUNDO: So, I think -- historically, we know the party in government battles in the midterm, so we`re looking at these numbers, and the tight race for Democrats to me looks like a good scenario still, although they still need to fight and it`s an uphill battle for them.\par \par \uc2 If they manage to keep this numbers tight, it looks like with the economy as it is and with the inflation as it is, it looks so far so good for them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are you seeing, Steve?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN HAYES, CEO & EDITOR, THE DISPATCH: Well, I think Julio is right. I mean, what`s really notable at this point is the fact that Republicans aren`t doing better, given the historical trends, given this economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, you look at --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, look at the economy, I mean, you`re talking about right track/wrong track of 27 to 65. You`re talking about groceries costing 13 percent more today --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re saying it should be a blowout already.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, it should be a blowout already. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said this in the past few weeks, candidates matter. I think the Republicans have nominated candidates in many of these races that out to have been easier races for Republicans, who appealed to a Trumpy Republican Party in the primaries, but have less appeal across the general electorate, and that`s - - I think that`s a challenge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, Symone, before you chime in here, we put together something, you know, gas prices have gone down, right?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS TOWNSEND, MSNBC HOST, "SYMONE": Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s really helped Democrats. So, guess what? The advertising against on the inflation issue has changed from gas to another liquid. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MANDELA BARNES (D-WI), U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Most senators couldn`t tell you the cost of a gallon of milk.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Just think about the cost of breakfast today. Milk is $4 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A gallon of milk in the last year has gone up so much. Everything costs more.\par \par \uc2 REP. DON BACON (R-NE): While wages are flat, milk is up 60 percent, eggs almost 40 percent, and even bacon is up. And I take that personally.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. That`s \uc2 Don Bacon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, Symone, I mean, part of it -- it`s funny, the gas price issue was so front and center in people`s minds that it did seem to, as it went down, Democratic numbers went up. Certainly, Biden`s numbers went up. So you saw a shift to milk.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: A shift to milk and groceries.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: And folks that probably hadn`t been to the grocery store in a while found themselves in the aisles --\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, I`ve seen a lot of candidates shopping.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: -- looking for their different kinds of brands.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you`re right, they don`t look like they`ve done this very often.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: They have not been there before.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think the issue of gas prices is something that the White House has a competition council and, a gas -- basically, a task force that meets every day. And it`s their job to figure out how to get prices down. Their focus was gas prices for a while. Obviously, the work that they did made a difference. Gas prices have gone down. It`s my understanding that now they are talking about more what is happening at the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 I will say this, though. I think that what I`m seeing in these races is that when you -- if you are a candidate that is being besieged by negative ads, the way to do something about how that is affecting the numbers is to do a targeted look on what is working.\par \par \uc2 And in every race that I have worked, that is what the candidates have had to do. So the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee has to work with these Senate campaigns. The \uc2 DGA\uc2  has to work with these gubernatorial campaigns to figure out what is sticking and then how they can counter that. I have no doubt that Gary -- Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , who is no stranger to this, is doing that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Susan, I want to pivot a little bit to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He has used some -- as always, his rhetoric seems to get more extreme and we get sometimes numb to it. He issued a death threat against -- I don`t know any other way to put it, against \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [09:40:02]\par \par \uc2 I`ll let viewers decide whether this is a death threat to them.\par \par \uc2 Is McConnell approving all these trillions of dollars worth of Democrat- sponsored bills without even the slightest bit of negotiation because he hates \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 ? And then, he has a death wish. Must immediately seek help and advice from his China-loving wife, Coco Chow.\par \par \uc2 So, very racist attack against his wife. It was just extraordinary. And there seems to be a numbness now to this, particularly among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: An attack not against a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: This is against the Capitol`s leading Republican figure. And as you said, the death threat, which the Trump people say was meant in a kind of rhetorical manner, you can`t explain away.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s nothing funnier than death threats.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Yeah, especially in this climate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I didn`t understand their spin. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: In this climate, where many members of Congress are getting a lot of death threats and have to spend money on security, it`s especially not humorous. And the racial slur I think is very hard to explain away.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, Trump is on the ballot this year in any number of these races, in races where he got candidates, the candidates he wanted on the ballot that Democrats are now exploiting their ties to. Maybe on some of these cases, he`ll win. But he is on the ballot as much as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: But he`s on the ballot. I mean, I think your first point is a really important point. He`s not spending his time attacking Democrats, right now? I mean, he`s attacking them in his speeches. He`s criticizing Democrats at his rallies. But he`s not spending money. I mean, Donald Trump`s PACs raised millions --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still isn`t spending money.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: -- $100 million dollars. Where is that money going to Republican candidates? It`s really not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Julio, we`re seeing him still more popular in South Florida than he was four years ago, right?\par \par \uc2 VAQUEIRO: Totally. Yeah. Yeah. It`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 But it`s also interesting how Trump is in the battle, and Democrats know it. And the message from Democratic candidates is also about defending democracy and criticizing Trump. So at the same time, Democrats I think are using him as an asset.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Before I go, Symone, I want to -- the \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2  moment from President Biden. I`m going to play a clip of it and of Karine Jean-Pierre`s response and get you to respond to it. Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Jackie, are you here? Where`s Jackie? I think she was going to be here.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I just explained. She was on top of mind, you know? This wasn`t -- what we were able to witness today and what the president was able to lift up in this -- at this conference, at this event, was how - her focus on wanting to deal with, combat food insecurity in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just to put it, \uc2 Jackie Walorski\uc2  died in a car accident in, I believe it was in August. She was a big part of this hunger -- and so that`s how this moment came about.\par \par \uc2 Symone, how would you have handled that situation?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Look, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  has a very tough job. And I think she goes out there every single day. And a number of people could not do what she does every day.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think it`s very clear that the president had a slip of the tongue, obviously. And I`m sure he is extremely apologetic to the family of the congresswoman if he caused them anymore trauma or agony. No one -- I mean, I think everyone understands that the president unfortunately uniquely understands how the family is feeling, and I`m sure he made that very clear to them when they came to the White House on Friday.\par \par \uc2 I think just to acknowledge it, he had a slip of the tongue. Move on. It`s unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 Again, life is here. He understands and empathized. I think that`s what`s most important here. And I think Karine did try to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: People make mistakes, and you to try to go from there.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Yeah. Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah. It is life. We`ve all done it before.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 All right. Before we go to break, this week on "Meet the Press Reports", the more armed Americans are, the more armed Americans are getting, and the farther the country moves away from agreeing on effective gun safety legislation.\par \par \uc2 My colleague Cal Perry talks to a diverse group of gun owners about the safety measures they want, the ones they fear, and the impact of gun culture on American life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE DOUGLAS, MARYLAND 10TH CAVALRY GUN CLUB: You`re never going to get rid of the guns off the streets. That ship has sailed. It`s too late. There are too many guns out here on the street. And there are too many people that don`t follow the rules. So lift the restrictions on people like me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can watch the full episode of Meet the Press Reports: America`s Gun Culture on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , Roku, \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV, or wherever you get NBC News.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, new numbers from the NBC News Telemundo Poll. How critical will Latinos be in deciding which party controls Congress?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:47:22]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, Latino voters made up about 13 percent of the presidential vote. The bloc though is only growing, meaning these voters could be key to understanding where the nation`s electorate is headed.\par \par \uc2 Our new NBC News Telemundo Poll offers some clues about how the Latino vote both differs from and aligns with the overall American electorate. Republicans do believe they can make some inroads.\par \par \uc2 So let`s take a look. To start, this is where Democrats have an advantage. Latino voters are much younger than the overall electorate. Thirty-eight percent fall between the ages of 18 to 34. Only a quarter of American voters overall are in that age group. Latino voters are less likely to have a college degree than voters in the electorate overall. But it is a gap that has been closing over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 And Latino voters, while they still lean pretty Democratic, this 21-point gap actually isn`t as big as it was in past elections. So there is that going on. And if you dig a little further, you`ll see a split in the Latino vote that is actually all too familiar in the broader electorate. It`s a political divide between urban and rural voters.\par \par \uc2 So even though Latinos prefer a Democratic Congress by 21 points overall, the advantage varies significantly depending on where you live. As you can see here, urban Latinos give Democrats a 28-point advantage. That continues to decrease basically as you get closer to rural America. Suburban voters, the advantage is just 13 points, and it`s down to single digits. This is why Republicans think they can make inroads in South Texas.\par \par \uc2 And then there are demographic divisions that also look similar. On the Supreme Court`s Dobbs decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, urban Latinos disapprove of that decision by 26 points. But again, as you move geographically, just over half of Latinos in suburbs disapprove, and in rural areas that gap was just four points.\par \par \uc2 And then finally, Latino Republicans are actually more likely to identify with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  over the party than the overall GOP electorate. Check this out. Fifty-five percent of Latino Republicans still say they`re a Republican more than they`re a Trump supporter. But 40 percent call themselves a Trump supporter. It`s a 15-point gap. In our most recent poll of Republicans overall, it was a 25-point gap. Fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  makes history as she takes her seat on the Supreme Court. But she joins at a time of growing public distrust in the High Court.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:53:23]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 I want to dig more into our poll, and we did it with my friends here at Telemundo.\par \par \uc2 Julio, a few other nuggets I want to throw out there. Among Latinos, the president`s job rating was at 51 percent.\par \par \uc2 VAQUEIRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s actually not good for a Democratic president. You know, we have a report in Nevada about how the biggest fear Democrats have is not Latinos voting Republican, it`s Latinos voting to not vote.\par \par \uc2 VAQUEIRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is the mood of the Latino electorate?\par \par \uc2 VAQUEIRO: So Latinos are the fastest-growing group of voters. And the numbers are there. Latino voters make up about one out of ten voters. But they are also very unhappy. I mean, the pandemic hit them very hard. The economy is hitting them very hard. Inflation is hitting. They are unhappy about the job quality.\par \par \uc2 And if you see the main issues Latinos care about, they are right there. They`re economy, job quality. So yeah, this is a concern. And this can make things very difficult for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And also, the way Republicans are gaining space in that gap between Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Symone, during the 2020 campaign, a lot of people were sounding this alarm. And whether it was because of the pandemic, did the campaign not respond well enough, or was that a pandemic issue? But it now looks more acute than ever.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: Look, I think it is very important that Democratic candidates -- Latino voters are persuadable. Voters, Black voters, are persuadable voters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But you have to treat them as persuadable voters, yeah.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS-TOWNSEND: And you have to treat them as persuadable voters.\par \par \uc2 And oftentimes, Democratic campaigns do not do that.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:01]\par \par \uc2 They show up late. They show up 37 days before Election Day, drop a couple ads, sprinkle a couple of leaflets, and think that that is enough. And that is not going to get the job done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 How much are Republicans counting on Latinos to get them? I mean, you look at South Texas. You look at Nevada, another state they`re trying to make inroads as the GOP?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah. I mean, I wouldn`t say that Republicans are counting on them, but I think they see an opportunity there. If you look at the gains that Republicans made among Latino voters in 2020, a number of the post-mortem studies of that election show that Republicans did well, did better than expected.\par \par \uc2 And I think if you look at the way that the economy is affecting Latino voters, it`s just like the economy`s affecting lots of voters. And there`s a polling question that we had that showed that 54 percent of Latinos say that they`re falling behind faster than the cost of living. That`s a problem for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Susan, I want to put up the -- what Latinos` views on the Supreme Court decision, on Roe v. Wade, because what`s been interesting, and what you see here, is that it looks like the electorate overall, the approval for it is only 37 percent. That`s the electorate overall. Disapproval, a little lower, but not that much lower.\par \par \uc2 But what`s interesting here, Democrats, for instance, are not talking about Dobbs in South Texas in those congressional races. It`s the one place. It is a slightly more conservative, religious conservative community.\par \par \uc2 PAGE: And that`s always been an entree for Republicans toward Hispanic voters, because they are culturally more conservative.\par \par \uc2 And one thing I think we`ve seen is where Hispanic voters used to be able to count on them for Democrats because of their ethnicity as an alliance with the party that has represented minorities. Now, I think they`re increasingly willing to vote their class, to vote the fact that they tend to be voters who do not have a college education, who are working in jobs that have been hard hit. And that`s I think account for some of the appeal, especially among Latino men who are drawn to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VAQUEIRO: Yeah, I think that`s something important that this polls shows off, is just how diverse the community is. And we talk a lot about that, but I don`t think it`s always understood.\par \par \uc2 Latinos are just like any other ethnic group. It`s important where they live. It`s important their age, where they come from, how they got to this country. And that makes the Latino vote such a diverse vote. There`s no one Latino vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Julio, I`m curious. The decision by Governor DeSantis to take Venezuelan migrants to Martha`s Vineyard, how did that play in South Florida with the Venezuelan community?\par \par \uc2 VAQUEIRO: Well, I mean, we will have to wait and see November 8th. But I don`t think it was -- they didn`t receive it properly. Because this has been a party has been condemning governments in Nicaragua and Venezuela and Cuba, and yet you see these immigrants coming in, asking for asylum, and they are just turning their backs to them. So we will wait and see for the reaction on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it certainly seemed like a riskier decision --\par \par \uc2 VAQUEIRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- the way South Florida politics works.\par \par \uc2 Really appreciate you guys today.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, "Meet the Press" was honored with an Emmy Award this week for Outstanding News Discussion and Analysis. More than a year after protests erupted across the country following the killing of George Floyd, our special broadcast was focused on schools, America, and race.\par \par \uc2 We examined the battle over critical race theory and how children should learn about America`s troubled racial history. We`re proud of the entire "Meet the Press" team, the "Meet the Press Reports" team, where this story first started. And it is an honor for all of us. So thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 That`s all for today. Thanks for watching. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press".\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020221002eia200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc94}{\*\bkmkend toc94}\b \uc2 Katie Couric`s Message On The Importance Of Those Yearly Mammograms As She Reveals Her Own Breast Cancer Diagnosis\b0\par\par\uc2 Craig Melvin, Hoda Kotb, Savannah Guthrie, Sheinelle Jones \par \uc2 1,360 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [07:41:32]\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN: We are back. It is 7:41. We`re back with In-Depth Today. And this morning, an important story that`s really close to our hearts.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB: Yes, every two minutes, a woman in this country is diagnosed with breast cancer and yesterday, our friend and our former colleague Katie Couric revealed she is now one of them.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE: And of course Katie has been on the frontlines of cancer awareness for decades, she lost her husband to cancer. And she`s using her story now to urge all women to get checked and to advocate for themselves. Sheinelle is here with more on that. Hi, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. As she battles breast cancer, Katie is turning her story into a cautionary tale in hopes of saving lives. She wants to educate women not only on the importance of screening but also about something called dense breast tissue. It affects nearly half of women in this country including Katie. And doctors say knowing you have it and how to ask for proper screenings could be lifesaving.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Former today anchor Katie Couric has revealed that for the past several months, she has been privately battling cancer.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Thank you all for your support.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): In an essay titled "Why Not Me?" the 65-year-old journalist says she was stunned by a stage one breast cancer diagnosis in June after missing her annual mammogram. I`m normally vigilant, bordering on neurotic, about taking care of my health.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: I want to make sure that you all don`t put off your mammograms.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Katie is among nearly half of women over 40 in the U.S. who have dense breast tissue, which makes it more difficult to detect abnormalities on a mammogram and also increases your risk of breast cancer. Armed with this knowledge, her mammogram was followed by additional screening using an ultrasound, doctors biopsied a mass.\par \par \uc2 The next day, Katie received the shocking results. It`s cancer. You`re going to be fine, but we need to make a plan. I felt sick and the room started to spin. What does this mean? What will the next weeks, months, even years look like? This isn`t the first time cancer has touched her life. Katie has long been an advocate for cancer awareness and screenings ever since her husband, Jay, was diagnosed with colon cancer and died of the disease at the age of 42.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Words of course will never describe how devastating this loss has been.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): She famously underwent an on air colonoscopy on Today to encourage others to get checked. As a result, screenings went up by 20 percent, some dubbing it the Couric effect.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: I have a pretty little colon.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): She also lost her sister, Emily, to pancreatic cancer and 2001. The deaths of her loved ones prompting Katie to co found Stand Up to Cancer.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: And I just realized I had to do something about this.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): But with no family history of breast cancer, her own diagnosis came as a particular shock. Katie`s surgeon Dr. Lisa Newman.\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN, KATIE COURIC`S SURGEON: More than three quarters of the breast cancers that we diagnose occur in women that do not have any established risk factors for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Katie`s cancer was caught early. She underwent a lumpectomy along with radiation and says she`ll need to take medication for the next five years.\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: It is wonderful that Katie has gone public with her experience and early detection of breast cancers will save millions of lives.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Now as she`s done before, Katie is determined to shine a light once again, on the importance of screening for cancer. Please get your annual mammogram. I was six months late this time. I shudder to think what might have happened if I had put it off longer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:45:08]\par \par \uc2 JONES: Just six months late, here`s what we know for sure. When we do stories like this, our hope is that a woman watching or maybe a family member, that we give you a little nudge to go ahead and get that checkup. That`s what`s most important.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Six months is nothing, I mean especially with COVID and people not going in for a checkups.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, and you know, you kind of think, when I feel something --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I did that. She thought she did it. She`s like, oh, it`s been that long.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Really, for a mammogram or any of those screening, I actually made an appointment yesterday for a yearly physical I need. And it`s like because you remember, like, oh, yes, you know what, I`m going to do it. I`m not going to put it off another day.\par \par \uc2 JONES: These kinds of stories save lives.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re going to hear from Katie too, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, she`s going to join us on Monday. It`ll be her first live interview since sharing her diagnosis. It`ll be good to see Katie and give her a hug. And obviously she`s got a really important message to share.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We look forward to that. We`re going to get another check of the weather from Bill Karins. He`s in for Al. And some people have been wondering during the storm, where is Al, Al happen to test positive for COVID. We`re happy to say he`s feeling fine, just mild symptoms. He`s home.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: He`s been sending us notes like he`s, you know, he wants to be here, he feels well. I can only imagine a step count in his house just like going back before, just like, you know, it`s like, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Disengage.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: But Al has help us build an incredible weather team here at NBC News. And, you know, we`re getting you through this storm. And Al we`ll see you back here very soon. So concerns for today, the one thing I`m going to be watching is the storm surge. We have a storm surge warning still in effect for areas of northeast Florida. We`re not done yet. Irma really surprised us with the storm surge in the Jacksonville area.\par \par \uc2 And here`s what we`re going to watch, the St. Johns River is one of the few rivers in our country that actually flows from south to north so that water that`s now falling in Orlando is going to trickle northwards up through the river at the same time. We`re going to see the storm surge coming in this afternoon at high tide about noon. This is all going to meet near Jacksonville. So we`re going to watch that area closely for the possibility of the four to six feet of storm surge. Right now they`ve already got a surge of about two feet. That`s look at your national weather. Now here`s a look at the weather outside your window.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 KARINS: And that`s your Thursday forecast.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right. Thank you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 Up next, he did it. Homerun history overnight, and we`re going to hear from Yankee`s superstar Aaron Judge after belting that 61st of the season to officially join baseball royalty. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020221004ei9t0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc95}{\*\bkmkend toc95}\b \uc2 Today for September 29, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Savannah Guthrie, Hoda Kotb, Sam Brock, Bill Karins, Lester Holt,  Blayne Alexander, Craig Melvin, Tom Costello, Kerry Sanders, Sheinelle  Jones, Jacob Soboroff, Tom Llamas \par \uc2 7,724 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Today\par \uc2 TODA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:00:16]\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hi everybody. It`s Thursday. Millions in Florida waking up to catastrophic damage from Ian.\par \par \uc2 HODA KOTB, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, the full impact being revealed as the sunrises this morning. It is September 29th. This is Today.\par \par \uc2 Path of destruction, massive flooding and power outages as Ian barrels across Florida crashing ashore as one of the strongest hurricanes to ever hit the state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All the sudden I felt something and then heard like a train coming right to the house.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Nearly 3 million people in the dark, the devastating storm surge leaving entire neighborhoods underwater, homes and cars floating down streets. This hospital swapped from above and below, losing part of its roof, its ground floor flooded, crews still carrying out emergency rescues on land and at sea. We`re live across the state with complete coverage and what millions need to know as the threat moves up the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 Get out, the urgent security alert for all Americans in Russia to leave the country immediately. And the new warning that anyone there with dual citizenship couldn`t be drafted into the Ukraine war, we`ll have the very latest.\par \par \uc2 Breast cancer battle, well wishes pouring in for Katie Couric this morning after she revealed her diagnosis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC, AMERICAN JOURNALIST: Thank you all for your support.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Just ahead, her decision to speak out and what she wants all women to know.\par \par \uc2 Those stories, plus, historic blast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Judge keep the left field. This could be it. See you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yankee`s superstar Aaron Judge crushes his record tying 61st homerun of the season, cementing his place among baseball royalty. Today, Thursday, September 29th, 2022.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From NBC News, this is Today with Savannah Guthrie and Hoda Kotb live from Studio 1A in Rockefeller Plaza.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Good Thursday morning. So happy you`re joining us today. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. The sun is coming up in Florida and we`re getting a closer look now at the widespread devastation left behind by Ian.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, there are two major concerns right now there, the flooding of course and the power outages. Nearly 3 million people across the state woke up this morning and could not turn the lights on, no electricity.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Ian made landfall yesterday as a major category four packing 150 mile an hour winds and causing massive storm surges across Florida`s Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: So right now, Ian is making its way east toward the eastern side of Florida. We have a live look at some of the conditions going on. This is Orlando. And while the storm did weakened overnight, it still remains a serious threat.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, it`s a rain story there. And then once Ian heads away from Florida, it is expected to then curve back toward the Georgia, South Carolina coastlines impacting millions across the South in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, we got it all covered for you this morning. We`re going to start off with NBC`s Sam Brock. He is in Venice, Florida. That`s just north of where Ian made landfall. Hi, Sam, what`s the assessment this morning?\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Hoda, Savannah, good morning. It is terrifying to think what we`re going to see in the light of day. I was just talking to a police officer here in Venice. They resume search and rescue operations at 2 o`clock this morning looking for critically injured people. But he said look, I got to warn you, block to block, there`s dead wire, there`s live wire. As you look behind me right now, you`ll see splintered signs and power lines down all through here. Now when East Naples guys, there were 30 rescue missions yesterday as the urgent efforts right now are underway to find those imperiled by Ian.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): It`s devastation that was predicted with the grim reality of this storm`s warped power defies imagination. Ian now with tropical storm hit the coast as a fierce hurricane shredding homes apart and Placida, Florida, tearing off roofs and splintering trees. While in the Tampa area, extremely causing this tree to crash through a home.\par \par \uc2 In one of the hardest hit areas, Fort Myers, there were storm surges nearly seven feet high, nearly double the previous record more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): The massive surge of water leaving entire communities completely underwater.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you want me to come out?\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): Roads looking more like the ocean for block after block. Ian packed catastrophic 150 mile an hour winds knocking out power to more than 2 million customers.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We have seen life threatening storm surge as was predicted. We`ve also seen major flooding, but that`s going to rank as one of the top five hurricanes to ever hit the Florida peninsula.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): This team of emergency responders unable to respond, a massive wall of water swamping their fire truck.\par \par \uc2 [07:05:06]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Now we have a truck issue and the guys are pushing the truck out of the bay.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): In Port Charlotte, hospital staff working through the storm to protect patients. Other first responders struggling to help those in need, unable to start rescues until after life threatening conditions subsided. Despite repeated warnings to leave, some people did not or could not evacuate. They found themselves stranded by a 12 foot storm surge. Mark and Elizabeth living in an apartment in Venice Island, Florida but at the last minute, grab their air mattresses and hunker down in this Sarasota office when her boss offered it to them.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Here you are with air mattresses in an office space. Your home is 50 or 100 miles away. But you consider yourself lucky.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Is that because you`re saved from the storm?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, yes, because somebody helped us.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): Others stayed behind in the flood zone, trying to help those who were unable to leave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I want to help the people in here because they`re good people here in paradise.\par \par \uc2 BROCK (voice-over): This morning, the destruction left behind by this massive blow from Mother Nature is just starting to come into focus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Well, hats off to those first responders, all those helpers out there, Sam. There were some viral images in Tampa that were just shocking. It was actually Tampa Bay, and it looked dry. It looked like you could walk on it. People were taking video and pictures of it. What`s the after effects there?\par \par \uc2 BROCK: Yes, so here`s the deal, Hoda, you`re seeing all this water being really sucked out. It`s called negative storm surge. And the reason behind it, you have counterclockwise winds and hurricanes. So on the northern edge of the eye wall, the water was being pulled out of Tampa Bay, into the Gulf of Mexico. The problem with this, Hoda, is the fact that it can then refill when wind directionality change. And it can happen in a matter of minutes. So if you see this, it might be tempting to go out there on the sand and check it out. Don`t do it for your own safety. Hoda?\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Sam Brock for us there in Venice, Sam, thanks.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes. These conditions can change in a split second, the water has finally stopped rising in Fort Myers. But this morning, the impact of the catastrophic flooding there is being felt across the city. And joining us now Fort Myers Mayor Kevin Anderson. Mr. Mayor, good morning. We`re glad to have you on the phone with us this morning. Have you been able to assess damage? I know it`s still early there. What`s your sense of how bad it was?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KEVIN ANDERSON, FORT MYERS, FL: We haven`t had a full assessment obviously with the sun just coming up, but it is pretty bad. I`m standing on a floating concrete dock. I`m down in the River District of downtown. The problem is the dock is in the middle of an intersection, a block, two blocks away from the water. This is about a piece of concrete that weighs a ton. And it was moved this far away. It shows you how powerful the waters were last night.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I know one of the things, that`s most concerning in a moment like this is what happened to the people who decided to stay or who were left behind. Have you guys been able to conduct rescues? Do you know how many people are still out there and in need of your help?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: We would estimate several dozen people who stayed behind were in need of some assistance. Our teams have been working on that. I`m happy to report at this time. I`ve been not -- I haven`t been told of any loss of life in the city. Now keep in mind, we have a metropolitan area and there may be some outside, but inside the city there`s been no reports to my knowledge.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Fort Myers more or less took a direct hit, Mr. Mayor, what`s your sense of how this compares to other storms that Fort Myers has experienced?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: I came here in the mid 70s. I was on the police department for almost 25 years, so a lot of storms here. This is by far the worst storm I have ever witnessed. They`re watching the water from my condo in the heart of downtown, watching that water rise and just flat out all the stories on the first floor. It was heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Boy, for -- Mayor for you who`s been there all those years and seen so many storms come and go to take a direct hit. So what is the plan now, like walk us through what will happen this morning and the days ahead?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: So the first thing we need to do is make sure the roads are passable. And they`re safe. We do have a curfew in place for next 48 hours until we get a handle on everything. Once we get the roads cleared, you know, \uc2 FPL\uc2  will start working on the electricity. Our teams will be out there going through the neighborhoods trying to make sure people are safe, getting assistance, anyone who needs it. We`ll start working on getting the other utilities up and functioning getting cleaned up. We`ve gotten some great support pledge by the President and the governor. So I think I`m optimistic it`s going to be a long recovery but nonetheless we`re going to recover because Floridians are very resilient.\par \par \uc2 [07:10:13]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: They are indeed. Mr. Mayor, thank you so much. Kevin Anderson of Fort Myers, you know, it`s going to be a busy day, so we thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, let`s --\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Thank you. Let`s turn now to Bill Karins. He`s been tracking the path of destruction where it`s heading and what it`s left behind, hey.\par \par \uc2 BILL KARINS, NBC NEWS METEOROLOGIST: Yes, good morning. So we`ve been showing you where all the worst has been as far as the storm surge went, that was the Fort Myers area, Bonita Springs all the way down to Naples. Overnight, we`ve still had a lot of heavy rain. Our NBC affiliate in Orlando and meteorologists were saying in Kissimmee, there`s been houses with homes with one to two feet of water in them. The little McIver River in Seminole County located right about here is one foot over record flood stage.\par \par \uc2 So again, there`s still problems and still water going into homes right now because of this situation. And look at Daytona Beach. A wind gust of 69 miles per hour, so this is down to a tropical storm, but it is not a pleasant morning in Volusia County, Flagler County heading up there to Jacksonville. That`s where the storm surge is going to be coming in during the day today. So even though it`s a weekend storm, there still could be some significant problems.\par \par \uc2 And then it may actually intensify a little bit on its way up tomorrow in between Savannah and Charleston here to South Carolina, we may actually get landfall that either a strong tropical storm or as a hurricane tomorrow afternoon. And of course, we`re going to get heavy rain in this area, winds could gust possibly up to 70 miles per hour. And that way we could be dealing with some more power outages, guys, in this area too. And storm surge problems, which we`ll explain more about during the show today.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Bill, thank you. And as we`ve come on the air President Biden has officially declared the state of Florida, a major disaster. So that opens up funding and a lot of help hopefully today. NBC Nightly News anchor Lester Holt rode out the storm overnight in Bradenton just south of Tampa Bay. Homes there flooded, the area`s airport badly damage too. Lester, good morning, the view from where you sit.\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Well, Savannah, I went out to with my trusty flashlight a little while ago surveying the immediate area around us. There appears to be no major damage here. We spoke when heard from the mayor of Sarasota a while ago. He was venturing outside his home for the first time, saw plenty of damage. He`s not aware of anything catastrophic. But certainly during the night, we can hear this thing howling, ultimately dying down now. The rain has stopped and we`re feeling relative breezes after what we saw and went through yesterday. But we`re all OK.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, difficult night for a lot of people. Lester, a lot of people spent the night in shelters. I`m sure they have tons of concerns about their homes. What are you hearing from them?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Yes, we went to a shelter a few hours before the eye wall came ashore and talked to people who made that choice. They`d heard the predictions. A lot of them thought it was a no brainer, immediately went into shelters. We talked to officials there who were very concerned that while they did have a number of people, they didn`t have nearly as many as they expected. And that`s why there`s a bit of trepidation this morning as the sun comes up here.\par \par \uc2 You heard from one of the other mayors the sense of what they`re going to find now in the light of day in some of these neighborhoods. One couple of we talked to said they lived in a trailer home, very worried obviously about their neighborhood, the vulnerability. They decided to get out but so many of their neighbors did not. So that will be the big focus today, trying to get to those folks who rode it out and make sure they`re OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, Lester, thank you. And of course, we`ll see you tonight on nightly news from Florida. I mean, bears (ph) repeating this is in the top five of strongest storms ever to hit the U.S.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, that`s an incredible statistic. And as Bill mentioned, the worst of Ian now passing through Central Florida, seeing some heavy rain, strong winds, hitting Orlando, Blayne Alexander, is there, Blayne, good morning, what are you seeing?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Hoda