e rain at this same speed and the same intensity of wind until at least noon if not later, guys.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, Blayne, thank you. I mean, hats off to that WESH reporter for helping that nurse. But hats off to that nurse who was getting in her car and driving to work to help people, so, wow.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Incredible sight.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: -- puts her on his back, I mean just incredible.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Wow.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: Thanks for helping the helper.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes. All right, let`s welcome in Craig.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG MELVIN, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hey, Hoda, Savannah, good morning. Good morning to you as well. Obviously the storm has brought air travel across much of Florida to a total standstill. This morning, that is having a ripple effect for passengers all over the country. NBC`s Tom Costello is at Reagan National Airport with that part of the story. Tom, good morning.\par \par \uc2 TOM COSTELLO, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Craig. I just checked 2,000 flight cancelations right now nationwide, but the vast majority are flights going into or out of Florida as you would expect. And as Ian continues to move up the east coast, we expect flight cancelations and airports to close in Georgia, South Carolina, North Carolina, as the storm tracks north.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Nothing brings travel to a standstill like a hurricane. In a time lapse, Flight Radar 24 capturing the mostly empty skies over Florida with only a handful of commercial flights staying out of harm`s way hugging the east coast into and out of Miami. The \uc2 FAA\uc2  status map showing closed runways and airports from Key West to Naples, Sarasota to Tampa to Orlando and Melbourne and Daytona Beach, leaving behind the few travelers who didn`t get out in time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In here you want to go over to the middle of the food court area because the airport is closing now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And we`re not set up as a shelter.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): In Orlando, Brittany and Paul Inglesby (ph) and their baby unable to get home to Rochester.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY INGLESBY (ph), TRAVELER: If you have a little kid with you, he takes up priority. So when he starts running low on stuff, that`s when it gets kind of terrifying.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): Tampa International tweeting video of the last flight out before closing down, Wednesday, leaving behind some British tourists who were unable to catch an early flight home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Had a lovely holiday. But then we were evacuated from the beach area. So we`re here at least until Friday. All the hotels are full.\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO (voice-over): On Wednesday more than 2,100 flights canceled nationwide with most airlines waiving change or cancelation fees at least through Friday. This morning, Tampa International remains empty, managers plan to assess the condition of the runways and terminal then set a reopening timeline. CEO \uc2 Joe Lopano\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (on camera): I know you`ve got to do a full evaluation today of the condition of the airport on the runways. How soon do you think you might open?\par \par \uc2 JOE LOPANO\uc2 , CEO, TAMPA INTERNATIONAL AIRPORT: There`s a lot that has to take place once we assess the airfield. So we think the earliest we could open is Friday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 COSTELLO: OK, so that`s Tampa, keep in mind, Tampa was really spared in the storm. We`re watching Orlando closely to see how they are impacted by the incredible amounts of water. I just checked by the way, Jacksonville technically is open despite that storm. But about 90 percent of flights canceled so just because technically they`re open nothing is going into Jacksonville. Atlanta is also experiencing flight cancelations, Charlotte too. Guys, back 