c2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Coming up, our What Works Today series focusing on good stories across the country.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re going to shed a light this morning on a little known national initiative giving women a chance, a better chance to raise healthy children who are ready to succeed. We`re going to hear from one new mom who says it actually saved her life after local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:26:20]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [07:30:29]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please tell me you are recording. You were?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, just what a moment. That was in Jacksonville overnight. And you can see a local reporter was right there when the winds so intense cause that shopping center signed to come crashing down. She was getting ready to go live. They got the shot, but it really demonstrates the power of this storm.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: That`s exactly right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: You know, one of the things that`s always striking is when storms like this hit as you talk about all the time, the helpers really show up in mass this time even before Ian made landfall, people have stepped up to help.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, and this morning, we are seeing these remarkable stories play out that just neighbors helping neighbors, NBC`s Kerry Sanders is right there in hard hit Punta Gorda. Hey, Kerry, good morning.\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, good morning. You know over my shoulder here you can see the incredible power of Hurricane Ian. But now comes as we`ve already seen the selfless power of people.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Slamming into Florida overnight, Hurricane Ian left communities like Sanibel Island, underwater, damaged, and in the dark. Florida`s Gulf coastline bearing the brunt of the storm, the wind and rain so dangerous and Punta Gorda, I had to grab onto the street sign just to stay on my feet. Cape Coral also severely impacted by Ian, making it difficult for emergency crews to reach those who did not evacuate.\par \par \uc2 ROB HERNANDEZ, CITY MANAGER FOR CAPE CORAL, FL: The minute that the winds subside to below 45 miles an hour, our crews are going to be responding out there, clearing the roads to make sure that our first responders can get to those individuals that are trapped in their homes and in their vehicles.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Through all of the uncertainty --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re going to keep moving in all the affected areas.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): Neighbors, friends, even complete strangers, doing whatever they can to help. Rescues on shore and on land, some even crossing state lines to pitch in.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We had all these power workers, these line workers from all these different states. I saw them from Texas, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (voice-over): New York`s National Guard sending two heavy lift helicopters and a contingent of soldiers to assist with rescue efforts, the Coast Guard assembling a small armada of ships for offshore rescue missions.\par \par \uc2 In hard hit Naples, firefighters helping a woman escaped floodwaters. But the fire department also thanking local jet skiers for helping out writing, sometimes we need a ride to the rescue ourselves. In Bonita Beach, one good Samaritan braving the elements to save a cat trapped outside on an air conditioner. In St. Petersburg, staff at a botanical gardens hunkering down with flamingos to see them through the storm.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: As we`re standing here on this street, just a short distance from where I am, you can see from this drone video as the light is beginning to show, we can take a look at some of the damage in the neighborhood here. This is just off U.S. 41 along a street called Easy Street. So this has not been easy for anybody and the proof of that is to show those who work through the storm. This is at one of the local hospitals here in Charlotte County.\par \par \uc2 This is a doctor wading through the water that filled portions of the hallways there and then you can see in this other image at the fossil hospital what it was really like, I think the message there head on a swivel. Guys, there`s going to be a lot of efforts with cleaning up but really it is the first daylight for people to assess how bad this was and where the worst damage is.\par \par \uc2 And of course, whether those who are out there volunteers, neighbors, et cetera whether they can safely help those who need the help right now on top of what the emergency officials are doing, guys.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, Kerry, thank you. That drone footage I think was the first time we`ve actually seen what a neighborhood looks like after the storm went through, roofs ripped off, real difficult times for folks there.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And we do think about those first responders, they have families too but here they are showing up to the hospital to the firehouse and doing what needs to be done. Speaking of, we`ve got Chief Pete DiMaria, he`s the head of the Naples Fire Department with us. And Chief, we know you have had a long 24 hours. First of all, you know as we have been saying the sun is just coming up. But are you able to assess the damage the situation there and Naples?\par \par \uc2 CHIEF PETE DIMARIA, NAPLES, FL FIRE DEPARTMENT: We were actually out last night doing assessment to see how bad it was and we still have a lot of floodwaters on the streets, a lot of horticultural debris down and a lot of vehicles and boats that need to be tended to. There still all over the streets right now. And so we