et`s get us over there and we`ll do our best to get there as quick as possible.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, good message to end on. Help them -- help, you guys get out there and doing that hard work. You need all the help you can get from the community too. Naples Fire Chief Pete DiMaria, thank you very much for your time this morning, sir.\par \par \uc2 DIMARIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, Naples is one of the most beautiful places on earth. I used to live there. And when you look at it like that, and you think of that fire chief and those guys just trying to save their city, it`s really heroic efforts.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And the water come rushing in, you know what, I`m sure there will be a lot of people who want to get out there, you want to see OK, what happened but they`re saying sit tight, let us do our jobs.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, indeed.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: When we come back this morning, we`re going to have everything that you need to know ahead of what could be a very bad flu season. We got the newest guidance on shots to protect your family. And what about that COVID booster, should you get it at the same time? It`s a question a lot of folks are asking.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, but first, Katie Couric has got a message on the importance of those yearly mammograms as our friend reveals her own breast cancer diagnosis, a very personal story she`s now sharing right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:41:32]\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We are back. It is 7:41. We`re back with In-Depth Today. And this morning, an important story that`s really close to our hearts.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Yes, every two minutes, a woman in this country is diagnosed with breast cancer and yesterday, our friend and our former colleague Katie Couric revealed she is now one of them.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: And of course Katie has been on the frontlines of cancer awareness for decades, she lost her husband to cancer. And she`s using her story now to urge all women to get checked and to advocate for themselves. Sheinelle is here with more on that. Hi, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SHEINELLE JONES, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Good morning. As she battles breast cancer, Katie is turning her story into a cautionary tale in hopes of saving lives. She wants to educate women not only on the importance of screening but also about something called dense breast tissue. It affects nearly half of women in this country including Katie. And doctors say knowing you have it and how to ask for proper screenings could be lifesaving.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Former today anchor Katie Couric has revealed that for the past several months, she has been privately battling cancer.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Thank you all for your support.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): In an essay titled "Why Not Me?" the 65-year-old journalist says she was stunned by a stage one breast cancer diagnosis in June after missing her annual mammogram. I`m normally vigilant, bordering on neurotic, about taking care of my health.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: I want to make sure that you all don`t put off your mammograms.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Katie is among nearly half of women over 40 in the U.S. who have dense breast tissue, which makes it more difficult to detect abnormalities on a mammogram and also increases your risk of breast cancer. Armed with this knowledge, her mammogram was followed by additional screening using an ultrasound, doctors biopsied a mass.\par \par \uc2 The next day, Katie received the shocking results. It`s cancer. You`re going to be fine, but we need to make a plan. I felt sick and the room started to spin. What does this mean? What will the next weeks, months, even years look like? This isn`t the first time cancer has touched her life. Katie has long been an advocate for cancer awareness and screenings ever since her husband, Jay, was diagnosed with colon cancer and died of the disease at the age of 42.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: Words of course will never describe how devastating this loss has been.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): She famously underwent an on air colonoscopy on Today to encourage others to get checked. As a result, screenings went up by 20 percent, some dubbing it the Couric effect.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: I have a pretty little colon.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): She also lost her sister, Emily, to pancreatic cancer and 2001. The deaths of her loved ones prompting Katie to co found Stand Up to Cancer.\par \par \uc2 COURIC: And I just realized I had to do something about this.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): But with no family history of breast cancer, her own diagnosis came as a particular shock. Katie`s surgeon Dr. Lisa Newman.\par \par \uc2 DR. LISA NEWMAN, KATIE COURIC`S SURGEON: More than three quarters of the breast cancers that we diagnose occur in women that do not have any established risk factors for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Katie`s cancer was caught early. She underwent a lumpectomy along with radiation and says she`ll need to take medication for the next five years.\par \par \uc2 NEWMAN: It is wonderful that Katie has gone public with her experience and early detection of breast cancers will save millions of lives.\par \par \uc2 JONES (voice-over): Now as she`s done before, Katie is determined to shine a light once again, on the importance of screening for cancer. Please get your annual mammogram. I was six months late this time. I shudder to think what might have happened if I had put it off longer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [07:45:08]\par \par \uc2 JONES: Just six months late, here`s what we know for sure. When we do stories like this, our hope is that a woman watching or maybe a family member, that we give you a little nudge to go ahead and get that checkup. That`s what`s most important.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: Six months is nothing, I mean especially with COVID and people not going in for a checkups.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, and you know, you kind of think, when I feel something --\par \par \uc2 KOTB: I did that. She thought she did it. She`s like, oh, it`s been that long.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Really, for a mammogram or any of those screening, I actually made an appointment yesterday for a yearly physical I need. And it`s like because you remember, like, oh, yes, you know what, I`m going to do it. I`m not going to put it off another day.\par \par \uc2 JONES: These kinds of stories save lives.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: We`re going to hear from Katie too, right?\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes, she`s going to join us on Monday. It`ll be her first live interview since sharing her diagnosis. It`ll be good to see Katie and give her a hug. And obviously she`s got a really important message to share.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: We look forward to that. We`re going to get another check of the weather from Bill Karins. He`s in for Al. And some people have been wondering during the storm, where is Al, Al happen to test positive for COVID. We`re happy to say he`s feeling fine, just mild symptoms. He`s home.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: He`s been sending us notes like he`s, you know, he wants to be here, he feels well. I can only imagine a step count in his house just like going back before, just like, you know, it`s like, yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Disengage.\par \par \uc2 KARINS: But Al has help us build an incredible weather team here at NBC News. And, you know, we`re getting you through this storm. And Al we`ll see you back here very soon. So concerns for today, the one thing I`m going to be watching is the storm surge. We have a storm surge warning still in effect for areas of northeast Florida. We`re not done yet. Irma really surprised us with the storm surge in the Jacksonville area.\par \par \uc2 And here`s what we`re going to watch, the St. Johns River is one of the few rivers in our country that actually flows from south to north so that water that`s now falling in Orlando is going to trickle northwards up through the river at the same time. We`re going to see the storm surge coming in this afternoon at high tide about noon. This is all going to meet near Jacksonville. So we`re going to watch that area closely for the possibility of the four to six feet of storm surge. Right now they`ve already got a surge of about two feet. That`s look at your national weather. Now here`s a look at the weather outside your window.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 KARINS: And that`s your Thursday forecast.\par \par \uc2 MELVIN: All right. Thank you, Bill.\par \par \uc2 Up next, he did it. Homerun history overnight, and we`re going to hear from Yankee`s superstar Aaron Judge after belting that 61st of the season to officially join baseball royalty. But first, these messages.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:50:25]\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: All right, in Today`s Can`t Miss, and gosh, finally, you know I watched a couple of the games this weekend. We wanted that 61st home run for Aaron Judge and he got it.\par \par \uc2 JACOB SOBOROFF, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: The big night wasn`t ever a big night. Aaron Judge crushed his 61st home running match the American League record and another Yankee icon for the most in a single season. Tom Llamas is here with more on the miles done. Hey, Tom.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: Hey guys, good morning to you. He`s the biggest thing in baseball. And last night Aaron Judge reached legend status. How`s this for a coincidence, it`s been 61 years since an American League player has hit 61 homers in a single season. And this morning, you won`t believe what happened to that homerun ball and how much it`s now worth.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And a three-two. Judge keep to left field. This could be it. See you.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (voice-over): With that blast, Aaron Judge rounding the bases and entering the history books, equaling Roger Maris`s American League record, 61 homeruns in a single season, a mark set more than six decades ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s been chasing history and now he makes it.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (voice-over): Judge crushing a sinker and sending a long drive into the left field seats against the \uc2 Toronto Blue Jays\uc2 . His mother, Patty, watching with Roger Maris Jr. celebrating the moment in the stands together.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And they are united in history around the magical mystical number 61.\par \par \uc2 AARON JUDGE, BASEBALL OUTFIELDER: I`m getting a chance to be, you know, associated with, you know, one of the Yankee greats, you know, one of the baseballs greats, you know, be enshrined with him forever is worse can describe it.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (voice-over): One unlucky fan, just barely missing his chance to take a priceless piece of baseball history home. The towering outfielder number 99, under so much pressure to reach the milestone, showing how he thrives under New York`s bright spotlight.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): The fans make you a better player?\par \par \uc2 JUDGE: Oh, definitely. So this place is packed out, they`re loud, they`re screaming, they expect you to win.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (voice-over): But winning not batting titles Judge told me is what matters.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE: So many of us are getting caught up in numbers as, you know, the minute you`re not a team player anymore, and I try to focus on being a team player.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (voice-over): His numbers eye popping, Judge, on pace to win the elusive triple crown, leading the league in batting average RBIs and homeruns. Only three players have ever hit more homers in a single season, all of them tainted by allege steroid use.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Number 60.\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS (voice-over): Judge tide the legendary Babe Ruth with 60 on September 20th, only to see the world watch every pitch fans chewing his every swing. Now with this homer, that pressure lifted, Judge etching his name into baseball glory.\par \par \uc2 JUDGE: Well, there was a lot of emotions, you know, it took me a little longer than I wanted to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: So beautiful to watch that with seven games left in the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  regular season, there`s a decent chance Judge could surpass Maris and also win that coveted triple crown. Without a doubt, we`re watching one of the greatest hitters having one of the greatest seasons of all time. By the way, in case you`re wondering about what happened to that ball, look at this, Judge got it back, gifted it to his number one fan, his mom, Patty, real quick, the fan who dropped it, the \uc2 Blue Jays\uc2  grabbed it, they sort of pass it around they finally gave it to the \uc2 Yankees\uc2 . It`s worth somewhere between 200,000 to $2 million.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: But that`s mom`s got it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LLAMAS: She deserves it.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KOTB: All right, coming up, we`ll be back after your local news.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [07:56:09]\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TODA000020220929ei9t000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc96}{\*\bkmkend toc96}\b \uc2 Meet The Press for September 18, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Peter Baker \par \uc2 9,657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST MEET THE PRESS: This Sunday Poll Vault.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : People are back to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP0\par \par \uc2 TODD: Our new NBC News Poll shows a jump in President Biden`s approval ratings.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We`re making progress. We`re getting places done as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And signs that Democrat standing may be improving ahead of November. One explanation the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We`re moving backwards. We`re taking one step forward, three steps back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Another the high profile presence of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We may have to do it again. Stay tuned everybody. Stay tuned.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: My guest this morning, the number two Democrat in the Senate, Dick Durbin of Illinois and Republican Senate candidate Joe O`Dea running to flip a Colorado seat. Plus, the Senate will look into charges by a former U.S. attorney that Mr. Trump tried to use the Justice Department to punish his enemies and protect his friends.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEOFFREY BERMAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Pissing off the president is not a Federal crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Geoffrey Berman\uc2  joins me this morning. Also, border wars amid a surge at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Some of them have been through, really horrific things.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ships four dozen unsuspecting Venezuelan migrants from Texas to Martha`s Vineyard in Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Every community in America should be sharing in the burdens. It shouldn`t all fall on a handful of red states.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: (Inaudible) is so cruel, so depraved that someone would do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me for inside and analysis are New York Times Chief White House Correspondent Peter Baker, former Obama White House Senior Advisor Stephanie Cutter, Republican Strategist Al Cardenas and Leanne Caldwell of the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . Welcome to Sunday. It`s Meet the Press.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest running show in television history this is Meet the Press with Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Good Sunday morning. President Biden is finally getting his polling bump. A spade of news from anger over the overturning of Roe v. Wade to falling gas prices, has helped push the president`s poll numbers to their highest point in almost a year, and it`s thanks mostly to Democrats coming home. In fact, among all registered voters Mr. Biden`s job approval in our new NBC News Poll is sitting at 45 percent, 52 disapproving. The big jump really was from Democrats, particularly younger ones, happy about the passage of the agenda, perhaps student loans as well, but those are hardly robust numbers and they`re a jump from last months 42-55 split.\par \par \uc2 And the race for control of Congress is dead even, 46 percent for Democrats, 46 percent for Republicans. The remarkable thing about Mr. Biden`s numbers is how he even manages to get to 45 percent. On issue after issue, his approval ratings are actually consistently below 45 on foreign policy, it`s just 42 percent. The economy even lower 40, border lower than that 36 and on cost of living even lower at a paltry 30 percent. So what`s happening? One is, of course, the Dobbs decision. It`s clearly energizing voters and not just Democrats. Only 37 percent approve of the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe. Sixty-one percent disapprove of that decision, but just as important to President Biden is getting a lift from simply Donald Trump`s presence.\par \par \uc2 The high-profile investigations, all of it, is preventing Republicans from turning the mid-terms into a referendum on Mr. Biden, and he loves to say judging by the alternative not the almighty and he may be benefiting from that right now. Voter feelings towards Mr. Trump are just 34 percent positive, 54 percent negative, 20 points underwater. It`s lowest point in a year and a half. Compare that to President Biden`s current personal ratings, 42 percent positive, 47 percent negative. Not great, but that`s only five points underwater. So where are we?\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is this, less than two months from the mid-terms each political party is running the campaign they want to run. Republicans on crime, economy and immigration and they`re winning with that battle with the voters. Democrats are running on Mr. Trump and abortion rights and they`re winning that battle with the voters. So, what`s going to happen? Joining me now is the number two Democrat in the Senate Dick Durbin of Illinois. Senator Durbin, welcome back to Meet the Press.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Good to be with you Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So I`m going to start with the -- the stunt I guess is the best way to call it that Governor DeSantis did when trying to fund the flight of migrants in Texas, that he claimed might have been coming to Florida. He - - you know and sent them to Massachusetts. Here was his rational for it and then I want to ask you more on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We are not a sanctuary state and it`s better to be able to go to a sanctuary jurisdiction.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:00]\par \par \uc2 And yes, we will help facilitate that transport for you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, do you think these bus, whether it`s these bus tickets to the Naval Observatory, New York, D.C. or Martha`s Vineyard, do you think the Federal government should stop them and prevent them from happening?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well I can tell you this. I`ve been to the Salvation Army Rescue Center in Chicago and I met with the people that came off the buses. I sat down with one of the families and they told me the story. It is pathetic that these governors are taking advantage of these helpless people, making promises to them to get on the bus and life is just going to be fine, and off they go to places far removed from where they`re supposed to be appearing under the requirements of our law in a matter of weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 So he`s jeopardizing their stay in the United States when they make -- each governor makes this decision, and the second part I want to raise is this Chuck. Why is it when the Republicans want to enforce their immigration theories? It`s always the kids that end up being the victims. We saw it with kids in cages. We saw it with a forcible removal of children from their parents, some who`ve never been reunited with their families and now once again, it`s the kids and families that are put on these buses and transported for political purposes across the United States.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe laws were broken here by Governor DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I don`t know if the laws were specifically broken but it broken -- they have broken every basic standard of decency when it comes to dealing with children and families. These people are at the moment legally in the United States. Some of them, one family I met, it`s been five months coming up from Venezuela, literally walking to our border. They went through every possible outrage that you could imagine from theft, stealing their cellphones, pushing them into a jungle for a period of time with this man didn`t think he could survive, and they finally made it to the United States only to be exploited by the governor of Texas in -- in their case. I just don`t think -- when you get down to the basic rules of politics, this passes the smell test.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Senator, let`s talk about the actual issue at the border. We`re getting upwards an average, I think, 8,000 folks a day. We`re already over 2 million for the year. Right now, I do not believe the Federal government has the authority that ICE or DHS has the funding authority to, you know, help move -- move these folks to other parts of the country. Do you think they should have that authority?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: We put a dramatic investment into our -- our border operations and President Biden has called for that and we have supported it on a bipartisan basis. But I want to tell you the bottom line and it`s a clich\u8218\'20\'1A about our broken immigration system is the truth, unless and until we come together in a bipartisan fashion in the Senate particularly, and once again entertain the comprehensive immigration bill we are just inviting trouble. Across my state of Illinois and virtually every other state, there is a desperate need for workers. Our birth rate is not keeping up with our demands for workers and -- and every walk of -- of life that you can imagine, we need more people.\par \par \uc2 And there are those who want to serve this country, but we need an orderly process at the border --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: To do that we need Congress to do its part on a bipartisan basis, Republicans complain about the situation and it -- it needs to be changed dramatically, but they won`t engage in helping us reach a -- a -- any kind of solution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play for you what the vice-president told me about her take on the border and get your comments. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VICE-PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: The border is secure but we also have a broken immigration system, in particular over the last four years before we came in and it needs to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to have 2 million people cross this border for the first time ever. You`re confident this border is secure.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We have a secure border in that is a priority for any nation including ours and our administration, but there are still a lot of problems that we are trying to fix given the deterioration that happened over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know Senator, I -- I understand your comment about the Republicans here and I think there`s a lot of evidence and many of them want -- would prefer the issue for the -- for the ballot box than a solution. But Democrats don`t look like their anxious to do something, you`re an exception. You`re -- you`re one of the few people that wants to talk about this issue and try to solve it, but you know there`s a lot of Democrats that would prefer to ignore it and hope that, hey, we`ll -- we`ll deal with it, maybe, at another time. How do you fix that mindset?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: There`s political danger, don`t get me wrong but we faced that eight years ago when we set down with our gang of eight with \uc2 John McCain\uc2 , myself and Schumer and even Senator Graham and came up with a comprehensive plan that was embraced by business and labor and groups across the board. We passed it in the Senate Chuck, you`ll remember that. Fourteen Republicans voted for it.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:00]\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Five of them are still around today and then sent it over to the House where it died unfortunately. The Republican leadership refused to take it up. It can be done. We can make the border safe and have a system of legal entry into the United States to work, put -- put these people on the books, have them pay taxes, make sure we`ve done a background check. All of these things can be done. Are they controversial? You bet. Some of them are very controversial but we know we need to do it. The United States is a nation of immigrants. I`m proud to be a son of an --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: -- immigrant who came to this country but -- but I will tell you, if we`re going to do it in this era we can`t wait another 30 years to get around to the solution.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to pivot to a couple other things that you -- that are under your umbrella as Senate Judiciary Chair in particular. If Democrats hold the Senate but Republicans take the House, are you prepared as Chairman of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , they essentially take over the January 6th investigation?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well I can tell you this. This is a remarkable investigation. I know they`re going to have at least one more hearing before the end of the year. I don`t know if they`ve concluded their remarks, concluded their investigation say or -- or more has to be done, but that was a seminal event in American history. I was there and many members of both chambers were there when this insurrection, this mob stormed the Capital.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Five people died, 149 law enforcement were injured and sadly most Republicans, the overwhelmingly majority of Republicans in Congress want to ignore it, not even ask the question. It has to be asked and it has to be part of our history. So I want the January 6th Committee to do its work in the House and if there`s more work to be done and I`m in a position to call for it, you bet I will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say during the lame duck, if there`s -- if the Republicans don`t want to continue the January 6th but the committee, you`re prepared to essentially take over the investigation if that`s what`s necessary to get, you know, to protect whatever materials could be -- could be threatened if this investigation isn`t over. Because it`s my understanding, they`re not going to finish by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: But there`s one key difference, in the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , in order to issue a subpoena you need bipartisan agreement to do it. That`s not the case I understand in the House.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: So, if -- if the Republicans want to resist this it`s going to be difficult to continue the investigation. I hope the House gets it done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Finally, really quickly, on same-sex marriage, are you all concerned that if Republicans win the Senate that in the lame duck, you won`t get the votes to codify same-sex marriage?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I am worried about that but my north star in this issue is \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: She has led the bipartisan effort and she believes this is the right way to deal with it. I defer to her, but this is a serious issue just like the Dobbs decision. \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  made it clear that he`s going to move toward Oberfeld the Supreme Court case related to gay rights in this country. We better take him seriously.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator Dick Durbin, number two in Senate leadership, also Chairman of the Judiciary Committee. Thanks for coming on, sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thanks Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: One reason Democrats are hopeful about holding the Senate is because Republican primary voters have nominated numerous candidates in Trump`s image, who just may be too controversial to win over swing voters. Democrats had hoped for the same result in Colorado. They even funded a Trump-like candidate in the primary but a more moderate Republican in Joe O`Dea won. O`Dea is trying to make the case that he`s no extremist.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: He`s a different kind of Republican too. My dad supports a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 JOE O`DEA, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE FOR COLORADO: For the first five months, that should be a woman`s decision between her and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And Joe O`Dea who will take on two-term Senator Michael Bennett joins me now. Mr. O`Dea, welcome to Meet the Press.\par \par \uc2 JOE O`DEA, REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE FOR COLORADO: Chuck, thanks for having me on today. I really appreciate it. My wife loves your show.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well thank you for saying that. Let me start with what you said about five months. I get it from five months. So let me ask about the other four months, who decides?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Look, I -- I -- I believe after the five month period that there should be some exceptions, rape, incest, medical necessity and that decision should also be a woman`s decision with her doctor. But I don`t believe in late-term abortion on demand.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can you write a law though that deals with that? Because it -- it seems that a lot of people are realizing, I think a lot of people are uncomfortable with late-term abortion and there really isn`t -- people don`t seem to do it by choice. The only time you really see it is when it`s a medical emergency. Does it make it that you really can`t write a law?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Well look, I think Senator Collins is on the right track. She`s got some traction. I think that bill could pass. There`s a couple things I`d like to see in it. Parental notification is something that we have in Colorado. I think that would be good to add to that bill.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It -- let me ask you this. You have said you weren`t -- there was one bill that you didn`t like, the -- the one abortion right`s bill that -- that Joe Manchin voted against and then there`s this one you pointed out was Senator Collins. Given where you are in this issue, are you more likely to vote for something that -- that it protects abortion rights, even if it doesn`t have everything you want?\par \par \uc2 [09:15:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Look, I -- I think the five -- the first five months are critical. That should be a woman`s decision with her doctor and after that exceptions, rape, incest, medical necessity. Those should be, again, between a woman and her doctor and I`m going to vote that way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious. You voted for a ballot initiative that didn`t have those exceptions. As Dobbs been overturned, have you found yourself thinking about this issue a little bit differently?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Well look, it`s the center of attention in -- in a lot of cases and -- and I`m exactly where I was when I started this campaign, haven`t changed. I didn`t write that bill, but I believe that, you know, it -- it should be a woman`s right in the first five months.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me play -- play an add that`s very positive for you. Let me play an ad that Senator Bennett is running against you. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: Joe O`Dea is the perfect candidate according to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . McConnell`s all in on his support for O`Dea. Joe O`Dea, perfect for McConnell, wrong for Colorado.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now put McConnell`s quote in context. He said that you were perfect for, sort of, the politics of Colorado. That`s how he was describing you but let me ask you this. Where you differ with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Well look, I -- I believe that the abortion issue, he and I are different on that. He`s -- he`s very prolife, I`ll buck the party. I`m not afraid to move forward. I`m disappointed with my opponent, Senator Bennett. He`s trying to distort the issue for Coloradans so they don`t get the truth and I`ve been consistent with my message through that period of time, and -- and I`m not going to change. I -- I -- I`m an independent thinker. I am who I am and you get what you get and that`s -- that`s how I`ll vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well it`s interesting, I -- I was looking up in immigration. You believe there should be citizenship for DREAMERS.\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: I do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you want to see border security and the -- the -- that compromise, we`re farther apart from that. Are you comfortable bucking your party on DREAMERS if it, you know, if it means you`ve had Republican primary voters upset with you?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Look, I -- I think the Democrats have -- have lost touch with this issue. Just like they`ve lost touch with thousands of \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, like they`ve lost touch with, you know, this debt transfer for tuition. Working Americans don`t want to pay for that and they`ve also lost touch with our border. Our border needs to be secure. Colorado is number two in fentanyl overdoses right now and that -- that fentanyl`s coming strait up I-25. We lost 1,800 Coloradans last year to drug overdose and -- and I think Democrats have lost touch. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  saying that the border is secure. We had 2 million people cross the border this last year. It`s not secure and they`re killing our kids. That`s what`s going on. Cartels coming right into our towns.\par \par \uc2 TODD: During a debate earlier this summer, and this was before we saw many of the January 6th hearings. You did not say -- you said \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not solely responsible for January 6th. Have you changed your mind on that after seeing the hearings over the summer?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Look, I -- I think our former president could have done a lot more to stop that, three and a half hours to say enough`s enough. That`s wrong. I -- I think January 6th was a black-eye on our country. Anybody that, you know, that did some violence or -- or hurt some people, they should be held accountable and I believe that and we need to move our country forward.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that. Here`s what former President Trump wants to do with the January 6th insurrectionists. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We`ll be looking very, very seriously at full pardons because we can`t let that happen. What`s happened here and I mean full pardons with an apology to -- to many an apology.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you hear that, because again, you seem to want to give him some -- some deference to what happened on January 6th, but when you hear that, how do you not hold him responsible for January 6th?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Look, I disagree with our former president. I -- I wouldn`t give full pardons to anybody that`s violent. We need to hold those people accountable. That should never happen again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you, like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  says, all Republican Senate candidates should want a rally with him because it`s the only way Republicans could win. I assume you don`t want Mr. Trump rallying in Colorado?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Look, I`m the only Senate candidate for the Republican party that hasn`t been endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , probably not going to send me a Christmas card. I don`t want to see him run again. I don`t want to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run again. I think that tears our county apart and I think I`m where most American`s are. We need to move this country forward. There`s some great Republican candidates out there and I`m going to help campaign for them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you -- one of the candidates you`ve promoted is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Are you comfortable with the idea of using migrants as a political tool?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Well look, I -- I -- I think -- I think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and -- and Governor Abbott were right to bring some visibility to this issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Was this the right way to do it though?\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: We`ve got fentanyl killing our kids. People call what he did cruel. You know what`s cruel, is ignoring this issue. Democrats are ignoring it, doing nothing while our kids are dying.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think it`s -- there`s no evidence Republicans are interested in-- in solving this problem in the Senate. You`ve seen it and you`ve -- you`ve criticized it. No interest here. So why is this only on the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 [09:20:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Well that`s why I`m running. I need to get in there so we can get a bipartisan bill together that solves this issue. We need to get the DREAMERS citizenship. We need to fix our immigration system and we dang sure need to close this border down and make sure that it`s secure. It`s a national security issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think though the problem we have at the border was, sort of, what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  kinked the hose? And when you do that, this is what you`re going to get. I mean, I guess I`m trying to figure out how this is all on the Democrats when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did nothing to -- to solve the border.\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Well look, the -- the -- the answer`s in the numbers. The numbers are up to 200,000-250,000 crossing our border each and every month. That didn`t happen under Donald Trump`s watch. It`s happened under Joe Biden`s watch.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Joe O`Dea, the Republican nominee for Senate in Colorado. Appreciate you coming on, sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 O`DEA: Thanks a lot for having me Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Stay safe on the trail. When we come back, the former U.S. attorney who says he was fired because he refused to use the office to protect Mr. Trump`s friends and punish his enemies. \uc2 Geoffrey Berman\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It has been an active week involving the investigations of former President Trump. On Friday the Justice Department asked an appeals court to give it access to roughly 100 classified documents seized from Mar-A-Lago and not send them to a Special Master. Earlier, Justice sent out roughly 40 subpoenas in a separate investigation of Mr. Trump`s efforts involving -- the efforts to overturn the election and around January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And then the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  says it`s going to investigate new allegations that the former president tried to use the U.S. attorney`s office in New York to protect his friends and punish his enemies and that last charge, that came in a new book. It`s called "Holding the Line" by \uc2 Geoffrey Berman\uc2 . He was fired by Mr. Trump as a U.S. attorney and not just in any district, it was the southern district of New York. He says it was for refusing to politicize the office. Well \uc2 Geoffrey Berman\uc2  joins me now. Mr. Berman, welcome to Meet the Press.\par \par \uc2 GEOFFREY BERMAN\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So let me start with this, you allege a lot of political interference both by the former Attorney General Barr, as well as the former president here. How confident are you that laws were not broken in their attempt to -- to quiet you?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Well, what -- what I focus on is the violation of the cardinal principle of the Department of Justice, which is that partisan political concerns should not enter into any decision making. And what I -- I wrote the book because I want people to understand the full scope of the improper and outrageous political interference by the Trump Justice Department in the cases of the southern district of New York. The book demonstrates what Trump is capable of and what he`s likely to do, and it provides a front line view of just how vulnerable our justice system is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well I wanted you to outline that because I`ve got play something that the former president said last night at a rally in Youngstown about the Justice Department. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 [09:25:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And now the unprecedented weaponization of the Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to break into and raid the home of a former president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You volunteered for the Trump campaign in 2016. You`re not just some, sort of, you know, never-Trumper, anti-Trumper, however people want to classify various Republicans. How -- what`s your reaction to that quote?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Well, it`s really quite outrageous. Trump is trying to portray himself, somehow, as the victim of a politicized Department of Justice, and I think my book shows the irony of that. I mean, it was under Trump and under Barr that the politicization of the Department of Justice actually occurred. There`s no evidence supporting that he was victimized but my book details in granular level, the -- the evidence that the Department of Justice politicized and tried to politicize the cases of the southern district of New York.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, there`s a -- a whole slew we could go through. I want to single out one because it`s probably the easiest one to describe in a short interview and that`s \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 . Here`s the former secretary of State. President Trump was mad about, cause he was trying to stop President Trump from pulling out of the Iran Nuclear Agreement, and suddenly based on a couple of tweets you get a referral to investigation \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 . Explain that.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: It was extraordinary. So President Trump attacks \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  in two tweets, saying that \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  engaged in possible illegal conversations with Iranian officials over the Iran Nuclear Deal.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: The very next day, the Trump Justice Department refers the \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  criminal case to the southern district of New York.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What case it existed based on this tweet?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Based -- based on the tweets. So two tweets from the president and the \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  criminal case is now a priority for the Department of Justice and the statute they wanted us to use was enacted in 1799 and had never been successfully prosecuted. So about 220 years on the books and there were no convictions, and so we investigated \uc2 John Kerry\uc2  was entirely innocent and yet the Justice Department continued to push us and push us and push us. And when I declined, Attorney General Barr did not take no for an answer. He transferred the case to another district and thankfully that district didn`t indict either.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It -- it -- the -- the various instances where you show this interference are pretty damming. One of them had to do with the case involving Stormy Daniels and \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  is upset because he believes you should have come out sooner. I`m going to put up a quote here about you. He said, "he had an obligation to come forward to Congress, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  or law enforcement to disclose, not release the information years later in a book. Berman`s actions are at the bear minimum unethical and quite possibly illegal." You did go before the House Judiciary Committee in 2020 before the campaign. Do you think you should have sounded the alarm louder or stronger?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: I -- I -- I sounded the alarm very loudly when-- when Attorney General Barr tried to fire me in June of 2020. I was about as noisy as I could be. I sent a press release to the entire country saying that the chief law enforcement officer of the country, Barr, lied when he said I was stepping down. And I used the language of the obstruction of justice statute, letting the country know what Barr was trying to do and how he had crossed the line, and then in my testimony before Congress I reiterated there would be that -- that -- imposing an outsider as Barr was trying to do. An outsider he trusted in charge of our cases an ongoing investigation were disrupted and delayed those investigations. So I was very upfront with the danger that was going on.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If Donald Trump`s elected president again what happens to the Justice Department in your view?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Well I shutter to think about it. The prospect of a second Trump Administration frightens me. All of the dangerous and outrageous things he did as president will be repeated if there`s a second administration, except this time he`s going to be more successful in doing that. And remember, when he was president he always knew he had to stand for re- election. If there`s a second administration, he`ll be free of those concerns.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Bill Barr, do you believe he committed any crimes in trying to obstruct your ability to do your job at SDNY?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Bill Barr wrote a book of 600 pages and in that book, he didn`t mention any of the improper and outrageous political interferences with our office. He never mentioned \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [09:30:05]\par \par \uc2 He never mentioned Greg Craig. He never mentioned Cohen. He never mentioned \uc2 Halkbank\uc2 . He never mentioned my firing.\par \par \uc2 It was a complete whitewash of what went on at the Justice Department under his leadership. And we are now beginning to take that whitewash away.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to play something \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said yesterday at Ellis Island and get you to respond to it. Here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2 , U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: The rule of law means that the law treats each of us alike. There is not one rule for friends, another for foes; one rule for the powerful, another for the powerless; one rule for the rich, another for the poor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: What kind of confidence do you have in \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  as attorney general?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Oh, I think what he`s done and said so far, I have a great deal of confidence in him.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that -- you know, he recently took an appeal regarding the Mar-a-Lago search. And there is an urgency to what the Justice Department is pursuing here. And I understand it entirely. And it stems from the revelation that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and those around \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  are being investigated not just for the mishandling of classified information, but for obstruction of the subpoena requiring the production of that classified information.\par \par \uc2 That is an extremely serious charge. If that were in the Southern District, it would have our highest priority, and we would be moving very quickly on it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, very quickly, everybody`s been wondering if indictments are coming, is this something we should expect in months, not years, or weeks, not months, or months, not years?\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Well, you know, there is the 60-day rule --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: -- at the Department of Justice --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would this qualify for it, in your mind?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The 60-day rule with Trump being -- he`s not on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Oh, yes. But you`re not -- it counsels against taking any action - -\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: -- that could impact an election.\par \par \uc2 And I think this would qualify for that. And so, you know, I think that counsels, you know, that, you know, I don`t think anything dramatic --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: -- should be expected prior to the election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. The book is "Holding the Line." \uc2 Geoffrey Berman\uc2 , a former U.S. Attorney, Southern District of New York. Like I said, not just any district, the Southern District of New York.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Berman, thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective.\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  ships migrants off to Massachusetts. He says the point is blue states should share the burden of immigration at the border. But is it simply a troll for his 2024 campaign?\par \par \uc2 Panel is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:35:17]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 Panel is here. I`ve got Leigh Ann Caldwell with "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 "; New York Times Chief White House Correspondent Peter Baker, who`s written a new book with his wife, Susan Glasser. It`s called "The Divider: Trump in the White House, 2017-2021" -- I don`t know if they want to write a volume two; Republican strategist Al Cardenas; and former Obama White House Senior Advisor Stephanie Cutter.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, all. Congrats on the book. We`ll get to that in a minute.\par \par \uc2 But Al, I want to start with you. You and I are South Floridians. And the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  move, I -- the first thing I thought of is, he`s shipping Venezuelans where? And he`s not -- you tell me the politics of this in Florida for DeSantis. This has to be complicated, no?\par \par \uc2 AL CARDENAS, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: It is complicated. And, frankly, it`s putting at play three or four state legislative seats, maybe one congressional seat, that are fairly close at this point, where all of these people live, all of these --\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Venezuelans?\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: -- the Democrat -- Venezuelans live.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t understand the timing of it. And, of course, I don`t understand why you`d do that.\par \par \uc2 If I was DeSantis, I`d fire those who put it together. He gave the greenlight to this project, but it could have been worse. People who were already qualified for asylum application, people who were -- they had the right to be, people who were lied to, people who were taken to Martha`s Vineyard, and people who weren`t in Florida, using Florida taxpayer dollars.\par \par \uc2 And so I didn`t understand any of it. And then when I saw the small children, it just broke my heart. I just didn`t understand the why of it, politically or personally.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Stephanie, it`s, I think, easy to condemn what Governor DeSantis did. I think the question for the administration is -- and I`ve talked to some folks at DHS who are, like, there is a concern that the administration just doesn`t want to deal with this right now because of the politics of it.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE CUTTER, FORMER ADVISER TO PRESIDENT OBAMA: Well, I -- you know, I`m not in there, so --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know that.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: -- I don`t know what the discussions are. But I think that they do want to deal with it. And they have been dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 You know, the president put in his budget this year record funding for the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  to fund more border agents, to make sure that these people can be processed faster. They`ve put in a system that is more sane and efficient than what we were dealing with under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Republicans, you know, I heard you say this earlier, like to campaign on immigration. But they don`t like to do anything about it. And, you know, the -- plenty of Republicans voted against that funding, including senators from Florida and Texas, the two states that are shipping --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: -- immigrants across the country.\par \par \uc2 So I do think they are dealing with it. It is a tough problem to solve.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: And it`s a tough problem to solve not only because of the substance of it. You know, if people are qualifying for political asylum, that`s what our country is built on. But it`s also a tough problem to solve politically.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, by the way, we have help wanted signs all over this, you know? We have a huge magnet, number one. And number two, there is huge -- this migration issue is challenging. Haiti is falling apart. Cuba is still in bad shape, Venezuela. You know, talk to Colombia about how many Venezuelans they have.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a timing question I have, Leigh Ann. And I believe it was when -- was it Tuesday or Wednesday, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  comes out with his 15-week abortion ban, and all of a sudden, the next day, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  gets that off the front page. I`m not saying they`re connected, but boy, it seemed convenient timing.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH ANN CALDWELL, EARLY 202 CO-AUTHOR, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, I think there`s a lot of things about timing with this decision by \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I mean, you previewed 2024. But I think 2022 is definitely a reason as well, even though DeSantis could be running for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have decided that immigration is one of the top three issues that vote -- that the base voters care about this midterm elections --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re right about the Republican base, by the way. Our poll shows that they`re right. I mean, immigration fires them up.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yeah, absolutely. And so, one of the reasons that Senate -- the Senate decided to punt on another culture issue, the same sex marriage, is in large part because they think that it`ll do better after the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have decided that they, even though that could play well in independent, suburban states and districts, they need their base to turn out in these midterm elections. And that is what they are saying is most important right now. TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Look, Chuck, the truth of the matter is that Stephanie`s right about this or that, but having the present issue, a few tweets doesn`t handle the issue. The border issue is number one on the voters` minds that Republicans have brought up. And the White House has done very little politically about it.\par \par \uc2 Why not, excuse me, in lieu of Congress`s inaction, why not appoint a committee? \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2 , \uc2 Michael Chertoff\uc2 , others --\par \par \uc2 [09:40:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Sure. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: -- to say, I`m going to appoint these people to give me recommendations on border security, and run with that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: He`s very passive on an issue that`s hurting his party a lot.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Peter, I`ve gotten a few people wondering if we`re just getting played by DeSantis. He`s pulling a Trump on us, right? We`re sitting here. This is not the center of attention, but boy, he changed the subject and we all went to it.\par \par \uc2 It`s like, it`s hard to -- you can`t -- we know it`s a political stunt. We know it`s for media attention, but these are human beings. We couldn`t ignore it.\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Yeah. Yeah. I mean, you could say, obviously, he`s getting criticized for exploiting people for a political purpose, but he`s got us talking about it. He`s got us talking about it. And the point that he`s making is, you know, a rational one to talk about, what level of responsibility do states that are not at the border have to help out?\par \par \uc2 Fair enough. Obviously, to do it in this way is not actually solving the problem. But look, this is the playbook we`ve seen now the last three election cycles. It worked for Republicans as far as they were concerned with Trump in 20 -- I`m sorry, 2016.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It didn`t work in 2018 when he tried it with the caravans, right? In the midterms last time, he tried to make immigration a big issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire McCaskill would disagree. I mean --\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It obviously worked in some places.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say -- BAKER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- it does work in red areas.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Right, exactly.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: But it -- but what it also did, it might get the Republican base all riled up, but it also has the effect, just like many other issues that we`re talking about today, of driving independent voters --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: -- particularly independent women, to Democrats. People are ashamed about what DeSantis did.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, this gets to though -- because DeSantis is clearly trying to borrow from Trump.\par \par \uc2 And I want to -- I want to do one excerpt from your book here, Peter. And it`s \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , who probably is the best symbol of sort of the folks that are trapped by Trump. He says: He`s a lying MF-er, Graham said of Trump, with a what-can-you-do shrug, but also a lot of fun to hang out with. He could kill 50 people on our side, and it wouldn`t matter, Graham said. Their reasoning was simple. He was the president from their party.\par \par \uc2 This Trump -- I mean, Trump wants to pardon January 6th. The fact that there aren`t more Republicans trying to distance themselves from Trump is astonishing to me. I look at our own poll and I can`t believe it.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It is remarkable here, 18, 20 months after he left office, after the insurrection of January 6th, that he is still obviously so dominant.\par \par \uc2 But I was struck by one thing in your poll that was interesting. For the first time since you guys have been asking the question, you asked the question of are you more -- of Republicans --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Are you more a supporter of Trump or more a supporter of the Republican Party? Thirty-three percent more --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s put up that graphic, guys. It`s unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: More for Trump than for the Republican Party. That`s the lowest number since you guys have been asking that question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: So there is, I think, some disconcerting feeling --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: -- for a lot of Republicans about whether they really want to be involved with him or not.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: I noticed that in that poll --\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Except that in primaries, the --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, that`s the problem --\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: -- numbers are --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That 33 percent is more than 50 percent --\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- in a primary electorate and that`s the issue.\par \par \uc2 Very quick, and I`ve got to wrap.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: You`re starting to see that in these --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: -- general election campaigns. People are distancing themselves from Trump, even the MAGA-ers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh. Our New Hampshire friend is probably the most 180.\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We don`t have time for that quote, but we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, when we come back, Democrats own the cities and Republicans own rural America. But the most persuadable voters are concentrated in the outer suburbs -- call them the exurbs -- and they may well determine the outcome of this year`s midterms. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:46:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Data Download time.\par \par \uc2 In every election, there are solid Democratic voters, solid Republican voters. And then there are voters who defy easy categorization, those persuadable voters. Sometimes we call them "soccer mom" or "office-park dads".\par \par \uc2 But this time, we`re going to talk about them geographically. They`re often the difference between winning and losing. And our new NBC News poll has some clues about who they are, and most importantly, where they live.\par \par \uc2 So we want to do this geographically. Persuadable voters, we define that as people that have voted both Republican and Democrat at least once in the last four years. And as you could see where they live, nearly half of them, a plurality, live in what we call the outer suburbs or exurbs.\par \par \uc2 You could see combined, the number of persuadable voters in cities or those close in urban suburbs basically collectively add up to what the exurbs have. And you could see in rural America, not too many persuadable voters. So what are the places where these persuadable voters could impact the battle in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 Well, in House races, you`ve got Arizona`s first Congressional district. It`s a huge, the eleventh largest Congressional district in the country. It`s parts of Phoenix, and north.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got Colorado 08. It starts in the suburbs of Denver, goes all the way up to Fort Collins.\par \par \uc2 North Carolina 13. It`s part of the research triangle and farther out, tipping some rural parts of that area.\par \par \uc2 Michigan 07 goes from the western suburbs of Detroit, all the way to Lansing.\par \par \uc2 And then you`ve got here in Minnesota 02, which is St. Paul, and it goes nearly all the way down to the southern border.\par \par \uc2 It`s this mix of districts. So why are they persuadable? Well, because they sort of fall on both sides of some issues.\par \par \uc2 A potential Democratic edge with these voters? 51 percent want Trump investigations to continue. 54 percent of these folks disapprove of the Dobbs decision.\par \par \uc2 But there`s some potential that they`re also Republican voters. 53 percent of them favor a GOP-controlled Congress. And 63 percent of them disapprove of Biden`s handling of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Look, we`re always in search of who`s this year`s swing voter? Well, here`s what I can tell you. I don`t know who they are. I can tell you where they live. They live in exurban America.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the growth of Christian nationalism in the United States and the pastor who says it`s time to break down that wall between church and state. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [11:21:58]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 We`ve launched our fifth season of "Meet the Press Reports". It`s our half- hour weekly show that does a deep dive on one issue per episode.\par \par \uc2 This week, we look at the rise of theocracy in the United States. It includes the growing belief by some on the far right that there is not, and never has been, an actual wall of separation separating between church and state.\par \par \uc2 We focus on Moscow, Idaho. It`s a small college town near the border with Washington state, where a pastor named Doug Wilson of Christ Church is embracing a form of Christian nationalism.\par \par \uc2 Pastor Wilson told NBC News Correspondent Anne Thompson that he`s fighting secularism and what he calls cold civil war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANNE THOMPSON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So in your version of a Christian town, would there be a place for non-believers?\par \par \uc2 DOUG WILSON, PASTOR: Oh, absolutely. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Would there be a place for same sex couples?\par \par \uc2 WILSON: Well, you mean legally?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: You mean like marriage?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: No. No marriage. But there`d be same sex couples--\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON: No marriage, even though it`s the law of the land in the United States?\par \par \uc2 WILSON: Just like Roe used to be, right?\par \par \uc2 THOMPSON (voice-over): Reverend Dr. Elizabeth Stevens leads the Unitarian Universalist Church of the Palouse.\par \par \uc2 REVEREND DR. ELIZABETH STEVENS, UNITARIAN UNIVERSALIS CHURCH OF THE PALOUSE: In the public square, I don`t see them representing Christianity. I don`t see them representing the values that I find in the Bible. I see them representing patriarchy. I see them fighting the culture war.\par \par \uc2 WILSON: You can`t have a naval warfare without ships. And you can`t have tank warfare without tanks. And as I tell Christians all around the country, you can`t have culture war unless you have a culture.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can see the full episode of "Meet the Press Reports" on Peacock, \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , Roku or anywhere you get NBC News videos. New episodes first air on NBC News Now on Thursdays at 10:30 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 So let me bring the panel back. Peter, this is not sort of disaggregated from the world of Trump. In many ways, it`s fused with Trumpism. In fact, let me put a mash together of some candidates that Trump has endorsed who are all sort of preaching this, there`s no wall between church and state.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LAUREN BOEBERT\uc2  (R-CO): And I`m tired of this separation of church and state junk.\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R), PENNSYLVANIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: In November, we`re going to take our state back. My God, we`ll make it so. We will.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): We need to be the party of nationalism. And I`m a Christian and I say it proudly. We should be Christian nationalists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Now, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has embraced this. Evangelicals embraced Trump. It was sort of a grand bargain, right? They seemed to get what they want, and it has fused into something else here.\par \par \uc2 In your reporting on Trump, how does he view this sort of Christian nationalism that is attaching itself to Trumpism?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, he`s very opportunistic, of course. Obviously, it`s not saying anything surprising to say that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not exactly the most religious person in the world. Certainly his values are not particularly Christian.\par \par \uc2 But he recognized in 2016 something important, which is these Christian conservatives were important to his victory, right? That was something therefore he needed to pay attention to.\par \par \uc2 [09:55:01]\par \par \uc2 He once told in fact, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , and you played that clip from him earlier.\par \par \uc2 He once told \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  about these pastors who called him to tell him they were praying for him in the middle of impeachment. He says, those F`ing Christians just love me.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he`s very opportunistic and kind of cynical about it, but he recognizes that it`s important to his political success. And they have recognized it`s the important -- that his success is important to them because he gave them a lot of what they wanted, in terms of judges, in terms of policies. He was the most overtly anti-abortion president and arguably the one most responsible for the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, look, some of this is reactionary, Leigh Ann. I want to put up this statistic.\par \par \uc2 Are you a member of a church, synagogue, or mosque? Membership over the decades. So in 1940, 73 percent of Americans were a member of a church, synagogue, or a mosque.\par \par \uc2 Look where we are in 2020. We`re down to 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, it does -- how much of this rise of Christian nationalism is almost sort of like a reaction to the fact that we`re becoming more secular as a country?\par \par \uc2 CALDWELL: Yeah, that`s a huge problem for some of these people. They don`t want that to happen. They don`t like these trends.\par \par \uc2 But they are also a very powerful minority in this country. I mean, you look at the abortion movement. They have -- they organized 50 years ago after Roe v. Wade was decided, and they got the result that they wanted.\par \par \uc2 And so, there is some political belief among this group that they can continue to move the country, even though they are in a minority, in the direction that they want.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Stephanie, there`s like -- we`ve noticed in our show for the most part there is one voting group in the Democratic coalition that does want to see, you know, religious texts sometimes be fused with public politics, and that`s churchgoing African Americans in particular. But Democrats in general are becoming more secular as a party.\par \par \uc2 Do you ever worry about that sort of uncomfortable partnership there?\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: Uncomfortable partnership with?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Just on religious stuff.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: No, I don`t worry about it. You`ve got, you know, a Catholic president who very much believed in people having their own beliefs. And, you know, there are plenty of opportunities for Democratic politicians or electeds to show their faith.\par \par \uc2 Look at what`s happening in Pennsylvania with Josh Shapiro. He`s Jewish, but he is not ceding churches to Mastriano, who is very much a Christian nationalist. And he`s going and he`s very comfortable preaching from the pulpit about why he`s running for office, how his faith informs what he believes politically. And that`s exactly what Democrats need to keep doing because we should not cede the faith issue to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Al, the Republican Party over the last 30 years was very much tied to many churchgoers--\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  became a --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is this going to become a liability?\par \par \uc2 CARDENAS: Probably. You know, I`m saddened by the fact that America is becoming more secular just from a spiritual point of view. I also am saddened by the fact that my Catholic Church and the non-Evangelical Protestants have weakened themselves to the point where they`re not as relevant.\par \par \uc2 And so the most relevant political group in the religious movement right now are Evangelicals. And they have always had a different perspective than my Catholic Church or non-Evangelical Protestants. And I think these guys are talking to Evangelicals, not the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I think you`re absolutely right. Well, I hope you catch the episode if you get a chance.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "Meet the Press". Thank you, John.\par \par \uc2 [10:00:00]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220918ei9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par {\*\bkmkstart toc97}{\*\bkmkend toc97}\b \uc2 NBC NIGHTLY NEWS for September 12, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Keir Simmons, Tom Llamas, Molly Hunter, Kerry  Sanders, Megan Fitzgerald, Miguel Almaguer, Yamiche Alcindor, Kelly  Cobiella \par \uc2 3,691 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Nightly News\par \uc2 NTLN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [19:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC NEWS ANCHOR (on camera): Tonight the emotional outpouring of Scotland bids farewell to Queen Elizabeth.\par \par \uc2 (voiceover): The Queen`s children, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  and his three siblings leading the solemn procession as crowds lining the streets of Scotland`s capital. The moving service. The 500-year-old crown of Scotland placed on the Queen`s casket. Thousands lining up for hours to pay tribute as the Queen lies in rest for 24 hours before her final journey to London. And \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  breaking his silence. His powerful tribute to the woman he called granny.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, Ukraine saying its lightning advance has retaken more than 1000 square miles from Russia. In just 48 hours Russia retaliated by striking Ukraine`s power grid. Is the war at a turning point?\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s legal team objecting to the DOJ`s special master candidates to review the classified documents ceased from Mar-a-Lago. What they are asking the judge to do?\par \par \uc2 Extreme weather in the West. Heavy rain triggering mudslides. More than 50 people rescued as dozens of wildfires rage in the region.\par \par \uc2 Caught on camera, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 ` unmanned \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  rocket bursting into flames after liftoff. And the tributes for the Queen and what officials are now asking mourners to stop leaving behind.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is NBC NIGHTLY NEWS with Lester Holt. Good evening. The coffin of Queen Elizabeth II lies at rest tonight in Edinburgh, Scotland where thousands have lined up to pay their respects. Tonight, the Queen`s daughter and three sons led by Charles holding a vigil for their mother at St. Giles cathedral.\par \par \uc2 It was a day filled with powerful and poignant images including a quiet stately procession of the Queen`s coffin down the cobblestone streets of Edinburgh`s Royal Mile to the Cathedral. The crown of Scotland placed atop her coffin. Her children walking behind the hearse, as it slowly made its way past a silent crowd gathered to say goodbye. Scotland, a country adored by the Queen and in fact the place she died on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The newly crowned king meantime making several appearances today including addressing parliament as he both grieves and consoles the nation.\par \par \uc2 Keir Simmons starts our reporting tonight from London.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, NBC NEWS SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It was a powerful tribute. The Queen`s daughter and sons including \uc2 King Charles\uc2  walking slowly behind her cortage. Thousands lining Edinburgh`s famous Royal Mile. Crowd so quiet, you could hear the hooves of the horses on the cobbled streets of Scotland`s capital. Queen Elizabeth loves this country. The silence speaking volumes about their respect for her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I can`t find my words. It`s -- I`m so sorry.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: The Queen helps plan every detail of today`s service and the many days of remembrance ahead. From the pageantry, like the precise military choreography, carrying her coffin inside the 12th-century Cathedral, to the intensely personal. In church, the Queen`s children looking on as the nearly 500-year-old Scottish crown is laid on her oak casket, taking its place alongside a wreath of flowers, including some of her favorites picked from her Balmoral garden, all on top of Scotland`s Royal Standard.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES III, KING, UNITED KINGDOM: The tangible connections to my darling late mother, we see all around us.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Earlier, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  speaking to the \uc2 British Parliament\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHARLES III: She set an example of selfless duty which, with God`s help and your counsels, I am resolved faithfully to follow.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Andrew, you`re a sick (BLEEP).\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Not everything was scripted today. One person protesting Prince Andrew was quickly removed from the crowd. Today`s events in Edinburgh capping off that solemn weekend journey that carried the Queen through 180 miles of stunning Scottish countryside after leaving her beloved Balmoral Castle. People on horseback paying respects along the road. These tractors a tribute to her from local farmers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We`re proud to be Scottish and we`re proud to be British. And it`s nice to say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: And another poignant moment in this long goodbye. The Queen`s children mounting a vigil standing on all sides of her casket heads bowed.\par \par \uc2 Even as many thousands of mourners kept coming, each given just a few minutes to say goodbye to a queen who gave them her life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [19:05:10]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Keir, it was quite a day. I know tomorrow, the focus of the solemn ceremonies will turn to London.\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS (on camera): That`s right, Lester. The Queen`s body will be brought here to Buckingham palace tomorrow. Then, on Wednesday, move to Westminster for four days where hundreds of thousands are expected to wait in line. And then, a week today, that historic State Funeral. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Keir Simmons, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And NBC is Tom Llamas has been on the ground for us in Edinburgh, the last few days. And Tom, you walk the Royal Mile today.\par \par \uc2 TOM LLAMAS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Lester, that`s right. The scene down there was remarkable because of the size of the crowd. It was double, if not triple what we experienced when the Queen`s hearse first pulled into Edinburgh on Sunday. But tonight, that solemn procession uphill through the medieval street with the king and the Queen`s children marching along through a sea of people so many onlookers were pouring over staircases on some corners.\par \par \uc2 But the impact of Queen Elizabeth`s reign, it hit us afterwards, Lester, it`s what many didn`t see, the line nine hours long in some cases to see the Queen`s casket up close. I spoke to several people in the crowd, some who said they wanted to witness history but so many other saying the Queen earned that devotion with her 70 years on the throne. Two female veterans told me they were waiting all that time to personally salut the coffin.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, Lester, this is a live image of the scene outside St. Giles Cathedral. The sun has gone down. It is chilly here. It`s very late. People are tired and yet they still wait to say goodbye. If you have any doubt of how the Queen touched people, look at that right there, Lester.\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Yes, remarkable but not surprising outpouring tonight. Tom, thanks. And one of the more striking scenes as these events unfolded, \uc2 King Charles\uc2 ` sons William and Harry emerging together with their wives over the weekend in a rare reunion. Here`s Molly Hunter.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY HUNTER, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): It was a welcome reunion that took the country by surprise. Getting out of the same car, the new Prince and Princess of Wales, William and Kate, alongside Harry and Meghan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We didn`t imagine all four of them to be together, but I think that was the Queen`s last wish.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: We now know it was Prince William, who reportedly offered the olive branch reaching out to Harry and Meghan inviting them to greet the crowds at Windsor, a suggestion perhaps coming from the new king before such a critical week.\par \par \uc2 DAISY MCANDREW, NBC NEW ROYAL COMMENTATOR: I firmly believe it was Charles who tried to metaphorically bang their heads together and say please, for me, for the future of the monarchy for your father, can you put on a brave face and go out and give us a sign of unity?\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: And unity, at least for that brief hour, was seen and felt by the lucky thousands.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That was really special, actually. I was really surprised all four of them were together. And yes, that`s quite emotional, I think, being a Brit seeing them, the two brothers side by side.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: Since Harry and Meghan famously left behind their Royal duties, fueling the brotherly rift, Prince William has moved into the role he was born for assuming his father`s former title. As the heir apparent, his official state responsibilities now a world away from Harry`s new life and California.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Growing up Princess Diana famously really wanted Harry and William to be equally important. Even though William`s destiny was very different, he was always going to be the monarch, she didn`t want Harry to feel less important, less significant.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: In a new tribute to granny, Harry says he`s forever grateful for my earliest childhood memories to meeting you for the first time as my commander-in-chief. But the brothers strike different notes. While Harry pledges to honor his father`s new role, William says he`ll be supporting my father the king in every way I can as the next in line.\par \par \uc2 And now it`s William and Kate, the new Princess of Wales, Princess Diana`s former title, who are the most popular members of the Royal Family, stepping into the spotlight, shouldering the responsibility of modernizing this monarchy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNTER: Now, looking ahead, there are a number of opportunities where we might see the two brothers together again. What`s clear, though, is they don`t want it to distract from the critical week ahead for the Royal Family. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Molly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Queen has been mourned well beyond Great Britain. She was also the official head of state and 14 other nations, including some that carry memories of a painful past. Kerry Sanders reports tonight from Jamaica.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KERRY SANDERS, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In former British colonies around the world, flags at half-staff including in India, once part of the sprawling British Empire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I remember her in such a way that she`s that`s like our mother`s figure.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: The Queen often visited Britain`s colonies and former colonies. Today, there are 14 Commonwealth realms where the king is still recognized as the head of state including here in Jamaica. Are you mourning the Queen`s death?\par \par \uc2 CLIFF HUGHES, JAMAICA HISTORIAN: No.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: No.\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: Because her passing is regrettable but she`s not that close to us for us to be mourning.\par \par \uc2 [19:10:10]\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Jamaica historian Cliff Hughes says this country`s beautiful shores hold an ugly past, slavery. The enslaved forced to the West Indies by the Royal-African company. They harvested sugar known as green gold.\par \par \uc2 FAE ELLINGTON, RESIDENT, KINGSTON, JAMAICA: It built England. It built the streets. It built the big businesses.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: That`s the value of sugar at the time.\par \par \uc2 ELLINGTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: The New King, Charles III, now inherits that legacy.\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: When slavery was finally abolished, those who were the enslavers were paid 20 million pounds, which was 40 percent of Britain`s annual budget.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And how much were the slaves themselves who were now free given?\par \par \uc2 HUGHES: Not one cent.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Which is why reparations remained a hot topic. When the now-Prince and Princess of Wales visited Jamaica in March, there were protests and demand for an official apology. Some now hope that comes from the new King.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SANDERS (on camera): Under the court system here which mirrors England`s, if there were an official apology, that could well open the door to reparations. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: All right, Kerry Sanders tonight in Jamaica, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll turn now to another major story, the lightning advance by Ukraine`s military reclaiming more ground against Russian forces in the northeast as Russia retaliates by striking Ukraine`s power grid. Our Megan Fitzgerald is in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEGAN FITZGERALD, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): In a fast and furious reaction, Russia launching missiles and Ukraine`s east, striking critical infrastructure plunging millions into the dark. The strike seen as retaliation as Ukraine sweeps through the Russian front line in a shocking advance. Ukraine now claims in the last 12 days, they`ve taken back 2300 square miles of Russian-held territory in the northeastern Kharkiv region.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine putting out this video, an incredible scene as the Ukrainian flag is raised in a village east of Kharkiv, as many residents overcome with emotion thanking the Ukrainian soldiers for liberating them. Ukraine releasing images showing Russian artillery and tanks left behind, as they say Russian soldiers were forced to withdraw.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy thanking his troops, saying the world is impressed and the enemy is panicking.\par \par \uc2 UK`s ministry of defense describes the territory Ukraine says it took back as twice the size of London, though Russia still controls the south and the east. Russia`s President Putin not commenting on Ukraine`s counter- offensive so far. A spokesperson only saying the Russian military operation is ongoing and will achieve its objective. But the question tonight, is this a turning point in the war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FITZGERALD (on camera): And tonight, Ukraine is claiming that it`s not just Russian equipment they`ve captured, it`s also Russian soldiers, saying they have so many POWs they`re running out of room. Lester?\par \par \uc2 HOLT: OK, Megan Fitzgerald in Ukraine, thank you. Back home now to the battle over the \uc2 FBI\uc2  search at Mar-a-Lago. Attorneys for former President Trump objecting to the candidates the Justice Department proposed to serve as special master to review the classified documents seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Mr. Trump`s team disputing the documents classified status and asking the judge to reject the DOJ -- the DOJ`s request to continue reviewing the files.\par \par \uc2 In 60 seconds, parts of the West can`t catch a break. The threat of more extreme weather after dozens are rescued in mudslides and wildfires raged on.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Back now with the extreme weather in the West. Flood watches in effect after mudslides forced dozens of rescues on the heels of record heat. Here`s Miguel Almaguer.\par \par \uc2 [19:15:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL ALMAGUER, NBC NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Tonight, a year of extremes in just a matter of days, historic heat fires, floods, and now mudslides. The volatile Western weather creating havoc for first responders and dangerous conditions for those suddenly in harm`s way.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: All these vehicles are stuck in about two feet of -- 20 mile an hour debris flow.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: Outside Los Angeles, firefighters rescued more than 50, including young families who suddenly became trapped and caked in debris as a mudslide covered a canyon road.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was terrifying.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: On the heels of record-shattering heat, the remnants of Tropical Storm Kay brought flash flooding that`s soaked the region. Downed trees, swamp neighborhoods, and sidewalk rescues.\par \par \uc2 From floods to fires, there`s been little relief. Across the West, more than 90 large wildfires are burning in a region mired in drought. Some 800,000 acres scorched from active fires as crews battle to gain the upper hand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The flames are 300 feet in the air, the trees were exploding.\par \par \uc2 ALMAGUER: With entire communities under evacuations, in recent days, four have been killed trying to escape the flames. And now, after this crash, three firefighting personnel nearly lost their lives also trying to battle them. Tonight, the West can`t catch a break from a sudden season of extremes. Miguel Almaguer, NBC news.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: A spectacular rocket failure today for \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 , a company owned by \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 . It happened about a minute into the launch, flame scene shooting out of the rocket. Luckily, there were no people on board. The capsule was able to separate and parachute to Earth. This is the same type of rocket that sends paying customers to the edge of space but the capsule is different.\par \par \uc2 Up next tonight, after Roe, abortion is rewriting November ballots. Ahead, one swing state`s looming battle in our series power of the vote.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:20:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: We`re back now with our series power of the vote. With less than two months until Election Day, in Michigan, voters will decide if abortion rights should be protected by the state constitution. Here`s Yamiche Alcindor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): Lifelong Republican Jessica Leech was devastated when the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEECH, LIFELONG REPUBLICAN: I cried for weeks. I cried for my daughter. I always considered myself pro-life.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Now, she changed her mind and her party. Jessica got pregnant at 19 and visited an abortion clinic to weigh her options. She decided to have her son who`s now 17 and says being able to make that choice is something every woman deserves.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to people who think your story is an example of why abortion shouldn`t be an option?\par \par \uc2 LEECH: I would tell those people that until recently, it was always a hypothetical. I would beg them to understand that having these options paved the way for me to be a good mother.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: In Michigan, residents will vote on a ballot referendum in November that would enshrine abortion rights into the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): You`re a doctor.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is also up for reelection.\par \par \uc2 What is at stake in this election and in the ballot referendum?\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: Well, abortion and the right to be a full American citizen with agency of your body is on this ballot.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Her views are also shaped by her experiences.\par \par \uc2 WHITMER: In college, in the days after I was raped, it dawned on me. Oh, my God, I might be pregnant. And the only thing that gave me a little bit of relief is knowing that I could make a decision that was right for me if that happened.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Her opponent, Republican Tudor Dixon, supports banning abortion with no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if you could just talk a bit more about your own views and talk to people who are worried about your views being too extreme.\par \par \uc2 I have shared my own pro-life story. I lost a baby 18 weeks. When I lost the baby and the doctor said this baby probably died in the womb at 16 weeks, I held a beautiful baby girl.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Dixon also does not believe abortion is a top issue for voters.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: They`re concerned with how to get their kids back on track after they were kept out of school. And if you look at our crime rates in the past couple of years, it`s just continued to rise. And why our businesses are being crushed by the government?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Both supporters and opponents of the referendum are mobilizing like anti-abortion rights activist Christen Polo, now working overtime after voters in Kansas recently voted to keep abortion protections in its state constitution.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTEN POLO, ACTIVIST: I`m very concerned. Frankly, the policies that are being put forth in this abortion amendment here in Michigan are some of the most extremist policies we`ve seen in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Meantime, Jessica hopes sharing her story helps keep abortion legal in Michigan. Her son Cameron sent her a text on the day of our interview.\par \par \uc2 Your life experiences, our relationship, after you decided to keep me and change your life around me, and now your perspective of life is what the world needs most.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: And up next, the tributes from everyday people honoring the Queen.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [19:25:00]\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Finally, beyond the formal tributes, the tokens people have left to honor the Queen, small gestures adding up to a powerful message. Here`s Kelly Cobiella.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voiceover): The ordinary, the extraordinary, messages and memories. Many shedding a tear for the Queen leaving a token. A small way just to say thank you for a life of service.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She means a lot to all the people here and she`s done so much for everyone. So, we kind of need to honor her.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: And a way to say goodbye, drawings cards, notes. Thank you for being the best Queen ever, one read. Another, with her beloved Corgi. Tributes for mourners around the world marking Queen Elizabeth`s extraordinary life. Katarina Mostova (PH) and her family fled the war in Ukraine and brought her country`s national flower, the sunflower, for the queen.\par \par \uc2 Why did you come?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because we want to make honor to Queen. This is so important for us.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Mountains of flowers and marmalade sandwiches, a nod to the Queen`s surprise skit with the children`s character Paddington Bear in June. Now, so many sandwiches and stuffed bears, they`re begging the public just to bring flowers. Members of the royal family are deeply touched.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Have you seen the messages?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM, PRINCE OF WALES: Yes, amazing. It`s the little 7-year-olds and 11- year-olds messages get straight through every time.\par \par \uc2 COBIELLA: Notes from children have only been alive for a fraction of the Queen`s long reign like this one: "Thank you for showing the world how strong a woman can be." Kelly Cobiella, NBC News, Edinburgh Scotland.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HOLT: Messages from the heart. That`s NIGHTLY NEWS for this Monday. Thank you for watching, everyone. I`m Lester Holt. Please take care of yourself and each other. Good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document NTLN000020220913ei9c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc98}{\*\bkmkend toc98}\b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS for September 11, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Yamiche Alcindor, Amy Walter, Claire McCaskill \par \uc2 9,042 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Meet the Press\par \uc2 MTPR\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [09:00:18]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC NEWS HOST " (voice over): This Sunday, my interview with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . The vice president opens up on the threat posed by election deniers...\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: I think that we have to admit that there are attacks from within and we need to take it seriously.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... on the rightward shift of the Supreme Court...\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think this is an activist court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... and its abortion decision.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: It is not right that we take a constitutional right from the women of America and deprive them of the ability to make decisions about their own body and instead say the government`s in a better position to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... on eliminating the filibuster for abortion and voting rights.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And the president has been very clear. He will sign into law and not let the filibuster get in the way.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... on January 6th and those top-secret documents at Mar-a-Lago...\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What do you say to the argument that it would be too divisive for the country to prosecute a former president?\par \par \uc2 (voice over): ... and on her plans for 2024.\par \par \uc2 Plus, with less than two months to go to the midterms, independent voters speak out on whether President Biden should run for re-election...\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Wow, you guys are all nos.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (voice over): ... and what they think of today`s GOP.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED INDEPENDENT VOTER: I just -- I don`t recognize that party anymore.\par \par \uc2 TODD (on camera): Joining me for insight and analysis are former Democratic senator Claire McCaskill; Yamiche Alcindor, moderator of "Washington Week" on \uc2 PBS\uc2 ; Republican political consultant Matt Gorman; and Amy Walter, editor in chief of The Cook Political Report.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to Sunday. It`s "MEET THE PRESS."\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: From NBC News in Washington, the longest-running show in television history, this is MEET THE PRESS with Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And a good Sunday morning. There`s been no shortage of unkind things said about the job of vice president of the United States. And many of those things have been said by vice presidents. The most famous may have come from the first, John Adams.\par \par \uc2 "I am vice president," he said. "In this I am nothing, but I may be everything."\par \par \uc2 The number one job of the vice president is to be a good number two, to help make progress without making waves. And after 20 months, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is familiar with the position`s unique challenges.\par \par \uc2 She was seen as a new symbol of a racially diverse country. But as President Biden`s approval ratings fell, so did hers. She`s been tasked with some intractable assignments, from enacting national voting reform to solving the immigration crisis at the border. Some of her supporters -- and there are quite a few -- argue she`s being set up to fail on some of those issues.\par \par \uc2 But she`s also well positioned to become president someday. Why? Fifteen vice presidents already have, including our current president, who was a former vice president.\par \par \uc2 Today is September 11th. It was 21 years ago that we were attacked by foreign terrorists. Not quite 20 years after 9/11 the capital came under attack from domestic terrorists. With the 9/11 anniversary in mind, I sat down with Vice President Harris on Friday at the Johnson Space Center in Houston.\par \par \uc2 She was there to talk about the space program in general and to speak with some astronauts on the International Space Station. I began by asking the vice president about how, over two decades, our focus has had to shift from foreign terror to the threat from within.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think it is very dangerous. And I think it is very harmful. And it makes us weaker. You know, I`ve -- I have met with and I`ve had conversations with over 100 foreign heads of state, presidents, prime ministers, chancellors, kings. And, you know, when we as the United States walk into those rooms around the world, we have had the honor and privilege, historically, of holding our head up as a defender and an example of a great democracy.\par \par \uc2 And that then gives us the legitimacy and the standing to talk about the importance of democratic principles, rule of law, human rights.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things, though, that comes with that privilege is that we hold ourselves out to be a role model, which means the rest of the world, like any role model, watches what we do to see if it matches up with what we say.\par \par \uc2 So you look at everything from the fact there are 11 people right now running for secretary of state, the keepers of the integrity of the voting system of their state, who are election deniers. You`ve got...\par \par \uc2 TODD: And what`s that sending -- what message does that send to the world?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, you couple that with people who hold some of the highest elected offices in our country who -- who refuse to condemn an insurrection on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [09:05:08]\par \par \uc2 And I think what it sends is a signal that is -- that causes people to question, hey, is America still valuing what they talk about, which is the integrity of democracies, which means protecting rule of law and the -- and then sanctity of these systems and speaking up when they are attacked.\par \par \uc2 And I`m -- and I`m very concerns about it, Chuck. I`ll tell you, I`m very concerned about it, because there are so many issues going on in the world that I think require at least how we as Americans have traditionally thought about what is right, what is good, what should be fought for, what should be human ideals, and certainly the ideals of democracies.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, through the process of what we`ve been through, we`re starting to allow people to call into question our commitment to those principles. And that`s a shame.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nearly 70 percent of Republicans don`t believe that the president and yourself won this -- the 2020 election legitimately. Do you think you`ll ever be able to change their minds?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I find that polling interesting. And I`m going to tell you why. I`m traveling the country. We`re here in Houston. I was in, you know, North Carolina. I was -- I mean, I`ve been traveling all over the country. I don`t find that. I don`t get that kind of sense or feedback.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think you talk to a lot of Republicans?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, I think I talk to a lot of Americans. And I`ll tell you, when -- when I talk to folks and they know that, for example, there was a bipartisan infrastructure law that, you know, in Durham, is going to fix I- 40, or here in Houston is going to fix Telephone Road, those are some of their priorities.\par \par \uc2 When I talk to people about what we`re doing, that we`re going to make sure, especially after the pandemic, that all families, all working families, have access to affordable, high-speed internet, and they`re going to -- we`re going to bring the cost of it down, less $30 a month, plus people are going to get a $100 voucher to buy a laptop or, you know, an iPad, people want to hear that.\par \par \uc2 Look what happened in Kansas, on the issue of choice. People, whoever they voted for in the last election or in the next, stood up and said, "It is not right that we take a constitutional right from the women of America and deprive them of the ability to make decisions about their own body, and instead say the government`s in a better position to do that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s -- you say you take issue with this idea that there are 70 percent. Do you think...\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I just don`t experience that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You don`t experience it. You don`t know if they exist?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think that there...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Or do you think they just say it?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, I`m no t-- I would not dare tell anyone what they should think. I`m just telling you that I do believe that, when it comes down to the things that wake people up in the middle of the night, that worry them about their future, about their family, about their children or their parents, most of those issues they think of not through the lens of the party with which they`re registered to vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is a semi-fascist?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Listen, I think that, when we -- let`s not get caught up in -- in politicizing the fact that most people in America know that it is not helpful to our country when we have people who are denying elections or trying to obstruct the outcome of an election, where the largest number of people in our country voted for the president of the United States. And when we look at where we are, I think that we have to admit that there are attacks from within, to your first question, and we need to take it seriously. And -- and we need to stand up together, all of us, and think of this not through partisan lens but as Americans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think back to the president`s inaugural address. And he said the following. Politics need not be a raging fire destroying everything in its path. And then I think about the construction, the language construction he used with "semi-fascist."\par \par \uc2 And I understand the dilemma. You`ve got to call it out in order to bring the country together. But it`s hard to do both at the same time, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, here`s the thing. I mean, let`s talk about our president for a moment. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has spent his entire career -- it is on for all to see -- working across the aisle, his whole career. Sometimes he`s been criticized for believing in bipartisanship, for believing in compromise, for believing in working across the aisle, finding common ground. His whole career has been that.\par \par \uc2 But there are moments in time when we have to also agree, all good people who care about our country, that there are those who right now are vividly not defending our democracy.\par \par \uc2 [09:10:13]\par \par \uc2 And I think we want that our commander in chief, that the president of the United States will speak up and raise the alarm about what this means to our strength and our future, much less our integrity.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious, when you see the Democratic Party and some parts of the party funding ads to promote some of these election deniers in primaries, whether it`s Michigan, the high-profile race there, Illinois, Colorado, New Hampshire. It looks like a cynical -- you know, a little bit cynical. And the president went out of his way to say there are good Republicans here.\par \par \uc2 Should you leave the good Republicans alone in a primary?\par \par \uc2 Is the Democratic Party making a mistake here by -- by, you know, those people could win, if you`re not careful?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I mean, listen, I`m not going to tell people how to run their campaigns. You know, I ran -- in terms of statewide office, I ran...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you have done this?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I ran -- I ran...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you have done this? Is this in your -- is this something you`d be comfortable doing?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I`m not going to tell people how to run their campaigns, Chuck. I ran for statewide, for attorney general, re-election, won both times; for Senate, won that race. And I know that it is best to -- to let a candidate, along with their -- their advisers, let them make the decision based on what they believe is in the best interest of their state. I`m not going to tell people what to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re not worried that this reflects poorly on the Democrats?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think that what we have to focus on is that, in 60 days as of this interview, in less than two months, we are looking at a midterm election in which so much is on the line.\par \par \uc2 Take, for example, the issue of choice. The \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , in the Dobbs decision, just took a constitutional right that had been recognized from the women of America, from the people of America. Well, how does that relate to the midterms?\par \par \uc2 Our president has said he will not let the filibuster get in the way. If the Senate, through a majority vote, votes to pass the Women`s Health Protection Act, he will sign it into law. You know what that means, in the midterms? We need to hold on to the Senate and get two more. And then we can put into law the protections of Roe v. Wade. Everything is on the line when you think about the millions of women and people in America who care about them, who understand the significance of protecting a woman`s right to make decisions about her own body, instead of her government...\par \par \uc2 TODD: The Democrats get 52 Senate seats...\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: ... telling her what to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fifty-two Senate seats or more, legislative filibuster gone, or just on this issue?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: The president has clear, on this issue and on a very important issue in addition to that important issue, which is voting rights. And the president has been very clear. He will sign into law and not let the filibuster get in the way the John Lewis Voting Rights Advancement Act and the Freedom to Vote Act. Because what is happening in our country, in states around our country, including this very state, they are passing laws making it more difficult for people to vote.\par \par \uc2 And so our president has said we need to have protections to make sure that every American, whoever they vote for, has the unobstructed ability to do that when it is otherwise their right. So everything is on the line in these elections, in just less than two months.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you were a senator, you -- you weren`t ready to get rid of the legislative filibuster, as of 2017. What -- what has changed?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, let`s talk about what we have seen around the country, in particular after I, together with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , when we won, and he became president and I became vice president, which was as a result of some of the highest number of people voting that have ever voted in a presidential election, and then you almost saw, almost immediately, so-called extremist leaders around the country starting to pass laws making it more difficult for people to vote. Because I guess it worried them that people realized, "Hey, I`m working two jobs, but a dropbox makes it easier for me to fill out the ballot in the middle of the night and go drop it off instead of standing in line. Hey, voting by mail, especially if I have -- if I have disabilities, physical disabilities that make it more difficult for me to stand in line for four hours. I can vote by mail."\par \par \uc2 And I think that scared some people, that the American people were voting in such large numbers.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you comfortable that this could end the legislative filibuster for good, probably, even if you only try to do it...\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: No, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... for two issues, that it won`t end up getting rid of it?\par \par \uc2 [09:15:04]\par \par \uc2 You think it will be held into place on other issues?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think that there are -- that is very likely, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How much should President -- former President Trump`s status as a former president and a potential 2024 candidate, how much should that factor into the decision to charge him?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, I wouldn`t dare tell the Department of Justice what to do. As a former prosecutor, I will tell you I have -- my -- I am not in the business of telling a prosecutor what to do with their case, because they know best the facts and the evidence as applied to the law.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m not going to tell them what to do. And certainly the president and I, and our administration, unlike the previous administration...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: ... have been very, very careful to make sure that there is no question about any kind of interference in terms of the decisions that the Department of Justice makes in that regard.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, but let`s -- let me try to go to 60,000 feet. What do you say to the argument that it would be too divisive for the country to prosecute a former president?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think that our country is a country that has gone through different periods of time, where the unthinkable has happened and where there has been a call for justice, and justice has been served. And I think that`s potentially going to always be the case in our country that people are going to demand justice, and they rightly do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Have you thought about -- have you thought through the scenarios that you might face on January 6, 2025?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 No. I mean, well, listen, what have I thought about? I have thought about the fact that right now we have an election in less than two months. And if we`re going to look at timelines, that`s one of the -- the most immediate timelines that I`m looking at.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know, but, I mean, have you thought about how you might handle a certification that did not reflect the outcome of the popular vote in the state?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I haven`t gotten to that point yet. I -- I have to believe that -- that the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  and all the people who have taken an oath to defend our democracy will ensure and will stand up against anyone who tries to destroy or circumvent the rules and the practices and procedures that we`ve had in place that have allowed a peaceful transfer of power, since the inception of our nation, and the founding of our nation.\par \par \uc2 And getting back to the ultimate point, that is -- that is what we`re talking about when we think about January 6th. We have always had a peaceful transfer of power in our nation, no matter our differences, no matter how...\par \par \uc2 TODD: But we can`t say that now, can we?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: ... bloody-knuckled we`ve been in campaigns...\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can we say that now?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, certainly we -- we were on the verge of having a very different outcome. And the injury was still an injury, for which we still are experiencing the wound.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: When we come back, more of my sit-down with the vice president, including what she thinks of the Supreme Court after the overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think this is an activist court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:20:35]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Returning to my sitdown with Vice President Harris, we talked about the Supreme Court, with its now 6-3 conservative majority. In its last term the court expanded gun rights, limited the government`s power to regulate greenhouse emissions, and of course eliminated the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 When I asked the vice president about her views on the court, she had a ready answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We have some polling that shows confidence in the Supreme Court is at its lowest level that we`ve measured in over 20 years. How much confidence do you have in the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think this is an activist court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: It means that we had an established right for almost half a century, which is the right of women to make decisions about their own body, as an extension of what we have decided to be the privacy rights to which all people are entitled. And this court took that constitutional right away. And we are suffering as a nation because of it.\par \par \uc2 That causes me great concern about the integrity of the court overall, especially as someone who -- my life was inspired by people like Thurgood Marshall, the work on that court of Earl Warren to bring a unanimous court to pass Brown v. Board of Education. This is the court that once, on one, sat Earl Warren and Thurgood Marshall, \uc2 Sandra Day O`Connor\uc2 . It`s a very different court.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There`s a bill in the Senate, Senator Kaine, Senator Collins are working on, that they say would essentially codify what Roe was, protect the right to abortion at 24 weeks, that would include some religious exemptions. Is that -- if that`s the bill that can pass the Senate, you OK with it?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I`d have to read it and see. The details obviously matter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Some abortion rights groups have not been as -- as high on it as the other bill that didn`t get \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 ` support earlier. This one should get \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 . Is it more important for the Senate to find some Republican votes here?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Ideally, we codify Roe; we -- we put into law a protection for what I believe is a constitutional right for women, to make these decisions. And it would be bipartisan; ideally, yes, it would be bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe the government should put any limit on abortion?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I believe government should not be telling women what to do with their bodies. I believe government should not be telling women how to plan their families. I believe government should not be criminalizing health care providers. I believe government should not be saying no exception for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 As a prosecutor, a former prosecutor who specialized in child sexual assault cases, understanding the violence that occurs against women and children, and then to further subject them to those kind of inhumane conditions, that`s what I believe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Final topic here. Since we`re here in Texas, I want to ask you about the border. Would you call the border secure?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I think that there is no question that we have to do what the president and I asked Congress to do -- the first request we made, pass a bill to create a pathway to citizenship. The border is secure. But we also have a broken immigration system, in particular over the last four years before we came in, and it needs to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re going to have 2 million people cross this border for the first time ever. You`re confident this border is secure?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: We have a secure border, in that that is a priority for any nation, including ours and our administration. But there are still a lot of problems that we are trying to fix, given the deterioration that happened over the last four years.\par \par \uc2 We also have to put in place a -- a law and a plan for a pathway for citizenship for the millions of people who are here and are prepared to do what is legally required to gain citizenship. We don`t have that in place because people are playing politics, in a state like this and in Congress, on -- by the way, you want to talk about bipartisanship -- on an issue that at one time was a bipartisan issue, both in terms of Republican senators and even presidents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right now, one of our inflationary problems is we don`t have enough labor, particularly...\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yeah, you`re right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: ... in the service economy.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: You`re right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: A -- a solution on immigration reform that increased, for particularly lower-wage job -- lower-wage labor, could actually have an impact on lowering inflation. How is that not the motivating force to get something done here?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I -- I do believe that, for all reasonable people, all reasonable people are motivated and desirous that we would get something done. I think a big part of the problem, though, is that there`s been political gamesmanship with this issue, suggesting that it`s a zero sum game.\par \par \uc2 If you want to deal with the issue, there are practical solutions, which include creating a pathway to citizenship, fixing a broken immigration system, dealing with the root causes of why people are fleeing their home, when most people don`t want to leave home, and if they do, it`s usually because they`re fleeing some harm or they simply can`t take care of their basic needs if they stay.\par \par \uc2 There are solutions. And, sadly, this has become such a partisan issue, instead of something where we work on it together, agreeing that what we`re doing is not working as a nation. It`s not working.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, there is more to come, including Vice President Harris`s plans for 2024. But when we come back, we`re going to take a break and get some reaction from our panel to this interview. So stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:30:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The panel is here, Yamiche Alcindor, moderator of "Washington Week" on PBS; Amy Walter, the editor-in-chief of "The Cook Political Report"; Republican political consultant Matt Gorman; and former Democratic Senator Claire McCaskill.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, all.\par \par \uc2 So I`ll shut up. Yamiche, initial reaction to the interview.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: My initial reaction to the interview is that she really wanted to get across how concerned she is about the state of the country. She talked about what she called the activist court in the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 But she also talked about, sort of the fact that it`s a shame, that`s the word that she used, when you think about election deniers being elected all over this country and the state of democracy.\par \par \uc2 I was very struck by the fact that she said she didn`t have an experience with the idea that 70 percent of Republicans don`t believe that she and President Biden were fairly elected. And that she hadn`t really thought about 2025, what she would do if she was presented with election results that didn`t reflect what the people had voted on because, as someone who`s been out on the campaign trail, I can tell you that a lot of Republicans around this country, and especially the ones that are showing up to campaign rallies, they do not believe that she and President Biden were elected fairly.\par \par \uc2 It`s a big, big issue. It`s a divisive one. But it`s one that`s real.\par \par \uc2 So those polls, I see that on the ground when I`m reporting. But you can tell that the vice president absolutely is really concerned about that and hopes that this country can figure out how to move forward.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah. But doesn`t that also -- it melds perfectly with the case that President Biden is trying to make in this election, which is, the folks who do believe that the election was stolen or overturned, they are a minority. We are bigger than they are, right? Uniting the soul of America, that piece of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 So, it, in some ways, is a way to react to suggestions that that speech was too divisive, right? By calling semi-fascism and MAGA America, we`re really separating a very small portion of the Republican Party from the bigger American electorate, that maybe 70 percent of Americans say they believe this, but I don`t buy that. I buy that we`re stronger than that. We`re more united than that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I got the impression she didn`t like having to define semi-fascism.\par \par \uc2 MATT GORMAN, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: No. And to your point though, I think it reminded me and it pointed out that this whole strategy it seems from Biden -- the strategy is very ham-handed. He goes and says, everyone`s a semi-fascist, walks it back. MAGA -- well, not everybody`s MAGA. And now you have her seeming to walk this back. It`s totally incongruous.\par \par \uc2 And look, again, I think all of that rings hollow if, you know, as you asked it, they`re boosting these candidates who are threats to democracy in Senate primaries across the country. So if you`re so concerned about that, what about Colorado Senate, New Hampshire Senate right now? Peter Meijer voted to impeach Trump. Well, he`s going to be gone. So it rings hollow.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire?\par \par \uc2 CLAIRE MCCASKILL, NBC NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, first of all, I thought she did a terrific job in the interview. I think she has had a rough road to go. I remember distinctly, Chuck, being in the cloakroom in the first two years of the Biden vice presidency, and how many of my colleagues were looking down their noses at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mock him, right?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Mocking him, you know? There is -- it`s a hard, hard job, the vice presidency. It`s even harder, I think, if you are a woman in that job who is seen as an opponent by a lot of the insiders in Washington for the presidency. And so there`s a lot of chattering behind the scenes of trying to diss \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I think this interview shows that maybe all the dissing of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is way premature. I think she did a good job and stood strong.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me -- I want to point out the court stuff because this was coincidental, but we heard from the Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  on Friday about this issue of court legitimacy. You heard what she called the court. Here`s his defense.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHIEF JUSTICE JOHN ROBERTS, U.S. SUPREME COURT: The court has always decided controversial cases. The decisions have always been subject to intense criticism, and that is entirely appropriate. But I don`t understand the connection between opinions that people disagree with and the legitimacy of the Court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Claire, I`m going to let you go first on this one. I -- this is one you`re, like, I guess he did not follow the confirmation process.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: He`s so, so out of touch. I mean, this -- really, this interview shows why the numbers for the Supreme Court are so bad. For him to say something like that, he just doesn`t get it. You don`t take away a right that has been around for 50 years, and then you don`t have a party go to the extremes of trying to make sure rape victims have to have forced birth.\par \par \uc2 You don`t do all that and not have it splash back on the Supreme Court. And they all said they respected precedent when they were confirmed. I heard them. America heard them. Clearly, they didn`t. And you know -- you can feel me getting angry at \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  right now because he knows better when he says that stuff.\par \par \uc2 [09:35:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s trying to defend the Court, and I do think he -- it gets harder and harder for him.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: It does. And look, that was his mission when he came in. He wanted to try and depoliticize the court.\par \par \uc2 But look, is the court politicized -- more politicized, or are we? If we had upheld Roe, if the court had upheld Roe, you wouldn`t be hearing that. I think activism really is lying in the eye of the beholder right now. And we`re in a situation where, if you don`t like it, then it`s really not about the decision, it`s about the legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is court -- by the way, is court a better issue than abortion specifically?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Is court a better issue than abortion? I will say that when I talk to women out on the campaign trail, they`re both attacking the court and attacking the fact that they feel like they do not have the rights to their body that they had for 50 years.\par \par \uc2 And I`m talking about Republican women because I`ve been surprised at the number of Republican women, especially young Republican women, who have come up to me and said, I voted for President Trump, former President Trump, but I now feel like this big government is in my doctor`s office, and I will now, for the first time, be voting for a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 I`ve heard that in Ohio, in Missouri, in Michigan, in other places because women in this country, I feel this passion and this anger from them. And it cuts across political lines.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Well, that`s the thing. We`ve been throwing around the term "pro- life" and "pro-choice" forever without really --\par \par \uc2 TODD: These are meaningless. WALTER: They are meaningless terms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They are meaningless labels now. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: The number of women that I have heard too who say, well, I`m pro- life, but I didn`t think that meant no exceptions. I, of course, wanted an exception for that.\par \par \uc2 Or I`m pro -- and even pro-choice. I`m pro-choice, but I do also believe in some of these restrictions, right? There should be some sort of framework here.\par \par \uc2 So I think, again, we`ve been debating this in a way that voters don`t talk about it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, do you think the GOP`s got to change how they`re talking about this?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: I don`t think it`s an issue in 2020 at all. I mean, I talk to Republicans every day who see these internal polls. It is not in the top four of issues. Even look at your poll last month. It was 8 percent. It was under climate change as an issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Don`t you think that democracy, though, is sharing some of those - that those who concerned about the abortion decision, maybe are in the democracy category?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: I don`t know how the question was phrased, but I think with abortion and threats to democracy, we tend to connote it with, okay, that`s Democratic base angry at Trump, or it`s pro-choice angry at the decision. Not necessarily the case.\par \par \uc2 That could be a single-issue voter on abortion who`s so thankful, you know, pro-life, or folks in the Republican base think Biden`s on the way to socialism.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: I can just say that, as someone who`s been out on the campaign trail, even if you ask a voter right now about health care or about the state of democracy, abortion comes up 90 percent of the time. And I think that to say it`s not an issue I think is very interesting to hear because, just as someone who`s out there reporting, abortion is absolutely an issue, along with inflation, along with the threat -\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: It`s not borne out in data. It`s just not. It`s not borne out in data.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: I hope, I hope, I hope Matt keeps saying that everywhere he goes, that abortion really isn`t an issue in this election. I think that is exactly what infuriates women when they hear that.\par \par \uc2 They may -- you`re asked in a poll what`s the most important issue, you may not be comfortable saying abortion. But if you say to a woman, we are now in your state, like they are in my state, forcing incest victims to give birth, when you are having doctors having to make life and death decisions around whether they go to prison or whether they take care of their patient, that is motivating voters, and it will in November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. I think an underrated headline from this interview is her declaration on the filibuster. Do you think two seats, it`s dead?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: I think the filibuster is on life support. And I think if the Democrats pick up more seats, I think it will probably go away. And frankly, I think it`ll probably go away if the Republicans take control.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you concur with that?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: I mean, they should change the filibuster right before the election. You know, the polls look pretty good for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 It could. But I think it would be a massive mistake because, again, they overreach on this and the pendulum goes right back. And, without a filibuster, Republicans will take back the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Amy, we know how this town works. You can take it away for two issues. The other side`s going to say, eh, forget it. All of it. I mean, it just feels like --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes, you can`t. This idea that there`s a carve-out, that is impossible. It`s either there or it`s not there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. When we come back, we`re going to get a little hint at Vice President Harris` plans for 2024 and beyond. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:42:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. There is always speculation around a vice president of the United States that he -- or she -- would like to lose the word "vice" from their title and become president someday. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is no exception to that speculation, particularly since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the oldest president in American history.\par \par \uc2 So, naturally I asked her about what she sees as her future:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: 2024. Should we expect some form of an announcement from the president and yourself after the midterms, right after the midterms, in a traditional - sort of that traditional timeframe?\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You`re talking about the election?\par \par \uc2 TODD: The election. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Listen, the president has been very clear that he intends to run again. And if he does, I will be running with him proudly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why do you say "probably?"\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I said proudly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, "proudly." I`m sorry, I thought -- I thought I heard "probably." I don`t want to get that -- that would create problems.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Proudly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Proudly.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Proudly. I`m very proud to be his vice president.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There is no job description, technically, other than tie-breaker vote in the Senate, which you`re now the record holder -- move over John Adams -- when it comes to tie-breaking votes in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Who would have thought --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That you`d break John Adams` record?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: -- that this kid born in Oakland, California, just broke a record with John Adams.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think that`s --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Kind of, you`ve got to appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s pretty interesting. But the one who was the very first one.\par \par \uc2 How do you prepare in case of the worst - the worst case scenario that your job may hand you? How do you prepare for that?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, I pray that it does not. And listen, I think that there is no question that the role and the responsibility of vice president is a very important one. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knows that more than anybody. He and I talk about it.\par \par \uc2 And it is the job of working with the president, doing what I can to be a great partner, to take on and help take on some of the biggest issues that impact our country, both domestically and in terms of foreign policy. And so that`s the work that I do.\par \par \uc2 So that has been the work of meeting with over a hundred heads of state around various issues, be it Ukraine or what we need to do around our space program. So that is the work of vice president, and it is important and it is something that I take very seriously.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to ask you one final question, and it`s a little off the beaten path, but it`s about Jackson, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Okay, and it`s more of a -- here we are, the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is a capital city of one of our 50 states.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And it`s tragic what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And there`s no --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: It`s tragic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, state, city, federal, everybody`s let these people down, haven`t they?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, I`ve talked to the mayor, Lumumba, and the president has talked to him, and we have -- the governor there has declared a state of emergency. We`re, FEMA is sending in --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, everyone is trying now.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But isn`t that the frustration? It`s now.\par \par \uc2 [09:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: However, that was -- it is a Jackson, Mississippi, that is an example of why we were pushing for the infrastructure law that now is the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 So that we are sending billions of dollars to places like Jackson to upgrade what is a decaying infrastructure across our country.\par \par \uc2 And so, help is coming because we actually came into office knowing that generations of leaders have been talking about fixing America`s infrastructure and failed. And we actually got it done.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You can see my entire interview with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  without edits, including what she says about her relationship with President Biden. That will be on our website, meetthepress.com.\par \par \uc2 Also, we have another programming note for you. We`re running a season four, a marathon, of our half-hour magazine show "Meet the Press Reports." That will begin today at 6:00 p.m. Eastern on NBC News NOW. So check that out.\par \par \uc2 And, good news, season five of "Meet the Press Reports" premiers this Thursday at 10:30 Eastern on NBC News NOW.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, independent voters will likely hold the key to November`s midterm elections, particularly in the state of Arizona. Up next, I spoke to some of those Arizona independents. They shared their thoughts on President Biden and a Republican Party that`s increasingly in Donald Trump`s image.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:50:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Panel is back with us.\par \par \uc2 I had a pretty busy week. Before I flew to Houston to interview Vice President Harris, I went to the battleground state of Arizona to report on the many races there this November. In some ways, this is sort of the center of election denialism on the GOP side of things. And with more than 70 percent of voters we`ve polled saying the country is on the wrong track, it wasn`t surprising to hear these Arizona independent voters feeling pretty sour about the state of our politics.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what they had to say about the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARILYN VAN OOSTEN, ARIZONA INDEPENDENT VOTERS: As a whole, I just -- I don`t recognize that party anymore.\par \par \uc2 AL BELL, ARIZONA INDEPENDENT VOTERS: What they`re really promoting is not effective programs or strategies to fix our problems. They`re promoting distrust.\par \par \uc2 AMY JENSEN, ARIZONA INDEPENDENT VOTERS: A more perfect union. That -- really, that necessitates compromise and discussion and listening. And I don`t see that from a lot of the candidates on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Amy Walter, I came away -- this is, you know, Arizona`s fascinating. For -- first of all, these voters were almost more focused on the secretary of state race with me than they were their Senate race or their gubernatorial race on that front.\par \par \uc2 But I came away believing Mark Kelly is stronger than I thought and so is Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right. And it`s -- being on television, as she -- Kari Lake, being a former TV anchor for, what was it, 20 years? There is a sense from voters that, I know this person. And whatever`s being said about them in attack ads, et cetera, well, maybe that`s true, but maybe it`s just politics. She can`t be like that because she`s been in my living room for the last 20 years. So she has a benefit there.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think this is what`s really fascinating about Arizona. It`s a swing state that never -- the voters there didn`t really expect to be a swing state.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: And the two parties haven`t accepted quite yet that it`s a swing state, especially the Republican Party --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- that is run by folks very much on the extreme. And so they continue to support --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- promote candidates who can`t win the middle. Now, Kari Lake may be an exception to this, but the kinds of candidates that are coming out, especially on the Senate side --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- are the candidates that can`t win those independent voters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Matt, this voter panel should panic you because two of those three, were, were, basically, they`re Republicans. They`re Paul Ryan Republicans. They were that wing of the party.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: John McCain Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: John McCain Republicans. And they`re, like, I`m out of this.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: So you watch that in a vacuum and you say, well, Kari Lake has to be down ten. And you`re right. And you see the polls, and it`s neck-and- neck.\par \par \uc2 And I think there are three countervailing forces that have -- make it to equilibrium. To your point, they`re not ready for it to be a swing state yet. They don`t have the Democratic bank.\par \par \uc2 Kelly came out of nowhere. He was a celebrity.\par \par \uc2 Sinema is a political talent. I`ll give her that. But \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is not a good candidate. And you combine that with, I think in the state we have economy and border security on our side as an issue. And also, we lose some of those white suburban voters, but we`re gaining in Latino voters as well.\par \par \uc2 So you have those three forces. It counterbalances a lot of that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah. Claire, Arizona`s fascinating when you have -- I do think it`s also a libertarian state.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Yep.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, abortion is -- it is -- there are so many odd crosscurrents. And I think we`re going to learn a lot.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Well, I, you know, maybe not. Maybe we`ll learn that candidates matter, which is not a new concept.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Maybe we`ll learn that extremes are not healthy for general elections. And I don`t think that`s a new thing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: I think more importantly, what we`re going to see, and this is what I really do believe, is that will we defy history by having the kind of turnout with the party who holds power and holds the presidency, will we have the kind of turnout that we never have in midterms? That`s what everybody should be looking at, I think, because, you know, the old saying about midterms, it`s all about who shows up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: And that`s what`s the struggle with midterms, is getting people to the polls. And I think we have more motivation on the Democratic side this year than anybody anticipated we would have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There is -- it feels a lot more like a `98, like, odd scenario that we had there.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche, let me play for you -- I did ask them about 2024 and President Biden. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: 2024, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , should he run?\par \par \uc2 VAN OOSTEN: I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 VAN OOSTEN: I honestly don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Wow. You guys are all nos.\par \par \uc2 VAN OOSTEN: No.\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: I`m a bit on the fence because I`m not too sure who`s going to fill the spot.\par \par \uc2 VAN OOSTEN: That`s the challenge.\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: And I think that was the reason why I voted for him in the first place, is it was a safer middle ground to kind of pull us away from the extremism we were tending toward on either side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yamiche, I wanted to play that clip because I wanted to also remind people these really are independent voters. There is this -- and in Arizona, they really have been turned off by both parties.\par \par \uc2 But the Biden situation, you know, I`ve asked this question to swing voters all over the country. Some of them are Democrats, some are independents, and they`ve all said the same answer. They all think he`s too old to run again.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Well, the key issue will be whether or not them thinking President Biden is too old outweighs them thinking that the future of our democracy is on the line. And when I talk to voters, they are -- even if, even Democrats who say, you know, Biden, he`s a little slower. He`s a little bit quieter. I`m not sure if I want to back him, when they think about the state of our democracy, they`re, like, well, that`s the bigger issue here. In our NBC poll, we saw people say that the sort of threats to democracy are their number one thing, even above cost of living.\par \par \uc2 So I think if you`re a Democrat, even if you`re \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  looking at this and saying, okay, I understand people think maybe I should be a little bit more lively, you`re still thinking they say -- they use the words unrecognizable, distrust, and that there should be some compromise.\par \par \uc2 Those are words that I think are probably worse for Republicans, especially when you think of while we might have a minority of Republicans who don`t believe that 2020 was fair, they`re the people with the loudest voices, right? They`re the people who are still winning the elections. That`s where the candidates are.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that, to me, is probably signaling that Republicans have a bigger problem here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Claire, I think it was Amy Jensen, I think is her name, the middle woman in our group there. I found what she said, well, it depends on what the other side is doing, right?\par \par \uc2 Her vote for Biden was exactly the premise that Biden ran on, which is he knew there were people like her that would vote for him, maybe not vote for a more progressive Democrat, but would vote for him because of Trump.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: And that`s what I think his Philadelphia speech was about. I think it was all about trying to invite the Amys of America --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: -- into the tent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not that Amy, right?\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Not that Amy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: The Amy that you interviewed in Arizona. I think that`s what it was about.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, I do think the Democratic Party is struggling with an issue that is real. And that is that all of our leadership, and I say this as somebody who`s ancient, you know --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s us.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: -- a lot of old folks --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: -- are running the Democratic Party now in every part of the Democratic Party. I think the Democratic Party will benefit from really doing some navel-gazing about how can we get more young people to the forefront? Because young people are going to be really important to us in 2024.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, ironically, Matt, if Republicans have a good election night, particularly on the House side, it`s actually going to be because it does seem like House Republicans, Kevin McCarthy`s campaign arm, really went after a diverse set of candidates.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: They did a phenomenal job recruiting --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Not like the Senate side. The House side is in a much better place - -\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: House side really went after it. I mean, I think a good story, a good candidate, is worth about two points on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And look, you want to talk about fundraising, we had about 20 candidates back in 2020 raise a million dollars through July 2020. Now, we have over 90. So it`s not only good on the ballot, it helps you raise money, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Boy, it`s amazing how poor the Senate Republican candidates are compared to the House candidates.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: The class. And yet, and yet --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And yet.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- no matter what happens on Election Day this year, it is not going to impact the views of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right? If the Senate stays in Democratic hands, even though every single one of those Senate -- Republican Senate candidates was endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I do not think that that`s going to make a reassessment --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: -- of whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  should be on the ballot in 2024.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Even though in every other political election --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- we`ve ever covered, that actually has mattered.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: That actually matters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But with him, it defies it. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: But it didn`t -- it didn`t matter in 2021. Didn`t matter in 2022. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And this Amy has the last word.\par \par \uc2 That`s all we have for today. Thank you for watching. We`ll be back next week because if it`s Sunday, it`s "MEET THE PRESS".\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal.  ALL RIGHTS   RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document MTPR000020220912ei9b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par {\*\bkmkstart toc99}{\*\bkmkend toc99}\b \uc2 DATELINE for September 9, 2022, NBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lester Holt, Andrea Canning \par \uc2 7,403 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2022\par \uc2 NBC News: Dateline NBC\par \uc2 DATL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 NBCUniversal. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Transcription Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:00:15]\par \par \uc2 LESTER HOLT, NBC DATELINE HOST: I`m Lester Holt. Tonight on Dateline, a dramatic new development in the story of a wife, a mistress, and maybe a murderer.\par \par \uc2 BONNIE CONTRERAS, BILL HALL`S MISTRESS: How many more bombshells is there going to be?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: She goes, I`m having an affair with your husband.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA CANNING, NBC DATELINE CORRESPONDENT: You`re getting text messages from Bonnie of the two of them having sex.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: She saw me and then made the U turn. I looked in my rearview mirror and it was Frances.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I get her little finger.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): The middle finger?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Right. I start following her.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: I said, what the hell.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: She brakes. And I brake and I forgot to stop.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He`s pronounced dead at the hospital.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I lost my husband, the man that I love.\par \par \uc2 TIMOTHY LOVETT, PROSECUTION COLLISION EXPERT: This is not an accident.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT SIMPSON, PROSECUTOR: She doesn`t stop the vehicle. If it`s an accident, you stop your vehicle.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I did not deliberately hurt him.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): A lot of hatred and anger in this case.\par \par \uc2 NICO LAHOOD, CRIMINAL DISTRICT ATTORNEY: A lot.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH CUTTER, FRANCES HALL`S ATTORNEY: To see manipulation, you have all the elements.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALL, BILL HALL`S DAUGHTER: Her story changed, I don`t know how many times.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): How do we know who to believe?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: That`s the world`s decision.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): Here`s Andrea Canning with "Collision."\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): This is the story of two intense women battling for the heart of one much loved man. It`s about sex, secrets, and lies, red hot Texas passion and a bitter feud, all leading to a final showdown on a two lane highway.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Bexar County 911.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I got a black Escalade and a Range Rover. They`re hauling ass, acting like idiots.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): A high speed chase that went horribly, horribly wrong.\par \par \uc2 N. HALL: It was a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): When the dust settled, one life would be over. Many others shattered.\par \par \uc2 HANK HALL, BILL HALL`S COUSIN: We all lost here.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): And tonight, there`s a Hall new chapter. San Antonio, Texas, home of the Alamo, The Riverwalk, and the Hall family. Three generations of truckers with deep roots in the city. It was here that Bill Hall met the love of his life at the tender age of 16. Her name was Frances.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What happened the first time you laid eyes on Bill?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I just fell in love with him. I knew this was the man I was going to marry.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: At 16?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: At 16.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): They married right out of high school. A year later, daughter Nikki was born.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Where your parents in love?\par \par \uc2 N. HALL: Oh yes. We definitely saw love growing up in the house.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I love my baby.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): For years after Nikki came Justin. Bill worked hard to give his young family a better life.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Bill used to clean airplanes. He went to college. And on the weekends if he was off, he`d go dig ditches. We made ends meet.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): But his true passion was big rigs. So one day he up and quit school started his own business, Bill Hall Jr. Trucking.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: That`s what his family did for a living so it`s in their blood.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bill got his first truck and went to work delivering topsoil, while Frances hit the phones to drum up new business.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: He was a dreamer. And he always said I`m going to go big vieja. Vieja is a Hispanic term, which means old lady.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): After just a few years, Bill and Frances turned that one truck into a fleet of 130. At its height, their empire was valued at more than $15 million.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): All his dreaming paid off.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: And hard work. He put his life his heart and soul into it. Bill was Bill Hall Jr. Trucking.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): But for Bill, it wasn`t all work and no play.\par \par \uc2 N. HALL: He was a daredevil. He loved racing. And in his later years he loved hunting. He was a man`s man.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): And also a bit of a ladies man according to Bill`s cousin Hank.\par \par \uc2 H. HALL: You know beautiful women. You work hard, you play hard, and you do things, you know, that you`re not supposed to be doing. He was a great man but he was not the perfect man.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bill`s imperfections became painfully clear nearly 32 years into their marriage when Frances says she got a call from a mysterious woman. It was September 13th, 2013, Friday the 13th.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:14]\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: She goes, you don`t know me but I`m having an affair with your husband.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): Wow.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I was in shock.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Heartbroken, furious. Frances says she confronted Bill and asked, was it true?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: By that time he is crying, and telling me that he`s sorry? That it`s true. I was devastated.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): This is three decades of marriage.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: And I finally asked him, how long has this been going on? He cried again. Told me, three years. And I go, this is not an affair, this is a relationship.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): About that and only that she and the other woman agreed.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): You don`t like the word mistress, do you?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): This is Bonnie Contreras.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What is it about that word that bothers you?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Mistress, it just sounds like I don`t want to say, yes, that I was the side person because I was more than that to him. This man was the man I wanted to marry and spend the rest of my life with.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bonnie was a 28-year-old single mom when they met, about 20 years younger than Bill and Frances. She