r \uc2 F. HALL: She knew where my kids lived. She knew my schedule. He said that I would go to my grocery store. And she`d be there following me.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Even said the Halls followed her and Bill into a movie theater.\par \par \uc2 N. HALL: Bonnie track them and was sitting three rows behind them. She was kind of like a fatal attraction just or cycle.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): She claimed that she was in love with Bill, that he was going to leave you, they were going to start a family, they were going to be married.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: She got an abortion. Tell me if she was starting with them. She got an abortion.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bill wanted Bonnie to have an abortion, said Frances. But Bonnie wouldn`t do it unless Bill gave her something in return.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: The only way she`d get an abortion if you bought her breast implants. Which she did, she got rid of the baby.\par \par \uc2 N. HALL: I despise her. And she has not stopped embarrassing our family. It`s like can you just go away? Can you just leave us alone, go find another married man because this is what she does for a living. She strips or she gets with married men.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): In the end said the Halls, Bonnie told Frances about the affair as a final act of desperation, an attempt to hold on to Bill who was about to drop her.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I think she finally thought, hey, I`m losing this man. I`m losing my bread and butter here, her sugar daddy. This girl was not in love with Bill. I don`t even think she knows what the word love means.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Of course, Bonnie sees things very differently.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): They`ve really gone after you with names.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Of course. And I understand where the Halls are coming from. It`s a pretty big pill to swallow when your husband wants to divorce you after 32 years, you know, and of course they`re never going to say anything good about me.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: Do you want to clear it up, were you an exotic dancer?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: I worked at a strip club. Yes. And that was way before.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: As a dancer?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Yes. And that was a mistake that, I mean, I regret that, no one is perfect.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): But the fake \uc2 Facebook\uc2  accounts stalking Frances, the abortion in exchange for breast implants. None of that ever happened, said Bonnie.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: For her to sit there and say that I switched a baby`s life that was going inside of me coming from a man that I love dearly for breast augmentation, ridiculousness.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Each woman clung to her truth. The battle for Bill raged.\par \par \uc2 H. HALL: He couldn`t stop Bonnie and really couldn`t stop Frances really, you know.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): It sounds like two hurricanes.\par \par \uc2 H. HALL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Then about a week before Bill`s death, the hurricanes collided. Not on the road. Not yet. This war was waged via text.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): You`re getting text messages from Bonnie of the two of them having sex.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): Coming up, the case heads to court where one expert testifies that Frances deliberately rammed Bonnie.\par \par \uc2 LOVETT: This is not an accident.\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): While another expert provides a demonstration that seems to show the SUVs never even touched.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES RUBLE, ACCIDENT RECONSTRUCTIONIST: That simple physics.\par \par \uc2 [22:38:57]\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): When Dateline continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): The battle over Bill Hall Jr. had gone on for weeks. And it all led up to a confrontation about a week before his death. Bill and Frances met at this hotel. She said they were trying to work things out. But then Bonnie showed up at the front desk.\par \par \uc2 N. HALL: Saying she was his wife and she needed to get access to his room and my dad told them, no, she`s not my wife. My wife is here with me.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Security asked Bonnie to leave though she insists she never claimed to be Bill`s wife. But all parties agree that night an all- out war began via text.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): You`re getting text messages from Bonnie of the two of them having sex.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Pictures of sexual positions.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Photos with captions like, this is what he really likes you fat old woman.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Normally the mistress keeps a very low profile.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: She wanted to make sure I knew now she was in the picture.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Did she ever? Many of the messages are too graphic to show. But you can get a good sense of them from these excerpts. Let me know if you want some good juicy pics of me and your hubby, you stupid old person, keep fixing yourself up maybe he`ll find you more attractive.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): What`s the worst text message she sent you?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: The hollow bitch.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: She actually called you --\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Hollow.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: Because?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Of my hysterectomy.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Complete with a diagram to reinforce the point. Bonnie admits she sent those nasty texts.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): You have to imagine though that people are going to see this and they`re going to think that some of those text messages are cruel.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: I never sent her pictures of me and Bill having sexual intercourse. What I did send her pictures of was three butt pics of me. And that`s because she infuriated me because she told me that my butt looked like a broken saddlebag. It looked like a broken toilet seat. Trust me. She was going at me very hard.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): In fact, Bonnie, says it was Frances who started the text war.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Plain and simple, I was defending myself.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (on camera): The texts that Frances said hurt her the most was the one where you called her hollow and sent the diagram of a woman who had had a hysterectomy.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: That is cruel.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: That would hit a woman hard.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: And I`m not happy about that.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: Why did you do it?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Because I was pissed at her. She was telling me vicious ugly thing.\par \par \uc2 [22:45:00]\par \par \uc2 CANNING: Did you send any back to her that were nasty as well not really, no, because it`d be hard not to engage.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Well, yes, I started to at the hotel, but I think I did one or two.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Had them in the palm of your hand and couldn`t keep him. Frances wrote to Bonnie, keep sending fatal attraction. Cops need all the pics.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I mean, I couldn`t keep up, I text slow. This girl`s young. She knows technology.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bonnie says Frances more than held her own. Don`t flatter yourself. He`s been cheating on you with me. But says Frances, the nastier text, Bonnie, received didn`t come from her. She says Bill sent them.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: Bill grabbed the phone and he said let me take care of her.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: No, he didn`t. That was her. It was her sending those text messages. Bill wouldn`t have never disrespected me like that.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): But if there was any doubt that she`d been following the Halls, she seemed to confirm it herself by sending photos of their house and Frances`s car.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Why were you sending those pictures?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: To piss her off. To defend myself.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: Why, what are you -- I mean, you`re the one --\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Her telling me that bill didn`t love me, her telling me that Bill was using me.\par \par \uc2 CANNING: But Bonnie, you`re the one who`s having the affair with Bill. So why are you attacking his wife?\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: Because she was attacking me and I will defend myself.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): One week after that text message viewed came the car chase that left Bill Hall dead. Nearly three years after that, Frances Hall went on trial for murder. Prosecutors Stephanie Paulissen and Scott Simpson laid out the case for the jury.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE PAULISSEN, PROSECUTOR: We understand why she was angry. But that`s not justification to go attempt to run down your husband`s mistress and kill him in the process.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): The star witness for the prosecution, Bonnie Contreras.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: I start to get hit from behind.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bonnie told the jury how she watched with horror as she believed Frances deliberately knocked Bill off the road.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: -- can fly off the motorcycle.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bonnie said she later discovered Frances`s daughter Nikki Hall had left a message on her voicemail during the car chase, a message that seemed to speak to Frances`s intent that evening.\par \par \uc2 N. HALL: I told you to watch your back, you -- stupid slut.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: What does that tell you? That they were out to cause harm to somebody.\par \par \uc2 LOVETT: This is not an accident.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Tim Lovett was the state`s collision reconstruction expert. He said the evidence showed Frances chased and hit Bonnie.\par \par \uc2 LOVETT: That Escalade definitely hit that Range Rover at least two maybe three times. But Frances`s defense attorneys, Leigh Cutter and Jean Brown said investigators made a big mistake when they bought into the story of Bonnie Contreras.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: Everyone got their information from one person at the scene. That`s Bonnie and Bonnie lied her ass off.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Defense accident reconstructionist Charles Ruble said there was no physical evidence to back up Bonnie`s claims that Frances had hit her over and over again, no evidence he said the SUVs had even touched.\par \par \uc2 RUBLE: Not even once.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): And remember how Bonnie said her purse flew forward off the seat when Frances hit her?\par \par \uc2 RUBLE: This is her purse in her car. I get hit in the rear, where`s my purse go? It doesn`t come on the front. That`s simple physics.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): In fact, he said the purse falling forward supports Frances`s claims that Bonnie was repeatedly slamming on her brakes.\par \par \uc2 RUBLE: She does a sudden break and she`s moving like this. Then the purse would go into the floorboard. Bonnie Contreras is not telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): What`s more, both defense and prosecution agreed Bonnie was wrong about her contention that Bill`s motorcycle was struck from behind. Evidence showed the motorcycle actually collided with the side of Frances`s SUV.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: I can`t believe a damn thing she said on there.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): In closing arguments, the defense said Bill`s death was simply a terrible accident. And Frances Hall had suffered enough.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: She`s been living in hell for three years. Let her go home. Let her fell her family and let her warn her husband.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): But the prosecution told the jury, Frances must be held accountable for her actions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Of course, the defendant hated Bonnie Contreras. We don`t blame her for that, any wife would. What we do blame her for getting in her Escalade chasing after Bonnie and then veering into Bill. There`s your felony murder.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): The jury deliberated into the night. Who would they believe?\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): Coming up, the verdict.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We the jury find the defendant --\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): And then this story about death on the highway takes off in a brand new direction.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:57]\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: How many more bombshells is there going to be?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): The jury deciding Frances Hall`s fate deliberated into the evening hours of September 8th, 2016. And then, just before 8:45, a verdict.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We, the jury, find the defendant, Frances Hall, guilty of murder --\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Guilty of murder and aggravated assault. Frances`s friends and family were shocked.\par \par \uc2 H. HALL: It was the worst feeling in my life. It was a moment Frances herself did not seem prepared for. But things weren`t over yet for Frances, there was still a second phase of trial sentencing. Texas law allows the defendant to be sentenced by either the judge or the jury. Frances chose the jury, the same one that found her guilty. As her attorneys made the case for leniency, they found a secret weapon and the Texas criminal code.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: We said this caveat this was written for Frances Hall, if any whatever.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:01]\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): The law calls it sudden passion, essentially that the defendant was immediately provoked to a degree of anger that would make an ordinary person incapable of cool reflection. That offense told the jury sudden passion gripped Frances when she spotted Bill and Bonnie on the road.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: She sees this woman who has not led up and tried to ruin her life for the last month.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): The stakes were enormous. A finding of sudden passion could drastically reduce Frances`s sentence from possible life in prison to a maximum of 20 years and a minimum of just two. For the first time, the jury heard from Frances Hall directly.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: You don`t understand, I love this man hearted whole. I gave him my life. I would have never hurt him, never.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): In her final arguments, Defense Attorney Leigh Cutter insisted Frances Hall`s actions were a textbook example of sudden passion.\par \par \uc2 CUTTER: The text messages, the calls, the taunting, those would probably make you really hate someone, but never having seen them until their drive by in your car. If that`s not sudden passion, where you are incapable of thinking clearly I don`t know what it is.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): But prosecutor Scott Simpson told jurors not to be swayed by emotions.\par \par \uc2 SIMPSON: You shouldn`t for one second, feel ashamed or bad for doing what you believe to be right.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): After about three hours of deliberations, the jury was ready.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We unanimously find the defendant caused the death of Bill Hall under the immediate influence of sudden passion. We assess her punishments return for two years.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Two years, the absolute minimum sentence. On September 7th, 2018, after serving her time, Frances walked out of prison a free woman. We thought that this was the end of the story, but we were wrong. There`s a whole new chapter. This summer Frances was indicted in a multimillion dollar fraud scheme. She`s accused of providing false payroll information to get lower insurance premiums for the company she and Bill founded.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Texas Department of Insurance\uc2  said the scheme allowed the company and its owners to avoid more than $9 million in premium payments. We plan to speak with Frances in person this week, but due to COVID concerns we met via Zoom. She denies the charges and the indictment.\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I`m an honest woman. I didn`t do anything illegal. I didn`t falsified anything. I`m innocent. I don`t have anything to hide.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: How many more bombshells is there going to be?\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): We also caught up again with Bonnie Contreras to hear her thoughts on Frances`s latest troubles.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: She looks like a liar. She looks like a con. She just -- she looks bad all the way around.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Bonnie now runs some businesses of her own, including a CBD spa. She believes the new charges just might change some people`s perceptions of Frances and herself.\par \par \uc2 CONTRERAS: They were constantly calling me a gold digger. So who looks like the gold digger now? Is it me? Or is it her?\par \par \uc2 F. HALL: I don`t even think she knows what the word gold digger means. What I`m going through right now has nothing to do with gold digging. Now what she did on her free time with my husband, that`s gold digging. I have Bill`s kids, I have Bill`s grandkids, something she`ll never have. And I have all the memories of Bill. That`s not a gold digger. That`s a wife. So she should have maybe looked it up on the dictionary first.\par \par \uc2 CANNING (voice-over): Frances told us if the new case goes to trial, she`ll take the stand. It worked for her at the sentencing hearing in Bill`s murder. But insurance fraud is a serious crime too. And there`s no sudden passion defense. If convicted. The woman who served just two years for killing her husband will face the possibility of life in prison yet again.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:16]\par \par \uc2 HOLT (voice-over): That`s all for this edition of Dateline. We`ll see you again next Friday at 9:00, 8:00 Central as we begin our 31st season with all new two our mysteries. And of course, I`ll see you each week night for NBC Nightly News. I`m Lester Holt for all of us at NBC News, good night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022